{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md'","aggregated_output":"# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n\nAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n\n| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n\n## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n\n- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n  system (D263).\n- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n  competition or pricing\".\n- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n  of the previous filing topic).\n- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n\n## 5. What to do\n\nStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n\n1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n     the existing rule).\n   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '10541,10594p;10660,10689p;10825,10950p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; rg -n 'D720|whole.and.part' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md docs/DECISIONS.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '10541,10594p;10660,10689p;10825,10950p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; rg -n 'D720|whole.and.part' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md docs/DECISIONS.md\"","aggregated_output":"## D702 · 2026-11-21 · The census of link decisions passes its registered gate; council R: probe a sharpened link question before buying judgements\n\n- **Result** (`docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/CENSUS-RESULT.md`, `census-pairs.md`; $0.0266 settled of Alex's $0.10, D700):\n  three counted draws over the whole window; the known positive (v9 Data Center reads v14 total revenue, together) right in 3 of 3,\n  margins 0.05, 0.09, 0.03; no link on any of the 38 pairs whose shown material names no connection (114 decisions). Gate PASS.\n- **Beside the score:** 8 pairs proposed in some draw; part-to-whole and whole-to-part both occur; v21–v9 flips direction; 4 of 8 vary\n  in presence or direction. Council R round 17 (`codex-r17.md`): v17 (FY2028 revenue growth) reads v16 (FY2027 CPU revenue), together,\n  in 3 of 3 draws is a likely sign error (a higher prior-year base lowers next-year growth at a fixed total; no next-year mechanism\n  shown). Claude's lean to measure the current wording was withdrawn.\n- **Consensus (council R, round 17):** before the paid judgement run, probe a sharpened READS/SIGN wording on the same 105 pairs at\n  cents (keep the known positive and the 38 negatives; remove unsupported links, including the base effect of a prior-year part on a\n  growth rate; no blanket ban on a whole reading its part). A wording change changes what the system does: it goes to Alex with the\n  options before its probe. If the wording keeps the useful links and drops the unsupported ones, it becomes the candidate for the paid\n  design; if it loses the known positive or adds wrong links, the current wording stays the candidate with its measured risks.\n\n## D703 · 2026-11-21 · Alex chooses to probe a sharpened link question before the paid run\n\n- **The question** (after D702): \"连题统计通过了事先定的关 … 问题是它在'部分读整体'和'整体读部分'之间拿不定主意，而且有一条很可能连反了 … 下一步怎么走？\"\n  Options: 先试新问法 (recommended: with Codex at $0, write the link question clearly, keeping Data Center reads total revenue and the\n  38 negatives, removing unsupported links including the base effect, no blanket ban on a whole reading its part; then ask the same\n  105 pairs three times, about 3 cents, from the 7 cents left of the approved 10; then decide the paid run); 直接正式运行 (the current\n  wording, about $7 estimated, ceiling later); 停在这里 (poc-v118 stays, the linking version paused).\n- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-21): \"先试新问法 (推荐)\".\n- **Decided (Alex):** a sharpened READS/SIGN wording is designed with the council at $0 and probed on the same 105 pairs, three draws,\n  within the remaining $0.073 of D700's ledger. Then the paid run is decided.\n\n## D704 · 2026-11-21 · Council R passes the version-2 link question for its probe (rounds 18–19); the probe is sent under D703\n\n- **The wording** (`docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/WORDING-V2.md`, harness-only, no product file changed): a tie-break (when\n  either question could read the other, the one less anchored by guidance, consensus or a reported figure for the quantity and full\n  period it asks about reads the other; no direction banned); a periods clause (quantities of different periods are linked only when\n  the supplied text states how one moves the other); a base-effect SIGN example. The tie-break and the periods clause are proposals\n  this probe tests under D703; D537 decided only the Data Center case.\n- **The council:** round 18 NOT READY (the gate could pass while keeping an unsupported cross-period link; the tie-break's period and\n  the periods example needed precision); round 19 READY FOR THE V2 PROBE. Registration for version 2, written before its answers: R 1,\n  W 40 (the census's 38 plus 321:v16 and 1238:v9, no bridge across periods), O 61, F 3 (a growth rate reading an earlier revenue amount\n  with the sign together). $0: the dry pass complete and equal to old-2 with every job carrying the version-2 texts; its mutant (texts\n  not patched) stops before any send; the stored census answers fail the version-2 gate, the round-18 counterexample fails it, a\n  control with both unsupported links removed passes.\n- **Sent:** `window_test.py probe-v2`, draws 1, 2, 3 one after another on D700's ledger ($0.0734 left); a ceiling stop before draw 3\n  is an incomplete probe with no verdict.\n\n## D705 · 2026-11-21 · The version-2 link question passes its registered gate; council R: version 2 goes to the paid design\n\n- **Result** (`docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PROBE-V2-RESULT.md`, `probe-v2-pairs.md`; $0.0309 settled, $0.0575 of D700's\n  $0.10 in all): the known positive right in 3 of 3 (margins 0.03, 0.07, 0.13); no W link (120 decisions) and no F link (9). Gate PASS.\n  Against the current wording: both unsupported links across periods removed, the v21–v9 flip gone; two defensible links lost (v2 reads\n  v1, v35 reads v14); one weak link added in 3 of 3, v14 (total revenue) reads v8 (stand-alone CPU), against the tie-break's direction.\n- **Consensus (council R round 20, `codex-r20.md`):** version 2 becomes the candidate: built as a separately versioned, reviewed\n  product configuration, then the paid design (the whole chain kept and read step by step; paired fresh controls for v14 and v9; the\n  CPU link's effect on the total measured before any Data Center credit). No third wording chosen on the same 105 pairs. The paid send\n  waits for the design's pass and a ceiling Alex names (D699).\n\n## D706 · 2026-11-21 · poc-v120 (link questions version 2) passes council review as a product configuration; the paid design needs four changes\n## D711 · 2026-11-21 · Pilot pair 1 is inconclusive (control double refusal); the comparison is closed; council round 37 recommends a citation-only judge version to Alex\n\n- **Alex's words** (2026-11-21, on the stopped control): \"好你跑一次council 看trace 同意了就开始下一步\".\n- **Registered verdict:** inconclusive. control-1 (poc-v118) stopped on 09-08 when v21's answer and its retry each cited an id outside\n  the listed evidence (`e362` for `e3620`; `e3111` beside `e3110`/`e3112`), PAID-DESIGN.md §4 (2). linked-1 completed with every\n  acceptance check true. control-1 equals old-2 on every H2 table through 09-07. Spend $1.70 of $2.50.\n- **Exploratory, one pair, one draw, beside the verdict** (`whole-window/PILOT-RESULT.md`):\n  - P: the parser counts 3 of 60 dates (linked-1) against 28 of 60 (control-1) on 07-10..09-07. All 12 flagged linked dates were read:\n    none has Data Center above total revenue (parser artefacts). The 5 sampled control dates are real.\n  - H4: four linked v14 answers (07-15..07-18) add v8's stand-alone CPU forecast to a total built from company guidance, read by Claude\n    and, shuffled, by Codex.\n  - H1: 5 linked against 1 control refusal on the 200 matched reader-days, at the harm line.\n  - Every refused judge answer seen is an id-membership error: 17 in 5,408 distinct attempts. 6 of linked-1's 7 fresh reader refusals\n    put an id in the wrong list, though the request's closing instruction forbids it.\n- **Council round 37** (`round37-brief.md`, `codex-r37.md`, `claude-r37.md`, `round37b-brief.md`, `codex-r37b.md`), consensus A:\n  - pair 1 is closed, with no redraw (B) and no resume (C);\n  - C would move H1 across its line through the refusals that invalidated the control, and its rule was proposed after the stop was\n    seen;\n  - Claude proposed C first and withdrew it on linked-1's H1 numbers;\n  - a resume-once rule may be registered only for a future comparison, before its send, in both arms, and proven on stored data.\n- **Recommendation to Alex (his to open, a new version of the frozen judge):**\n  - The version: a judge version that changes the citation format only. Every id field (cites, push ids, `cites_views`, theme\n    pushes) takes a closed list built from the exact request.\n  - The test, at $0, end to end: stored accepted answers fit; observed bad ids are excluded; an empty list is the only answer for a\n    field with no allowed id.\n  - A separate design, not part of this version: the link direction (a whole reading its own part). The keys label stand-alone CPU\n    `other`, and the direction is a judgement, which D704 left open on purpose.\n- **Not done:** no paid call after the pilot, no product file changed.\n\n## D712 · 2026-11-21 · Alex opens a citation-only judge version (closed per-request id lists), tested at $0 first\n## D719 · 2026-11-22 · Alex stops the citation line: judging-v12 is not adopted; poc-v118 stays the product\n\n- **The question:** \"新的引用格式（每栏只能从名单里选编号）实测的正式结果是\"未就绪\"。它确实消除了编号写错导致的退回：48 个回答 0 次退回，原来是 17 次。但有一组对照里，它两次写\"彭博预测 3,930 亿\"，引的却是雅虎汇总的同一数字。这种\"机构名和引用对不上\"的毛病旧格式也有，目前样本太小，分不清新格式是否让它变多。下一步怎么做？\"\n  Options:\n  - 先停在这里 (recommended: poc-v118 stays; the new format's code is kept but not enabled; no further spend);\n  - 设计一个后续测试;\n  - 推翻结论、直接采用新格式 (not recommended).\n- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22): \"先停在这里 (推荐)\".\n- **Effect:**\n  - poc-v118 remains the product configuration.\n  - judging-v12, poc-v121, the validator's `maxItems`, preflight's saved-format check and their tests stay in the working tree,\n    uncommitted and enabled by no product launcher.\n  - The link-direction design (D711) and the citation follow-up are not started. No further spend.\n\n## D720 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens the double-count design; the diagnosis finds the class in the frozen product too\n\n- **The context:** Claude's report after D719 listed two items not started, each waiting for Alex: the design of the fix for the\n  total revenue adding the CPU forecast again (D711, H4), and a citation follow-up.\n- **Alex's words** (2026-11-22): \"开\".\n- **Claude's reading, not confirmed by Alex:** \"开\" opens the double-count design. The citation follow-up stays stopped, because\n  Alex declined it under D719 minutes earlier.\n- **Scope:** diagnosis and design at $0 with the council. Any paid step, and any new version of a frozen piece, asks Alex first.\n- **The diagnosis** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/DIAGNOSIS.md`, $0):\n  - The class is a judge adding a component's target to a total whose company-wide basis already contains it.\n  - It occurs in the frozen product without any link. In old-1, 32 total-revenue answers add the $20B stand-alone CPU target, which\n    was filed under total revenue as evidence. The addition decides the rung on at least 4 days.\n  - In linked-1 it occurs through the link: 4 answers, plus 4 copied by Data Center.\n  - old-2, old-3 and control-1 do not add it into the figure.\n  - The request sentences behind it:\n    - the product-target sentence of judging-v7, applied to a whole;\n    - a consistency clause that covers only a part exceeding its whole.\n  - The fix's owner is the judge text, not the link question.\n- **Next:** council round 43 on the diagnosis, then one recommendation and one test to Alex.\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1064:| D720 | 2026-11-22 | Alex opens the double-count design (\"开\"; Claude's reading, the citation follow-up stays stopped); the $0 diagnosis finds the class in the frozen product too (old-1: 32 total-revenue answers add the stand-alone CPU target filed under them, 4 rung decisions; linked-1: 4 through the link); the owner is the judge text | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/DIAGNOSIS.md` |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v7.txt; cmp src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v{7,8}.txt; rg -n '\"'^def |accounting identities'\"' src/hivemind/views/judging.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v7.txt; cmp src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v{7,8}.txt; rg -n '\"'^def |accounting identities'\"' src/hivemind/views/judging.py\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\n     4\t- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- “Themes in force” lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked “updated since this thesis was last judged” may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- A dependency marked “not yet created” supplies no input. A dependency marked “historical only” is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\n     9\t- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\n    10\t- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n    11\t- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\n39:def require_supported(prompt_version):\n45:def evidence_in_view(conn, view_id):\n58:def schema_for(prompt_version, lists=None):\n89:def _open_format(prompt_version):\n111:def themes_in_force(conn, view_id):\n137:def topological(conn, view_ids):\n156:def _indented(text, indent='        '):\n161:def _dependency_lines_full(conn, target, d, state, rung_label, tv, flag):\n172:def _lines_english(conn, view_id, v, rungs, pieces, deps, last, themes=(), hidden=0, full_dependencies=False):\n220:def build_prompt(conn, view_id, prompt_version):\n225:def request_parts(conn, view_id, prompt_version):\n243:def retry_note(errors):\n247:def themes_shown_in(request_text):\n253:def evidence_shown_in(request_text):\n259:def check_answer(conn, view_id, data, request_text=None, lists=None):\n306:def _record(conn, cfg, view_id, seq, item, answer):\n322:def upstream(conn, view_id):\n334:def open_asset_views(conn):\n338:def queued_by_change(conn, view_id):\n347:def queued_by_ledger(conn, seq):\n356:def round_of_judgements(archive, cfg, conn, changed_views, seq, item, *, transport=None):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/views.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(sql for t,sql in c.execute(\\\"select name,sql from sqlite_master where type=\\\\x27table\\\\x27\\\") if t in {\\\"judgements\\\",\\\"rungs\\\",\\\"views\\\",\\\"dependencies\\\",\\\"evidence\\\",\\\"evidence_links\\\"}))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/views.sqlite?mode=ro\\\",uri=True); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(sql for t,sql in c.execute(\\\"select name,sql from sqlite_master where type=\\\\x27table\\\\x27\\\") if t in {\\\"judgements\\\",\\\"rungs\\\",\\\"views\\\",\\\"dependencies\\\",\\\"evidence\\\",\\\"evidence_links\\\"}))'\"","aggregated_output":"CREATE TABLE views (view_id text primary key, object_id text, object_kind text, kind text, looks_at text, question text, due_day text, year text, created_seq integer, created_from_input text, created_at text)\nCREATE TABLE rungs (view_id text, position integer, label text, condition text, lo real, hi real)\nCREATE TABLE judgements (judgement_id integer primary key autoincrement, view_id text, seq integer, input_id text, rung integer, reasons text, cited_evidence_json text, cited_views_json text, pushes_json text, changed integer, why_changed text, config_checksum text, call_id integer, request_sha256 text, publication_day text, theme_pushes_json text, reference text, implied_outcome text)\nCREATE TABLE evidence (evidence_id text primary key, quote text, one_line text, speaker text, original_who text, original_day text, tier text, event_time text, created_seq integer, created_from_input text)\nCREATE TABLE evidence_links (id integer primary key autoincrement, evidence_id text, view_id text, seq integer)\nCREATE TABLE dependencies (dependency_id integer primary key autoincrement, view_id text, depends_on text, wait_chain_view_id text, wait_year text, direction text, seq integer)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nfor arm,vs in [(\\\"old-1\\\",(4,15)),(\\\"old-2\\\",(14,16)),(\\\"old-3\\\",(3,14)),(\\\"control-1\\\",(16,)),(\\\"linked-1\\\",(14,9,21,16))]:\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n for vn in vs:\n  v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n  rows=c.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n  print(arm,v,len(rows),c.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),c.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall())\n  print(\\\"range\\\",rows[0][:3] if rows else None,rows[-1][:3] if rows else None)\n  for row in rows:\n   print(\\\"|\\\".join(str(x).replace(\\\"\\\\n\\\",\\\" \\\") for x in row[:5]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nfor arm,vs in [(\\\"old-1\\\",(4,15)),(\\\"old-2\\\",(14,16)),(\\\"old-3\\\",(3,14)),(\\\"control-1\\\",(16,)),(\\\"linked-1\\\",(14,9,21,16))]:\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\n c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n for vn in vs:\n  v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n  rows=c.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n  print(arm,v,len(rows),c.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),c.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall())\n  print(\\\"range\\\",rows[0][:3] if rows else None,rows[-1][:3] if rows else None)\n  for row in rows:\n   print(\\\"|\\\".join(str(x).replace(\\\"\\\\n\\\",\\\" \\\") for x in row[:5]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"old-1 v4 80 (\"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\",) [(1, 'Strongly bullish', 431200000000.0, None), (2, 'Bullish', 392000000000.0, 431200000000.0), (3, 'Neutral', 352800000000.0, 392000000000.0), (4, 'Bearish', 313600000000.0, 352800000000.0), (5, 'Strongly bearish', None, 313600000000.0)]\nrange (70, '2026-07-02', 2) (9014, '2026-09-20', 2)\n70|2026-07-02|2|Wall Street analysts estimate FY2027 revenue of $392 billion; that is rung 2.|About $392 billion in FY2027, using the stated analyst estimate as the market reference. The $1 trillion orders expectation is for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell through 2027, not a quantified FY2027 revenue figure, so it cannot be translated into a higher annual outcome from the supplied evidence.\n101|2026-07-03|2|Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion in FY2027; this is rung 2, at its $392 billion lower boundary.|About $392 billion in FY2027, using the stated Wall Street estimate as the forecast anchor. The $1 trillion product-family sales expectation spans through the end of 2027 and is not allocated to FY2027, while the CPU and GPU deployment figures lack a usable FY2027 revenue contribution.\n124|2026-07-04|2|Wall Street's $392bn FY2027 revenue estimate; this is rung 2.|Approximately $392bn, using the stated FY2027 analyst estimate as the base case. The demand and capex signals add upside skew, but the supplied evidence does not quantify an increase to $431.2bn or above.\n150|2026-07-05|2|Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion in FY2027 revenue (e28); the $392 billion reference is rung 2, at its lower boundary.|The evidence implies $392 billion plus an upward contribution from the second-half data-center compute beat, but does not provide the dollar base or revenue mix needed to calculate that contribution. The 20% applies to one segment for half the fiscal year, so the evidence supports an outcome above the reference but does not make the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary the most likely result.\n286|2026-07-06|2|Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is $392 billion (e28), also described as 82% growth (e51). That is rung 2, at its $392 billion lower boundary.|Around $420 billion, in the low $400 billions: start from the $392 billion consensus reference, then allow a single-digit-percent gross uplift because SemiAnalysis's 20% upside applies only to roughly half-year second-half data-center compute revenue, a subset of total revenue. The delay/cancellation and China evidence temper that uplift but do not quantify an offset that moves the estimate below the reference.\n354|2026-07-07|2|Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion in FY2027 revenue (e28), also expressed as 82% FY2027 growth (e51). The $392 billion reference is the lower boundary of rung 2.|About $425 billion for FY2027: retain the prior roughly $420 billion central outlook supported by reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance, then allow a limited uplift for the $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast because the planned fall launch leaves few selling quarters and shipments are not yet shown. The resulting estimate remains below $431.2 billion.\n439|2026-07-08|2|Analyst consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e28, e234), within rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.|About $430 billion. Starting from the roughly $393 billion consensus, the reported $81.6 billion Q1 revenue and $91 billion Q2 guidance support a strong run-rate; applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% upside only to H2 data-center compute, rather than all revenue, implies an uplift that brings the total to roughly $430 billion.\n514|2026-07-09|2|Analyst consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234), placing the reference in rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.|About $430 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion reference and apply SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to the estimated H2 FY2027 data-center compute portion, adding roughly $37 billion rather than applying the uplift to total revenue. The resulting estimate is below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\n572|2026-07-10|2|Wall Street consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234, e299), placing the reference in rung 2.|About $430 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion consensus and add about $37 billion implied by SemiAnalysis’s 20% premium on H2 FY2027 data-center compute only, not on total revenue. The reported Rubin changes and other new risks are not sized into an FY2027 revenue reduction, so the evidence does not support a precise further adjustment.\n646|2026-07-12|2|Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $430 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion consensus and add about $37 billion by applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% premium only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute, not to total revenue.\n720|2026-07-13|2|Wall Street consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234), within rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $430 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion FY2027 consensus and apply SemiAnalysis’s 20% premium only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute, which implies about $37 billion of incremental revenue rather than a 20% increase to total revenue.\n853|2026-07-14|2|Wall Street consensus is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|Approximately $430 billion: apply SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute, implying roughly $37 billion above the approximately $393 billion consensus total. This remains below the $431.2 billion rung 1 threshold.\n978|2026-07-15|1|Bloomberg-tracked analyst estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234) are the market reference, in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $435 billion: starting from the prior estimate of roughly $430 billion after applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% second-half data-center compute upside to consensus (e154), I allow a net few billion dollars for a discounted share of the $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast (e65), given the planned fall launch and limited selling quarters (e64).\n1142|2026-07-16|1|Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue estimates of about $393 billion; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|Roughly $435 billion: about $393 billion consensus plus the previously derived roughly $37 billion SemiAnalysis second-half data-center compute uplift and a discounted few billion net contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target. The newly reported projects and supply outlook do not support a further quantified adjustment, while rack delays temper the estimate.\n1208|2026-07-17|1|Bloomberg-tracked analyst expectations of roughly $392–$393 billion for FY2027; rung 2 (Bullish).|About $435 billion: retain the prior roughly $393 billion consensus base, add about $37 billion implied by SemiAnalysis's estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue is 20% above Wall Street's expectation, and add only a few billion net for the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target after discounting it for execution and the short selling window. This is above the $431.2 billion rung 1 threshold.\n1236|2026-07-18|2|Bloomberg-tracked analyst estimates put FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (with other estimates around $392–$393 billion). That reference is rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.|About $425 billion. Starting from the previously supported bridge of roughly $393 billion consensus plus approximately $37 billion of SemiAnalysis-tracked second-half data-center compute upside and a few billion net from discounted CPU sales, I apply an approximately $10 billion risk haircut for the specific rack delays and cancellations, partly offset by expected substitution toward B300 and more Oberon-Rubin racks.\n1250|2026-07-19|2|Market expectations are about $393 billion for FY2027 (Bloomberg-tracked estimates), which is rung 2; the $392.7 billion analyst estimate is also rung 2.|About $425 billion, retaining the prior estimate’s bridge from the roughly $393 billion reference: second-half data-center compute upside, a discounted stand-alone CPU contribution, and a haircut for rack delays/cancellations. Q1 FY2027 revenue was $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion; the newly surfaced deployment plans are not assigned their full stated value because FY2027 shipment timing is not established.\n1334|2026-07-20|2|Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 2; the analyst estimate of $392.7 billion is in the same rung.|About $425 billion, carrying forward the prior estimate: reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance, together with SemiAnalysis’s estimate of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above Wall Street, imply upside to the $393 billion reference. The Noetra GPU plan is discounted because no deployment timing is given, and the rack delays/cancellation remain an offset; $425 billion is below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\n1462|2026-07-21|2|Wall Street's FY2027 total-revenue estimate is about $393 billion, placing the reference at rung 2; the comparable estimates of $392 billion and $392.7 billion are also rung 2.|About $425 billion for FY2027: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, combined with SemiAnalysis's quantified 20% upside estimate for second-half data-center compute versus Wall Street, support a total above the roughly $393 billion reference. The new Vera Rubin production and deployment evidence improves execution confidence but provides no quantified FY2027 revenue amount to revise that estimate further.\n1569|2026-07-22|2|Wall Street's roughly $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $425 billion: carry forward the prior estimate's translation of SemiAnalysis's expectation for second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above Wall Street, alongside the $81.6 billion Q1 result and $91 billion Q2 guidance. The newer production and customer evidence strengthens execution confidence but is not sized enough to revise that total.\n1691|2026-07-23|2|Wall Street's approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e811, e234) is in rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.|About $425 billion. This carries forward the prior run-rate calculation from Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, applying SemiAnalysis's 20% above-consensus estimate only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue, not to total revenue.\n1782|2026-07-24|2|Wall Street’s approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $425 billion: carry the Q1 actual and Q2 guidance run-rate forward, then apply the existing estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above Wall Street expectations only to that portion of revenue. This remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\n1853|2026-07-25|2|Wall Street’s average FY2027 revenue estimate is approximately $393 billion, which is rung 2.|About $425 billion, based on the Q1 actual and Q2 revenue guidance carried forward with the existing SemiAnalysis 20% uplift applied only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute. This is above the $392 billion rung-2 floor but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\n1924|2026-07-26|2|Wall Street’s approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $425 billion. Carrying forward the revenue run-rate indicated by Q1’s $81.62 billion and Q2 guidance of approximately $91 billion, with the existing SemiAnalysis estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute will be 20% above Wall Street expectations, supports roughly $425 billion; that remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\n2162|2026-07-27|2|Wall Street estimates about $393 billion for FY2027 (e28, e234), which is rung 2.|About $430 billion: retain the prior roughly $425 billion run-rate implied by Q1 revenue, Q2 guidance and the forecast of second-half data-center compute 20% above Wall Street expectations, then lift it modestly for confirmed Vera Rubin production/customer deployments and stronger infrastructure spending signals. The broad multi-year deal and spending totals are not counted in full as FY2027 NVIDIA revenue.\n2313|2026-07-28|2|Wall Street estimates of approximately $392–393 billion for FY2027 (e28, e234), placing the reference in rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.|Approximately $427 billion. The roughly $393 billion reference is lifted by strong reported Q1 revenue, $91 billion Q2 guidance, and SemiAnalysis’s 20% above-consensus estimate for second-half data-center compute (applied only to that portion, not total revenue); I temper the roughly $430 billion prior estimate slightly for competitive risk and discount the unshipped CPU sales target.\n2428|2026-07-29|1|Wall Street expects approximately $393 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 revenue, which is rung 2 (Bullish).|About $435 billion: the $393 billion reference, with $172.6 billion represented by Q1 actual revenue and Q2 guidance, leaves $220.4 billion for H2; the quantified H2 data-center compute upside and Rubin's production ramp support an outcome above the reference and above rung 1's $431.2 billion floor.\n2539|2026-07-30|1|Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is about $393 billion (e234), placing the reference in rung 2, Bullish.|About $440 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion consensus, add an uplift from SemiAnalysis's 20% H2 data-center compute view only to NVIDIA's relevant H2 compute revenue, and allow only a modest contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target because the product is not yet shipping and the target is not volume in hand.\n2631|2026-07-31|1|Wall Street estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027 place the reference in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $435–440 billion: carry the strong reported Q1 and guided Q2 growth through the remaining comparable quarters, then apply SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside only to the data-center compute component, not total revenue. Add only a modest, discounted contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target; Rubin’s multi-year revenue/order figures are not counted in full as FY2027 revenue.\n2674|2026-08-01|1|Wall Street's roughly $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e234, e811) is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $438 billion. Starting from the roughly $393 billion consensus, the prior forecast's quantified 20% second-half data-center compute upside to Wall Street expectations, applied only to that component, supported an estimate of $435–440 billion; the Q1 result, Q2 guide, and production evidence support retaining a midpoint near $438 billion, with no full-year credit for the multi-year Blackwell/Rubin target.\n2695|2026-08-02|1|Wall Street’s approximately $392–393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, at rung 2.|Approximately $445–455 billion, centered near $450 billion. The $81.62 billion Q1 actual, $91 billion Q2 guide, and the estimated second-half data-center compute upside support revenue above the consensus reference; the unshipped CPU target is discounted, and the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure is not treated as FY2027 revenue in full.\n2727|2026-08-03|1|Wall Street’s roughly $392–393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $445–455 billion, centered near $450 billion. Starting from the consensus reference, strong reported growth and the $91 billion Q2 guide support the run-rate; SemiAnalysis’s 20% second-half data-center-compute upside and the Rubin ramp add upside, with only a discounted contribution from the $20 billion standalone-CPU target and the multi-year $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure.\n2756|2026-08-04|1|Wall Street estimates of about $392–393 billion in FY2027, in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $445–455 billion, centered near $450 billion. Carry forward the strong reported Q1 growth and Q2 revenue guidance, then incorporate SemiAnalysis’s 20% upside estimate only for second-half data-center compute; do not apply it to all revenue.\n2807|2026-08-05|1|Wall Street's roughly $392–393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; this is rung 2.|About $445–455 billion, centered near $450 billion. This carries forward the strong FY2027 growth trajectory supported by $81.62 billion of Q1 revenue, $91 billion Q2 guidance, the supplied Q2/Q3 growth estimates, and the Rubin ramp; the multi-year $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure and unshipped CPU target are not counted in full.\n2870|2026-08-06|1|Wall Street estimates of about $392–393 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2 (Bullish).|About $440 billion for FY2027. Starting from the roughly $393 billion consensus, I apply SemiAnalysis’ 20% upside estimate to second-half data-center compute revenue only, then include modest, discounted upside from standalone CPU sales and specified deployments; this implies a several-tens-of-billions uplift, partly offset by China exposure, delays and competition.\n2907|2026-08-07|1|Wall Street’s FY2027 revenue estimate is about $392–393 billion, in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $440 billion. Starting from the roughly $393 billion consensus, SemiAnalysis’s 20% above-consensus estimate for second-half data-center compute supports an uplift to the total without applying 20% to all revenue; discounted FY2027 CPU sales and Rubin ramp/deployment evidence provide additional support, while the broader-period orders figure is not taken in full.\n2946|2026-08-08|1|Wall Street’s roughly $392–393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e28; corroborated by e218, e234 and e811) is in rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.|Approximately $435 billion. Starting from the roughly $393 billion reference, apply the reported 20% uplift to second-half data-center compute only—not total revenue—and add a discounted contribution from NVIDIA’s $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU target; competitive deployments and China constraints partly offset those gains.\n2978|2026-08-09|1|Wall Street’s approximately $392–393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|Approximately $435 billion. Q1 reported revenue and Q2 guidance, which is close to the Q2 market estimate, anchor the forecast near consensus; the specific estimate of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above expectations supports an uplift, partly offset by China constraints and competitive risks, with only a discounted contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target.\n3056|2026-08-10|1|Wall Street's roughly $392–393 billion FY2027 estimate; rung 2.|About $440 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion market estimate, add the implied uplift from SemiAnalysis's 20%-above-consensus second-half data-center compute view, and include only a discounted contribution from NVIDIA's $20 billion standalone-CPU target because the product is newly launching and the target volume is not shown in hand.\n3224|2026-08-11|1|Wall Street estimates of about $392–393 billion for FY2027; rung 2.|About $440 billion: start from the roughly $392–393 billion reference, apply a discounted uplift for SemiAnalysis's estimate that second-half data-center compute revenue will exceed expectations by 20%, and include only part of the $20 billion standalone-CPU target. The Q3 estimate and current-quarter guidance support the revenue trajectory; the SpaceX target is not counted as firm FY2027 NVIDIA revenue.\n3293|2026-08-12|1|Wall Street's roughly $392–393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e28, e218, e234, e811), which is rung 2.|About $440 billion. This carries forward the quantified second-half data-center compute uplift over Wall Street expectations and discounts the $20 billion standalone-CPU target; Q1 actual revenue, Q2 guidance, and the Q3 estimate are consistent with that run-rate, while multi-year and post-deadline targets are not counted in full.\n3403|2026-08-13|1|Wall Street's roughly $392–393 billion FY2027 estimate (e811; also e234), at rung 2.|About $440 billion for FY2027. Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, and the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate total about $275.7 billion through Q3; the remaining forecast and carried evidence of H2 data-center compute strength support a full-year outcome above the $393 billion reference, but the SpaceX opportunity is discounted because its capacity target is mainly after FY2027.\n3464|2026-08-14|1|Wall Street estimates of roughly $392–393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $440 billion: carry the reported Q1 and guided Q2 growth trajectory through FY2027, incorporate the analyst Q3 estimate, and apply the 20% second-half upside only to data-center compute, with the separately forecast $20 billion standalone-CPU contribution. This places the forecast above $431.2 billion without treating multi-period orders or SpaceX’s later capacity target as FY2027 revenue.\n3561|2026-08-15|1|Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is about $392–393 billion, at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $435 billion for FY2027. Starting from the market's roughly $392–393 billion expectation, the strong Q1/Q2 guide and Q3 estimate support a higher run-rate; I apply the 20% second-half compute upside only to that portion of revenue and include only a discounted share of the $20 billion CPU target because shipments start in Q3 and ramp in Q4.\n3612|2026-08-16|2|Wall Street estimates of about $392–393 billion for NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; this is rung 2.|About $410 billion. The reported Q1, Q2 guidance, Q3 estimate, and a 12% sequential carry-forward imply about $391 billion before applying the in-force 20% upside view only to second-half data-center compute and a discounted contribution from the standalone-CPU target.\n3767|2026-08-17|2|Market estimates of approximately $392–$393 billion for FY2027; rung 2.|About $410 billion. The reported Q1, Q2 guidance, Q3 estimate and a 12% sequential-growth Q4 assumption imply roughly $391 billion; a limited second-half data-center compute upside and discounted CPU/Rubin contributions support the higher central estimate, without applying those inputs to total revenue wholesale.\n3902|2026-08-18|2|Wall Street estimates of about $392–$393 billion for NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; this reference is rung 2.|About $410 billion. Q1 reported revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 analyst expectation, and Q4 carried forward at 12% sequential growth imply about $391 billion; the in-force, specifically second-half compute upside and discounted CPU/Rubin contributions support a modest uplift, while the new 2028 OpenAI capacity is not counted as FY2027 revenue.\n4037|2026-08-19|2|Wall Street estimates of roughly $392–$393 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $425 billion: the Q1 result, BofA's $94–$95 billion Q2 estimate and $107–$108 billion potential Q3 guide, with 12% sequential growth carried into Q4, imply about $404 billion before a discounted uplift for stronger second-half data-center demand, CPU sales and SpaceX-related demand.\n4074|2026-08-20|2|Wall Street's roughly $392–$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the reference; it sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|The quarterly path of $81.6 billion Q1, about $94–$95 billion Q2, $107–$108 billion Q3, and roughly $120 billion Q4 using 12% sequential growth implies about $404 billion before uncertain incremental CPU and SpaceX upside. Discounting that upside for timing and execution, and weighing Morningstar's 80% growth estimate, implies roughly $420 billion for FY2027.\n4163|2026-08-21|1|Wall Street’s roughly $392–$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference; that sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $434 billion: Q1’s $81.6 billion, BofA’s $94–$95 billion Q2 estimate, its $107–$108 billion potential Q3 guidance, and roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply about $404 billion before allowing roughly $20 billion for standalone CPU sales and a heavily discounted ~$10 billion FY2027 contribution from SpaceX-related demand.\n4200|2026-08-22|2|Wall Street’s roughly $392–$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; rung 2 (Bullish).|Approximately $420 billion: the Q1 result, Q2 guide, Q3 estimates and a continued sequential-growth path imply roughly $392–$400 billion before allowing a discounted $20–$25 billion of incremental standalone-CPU and SpaceX revenue.\n4263|2026-08-23|2|Wall Street’s roughly $392–$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $430 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus an estimated $94–$95 billion Q2, $107–$108 billion Q3, and roughly $120 billion Q4 from continued sequential growth gives about $404 billion before incremental contributions. Discounted additions from standalone CPUs and SpaceX demand lift the central estimate to roughly $430 billion, while allowing for timing, overlap and execution risk.\n4456|2026-08-24|2|Wall Street’s roughly $392–$393 billion FY2027 estimate is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $430 billion: Q1 actual revenue, Q2 guidance with a modest beat allowance, Q3 estimates, and continued sequential growth imply roughly $394–$406 billion before discounted CPU and SpaceX additions. Those additions lift the central estimate near $430 billion, still below $431.2 billion.\n4580|2026-08-25|2|LSEG analysts forecast $396 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). Other supplied Wall Street estimates around $393 billion are also in rung 2.|A forward quarterly path of about $81.6 billion in Q1, $94–95 billion in Q2, $107–108 billion in Q3, and roughly $120 billion in Q4 totals about $403–405 billion. Partial credit for the unshipped standalone-CPU opportunity and early SpaceX demand lifts the central estimate to about $410 billion, still within rung 2.\n5092|2026-08-26|2|LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion (e2577), in rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.|Q1 reported $81.62 billion and Q2 reported $96.2 billion; Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion. Carrying Q3's approximately 12.3% sequential growth over Q2 into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4, or approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027 in total.\n5296|2026-08-27|2|LSEG’s FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 2.|About $414 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to Q3 guidance of $108 billion, then carry Q3’s roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward to Q4’s $68 billion FY2026 comparison quarter, implying about $128.5 billion for Q4 FY2027.\n5652|2026-08-28|2|LSEG FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion; rung 2.|About $415 billion: $81.62B Q1 actual + $96.2B Q2 actual + $108B Q3 guidance + approximately $128.8B for Q4, applying Q3’s roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate to Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68B.\n5794|2026-08-29|2|LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2.|Approximately $414 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 + $96.2 billion in Q2 + $108 billion Q3 guidance, plus an estimated $128.5 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward against the prior-year Q4 base of about $68 billion.\n5935|2026-08-30|2|LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2.|About $414 billion: add Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 actual revenue of $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108 billion; carry Q3's 89% year-over-year guided growth into Q4, or about $128.5 billion on the roughly $68 billion FY2026 Q4 base.\n6161|2026-08-31|2|LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2. The additional $397 billion analyst estimate also falls in rung 2.|About $414 billion: Q1 $81.6 billion + Q2 $96.2 billion + Q3 guidance midpoint $108 billion, plus an estimated Q4 of about $128.5 billion by carrying Q3's approximately 89% year-over-year guided growth forward from the prior-year Q4 base of about $68 billion.\n6387|2026-09-01|2|The latest supplied market estimate is about $411 billion for FY2027 (e3837), which is rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.|About $414 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1, $96.2 billion in Q2, and the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.5 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from prior-year Q4 revenue of $68 billion. The implied total is in rung 2.\n6412|2026-09-02|2|Market revenue estimates shown range from about $397 billion to $411 billion for FY2027 (e3858, e3837); both fall in rung 2.|Approximately $414.6 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.8 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3’s roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from the $68 billion prior-year Q4 revenue. This is rung 2.\n6607|2026-09-03|2|The latest stated Wall Street consensus is $411 billion (e3837), within rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). Other cited estimates around $397 billion are also in rung 2.|About $414.6 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.8 billion in Q4 by carrying the Q3 guide’s approximately 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion. This is rung 2.\n6818|2026-09-04|2|FY2027 revenue estimates include $397 billion and about $411 billion, both in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|Using the reported $81.62 billion Q1 and $96.2 billion Q2, the $108 billion Q3 guide, and carrying approximately 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion gives Q4 FY2027 of about $128.5 billion and FY2027 total revenue of about $414.3 billion. This is rung 2, below $431.2 billion.\n6928|2026-09-05|2|FY2027 revenue estimates of about $397 billion and $411 billion are the supplied market references; both sit in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $414.3 billion: carry the Q3 guide’s approximately 89% year-over-year growth into Q4, implying about $128.5 billion against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion, then add reported Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion, reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, and the $108 billion Q3 guide.\n7116|2026-09-06|2|Analyst consensus is approximately $411 billion, with another supplied estimate around $397 billion; both references are in rung 2.|About $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion Q1 + $96.2 billion Q2 + $108 billion Q3 guidance + approximately $128.5 billion for Q4, derived by carrying the Q3 guide's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\n7206|2026-09-07|2|Wall Street consensus is approximately $411 billion for FY2027, placing the reference at rung 2.|About $416–421 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, then an estimated roughly $129–135 billion in Q4. The baseline Q4 estimate carries the Q3 guide's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion; the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration supports a modest uplift.\n7278|2026-09-08|2|Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion for FY2027 (e4333), which is rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.|Q1 and Q2 total $177.82 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate forward to the $68 billion prior-year Q4 base implies about $128.5 billion in Q4, or $414.3 billion for FY2027. Allowing for the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration and Rubin ramp, but tempering for supply limits, supports roughly $414–421 billion.\n7395|2026-09-09|2|Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e4333) is the market reference and sits in rung 2.|Q1 and Q2 reported revenue totals $177.82 billion; adding the $108 billion Q3 guide and carrying its 89% year-over-year growth rate onto the $68 billion prior-year Q4 implies about $128.5 billion for Q4 and $414.3 billion total FY2027 revenue.\n7594|2026-09-10|2|Wall Street consensus is $411 billion for FY2027 (e4333), placing the reference in rung 2.|About $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2, plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.5 billion in Q4 by carrying roughly 89% year-over-year growth onto Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\n7753|2026-09-11|2|Wall Street consensus is approximately $411 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $414.3 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion, reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108 billion; estimate Q4 at about $128.5 billion by carrying the guided 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\n7888|2026-09-12|2|Wall Street’s FY2027 revenue estimate of $411 billion (e4333) is the market reference and sits in rung 2. NVIDIA’s separate 70% full-year growth statement is a forecast input, not the market reference.|About $414.3 billion: Q1 actual of $81.62 billion plus Q2 actual of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and an estimated Q4 of about $128.5 billion by carrying the Q3 guide’s 89% year-over-year growth forward from prior-year Q4 revenue of $68 billion. This falls in rung 2.\n7986|2026-09-13|2|Wall Street’s $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; rung 2.|About $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.5 billion for Q4 by carrying Q3’s 89% year-over-year growth rate forward against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\n8180|2026-09-14|2|Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate is in rung 2. The newer Zacks consensus of 87.4% growth applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $404.5 billion, also in rung 2.|About $414.6 billion: $81.62 billion (Q1) + $96.2 billion (Q2) + $108 billion (Q3 guidance) + about $128.8 billion for Q4, calculated by carrying Q3's roughly 89.4% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\n8330|2026-09-15|2|Wall Street’s $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; rung 2 (Bullish).|About $427 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2, an estimated $113.6 billion in Q3 (the $110.16 billion guide high end increased by the reported 3.1% average beat), and about $135.5 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3’s roughly 99% year-over-year growth onto Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\n8504|2026-09-16|2|Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2.|Q1 and Q2 actual revenue totaled $177.82 billion. Using the analyst's $113.6 billion Q3 estimate, based on NVIDIA's recent guidance-beat record, implies about 99% Q3 year-over-year growth; carrying that growth rate onto Q4 FY2026 revenue of about $68 billion gives roughly $135.5 billion for Q4 FY2027 and about $426.9 billion for the full year.\n8609|2026-09-17|2|Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e4333; also e4594) is the market reference and sits in rung 2.|About $404 billion: $81.62 billion Q1 plus $96.2 billion Q2, plus a $113.6 billion Q3 estimate, plus roughly $112.2 billion for Q4 (Q4 FY2026 revenue of about $68 billion grown by analysts' 65% estimate). This is an estimate-based forecast, not a claim that the FY2027 total is already established.\n8810|2026-09-18|2|Wall Street consensus is $411 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.82 billion. Applying the recent average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of Q3 guidance gives about $113.6 billion for Q3; applying analysts' 65% Q4 growth estimate to FY2026 Q4 revenue of $68 billion gives about $112.2 billion, for roughly $403.6 billion total.\n8928|2026-09-19|2|Wall Street consensus is about $411 billion, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $398 billion: add FY2027 Q1 actual revenue of $81.62 billion, Q2 actual revenue of $96.2 billion, and the Q3 guidance midpoint of $108 billion, then apply the 65% Q4 year-over-year growth expectation to an estimated FY2026 Q4 base of about $68.1 billion (about $112.4 billion).\n9014|2026-09-20|2|Wall Street consensus is about $411 billion for FY2027, in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $367 billion, in rung 3.|About $404 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, an estimated Q3 of about $113.6 billion (the cited 3.1% historical beat applied to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance), and Q4 of about $112.2 billion (65% growth on Q4 FY2026's $68 billion actual).\nold-1 v15 55 ('How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?',) [(1, 'Strongly bullish', 25000000000.0, None), (2, 'Bullish', 20000000000.0, 25000000000.0), (3, 'Neutral', 15000000000.0, 20000000000.0), (4, 'Bearish', 10000000000.0, 15000000000.0), (5, 'Strongly bearish', None, 10000000000.0)]\nrange (972, '2026-07-15', 3) (9029, '2026-09-20', 1)\n972|2026-07-15|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility of nearly $20 billion; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to below $20 billion).|Nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, based on management’s stated visibility. The figure is close to, but below, $20 billion, placing it in rung 3.\n1139|2026-07-16|3|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to less than $20 billion).|Approximately just under $20 billion: management’s direct FY2027 visibility is the quantified anchor. The planned 13,750 CPUs add potential upside, but with no deployment date or price stated, they do not support a defensible dollar adjustment.\n1246|2026-07-19|3|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; that reference sits in rung 3 because it is just below $20 billion.|Nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, using management’s direct FY2027 visibility statement as the forecast. The other evidence indicates potential demand and planned CPU deployments but supplies no date, price, or volume-in-hand basis for adding a quantified amount.\n1332|2026-07-20|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3, below $20 billion.|Just under $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, following management's FY2027-specific visibility. The undated $20 billion standalone-business target and deployments without a disclosed FY2027 revenue contribution do not establish an amount at or above $20 billion.\n1463|2026-07-21|3|NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue; this is just below $20 billion and therefore rung 3.|Around, but below, $20 billion for FY2027: management's period-specific visibility anchors the estimate. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million Vera shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion of Vera revenue in calendar 2026, but does not revise the total FY2027 figure or establish that total revenue falls below $15 billion.\n1573|2026-07-22|3|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the reference and sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion).|Near, but below, $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on management’s FY2027-specific visibility statement. Production and customer-deployment evidence supports delivery within the period; the separate Wolfe estimate implies about $6.5 billion from 1.3 million Vera CPUs at roughly $5,000 each, but is not a total-FY2027 revenue forecast.\n1685|2026-07-23|2|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the reference; it sits just below $20 billion and maps to rung 3.|About $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue: NVIDIA’s forecast of $20 billion in standalone Vera CPU sales for calendar 2026 is the closest quantified forecast, and the begun production and deliveries support carrying it into the overlapping FY2027 period. That implies roughly the rung-2 boundary, not the $25 billion needed for rung 1.\n1781|2026-07-24|2|No separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA’s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the principal reference and sits just below the $20 billion threshold, rung 3; the company’s $20 billion calendar-2026 standalone-CPU expectation sits at rung 2’s threshold.|About $20 billion for FY2027. The company’s calendar-2026 standalone-CPU expectation is at $20 billion, and the FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion; customer deliveries, production, and planned large-quantity deployments support a forecast at the threshold, though not materially above it.\n1854|2026-07-25|2|No separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA’s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the company reference and sits in rung 3 (below $20 billion); its separate $20 billion calendar-2026 CPU-sales target sits at rung 2.|About $20 billion for FY2027, based on NVIDIA’s nearly $20 billion FY2027 visibility and its $20 billion calendar-2026 CPU-sales target, with production and customer deployment evidence supporting the ramp; Wolfe Research’s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for that shipment estimate.\n1923|2026-07-26|2|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the closest direct reference and sits in rung 3, below the $20 billion boundary. The separate $20 billion calendar-2026 CPU-sales expectation is at the rung-2 boundary.|About $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue: NVIDIA’s nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility, together with its $20 billion CPU-sales expectation and the CEO’s standalone-CPU clarification, implies an outcome at or around the rung-2 threshold.\n2158|2026-07-27|2|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion”; because “nearly” is below $20 billion, this reference sits in rung 3.|About $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027. The direct FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, while the separate $20 billion standalone CPU outlook and evidence of production, deliveries, and planned deployments support a forecast around the threshold; Wolfe Research’s shipment-and-price estimate implies roughly $6.5 billion for its forecast shipments, not necessarily the full year’s total CPU revenue.\n2311|2026-07-28|2|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion”; this is below $20 billion and sits in rung 3.|Approximately $20 billion, with the balance of the evidence favoring at least $20 billion: management cited nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue and separately said it was on track for $20 billion of CPU sales in calendar 2026. Wolfe Research’s 1.3 million CPUs at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for those forecast shipments, but does not establish total FY2027 CPU revenue.\n2427|2026-07-29|2|NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” is the reference; because it is just under $20 billion, it sits in rung 3.|Around $20 billion, with a slight tilt above the boundary: management's FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, while its $20 billion calendar-2026 CPU-sales outlook and evidence of production, deliveries, and planned deployment support crossing into rung 2. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-unit forecast multiplied by about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for those forecast shipments, a significant but not conclusive counter.\n2537|2026-07-30|5|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” is the reference; because it is just under $20 billion, it sits in rung 3.|About $6.5 billion, derived from Wolfe Research’s 1.3 million Vera CPU shipment forecast for calendar 2026 multiplied by its approximately $5,000 average selling price. Calendar 2026 covers nearly all of FY2027, ending January 2027, and the evidence provides no quantified January bridge.\n2627|2026-07-31|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue is the reference; nearly $20 billion falls in rung 3 ($15 billion to less than $20 billion).|Near, but below