 billion).|Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, using NVIDIA's period-matched guidance directly; the delivery and deployment evidence supports execution but does not quantify an amount above that guide.\n2972|2026-08-09|2|NVIDIA guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2, at its $20 billion lower boundary.|Approximately $20 billion in FY2027. NVIDIA’s around-$20-billion FY2027 guidance is the direct period-matched anchor; the separately reported 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and do not justify annualizing a flat run-rate or lifting the FY2027 estimate above guidance.\n3220|2026-08-11|2|NVIDIA’s guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2.|Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, anchored to NVIDIA’s period-matched guidance and nearly $20 billion visibility. Wolfe Research’s 1.3 million calendar-2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per CPU implies roughly $6.5 billion for that calendar year, not a flat annualized FY2027 forecast; the disclosed customer deployments do not quantify an increase beyond the guide.\n3286|2026-08-12|2|NVIDIA’s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in CPU sales; this reference sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|Roughly $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on the explicit FY2027 guide and management’s nearly-$20-billion visibility. The disclosed deployment and shipment evidence supports the guide, but does not establish revenue at or above $25 billion.\n3404|2026-08-13|2|NVIDIA’s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion is the reference and sits in rung 2. No separate market consensus is supplied.|About $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027. The FY2027 guidance and nearly-$20-billion visibility anchor the estimate; production, customer deliveries and signed deployments support the ramp but are not sized to imply an outcome above that level.\n3563|2026-08-15|2|NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion is the reference and sits in rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied.|About $20 billion for FY2027, based on the company's guidance and nearly-$20-billion visibility. Deployment commitments and anticipated second-half rack shipments support the guided scale, but are unsized and do not substantiate a figure at or above $25 billion.\n3603|2026-08-16|2|NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027 is the reference; it sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). No separate market consensus is supplied.|Around $20 billion for FY2027. The direct FY2027 guidance and management's nearly-$20-billion visibility establish that level; the disclosed deployments and expected second-half rack shipments support it, but provide no quantified increment.\n3761|2026-08-17|2|NVIDIA management's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion is the reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate market consensus is supplied.|Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, anchored by the company's around-$20-billion guidance and nearly-$20-billion visibility. The 1.3 million CY2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that shipment forecast, but it does not supersede the direct FY2027 guidance or imply revenue above $25 billion.\n3901|2026-08-18|2|NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance is around $20 billion, placing the reference in rung 2.|Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, anchored by NVIDIA's around-$20-billion FY2027 Vera-sales guidance and nearly-$20-billion CPU-revenue visibility. Signed-up deployments and expected second-half CPU-rack shipments support delivery, but do not establish revenue above $25 billion.\n4035|2026-08-19|2|NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|About $20 billion for FY2027, anchored by the around-$20-billion Vera sales guide and clarified as standalone CPU revenue. The deployment commitments and expected second-half shipments support that figure, while Wolfe's 1.3 million CY2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that different period and tempers, but does not replace, the FY2027 guide.\n4152|2026-08-21|2|NVIDIA’s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera sales for FY2027 is the reference; that corresponds to rung 2.|Around $20 billion for FY2027, anchored by the company’s guide and nearly $20 billion revenue visibility. Production and planned deployments support the run-rate, but the 13,750-CPU Noetra deployment is only about $69 million at Wolfe’s roughly $5,000 ASP, and the broader commitments are unsized.\n4198|2026-08-22|2|NVIDIA’s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera sales for FY2027 (e1590), corroborated by management’s stated visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue (e899), sits at rung 2.|About $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on the direct FY2027 sales guidance and management’s nearly $20 billion visibility, rather than extrapolating a quarterly run-rate. The shipment estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but is a third-party estimate for a different period and does not displace the FY2027 guidance.\n4261|2026-08-23|2|NVIDIA’s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera sales for FY2027 is the reference; that figure is at rung 2’s $20 billion lower boundary.|About $20 billion for FY2027, using NVIDIA’s around-$20-billion guidance and nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility. The 1.3 million-unit calendar-2026 shipment forecast at about $5,000 per CPU implies roughly $6.5 billion for that calendar year, but it is not a FY2027 estimate and does not displace the company’s FY2027 guidance.\n4454|2026-08-24|2|NVIDIA’s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera sales for FY2027 is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2.|About $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, anchored by NVIDIA’s FY2027 guidance and its nearly $20 billion visibility statement. The unsized deployment plans do not establish additional revenue sufficient to imply at least $25 billion.\n4587|2026-08-25|2|NVIDIA's guidance is around $20 billion for FY2027, at rung 2.|About $20 billion for FY2027, based on NVIDIA's around-$20-billion guidance and nearly-$20-billion visibility. No disclosed amount for the newly named deployments justifies adding enough to reach $25 billion.\n5094|2026-08-26|1|NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU/Vera outlook is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).|Around or slightly above $25 billion: roughly $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera revenue, plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on the reported trailing-12-month basis, carried forward into FY2027.\n5299|2026-08-27|1|NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the market/company reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).|About $25 billion or more: the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, with the Grace run-rate carried into FY2027.\n5659|2026-08-28|1|NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the reference; that figure alone sits in rung 2, Bullish.|Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU revenue plus a separate Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion, placing the forecast in rung 1.\n5929|2026-08-30|1|NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).|About $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue at a reported trailing-12-month run-rate exceeding $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion. The Vera outlook is supported by shipments beginning at scale and customer deployment plans; carrying the separately reported Grace run-rate alongside it crosses the rung-1 boundary.\n6169|2026-08-31|1|No independent consensus figure is supplied. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the reference, at rung 2.|About $25 billion or more: NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook plus Grace CPU revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\n6389|2026-09-01|1|NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook is the market/company reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). The company characterized the $20 billion as standalone CPU revenue.|More than $25 billion for FY2027: take the approximately $20 billion standalone Vera outlook and add Grace’s reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion, carrying that Grace run-rate into FY2027.\n6605|2026-09-03|1|NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).|Just over $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue, whose trailing-12-month run-rate already exceeded $5 billion.\n6822|2026-09-04|1|NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera standalone-CPU outlook is the relevant company reference; $20 billion sits in rung 2. Management clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU, distinct from Grace CPU revenue.|Approximately $25 billion or more for FY2027: roughly $20 billion from Vera standalone CPU sales, plus Grace CPU revenue carried at a run-rate consistent with its reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion.\n6925|2026-09-05|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|About $25 billion or more: take the roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera outlook and add Grace CPU revenue, whose trailing-12-month figure was already above $5 billion, as a baseline contribution.\n7107|2026-09-06|1|NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU sales outlook is the reference; that amount is rung 2.|At least approximately $25 billion: $20 billion of guided FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU sales plus more than $5 billion of Grace CPU revenue on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\n7399|2026-09-09|1|NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook; this reference is in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|About $25 billion or more: carry forward the roughly $20 billion Vera/standalone CPU outlook and add Grace revenue at its reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate.\n7599|2026-09-10|1|NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the supplied market/company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|About $25 billion or somewhat more: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera outlook forward and add Grace’s greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue at its reported run-rate, treating the figures as additive because NVIDIA clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU.\n7760|2026-09-11|1|NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|About $25 billion or somewhat more: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera outlook forward and add Grace’s reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue as a run-rate contribution. This reaches rung 1’s $25 billion threshold, though the figure is a forecast rather than an established FY2027 result.\n7887|2026-09-12|1|NVIDIA's FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU sales guidance of about $20 billion is the relevant reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate market consensus is supplied.|About $20 billion of guided FY2027 Vera revenue plus Grace revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies slightly more than $25 billion in total CPU revenue, assuming no unquantified Grace step-down. This places the forecast in rung 1.\n7991|2026-09-13|1|NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU expectation is the reference and sits at rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied.|About $20 billion of Vera/standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue carried forward from NVIDIA's reported trailing-12-month run-rate of more than $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue slightly above $25 billion. That clears rung 1's $25 billion boundary.\n8184|2026-09-14|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU sales expectation; this reference is in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). No separate market consensus is supplied.|Approximately $20 billion of guided Vera/standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue at a trailing-12-month run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of about $25 billion or more. That reaches rung 1's $25 billion threshold.\n8328|2026-09-15|2|NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation is the available reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). No separate market consensus is supplied.|Approximately $20 billion in total FY2027 CPU revenue: NVIDIA’s nearly $20 billion total-CPU visibility and subsequent $20 billion-level Vera outlook anchor the forecast, while the more-than-$5 billion Grace figure is trailing-12-month revenue and is not established as incremental to that total.\n8500|2026-09-16|2|NVIDIA management’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation is the reference and sits in rung 2; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied.|About $20 billion in FY2027 total CPU revenue: management’s total-CPU visibility is nearly $20 billion and its Vera outlook is around $20 billion. Grace’s more-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue is not a separately stated FY2027 increment, so it is not added in full to that outlook.\n8615|2026-09-17|1|NVIDIA management’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the available reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.|Approximately $25 billion or somewhat more: the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, clarified as such, plus an approximate $5 billion Grace contribution based on its disclosed trailing-12-month revenue scale. Grace’s FY2027 contribution is not separately quantified, so the total is a forecast rather than a reported or guaranteed amount.\n8815|2026-09-18|1|NVIDIA’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, clarified as standalone/Vera CPU revenue, is the relevant reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).|About $25 billion: carry forward the roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera outlook and add roughly $5 billion for Grace, using its reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion as an approximate run-rate rather than assuming a separately guided FY2027 amount.\n8927|2026-09-19|1|NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook; this reference is in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|About $25 billion: the roughly $20 billion Vera/standalone CPU outlook plus approximately $5 billion for Grace, using NVIDIA's reported trailing-twelve-month Grace revenue as a run-rate estimate for FY2027.\n9029|2026-09-20|1|NVIDIA management's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the market reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).|About $25 billion for FY2027: roughly $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue, with the reported more-than-$5-billion Grace trailing-12-month revenue used as an approximate annual run-rate contribution. The sum reaches rung 1's $25 billion threshold, though the Grace carry-forward and resulting boundary estimate are uncertain.\nold-2 v14 71 ('How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?',) [(1, 'Strongly bullish', 450000000000.0, None), (2, 'Bullish', 400000000000.0, 450000000000.0), (3, 'Neutral', 350000000000.0, 400000000000.0), (4, 'Bearish', 300000000000.0, 350000000000.0), (5, 'Strongly bearish', None, 300000000000.0)]\nrange (439, '2026-07-10', 3) (8729, '2026-09-20', 2)\n439|2026-07-10|3|Bloomberg-compiled projections expect FY2027 sales of $393 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).|About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg’s direct sales projection; this is below $400 billion and therefore falls in rung 3.\n455|2026-07-11|3|Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion; this market reference sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).|About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's specific projection. The AI-lab demand share and supportive capex and export-policy themes add upside pressure, but state no FY2027 NVIDIA revenue adjustment, so they do not imply a figure above $400 billion.\n517|2026-07-12|3|Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the specific market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion), $7 billion short of rung 2.|About $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of approximately $91 billion, I carry the roughly 11.5% sequential increase forward to Q3 and Q4. This remains within rung 3 and below $400 billion.\n592|2026-07-13|3|Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion; this sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).|Approximately $387 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth through Q3 and Q4 gives roughly $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively.\n722|2026-07-14|3|Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference; it sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).|About $387 billion: starting with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carry the approximately 11.5% sequential increase into Q3 and Q4, yielding about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively.\n854|2026-07-15|3|Bloomberg projects FY2027 revenue of $393 billion; that market reference is in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).|About $387 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, an approximately 11.5% sequential increase. Carrying that growth forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, for about $387.3 billion total.\n1016|2026-07-16|3|Bloomberg’s FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).|About $387 billion: start with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry their roughly 11.5% sequential increase forward to Q3 and Q4, implying about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion.\n1093|2026-07-17|3|Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference; it sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).|About $387 billion: start with reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91.0 billion, then carry their approximately 11.5% sequential growth rate into Q3 and Q4, implying about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion for those quarters.\n1120|2026-07-18|3|Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3.|About $387 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $91.0 billion guided for Q2, then carrying the roughly 11.5% sequential growth rate forward gives about $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4.\n1217|2026-07-20|3|Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).|About $387 billion: start with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry their roughly 11.5% sequential growth forward to implied Q3 revenue of about $101.5 billion and Q4 revenue of about $113.2 billion.\n1347|2026-07-21|3|Wall Street’s FY2027 revenue estimate is about $393 billion (e195, e669), which sits in rung 3.|About $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and the Q2 guide of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, for approximately $387.3 billion total.\n1450|2026-07-22|3|Wall Street's average FY2027 revenue estimate is about $393 billion (e669), placing the reference in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion. Zacks' 80% FY2027 growth estimate is a similar market reference (e751).|About $387.3 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying it through Q3 and Q4 gives about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. As a cross-check, 80% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $388.6 billion; both estimates fall in rung 3.\n1572|2026-07-23|3|Market expectations are about $393 billion for FY2027 (e195, e669), placing the reference at rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.|About $391 billion: carrying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate from $81.6 billion to $91 billion through Q3 and Q4 implies roughly $387 billion for FY2027, while 80%–82% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389–$393 billion. These approaches support an estimate near $391 billion.\n1643|2026-07-24|3|Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection is $393 billion, rung 3. Zacks' 80% FY2027 growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $388.6 billion, also rung 3.|Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by $91 billion Q2 guidance implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through the remaining quarters gives approximately $387 billion FY2027 revenue. This is below $400 billion and above $350 billion, so it falls in rung 3.\n1718|2026-07-25|3|Market expectations are approximately $393 billion for FY2027 (also described as 82% growth); this is rung 3, $350 billion to below $400 billion.|The Q1 reported $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through Q3 and Q4 gives about $387 billion for FY2027. Allowing for unquantified demand and export-access upside, while discounting supply constraints, the forecast is near $390 billion.\n1788|2026-07-26|2|Bloomberg/Wall Street expects about $393 billion for FY2027 (roughly 82% growth from FY2026), which is rung 3: $350 billion to below $400 billion.|The $81.6 billion Q1 result plus $91 billion Q2 guidance, with the guided roughly 11.5% sequential increase carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $387 billion. Allowing a measured uplift for $119 billion in booked supply commitments, the Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales forecast, and improved export access, while discounting multi-year targets and accounting for memory constraints, implies roughly $400–$420 billion.\n2012|2026-07-27|2|Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate is $393 billion, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies a rung-3 figure.|About $405 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that forward gives about $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113 billion in Q4, or roughly $387 billion for the year, with booked supply commitments and demand evidence supporting a modest uplift above $400 billion.\n2163|2026-07-28|2|Bloomberg's FY2027 estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.|About $401 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the embedded 11.5% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4, implies about $387 billion. I add a modest uplift for demand, deployment and investment signals, discounted because multiyear targets are not FY2027 revenue in hand and supply and China-related constraints remain.\n2290|2026-