llion across FY2027.\n8620|2026-09-19|2|Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion of FY2027 revenue, placing the reference at rung 2. Zacks' estimate of 87.4% growth applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2.|Approximately $418 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2, about $113.5 billion estimated for Q3, and about $127 billion for Q4 by carrying roughly 12% sequential growth forward from the estimated Q3 level.\n8729|2026-09-20|2|Market expectation is about $411 billion for FY2027, rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). Zacks' 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2.|About $419 billion: Q1 $81.6 billion plus Q2 $96.2 billion, estimated Q3 of about $113.5 billion (the $110.16 billion guidance high end plus the reported average 3.1% beat), and Q4 of about $127 billion by carrying forward the guided 12% sequential growth.\nold-2 v16 56 ('How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?',) [(1, 'Strongly bullish', 25000000000.0, None), (2, 'Bullish', 20000000000.0, 25000000000.0), (3, 'Neutral', 15000000000.0, 20000000000.0), (4, 'Bearish', 10000000000.0, 15000000000.0), (5, 'Strongly bearish', None, 10000000000.0)]\nrange (849, '2026-07-15', 3) (8735, '2026-09-20', 1)\n849|2026-07-15|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in FY2027 CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3.|Nearly $20 billion in FY2027, based on management's stated visibility. The $200 billion TAM is not an annual revenue forecast, so it does not justify projecting FY2027 revenue above the stated visibility.\n1012|2026-07-16|3|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this is rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|Nearly $20 billion, but below $20 billion: management directly stated visibility to nearly that amount for FY2027. The other evidence does not provide a quantified basis to move the forecast above $20 billion.\n1088|2026-07-17|3|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|Nearly $20 billion in FY2027, based on management’s direct visibility statement. The qualitative CPU-demand view and higher AI-capex outlook are supportive but do not supply a quantified basis to raise the implied figure above $20 billion.\n1118|2026-07-18|3|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|Approximately, but just under, $20 billion in FY2027, based on management’s stated visibility to nearly $20 billion. The remaining evidence provides no quantified adjustment that would imply crossing above $20 billion.\n1212|2026-07-20|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3.|Approximately, but not clearly above, $20 billion in FY2027: the direct FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion. The separate $20 billion standalone-business target is not period-specific, so it does not justify carrying revenue above $20 billion for FY2027.\n1348|2026-07-21|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference is in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|Nearly $20 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management's stated visibility. Wolfe Research's estimate implies about $6.5 billion of Vera CPU shipments in calendar 2026 (1.3 million × about $5,000), but it is not a FY2027 total-revenue estimate and does not warrant lifting the implication above $20 billion.\n1452|2026-07-22|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue; this is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).|Near $20 billion in FY2027, based on management’s stated visibility. Wolfe Research’s 1.3 million-chip CY2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for that calendar year, while deliveries and planned large OpenAI deployments support the ramp; those figures do not quantify FY2027 revenue above $20 billion.\n1568|2026-07-23|3|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference; nearly $20 billion falls in rung 3.|Approximately $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027: management’s direct total-revenue visibility is the primary forecast, while the $20 billion standalone Vera target is discounted because disclosed deliveries and deployment plans do not establish the target volume. Wolfe’s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, a caution against treating the standalone target as already secured.\n1644|2026-07-24|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference is rung 3. No separate market consensus figure is supplied.|Approximately $20 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management's stated visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue. The standalone Vera targets and the calendar-2026 shipment estimate are useful checks, but do not establish revenue above $20 billion for the full FY2027 total.\n1790|2026-07-26|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).|Nearly $20 billion in FY2027. I use management's direct FY2027 visibility, clarified as standalone CPU revenue, as the main forecast; the 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 but does not quantify a full-FY2027 total.\n2011|2026-07-27|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue is the market/company reference and sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).|Approximately $19 billion to $20 billion in FY2027, carried from management’s direct ‘nearly $20 billion’ total-CPU visibility. The standalone-CPU clarification and qualitative deployment evidence do not quantify additional revenue above $20 billion; the available shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not a replacement FY2027 total.\n2172|2026-07-28|3|NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue is the operative market reference; it sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|Approximately $19 billion to just under $20 billion in FY2027: the direct FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, and the standalone-sales clarification makes it relevant to this question. The 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but does not supply a quantified FY2027 revision.\n2288|2026-07-29|3|NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue, clarified by the CEO as standalone CPU sales, is the reference and sits in rung 3.|Approximately $20 billion, but just below it: the CFO's nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility is the strongest period-matched forecast. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but neither that estimate nor the AMD comparisons provides a quantified FY2027 revision.\n2386|2026-07-30|3|NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue, clarified by Jensen Huang as standalone CPU sales; this reference sits in rung 3.|Roughly $20 billion, but below the boundary—consistent with management's 'nearly $20 billion' FY2027 visibility. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million 2026 units at about $5,000 each imply about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a downside check that does not directly replace the FY2027 company visibility.\n2462|2026-07-31|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, clarified as standalone CPU sales, is the reference and sits in rung 3.|About $19 billion to just under $20 billion in FY2027. The period-matched management visibility anchors the estimate; deliveries and OpenAI's planned large-quantity deployment support the ramp, while the $6.5 billion implied by Wolfe Research's 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 each is a calendar-2026 cross-check, not a FY2027 replacement.\n2515|2026-08-01|3|No separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The reference is NVIDIA's guidance of visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue in FY2027, with the CEO clarifying this is standalone CPU revenue; nearly $20 billion is in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).|About $19 billion to just under $20 billion in FY2027, carrying forward the company's period-matched visibility guidance rather than annualizing a quarter. Delivery and deployment evidence supports the ramp, but the $6.5 billion calendar-2026 shipment estimate and competition evidence do not establish a quantified change to that FY2027 outlook.\n2720|2026-08-06|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this company-guidance reference sits in rung 3. No separate consensus figure is supplied.|Just below $20 billion in FY2027. This carries forward management's nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility; the supplied delivery and demand evidence supports execution, but the shipment estimate and competitive evidence do not quantify a change to that guidance.\n2763|2026-08-07|3|NVIDIA management’s guidance is visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; that reference sits just below $20 billion, in rung 3.|The evidence implies FY2027 CPU revenue near, but below, $20 billion. The nearly-$20-billion guidance remains the best direct forecast; Wolfe’s 1.3 million-unit shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in exceeding the guidance, while the announced deliveries and planned OpenAI deployment support the ramp.\n2824|2026-08-09|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; that reference is just below $20 billion and therefore sits in rung 3.|Nearly $20 billion, but slightly below the boundary: the FY2027 management visibility is the direct figure, supported by customer deliveries and planned large-quantity deployment. Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is not a quantified revision to total FY2027 CPU revenue.\n2912|2026-08-10|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the market/company reference; it sits just below $20 billion, in rung 3.|Approximately, but just under, $20 billion in FY2027: management's near-$20 billion visibility remains the strongest period-specific input, supported by Vera deliveries and planned deployments; Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, while the competitive evidence supplies risk but no quantified reduction.\n3104|2026-08-11|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue is the available reference; it sits in rung 3, which includes outcomes up to and including $20 billion. No market-consensus figure is supplied.|Near, but most likely just below, $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027. This follows the period-specific management visibility, clarified as standalone CPU sales; the shipment estimate supports a real Vera ramp but covers only one product and calendar 2026, so it does not replace or cap the FY2027 total.\n3284|2026-08-13|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue; this is at most $20 billion and therefore sits in rung 3.|About $20 billion, but slightly below it: management’s direct FY2027 visibility remains the best quantified forecast, and the delivery and deployment evidence supports ramping sales without providing a quantified basis to exceed $20 billion.\n3421|2026-08-15|3|No separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management’s visibility to “nearly $20 billion” of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference, at rung 3.|Close to, but below, $20 billion in FY2027, anchored to management’s “nearly $20 billion” visibility. Wolfe’s 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026 Vera shipments, but that product-level estimate is not a full-FY2027 total CPU revenue forecast or a quantified revision to management’s figure.\n3467|2026-08-16|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” is the stated reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion–$20 billion).|About $20 billion, but just below it, based primarily on management’s direct FY2027 visibility. Wolfe’s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, but covers Vera shipments rather than total CPU revenue for FY2027 and does not replace or quantify a revision to that visibility.\n3611|2026-08-17|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. This reference is rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|Nearly $20 billion, but below $20 billion, based primarily on management’s FY2027 visibility and the CEO’s clarification that it refers to standalone CPU sales. Wolfe’s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a narrower, different-period cross-check rather than a quantified FY2027 revision.\n3737|2026-08-18|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue; this reference is rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|Approximately $19–20 billion, below $20 billion: management’s period-specific “nearly $20 billion” visibility is the direct estimate, with the CEO specifying standalone CPU sales. Wolfe’s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that narrower-period shipment estimate does not replace the FY2027 total.\n3870|2026-08-19|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue; this reference is in rung 3.|Approximately $20 billion in FY2027. The direct period-specific management visibility remains the anchor; Wolfe’s 1.3 million-unit forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but is a Vera shipment forecast rather than a quantified revision to the FY2027 total.\n3985|2026-08-21|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).|Approximately $20 billion in FY2027: use management’s direct visibility statement as the central forecast, with Wolfe’s 1.3 million × about $5,000 (= about $6.5 billion) Vera estimate as a downside cross-check for calendar 2026 rather than as a replacement FY2027 total.\n4028|2026-08-22|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue; that reference sits in rung 3.|About $20 billion, but still just below the greater-than-$20-billion rung-2 threshold: the direct FY2027 visibility remains the strongest forecast, with deliveries and planned deployment supporting execution and no quantified revision displacing it.\n4087|2026-08-23|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|Approximately $20 billion in FY2027, based on NVIDIA’s period-specific “nearly $20 billion” visibility. The other $20 billion targets are not a quantified FY2027 revision, and the broader market forecasts do not establish NVIDIA’s share; the implied figure remains at or below $20 billion.\n4282|2026-08-24|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue is the market/company reference; it sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|About $20 billion in FY2027: management’s “nearly $20 billion” visibility and $20 billion revenue statements anchor the estimate. Wolfe Research’s 1.3 million 2026 shipments at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for that shipment estimate, a counterweight, but do not replace the stronger FY2027 company-level outlook; the wider market forecasts and unsized deployment plans do not quantify NVIDIA revenue above $20 billion.\n4427|2026-08-25|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue; this reference is in rung 3 ($15B–$20B inclusive).|About $20 billion in FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA’s FY2027 visibility statement and its clarification that the figure is for standalone CPU sales. Customer deployment evidence supports the forecast but is not sized to imply revenue above $20 billion.\n4942|2026-08-26|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).|About $20 billion in FY2027. The current FY2027 visibility remains the strongest direct forecast; the specific estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while the more-than-doubling expectation is for FY2028 and does not raise the FY2027 figure.\n5129|2026-08-27|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to “nearly $20 billion” in total CPU revenue is the market/company reference and sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).|Approximately $20 billion, likely just below that amount: management’s FY2027 visibility is the direct year-specific anchor. The more-than-doubling statement is for FY2028, so it is not carried backward into FY2027.\n5472|2026-08-28|1|No independent consensus figure is supplied. NVIDIA management’s nearly $20 billion / $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook is the reference, at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).|Roughly $25 billion or somewhat more: take the approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook and add the distinct Grace CPU revenue stream, reported above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. The ongoing Vera shipments and full production support taking the standalone outlook substantially into account.\n5594|2026-08-29|1|NVIDIA management’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook; this is rung 3 (Neutral: $15 billion to $20 billion).|Around or modestly above $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of FY2027 Vera standalone CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion, carried forward from the reported trailing-12-month figure.\n5746|2026-08-30|1|NVIDIA management’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook is the supplied reference; it sits at rung 3 (at the upper boundary, if taken as $20 billion). No separate market consensus figure is supplied.|Just above $25 billion for FY2027: approximately $20 billion of Vera standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue whose trailing-12-month total was already more than $5 billion. This is a narrow estimate above the rung 1 boundary, not a claim that the FY2027 total has already been reported.\n5971|2026-08-31|1|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 expectation of about $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue is the reference. At $20 billion, it sits at rung 3, which includes $20 billion.|Use about $20 billion of FY2027 Vera standalone revenue and add the greater-than-$5-billion Grace trailing-12-month revenue run-rate reported in the evidence. This implies slightly more than $25 billion in total CPU revenue, subject to Grace sustaining that run-rate through FY2027.\n6192|2026-09-01|1|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 outlook is about $20 billion in standalone CPU sales, placing the reference at rung 3 (the $15–$20 billion band).|About $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion. The resulting estimate crosses the strictly-greater-than-$25-billion threshold for rung 1.\n6407|2026-09-03|1|NVIDIA management's FY2027 outlook of about $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue is the reference; $20 billion alone falls at rung 3 ($15–20 billion).|About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue, plus Grace CPU revenue carried at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate of more than $5 billion, implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion.\n6593|2026-09-04|1|NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference; the stated amount alone sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).|Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, assuming Grace sustains roughly that run-rate.\n6716|2026-09-05|1|NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook is nearly $20 billion for standalone CPU revenue, placing the reference in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).|Approximately $25 billion-plus: carry the near-$20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook forward and add Grace CPU revenue, which was above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. This implies a modest crossing above the $25 billion rung 1 boundary, rather than treating the standalone outlook as total CPU revenue.\n6973|2026-09-07|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion falls in rung 3 (Neutral: at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|More than $25 billion in FY2027: NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion standalone-CPU outlook plus Grace CPU revenue already above a $5 billion trailing-12-month level. The standalone clarification means these amounts are additive for total CPU revenue.\n7069|2026-09-08|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the reference; it sits at rung 3, whose range is $15 billion to $20 billion inclusive.|More than $25 billion: add the approximately $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast to Grace CPU revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. The resulting total crosses rung 1's strict $25 billion threshold.\n7175|2026-09-09|1|NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook is approximately $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue (rung 3); management’s clarification that the figure is standalone distinguishes it from Grace CPU revenue.|Above $25 billion: use the $20 billion standalone FY2027 outlook and add the greater-than-$5 billion Grace CPU TTM revenue reported as of Q2 FY2027 as a run-rate proxy for FY2027.\n7402|2026-09-10|1|NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook; rung 3 (Neutral), at the top of the $15–$20 billion band.|Above $25 billion, approximately $25 billion-plus: about $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run rate. The $6.5 billion implied by Wolfe’s calendar-2026 Vera shipment estimate is not added separately because it falls within standalone CPU revenue.\n7560|2026-09-11|1|NVIDIA’s stated FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is about $20 billion, at the top of rung 3 ($15–$20 billion).|Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace’s greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue roughly above $25 billion. The Grace run-rate is a proxy rather than a separately stated FY2027 forecast, so the implied crossing is marginal rather than assured.\n7681|2026-09-12|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion sits at rung 3.|Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue carrying forward at more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion, at rung 1.\n7773|2026-09-13|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the market/company reference; $20 billion total CPU revenue is rung 3.|Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion if Grace sustains roughly that run-rate. That places the forecast in rung 1.\n7937|2026-09-14|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion is the top of rung 3.|Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 Vera revenue plus Grace revenue continuing from its reported trailing-12-month level above $5 billion implies total CPU revenue just above $25 billion. This assumes Grace revenue is sustained into the FY2027 total; the forecasted Vera amount is the stronger direct FY2027 input, while Wolfe's roughly $6.5 billion calendar-2026 shipment estimate is a material downside counterweight.\n8044|2026-09-15|1|NVIDIA's nearly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion is in rung 3 (Neutral: at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|About $20 billion of Vera CPU revenue plus Grace revenue continuing at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion, in rung 1.\n8217|2026-09-16|1|NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook of about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales is the relevant market/company reference, and sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).|About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera revenue plus Grace revenue carried at its reported trailing-12-month pace of more than $5 billion implies total CPU revenue just over $25 billion. This assumes the Grace pace is broadly maintained; the resulting estimate only narrowly clears rung 1’s greater-than-$25-billion boundary.\n8322|2026-09-17|1|NVIDIA’s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the reference, at the top of rung 3 (Neutral).|Just over $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace’s reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate carried into FY2027. The Vera figure implies roughly 4 million chips at Wolfe’s approximately $5,00