ference and sit at rung 2.|Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the Q3 estimate of $103.1 billion, and about 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply a baseline around $391–$392 billion. Applying the carried estimate of 20% upside to second-half Data Center compute adds roughly $40 billion, implying total FY2027 revenue around $431–$433 billion.\n4101|2026-08-20|1|About $392 billion in FY2027 analyst estimates (with estimates near $393 billion), placing the reference at rung 2.|Roughly $434–$437 billion: carry forward the prior $391–$392 billion quarterly baseline plus about $40 billion from the estimated 20% second-half Data Center compute upside, then add about $3–$4 billion for BofA’s $94–$95 billion Q2 forecast versus the $91 billion guide midpoint. The higher Q3 expectation is not added separately because it overlaps the second-half tracking uplift.\n4186|2026-08-21|1|About $392 billion in FY2027 analyst estimates (including estimates near $393 billion), which is rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.|Approximately $432–436 billion: applying the roughly $40 billion implied second-half Data Center compute upside from SemiAnalysis to the roughly $392 billion annual reference, with BofA's Q2 estimate adding about $3–4 billion versus guidance, while avoiding a separate overlapping Q3 addition.\n4222|2026-08-22|1|Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is about $392 billion, placing the reference at rung 2.|About $432–436 billion: apply SemiAnalysis's 20% upside to second-half FY2027 Data Center compute, implying roughly $40 billion above the $392 billion consensus, then include about $3–4 billion for BofA's $94–95 billion Q2 forecast versus the roughly $91 billion guide/consensus. This is a forecast, not an established result, and sits just above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\n4298|2026-08-23|1|Wall Street’s FY2027 total-revenue estimate is $392 billion, placing the reference at rung 2.|About $438–440 billion: start from the $392 bi