nsensus of $396 billion (e2624), which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $415 billion: add reported first-half revenue of $177.837 billion to the $108 billion Q3 guide, then estimate Q4 near $129 billion by carrying the guide’s roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate forward to the prior-year Q4 base of about $68.1 billion.\n5859|2026-08-29|2|Latest supplied analyst consensus is $396 billion (e2624), rung 2; the ladder’s stated $392 billion reference is also rung 2.|About $415 billion: $177.837 billion reported for H1 plus $108 billion guided for Q3, plus approximately $129 billion for Q4 by carrying Q3’s roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward to an estimated $68.1 billion FY2026 Q4 comparison. The estimate is above $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.\n6002|2026-08-30|2|Latest supplied FY2027 analyst consensus is about $396 billion (e2624), placing the reference at rung 2; the ladder's stated $392 billion estimate is also rung 2.|About $415 billion: the reported $177.837 billion first half plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, then Q4 estimated by carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward against the implied FY2026 Q4 base of about $68.1 billion. The 70% full-year growth guide implies about $367 billion instead, but would require Q4 revenue near $81 billion, below Q3.\n6242|2026-08-31|2|Latest supplied FY2027 analyst consensus is about $397 billion, at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|Approximately $415 billion: $177.837 billion reported for the first half plus $108 billion Q3 guidance and an estimated $129 billion Q4, carrying roughly 89% Q3 year-over-year growth forward from an approximately $68.1 billion FY2026 Q4 base.\n6457|2026-09-01|2|Latest supplied analyst expectation is about $397 billion for FY2027 (e3726), within rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|First-half revenue was $177.837 billion; the $108 billion Q3 guide implies roughly 89% year-over-year growth. Carrying that growth into Q4, whose FY2026 comparison is about $68.1 billion ($215.9 billion FY2026 revenue less $90.805 billion first-half revenue and $57.01 billion FY2026 Q3 revenue), implies roughly $129 billion for Q4 and about $415 billion for FY2027.\n6486|2026-09-02|2|FY2027 analyst estimates of about $396–397 billion (with the ladder anchored to $392 billion) sit in rung 2.|Approximately $415 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus roughly $129 billion for Q4 by carrying Q3’s approximately 89% year-over-year growth forward against the prior-year Q4 base.\n6671|2026-09-03|2|Analyst expectations of $392 billion originally and approximately $396–397 billion in later estimates; each sits in rung 2.|Approximately $415 billion for FY2027: $177.837 billion reported in the first half plus $108 billion guided for Q3, with roughly $129 billion implied for Q4 by carrying the Q3 guide’s approximately 89% year-over-year growth against the retained FY2026 Q4 base.\n6880|2026-09-04|2|The market reference is FY2027 revenue of $392 billion in the original estimate, with later estimates of $396–397 billion; all sit in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $415 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue plus the $108 billion Q3 guide and approximately $129 billion for Q4 if the Q3 year-over-year growth rate carries forward against the FY2026 Q4 base. The stated 70% full-year growth outlook instead implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, a significant but less persuasive counter-signal given the reported first half and Q3 guide.\n6989|2026-09-05|2|Updated analyst expectations are about $396–$400 billion for FY2027: LSEG gives $396 billion, another analyst estimate is $397 billion, and Zacks’ 85.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $400 billion. These references sit in rung 2.|About $415 billion: add reported first-half FY2027 revenue of $177.837 billion and the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, then carry Q3’s roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward to the FY2026 Q4 base of about $68.1 billion (FY2026 revenue less its reported first half and Q3).\n7164|2026-09-06|2|Wall Street's latest supplied FY2027 revenue estimate is $411 billion (e4360), placing the reference in rung 2.|About $407 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half plus $108 billion of Q3 guidance and an estimated $121 billion for Q4, carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance versus Q2.\n7296|2026-09-07|2|Wall Street analysts’ latest supplied FY2027 total-revenue estimate is $411 billion, placing the reference in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $407 billion: $177.837 billion reported for the first half, plus $108 billion guided for Q3, plus about $121 billion for Q4 by carrying the 12% sequential growth pace implied by Q3 guidance forward once. The separate 70% full-year growth statement implies about $367 billion from the $215.9 billion FY2026 base, but would require a sharp Q4 drop and is outweighed by the reported run-rate and near-term guidance.\n7392|2026-09-08|2|Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate (e4360); rung 2.|About $406.8 billion: reported first-half revenue of $177.837 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, plus approximately $121 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\n7515|2026-09-09|2|Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2.|About $406.8 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, plus the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint, plus approximately $120.96 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\n7753|2026-09-10|2|Wall Street's latest FY2027 revenue estimate of about $411 billion is in rung 2.|FY2027 revenue of about $406.8 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, plus $108 billion at the Q3 guidance midpoint, plus approximately $121.0 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\n7915|2026-09-11|1|Latest supplied Wall Street FY2027 estimate: about $411 billion, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $435 billion: the reported $177.837 billion first half plus $108 billion Q3 guidance and a 12% sequential Q4 carry imply about $406.8 billion before incremental upside. SemiAnalysis’s 20% above-consensus forecast is applied only to a conservatively discounted share of second-half revenue, rather than to NVIDIA’s whole revenue base, adding roughly $28 billion.\n8046|2026-09-12|1|The latest supplied analyst estimate is $411 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2.|The first-half reported revenue of $177.837 billion, plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion and a 12% sequential carry into Q4, implies about $406.8 billion before the SemiAnalysis forecast of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus. Applying a discounted uplift only to that portion supports an approximate FY2027 outcome of $435 billion.\n8146|2026-09-13|1|Latest supplied FY2027 analyst expectation is $411 billion (e4360, e4641), in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $435 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, plus the $108 billion Q3 guide and approximately $120.96 billion for Q4 using the 12% sequential carry, gives about $406.8 billion before the discounted uplift implied by SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus.\n8311|2026-09-14|1|The analyst reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The Zacks consensus growth estimate of 87.4%, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $404.6 billion, also rung 2; these are reference points, not the forecast conclusion.|About $435 billion for FY2027. The reported first-half total plus the Q3 midpoint and a Q4 carry-forward of Q3's 12% sequential growth imply about $406.8 billion before applying a discount to SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half data-center compute revenue would be 20% above consensus; the prior calculation put the resulting total near $435 billion.\n8463|2026-09-15|1|Latest supplied Wall Street expectation: $411 billion, rung 2; the ladder's original $392 billion analyst estimate is also rung 2.|About $445–447 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, approximately $113–114 billion for Q3 using the specific estimate and guidance-beat evidence, and about $127 billion for Q4 if the 12% sequential growth assumption carries forward yield roughly $418 billion before the discounted uplift from the second-half Data Center compute forecast. Applying that uplift only to the relevant portion of revenue puts the total above $431.2 billion.\n8633|2026-09-16|1|Wall Street analysts’ latest supplied FY2027 estimate is $411 billion, placing the reference at rung 2; the earlier $392 billion estimate is also rung 2.|Approximately $445–447 billion: first-half revenue of $177.837 billion plus an estimated $113–114 billion Q3 and roughly $127–128 billion Q4 using 12% sequential growth yields about $418–420 billion. A discounted uplift for SemiAnalysis’s second-half Data Center compute projection, applied only to that revenue share, takes the implied total above $431.2 billion.\n8757|2026-09-17|1|The latest explicit Wall Street FY2027 estimate shown is $411 billion (e4360), rung 2. Later analyst growth estimates of 87.4%–88% applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion imply about $405–406 billion, also rung 2.|About $445–447 billion: first-half actual revenue of $177.837 billion plus an estimated $113–114 billion Q3 and roughly 12% sequential Q4 growth implies about $418–420 billion before the second-half Data Center compute uplift. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% projection only to that compute share lifts the forecast above $431.2 billion.\n8969|2026-09-18|1|The latest supplied Wall Street FY2027 revenue estimate is $411 billion (e4360), which sits in rung 2; the ladder’s stated $392 billion estimate also sits in rung 2.|About $445–447 billion: $177.837 billion reported for the first half, plus about $113–114 billion for Q3 (e5088), then roughly 12% sequential growth for Q4, gives about $418–420 billion before the second-half data-center-compute adjustment. Applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift only to that compute portion (e60), not total revenue, gives approximately $445–447 billion.\n9077|2026-09-19|1|Analyst consensus is about $411 billion for FY2027, placing the reference at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).|About $446 billion: $177.837 billion reported in H1, roughly $113.6 billion for Q3 using the historical beat over guidance, and about $127 billion for Q4 carrying forward 12% sequential growth, plus approximately $27–28 billion from SemiAnalysis’s 20% uplift applied only to second-half data-center compute.\n9183|2026-09-20|1|Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion for FY2027 (e4360, e5406), placing the reference in rung 2.|About $446 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue, approximately $113.6 billion for Q3 using the guidance midpoint and the cited average beat, and approximately $127 billion for Q4 by carrying forward 12% sequential growth, then adding about $27–28 billion for the estimated 20% second-half data-center-compute uplift.\nold-3 v14 60 ('What will NVIDIA report as total CPU revenue for FY2027?',) [(1, 'Strongly bullish', 25000000000.0, None), (2, 'Bullish', 20000000000.0, 25000000000.0), (3, 'Neutral', 15000000000.0, 20000000000.0), (4, 'Bearish', 10000000000.0, 15000000000.0), (5, 'Strongly bearish', None, 10000000000.0)]\nrange (988, '2026-07-15', 3) (9182, '2026-09-20', 1)\n988|2026-07-15|3|No market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion for FY2027 is the available reference, placing expectations in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion).|Nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027, based on management's stated visibility. The unperiodized $200 billion TAM does not provide a basis to convert that figure into revenue above the $20 billion rung boundary.\n1145|2026-07-16|3|NVIDIA management's stated visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue; this reference is in rung 3, below the $20 billion rung-2 threshold.|Total FY2027 CPU revenue remains near, but below, $20 billion. The Noetra announcement specifies 13,750 Vera CPUs but no timing or revenue terms, and LANL's deployment schedule spans 2027 and 2028, so neither supports quantifying enough FY2027 revenue to move above the management visibility figure.\n1217|2026-07-17|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; because “nearly” indicates below $20 billion, the reference sits in rung 3.|FY2027 CPU revenue remains just under $20 billion, based on management's nearly $20 billion visibility. Noetra's 13,750-CPU plan has no timing or revenue terms, and LANL's 2027–2028 deployment gives no FY2027 share, so neither supports a quantified addition taking the outcome to $20 billion.\n1251|2026-07-18|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue; this is just below $20 billion and sits at rung 3.|Near, but below, $20 billion for FY2027, based on management's stated visibility. The disclosed Noetra CPU count and LANL deployments do not provide enough timing or revenue-per-CPU information to derive an additional FY2027 amount.\n1264|2026-07-19|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this is just below $20 billion and sits at rung 3.|Near, but below, $20 billion for FY2027, based on management's stated visibility. The announced Noetra CPU quantity and LANL deployments support demand, but without revenue, pricing, or timing information they do not establish enough incremental FY2027 revenue to move the estimate above the rung 2 threshold.\n1349|2026-07-20|3|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this reference is rung 3 (at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion). No separate consensus figure is supplied.|Near, but just below, $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on management's FY2027 visibility statement. The $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period, and the announced CPU deployments lack enough FY2027 timing and pricing information to derive additional revenue.\n1472|2026-07-21|3|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027; this is below $20 billion and therefore sits in rung 3.|FY2027 CPU revenue remains near, but just below, $20 billion, consistent with management’s stated visibility. Wolfe Research’s estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion of shipments in 2026, but it does not establish FY2027 revenue above the stated visibility or support adding the Noetra units as separate, dated revenue.\n1570|2026-07-22|3|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue; “nearly $20 billion” is below the $20 billion threshold and sits in rung 3.|Approximately, but below, $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, based on management’s direct visibility figure. The separate $20 billion standalone-business target has no period, while the 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion (1.3 million × $5,000) and does not quantify an FY2027 revision.\n1690|2026-07-23|2|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue; just below $20 billion, this reference sits in rung 3.|Approximately $20 billion: NVIDIA anticipates $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue in 2026 and says it is on track for $20 billion in CPU sales this year. That amount is at rung 2's $20 billion lower boundary.\n1772|2026-07-24|2|NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue; because that is just below $20 billion, the reference sits in rung 3.|Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, based on management's nearly-$20-billion visibility and its statements that it was on track for $20 billion in CPU sales and anticipated $20 billion from standalone Vera CPUs in calendar 2026. The calendar-year figure is broadly aligned with, but not additive to, FY2027.\n1837|2026-07-25|2|NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the reference, just below the $20 billion boundary and therefore in rung 3; the related “on track for $20 billion” wording is at the rung 2 threshold.|Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, with the base case at or just above the $20 billion rung boundary. This is anchored in NVIDIA's direct FY2027 visibility and on-track statements; the 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion but is for a different period and does not replace the FY2027 outlook.\n1918|2026-07-26|2|NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is just below the $20 billion boundary and sits in rung 3; the reported outlook of being on track for $20 billion sits in rung 2. The primary reference is therefore approximately $20 billion at the rung 2 boundary.|About $20 billion in FY2027: the direct FY2027 visibility and on-track outlook center on that figure. Production and deployment evidence support the outlook, while the 2026 shipment estimate and Noetra plan are not added as separate full-year revenue because they cover different periods or have unspecified timing.\n2161|2026-07-27|2|No external market consensus is provided. The company reference is nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, literally just below the $20 billion cutoff and thus rung 3; the reported “on track for $20 billion” formulation is at the cutoff, rung 2.|About $20 billion for FY2027: NVIDIA’s visibility and on-track statements anchor the forecast, reinforced by its $20 billion calendar-2026 expectation and evidence that production and deliveries have begun. Wolfe’s 1.3 million-chip estimate at roughly $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a third-party estimate, not a quantified revision to NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook.\n2325|2026-07-28|2|No external consensus is supplied. NVIDIA's FY2027 reference is “visibility to nearly $20 billion,” just below $20 billion and therefore rung 3 by the literal boundaries; its separate “on track for $20 billion” statement is exactly at the rung-2 threshold.|About $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, based chiefly on NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility and on-track statements, reinforced by the reported production and customer deliveries. The specific $6.5 billion Wolfe Research calculation (1.3 million chips × about $5,000) is a material counterweight, but does not displace the company's direct FY2027 outlook; the implied figure reaches rung 2's $20 billion floor, not rung 1's $25 billion.\n2445|2026-07-29|2|No external market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA’s reference is nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027, with its “on track for $20 billion” statement placing the reference at rung 2 (at the $20 billion boundary).|About $20 billion for FY2027: NVIDIA says it has visibility to nearly $20 billion and is on track for $20 billion, and its CEO confirmed the figure is for standalone CPUs. The Noetra CPU quantity is only about $69 million at Wolfe Research’s $5,000 ASP and has no timing, so it supports the ramp but does not materially add to the FY2027 estimate.\n2553|2026-07-30|2|NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook of nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, alongside its statement that it is on track for $20 billion; this reference sits at rung 2.|About $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA’s nearly $20 billion visibility statement and its “on track for $20 billion” statement, with the CEO clarifying that the figure is for standalone CPUs. That is at the rung 2 boundary; the newer competitive and demand evidence does not quantify a revision across it.\n2633|2026-07-31|2|NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is nearly $20 billion, with a separate statement that it is on track for $20 billion. At the stated rounded target, the reference sits at rung 2 (at least $20 billion and less than $25 billion).|Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, using NVIDIA's nearly-$20-billion visibility and on-track-$20-billion statements as the direct forecast, supported by production, customer deliveries, and deployment plans. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, but it is an earlier estimate and does not establish a revised FY2027 total.\n2685|2026-08-01|2|NVIDIA’s FY2027 outlook of nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, reinforced by its statement that it is on track for $20 billion; at the stated rounded target, this is rung 2.|About $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA’s nearly-$20-billion visibility and $20-billion on-track statements. Wolfe Research’s 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment estimate at approximately $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for 2026, but does not by itself revise the FY2027 outlook.\n2724|2026-08-02|2|NVIDIA management’s nearly $20 billion FY2027 visibility and $20 billion on-track statement; the rounded $20 billion target sits at rung 2.|Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA’s stated visibility and on-track outlook. Wolfe’s 1.3 million 2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026; the available evidence does not establish that this revises the FY2027 outlook.\n2761|2026-08-03|2|NVIDIA’s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion (with earlier visibility described as “nearly $20 billion”) is the reference, centered at $20 billion and on rung 2.|Around $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA’s direct guidance; this is rung 2. At Wolfe’s roughly $5,000 ASP, its 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion, but it is an execution check rather than a revision to the later FY2027 guidance.\n2837|2026-08-05|2|NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027 is the reference; $20 billion is rung 2 (at least $20 billion and less than $25 billion).|Around $20 billion for FY2027, anchored by NVIDIA's current guidance. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, while Noetra's 13,750 CPUs imply about $69 million at that ASP; neither supplies a quantified basis to revise the FY2027 guidance above $25 billion.\n2908|2026-08-06|2|NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027 is the reference and sits at rung 2.|Roughly $20 billion for FY2027, based on NVIDIA's around-$20-billion guidance and reiterated visibility to nearly $20 billion; the disclosed deployments support execution but do not establish additional revenue sufficient to imply $25 billion or more.\n2951|2026-08-07|2|NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027 is the market reference supplied here and sits at rung 2.|About $20 billion for FY2027: management's current guidance anchors the estimate, and the production, delivery and customer-deployment evidence supports execution but supplies no quantified revision. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, while Noetra's 13,750 disclosed CPUs imply about $69 million at that ASP; neither supports lifting the FY2027 estimate to $25 billion.\n3260|2026-08-11|5|NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales; this is rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million chips in calendar 2026 multiplied by its roughly $5,000 ASP implies about $6.5 billion for 2026. Since FY2027 includes nearly all of calendar 2026 and one additional month, the estimate points to revenue below $10 billion absent unquantified incremental shipments.\n3345|2026-08-12|5|NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027; this reference sits at rung 2.|About $6.5 billion for FY2027: Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment forecast multiplied by its approximately $5,000 average selling price, with FY2027 overlapping almost all of calendar 2026. The disclosed deployments and commitments are not sized or timed enough to support a materially higher figure.\n3454|2026-08-13|5|NVIDIA’s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027 is the reference, at rung 2 (at least $20 billion and less than $25 billion).|About $6.5 billion: Wolfe Research’s 1.3 million 2026-chip shipment estimate multiplied by its roughly $5,000 ASP. Noetra’s 13,750 CPUs imply about $69 million at that ASP, but no timing is stated, so that deployment cannot be confidently added to FY2027.\n3625|2026-08-16|5|NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales is the reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|About $6.5 billion from Wolfe Research's 1.3 million 2026-chip shipment forecast multiplied by its roughly $5,000 ASP; the 13,750-unit Noetra deployment would be about $69 million at that ASP, with timing unspecified. The disclosed evidence does not quantify enough additional FY2027 units to reach $10 billion.\n3902|2026-08-18|2|NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027; this reference is rung 2.|Around $20 billion for FY2027, using NVIDIA's FY2027 sales guidance as the forecast and the delivery, production, and deployment evidence as support for execution. The disclosed customer deployments do not provide enough additional volume to support an estimate above $25 billion.\n4050|2026-08-19|2|NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027; rung 2.|Around $20 billion for FY2027, anchored to NVIDIA's direct guidance. Production and delivery evidence and customer commitments support execution, while Wolfe Research's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for 2026 at its stated ASP and 1.3 million-chip shipment forecast; that downside check does not quantify a revision to the FY2027 guide.\n4113|2026-08-20|2|NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion; this reference sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|Around $20 billion for FY2027, in rung 2. This follows the direct FY2027 guidance; the Wolfe Research estimate implies about $6.5 billion for 2026 (1.3 million chips × about $5,000), but does not quantify a revision to the FY2027 guide.\n4183|2026-08-21|2|NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance is around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales; that reference sits at rung 2.|Around $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA's stated guidance and its statement that it is on track for $20 billion. Customer deployments and production support execution, but the quantified AMD performance comparison is not tied to a revenue reduction.\n4219|2026-08-22|2|NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2.|Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, based chiefly on NVIDIA's direct guidance and nearly-$20-billion CPU-revenue visibility; no quantified revision or sufficiently sized customer commitment changes that total. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies approximately $6.5 billion, but is an earlier third-party estimate and not a revised FY2027 guide.\n4292|2026-08-23|2|NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027 is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2.|About $20 billion for FY2027, anchored to NVIDIA's direct guidance; the production, delivery, and customer-deployment evidence supports execution but is not sized enough to revise that figure. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, a downside signal, but does not supersede the later FY2027 guidance.\n4501|2026-08-24|2|NVIDIA’s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate market consensus is supplied.|About $20 billion for FY2027, anchored to NVIDIA’s around-$20-billion guidance and its stated visibility to nearly $20 billion. Wolfe’s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not FY2027; the disclosed Noetra deployment implies only about $69 million at that ASP and has no stated timing.\n4662|2026-08-25|2|NVIDIA’s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027 is the reference; it sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|About $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue: NVIDIA’s current fiscal-year guide is around $20 billion, and its clarification says the nearly $20 billion visibility figure is for standalone CPUs. The evidence supplies no quantified revision that would move this figure above $25 billion or below $20 billion.\n5194|2026-08-26|2|NVIDIA’s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027; rung 2. No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.|Approximately $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027, using the current FY2027 guide and the company’s near-$20-billion visibility as the direct estimate. Production, shipment and customer evidence support that level, but the more-than-double statement applies to FY2028 and does not provide a quantified FY2027 uplift.\n5371|2026-08-27|1|NVIDIA’s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales is the supplied reference; it sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).|Approximately $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue running at more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies total CPU revenue around or above $25 billion for FY2027, assuming the reported Grace run-rate carries forward.\n5726|2026-08-28|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the market/company reference; that figure alone sits at rung 2. The total-CPU question also includes Grace revenue.|Approximately $20 billion of guided standalone CPU revenue plus Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried as an annual run-rate, implies total FY2027 CPU revenue around or above $25 billion.\n5854|2026-08-29|1|NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).|More than $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue at a greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month run-rate.\n5998|2026-08-30|1|NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in FY2027 standalone Vera CPU sales is the available reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). No separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.|Above $25 billion: add NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook to Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carrying that reported run-rate into FY2027.\n6238|2026-08-31|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU sales guidance is the reference; it sits at rung 2. No separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.|Approximately $25 billion or more: about $20 billion of guided FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as the current run-rate.\n6459|2026-09-01|1|NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the relevant supplied reference and sits at rung 2. No separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.|About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus a roughly $5 billion-or-higher Grace CPU run-rate implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion, assuming the reported Grace run-rate carries through FY2027.\n6666|2026-09-03|1|NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the reference and sits at rung 2. It is guidance for standalone CPUs, not total CPU revenue.|Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus a continuing Grace CPU revenue base above its reported $5 billion traili