n FY2027 is the supplied reference; it sits in rung 3, which includes revenue of $15 billion to $20 billion.|The implied outcome remains near, but slightly below, $20 billion for FY2027, based on NVIDIA's stated visibility; the evidence does not support carrying it above the $20 billion threshold. Wolfe Research's estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026 for that product and period, not a replacement for NVIDIA's total-CPU FY2027 outlook.\n8879|2026-08-24|3|NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, and its stated $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation; this reference sits in rung 3.|About $20 billion in FY2027: management's current-year visibility and stated FY2027 expectation anchor the forecast at that level. Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while deliveries and planned deployments support a ramp; the evidence does not quantify enough additional FY2027 revenue to imply more than $20 billion.\n8883|2026-08-25|3|NVIDIA management’s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the operative reference; approximately $20 billion lies in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).|Approximately $20 billion in FY2027: use management’s explicit FY2027 visibility/expectation as the direct forecast, supported by deliveries and planned deployments, while not carrying the undated $20 billion business target forward as a higher figure. No supplied quarterly revenue figure warrants a separate annualized run-rate.\n8887|2026-08-26|3|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue is the available reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). No separate consensus figure is supplied.|About $20 billion in FY2027: management’s nearly $20 billion visibility anchors the forecast, and the latest production, shipment, and custo