ters gives approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. Robust booked demand supports a modest lift, but after discounting orders that span products and periods, the central estimate is roughly $390 billion.\n8824|2026-08-04|3|Bloomberg and Wall Street estimates are about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference at rung 3.|About $390 billion for FY2027: the reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, with Q2's 11% sequential growth carried forward through Q3 and Q4, imply about $386 billion before a modest demand-and-order uplift.\n8828|2026-08-05|3|Bloomberg and Wall Street estimates put FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (with estimates of 80–82% growth), which is rung 3.|Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Strong cloud demand and spending support a modest uplift to roughly $393 billion, partly offset by supply constraints and China exposure.\n8833|2026-08-06|3|Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectations are about $393 billion (also consistent with roughly 80–82% growth on FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion); that reference is rung 3.|Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Customer spending and order/supply signals support a modest uplift, while memory and China headwinds temper it, giving an implied FY2027 total of roughly $390–395 billion.\n8837|2026-08-07|2|The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027 (Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates), which is rung 3: $350 billion to below $400 billion.|Approximately $400–410 billion, with a central estimate near $405 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion and an 11% sequential carry-forward through Q3 and Q4 implies about $386 billion; stronger order visibility and customer spending support a modest uplift, while the broad periods covered by the large product-order figures limit how much to assign to FY2027.\n8841|2026-08-08|2|Bloomberg/Yahoo Finance market estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027, corresponding to rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).|About $410 billion (roughly $405–415 billion): Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided sequential growth through the remaining quarters, implies a base near $386 billion; customer demand and spending evidence supports a modest uplift.\n8844|2026-08-09|2|Wall Street's average estimate is about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the market reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).|About $415 billion. Carrying the roughly 11% sequential increase in NVIDIA's $91 billion Q2 guide forward gives an operating base near $386 billion for FY2027; customer investment, demand visibility, and some improved supply support a modest uplift, while China exposure and memory constraints temper it.\n8849|2026-08-10|2|Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).|Approximately $415 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus the $91 billion Q2 guide, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion before an uplift for demand and production evidence, tempered by supply and China risks.\n8853|2026-08-11|2|Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).|About $410 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion and the cited Q3 estimate of $103.1 billion, with roughly 11% sequential growth carried into Q4, imply approximately $390 billion before an uplift for strong demand, booked commitments and pricing. Memory and China-related constraints temper that uplift.\n8859|2026-08-12|2|Bloomberg/Wall S