data and Wall Street estimates put FY2027 revenue near $393 billion; the 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion. Both figures are in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).|About $396 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply roughly $391 billion; demand and orders support a modest uplift, but not enough to reach $400 billion.\n8879|2026-08-17|3|Market estimates put FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (e195, e669), rung 3: at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion. The 80% growth estimate also implies about $389 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion (e751, e577), likewise rung 3.|About $391 billion on the operating path: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, $103.1 billion estimated for Q3, and approximately $115.5 billion for Q4 if sequential growth continues at roughly 12%. The demand commitments support the upper end, but their multi-year periods do not establish enough FY2027 shipments to lift this estimate to $400 billion.\n8883|2026-08-18|3|Bloomberg data and Wall Street estimates put NVIDIA FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).|About $391 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 + $91 billion guided for Q2 + the cited $103.1 billion Q3 estimate + about $115.5 billion for Q4 by carrying 12% sequential growth from Q3. The Q4 calculation and total are estimates, not reported results.\n8887|2026-08-19|2|Market expectations put NVIDIA FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (e195), in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.|Approximately $403.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported for Q1, BofA's $94.5 billion Q2 midpoint and $107.5 billion Q3 estimate, then about $119.9 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 11.5% sequential growth implied by Q2 guidance.\n8892|2026-08-20|2|The market reference is Bloomberg/Yahoo! 