’s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and the disclosed but unsized customer plans do not justify extrapolating above the $20 billion management figure.\n8920|2026-08-26|3|NVIDIA management’s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the reference; it sits at rung 3, whose range is $15 billion to $20 billion inclusive.|Approximately $20 billion in FY2027. I carry forward management’s FY2027 outlook: the new more-than-double statement applies to FY2028, while the Grace figure is trailing-12-month revenue and the AWS CPU purchases have no specified period or exact quantity.\n8924|2026-08-27|2|NVIDIA management’s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the reference, on rung 3 (at the top boundary). Jensen Huang’s clarification that this is standalone CPU sales affects how it informs total CPU revenue.|About $25 billion: roughly $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus an approximately $5 billion annual Grace CPU run-rate based on the disclosed TTM figure. That implies revenue above $20 billion but does not make revenue above $25 billion most likely.\n8928|2026-08-28|2|NVIDIA management's FY2027 outlook is approximately $20 billion in CPU revenue (with the CEO clarifying that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales); this reference sits at rung 3. No separate consensus figure is supplied.|About $25 billion total: approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace's more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue, carried forward as a run-rate. That implies rung 2; the evidence does not make a total above $25 billion the most likely outcome.\n8933|2026-08-29|1|NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue outlook is the reference; at $20 billion, it sits on rung 3 (Neutral: $15 billion to $20 billion).|Approximately $25 billion or slightly more: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera outlook forward and add Grace CPU revenue at its disclosed trailing-12-month run rate of more than $5 billion. The resulting forecast is just over the rung 1 threshold of $25 billion.\n8937|2026-08-30|1|NVIDIA management's FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is about $20 billion, at rung 3. Management clarified that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales.|Approximately $25 billion plus: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera outlook alongside Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. This narrowly exceeds the $25 billion rung-1 boundary.\n8942|2026-08-31|1|NVIDIA management’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the relevant reference and sits at rung 3 (greater than $15 billion and at most $20 billion).|About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion, assuming that Grace run-rate carries through FY2027.\n8947|2026-09-01|1|NVIDIA management’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference and sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive).|Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. That clears rung 1’s greater-than-$25-billion boundary, though the amount above it is uncertain because quantified Vera shipment tracking implies materially less standalone revenue.\n8953|2026-09-03|1|NVIDIA management’s FY2027 outlook is about $20 billion for standalone CPU revenue; that reference is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).|Approximately $25 billion-plus for FY2027: about $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue, with Grace contributing on a run-rate basis after exceeding $5 billion in trailing-12-month revenue during FY2027. This is a forecast, not a proved FY2027 total, and it narrowly clears the rung 1 boundary of more than $25 billion.\n8957|2026-09-04|1|NVIDIA management's FY2027 outlook is about $20 billion for standalone CPU revenue; as a total-revenue reference at about $20 billion, it sits at rung 3. The market's broader CPU-demand estimates are not company revenue guidance.|About $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus more than $5 billion of Grace CPU revenue on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. This crosses the rung 1 boundary; the unsized customer deployments are not counted as additional revenue.\n8961|2026-09-05|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion is rung 3. It is for standalone Vera CPU revenue, not the total CPU revenue asked here.|Approximately $25 billion-plus for FY2027: combine the approximately $20 billion standalone Vera outlook with Grace CPU revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, using that reported run-rate as the estimate for Grace's FY2027 contribution.\n8965|2026-09-06|1|NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation is about $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue; that figure alone is rung 3 on this total-CPU-revenue ladder.|About $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. That is above the rung-1 threshold.\n8970|2026-09-07|1|NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation is the relevant company reference; taken alone as total CPU revenue, it sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).|Approximately $25 billion or somewhat more: the roughly $20 billion standalone CPU expectation plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, treated as a run-rate into FY2027. This is an estimate, not a reported FY2027 total, and it lies just above the rung-1 boundary of $25 billion.\n8975|2026-09-08|1|NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation; that reference sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).|Approximately $25 billion and slightly above: the $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation plus Grace CPU revenue exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as an annual run-rate. This is above the $25 billion rung-1 threshold.\n8979|2026-09-09|1|NVIDIA management’s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation is the reference; it sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).|About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace’s more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate yields total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, implying rung 1.\n8982|2026-09-10|1|NVIDIA management's roughly $20B FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation is the available reference and sits in rung 3 ($15B–$20B). The nearly $20B figure was clarified as standalone CPU sales; no separate market consensus figure is supplied.|Approximately $20B of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace's more-than-$5B trailing-12-month revenue run-rate carried through FY2027 implies total CPU revenue above $25B. That crosses the rung-1 boundary.\n8987|2026-09-11|1|NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue expectation is the reference; it sits at rung 3 (at most $20 billion).|Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue with a trailing-12-month run-rate already above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, in rung 1.\n8991|2026-09-12|1|NVIDIA management’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue expectation is the available reference; treating that as the total-revenue reference places it at rung 3 (at most $20 billion).|Approximately $25 billion-plus: carry NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook forward and add the continuing Grace CPU contribution, whose trailing-12-month revenue had already exceeded $5 billion. The latter is a run-rate input, not a claim that a single trailing-12-month figure by itself proves FY2027 revenue.\n8996|2026-09-13|1|NVIDIA’s FY2027 expectation is about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales (rung 3 if read as total CPU revenue). Since the CEO clarified that this is standalone and Grace is separate, the relevant total-CPU reference, adding the reported Grace run rate, sits just above $25 billion at rung 1.|About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue carried at its reported trailing-12-month run rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion. That is above rung 1’s $25 billion threshold; it remains well below the approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast in v14.\n9000|2026-09-14|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU revenue expectation is the market/company reference; it sits in rung 3. It is a stand-alone figure, not total CPU revenue.|A little over $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of stand-alone CPU revenue plus a roughly $5 billion-or-more Grace contribution, using Grace's reported above-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue as the available scale indicator. The TTM amount is not a guaranteed FY2027 Grace figure, so the estimate is close to the boundary rather than a precise point forecast.\n9003|2026-09-15|1|NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation is the closest supplied reference; it sits in rung 3. No separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.|About $25–26 billion: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a roughly $5 billion-or-more Grace contribution, using Grace’s reported trailing-12-month revenue as the run-rate indication. This is marginally above the $25 billion rung-1 boundary, though the TTM period partly predates FY2027.\n9007|2026-09-16|1|No separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied. NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation of about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales is the reference; that figure is in rung 3.|About $25–26 billion: carry Grace's more-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue into FY2027 and add NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion standalone CPU expectation. The estimate is narrowly above the $25 billion cutoff for rung 1.\n9012|2026-09-17|1|NVIDIA's expectation of about $20 billion in FY2027 standalone CPU revenue; exactly $20 billion is rung 3.|About $25 billion-plus: add NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation to Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried as a run-rate through FY2027. That clears the rung 1 boundary of more than $25 billion, narrowly.\n9016|2026-09-18|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation is the reference and sits at rung 3 (Neutral, $15–20 billion).|Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried as a FY2027 run-rate, implies slightly more than $25 billion in total CPU revenue—rung 1.\n9019|2026-09-19|1|NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation is the supplied reference; at exactly $20 billion it sits at rung 3. The supplied evidence does not give a separate market-consensus figure.|About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue carried forward at its reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies just over $25 billion in total FY2027 CPU revenue. That clears the rung-1 threshold narrowly; the Vera shipment estimate of about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 is a material execution risk, but not a quantified revision to NVIDIA's expectation.\n9025|2026-09-20|1|NVIDIA's stated expectation of nearly $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue for FY2027; that reference is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).|About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue carried forward at its reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\narm=\\\"control-1\\\"\np=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nfor vn in [16]:\n v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n rows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n import json\n print(json.dumps([arm,v,rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\narm=\\\"control-1\\\"\np=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nfor vn in [16]:\n v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n rows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n import json\n print(json.dumps([arm,v,rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"[\"control-1\", \"v16\", [[8749, \"2026-07-15\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility of nearly $20 billion is the available reference and sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Nearly $20 billion in FY2027, based on management's stated visibility for the year. That is below the greater-than-$20-billion threshold for rung 2 and within rung 3.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility of nearly $20 billion, which sits in rung 3. The $200 billion TAM statement describes market opportunity, not NVIDIA revenue or volume in hand; it does not imply revenue above $20 billion. The evidence therefore points to revenue near, but not above, the rung 2 boundary, so the forecast remains in rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e442\\\", \\\"e443\\\"]\"], [8752, \"2026-07-16\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Nearly $20 billion in FY2027, using management\\u2019s stated revenue visibility as the forecast; the other evidence does not quantify an adjustment to that figure or establish revenue above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The forecast remains near that level: the $200 billion TAM is an opportunity, not NVIDIA revenue; the CPU-to-GPU ratio view is directional and has no period or revenue estimate; and the scheduled first Vera CPU blade delivery is for initial testing, not evidence of volume in hand. Nothing supplied supports revenue above $20 billion, the rung 2 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e442\\\", \\\"e443\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [8754, \"2026-07-17\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately, but slightly below, $20 billion in FY2027, based on management's visibility statement. The TAM, workload-ratio view, and initial-test delivery schedule do not quantify additional FY2027 revenue or establish volume that would lift the forecast above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies revenue near, but below, $20 billion: the $200 billion TAM is not a revenue forecast, the GPU-to-CPU ratio view has no period or revenue estimate, and the scheduled first Vera blade delivery is for initial testing rather than evidence of volume in hand. The implied figure therefore does not clear the greater-than-$20 billion boundary for rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e584\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [8758, \"2026-07-18\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Nearly $20 billion in FY2027, using management\\u2019s direct FY2027 revenue visibility as the strongest forecast input. \\u201cNearly\\u201d implies below $20 billion, so the outcome remains within rung 3.\", \"The market/company reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies revenue near but below $20 billion: the $200 billion TAM is not a revenue forecast, the GPU-to-CPU ratio opinions give no FY2027 revenue estimate, and the scheduled first Vera blade delivery is for initial testing, not volume in hand. The implied figure therefore does not cross the greater-than-$20 billion boundary into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e584\\\", \\\"e597\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e596\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [8762, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this is in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Near, but below, $20 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management\\u2019s stated visibility to nearly $20 billion for that year. The untimed $20 billion standalone-business target does not establish an FY2027 figure above that visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The implied FY2027 outcome remains near, but below, $20 billion: the new $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period, while the $200 billion TAM claims and the GPU-to-CPU ratio commentary do not quantify FY2027 revenue. The first Vera blade is scheduled for initial testing, not a disclosed volume shipment. Nothing in the evidence supports crossing the greater-than-$20 billion boundary into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e584\\\", \\\"e597\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e596\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [8765, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion, at or below $20 billion: management\\u2019s nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility remains the strongest forecast input. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a partial-period/product check rather than an amount to add to the total FY2027 visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The new shipment and deployment evidence makes that outlook more credible, but does not quantify an upward revision above $20 billion: the $20 billion standalone-business target has no period, and the $200 billion TAM is not FY2027 revenue. The $20 billion rung-2 boundary is strictly greater than $20 billion, so an implied outcome near but not above $20 billion remains rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\"]\"], [8768, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference, and it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $20 billion, likely just below it: the direct FY2027 visibility remains the best quantified total, while the $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period. Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 imply about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 Vera shipments, a product-and-period-specific estimate that does not replace or add to the total FY2027 outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion, which sits in rung 3; rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. The disclosed deliveries and planned large OpenAI deployments support the credibility of that outlook, but neither they nor the undated $20 billion standalone-business target quantify an FY2027 revision above the boundary. Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for 1.3 million Vera CPUs at $5,000 each in calendar 2026, but that is a Vera estimate for a different period, not a measure of total FY2027 CPU revenue. The evidence therefore still implies roughly, and likely just below, $20 billion\\u2014not a rung-crossing outcome.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\"]\"], [8771, \"2026-07-23\", 3, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this is rung 3, which covers $15 billion through $20 billion.\", \"Around $20 billion in FY2027. The explicit $20 billion stand-alone Vera target for calendar 2026 is upward evidence, but is discounted as a target; Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion of Vera revenue for that calendar year, while reported deliveries and planned deployments lack disclosed volumes.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The implied outcome remains around $20 billion: NVIDIA\\u2019s separately stated $20 billion target for stand-alone Vera CPU revenue in calendar 2026 points upward, but it is a target rather than a reported run-rate, and the specific shipment estimate of 1.3 million units at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026. June deliveries and planned large OpenAI deployments support ramping sales but do not disclose volume sufficient to establish a total above $20 billion. The evidence therefore does not take the forecast across rung 2\\u2019s strict greater-than-$20-billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e612\\\"]\"], [8774, \"2026-07-24\", 3, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; rung 3.\", \"Nearly $20 billion in FY2027, remaining within rung 3. The $20 billion standalone CPU target is discounted as a target, while the cited 1.3 million-unit shipment forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026; the reported deliveries and planned deployment support growth but do not quantify enough volume to imply more than $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive). The forecast remains near $20 billion: management\\u2019s $20 billion standalone Vera statements are targets rather than demonstrated revenue at that scale, while the $5,000 ASP and 1.3 million-unit shipment forecast imply about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026. Deliveries and planned large OpenAI deployments support the ramp, but disclose no volume that lifts the FY2027 estimate above rung 2\\u2019s strict greater-than-$20-billion threshold.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e832\\\"]\"], [8781, \"2026-07-26\", 3, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"About $20 billion in FY2027, carrying forward management\\u2019s nearly-$20-billion revenue visibility rather than treating the $20 billion as an amount exceeded. The $5,000 ASP multiplied by Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million calendar-2026 Vera shipment forecast gives about $6.5 billion for that product, but does not quantify the full FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits at rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). The evidence still implies revenue around $20 billion, not above $20 billion: management\\u2019s forecast is near that boundary, while the $20 billion standalone-CPU statements do not establish revenue greater than $20 billion. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, but it is a product-specific shipment forecast and does not quantify all FY2027 CPU revenue. Deliveries and planned deployments support the ramp, while AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence adds execution risk; neither gives a quantified revision that moves the implied total across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\"]\"], [8783, \"2026-07-27\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s reference is visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, clarified as standalone CPU sales; this sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion, at or just below $20 billion, based principally on management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, but that is a product-specific estimate spanning periods and is part of\\u2014not additive to\\u2014the total CPU-revenue outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, with the CEO clarifying that the figure is for standalone CPU sales; that reference sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies about $20 billion, at or just below the rung-3 ceiling: the $1.3 million Vera shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, but it spans calendar and fiscal periods and is only one part of total CPU revenue, so it should not be added to the company\\u2019s total-revenue visibility. Deliveries and planned large deployments support the ramp, while AMD\\u2019s comparative-performance claims and market-share evidence add execution risk. Neither side supplies a quantified FY2027 revision that moves the total above $20 billion or below $15 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8789, \"2026-07-28\", 3, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"About $20 billion, just below the rung-3 ceiling, based primarily on the company's FY2027 visibility. The 1.3 million Vera-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026 for that product alone, not a replacement for or addition to the FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, with the CEO clarifying that the figure is for standalone CPU sales; that reference sits in rung 3. The implied outcome remains about $20 billion, just below the rung-3 ceiling. Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, but that is one product and a different period, so it does not replace or add to the FY2027 total-revenue guidance. Customer deliveries, planned large deployments, and the HPE server announcement support the ramp; NVIDIA's separate $20 billion 2026 product anticipation is not backed by stated shipment volume, while AMD's comparative-performance claims add execution risk. The evidence does not imply a quantified FY2027 revision crossing $20 billion or falling below $15 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8793, \"2026-07-29\", 3, \"No separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue in FY2027, clarified by Jensen Huang as standalone CPU sales, is the reference; nearly $20 billion sits in rung 3.\", \"About $20 billion, slightly below the $20 billion boundary: the direct FY2027 visibility statement anchors the estimate. Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, but does not determine total CPU revenue for FY2027.\", \"NVIDIA's nearly $20 billion FY2027 total-CPU-revenue visibility is the relevant reference and sits in rung 3. That direct FY2027 guidance still anchors the forecast near $20 billion, at the top of rung 3 but not above its $20 billion ceiling. Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000), but it is a forecast for one product and a different period, not a revision to total FY2027 revenue. Deliveries, planned large deployments and the HPE server support the ramp, while AMD's comparative performance and share evidence add execution risk; the evidence does not imply a total above $20 billion or below $15 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8797, \"2026-07-30\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s guidance is visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue in FY2027; that reference is rung 3 (at the top of the rung, but not above $20 billion).\", \"Near, but below, $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue. The direct FY2027 visibility remains the anchor; Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, a product- and period-specific estimate rather than a revision to total FY2027 revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 total-CPU-revenue visibility, which sits in rung 3. The additional evidence supports a substantial Vera ramp but does not quantify a revision above that guidance: OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment and the reported June deliveries support execution, while Wolfe\\u2019s estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000), not total FY2027 CPU revenue. AMD\\u2019s performance comparisons add competitive risk, but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA\\u2019s forecast. The evidence therefore points to revenue near, but not above, $20 billion\\u2014above $15 billion and within rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8799, \"2026-07-31\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion total CPU revenue visibility for FY2027 is the relevant market/company reference; it sits at the top of rung 3 (at most $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, based on management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility (e896) and its clarification that the figure is for standalone CPU sales (e897). Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit, roughly $5,000-per-chip estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, a product-and-period-specific supporting estimate rather than a basis to raise the FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue visibility for FY2027, which sits at the top of rung 3. The evidence still implies revenue near $20 billion: deliveries, planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment, and Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 support execution, but do not establish an upward revision to the FY2027 total. The newer AMD performance comparisons quantify a competitive risk, but provide no revenue or shipment revision from which to derive a haircut. Thus the forecast remains within rung 3 rather than crossing above $20 billion or falling below $15 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8805, \"2026-08-01\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's stated visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference is at the top of rung 3. No independent market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Near, but below, $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, placing the forecast in rung 3. The direct FY2027 visibility is the main anchor; Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion in calendar 2026 only, and is not extrapolated as the FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The evidence still implies revenue near, but below, $20 billion: the specific FY2027 visibility is the strongest forecast, while the $20 billion standalone-business target with no stated period and the separate 2026 estimate do not quantify an upward revision to FY2027. Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit shipment forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that is only one product's forecast for part of the relevant period, not the FY2027 total. Deliveries and planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment support execution. AMD's quantified performance comparisons and share gains are competitive risks, but do not supply a shipment or revenue revision from which to derive a haircut below $15 billion. The implied outcome therefore remains in rung 3, without evidence to cross above $20 billion or below $15 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8815, \"2026-08-06\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. This reference is in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion, but below $20 billion: the specific FY2027 visibility statement remains the strongest forecast, while the $20 billion standalone-business targets lack a period or do not revise that FY2027 figure. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 imply about $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026, but this is only a partial-period product estimate, not the FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue; there is no separate market-consensus figure, and that reference sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies revenue near but below $20 billion, not above the rung-2 threshold: additional deployment and market-demand signals support the ramp, but no quantified revision lifts the FY2027 forecast above the stated visibility. AMD's competitive position and performance claims are a counterweight, not a quantified cut to NVIDIA's forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8820, \"2026-08-07\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference is in rung 3. No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Near but below $20 billion for FY2027. The management visibility remains the best direct FY2027 figure; no quantified revision takes it above $20 billion, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million CY2026 units at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for that shipment estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; that sits in rung 3. The additional evidence supports a real Vera ramp, including deliveries and a planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment, but does not quantify an upward revision above $20 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion of CY2026 shipments; it is a meaningful counterweight, but a third-party estimate for CY2026 rather than a quantified revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue visibility. AMD\\u2019s performance and market-share evidence is also a counterweight, without a quantified cut to NVIDIA\\u2019s forecast. The implied outcome remains near but below $20 billion, so it does not cross the rung-2 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8826, \"2026-08-09\", 3, \"No separate consensus figure is supplied. NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference, and it sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately high-teens revenue, near but just below $20 billion in FY2027: management's nearly-$20-billion visibility anchors the estimate, while the customer deployment evidence supports the ramp and the quantified AMD performance comparisons temper confidence without specifying a cut.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion\\u2013$20 billion). The evidence still points to a Vera ramp: CPUs were delivered to named customers and OpenAI planned large-quantity deployment, while the new AMD performance comparisons add execution and competitive risk. Those comparisons do not quantify a revenue reduction, and no quantified revision lifts the outcome above $20 billion. The forecast remains near, but just below, $20 billion, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8830, \"2026-08-10\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Near, but not above, $20 billion in FY2027: use the CFO\\u2019s direct visibility figure for total CPU revenue, with the CEO\\u2019s clarification that it is standalone CPU sales. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip forecast multiplied by its roughly $5,000 ASP implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a lower-scale check, but it does not replace the company\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. That remains the best direct forecast: the CFO\\u2019s figure is for total CPU revenue and the CEO clarified it is standalone CPU sales. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a meaningful scale check that leans lower but does not replace the FY2027 company-wide visibility. The new AMD performance comparisons indicate competitive risk, but do not quantify a revenue cut; the additional demand commentary and repeated $20 billion claims do not establish revenue above $20 billion. The implied outcome remains near, but not above, $20 billion, so it does not cross the rung-2 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8835, \"2026-08-11\", 3, \"No separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference, and it sits in rung 3.\", \"Near $20 billion in FY2027: use management\\u2019s stated visibility for the year as the direct forecast. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but is a lower-scale check rather than a quantified revision to the FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The new delivery and deployment evidence supports a Vera ramp, but supplies no volume or revenue revision that moves the estimate above $20 billion. Wolfe Research\\u2019s estimate implies about $6.5 billion for 1.3 million chips at $5,000 each in calendar 2026, a lower-scale check, while AMD\\u2019s performance and market-share evidence adds competitive risk without quantifying a revenue cut. Taken together, the evidence still implies revenue near, but not above, $20 billion; it does not cross the rung-2 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8841, \"2026-08-13\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $20 billion, but not clearly above it: management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, and the CEO specified it is for standalone CPU sales (e896, e897). Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit CY2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for that calendar-year shipment estimate, a scale check rather than a replacement for the FY2027 guidance (e666).\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). The evidence still implies revenue near, but below or around, $20 billion: deliveries and planned large-quantity deployment support the ramp, while the new AMD comparisons add competitive risk without quantifying a revenue reduction. Neither the shipment estimate nor the other evidence establishes a move above $20 billion into rung 2, or a reduction below $15 billion into rung 4.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8848, \"2026-08-15\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the supplied reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion\\u2013$20 billion). No separate market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $20 billion, likely just below or around that level: management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility is period-specific, and the CEO clarified it refers to standalone CPU sales. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a narrower product-and-period estimate and does not replace the total FY2027 outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. That direct, period-specific outlook remains the strongest forecast input. Vera deliveries and planned large-quantity deployment support execution, while Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not a contrary estimate of total FY2027 CPU revenue. AMD\\u2019s quantified performance comparisons and market-share evidence add competitive risk, but do not quantify a revenue reduction; the evidence therefore keeps the forecast near, but not clearly above, $20 billion or below $15 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e443\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8850, \"2026-08-16\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the stated reference; it sits in rung 3. No market consensus is supplied.\", \"Near, but not demonstrably above, $20 billion in FY2027: the period-specific management visibility points to nearly $20 billion, and the delivery and deployment evidence supports a ramp. Wolfe's 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that is not a total FY2027 estimate.\", \"No market consensus is supplied. The strongest stated reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The period-specific visibility remains the best forecast input; deliveries, planned large-scale deployment and AI-infrastructure demand support execution, while the 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is not a forecast of total FY2027 revenue. AMD's competitive evidence adds downside risk but does not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's FY2027 figure. The implied result remains near, but not demonstrably above, $20 billion, so it does not cross into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8856, \"2026-08-17\", 3, \"No market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the strongest stated reference; that sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, with no evidence in force that implies revenue above $20 billion. This follows the period-specific management visibility, weighed against the lower calendar-2026 shipment estimate and execution and competition evidence.\", \"No market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 places the reference in rung 3. Evidence of deliveries and planned large-scale deployment supports execution, while Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a meaningful, but older and not FY2027-specific, counterweight. AMD's market-share and performance evidence adds competitive risk but does not quantify a reduction in NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue. The newer evidence does not establish revenue above $20 billion, so the forecast remains near $20 billion and does not cross into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8860, \"2026-08-18\", 3, \"No market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the reference; that is rung 3.\", \"About $20 billion in FY2027, based on management's explicit-period visibility guidance, clarified as standalone CPU revenue. This is near rung 3's $20 billion ceiling; Wolfe's roughly $6.5 billion calendar-2026 shipment estimate is a counterweight, not a replacement for the newer FY2027 guidance.\", \"No market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility of nearly $20 billion is the reference and sits in rung 3. That explicit-period guidance, with the CEO clarifying it is standalone CPU revenue, implies a FY2027 outcome near $20 billion\\u2014not above $20 billion. Deliveries, OpenAI's planned large-quantity deployment, and fall availability of a Vera-powered HPE server support execution, while Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000) and AMD's market-share and performance evidence are counterweights. Those risks temper confidence but do not quantify a revision to the FY2027 guidance. The evidence therefore keeps the forecast at rung 3, whose upper boundary is $20 billion; it does not imply crossing into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8864, \"2026-08-19\", 3, \"No explicit market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the reference and sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Nearly $20 billion for FY2027, carried from NVIDIA\\u2019s explicit-period visibility statement; \\u201cnearly\\u201d implies an outcome below $20 billion, so it remains in rung 3. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million calendar-2026 chips at about $5,000 each imply about $6.5 billion for that narrower estimate, but do not provide a quantified FY2027 revision.\", \"No market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 visibility of nearly $20 billion is the reference, and sits in rung 3. That explicit-period guidance remains the strongest forecast input and implies revenue just below $20 billion, within rung 3; it does not imply crossing above the rung\\u2019s $20 billion ceiling. Vera deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution, while Wolfe\\u2019s estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000) and the newer AMD performance comparisons are competitive headwinds. Neither those headwinds nor the broader market evidence supplies a quantified revision to the FY2027 guidance. The $20 billion standalone-business target without a period is not an independent FY2027 forecast, and the longer-dated $170 billion market estimate does not establish higher FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8866, \"2026-08-21\", 3, \"No independent market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the guidance reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive).\", \"NVIDIA\\u2019s explicit FY2027 visibility implies total CPU revenue just below $20 billion, in rung 3. The newer market-growth evidence supports the demand backdrop but supplies no quantified reason to lift NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 figure above that guidance.\", \"No independent market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 visibility is the relevant reference and sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies revenue just below $20 billion: the explicit-period guidance remains the strongest forecast, while deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned Q3 deployment support execution. AMD\\u2019s higher market forecast enlarges the opportunity but does not quantify NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 revenue or revise its guidance; AMD\\u2019s performance claims are competitive headwinds, not a quantified revenue revision. Neither changes the implied figure enough to cross the $20 billion boundary into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\"]\"], [8871, \"2026-08-22\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's stated visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the relevant reference; it sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). No independent consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Just under $20 billion in FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA's stated visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue and the CEO's clarification that the figure is for standalone CPU sales. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-unit estimate multiplied by its approximately $5,000 ASP implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, a cautionary estimate that does not directly replace the company's total-CPU FY2027 visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility of nearly $20 billion, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still points to revenue just below $20 billion, not above the rung-2 boundary. Wolfe Research's estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion of Vera revenue in calendar 2026 and is a material caution, but it is a product- and period-specific estimate rather than a replacement for NVIDIA's total-CPU FY2027 visibility. Deliveries and OpenAI's planned large-quantity deployment support execution; the larger server-CPU market outlook supports demand but does not establish NVIDIA's FY2027 share. AMD's performance comparisons are a competitive headwind, but do not quantify a revenue reduction. No quantified revision to NVIDIA's FY2027 outlook moves the implied figure across $20 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8876, \"2026-08-23\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the supplied reference; it sits in rung 3, which includes revenue of $15 billion to $20 billion.\", \"The implied outcome remains near, but slightly below, $20 billion for FY2027, based on NVIDIA's stated visibility; the evidence does not support carrying it above the $20 billion threshold. Wolfe Research's estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026 for that product and period, not a replacement for NVIDIA's total-CPU FY2027 outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). The evidence continues to imply revenue near, but not above, $20 billion: the newer AMD estimate of a much larger server-CPU market supports demand, but does not quantify NVIDIA's FY2027 share or revise NVIDIA's outlook. Deliveries and planned large-scale OpenAI deployment support execution, while Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 and AMD's performance claims are competitive headwinds; neither changes the total FY2027 outlook enough to cross the greater-than-$20-billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8879, \"2026-08-24\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, and its stated $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $20 billion in FY2027: management's current-year visibility and stated FY2027 expectation anchor the forecast at that level. Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while deliveries and planned deployments support a ramp; the evidence does not quantify enough additional FY2027 revenue to imply more than $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3; management also describes a $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation. The evidence implies revenue around $20 billion, but does not imply it will exceed $20 billion: deliveries and planned customer deployments support execution, while Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, and AMD's competitive claims are a counterweight. The larger server-CPU market and additional unquantified demand signals do not specify NVIDIA's FY2027 share or revise the company outlook enough to cross the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2262\\\", \\\"e2294\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2353\\\", \\\"e2405\\\"]\"], [8883, \"2026-08-25\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the operative reference; approximately $20 billion lies in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $20 billion in FY2027: use management\\u2019s explicit FY2027 visibility/expectation as the direct forecast, supported by deliveries and planned deployments, while not carrying the undated $20 billion business target forward as a higher figure. No supplied quarterly revenue figure warrants a separate annualized run-rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3; management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 expectation is also at rung 3. The evidence supports that level: Vera has been delivered to customers and OpenAI plans large-scale deployment, but the specific $20 billion figure is not a quantified upward revision. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, a meaningful execution counterweight, while AMD\\u2019s competitive claims temper the upside. The larger market and additional customer plans do not establish NVIDIA revenue above $20 billion. The implied outcome therefore remains around $20 billion, not above the rung-2 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2294\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2439\\\"]\"], [8887, \"2026-08-26\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue is the available reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). No separate consensus figure is supplied.\", \"About $20 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility anchors the forecast, and the latest production, shipment, and customer-demand evidence supports execution toward that level. The FY2028 more-than-doubling expectation is for the following year, while the $6.5 billion Wolfe estimate covers Vera in calendar 2026 rather than total CPU revenue for FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. The newer evidence supports a roughly $20 billion FY2027 outcome, not a clear move above the rung-2 boundary: NVIDIA reported Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, Vera shipments underway and full production, and AWS\\u2019s planned CPU purchases, while its preliminary forecast of more than doubling CPU revenue applies to FY2028. Wolfe Research\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, a meaningful execution counterweight, but it is not a total-CPU FY2027 forecast. The later ramp and demand evidence strengthen the prospect of reaching the guided level; they do not quantify a FY2027 revision above $20 billion. The implied outcome remains around $20 billion, within rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8891, \"2026-08-27\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, based on the FY2027 management visibility and supported by shipments, production and customer-demand evidence. The FY2028 more-than-doubling outlook is not carried backward into FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits on rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion for FY2027, not a clear amount above the $20 billion rung-2 boundary. Vera production, shipments and customer demand support reaching that scale, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026\\u2014not total FY2027 CPU revenue. NVIDIA\\u2019s more-than-doubling expectation applies to FY2028, and the later repetition of that outlook does not revise the FY2027 estimate. The reported Grace trailing-12-month figure and the AWS purchase reports support demand but do not establish a quantified FY2027 uplift beyond the guidance. No dependency conclusions are supplied.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8895, \"2026-08-28\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in FY2027 total CPU revenue, clarified by the CEO as $20 billion for standalone CPU sales; the reference is rung 3.\", \"Roughly the $20 billion standalone-CPU FY2027 outlook plus some additional Grace CPU revenue, implying total CPU revenue above $20 billion and most plausibly in the low-to-mid $20 billions. The reported Grace figure is over $5 billion trailing twelve months, but because that period straddles fiscal years it is not added in full as an FY2027 amount.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 total-CPU-revenue visibility, which sits at rung 3. The later clarification that the $20 billion FY2027 figure is for standalone CPU sales, together with Grace CPU revenue already exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, makes total CPU revenue above $20 billion more likely. I place the forecast in the $20\\u201325 billion range: the Grace figure is a trailing-12-month result, not a full-FY2027 forecast, so it does not establish that total revenue will exceed $25 billion. NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2028 more-than-doubling outlook is beyond this thesis\\u2019s period and does not by itself raise the FY2027 estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2439\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3175\\\"]\"], [8901, \"2026-08-29\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue visibility for FY2027 is the reference; nearly $20 billion falls at rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion).\", \"Approximately $24\\u201325 billion for FY2027: start from the roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU outlook and consider Grace\\u2019s greater-than-$5 billion TTM revenue as an additional contribution, but discount that historical TTM amount because it is not a distinct FY2027 forecast. This points to the upper end of rung 2, without making revenue above $25 billion the likeliest outcome.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 total-CPU-revenue visibility, which sits at rung 3. The standalone-CPU clarification, Grace\\u2019s greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue, and the newer Vera shipment, production, and customer-adoption evidence put the likely total above that reference. But the Grace figure is backward-looking TTM rather than a separate FY2027 forecast, and the additional AWS CPU demand is not quantified by period; the evidence does not make total FY2027 revenue above $25 billion the most likely outcome.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\"]\"], [8903, \"2026-08-30\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, clarified as standalone CPU sales, is the reference; nearly $20 billion sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a separate Grace CPU stream running above $5 billion on a TTM basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion, assuming Grace carries forward at roughly its current run-rate. That is above the rung 1 boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, clarified as standalone CPU sales; nearly $20 billion sits in rung 3. For the total-revenue question, the evidence now supports roughly $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a separate Grace CPU revenue stream already above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate. That implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion, crossing into rung 1. This is a forecast rather than a result already established: Grace\\u2019s TTM figure must carry forward, and the Vera outlook is a target. Production and customer shipments support execution, while competitive-performance claims and the analyst view of CPU sales as a low-single-digit share of NVIDIA revenue temper confidence.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8907, \"2026-08-31\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 outlook is about $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue. For this total-CPU-revenue question, that reference sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"About $25 billion and slightly above: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook forward, then add Grace CPU revenue, already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. The $20 billion Vera target is supported by shipments and production, while the Grace figure is a run-rate input rather than a confirmed FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook of about $20 billion for standalone CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3 for this question. The forecast adds the separate Grace CPU stream, whose trailing-12-month revenue was already above $5 billion, to the Vera outlook; that puts total FY2027 CPU revenue around $25 billion and slightly above the rung-1 boundary. This is a forecast, not an established FY2027 result. Wolfe Research\\u2019s older estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a material downside check, but it is outweighed by the direct FY2027 outlook, current shipments/full production, and subsequent customer adoption evidence. The AWS adoption is not sized in CPUs, so it does not justify adding a separate revenue amount.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\"]\"], [8913, \"2026-09-01\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 3 ($15\\u2013$20 billion). Management\\u2019s wording includes both nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue and a clarification that the $20 billion figure is for standalone CPU sales; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Roughly $25 billion or slightly more for FY2027: the approximately $20 billion standalone Vera outlook plus Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion, carried forward as a run-rate. This is an estimate, not an established FY2027 result.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. The wording is mixed: management described nearly $20 billion as total CPU revenue, while the CEO clarified that the $20 billion figure was for standalone CPU sales. Taking that standalone Vera outlook together with Grace\\u2019s already-over-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate implies roughly $25 billion or slightly more in total FY2027 CPU revenue. That forecast is only modestly above rung 1\\u2019s strict $25 billion boundary, but it crosses it. The reported Grace run-rate is not a guaranteed FY2027 figure, and the Wolfe shipment estimate is a meaningful downside check; later FY2028 growth expectations and CPU-market demand are not added to FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3416\\\"]\"], [8918, \"2026-09-03\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 3. Management clarified that the $20 billion figure is for standalone CPU sales.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU sales plus Grace CPU revenue at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate of over $5 billion implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion, in rung 1.\", \"NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU outlook is the reference: approximately $20 billion, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies a higher total: management's $20 billion FY2027 figure is clarified as standalone CPU sales, and Grace had already generated over $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that Grace run-rate alongside the standalone outlook implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, in rung 1. The latest approximately $20 billion server-CPU-demand statement is a meaningful qualification, but it is a demand figure rather than a revenue revision, and it does not displace the more specific standalone-sales outlook or the reported Grace revenue. The Wolfe shipment estimate is a downside check, while confirmed shipments and customer adoption support execution; neither changes the implied total across the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e3087\\\"]\"], [8923, \"2026-09-04\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook (including the stated standalone-CPU projection) is the reference; exactly $20 billion is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue, for which the latest reported trailing-12-month figure exceeded $5 billion, implies total CPU revenue around or above $25 billion. Carrying the Grace run-rate alongside the standalone outlook places the most likely outcome in rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at rung 3. The implied outcome is higher: management clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU sales, while NVIDIA reported Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that Grace revenue alongside the standalone outlook implies total FY2027 CPU revenue around or above $25 billion, making rung 1 the most likely outcome. The approximately $20 billion server-CPU-demand figure is demand, not a downward revenue revision; it does not displace the standalone-sales outlook. Wolfe's earlier shipment estimate is a downside check, while current shipments and customer deployments support execution. The implied total is close to the rung boundary, but the evidence favors exceeding $25 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8926, \"2026-09-05\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue outlook is the reference; it sits at rung 3. NVIDIA's separate wording of nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue also corresponds to rung 3 before accounting for Grace revenue.\", \"About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above about $25 billion. The CEO's clarification that the $20 billion is standalone supports treating Grace as additional.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 outlook of about $20 billion for standalone CPU revenue, which is rung 3; the roughly $20 billion total-CPU-revenue wording also sits at rung 3. For the total asked here, the CEO clarified that the $20 billion is standalone CPU, while NVIDIA separately reported Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Treating that Grace run-rate as additional to the standalone outlook implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of more than about $25 billion, crossing into rung 1. The CFO's phrase \\u201ctotal CPU revenue\\u201d is a countervailing formulation, but the CEO's clarification that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU resolves the scope in favor of adding Grace. The FY2028 more-than-double expectation and shipments underway support execution but do not independently revise the FY2027 estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e3087\\\"]\"], [8935, \"2026-09-07\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the market/company reference and sits at rung 3 (Neutral: $15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue, plus Grace revenue at its reported TTM scale of more than $5 billion, implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion. This assumes Grace remains around that run-rate; the implied figure is above the rung 1 threshold.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at rung 3. I interpret the CEO\\u2019s explicit clarification that the $20 billion is for standalone CPUs as resolving the CFO\\u2019s less-specific \\u201ctotal CPU revenue\\u201d wording: adding Grace\\u2019s reported TTM revenue of more than $5 billion gives an implied total above $25 billion. The new Vera shipment and production evidence supports execution, while Wolfe\\u2019s estimate implies about $6.5 billion of Vera shipments in calendar 2026 (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000), a meaningful counterweight that does not by itself displace NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 visibility and outlook or the Grace contribution. The resulting forecast remains above the rung 1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"]], [\"How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 25000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 20000000000.0, 25000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 15000000000.0, 20000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 10000000000.0, 15000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 10000000000.0]]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\narm=\\\"old-1\\\"\np=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nfor vn in [4,15]:\n v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n rows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n import json\n print(json.dumps([arm,v,rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\narm=\\\"old-2\\\"\np=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nfor vn in [14,16]:\n v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n rows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n import json\n print(json.dumps([arm,v,rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\narm=\\\"old-3\\\"\np=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nfor vn in [3,14]:\n v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n rows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n import json\n print(json.dumps([arm,v,rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\narm=\\\"old-2\\\"\np=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nfor vn in [14,16]:\n v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n rows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n import json\n print(json.dumps([arm,v,rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"[\"old-2\", \"v14\", [[439, \"2026-07-10\", 3, \"Bloomberg-compiled projections expect FY2027 sales of $393 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg\\u2019s direct sales projection; this is below $400 billion and therefore falls in rung 3.\", \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 sales projection sits in rung 3, just below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. The in-force themes are supportive of NVIDIA demand, but supply no quantified revenue adjustment that takes the forecast across that boundary; the most likely outcome therefore remains in rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\"]\"], [455, \"2026-07-11\", 3, \"Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion; this market reference sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $393 billion for FY2027, using Bloomberg's specific projection. The AI-lab demand share and supportive capex and export-policy themes add upside pressure, but state no FY2027 NVIDIA revenue adjustment, so they do not imply a figure above $400 billion.\", \"The Bloomberg FY2027 sales projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3. The other evidence and in-force themes point to demand support, but provide no quantified revenue revision that moves the most likely outcome from the $393 billion projection across the $400 billion rung-2 boundary; $393 billion remains $7 billion short.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e213\\\"]\"], [517, \"2026-07-12\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the specific market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion), $7 billion short of rung 2.\", \"About $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of approximately $91 billion, I carry the roughly 11.5% sequential increase forward to Q3 and Q4. This remains within rung 3 and below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3 and is $7 billion below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. The new Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply a lower run-rate: carrying the 11.5% sequential increase from $81.6 billion in Q1 to approximately $91 billion in Q2 forward for Q3 and Q4 gives about $387 billion for the year. That remains in rung 3, so the evidence does not cross a boundary. Demand and capex themes, plus the AWS GPU deployment plan, support upside, while supply, competition and China-related evidence temper it; none supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue revision sufficient to bridge the gap to $400 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e213\\\", \\\"e216\\\", \\\"e217\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e219\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e223\\\", \\\"e224\\\", \\\"e229\\\", \\\"e230\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e233\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e235\\\", \\\"e236\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e241\\\", \\\"e248\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e254\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e257\\\"]\"], [592, \"2026-07-13\", 3, \"Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion; this sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"Approximately $387 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth through Q3 and Q4 gives roughly $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The evidence-implied run-rate is about $387 billion: using Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the 11.5% sequential increase from Q1 to Q2 into Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $81.6B + $91B + $101.5B + $113.2B. That remains in rung 3, below the $400 billion boundary; competition, supply and China-related evidence temper the outlook, while the updated capex theme supports upside but does not quantify enough incremental NVIDIA revenue to cross that boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e284\\\"]\"], [722, \"2026-07-14\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference; it sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $387 billion: starting with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carry the approximately 11.5% sequential increase into Q3 and Q4, yielding about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively.\", \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3. The reported Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for FY2027: carrying the guided 11.5% sequential increase from Q1 to Q2 into Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $81.6B + $91B + $101.5B + $113.2B. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong AI-capex and deployment signals support the run-rate, while China-related restrictions, custom-chip alternatives and memory constraints temper it; none supplies a quantified adjustment large enough to cross a boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\"]\"], [854, \"2026-07-15\", 3, \"Bloomberg projects FY2027 revenue of $393 billion; that market reference is in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $387 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, an approximately 11.5% sequential increase. Carrying that growth forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, for about $387.3 billion total.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for FY2027 when the guided sequential increase from Q1 to Q2 is carried forward for Q3 and Q4. New order and supply-commitment signals support the run-rate, but they do not specify a period or revenue amount that can be added to it; the implied outcome remains below rung 2's $400 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e321\\\"]\"], [1016, \"2026-07-16\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $387 billion: start with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry their roughly 11.5% sequential increase forward to Q3 and Q4, implying about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The run-rate implied by reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance is about $387 billion: carrying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential increase of roughly 11.5% into Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and management\\u2019s order commentary support demand but are not stated as FY2027 revenue, so they cannot be added to the run-rate. The updated capex themes support demand, while the China-controls theme is a counterweight; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue revision. The implied figure remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e408\\\"]\"], [1093, \"2026-07-17\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion is the market reference; it sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $387 billion: start with reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91.0 billion, then carry their approximately 11.5% sequential growth rate into Q3 and Q4, implying about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion for those quarters.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is rung 3. The Q1 reported revenue and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion if the roughly 11.5% sequential increase from Q1 to guided Q2 carries into Q3 and Q4: $81.6B + $91.0B + about $101.5B + about $113.2B. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and management's order commentary support demand but are not stated as FY2027 revenue, so they are not added to that run-rate. Likely Chinese authorization and the capex themes push upward, while supply and China-related risks push downward; none gives a quantified revenue addition or reduction that moves the estimate across the $400 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\"]\"], [1120, \"2026-07-18\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $387 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $91.0 billion guided for Q2, then carrying the roughly 11.5% sequential growth rate forward gives about $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion if the 11.5% sequential increase from $81.6 billion to $91.0 billion continues through Q3 and Q4; that remains in rung 3, below the $400 billion boundary. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and management's order commentary support demand but are not FY2027 revenue and cannot be added to the run-rate. SK Hynix's HBM4 capacity reallocation is a supply risk, while NVIDIA says it maintained delivery performance; neither provides a quantified FY2027 revenue adjustment. The planned AWS GPU deployment and eased UAE export authorization are potential upside, but the deployment spans 2027\\u20132028 and neither item gives revenue in hand for this fiscal year. The three themes push upward, consistent with their conclusions, but do not supply a quantified addition that crosses the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [1217, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $387 billion: start with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry their roughly 11.5% sequential growth forward to implied Q3 revenue of about $101.5 billion and Q4 revenue of about $113.2 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The Q1 actual and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion if the roughly 11.5% sequential increase from $81.6 billion to $91 billion continues through Q3 and Q4. That is below the $400 billion boundary, so the forecast remains in rung 3. Strong demand and the updated capex and China-access themes support upside, while the HBM capacity shift is a supply risk; none provides a quantified FY2027 revenue adjustment sufficient to cross a rung boundary. The $2 million AWS GPU plan spans 2027\\u20132028 and is not sized in revenue, so it does not justify adding its full value to FY2027.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [1347, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"Wall Street\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate is about $393 billion (e195, e669), which sits in rung 3.\", \"About $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and the Q2 guide of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, for approximately $387.3 billion total.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, in rung 3. The current run-rate calculation is about $387 billion, also rung 3: the $91 billion Q2 guide is roughly 11.5% above Q1\\u2019s $81.6 billion; carrying that sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. The $119 billion of booked supply commitments and management\\u2019s comments on orders support demand, but no period or revenue-recognition schedule is supplied, so they do not justify adding that amount to FY2027. The implied total remains above $350 billion and below $400 billion. The HBM ramp and China factors are risks, while the China-access theme is an offsetting potential upside; none provides a quantified adjustment that moves the forecast across a boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e698\\\"]\"], [1450, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"Wall Street's average FY2027 revenue estimate is about $393 billion (e669), placing the reference in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion. Zacks' 80% FY2027 growth estimate is a similar market reference (e751).\", \"About $387.3 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying it through Q3 and Q4 gives about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. As a cross-check, 80% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $388.6 billion; both estimates fall in rung 3.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The implied run-rate is about $387.3 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion is roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided growth through Q3 and Q4 implies about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. This remains within rung 3. The 80% growth consensus applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion also implies about $388.6 billion, consistent with that rung. Wistron beginning mass production of GB300 is positive execution evidence, but no volume or NVIDIA revenue contribution is disclosed; the up-to-$500 billion infrastructure plan spans several years and is discounted, so neither changes the FY2027 run-rate. Booked supply commitments and management's order comments support demand but have no stated FY2027 recognition schedule. HBM and China-related risks temper the upside; the China-access theme is an offset, and the updated AI-capex theme supports demand. No quantified evidence moves the forecast across the $400 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e698\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e284\\\"]\"], [1572, \"2026-07-23\", 3, \"Market expectations are about $393 billion for FY2027 (e195, e669), placing the reference at rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"About $391 billion: carrying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate from $81.6 billion to $91 billion through Q3 and Q4 implies roughly $387 billion for FY2027, while 80%\\u201382% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389\\u2013$393 billion. These approaches support an estimate near $391 billion.\", \"The reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. The revenue outlook implied by the latest reported quarter and Q2 guidance, carried forward at their sequential growth rate, is about $387 billion for the year; FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion grown by the cited 80%\\u201382% estimates implies about $389\\u2013$393 billion. These estimates center near $391 billion, within rung 3. The newly cited $1 trillion of potential Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and does not specify NVIDIA's FY2027 share or shipment-backed volume; AWS's additional 2 million GPUs are planned across 2027\\u20132028. They are upside evidence, but do not establish a FY2027 figure above $400 billion. China-related restrictions and the company's exclusion of China Data Center compute from Q2 guidance are counterweights, but do not quantify a FY2027 shortfall.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e621\\\"]\"], [1643, \"2026-07-24\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection is $393 billion, rung 3. Zacks' 80% FY2027 growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $388.6 billion, also rung 3.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by $91 billion Q2 guidance implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through the remaining quarters gives approximately $387 billion FY2027 revenue. This is below $400 billion and above $350 billion, so it falls in rung 3.\", \"The reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $388.6 billion, also rung 3. Independently, Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through Q3 and Q4 gives roughly $387 billion for the fiscal year, within rung 3. Reported growth, intact delivery performance, and order commentary support demand, while reported China sales permission and expected large batches are positive but unquantified; the reported HBM supply outlook is a counterweight. The $1 trillion potential-revenue target spans 2026 and 2027 and is not shipment-backed FY2027 revenue for this question, so it does not justify moving the forecast above $400 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e882\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e234\\\"]\"], [1718, \"2026-07-25\", 3, \"Market expectations are approximately $393 billion for FY2027 (also described as 82% growth); this is rung 3, $350 billion to below $400 billion.\", \"The Q1 reported $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through Q3 and Q4 gives about $387 billion for FY2027. Allowing for unquantified demand and export-access upside, while discounting supply constraints, the forecast is near $390 billion.\", \"The reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, based on the reported 82% growth estimate, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The Q1 result of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for the year if Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth of roughly 11.5% carries through Q3 and Q4. Order and delivery evidence, as well as easing export access, support some upside toward the reference; memory constraints and the lack of quantified FY2027 China sales or product volumes limit it. The implied outcome remains below $400 billion, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e621\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e882\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e949\\\"]\"], [1788, \"2026-07-26\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street expects about $393 billion for FY2027 (roughly 82% growth from FY2026), which is rung 3: $350 billion to below $400 billion.\", \"The $81.6 billion Q1 result plus $91 billion Q2 guidance, with the guided roughly 11.5% sequential increase carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $387 billion. Allowing a measured uplift for $119 billion in booked supply commitments, the Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales forecast, and improved export access, while discounting multi-year targets and accounting for memory constraints, implies roughly $400\\u2013$420 billion.\", \"The reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, based on the Bloomberg/Wall Street estimate and roughly 82% growth from FY2026; that is rung 3. The Q1 result and $91 billion Q2 guide, carrying forward the roughly 11.5% sequential growth implied by guidance for Q3 and Q4, give a base path of about $387 billion. I raise that path modestly: NVIDIA reported $119 billion of booked supply commitments against the $91 billion Q2 guide, its $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales forecast supports further growth, and export access and memory-supply arrangements offer additional support. Those figures are not all FY2027 revenue: the product forecast spans 2026 and 2027, and the bookings are not recognized revenue. Memory constraints also temper the uplift. On balance, I imply roughly $400\\u2013$420 billion, above the $400 billion rung boundary but below $450 billion. The shown dependencies do not impose a conflicting whole/part conclusion; the capex and export-policy themes support the upward adjustment.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e284\\\"]\"], [2012, \"2026-07-27\", 2, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate is $393 billion, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies a rung-3 figure.\", \"About $405 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that forward gives about $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113 billion in Q4, or roughly $387 billion for the year, with booked supply commitments and demand evidence supporting a modest uplift above $400 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 3; the separate Zacks estimate of 80% growth on FY2026 revenue also implies a rung-3 figure. The reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for FY2027 if the 11.5% sequential growth embedded in guidance carries through Q3 and Q4. I put the forecast modestly above that run-rate, at about $405 billion: booked supply commitments and the Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales outlook support an uplift, with updated hyperscaler-spending conclusions and specific deployment and supply announcements adding support. These do not justify taking multiyear or unsized targets in full, and HBM constraints and export restrictions temper the uplift; the resulting estimate crosses $400 billion into rung 2, but not $450 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e698\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e905\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e907\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e883\\\"]\"], [2163, \"2026-07-28\", 2, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"About $401 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the embedded 11.5% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4, implies about $387 billion. I add a modest uplift for demand, deployment and investment signals, discounted because multiyear targets are not FY2027 revenue in hand and supply and China-related constraints remain.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue also implies roughly $389 billion, rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and $91 billion Q2 guidance, carried forward at the 11.5% sequential growth embedded in guidance, imply about $387 billion for FY2027\\u2014also rung 3. I forecast about $401 billion: strong demand and investment signals support a modest uplift over that run-rate and consensus, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales figure spans products and periods and is not FY2027 revenue in hand. Memory constraints and China-related limits temper the uplift. The estimate narrowly crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e882\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e996\\\"]\"], [2290, \"2026-07-29\", 2, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 sales estimate of $393 billion, consistent with the cited Wall Street average estimate, is the market reference and sits at rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $400 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carried forward at the implied 11.5% sequential growth, produce a roughly $387 billion run-rate; demand and booked-supply signals support a modest uplift toward $400 billion, but are not quantified as incremental FY2027 revenue.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 estimate of $393 billion, also reflected in Wall Street\\u2019s average estimate; that is rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and $91 billion Q2 guidance imply a roughly $387 billion FY2027 run-rate if the 11.5% sequential growth embedded in the guidance carries forward. I forecast about $400 billion: specific demand and supply signals support a modest uplift over that run-rate and consensus, while memory constraints and China-related limits temper it. That estimate only just crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales forecast spans products and periods, and the $500 billion SK initiative is not NVIDIA FY2027 revenue, so neither is counted in full.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1260\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e975\\\"]\"], [2387, \"2026-07-30\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectations of about $393 billion (approximately 82% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion); this is rung 3.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for FY2027 if the embedded roughly 11.5% sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4. I forecast roughly $400 billion after a modest uplift for strong orders and booked supply commitments, tempered by HBM constraints and no China data-center compute revenue in Q2 guidance.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street's FY2027 estimate of about $393 billion (roughly 82% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion), which sits in rung 3. Carrying forward the sequential growth embedded in Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion gives about $387 billion for FY2027; I raise that to roughly $400 billion, but not materially higher, on booked supply commitments and continued demand. The $119 billion commitment figure is not a quarter's revenue, and the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales outlook spans products and periods, so neither is counted as FY2027 revenue in full. HBM constraints and the exclusion of China data-center compute revenue from Q2 guidance temper the demand signals. Around $400 billion just crosses the rung-2 boundary. The export-control and hyperscaler-capex themes in force also favor revenue upside; no thesis dependencies are supplied.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [2459, \"2026-07-31\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue estimate of about $393 billion (roughly 82% growth), which sits in rung 3.\", \"Carrying Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward at their roughly 11.5% sequential growth rate implies about $387 billion for FY2027. Confirmed through-2027 product orders and stronger AI-capex and supply signals lift the forecast to approximately $400 billion, but those multi-period figures are not counted as FY2027 revenue in full.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street's FY2027 estimate of about $393 billion, which is rung 3. Carrying the sequential increase from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion to Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward implies roughly $387 billion for the year, below the $400 billion rung boundary. The confirmed Blackwell/Vera Rubin order outlook and other demand and capacity signals lift the forecast to around $400 billion, just into rung 2; they do not support $450 billion because the large order, spending and partnership figures span multiple products or periods and cannot be treated as FY2027 revenue. HBM supply constraints and the exclusion of China data-center compute from Q2 guidance temper the upside.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e252\\\"]\"], [2517, \"2026-08-01\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue estimates of about $393 billion, rung 3; the cited 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus the $91 billion Q2 guide, with the guided sequential growth carried forward, implies about $386 billion before later-period adjustments. Strong demand and order indicators lift the forecast to around $400 billion, just into rung 2, without counting multi-year or multi-product commitments as FY2027 revenue in full.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027 (Bloomberg/Wall Street), which is rung 3; the 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion also implies about $389 billion, in rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue and $91 billion Q2 guide, carried forward at the guided sequential growth rate, imply roughly $386 billion before later demand indicators; strong orders, customer commitments and hyperscaler spending support an uplift to around $400 billion, just across the rung-2 boundary. The large multi-year and multi-product order and spending figures are not treated as FY2027 revenue in full, while HBM constraints and the China exclusion limit the upside.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e882\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e905\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e996\\\", \\\"e1240\\\", \\\"e1260\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e1309\\\", \\\"e1310\\\", \\\"e1311\\\", \\\"e1312\\\", \\\"e1317\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1379\\\", \\\"e1380\\\"]\"], [2568, \"2026-08-03\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectations are approximately $393 billion, placing the reference in rung 3. The 80% growth estimate against FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion likewise implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"Carrying Q2's approximately 11.5% sequential growth rate forward from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion gives an indicative FY2027 total of about $387 billion. Strong order and customer-spending evidence supports a modest uplift to roughly $400 billion, but the multi-year, multi-product figures are discounted rather than counted in full.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3; the 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue also implies about $389 billion, in rung 3. The Q1 actual and Q2 guide, carrying the guided sequential growth rate forward across the remaining quarters, imply roughly $387 billion. Continued order and customer-spending evidence supports a modest uplift to about $400 billion, just over the rung-2 boundary; the multi-year product-sales and capacity figures are not counted as FY2027 revenue in full. HBM supply constraints and the Q2 China exclusion temper that uplift.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e252\\\"]\"], [2600, \"2026-08-04\", 2, \"Bloomberg's approximately $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with Q2's roughly 11.5% sequential growth carried forward for Q3 and Q4, implies about $387 billion. Stronger order and production evidence supports a modest uplift to around $400 billion, at the lower boundary of rung 2.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. Carrying Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward at its approximately 11.5% sequential growth rate gives about $387 billion for FY2027 ($81.6B + $91B + $101.5B + $113.2B). The newer evidence of full production and substantial orders supports a modest uplift to around $400 billion, but not a larger uplift: the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin figure spans products and years, and the $2 million AWS GPU deployment spans 2027\\u20132028. That modest uplift puts the most likely outcome at the rung-2 boundary; the HBM supply constraint and exclusion of China compute revenue from Q2 guidance limit the upside.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1317\\\"]\"], [2656, \"2026-08-05\", 2, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 sales estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion).\", \"The reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guidance, carried forward at roughly 11.5% sequential growth, imply about $387 billion for FY2027. Allowing a modest uplift for demand and order visibility, while discounting the multi-period, multi-product order figures and accounting for supply and China constraints, implies approximately $405 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 estimate of $393 billion, which is rung 3. Carrying the reported $81.6 billion Q1 revenue and $91 billion Q2 guidance forward at the implied approximately 11.5% sequential growth rate gives about $387 billion for FY2027. Strong demand and order visibility support a modest uplift to about $405 billion, above the $400 billion rung boundary but below $450 billion. This remains a modest uplift rather than a full allocation of the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin figure, which spans products and periods. HBM supply constraints and the exclusion of China data-center compute revenue from Q2 guidance limit the upside; the reported China sales permission and demand do not specify enough volume to justify a larger adjustment.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e882\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\"]\"], [2722, \"2026-08-06\", 2, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 sales estimate of $393 billion, consistent with the cited Wall Street estimates, is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Carrying the roughly 11.5% sequential increase from $81.6 billion in Q1 to $91 billion of Q2 guidance forward implies about $387 billion for FY2027. Allowing a modest uplift for confirmed multi-period Blackwell/Vera Rubin orders, customer demand and improved memory-supply assurance\\u2014but discounting orders that span products and years\\u2014implies approximately $410 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 estimate of $393 billion, also reflected in Wall Street estimates; that is rung 3. The revenue run-rate implied by $81.6 billion in Q1 and $91 billion of Q2 guidance, carrying the approximately 11.5% sequential growth forward, is about $387 billion. The confirmed Blackwell/Vera Rubin order book, continuing customer demand and improving supply assurance justify a modest uplift to about $410 billion, but the orders span products and periods and cannot be assigned in full to FY2027. Memory constraints and the limited China sales evidence temper the uplift. About $410 billion is above the $400 billion boundary but below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e905\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e1426\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e882\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e1309\\\", \\\"e1377\\\"]\"], [2768, \"2026-08-07\", 2, \"Bloomberg and Wall Street estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027 are the market reference; that amount is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $405 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply roughly $387 billion for the year when the approximately 11.5% sequential growth is carried through Q3 and Q4; strong orders and customer demand justify a limited uplift, tempered by supply constraints and orders spanning multiple products and periods.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion, which is rung 3. The reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for the year if the roughly 11.5% sequential growth from Q1 to Q2 carries through Q3 and Q4. Strong orders, customer demand and hyperscaler spending support a modest uplift to about $405 billion, while HBM supply constraints, export controls and the fact that the large order figures span products and periods limit it. About $405 billion clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e905\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e1260\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e1309\\\", \\\"e1310\\\", \\\"e1311\\\", \\\"e1312\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1426\\\", \\\"e1427\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e1459\\\", \\\"e1466\\\", \\\"e1475\\\", \\\"e1491\\\", \\\"e1492\\\", \\\"e1493\\\", \\\"e1494\\\", \\\"e1502\\\", \\\"e1503\\\", \\\"e1505\\\"]\"], [2798, \"2026-08-08\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue estimates of about $393 billion; this is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $415 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carried forward at the approximately 11.5% sequential increase, implies about $387 billion for the year; recent demand evidence supports a measured uplift of roughly $28 billion, rather than assigning the full multi-period order figures to FY2027.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. The Q1 actual and Q2 guidance imply roughly $387 billion if Q2's approximately 11.5% sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4. Recent cloud-demand, customer-order and supply-partnership evidence supports an uplift to about $415 billion, while the orders span products and periods and memory constraints and limited China sales temper the uplift. That crosses the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1502\\\", \\\"e1503\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e1517\\\"]\"], [2830, \"2026-08-09\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectations are about $393 billion, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $415 billion. Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with roughly 11.5% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $387 billion; customer demand and order visibility support a moderate uplift, tempered by supply and China-sales constraints.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for the year if Q2's roughly 11.5% sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4. The forecast remains about $415 billion: recent customer and cloud demand, order visibility, and the updated hyperscaler-capex themes support an uplift from that run-rate, while the cited trillion-dollar product orders span multiple products and periods and are not taken as FY2027 revenue in full. Memory constraints and limited China compute sales temper the uplift. At roughly $415 billion, the forecast clears $400 billion but remains below $450 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1517\\\", \\\"e1520\\\", \\\"e1534\\\"]\"], [2916, \"2026-08-10\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue estimates are about $393 billion, an 82% year-over-year increase; this is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $410 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus the $91 billion Q2 guide, with approximately 11.5% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $387 billion; I allow a moderate uplift for customer demand and AI spending, discounted for shipment constraints and multiyear or multi-product commitments.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The Q1 actual and Q2 guidance imply a roughly $387 billion FY run-rate if Q2\\u2019s approximately 11.5% sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4. I forecast about $410 billion: strong AI-customer spending and demand visibility support an uplift from that run-rate, but the reported memory constraints and the fact that large order figures span multiple products and periods limit the uplift. At about $410 billion, the forecast clears the $400 billion rung-2 boundary but remains below $450 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1309\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e906\\\"]\"], [3106, \"2026-08-11\", 3, \"Wall Street's FY2027 revenue expectation is about $393 billion (e195), which sits in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"About $392.5 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, Q3 at the analyst estimate of $103.1 billion, and Q4 at approximately $116.8 billion by carrying Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth forward.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue, Q2 guidance, and specific Q3 analyst estimate imply about $392.5 billion if Q4 continues Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth: $81.6B + $91B + $103.1B + approximately $116.8B. Strong demand indicators support the run-rate, but the cited large order and infrastructure figures span products or multiple periods and do not establish enough FY2027 revenue to lift this estimate above $400 billion. Memory-supply constraints and the absence of China compute revenue from Q2 guidance also temper the upside. The implied figure remains below the rung-2 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1317\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e1696\\\"]\"], [3177, \"2026-08-12\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectations are about $393 billion, placing the reference in rung 3.\", \"About $392.5 billion: $81.6B reported for Q1 plus $91.0B guided for Q2, $103.1B estimated for Q3, and approximately $116.8B for Q4 by carrying forward the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. Applying the current sequential trend to the company\\u2019s reported Q1 and Q2 guidance, then carrying the Q2-to-Q3 increase forward one more quarter, implies about $392.5 billion: $81.6B + $91.0B + $103.1B + approximately $116.8B. That is about $7.5B below rung 2\\u2019s $400B threshold. Strong AI spending, orders, and deployment plans support the outlook, but the disclosed orders and targets span products and periods and do not establish enough FY2027 revenue to bridge that gap; memory constraints and the absence of China compute revenue from Q2 guidance temper the upside. The newer $630B opinion is not supported by the company\\u2019s quarterly run-rate evidence, while the $330B figure annualizes the latest quarter flat and is not a suitable FY2027 forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1750\\\", \\\"e1752\\\", \\\"e1757\\\"]\"], [3286, \"2026-08-13\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate and Wall Street's average estimate place the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies roughly $388.6 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $392 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual + $91.0 billion Q2 guidance + $103.1 billion Q3 analyst expectation + about $116.7 billion for Q4, carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate forward once.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, also reflected in Wall Street estimates; it sits in rung 3. The forecast remains around $392 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, plus $91.0 billion guided for Q2, plus the $103.1 billion analyst expectation for Q3, and an estimated Q4 of about $116.7 billion by carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate forward once. That is below rung 2's $400 billion threshold. Strong AI spending, booked supply commitments and orders support the run-rate, but the $1 trillion orders claim spans products and periods; it does not establish $400 billion of FY2027 revenue. Memory constraints and competition from customers' own chips temper the upside. The $630 billion opinion and the latest-quarter annualized figure are not suitable replacements for the quarter-by-quarter run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1811\\\", \\\"e1816\\\", \\\"e1821\\\", \\\"e1822\\\", \\\"e1750\\\", \\\"e1752\\\"]\"], [3333, \"2026-08-14\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street expects approximately $393 billion for FY2027; that is rung 3, Neutral ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"Approximately $392.5 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual + $91 billion Q2 guidance + $103.1 billion Q3 expectation, plus approximately $116.8 billion for Q4 by carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate forward.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street's approximately $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The implied forecast is about $392.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, $103.1 billion expected for Q3, and approximately $116.8 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate. That remains just below rung 2's $400 billion boundary. Strong reported growth, guidance, demand and AI spending support the run-rate, while memory supply constraints and customer alternatives temper the upside. The $1 trillion orders claim spans products and periods and does not establish $400 billion of FY2027 revenue; the China-related opportunity is not sized or included in guidance.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e883\\\"]\"], [3419, \"2026-08-15\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate is also consistent with a reference near $389 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion.\", \"About $392.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, $103.1 billion expected for Q3, and approximately $116.8 billion for Q4 by extending the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate. This remains below the $400 billion rung boundary.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's approximately $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion); the 80% growth consensus also implies about $389 billion against FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The forecast implied by the available quarterly inputs is about $392.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, $103.1 billion expected for Q3, and about $116.8 billion for Q4 by carrying Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth forward. That is below the $400 billion boundary; crossing it would require materially faster Q4 growth, and no quantified NVIDIA revenue revision establishes that. Strong demand, AI spending and foundry growth support the run-rate, while HBM constraints and credible customer alternatives temper the upside. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin orders claim spans products and periods and does not establish $400 billion of FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1804\\\", \\\"e1895\\\", \\\"e1896\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1811\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\", \\\"e1817\\\", \\\"e1819\\\", \\\"e1820\\\", \\\"e1822\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1686\\\", \\\"e1741\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e1696\\\"]\"], [3472, \"2026-08-16\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"About $389 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, then 12% sequential growth in each of Q3 and Q4 implies approximately $101.9 billion and $114.1 billion, for about $388.7 billion total.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion); Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion, also rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $389 billion: using $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, and 12% sequential growth for Q3 and Q4 gives about $101.9 billion and $114.1 billion, respectively. That remains below $400 billion. Crossing the boundary would require materially faster growth, while the $1 trillion Blackwell and Vera Rubin order figure spans products and periods and does not establish $1 trillion of FY2027 recognized revenue. Rapid reported and guided growth, customer spending and foundry growth support the run-rate; memory constraints and competing in-house accelerators temper it.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e1895\\\", \\\"e1897\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1811\\\"]\"], [3603, \"2026-08-17\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue projection (also reported as Wall Street's average estimate) is rung 3. Zacks' estimate of 80% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"About $389 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and guided Q2 revenue of $91 billion, then carry 12% sequential growth into Q3 (about $101.9 billion) and Q4 (about $114.1 billion). The resulting roughly $388.7 billion is below the $400 billion rung boundary.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion, which is rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion also implies about $389 billion, rung 3. The evidence-based estimate remains below the $400 billion rung boundary: the reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, followed by 12% sequential growth in each remaining quarter, imply about $388.7 billion for FY2027. Strong customer demand and higher hyperscaler spending support that run-rate, but the $600 billion OpenAI commitment extends through 2030 and its first 800 MW is expected in 2028, so it does not establish equivalent FY2027 revenue. Reported memory constraints and competing custom-chip plans temper the upside; the evidence does not support lifting the forecast across $400 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1982\\\", \\\"e2016\\\", \\\"e2017\\\", \\\"e1810\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1819\\\", \\\"e1820\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1424\\\"]\"], [3742, \"2026-08-18\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street's FY2027 estimate is about $393 billion, rung 3. Zacks' estimate of 80% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $388.6 billion, also rung 3.\", \"About $391 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual plus $91 billion Q2 guidance, $103.1 billion expected for Q3, and about $115.5 billion for Q4 by carrying roughly 12% sequential growth from Q3. This is below $400 billion and therefore rung 3.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027 (Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates), with Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue implying about $388.6 billion; both sit at rung 3. The operating path implied by the newer quarterly inputs is about $391 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, $103.1 billion expected for Q3, and about $115.5 billion for Q4 if the roughly 12% sequential growth assumption is carried forward. That remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. Strong customer demand and the reported order pipeline support the forecast, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and periods, and the $600 billion OpenAI figure extends through 2030; neither can be counted in full as FY2027 revenue. Memory-supply limits and competing custom chips also temper the upside. These inputs lift the implied figure from the previous $388.7 billion estimate, but do not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e2001\\\", \\\"e2057\\\", \\\"e2059\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1985\\\", \\\"e2017\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1686\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1811\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1821\\\", \\\"e1822\\\"]\"], [3862, \"2026-08-19\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue estimates of about $393 billion sit at rung 3 (at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion).\", \"Approximately $406 billion: use $81.6 billion reported in Q1, BofA's $94\\u201395 billion Q2 expectation and $107\\u2013108 billion potential Q3 guidance, then carry roughly 14% sequential growth into Q4 (about $122 billion).\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; Zacks\\u2019 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue also implies roughly $389 billion, rung 3. The latest operating path instead points just above $400 billion: the Q2 management guide is $91 billion, but BofA expects $94\\u201395 billion; BofA also sees potential Q3 guidance of $107\\u2013108 billion. Carrying the roughly 14% Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth implied by those estimates into Q4 gives about $122 billion, for approximately $406 billion across the year. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. This is a forecast, not established revenue; the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin sales and $600 billion OpenAI figures span multiple products or years and are not counted as FY2027 revenue in full.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e2017\\\"]\"], [3910, \"2026-08-20\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Yahoo Finance consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027, rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion also implies about $389 billion, rung 3.\", \"Approximately $406 billion: use reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, BofA's $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate and $107\\u2013108 billion potential Q3 guidance, then carry the roughly 13.8% Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate into Q4 (about $122 billion).\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027 (and Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion); both sit in rung 3. The more likely outcome is just over $400 billion: BofA's specific estimates of $94\\u201395 billion for Q2 and potential Q3 guidance of $107\\u2013108 billion imply roughly 13.8% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3; carrying that rate into Q4 gives about $122 billion, or approximately $406 billion for the year including reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion. This crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The official Q2 guide of $91 billion and the $91.9 billion analyst average temper that path, but the upward revisions and BofA's quantified upside view support a forecast above the reference. Supply constraints and competing custom chips are risks; large multi-year orders and revenue commitments are not counted in full as FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2059\\\", \\\"e2143\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1985\\\"]\"], [3989, \"2026-08-21\", 2, \"Market consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion, also rung 3; no FY2027 company revenue guidance is supplied.\", \"Using BofA's $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate and potential $107\\u2013108 billion Q3 guidance, their midpoints imply about 13.7% sequential growth; carrying that rate into Q4 gives roughly $122 billion. Adding reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion implies approximately $406 billion for FY2027.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027 (and Zacks' 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion); both are rung 3. The forecast remains just above $400 billion: BofA's estimate of $94\\u201395 billion for Q2 and potential $107\\u2013108 billion Q3 guidance imply, using their midpoints, roughly 13.7% sequential growth; carrying that rate into Q4 gives about $122 billion, or approximately $406 billion for FY2027 including reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The $91 billion Q2 guide and $91.9 billion analyst average are below this path, but Q1 exceeded its guidance range and analyst revisions were upward. HBM supply constraints and custom-chip competition are offsets; the supply-partnership and delivery evidence temper, but do not overturn, the quantified revenue path.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e2059\\\", \\\"e2143\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\", \\\"e1811\\\", \\\"e2033\\\"]\"], [4030, \"2026-08-22\", 2, \"FY2027 market consensus is about $393 billion (rung 3); the 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"About $405\\u2013406 billion: start with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, use an upside-tilted Q2 estimate of roughly $94\\u201395 billion against the $91 billion company guide, then use BofA's $107\\u2013108 billion Q3 expectation and carry the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate into Q4 (about $122 billion).\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027 (Bloomberg/Yahoo Finance consensus), and Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion; both are rung 3. The forecast is above $400 billion: Q1 was $81.6 billion, Q2 is guided at $91 billion but estimates and the recent upward-revision pattern leave room for an upside-tilted Q2 around $94\\u201395 billion; BofA's Q3 expectation is $107\\u2013108 billion. Carrying the implied sequential growth from roughly $94.5 billion in Q2 to $107.5 billion in Q3 into Q4 gives about $122 billion, or roughly $405\\u2013406 billion for the year. That crosses the $400 billion boundary, but not $450 billion. The official Q2 guide and consensus are below this path, while the higher quarterly estimates are less authoritative; supply constraints remain an offset.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e2143\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e2208\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1895\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2244\\\", \\\"e2179\\\"]\"], [4090, \"2026-08-23\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Yahoo Finance market expectations are about $393 billion for FY2027, rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"About $411 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, approximately $95 billion in Q2, approximately $109 billion in Q3, and about $125 billion in Q4 by carrying roughly 15% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3 forward. The Q2/Q3 estimates are forecasts, not reported outcomes; the Q3 calculation discounts the more speculative $112 billion estimate against lower consensus and BofA estimates.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion, also rung 3. The forecast is modestly above that reference: Q1 was $81.6 billion; I use about $95 billion for Q2, between the $91 billion company guide and higher analyst estimates, and about $109 billion for Q3, balancing the $103 billion consensus expectation with BofA's $107\\u2013108 billion view and the more speculative $112 billion estimate. Carrying the resulting roughly 15% sequential Q2-to-Q3 growth into Q4 gives about $125 billion, or approximately $411 billion for FY2027. That crosses $400 billion but remains below $450 billion, so rung 2 is the most likely outcome, not a claim that this revenue level is already established.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e2277\\\", \\\"e2291\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e2205\\\"]\"], [4286, \"2026-08-24\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue estimates of about $393 billion, with Zacks estimating 80% growth from FY2026; both references sit in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"Approximately $409 billion: start with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, forecast Q2 around $94\\u201395 billion, Q3 around $107\\u2013108 billion, and carry roughly that sequential growth into Q4 at about $124 billion. The resulting FY2027 total is above $400 billion but below $450 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027 (Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates), which is rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue also implies about $389 billion, rung 3. The forecast is higher: Q1 was $81.6 billion; I use roughly $94\\u201395 billion for Q2, weighting the $91 billion company guide and roughly $92 billion consensus against the higher $94\\u201395 billion BofA estimate and the less-established $98.18 billion estimate. For Q3, I use about $107\\u2013108 billion, between the roughly $103 billion consensus expectation and BofA's $107\\u2013108 billion view; carrying roughly that sequential growth into Q4 gives about $124 billion. That implies approximately $409 billion for FY2027: above $400 billion but below $450 billion. HBM supply constraints temper the upside, while the specific higher Q2 estimate and continuing demand support it; neither changes the most likely rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e2143\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e2406\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e408\\\"]\"], [4433, \"2026-08-25\", 2, \"The latest FY2027 analyst estimate is $396 billion (e2421), placing the market reference at rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"Approximately $408 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, about $94\\u201395 billion for Q2, about $107\\u2013108 billion for Q3, and about $122\\u2013123 billion for Q4 by carrying forward roughly the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth pace.\", \"The latest FY2027 market estimate is $396 billion, which is rung 3. The forecast is modestly higher: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion; for Q2, I anchor on the $91 billion company guide but allow for the quarter\\u2019s prior outperformance and specific $94\\u201395 billion estimates; for Q3 I use roughly $107\\u2013108 billion, above the roughly $103 billion consensus, and carry that sequential pace into Q4 for about $122\\u2013123 billion. That yields roughly $408 billion for FY2027\\u2014above the $400 billion boundary but below $450 billion. The $1 trillion order-book claim supports demand but spans 2026 and 2027 and is not all FY2027 revenue; supply constraints temper the upside. No reported Q2 result is supplied, so I do not treat the absence of one as evidence of a miss.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2402\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e2486\\\", \\\"e2277\\\", \\\"e2487\\\", \\\"e2244\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\"]\"], [4941, \"2026-08-26\", 2, \"LSEG analysts\\u2019 FY2027 revenue estimate is $396 billion, which sits at rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and an estimated Q4 of about $121 billion by carrying Q3\\u2019s roughly 12.3% sequential growth forward.\", \"The market reference is LSEG\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion, which is rung 3. The reported Q2 result and official Q3 guidance now imply a higher run-rate: carrying Q3\\u2019s approximately 12.3% sequential guided growth ($108 billion versus Q2\\u2019s $96.2 billion) into Q4 gives about $121 billion, or roughly $407 billion for FY2027 including reported Q1 and Q2. That is above the $400 billion rung boundary but below $450 billion. Supply constraints temper the forecast, while the multi-year AWS deployment supports demand but is not counted as FY2027 revenue in full.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2001\\\", \\\"e2057\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2729\\\"]\"], [5138, \"2026-08-27\", 2, \"LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Using reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, carrying Q3's approximately 12.3% sequential growth over Q2 implies Q4 revenue of about $121 billion. The resulting FY2027 total is approximately $407 billion.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues and Q3 guidance imply about $407 billion: carrying Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth over Q2 forward gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion, and $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B + $121B is approximately $407B. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. Newly quantified supply limitations temper the outlook, while the AWS deployment and broader order book support demand; neither supplies a quantified adjustment to the run-rate calculation, and the multi-period commitments are not counted in full as FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2057\\\", \\\"e2594\\\", \\\"e2596\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e2536\\\", \\\"e1336\\\"]\"], [5471, \"2026-08-28\", 2, \"LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 revenue consensus is the market reference; it sits at rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 + $96.2 billion Q2 + $108 billion Q3 guidance, plus an implied Q4 of about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to guided Q3.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues are $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance is $108 billion. Carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3% forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.3 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $407.1 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not $450 billion. The new evidence of supply constraints tempers confidence in that run-rate, but does not quantify a reduction to FY2027 revenue; multi-year GPU deployments and order commitments are not counted in full as FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2573\\\", \\\"e2592\\\", \\\"e3094\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e2536\\\", \\\"e2244\\\"]\"], [5602, \"2026-08-29\", 2, \"LSEG's FY2027 revenue consensus is $396 billion (rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion); Bloomberg's $393 billion projection is also rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and Q3 guidance of $108 billion, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to guided Q3. This is rung 2, at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion.\", \"The reference is LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion, which is rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also rung 3. The evidence-implied estimate is about $407 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2 but remaining below $450 billion. New evidence makes the supply constraint more concrete, but does not quantify a reduction large enough to change that run-rate estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3094\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e3296\\\", \\\"e2244\\\", \\\"e2536\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\"]\"], [5736, \"2026-08-30\", 2, \"LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also in rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and an estimated Q4 of about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth indicated for Q3.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion (e2421), with Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate also in rung 3 (e195). That is the market's reference, not the forecast implied by the newer results and guidance. Q1 and Q2 revenue totaled $177.8 billion; adding the $108 billion Q3 midpoint and carrying the guided roughly 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $121.3 billion for Q4 and $407.1 billion for FY2027. This crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but remains below $450 billion. Supply constraints are significant, but the 70% demand coverage statement does not quantify an additional revenue reduction, and the Q3 guidance already reflects current conditions.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e3094\\\"]\"], [5965, \"2026-08-31\", 2, \"LSEG FY2027 revenue consensus of $396 billion (e2421), in rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate (e195) is also in rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 + $96.2 billion Q2 + $108 billion Q3 guidance, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2 to Q3.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, which sits in rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also in rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate is higher: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue totaled $177.8 billion, NVIDIA guided Q3 to $108 billion, and carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate (about 12.3%) forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.3 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $407.1 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. The demand/supply constraint evidence is a downside risk, but it gives no quantified period-specific reduction to apply beyond the current guidance.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e3296\\\", \\\"e3575\\\"]\"], [6189, \"2026-09-01\", 2, \"LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also in rung 3.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108.0 billion, with Q4 estimated by carrying forward Q3's approximately 12.3% sequential growth ($108.0 billion \\u00d7 $108.0 billion / $96.2 billion \\u2248 $121.3 billion), implies FY2027 revenue of about $407.1 billion.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, in rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also in rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 results, followed by Q3 guidance, imply a higher full-year outcome: carrying Q3's sequential growth forward to Q4 gives about $121.3 billion and total FY2027 revenue of about $407.1 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. Supply constraints remain a downside risk, but the evidence gives no quantified FY2027 reduction to apply; the new >$400 billion estimates are supportive but do not replace the run-rate calculation.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3296\\\", \\\"e3469\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e3732\\\", \\\"e3633\\\"]\"], [6219, \"2026-09-02\", 2, \"LSEG FY2027 revenue consensus is $396 billion, placing the market reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is consistent with that rung.\", \"FY2027 revenue of about $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $96.2 billion reported in Q2, $108.0 billion guided for Q3, and approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 if Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth carries forward.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, which is rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also rung 3. The reported first two quarters and Q3 guidance imply a higher full-year outcome: carrying the Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3% into Q4 gives about $121.3 billion for Q4 and about $407.1 billion for FY2027. That is above the $400 billion boundary but below $450 billion, so the forecast remains rung 2. CEO comments confirm demand exceeds available supply, a downside constraint, while orders, customer deployments and higher TSMC capex support the outlook; none supplies a quantified FY2027 adjustment that displaces the run-rate calculation.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2057\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3383\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2536\\\", \\\"e3482\\\", \\\"e3296\\\", \\\"e3633\\\"]\"], [6392, \"2026-09-03\", 2, \"LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also in rung 3.\", \"Q1 $81.6 billion + Q2 $96.2 billion + Q3 guided midpoint $108.0 billion + implied Q4 about $121.3 billion = approximately $407.1 billion. Q4 is derived by carrying forward the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3%.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 figures and Q3 guidance imply a higher forecast: carrying the Q2-to-Q3 guided sequential growth rate forward gives about $121.3 billion for Q4 and roughly $407.1 billion for FY2027. That exceeds the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Huang's supply-constraint comments temper the outlook but do not quantify a FY2027 reduction that changes this run-rate calculation.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e1336\\\"]\"], [6590, \"2026-09-04\", 2, \"LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 plus $96.2 billion Q2 plus $108 billion Q3 guidance, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate.\", \"The reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The implied forecast is about $407.1 billion: Q1 and Q2 reported revenue total $177.8 billion, Q3 guidance is $108 billion, and carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3% forward implies roughly $121.3 billion in Q4. The $400 billion rung boundary is crossed, but the result remains below $450 billion. Management's comments that supply meets only 70% of current-customer demand and that memory, power, and data-center capacity constrain output temper confidence, but do not quantify a reduction to this guidance-based run-rate. The themes supporting AI infrastructure spending and limited export-policy barriers generally push toward higher revenue; they do not change the numerical run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e2723\\\"]\"], [6715, \"2026-09-05\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 revenue expectation of $396 billion sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion); Bloomberg's $393 billion projection is also in rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate (about 12.3%) into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion. The resulting FY2027 total is approximately $407.1 billion.\", \"The $400 billion boundary is crossed by the guidance-based run-rate, but the implied total remains below $450 billion. Strong reported growth, Q3 guidance, and the report that management raised guidance for the rest of the year support the higher outcome; memory and infrastructure constraints temper it, but none quantifies a reduction to the run-rate. The long-term order and deployment figures are not treated as FY2027 revenue in full. No dependency conclusions are supplied for this thesis.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e4010\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e3482\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e2536\\\", \\\"e1336\\\"]\"], [6860, \"2026-09-06\", 2, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection and LSEG's $396 billion consensus put the market reference at rung 3 (at least $350 billion but below $400 billion). A separate Wall Street estimate of $411 billion is rung 2, indicating some estimates already sit above the $400 billion boundary.\", \"About $415 billion: using LSEG's $396 billion estimate and 83% growth to infer an FY2026 base of about $216 billion, the reported Q1 and Q2 growth and Q3 guidance imply roughly $81.6 billion, $96.2 billion, and $108 billion for the first three quarters; carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth into Q4 implies about $129 billion there. This is a run-rate estimate, not a claim that FY2027 revenue is already established.\", \"The reference is mixed but centered below $400 billion: Bloomberg's $393 billion projection and LSEG's $396 billion consensus are rung 3; a separate Wall Street estimate of $411 billion is rung 2. The evidence-based projection is about $415 billion, so it crosses the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion. Strong reported growth and Q3 guidance support that higher forecast, while supply constraints temper it; the order-book and multi-year deployment figures are not counted as FY2027 revenue in full.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e3296\\\", \\\"e3482\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4137\\\"]\"], [6966, \"2026-09-07\", 2, \"The latest supplied Wall Street analyst expectation is approximately $411 billion (e4097), rung 2. Other cited market estimates are lower: LSEG's $396 billion and Bloomberg's $393 billion sit in rung 3, so the reference is mixed around the $400 billion boundary.\", \"Approximately $407 billion: FY2027 Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2's $96.2 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108.0 billion totals $285.8 billion; carrying Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth forward implies Q4 of about $121.3 billion. The total is about $407.1 billion, within rung 2.\", \"The working market reference is about $411 billion from the cited Wall Street analyst expectation, which is rung 2; estimates are mixed, with Bloomberg at $393 billion and LSEG at $396 billion, both rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate is about $407 billion: it clears $400 billion but remains below $450 billion. Strong recent reported growth and the next-quarter guidance support that level, while capacity and memory constraints temper the outlook. Multi-year orders and deployment plans are not counted in full as FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2536\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e3296\\\", \\\"e3482\\\", \\\"e3575\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e3732\\\", \\\"e3836\\\", \\\"e3893\\\", \\\"e3902\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4049\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4115\\\", \\\"e4117\\\", \\\"e4120\\\", \\\"e4137\\\"]\"], [7058, \"2026-09-08\", 2, \"The latest explicit Wall Street expectation is approximately $411 billion (e4097), which is rung 2. Earlier Bloomberg and LSEG estimates of $393 billion and $396 billion are rung 3.\", \"About $407 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion plus Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward Q3's 12.3% sequential growth once. This is an extrapolation rather than Q4 guidance, and it falls in rung 2.\", \"The reference is the latest explicit Wall Street expectation, about $411 billion, which sits at rung 2; earlier Bloomberg and LSEG estimates of $393 billion and $396 billion sit at rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $407 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion, Q3 midpoint guidance is $108 billion, and carrying Q3's 12.3% sequential growth forward once implies roughly $121.3 billion for Q4. That is above $400 billion but below $450 billion, so it remains rung 2. This is a run-rate extrapolation, not a reported or guided Q4 figure; supply constraints temper the estimate, while strong demand and current guidance support it.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2536\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e3296\\\", \\\"e3383\\\", \\\"e3575\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e906\\\"]\"], [7169, \"2026-09-09\", 2, \"Latest explicit Wall Street expectation shown: approximately $411 billion, rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). Earlier Bloomberg and LSEG estimates of $393 billion and $396 billion are rung 3.\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 plus $96.2 billion Q2, plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, plus an estimated $121.3 billion Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\", \"The reference is the latest explicit Wall Street expectation shown, about $411 billion, which is rung 2; the older Bloomberg and LSEG estimates of $393 billion and $396 billion are rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate remains about $407 billion: Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion, Q3 guidance is $108 billion, and carrying the 12.3% sequential growth implied by that guidance into Q4 gives about $121.3 billion. That is below the reference but still above the $400 billion rung boundary. Explicit supply constraints argue against treating the extrapolation as assured, while strong orders and demand support it; none supplies a quantified adjustment that moves the estimate below $400 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3094\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e3482\\\", \\\"e3575\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e4120\\\"]\"], [7396, \"2026-09-10\", 2, \"The latest explicit Wall Street expectation shown is approximately $411 billion for FY2027, at rung 2. Older Bloomberg and LSEG expectations of $393 billion and $396 billion are at rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, then approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by the Q3 midpoint.\", \"The current market reference is about $411 billion, which sits at rung 2; the older Bloomberg and LSEG estimates of $393 billion and $396 billion sit at rung 3. The operating run-rate implies about $407.1 billion: Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion, Q3 guidance is $108 billion, and carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to the Q3 midpoint into Q4 gives about $121.3 billion. That is below the reference but remains above the $400 billion rung boundary. Supply constraints argue against treating the run-rate as assured, but no supplied evidence quantifies an adjustment that takes it below $400 billion; strong commitments and investment support demand without establishing additional FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4117\\\", \\\"e4120\\\", \\\"e3482\\\", \\\"e4137\\\", \\\"e4115\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e906\\\"]\"], [7568, \"2026-09-11\", 2, \"Analyst expectations of about $411 billion for FY2027 sit at rung 2. The older LSEG estimate of $396 billion sits at rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion (Q1) + $96.2 billion (Q2) + $108 billion (Q3 guidance) + approximately $121.3 billion for Q4, carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance.\", \"The current market reference is about $411 billion, which sits at rung 2; the earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion sits at rung 3. The operating estimate is about $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance. Recent statements confirm that supply constraints are significant, but they do not quantify a revision to this run-rate; the announced early-2027 price increase supports revenue but likewise has no quantified revenue impact. The estimate remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it stays at rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e3633\\\"]\"], [7684, \"2026-09-12\", 2, \"The latest cited Wall Street analyst estimate is $411 billion, rung 2. Older Bloomberg and LSEG estimates of $393 billion and $396 billion, respectively, are rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 + $96.2 billion Q2 + $108.0 billion Q3 guidance midpoint + approximately $121.3 billion for Q4, carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied from Q2 to Q3.\", \"The market reference is the latest cited Wall Street analyst estimate of $411 billion, which is rung 2; older cited estimates of $396 billion and $393 billion sit in rung 3. The operating run-rate estimate is about $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance. The estimate is above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it remains rung 2. Supply bottlenecks are reiterated but not quantified as a change to that guided-growth run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\"]\"], [7776, \"2026-09-13\", 2, \"Latest cited Wall Street analyst expectation: $411 billion for FY2027, in rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). Older cited estimates of $396 billion and $393 billion are in rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion (Q1) + $96.2 billion (Q2) + $108 billion (Q3 guidance midpoint) + about $121.3 billion for Q4, carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance. This is rung 2.\", \"The reference is the latest cited Wall Street estimate of $411 billion, which sits in rung 2; earlier estimates of $396 billion and $393 billion sit in rung 3. The operating estimate is about $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance. This remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it does not cross a rung boundary. Recent statements make the supply constraint more specific\\u2014Huang said supply met 70% of current customer demand, while memory and data-center bottlenecks limited output\\u2014but they provide no period-specific revenue reduction to apply to the guided run-rate. Strong demand and orders add upside support, but do not quantify FY2027 shipments enough to displace the guidance-led estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2536\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e4490\\\", \\\"e4513\\\"]\"], [7934, \"2026-09-14\", 2, \"The latest cited Wall Street estimate is $411 billion (e4061), which sits in rung 2. The updated Zacks consensus growth estimate implies about $404.5 billion from FY2026 revenue, also rung 2.\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1, $96.2 billion in Q2, and $108 billion at the Q3 guidance midpoint, plus an estimated $121.3 billion in Q4 by extending the roughly 12.3% Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate. This is above $400 billion and below $450 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion, which is rung 2; the updated Zacks growth estimate of 87.4% applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies roughly $404.5 billion, also rung 2. The operating estimate is about $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 and the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3 guidance. Supply and data-center bottlenecks temper the upside, but provide no quantified period-specific revenue reduction to apply; the implied total remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\"]\"], [8048, \"2026-09-15\", 2, \"The market reference is Wall Street's approximately $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, in rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion). The Zacks FY2027 growth estimate of 87.4% applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies approximately $404.5 billion, also rung 2.\", \"FY2027 revenue of approximately $418.9 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 plus $96.2 billion Q2, an estimated $113.6 billion Q3 (3.1% above the $110.16 billion high end of guidance), and approximately $127.5 billion Q4 carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion, which is rung 2; the cited Zacks growth estimate also implies about $404.5 billion from FY2026 revenue, within rung 2. The operating estimate is higher: applying the recent average 3.1% beat to the high end of Q3 guidance gives about $113.6 billion for Q3; carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance gives about $127.5 billion for Q4. Together with reported Q1 and Q2 revenue, that implies about $418.9 billion for FY2027. This is above $400 billion but remains about $31 billion below the rung-1 boundary of $450 billion. Supply bottlenecks temper the upside, but no supplied evidence quantifies a revenue reduction sufficient to move the estimate below $400 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2536\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e3575\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e3902\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4117\\\", \\\"e4120\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e4609\\\", \\\"e4613\\\", \\\"e4634\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e4680\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4772\\\"]\"], [8204, \"2026-09-16\", 2, \"Wall Street analyst expectations of about $411 billion (e4097, e4348) are in rung 2; Zacks' 87.4% growth estimate implies about $404.5 billion using FY2026 revenue, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion; Q3 is estimated at about $113.6 billion by applying the reported 3.1% average beat to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance, and Q4 at about $127.6 billion by carrying forward approximately 12.3% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is roughly $411 billion from Wall Street analysts, with Zacks' 87.4% growth estimate implying about $404.5 billion from FY2026 revenue; both sit in rung 2. The operating estimate is higher: Q1 and Q2 were $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion; applying the recent 3.1% average beat to the $110.16 billion high end of Q3 guidance gives about $113.6 billion, and carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance gives about $127.6 billion for Q4. That implies approximately $419 billion for FY2027\\u2014above $400 billion but below the $450 billion rung-1 boundary. The new analyst Q3 estimate of $113\\u2013114 billion corroborates this run-rate. Supply constraints and memory-cost pressure limit confidence in further upside, but no supplied evidence quantifies a reduction large enough to move the estimate below $400 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e2705\\\"]\"], [8312, \"2026-09-17\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion for FY2027 (rung 2); Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate implies about $404.5 billion using FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion (also rung 2).\", \"Using Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, estimate Q3 at about $113.6 billion by applying the reported 3.1% average beat to the $110.16 billion high end of the $108 billion guidance. Carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance gives Q4 of about $127.6 billion, or approximately $419 billion for FY2027.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 revenue, which is rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate implies about $404.5 billion from FY2026 revenue, also rung 2. The operating run-rate implies about $419 billion, above the $400 billion floor but below the $450 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4832\\\", \\\"e4833\\\", \\\"e4769\\\"]\"], [8508, \"2026-09-18\", 2, \"The latest supplied direct analyst expectation is $411 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. Zacks' 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies roughly $404.5 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 reported revenue total $177.8 billion; the $108 billion Q3 guide, adjusted toward the $113\\u2013114 billion analyst estimate and the recent 3.1% average beat of guidance's high end, implies roughly $113.6 billion for Q3. Carrying approximately 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $127.2 billion, for approximately $418.6 billion across FY2027.\", \"The reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 revenue, which sits in rung 2. The reported first-half total and Q3 guidance support a strong run-rate; applying the specific $113\\u2013114 billion Q3 estimate, corroborated by NVIDIA's recent above-guidance history, and carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $419 billion. Supply constraints temper that estimate, but it remains above $400 billion and below the $450 billion rung-1 boundary, so the forecast stays at rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4704\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4901\\\"]\"], [8620, \"2026-09-19\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion of FY2027 revenue, placing the reference at rung 2. Zacks' estimate of 87.4% growth applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2.\", \"Approximately $418 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2, about $113.5 billion estimated for Q3, and about $127 billion for Q4 by carrying roughly 12% sequential growth forward from the estimated Q3 level.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2; the Zacks estimate of 87.4% growth on FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies roughly $405 billion, also rung 2. The run-rate points somewhat higher: use reported Q1 and Q2 revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, estimate Q3 at about $113.5 billion (the specific $113\\u2013114 billion forecast, consistent with NVIDIA's recent history of beating the high end of guidance), then carry roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4, or about $127 billion. That implies approximately $418 billion for FY2027. Supply limits temper the estimate, but the evidence does not quantify a further reduction within FY2027; $418 billion remains above $400 billion and below the $450 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4899\\\", \\\"e4901\\\"]\"], [8729, \"2026-09-20\", 2, \"Market expectation is about $411 billion for FY2027, rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). Zacks' 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: Q1 $81.6 billion plus Q2 $96.2 billion, estimated Q3 of about $113.5 billion (the $110.16 billion guidance high end plus the reported average 3.1% beat), and Q4 of about $127 billion by carrying forward the guided 12% sequential growth.\", \"The reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2; the cited Zacks growth estimate also implies about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $419 billion: estimate Q3 at roughly $113.5 billion by applying the cited average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance, then carry the guided 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, about $127 billion, alongside reported Q1 and Q2 revenue. Supply constraints are a meaningful downside risk, but the cited estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled demand is for \\u201cnext year\\u201d and is not quantified as a reduction to FY2027 revenue. The implied outcome remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it stays at rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e5056\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e5073\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4901\\\"]\"]], [\"How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 450000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 400000000000.0, 450000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 350000000000.0, 400000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 300000000000.0, 350000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 300000000000.0]]]\n[\"old-2\", \"v16\", [[849, \"2026-07-15\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in FY2027 CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Nearly $20 billion in FY2027, based on management's stated visibility. The $200 billion TAM is not an annual revenue forecast, so it does not justify projecting FY2027 revenue above the stated visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility of nearly $20 billion, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies revenue near but below $20 billion, so it does not cross the greater-than-$20-billion boundary into rung 2. The $200 billion TAM statement is a broad opportunity, not a quantified FY2027 revenue forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e442\\\", \\\"e443\\\"]\"], [1012, \"2026-07-16\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this is rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Nearly $20 billion, but below $20 billion: management directly stated visibility to nearly that amount for FY2027. The other evidence does not provide a quantified basis to move the forecast above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. That direct forecast implies revenue near but below $20 billion, so it does not cross the greater-than-$20-billion boundary into rung 2. The $200 billion TAM and the GPU-to-CPU ratio view suggest longer-term opportunity but do not quantify FY2027 revenue; the scheduled first Vera blade testbed delivery is execution context, not evidence that total CPU revenue will fall below management\\u2019s stated visibility.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e442\\\", \\\"e443\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [1088, \"2026-07-17\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Nearly $20 billion in FY2027, based on management\\u2019s direct visibility statement. The qualitative CPU-demand view and higher AI-capex outlook are supportive but do not supply a quantified basis to raise the implied figure above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The additional CPU-to-GPU ratio view points to higher CPU demand but gives no period or revenue estimate; the $200 billion TAM is an opportunity measure, not FY2027 revenue. The scheduled first Vera blade delivery is for initial testbed deployment, not evidence of commercial volume that would revise the total-revenue forecast. Nothing supplied quantifies revenue above $20 billion, so the forecast does not cross into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e584\\\"]\"], [1118, \"2026-07-18\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately, but just under, $20 billion in FY2027, based on management\\u2019s stated visibility to nearly $20 billion. The remaining evidence provides no quantified adjustment that would imply crossing above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. Carrying that FY2027 outlook through implies revenue near, but not established above, $20 billion. The CPU/GPU-ratio view is directionally supportive but supplies no FY2027 revenue estimate; the $200 billion TAM is not a revenue forecast, and the scheduled first Vera blade is for testbed deployment rather than commercial volume. The evidence therefore does not imply revenue above the $20 billion boundary needed for rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e584\\\", \\\"e596\\\", \\\"e597\\\"]\"], [1212, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately, but not clearly above, $20 billion in FY2027: the direct FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion. The separate $20 billion standalone-business target is not period-specific, so it does not justify carrying revenue above $20 billion for FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The newer $20 billion standalone-CPU-business target has no stated period and is paired with Rubin GPUs, so it does not establish FY2027 revenue above $20 billion. The broader CPU/GPU-ratio view and AI-capex themes support upside but do not quantify NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU revenue; the evidence therefore does not cross the rung-2 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e596\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e584\\\", \\\"e597\\\"]\"], [1348, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference is in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Nearly $20 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management's stated visibility. Wolfe Research's estimate implies about $6.5 billion of Vera CPU shipments in calendar 2026 (1.3 million \\u00d7 about $5,000), but it is not a FY2027 total-revenue estimate and does not warrant lifting the implication above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. Recent delivery reports, a stated plan for OpenAI deployments, and Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip forecast at about $5,000 per chip (about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026) support a real ramp, but the shipment estimate is for a different period and does not imply FY2027 revenue above $20 billion. The $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period, and the $200 billion TAM is not revenue; neither establishes a higher FY2027 figure. The evidence therefore still implies revenue near, but not demonstrably above, $20 billion, so it does not cross into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e689\\\"]\"], [1452, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue; this is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Near $20 billion in FY2027, based on management\\u2019s stated visibility. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip CY2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for that calendar year, while deliveries and planned large OpenAI deployments support the ramp; those figures do not quantify FY2027 revenue above $20 billion.\", \"The market reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The delivery, deployment and shipment evidence supports a real ramp, while the AMD competition evidence is a counterweight. None quantifies FY2027 revenue above the nearly-$20-billion reference, so the implied outcome remains near $20 billion and does not cross the rung 2 boundary of greater than $20 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\"]\"], [1568, \"2026-07-23\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference; nearly $20 billion falls in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027: management\\u2019s direct total-revenue visibility is the primary forecast, while the $20 billion standalone Vera target is discounted because disclosed deliveries and deployment plans do not establish the target volume. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, a caution against treating the standalone target as already secured.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The newer $20 billion standalone Vera ambition points toward the rung 2 boundary, but it is a target rather than demonstrated volume: reported June deliveries and planned Q3 deployments establish a ramp, not the units needed to deliver that target. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip shipment forecast at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion of Vera revenue in calendar 2026, a concrete check against taking the target fully; the stronger, directly stated total-revenue visibility still implies approximately $20 billion, not clearly more than $20 billion. Competition is a counterweight, so the forecast remains in rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\"]\"], [1644, \"2026-07-24\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference is rung 3. No separate market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $20 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management's stated visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue. The standalone Vera targets and the calendar-2026 shipment estimate are useful checks, but do not establish revenue above $20 billion for the full FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion). The evidence still implies approximately $20 billion, not more than $20 billion: the $20 billion standalone Vera statements are targets without a clearly defined period, while the specific shipment estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is not a full-year, all-CPU revenue figure. June deliveries and planned Q3 deployments support a ramp, but do not quantify additional volume sufficient to move the forecast above $20 billion. New AMD performance comparisons are a competitive counterweight, but do not overturn NVIDIA's direct FY2027 visibility; the implied figure therefore remains within rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [1790, \"2026-07-26\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Nearly $20 billion in FY2027. I use management's direct FY2027 visibility, clarified as standalone CPU revenue, as the main forecast; the 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 but does not quantify a full-FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue, which is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion). I still imply revenue near, but not above, $20 billion: this direct FY2027 forecast outweighs the less direct shipment estimate, while the $20 billion standalone-CPU clarification does not establish revenue above that amount. The specific estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a meaningful counterweight, but it is not itself a full-FY2027 total-revenue forecast. June deliveries and planned Q3 deployments support the ramp but give no additional volume that takes the implied figure over $20 billion; competitive evidence is also a downside counterweight. The implied figure does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e689\\\"]\"], [2011, \"2026-07-27\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue is the market/company reference and sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $19 billion to $20 billion in FY2027, carried from management\\u2019s direct \\u2018nearly $20 billion\\u2019 total-CPU visibility. The standalone-CPU clarification and qualitative deployment evidence do not quantify additional revenue above $20 billion; the available shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not a replacement FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. I still expect revenue near, but not above, $20 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility remains the strongest period-matched forecast, and the clarification that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU sales does not quantify any additional revenue that would take the total above the rung-3 ceiling. OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment and announced customer deliveries support the ramp, while the 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and the newer AMD comparisons add competitive risk. Those items do not provide a quantified revision to the FY2027 total, so the implied outcome remains within rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [2172, \"2026-07-28\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue is the operative market reference; it sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $19 billion to just under $20 billion in FY2027: the direct FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, and the standalone-sales clarification makes it relevant to this question. The 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but does not supply a quantified FY2027 revision.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; the CEO clarified that the figure is for standalone CPU sales. That remains the strongest period-matched forecast and implies revenue close to, but not above, $20 billion. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a third-party shipment estimate for a different period and does not establish a FY2027 total or quantified growth rate. Customer deliveries and OpenAI's planned Q3 large-quantity deployment support the ramp, while the AMD comparisons add execution and competitive risk. Neither those comparisons nor the other disclosed targets quantify a revision above $20 billion or enough downside to move the implied outcome below $15 billion, so it remains in rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [2288, \"2026-07-29\", 3, \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue, clarified by the CEO as standalone CPU sales, is the reference and sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $20 billion, but just below it: the CFO's nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility is the strongest period-matched forecast. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but neither that estimate nor the AMD comparisons provides a quantified FY2027 revision.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; Jensen Huang clarified that the figure is for standalone CPU sales. That remains the strongest period-matched forecast and implies revenue near, but not above, $20 billion. Wolfe Research's estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a third-party estimate for a different period and does not quantify a FY2027 revision. Deliveries to named customers, OpenAI's planned large-quantity deployment, and HPE's forthcoming Vera server support the ramp. New AMD performance comparisons add competitive risk, but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility. The implied outcome therefore remains within rung 3, rather than crossing the $20 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [2386, \"2026-07-30\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue, clarified by Jensen Huang as standalone CPU sales; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Roughly $20 billion, but below the boundary\\u2014consistent with management's 'nearly $20 billion' FY2027 visibility. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million 2026 units at about $5,000 each imply about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a downside check that does not directly replace the FY2027 company visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; Jensen Huang clarified that the figure is for standalone CPU sales. Nearly $20 billion sits in rung 3, just below the $20 billion boundary. The best period-matched forecast still implies revenue near that level. Wolfe Research's estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a meaningful downside check, but it is a third-party estimate for a different period and does not quantify a revision to NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility. Recent deliveries and OpenAI's planned large-quantity deployment support the ramp, while AMD's performance comparisons and share gains add competitive risk without a quantified FY2027 revenue reduction. The evidence therefore does not move the implied outcome across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [2462, \"2026-07-31\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, clarified as standalone CPU sales, is the reference and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $19 billion to just under $20 billion in FY2027. The period-matched management visibility anchors the estimate; deliveries and OpenAI's planned large-quantity deployment support the ramp, while the $6.5 billion implied by Wolfe Research's 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 each is a calendar-2026 cross-check, not a FY2027 replacement.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, clarified by Jensen Huang as standalone CPU sales; that reference sits in rung 3. The implied outcome remains about $19 billion to just under $20 billion: the guidance is the strongest period-matched input, and deliveries plus OpenAI's planned large-quantity deployment support it, but none of the new evidence quantifies an upward revision above $20 billion. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a meaningful downside check for a different period, not a replacement for FY2027 visibility. AMD's reported share gains and performance comparisons increase competitive risk but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue. The outcome therefore remains below the rung 2 boundary of greater than $20 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [2515, \"2026-08-01\", 3, \"No separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The reference is NVIDIA's guidance of visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue in FY2027, with the CEO clarifying this is standalone CPU revenue; nearly $20 billion is in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"About $19 billion to just under $20 billion in FY2027, carrying forward the company's period-matched visibility guidance rather than annualizing a quarter. Delivery and deployment evidence supports the ramp, but the $6.5 billion calendar-2026 shipment estimate and competition evidence do not establish a quantified change to that FY2027 outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, clarified by Jensen Huang as standalone CPU sales; that guidance sits in rung 3. The new evidence supports both sides: deliveries and planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment support a ramp, while Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and the AMD comparisons add competitive risk. Those points do not quantify a revision to FY2027 guidance, so the most likely outcome remains just below $20 billion, within rung 3. No dependency conclusion shown supplies a conflicting constraint.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e832\\\"]\"], [2720, \"2026-08-06\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this company-guidance reference sits in rung 3. No separate consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Just below $20 billion in FY2027. This carries forward management's nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility; the supplied delivery and demand evidence supports execution, but the shipment estimate and competitive evidence do not quantify a change to that guidance.\", \"The company-guidance reference is nearly $20 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. Delivery to customers and planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment support the ramp, while Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000) and the new AMD comparisons add competitive risk. Neither those comparisons nor the broader demand evidence quantifies a revision to FY2027 guidance. The implied outcome therefore remains just below $20 billion, within rung 3 rather than crossing the $20 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [2763, \"2026-08-07\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s guidance is visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; that reference sits just below $20 billion, in rung 3.\", \"The evidence implies FY2027 CPU revenue near, but below, $20 billion. The nearly-$20-billion guidance remains the best direct forecast; Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in exceeding the guidance, while the announced deliveries and planned OpenAI deployment support the ramp.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue visibility, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3. Customer deliveries and planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment support the ramp, but Wolfe\\u2019s estimate implies about $6.5 billion from 1.3 million Vera CPUs at roughly $5,000 each in calendar 2026, while the AMD performance comparisons add competitive risk. Those estimates and comparisons do not quantify a revision to FY2027 guidance; the likely outcome remains near, but below, $20 billion, so it does not cross into rung 2. The later demand and market-size commentary does not establish additional NVIDIA revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [2824, \"2026-08-09\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; that reference is just below $20 billion and therefore sits in rung 3.\", \"Nearly $20 billion, but slightly below the boundary: the FY2027 management visibility is the direct figure, supported by customer deliveries and planned large-quantity deployment. Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is not a quantified revision to total FY2027 CPU revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3. The shipment and customer evidence supports a real FY2027 ramp, while the quantified Wolfe estimate implies about $6.5 billion from 1.3 million Vera CPUs at roughly $5,000 each in calendar 2026. That estimate is below management's total-CPU visibility, but it is not a quantified revision to that full-year figure; the newer AMD performance comparisons add competitive risk without quantifying an NVIDIA revenue shortfall. The evidence therefore still implies revenue near, but below, $20 billion, not above the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [2912, \"2026-08-10\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the market/company reference; it sits just below $20 billion, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately, but just under, $20 billion in FY2027: management's near-$20 billion visibility remains the strongest period-specific input, supported by Vera deliveries and planned deployments; Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, while the competitive evidence supplies risk but no quantified reduction.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits just below $20 billion and therefore in rung 3. Deliveries to customers, planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment, and HPE's announced Vera server support a real revenue ramp. Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is not a quantified revision to management's total-CPU FY2027 visibility. AMD's reported performance advantages and market-share gains add competitive downside risk, but do not quantify a revenue shortfall; the evidence still implies revenue near, but below, $20 billion. Rung 3 includes outcomes through $20 billion, while rung 2 requires revenue greater than $20 billion, so the evidence does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e689\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [3104, \"2026-08-11\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue is the available reference; it sits in rung 3, which includes outcomes up to and including $20 billion. No market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Near, but most likely just below, $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027. This follows the period-specific management visibility, clarified as standalone CPU sales; the shipment estimate supports a real Vera ramp but covers only one product and calendar 2026, so it does not replace or cap the FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 (at most $20 billion). The strongest direct forecast remains that guidance, with the CEO clarifying that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales. Deliveries to named customers, planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment, and HPE\\u2019s announced Vera server support a substantial FY2027 business; Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, but it is a forecast for one product and period, not a ceiling on total FY2027 CPU revenue. Newer AMD performance comparisons add competitive downside, but neither they nor the market-share evidence quantify a revenue reduction or a delay sufficient to move the implied result below the near-$20 billion reference. The updated hyperscaler capex views support demand, while the evidence still does not make revenue above $20 billion more likely than not. The most likely outcome remains near, but not clearly above, $20 billion; it does not cross the rung-2 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [3284, \"2026-08-13\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this is at most $20 billion and therefore sits in rung 3.\", \"About $20 billion, but slightly below it: management\\u2019s direct FY2027 visibility remains the best quantified forecast, and the delivery and deployment evidence supports ramping sales without providing a quantified basis to exceed $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. Deliveries to named customers and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution, while AMD\\u2019s competitive position and performance comparisons are downside risks. The newer demand commentary and market-size estimates do not quantify an FY2027 revenue revision; the evidence still implies revenue near, but below, $20 billion, so it does not cross into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [3421, \"2026-08-15\", 3, \"No separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference, at rung 3.\", \"Close to, but below, $20 billion in FY2027, anchored to management\\u2019s \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026 Vera shipments, but that product-level estimate is not a full-FY2027 total CPU revenue forecast or a quantified revision to management\\u2019s figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits in rung 3 (at most $20 billion). The implied outcome remains close to, but below, $20 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility is the strongest forecast input, while named-customer deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate implies about $6.5 billion of Vera shipments in calendar 2026 (1.3 million \\u00d7 about $5,000), a meaningful downside cross-check, but it is not a full FY2027 estimate of total CPU revenue and does not quantify a revision to management\\u2019s total. AMD\\u2019s share and performance evidence adds competitive risk without establishing a lower total. The evidence therefore does not support crossing below $15 billion or above $20 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [3467, \"2026-08-16\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d is the stated reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion\\u2013$20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion, but just below it, based primarily on management\\u2019s direct FY2027 visibility. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, but covers Vera shipments rather than total CPU revenue for FY2027 and does not replace or quantify a revision to that visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). That current-year visibility is the strongest forecast input and implies revenue close to, but below, $20 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a meaningful downside cross-check, but it is not a total FY2027 estimate and does not quantify a revision to management\\u2019s visibility. Deliveries and planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment support execution; new AMD performance comparisons increase competitive risk but do not quantify an FY2027 revenue reduction. The evidence therefore does not imply crossing either the $15 billion or $20 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [3611, \"2026-08-17\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. This reference is rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Nearly $20 billion, but below $20 billion, based primarily on management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility and the CEO\\u2019s clarification that it refers to standalone CPU sales. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a narrower, different-period cross-check rather than a quantified FY2027 revision.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. That direct current-year forecast remains the strongest input, and the CEO\\u2019s statement clarifies that the nearly $20 billion is for standalone CPU sales. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment forecast at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a meaningful lower cross-check, but it is not a total FY2027 revenue estimate and does not quantify a revision to management\\u2019s visibility. Deliveries, planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment, and expected server availability support execution; AMD\\u2019s performance comparisons and market position are competitive risks but do not quantify a FY2027 revenue reduction. The implied outcome remains just below $20 billion, so it does not cross the $20 billion boundary into rung 2 or fall below the $15 billion boundary into rung 4. The updated hyperscaler-capex outlook strengthens the demand backdrop, but supplies no CPU-specific revenue increment.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [3737, \"2026-08-18\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this reference is rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion, below $20 billion: management\\u2019s period-specific \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility is the direct estimate, with the CEO specifying standalone CPU sales. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that narrower-period shipment estimate does not replace the FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3. That direct, period-specific forecast remains the strongest input, and the CEO clarifies that it is for standalone CPU sales. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment forecast at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a bearish cross-check, but it is neither a total FY2027 revenue estimate nor a quantified revision to management\\u2019s visibility. Deliveries, planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment, and expected server availability support execution; AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence is a risk but does not quantify a FY2027 revenue reduction. The updated AI-capex theme supports demand, but does not establish a higher CPU-revenue figure. The implied outcome therefore remains below $20 billion and within rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e689\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [3870, \"2026-08-19\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this reference is in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $20 billion in FY2027. The direct period-specific management visibility remains the anchor; Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but is a Vera shipment forecast rather than a quantified revision to the FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The implied outcome remains around $20 billion: the specific Wolfe forecast of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a meaningful downside cross-check, but it is a forecast for Vera shipments rather than a revised FY2027 total. Deliveries to customers, OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment, and HPE\\u2019s expected fall availability support execution; AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence adds risk but no quantified NVIDIA revenue cut. Together, the evidence does not imply a figure clearly below $15 billion or above $20 billion, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [3985, \"2026-08-21\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $20 billion in FY2027: use management\\u2019s direct visibility statement as the central forecast, with Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million \\u00d7 about $5,000 (= about $6.5 billion) Vera estimate as a downside cross-check for calendar 2026 rather than as a replacement FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. That direct forecast remains the best evidence for the due-date outcome. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a material downside cross-check, but it is a Vera shipment estimate for a different period, not a revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 total. June deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution. AMD\\u2019s larger market forecast supports the opportunity, while its competitive performance claims add execution risk but do not quantify a reduction in NVIDIA revenue. The evidence therefore implies revenue near $20 billion, within rung 3; it does not establish an outcome above $20 billion or imply one below $15 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [4028, \"2026-08-22\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $20 billion, but still just below the greater-than-$20-billion rung-2 threshold: the direct FY2027 visibility remains the strongest forecast, with deliveries and planned deployment supporting execution and no quantified revision displacing it.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies revenue near, but not above, $20 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility is the best period-specific forecast. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a different period and does not quantify a revision to FY2027 total revenue. Deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution, while AMD\\u2019s market-growth estimates support demand and its performance claims add competitive risk; none quantifies a change to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 revenue. The implied figure therefore remains within rung 3, below rung 2\\u2019s greater-than-$20-billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e832\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2262\\\"]\"], [4087, \"2026-08-23\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion in FY2027, based on NVIDIA\\u2019s period-specific \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility. The other $20 billion targets are not a quantified FY2027 revision, and the broader market forecasts do not establish NVIDIA\\u2019s share; the implied figure remains at or below $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies revenue near $20 billion, not above $20 billion: NVIDIA\\u2019s statements about a $20 billion CPU business and standalone Vera sales support the outlook, but do not quantify an upward revision for FY2027. AMD\\u2019s larger server-CPU market forecast and the AI-capex themes strengthen the demand backdrop, while AMD\\u2019s competitive performance claims are a counterweight; neither quantifies NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 revenue. The implied outcome therefore remains within rung 3 and does not cross rung 2\\u2019s greater-than-$20-billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\"]\"], [4282, \"2026-08-24\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue is the market/company reference; it sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility and $20 billion revenue statements anchor the estimate. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million 2026 shipments at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for that shipment estimate, a counterweight, but do not replace the stronger FY2027 company-level outlook; the wider market forecasts and unsized deployment plans do not quantify NVIDIA revenue above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3; rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion, not a figure above that boundary: the company\\u2019s $20 billion statements are the strongest direct forecast, while the new AMD market-growth estimate supports demand but does not establish NVIDIA\\u2019s share or an upward revision. AMD\\u2019s reported performance advantages are a counterweight. No supplied evidence quantifies the SpaceXAI deployment or other new demand sufficiently to lift the FY2027 estimate across the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2294\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2353\\\", \\\"e2405\\\"]\"], [4427, \"2026-08-25\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this reference is in rung 3 ($15B\\u2013$20B inclusive).\", \"About $20 billion in FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 visibility statement and its clarification that the figure is for standalone CPU sales. Customer deployment evidence supports the forecast but is not sized to imply revenue above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 ($15B\\u2013$20B inclusive); rung 2 requires more than $20B. The best implied FY2027 outcome remains about $20B, not above the rung boundary: NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance is the strongest forecast input, and Vera deliveries, OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment and SpaceXAI\\u2019s announced plan support execution, but none of those deployments is sized to justify an upward revision. AMD\\u2019s larger market forecast supports demand, while its reported performance advantages and the third-party 2026 shipment estimate temper confidence. These signals do not establish an FY2027 figure above $20B, so the forecast remains in rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e2294\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2479\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [4942, \"2026-08-26\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"About $20 billion in FY2027. The current FY2027 visibility remains the strongest direct forecast; the specific estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while the more-than-doubling expectation is for FY2028 and does not raise the FY2027 figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which is rung 3. The added evidence supports a substantial CPU business and a strong subsequent-year ramp, but does not quantify FY2027 revenue above $20 billion: AWS\\u2019s \\u201cmillions\\u201d of CPUs lacks a period and exact quantity, and the specific Vera shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 rather than total FY2027 CPU revenue. AMD\\u2019s competitive position and performance claims temper the upside. The implied FY2027 outcome therefore remains around $20 billion, within rung 3; the FY2028 more-than-doubling expectation is not carried backward into FY2027.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2479\\\"]\"], [5129, \"2026-08-27\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue is the market/company reference and sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $20 billion, likely just below that amount: management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility is the direct year-specific anchor. The more-than-doubling statement is for FY2028, so it is not carried backward into FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies FY2027 revenue around, and likely just below, $20 billion\\u2014not above the rung-2 boundary. Grace\\u2019s more than $5 billion TTM revenue is part of total CPU revenue, not an additional amount to add to the FY2027 outlook. Vera being in full production and the expectation of more than doubling CPU revenue in FY2028 support a strong subsequent ramp, but do not quantify FY2027 above $20 billion. The specific shipment and customer evidence is positive, while competitors\\u2019 share and performance claims temper the upside; neither side shifts the implied FY2027 figure across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2439\\\", \\\"e2479\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\"]\"], [5472, \"2026-08-28\", 1, \"No independent consensus figure is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion / $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook is the reference, at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Roughly $25 billion or somewhat more: take the approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook and add the distinct Grace CPU revenue stream, reported above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. The ongoing Vera shipments and full production support taking the standalone outlook substantially into account.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 CPU outlook of nearly $20 billion / $20 billion, which sits at rung 3. The newer clarification says the $20 billion is for standalone CPU sales, and NVIDIA separately reported Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Treating the Vera standalone outlook and the existing Grace revenue stream as distinct implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of roughly $25 billion or somewhat more; Vera shipments are underway and in full production, supporting the outlook. That implied total crosses the rung-1 boundary of more than $25 billion, though the exact amount remains uncertain.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2735\\\"]\"], [5594, \"2026-08-29\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook; this is rung 3 (Neutral: $15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Around or modestly above $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of FY2027 Vera standalone CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion, carried forward from the reported trailing-12-month figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3; the reference alone does not determine the forecast. Carrying the $20 billion Vera standalone outlook forward and adding the distinct Grace CPU revenue run-rate of more than $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue around or modestly above $25 billion. That just clears rung 1\\u2019s strictly-greater-than-$25-billion boundary, although the margin is narrow and Grace\\u2019s trailing-12-month figure is not itself a FY2027 reported total. Vera shipments being underway, full production, and the AWS CPU adoption support the outlook; Wolfe\\u2019s older 1.3 million-unit 2026 forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion and is a counterweight, but is weaker than the current management outlook.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2311\\\"]\"], [5746, \"2026-08-30\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook is the supplied reference; it sits at rung 3 (at the upper boundary, if taken as $20 billion). No separate market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Just above $25 billion for FY2027: approximately $20 billion of Vera standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue whose trailing-12-month total was already more than $5 billion. This is a narrow estimate above the rung 1 boundary, not a claim that the FY2027 total has already been reported.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3; the company\\u2019s clarification says that figure is for standalone CPU sales. The implied total is just above $25 billion: carry the roughly $20 billion Vera standalone outlook for FY2027 and add Grace CPU revenue, which management reported at more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. That narrowly crosses rung 1\\u2019s strictly-greater-than-$25-billion boundary. The FY2028 more-than-doubling outlook and the AWS deployment add support for future demand, but do not independently increase the FY2027 estimate; Wolfe\\u2019s older 1.3 million-chip forecast at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a counterweight, but is weaker than current management guidance. AMD\\u2019s competitive claims are also a risk, but do not quantify a FY2027 reduction sufficient to move the estimate below the boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [5971, \"2026-08-31\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 expectation of about $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue is the reference. At $20 billion, it sits at rung 3, which includes $20 billion.\", \"Use about $20 billion of FY2027 Vera standalone revenue and add the greater-than-$5-billion Grace trailing-12-month revenue run-rate reported in the evidence. This implies slightly more than $25 billion in total CPU revenue, subject to Grace sustaining that run-rate through FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 target of about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales, which is rung 3 (at the $20 billion boundary). That is not the whole CPU business: management clarified that the $20 billion is standalone, while Grace CPU revenue was already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that Grace run-rate alongside the Vera standalone outlook implies slightly more than $25 billion in total FY2027 CPU revenue, crossing rung 1\\u2019s strictly-greater-than-$25-billion boundary. Vera production and customer deployments support execution; the older shipment estimate and AMD competition are counterweights, but do not outweigh the current management outlook and reported Grace run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\"]\"], [6192, \"2026-09-01\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 outlook is about $20 billion in standalone CPU sales, placing the reference at rung 3 (the $15\\u2013$20 billion band).\", \"About $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion. The resulting estimate crosses the strictly-greater-than-$25-billion threshold for rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook of about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales, which sits at rung 3. That standalone figure is not the total CPU outcome: management clarified that the $20 billion is standalone, while Grace CPU revenue had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that Grace run-rate into FY2027 alongside the Vera standalone outlook implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion, crossing the rung 1 boundary. Vera shipments are underway and production is full, supporting execution; the older 1.3 million-unit shipment estimate is a counterweight, but the later production, demand, and customer-deployment evidence supports the current outlook.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\"]\"], [6407, \"2026-09-03\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 outlook of about $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue is the reference; $20 billion alone falls at rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue, plus Grace CPU revenue carried at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate of more than $5 billion, implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 $20 billion standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. That is not the total-CPU forecast: management clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU sales, while Grace CPU revenue was already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying Grace at that reported run-rate alongside the standalone outlook implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, crossing into rung 1. This assumes the Grace run-rate is maintained; the newer approximately $20 billion server-CPU demand statement and preliminary FY2028 growth outlook do not quantify a lower FY2027 revenue figure. The older 1.3 million-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 and is a counterweight, but later shipment, production, customer-demand, and standalone-revenue guidance support the higher outcome.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e897\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e3416\\\"]\"], [6593, \"2026-09-04\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference; the stated amount alone sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, assuming Grace sustains roughly that run-rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3 if read as the total for this question. Jensen Huang clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU sales; adding Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion, crossing the rung 1 boundary. Vera shipments are underway and the company says it is in full production, supporting execution, although Wolfe\\u2019s earlier shipment estimate implies a materially lower Vera run-rate. The approximately $20 billion server-CPU demand figure is not itself a revised FY2027 NVIDIA revenue forecast. No dependency conclusions are supplied for this thesis.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\"]\"], [6716, \"2026-09-05\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook is nearly $20 billion for standalone CPU revenue, placing the reference in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $25 billion-plus: carry the near-$20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook forward and add Grace CPU revenue, which was above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. This implies a modest crossing above the $25 billion rung 1 boundary, rather than treating the standalone outlook as total CPU revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits in rung 3. The question asks for total CPU revenue: adding Grace\\u2019s greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate to the near-$20 billion standalone outlook implies total FY2027 revenue modestly above $25 billion, crossing the rung 1 boundary. Wolfe\\u2019s older estimate of about 1.3 million Vera chips at roughly $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion of Vera shipments in calendar 2026 and tempers confidence, but it does not outweigh the company\\u2019s FY2027 outlook; the AWS CPU commitment is supportive but not sized.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e3087\\\"]\"], [6973, \"2026-09-07\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion falls in rung 3 (Neutral: at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"More than $25 billion in FY2027: NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion standalone-CPU outlook plus Grace CPU revenue already above a $5 billion trailing-12-month level. The standalone clarification means these amounts are additive for total CPU revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. The implied total is above $25 billion: the $20 billion standalone outlook is separate from Grace, whose reported trailing-12-month revenue already exceeded $5 billion. Vera production and customer shipments support execution; AMD's competitive claims and the older Vera shipment estimate temper confidence but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's outlook or Grace run-rate, so they do not move the forecast below the rung 1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e3087\\\"]\"], [7069, \"2026-09-08\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the reference; it sits at rung 3, whose range is $15 billion to $20 billion inclusive.\", \"More than $25 billion: add the approximately $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast to Grace CPU revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. The resulting total crosses rung 1's strict $25 billion threshold.\", \"The company-guidance reference is about $20 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 3 (at the top of the $15\\u2013$20 billion band). The implied outcome is above $25 billion: NVIDIA clarified that the $20 billion forecast is for standalone CPUs, and Grace CPU revenue had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Treating that Grace revenue as additive to the standalone forecast puts total CPU revenue above rung 1's $25 billion boundary. Recent production, customer-adoption, and FY2028 growth disclosures support execution, while AMD's competitive claims and the older Vera shipment estimate temper confidence but do not quantify a cut to the FY2027 forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e3416\\\"]\"], [7175, \"2026-09-09\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook is approximately $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue (rung 3); management\\u2019s clarification that the figure is standalone distinguishes it from Grace CPU revenue.\", \"Above $25 billion: use the $20 billion standalone FY2027 outlook and add the greater-than-$5 billion Grace CPU TTM revenue reported as of Q2 FY2027 as a run-rate proxy for FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3 (the upper boundary of the $15\\u2013$20 billion band). The implied total is above $25 billion: carry the reported greater-than-$5 billion Grace CPU trailing-12-month revenue at Q2 FY2027 forward as a run-rate proxy and add it to the $20 billion standalone outlook. Grace is separate from the standalone forecast, so this clears rung 1\\u2019s boundary. This is a forecast, not a claim that the full-year Grace figure is already established. Vera production, customer deployments, and the FY2028 growth outlook support execution; AMD\\u2019s performance and share claims are a competitive counterweight but do not quantify a reduction to FY2027 guidance.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [7402, \"2026-09-10\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook; rung 3 (Neutral), at the top of the $15\\u2013$20 billion band.\", \"Above $25 billion, approximately $25 billion-plus: about $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run rate. The $6.5 billion implied by Wolfe\\u2019s calendar-2026 Vera shipment estimate is not added separately because it falls within standalone CPU revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3, the top of the $15\\u2013$20 billion band. The implied total remains above $25 billion: add the standalone outlook to Grace\\u2019s greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue as a run-rate proxy. The Wolfe estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion of Vera revenue for calendar 2026, but it is a third-party shipment forecast for a period that overlaps FY2027 and is not incremental to the standalone-CPU outlook; it does not displace that guidance. The Grace figure is a forecast proxy, not proof that FY2027 Grace revenue will exceed $5 billion. New shipment and customer evidence supports execution, while AMD\\u2019s performance and share claims are counterweights that do not quantify a cut to FY2027 guidance. The implied figure still crosses rung 1\\u2019s greater-than-$25 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e3416\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [7560, \"2026-09-11\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s stated FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is about $20 billion, at the top of rung 3 ($15\\u2013$20 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace\\u2019s greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue roughly above $25 billion. The Grace run-rate is a proxy rather than a separately stated FY2027 forecast, so the implied crossing is marginal rather than assured.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook of about $20 billion, which sits at rung 3. The implied total is above that reference because the $20 billion Vera/standalone outlook is supplemented by Grace revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis; carrying that run-rate into FY2027 puts the total roughly above $25 billion, just across rung 1\\u2019s boundary. Vera deliveries, full production, and customer deployment plans support the outlook being achievable, while AMD\\u2019s performance and share claims are competitive counterweights. The implied total is close to the boundary, but the evidence in force makes exceeding $25 billion more likely than not.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e3416\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [7681, \"2026-09-12\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion sits at rung 3.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue carrying forward at more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion, at rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which is rung 3 (at the $20 billion boundary). Separately, NVIDIA reports Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis; carrying that run-rate into FY2027 alongside the standalone Vera outlook implies total CPU revenue just above $25 billion, crossing into rung 1. Vera shipments and full production support the outlook, though Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a meaningful counterweight. AMD's performance and market-share claims also weigh against the forecast, but do not outweigh the company outlook, the reported Grace run-rate, and evidence that Vera deliveries and deployments are underway.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e3416\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [7773, \"2026-09-13\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the market/company reference; $20 billion total CPU revenue is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion if Grace sustains roughly that run-rate. That places the forecast in rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook of approximately $20 billion, which sits at the top of rung 3 for total CPU revenue. The forecast for total CPU revenue remains above $25 billion: the $20 billion Vera/standalone outlook plus Grace revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies more than $25 billion, assuming Grace continues at roughly that run-rate. Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each imply about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a meaningful counterweight, but that is an earlier-period shipment estimate rather than a revision to FY2027 guidance. AMD's performance claims add competitive risk; Vera deliveries, AWS adoption and NVIDIA's FY2028 growth outlook support the forecast. The implied total crosses the $25 billion rung boundary, so the most likely outcome remains rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [7937, \"2026-09-14\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion is the top of rung 3.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 Vera revenue plus Grace revenue continuing from its reported trailing-12-month level above $5 billion implies total CPU revenue just above $25 billion. This assumes Grace revenue is sustained into the FY2027 total; the forecasted Vera amount is the stronger direct FY2027 input, while Wolfe's roughly $6.5 billion calendar-2026 shipment estimate is a material downside counterweight.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. I forecast total CPU revenue above $25 billion: Vera's FY2027 outlook plus Grace revenue continuing from its reported trailing-12-month level above $5 billion puts the total just over the rung-1 boundary. The main counterweight is Wolfe's much lower 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment estimate for calendar 2026, implying about $6.5 billion at its stated ASP, alongside AMD's competitive performance claims. But those do not replace NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue outlook, and the reported shipments, full production, and customer adoption support execution. The result clears $25 billion, though with less margin and confidence than the standalone outlook alone might suggest.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [8044, \"2026-09-15\", 1, \"NVIDIA's nearly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion is in rung 3 (Neutral: at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of Vera CPU revenue plus Grace revenue continuing at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion, in rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's nearly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. Adding Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion to the Vera outlook implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. Wolfe's 2026 shipment estimate implies only about $6.5 billion for Vera at its stated ASP, and AMD's performance comparisons are competitive counterweights, but neither replaces the company's FY2027 outlook; NVIDIA also reports Vera shipments and broadening customer adoption. The FY2028 more-than-double expectation supports demand and supply momentum, but is not itself a revision to FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\"]\"], [8217, \"2026-09-16\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook of about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales is the relevant market/company reference, and sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera revenue plus Grace revenue carried at its reported trailing-12-month pace of more than $5 billion implies total CPU revenue just over $25 billion. This assumes the Grace pace is broadly maintained; the resulting estimate only narrowly clears rung 1\\u2019s greater-than-$25-billion boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits in rung 3. For total CPU revenue, carrying Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion forward alongside the $20 billion Vera outlook implies just over $25 billion and therefore crosses into rung 1. This is a narrow boundary crossing, not a separately guided total: the approximately $20 billion estimate for server-CPU demand and AMD\\u2019s competitive claims are counterweights, while the company\\u2019s Vera production, customer shipments and AWS adoption support the outlook.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\"]\"], [8322, \"2026-09-17\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the reference, at the top of rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"Just over $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace\\u2019s reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate carried into FY2027. The Vera figure implies roughly 4 million chips at Wolfe\\u2019s approximately $5,000 ASP, so the shipment ramp remains a material execution risk.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at rung 3. I interpret the CEO\\u2019s clarification that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU as Vera revenue, separate from Grace: adding Grace\\u2019s reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate puts the implied total just over $25 billion. That crosses the rung-1 boundary. This is not risk-free: Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera shipments at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, far below the volume implied by $20 billion at that ASP, and NVIDIA also described total server CPU demand as approximately $20 billion. But the current-year revenue visibility, Vera shipments already underway and full production make the higher outcome more likely than the lower run-rate alone would suggest.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e3416\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e4796\\\", \\\"e4797\\\"]\"], [8504, \"2026-09-18\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, clarified as standalone CPU revenue, is the reference; $20 billion falls in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion, assuming the Grace run-rate broadly carries through FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, clarified as standalone CPU revenue; $20 billion sits at rung 3. The implied total is just above $25 billion: carry the approximately $20 billion Vera outlook and add Grace's greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate. That narrowly clears rung 1's strict greater-than-$25 billion boundary. Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera shipments at about $5,000 each implies only about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a material execution risk, but NVIDIA reports Vera shipments, full production, and broad planned deployments; on balance, the current-year outlook plus Grace run-rate makes a total above $25 billion most likely.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e4395\\\"]\"], [8630, \"2026-09-19\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, clarified as standalone CPU sales, is the reference; $20 billion falls at rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $25 billion or slightly more: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera CPU outlook forward and add Grace\\u2019s greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate reported during FY2027. That clears rung 1\\u2019s strict greater-than-$25 billion threshold, narrowly.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, clarified as standalone CPU sales; that reference is rung 3. The most likely total is just over $25 billion: the more specific FY2027 Vera statements put Vera CPU sales at about $20 billion, while Grace had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027. The CFO\\u2019s broader \\u201ctotal CPU revenue\\u201d wording makes the addition less certain, but the separate Vera and Grace figures support treating Grace as additional rather than replacing the Vera outlook. Wolfe\\u2019s 2026 shipment estimate implies only about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, and AMD\\u2019s performance claims add execution risk; neither provides a quantified revision to the FY2027 outlook or enough to move the central estimate below rung 1\\u2019s strict $25 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e3416\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e4796\\\", \\\"e4797\\\", \\\"e5047\\\", \\\"e5048\\\", \\\"e5049\\\", \\\"e5050\\\", \\\"e5051\\\", \\\"e5052\\\", \\\"e5053\\\", \\\"e5054\\\"]\"], [8735, \"2026-09-20\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion falls at rung 3.\", \"Approximately $25 billion or slightly more for FY2027: about $20 billion from Vera standalone CPU sales plus a roughly $5 billion Grace contribution, using Grace\\u2019s reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue as the recent run-rate proxy.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. The most likely total is just above $25 billion: the specific FY2027 Vera outlook is about $20 billion, and Grace had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027; carrying that recent Grace run-rate through the fiscal year puts the total slightly above the rung-1 boundary. Vera shipments and full production support the outlook, though the estimate is close to the boundary: Wolfe\\u2019s 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and AMD\\u2019s performance claims are competitive risks without a quantified revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook. The approximately $20 billion server-CPU demand figure is a headwind, but it is not an explicit cap on NVIDIA\\u2019s specific Vera sales outlook.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4797\\\", \\\"e5047\\\", \\\"e5048\\\", \\\"e5049\\\", \\\"e5050\\\", \\\"e5051\\\", \\\"e5052\\\", \\\"e5053\\\", \\\"e5054\\\"]\"]], [\"How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 25000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 20000000000.0, 25000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 15000000000.0, 20000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 10000000000.0, 15000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 10000000000.0]]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\narm=\\\"old-1\\\"\np=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nfor vn in [4,15]:\n v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n rows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n import json\n print(json.dumps([arm,v,rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"[\"old-1\", \"v4\", [[70, \"2026-07-02\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts estimate FY2027 revenue of $392 billion; that is rung 2.\", \"About $392 billion in FY2027, using the stated analyst estimate as the market reference. The $1 trillion orders expectation is for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell through 2027, not a quantified FY2027 revenue figure, so it cannot be translated into a higher annual outcome from the supplied evidence.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's $392 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits exactly at rung 2's lower boundary. e27 is bullish demand evidence, but $1 trillion in expected orders for two chip families through 2027 is not FY2027 revenue: it spans products and time, and the evidence does not quantify shipments or revenue attributable to FY2027. It therefore does not support the $431.2 billion needed to cross into rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e27\\\", \\\"e28\\\"]\"], [101, \"2026-07-03\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion in FY2027; this is rung 2, at its $392 billion lower boundary.\", \"About $392 billion in FY2027, using the stated Wall Street estimate as the forecast anchor. The $1 trillion product-family sales expectation spans through the end of 2027 and is not allocated to FY2027, while the CPU and GPU deployment figures lack a usable FY2027 revenue contribution.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's $392 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits at rung 2's lower boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales expectation spans products and time through the end of 2027, so it does not establish NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue or support the $431.2 billion needed for rung 1; the new CPU sales estimate has no stated period, while the deployment figures have no revenue or FY2027 allocation. These inputs do not justify moving the forecast from the reference rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e27\\\", \\\"e28\\\", \\\"e35\\\", \\\"e36\\\", \\\"e37\\\", \\\"e38\\\", \\\"e39\\\", \\\"e40\\\", \\\"e44\\\", \\\"e45\\\"]\"], [124, \"2026-07-04\", 2, \"Wall Street's $392bn FY2027 revenue estimate; this is rung 2.\", \"Approximately $392bn, using the stated FY2027 analyst estimate as the base case. The demand and capex signals add upside skew, but the supplied evidence does not quantify an increase to $431.2bn or above.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's $392bn FY2027 estimate, at the bottom of rung 2. The evidence points to strong demand, but does not quantify an FY2027 revenue uplift large enough to reach rung 1's $431.2bn threshold\\u2014a $39.2bn, or 10%, increase over the reference. The $1tn Blackwell/Vera Rubin expectation spans products and time through 2027 and is not an FY2027 NVIDIA revenue figure; deployment plans and the CPU-sales estimate likewise lack a relevant FY2027 revenue allocation. The demand and capex outlooks support upside, while competition and pricing concerns are counterweights, but neither side supplies a quantified revision that changes the roughly $392bn base case.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e27\\\", \\\"e28\\\", \\\"e35\\\", \\\"e36\\\", \\\"e37\\\", \\\"e38\\\", \\\"e39\\\", \\\"e40\\\", \\\"e44\\\", \\\"e45\\\", \\\"e48\\\", \\\"e51\\\"]\"], [150, \"2026-07-05\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion in FY2027 revenue (e28); the $392 billion reference is rung 2, at its lower boundary.\", \"The evidence implies $392 billion plus an upward contribution from the second-half data-center compute beat, but does not provide the dollar base or revenue mix needed to calculate that contribution. The 20% applies to one segment for half the fiscal year, so the evidence supports an outcome above the reference but does not make the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary the most likely result.\", \"The market reference is $392 billion, the lower boundary of rung 2. The new SemiAnalysis estimate is a meaningful upside input, but its 20% comparison applies only to second-half data-center compute revenue, not to all FY2027 revenue. Even treating the second half as roughly half of the year and compute as the whole company would imply about a 10% uplift\\u2014the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold; compute is only part of revenue, and the evidence supplies no dollar denominator or mix showing the total-company uplift clears that threshold. The $1 trillion chip-sales expectation spans products and time through 2027, while the $20 billion FY2027 CPU-sales forecast is for a product not yet shipping and is discounted. Those support upside, but do not establish a most-likely total at or above $431.2 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e27\\\", \\\"e28\\\", \\\"e35\\\", \\\"e36\\\", \\\"e37\\\", \\\"e38\\\", \\\"e39\\\", \\\"e40\\\", \\\"e44\\\", \\\"e45\\\", \\\"e48\\\", \\\"e51\\\", \\\"e61\\\", \\\"e62\\\", \\\"e63\\\", \\\"e64\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e67\\\", \\\"e68\\\", \\\"e69\\\"]\"], [286, \"2026-07-06\", 2, \"Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is $392 billion (e28), also described as 82% growth (e51). That is rung 2, at its $392 billion lower boundary.\", \"Around $420 billion, in the low $400 billions: start from the $392 billion consensus reference, then allow a single-digit-percent gross uplift because SemiAnalysis's 20% upside applies only to roughly half-year second-half data-center compute revenue, a subset of total revenue. The delay/cancellation and China evidence temper that uplift but do not quantify an offset that moves the estimate below the reference.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's $392 billion FY2027 estimate, the lower boundary of rung 2. Q1 reported revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of about $91 billion, with 95% year-over-year Q2 growth, support carrying strong growth into the forecast period. SemiAnalysis's 20% upside applies only to second-half data-center compute, not all FY2027 revenue: treating H2 as roughly half the year and compute as a subset implies a gross total-revenue uplift below roughly 10%, or less than the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold. The resulting central estimate is in the low $400 billions, around $420 billion. Product-transition delay/cancellation reports and the China substitution evidence temper that upside, but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to put the forecast below $392 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e51\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e130\\\", \\\"e126\\\", \\\"e133\\\"]\"], [354, \"2026-07-07\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion in FY2027 revenue (e28), also expressed as 82% FY2027 growth (e51). The $392 billion reference is the lower boundary of rung 2.\", \"About $425 billion for FY2027: retain the prior roughly $420 billion central outlook supported by reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance, then allow a limited uplift for the $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast because the planned fall launch leaves few selling quarters and shipments are not yet shown. The resulting estimate remains below $431.2 billion.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s $392 billion FY2027 estimate, at the lower boundary of rung 2. The implied outcome is about $425 billion: the previously supported roughly $420 billion outlook remains the base, while the $20 billion stand-alone CPU FY2027 forecast adds some upside but is heavily discounted because the planned fall launch leaves few selling quarters and no volume in hand. The reported Kyber delay and Rubin Ultra cancellation temper that upside, but neither quantifies a revenue reduction sufficient to move the estimate below $392 billion. At roughly $425 billion, the forecast remains below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion threshold.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e51\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e64\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e74\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e171\\\"]\"], [439, \"2026-07-08\", 2, \"Analyst consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e28, e234), within rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.\", \"About $430 billion. Starting from the roughly $393 billion consensus, the reported $81.6 billion Q1 revenue and $91 billion Q2 guidance support a strong run-rate; applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% upside only to H2 data-center compute, rather than all revenue, implies an uplift that brings the total to roughly $430 billion.\", \"The market reference is analyst consensus of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 2. The evidence lifts the outlook above that reference: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, while SemiAnalysis tracks H2 data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus. That 20% applies only to H2 compute, not to total revenue; allowing for that segment\\u2019s share implies about $430 billion for FY2027. This is near, but below, rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary, so the forecast remains rung 2. The $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is upside but is discounted because the fall launch leaves limited selling time and no volume in hand. Reported Kyber delay and Rubin Ultra cancellation are downside risks, but no quantified FY2027 revenue reduction is supplied.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e64\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e243\\\", \\\"e245\\\", \\\"e254\\\"]\"], [514, \"2026-07-09\", 2, \"Analyst consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234), placing the reference in rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.\", \"About $430 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion reference and apply SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% uplift only to the estimated H2 FY2027 data-center compute portion, adding roughly $37 billion rather than applying the uplift to total revenue. The resulting estimate is below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion (e234), which is rung 2. The latest reported quarter and next-quarter guidance show strong growth continuing (e111, e127, e269). Applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% premium only to the estimated H2 FY2027 data-center compute portion\\u2014not to all revenue\\u2014implies roughly $37 billion of upside to the $393 billion reference, or about $430 billion overall. That is bullish versus consensus but remains just below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. The $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast and roughly $1 trillion of Blackwell/Vera Rubin revenue visibility are upside inputs, but the CPU product has no shipments in evidence and the visibility spans multiple products and calendar years, so neither is taken in full for FY2027 (e65, e272). Rubin-related delays/cancellations and newer memory-cost, compute-pricing and custom-chip concerns temper the forecast, but do not quantify an FY2027 revenue reduction sufficient to move it below the rung-2 range (e141, e144, e257, e258, e259, e261).\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e234\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e269\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e272\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e257\\\", \\\"e258\\\", \\\"e259\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\"]\"], [572, \"2026-07-10\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234, e299), placing the reference in rung 2.\", \"About $430 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion consensus and add about $37 billion implied by SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% premium on H2 FY2027 data-center compute only, not on total revenue. The reported Rubin changes and other new risks are not sized into an FY2027 revenue reduction, so the evidence does not support a precise further adjustment.\", \"The reference is Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion support a strong growth trajectory; applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% premium only to H2 FY2027 data-center compute, as in the prior estimate, implies roughly $37 billion above the $393 billion reference, or about $430 billion. That is below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. The new China-substitution, memory-cost and older-GPU rental-price evidence raises downside risk, but does not quantify a reduction to FY2027 revenue; the reported Rubin changes also include a plan to sell more existing-form-factor racks. The implied outcome therefore remains near $430 billion, not enough to cross the boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e74\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e299\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e257\\\", \\\"e258\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e245\\\", \\\"e291\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e272\\\"]\"], [646, \"2026-07-12\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $430 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion consensus and add about $37 billion by applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% premium only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute, not to total revenue.\", \"The reference is Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). Applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% premium only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute, as in the prior estimate, implies roughly $37 billion above that reference, or about $430 billion. Strong Q1 results and Q2 guidance support the growth trajectory; the China substitution, memory-cost and competitive evidence adds downside risk, but does not quantify a revenue reduction sufficient to move the estimate below the rung boundary. The reported Rubin changes are also partly offset by the plan to sell more existing-form-factor racks. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility spans products and calendar periods, so it is not taken as FY2027 revenue in full. At about $430 billion, the implied outcome remains below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e234\\\", \\\"e247\\\", \\\"e373\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e272\\\", \\\"e310\\\", \\\"e245\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e257\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e301\\\"]\"], [720, \"2026-07-13\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234), within rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $430 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion FY2027 consensus and apply SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% premium only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute, which implies about $37 billion of incremental revenue rather than a 20% increase to total revenue.\", \"The reference is Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate is specifically 20% above consensus for second-half FY2027 data-center compute, not for NVIDIA\\u2019s entire revenue; applying it only to that portion implies roughly $37 billion of upside, or about $430 billion in total. Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance support the growth run-rate, while the reported China substitution and product-delay risks are not quantified enough to offset that uplift. The estimate remains just below the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e245\\\", \\\"e242\\\", \\\"e291\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e272\\\"]\"], [853, \"2026-07-14\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"Approximately $430 billion: apply SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% uplift only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute, implying roughly $37 billion above the approximately $393 billion consensus total. This remains below the $431.2 billion rung 1 threshold.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, which sits in rung 2. The evidence continues to imply an outcome near $430 billion: SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate is 20% above consensus for second-half data-center compute, not for all revenue, and the prior calculation attributes roughly $37 billion of upside to that portion. That leaves the forecast just below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. Q1 actual revenue and Q2 guidance support a strong run-rate, while the newly specified product delays and cancellations, China substitution, and limited licensed H200 shipments do not establish a quantified revenue reduction sufficient to move the estimate across a boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and calendar years and is not FY2027 total revenue, so it is discounted rather than applied directly.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e269\\\", \\\"e431\\\", \\\"e310\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e491\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e503\\\", \\\"e257\\\", \\\"e259\\\"]\"], [978, \"2026-07-15\", 1, \"Bloomberg-tracked analyst estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027 (e234) are the market reference, in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $435 billion: starting from the prior estimate of roughly $430 billion after applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% second-half data-center compute upside to consensus (e154), I allow a net few billion dollars for a discounted share of the $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast (e65), given the planned fall launch and limited selling quarters (e64).\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion, in rung 2. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus was previously calculated to add roughly $37 billion, implying about $430 billion\\u2014just below rung 1. The new $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast is for a product planned to launch in fall, so I discount it substantially for the short selling window and execution risk; allowing for partial contribution and possible consensus inclusion, a net few billion dollars takes the implied result to roughly $435 billion, above the $431.2 billion boundary. The reported rack delays and Rubin Ultra cancellation temper that forecast, while the expected B300 substitution mitigates some of the risk. China-related substitution is a headwind, but the Q2 guidance already assumes no China data-center compute revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e234\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e64\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e527\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e171\\\"]\"], [1142, \"2026-07-16\", 1, \"Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue estimates of about $393 billion; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"Roughly $435 billion: about $393 billion consensus plus the previously derived roughly $37 billion SemiAnalysis second-half data-center compute uplift and a discounted few billion net contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target. The newly reported projects and supply outlook do not support a further quantified adjustment, while rack delays temper the estimate.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 2. The existing estimate remains roughly $435 billion: the previously assessed SemiAnalysis estimate of second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus was calculated to add about $37 billion to the reference, and the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target is discounted for its short selling window and execution risk, contributing only a few billion net. That puts the forecast about $42 billion above the reference and roughly $4 billion above the rung 1 boundary. New evidence adds support from sovereign-AI revenue and TSMC\\u2019s strong growth outlook, but does not quantify a further FY2027 uplift; the Noetra project is not dated, and reported rack delays remain a risk. The expected B300 substitution mitigates some of that risk. The forecast therefore remains just inside rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e299\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e619\\\", \\\"e695\\\", \\\"e623\\\", \\\"e624\\\", \\\"e625\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e527\\\"]\"], [1208, \"2026-07-17\", 1, \"Bloomberg-tracked analyst expectations of roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027; rung 2 (Bullish).\", \"About $435 billion: retain the prior roughly $393 billion consensus base, add about $37 billion implied by SemiAnalysis's estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue is 20% above Wall Street's expectation, and add only a few billion net for the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target after discounting it for execution and the short selling window. This is above the $431.2 billion rung 1 threshold.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 2. The implied forecast remains about $435 billion: the prior assessment's roughly $37 billion uplift from SemiAnalysis's estimate for second-half FY2027 data-center compute, plus a few billion net from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target after discounting for execution and the short selling window. That is about $4 billion above rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. The latest items do not establish a quantified change to that estimate: the reported rack delays and cancellations add execution risk, while the B300 substitution, TSMC outlook, sovereign-AI sales and newly disclosed Noetra deployment provide offsets or support but no sufficiently timed FY2027 revenue uplift. Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance support a strong near-term run-rate, but do not by themselves justify replacing the existing FY2027 estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e299\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e405\\\", \\\"e269\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e527\\\", \\\"e619\\\", \\\"e623\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e693\\\", \\\"e695\\\", \\\"e491\\\", \\\"e493\\\"]\"], [1236, \"2026-07-18\", 2, \"Bloomberg-tracked analyst estimates put FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (with other estimates around $392\\u2013$393 billion). That reference is rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.\", \"About $425 billion. Starting from the previously supported bridge of roughly $393 billion consensus plus approximately $37 billion of SemiAnalysis-tracked second-half data-center compute upside and a few billion net from discounted CPU sales, I apply an approximately $10 billion risk haircut for the specific rack delays and cancellations, partly offset by expected substitution toward B300 and more Oberon-Rubin racks.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion, in rung 2; other analyst estimates are also around $392\\u2013$393 billion. The evidence-supported forecast is about $425 billion: the prior bridge from that reference was roughly $37 billion of SemiAnalysis-tracked second-half data-center compute upside, plus a few billion net from the stand-alone CPU target after discounting it for execution and the short selling window. I now haircut that roughly $435 billion estimate by about $10 billion for specific rack delays and cancellations that threaten some of the second-half upside. More Oberon-Rubin racks and the expected B300 substitution offset part, but not all, of that risk. The resulting estimate is below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary and remains above rung 2\\u2019s $392 billion floor. The $20 billion CPU target is not taken in full without evidence of shipments; the Noetra deployment likewise has no stated delivery timeline and is not counted as a full FY2027 contribution.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e64\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e527\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e619\\\", \\\"e623\\\", \\\"e624\\\", \\\"e625\\\", \\\"e730\\\"]\"], [1250, \"2026-07-19\", 2, \"Market expectations are about $393 billion for FY2027 (Bloomberg-tracked estimates), which is rung 2; the $392.7 billion analyst estimate is also rung 2.\", \"About $425 billion, retaining the prior estimate\\u2019s bridge from the roughly $393 billion reference: second-half data-center compute upside, a discounted stand-alone CPU contribution, and a haircut for rack delays/cancellations. Q1 FY2027 revenue was $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion; the newly surfaced deployment plans are not assigned their full stated value because FY2027 shipment timing is not established.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion (e234, rung 2); analyst estimates of $392.7 billion are in the same rung (e218). The forecast remains around $425 billion: the prior conclusion\\u2019s carried bridge was roughly $37 billion of second-half data-center compute upside, a few billion net from the discounted stand-alone CPU target, and about $10 billion of haircut for rack delays and cancellations. Q1 actual revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion support a strong growth trajectory, but are not grounds to annualize Q1 flat or add the same second-half tracking upside again. New deployment plans add potential demand, but their timing and shipments do not establish enough incremental FY2027 revenue to revise the central estimate. At about $425 billion, the forecast remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary, so it does not cross a rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e405\\\", \\\"e269\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e503\\\", \\\"e623\\\", \\\"e625\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e491\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e527\\\", \\\"e585\\\", \\\"e693\\\", \\\"e695\\\", \\\"e730\\\"]\"], [1334, \"2026-07-20\", 2, \"Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 2; the analyst estimate of $392.7 billion is in the same rung.\", \"About $425 billion, carrying forward the prior estimate: reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance, together with SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above Wall Street, imply upside to the $393 billion reference. The Noetra GPU plan is discounted because no deployment timing is given, and the rack delays/cancellation remain an offset; $425 billion is below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg-tracked FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion (e234), which is rung 2; the $392.7 billion analyst estimate is also rung 2 (e218). The evidence still implies about $425 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion support continued strong growth, while SemiAnalysis\\u2019s quantified estimate of 20% above Wall Street for second-half FY2027 data-center compute supports upside beyond the reference (e405, e269, e154). The reported rack delays and cancellation remain a meaningful offset, but expected B300 substitution and the new Noetra project do not quantify enough FY2027 shipments to establish a different total-revenue estimate (e141, e144, e527, e623, e625, e626). About $425 billion is above $392 billion but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary, so the forecast remains rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e405\\\", \\\"e269\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e527\\\", \\\"e623\\\", \\\"e625\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e585\\\", \\\"e693\\\", \\\"e695\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e730\\\"]\"], [1462, \"2026-07-21\", 2, \"Wall Street's FY2027 total-revenue estimate is about $393 billion, placing the reference at rung 2; the comparable estimates of $392 billion and $392.7 billion are also rung 2.\", \"About $425 billion for FY2027: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, combined with SemiAnalysis's quantified 20% upside estimate for second-half data-center compute versus Wall Street, support a total above the roughly $393 billion reference. The new Vera Rubin production and deployment evidence improves execution confidence but provides no quantified FY2027 revenue amount to revise that estimate further.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2 (e234, e811; the similar $392\\u2013$392.7 billion estimates are also rung 2, e28, e218). The evidence still implies about $425 billion: the $81.6 billion Q1 result and $91 billion Q2 guidance, together with SemiAnalysis's estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above Wall Street's expectations, support revenue above consensus; the prior estimate translates that uplift to about $425 billion total. Recent evidence that Vera Rubin is in production and already running at customers strengthens execution confidence, but does not quantify additional FY2027 sales. The rack delays and cancellations remain an offset. About $425 billion is above $392 billion but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary, so the stronger execution evidence does not establish a move across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e269\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e527\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e623\\\", \\\"e625\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e840\\\", \\\"e841\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e852\\\", \\\"e853\\\", \\\"e854\\\", \\\"e863\\\", \\\"e864\\\", \\\"e865\\\", \\\"e866\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e491\\\", \\\"e493\\\"]\"], [1569, \"2026-07-22\", 2, \"Wall Street's roughly $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $425 billion: carry forward the prior estimate's translation of SemiAnalysis's expectation for second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above Wall Street, alongside the $81.6 billion Q1 result and $91 billion Q2 guidance. The newer production and customer evidence strengthens execution confidence but is not sized enough to revise that total.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The evidence still implies about $425 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion; carrying the existing SemiAnalysis estimate of second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above Wall Street's expectations through the prior calculation puts total revenue near $425 billion. That is above the $392 billion rung-2 floor but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Vera Rubin is now in production ramp and operating at named customer sites, improving execution confidence, but the evidence does not quantify additional FY2027 revenue or establish the volume needed to count the $20 billion standalone-CPU target in full. Reported rack delays/cancellations and the China headwind remain offsets, but do not move the implied total below rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e234\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e863\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e527\\\"]\"], [1691, \"2026-07-23\", 2, \"Wall Street's approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e811, e234) is in rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.\", \"About $425 billion. This carries forward the prior run-rate calculation from Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, applying SemiAnalysis's 20% above-consensus estimate only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue, not to total revenue.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 2. The evidence-implied estimate remains about $425 billion: carry the Q1 actual and Q2 guidance run-rate forward, with the existing SemiAnalysis estimate lifting second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above Wall Street expectations applied only to that portion of total revenue. The implied figure is about $6.2 billion below rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. Rubin production and customer deployments now provide stronger execution evidence, but they give no FY2027 revenue volume to add; likewise, the $20 billion standalone-CPU target is not counted in full without disclosed volume. Reported rack delays/cancellations and the China headwind remain counterweights. Thus the forecast stays in rung 2 rather than crossing a boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e811\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e903\\\", \\\"e904\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e64\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e269\\\"]\"], [1782, \"2026-07-24\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $425 billion: carry the Q1 actual and Q2 guidance run-rate forward, then apply the existing estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above Wall Street expectations only to that portion of revenue. This remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s approximately $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. Carrying forward the evidence-implied estimate of about $425 billion\\u2014based on Q1 actual revenue and Q2 guidance carried forward, with the existing SemiAnalysis uplift applied only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute\\u2014leaves the forecast about $6.2 billion short of rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. Rubin\\u2019s production and customer use, and the scheduled Q3 shipments/Q4 ramp, strengthen execution confidence but do not disclose FY2027 revenue volume. The $20 billion standalone-CPU target is therefore discounted rather than added in full. Delays/cancellations, the absent China compute contribution in guidance, and competing AMD deployments also temper the upside. The new supply and customer announcements do not quantify enough FY2027 NVIDIA revenue to move the forecast across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e997\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1016\\\", \\\"e1025\\\"]\"], [1853, \"2026-07-25\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s average FY2027 revenue estimate is approximately $393 billion, which is rung 2.\", \"About $425 billion, based on the Q1 actual and Q2 revenue guidance carried forward with the existing SemiAnalysis 20% uplift applied only to second-half FY2027 data-center compute. This is above the $392 billion rung-2 floor but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s approximately $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The evidence-implied forecast remains about $425 billion: Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance, carried forward with the existing SemiAnalysis uplift to second-half FY2027 data-center compute, support a figure above consensus but still about $6.2 billion below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. Rubin\\u2019s production and customer deployments and the separate $20 billion CPU sales forecast support upside, but do not establish enough FY2027 revenue volume to cross that boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell-and-Rubin forecast spans calendar 2025\\u20132027 and is a product-revenue subtotal, not FY2027 total revenue; the SK initiative is multi-year and does not state NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 revenue share. Delays and cancellations, China compute excluded from Q2 guidance, and AMD\\u2019s competing deployments remain offsets.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e811\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1017\\\", \\\"e1065\\\", \\\"e1101\\\", \\\"e997\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\"]\"], [1924, \"2026-07-26\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $425 billion. Carrying forward the revenue run-rate indicated by Q1\\u2019s $81.62 billion and Q2 guidance of approximately $91 billion, with the existing SemiAnalysis estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute will be 20% above Wall Street expectations, supports roughly $425 billion; that remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The evidence-based forecast remains about $425 billion: Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance, carried forward with the existing estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute will exceed Wall Street expectations, imply a result above consensus but below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. Recent confirmation that Vera Rubin is in production and running at customer sites strengthens the prospect of the Q3/Q4 ramp, but neither the shipment evidence nor the new AI-factory commitments quantify enough FY2027 revenue to add the roughly $6.2 billion needed to cross that boundary. The more-than-$500 billion SK initiative spans multiple years and includes reciprocal purchases, so it is not NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; AMD\\u2019s GPU commitments are competitive context, not evidence of a specified NVIDIA revenue loss.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e234\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1017\\\", \\\"e1101\\\", \\\"e1016\\\", \\\"e1110\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e491\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e730\\\"]\"], [2162, \"2026-07-27\", 2, \"Wall Street estimates about $393 billion for FY2027 (e28, e234), which is rung 2.\", \"About $430 billion: retain the prior roughly $425 billion run-rate implied by Q1 revenue, Q2 guidance and the forecast of second-half data-center compute 20% above Wall Street expectations, then lift it modestly for confirmed Vera Rubin production/customer deployments and stronger infrastructure spending signals. The broad multi-year deal and spending totals are not counted in full as FY2027 NVIDIA revenue.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The evidence still implies revenue above that reference, but not enough to cross rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary: the earlier roughly $425 billion run-rate remains supported by Q1 revenue, Q2 guidance and SemiAnalysis's estimate for second-half data-center compute. I raise the forecast modestly to about $430 billion because Vera Rubin is now reported in production and operating at customer sites, while the updated AI-capex outlook and newly announced infrastructure plans add demand support. I do not count the $500-billion-plus SK headline or OpenAI's spending through 2030 as FY2027 NVIDIA revenue: their amounts span multiple years and include reciprocal or otherwise unspecified spending, and Bernstein cautions that the SK figure may overstate near-term NVIDIA revenue. Nor do I take unshipped product targets or preliminary financing reports at face value. Competitive AMD commitments and reported rack delays remain offsets. The forecast therefore stays below $431.2 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e64\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1017\\\", \\\"e1101\\\", \\\"e1110\\\", \\\"e1157\\\", \\\"e1236\\\", \\\"e1167\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1219\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e493\\\"]\"], [2313, \"2026-07-28\", 2, \"Wall Street estimates of approximately $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027 (e28, e234), placing the reference in rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.\", \"Approximately $427 billion. The roughly $393 billion reference is lifted by strong reported Q1 revenue, $91 billion Q2 guidance, and SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% above-consensus estimate for second-half data-center compute (applied only to that portion, not total revenue); I temper the roughly $430 billion prior estimate slightly for competitive risk and discount the unshipped CPU sales target.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. Carrying forward the roughly $430 billion estimate implied by the strong Q1 result, $91 billion Q2 guidance and SemiAnalysis\\u2019s above-consensus second-half data-center compute view, I modestly temper the forecast for newly explicit AMD commitments and a reported workload migration. The $500 billion SK headline is not FY2027 NVIDIA revenue: it spans an unspecified period and includes reciprocal purchases. The resulting estimate remains below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary; the CPU sales target is discounted because the product was not yet shipping and the disclosed launch leaves limited FY2027 selling time.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e64\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1219\\\", \\\"e1016\\\", \\\"e1110\\\", \\\"e997\\\"]\"], [2428, \"2026-07-29\", 1, \"Wall Street expects approximately $393 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 revenue, which is rung 2 (Bullish).\", \"About $435 billion: the $393 billion reference, with $172.6 billion represented by Q1 actual revenue and Q2 guidance, leaves $220.4 billion for H2; the quantified H2 data-center compute upside and Rubin's production ramp support an outcome above the reference and above rung 1's $431.2 billion floor.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion leaves $220.4 billion for H2 at the reference; the quantified view that H2 data-center compute could exceed expectations, now supported by Vera Rubin entering production and ramping at customers, lifts the implied total to about $435 billion. That clears rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary, though the $500 billion-plus SK initiative is not FY2027 NVIDIA revenue and AMD commitments and China risks temper the upside.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1219\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e419\\\"]\"], [2539, \"2026-07-30\", 1, \"Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is about $393 billion (e234), placing the reference in rung 2, Bullish.\", \"About $440 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion consensus, add an uplift from SemiAnalysis's 20% H2 data-center compute view only to NVIDIA's relevant H2 compute revenue, and allow only a modest contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target because the product is not yet shipping and the target is not volume in hand.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion, which sits in rung 2. The $91 billion Q2 guide and the 85% Q1 growth are consistent with that strong baseline, while the quantified forecast for H2 data-center compute to exceed Wall Street expectations by 20% supports an uplift when applied only to that component, not to all revenue. Vera Rubin is now reported in full production and shipping/ramping at customers, strengthening the case for the compute uplift. I estimate about $440 billion, including only a modest contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target because shipments are only planned for fall and the target is not volume in hand. That is above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary, though AMD competition and China-related risks keep the estimate close enough to the boundary that the move is not overwhelming.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e234\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e64\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\"]\"], [2631, \"2026-07-31\", 1, \"Wall Street estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027 place the reference in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $435\\u2013440 billion: carry the strong reported Q1 and guided Q2 growth through the remaining comparable quarters, then apply SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% H2 upside only to the data-center compute component, not total revenue. Add only a modest, discounted contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target; Rubin\\u2019s multi-year revenue/order figures are not counted in full as FY2027 revenue.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The reported Q1 result and $91 billion Q2 guide support a strong baseline, while the quantified forecast for H2 data-center compute to exceed Wall Street expectations by 20% implies an uplift when applied only to that component. Rubin is now reported in production and running at customers, which strengthens the execution case; I estimate about $435\\u2013440 billion overall, including only a modest contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target because the target is not volume in hand. That estimate is above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. China exposure and AMD competition temper the upside, but the evidence in force does not make them sufficient to pull the forecast below that boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e64\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e491\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e1521\\\"]\"], [2674, \"2026-08-01\", 1, \"Wall Street's roughly $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e234, e811) is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $438 billion. Starting from the roughly $393 billion consensus, the prior forecast's quantified 20% second-half data-center compute upside to Wall Street expectations, applied only to that component, supported an estimate of $435\\u2013440 billion; the Q1 result, Q2 guide, and production evidence support retaining a midpoint near $438 billion, with no full-year credit for the multi-year Blackwell/Rubin target.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The forecast remains above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary: the prior estimate was $435\\u2013440 billion, based in part on the quantified expectation that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue would be 20% above Wall Street's expectations. Q1's $81.62 billion result and the $91 billion Q2 guide remain a strong near-term baseline; Rubin's reported full production and customer deployments support execution, while the cumulative Blackwell/Rubin sales outlook is supportive but spans multiple calendar years and is not counted as FY2027 revenue in full. China shipment restrictions, reported platform delays, and AMD deployments temper the upside but do not, on the supplied evidence, pull the forecast below the boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1510\\\"]\"], [2695, \"2026-08-02\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s approximately $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, at rung 2.\", \"Approximately $445\\u2013455 billion, centered near $450 billion. The $81.62 billion Q1 actual, $91 billion Q2 guide, and the estimated second-half data-center compute upside support revenue above the consensus reference; the unshipped CPU target is discounted, and the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure is not treated as FY2027 revenue in full.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The evidence still implies a result above the $431.2 billion boundary: Q1 revenue was $81.62 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, while SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate of second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above Wall Street expectations supports upside to the reference. I estimate about $445\\u2013455 billion, centered near $450 billion. Vera Rubin is in production and deployed at customers, supporting execution; the $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast is discounted because shipments are not yet established, and the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans calendar 2025\\u20132027 rather than being FY2027 revenue. China restrictions and AMD deployments temper the forecast but do not offset the indicated upside enough to put it below rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e1528\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1464\\\", \\\"e1558\\\"]\"], [2727, \"2026-08-03\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $445\\u2013455 billion, centered near $450 billion. Starting from the consensus reference, strong reported growth and the $91 billion Q2 guide support the run-rate; SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% second-half data-center-compute upside and the Rubin ramp add upside, with only a discounted contribution from the $20 billion standalone-CPU target and the multi-year $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The implied outcome remains about $445\\u2013455 billion, centered near $450 billion: start from that consensus, carry forward the strong reported growth and $91 billion Q2 guide, and incorporate SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above expectations. That 20% applies to second-half compute, not total annual revenue. The confirmed Vera Rubin deployments and expected Q3/Q4 architecture ramp support execution; the $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast and the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure are discounted because the former is a new-product target and the latter spans multiple products and calendar years. This estimate clears the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. China restrictions, reported rack delays, and competing AMD deployments temper the upside but do not pull the forecast below that boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e1475\\\"]\"], [2756, \"2026-08-04\", 1, \"Wall Street estimates of about $392\\u2013393 billion in FY2027, in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $445\\u2013455 billion, centered near $450 billion. Carry forward the strong reported Q1 growth and Q2 revenue guidance, then incorporate SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% upside estimate only for second-half data-center compute; do not apply it to all revenue.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The forecast remains about $445\\u2013455 billion, centered near $450 billion: that estimate carries forward the strong reported and guided growth and applies SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% upside only to second-half data-center compute, not to total revenue. Q1 growth of 85% and Q2 guidance implying nearly 95% year-over-year growth support that trajectory; the latest confirmation that Vera Rubin NVL72 entered full production in June and is deployed across cloud providers strengthens execution. I discount the $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast because it is a new product, and the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure because it spans multiple years and products. China sales restrictions and AMD deployments are offsets, but do not reduce the implied estimate below $431.2 billion. The implied outcome therefore remains in rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e1475\\\", \\\"e1464\\\", \\\"e1586\\\"]\"], [2807, \"2026-08-05\", 1, \"Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; this is rung 2.\", \"About $445\\u2013455 billion, centered near $450 billion. This carries forward the strong FY2027 growth trajectory supported by $81.62 billion of Q1 revenue, $91 billion Q2 guidance, the supplied Q2/Q3 growth estimates, and the Rubin ramp; the multi-year $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure and unshipped CPU target are not counted in full.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The forecast remains above rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary: Q1 revenue was $81.62 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, and the supplied analyst growth estimates are 96% for Q2 and 81% for Q3. Vera Rubin's reported production and shipping ramp, together with the reported Blackwell/Rubin order book, support carrying strong growth into the remaining quarters. I do not treat the $1 trillion multi-year figure as FY2027 revenue, and I discount the $20 billion standalone-CPU target because shipments and realized volume are not established. China restrictions, reported rack delays, and AMD deployments are offsets, but the evidence shown does not imply a reduction sufficient to bring the forecast below $431.2 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1157\\\", \\\"e1219\\\", \\\"e1464\\\", \\\"e1475\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1521\\\", \\\"e1569\\\", \\\"e1571\\\", \\\"e1586\\\"]\"], [2870, \"2026-08-06\", 1, \"Wall Street estimates of about $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2 (Bullish).\", \"About $440 billion for FY2027. Starting from the roughly $393 billion consensus, I apply SemiAnalysis\\u2019 20% upside estimate to second-half data-center compute revenue only, then include modest, discounted upside from standalone CPU sales and specified deployments; this implies a several-tens-of-billions uplift, partly offset by China exposure, delays and competition.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. My implied outcome is about $440 billion: I start from the consensus baseline, then apply SemiAnalysis\\u2019 estimate that second-half data-center compute revenue will be 20% above Wall Street expectations to that large, but partial, revenue stream\\u2014not to total revenue\\u2014and allow for some additional, discounted CPU and deployment upside. China-related weakness, reported platform delays and competing AMD deployments temper that uplift, but do not bring the estimate below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1167\\\", \\\"e1236\\\", \\\"e1643\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e1475\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e1016\\\", \\\"e1110\\\", \\\"e1157\\\"]\"], [2907, \"2026-08-07\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate is about $392\\u2013393 billion, in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $440 billion. Starting from the roughly $393 billion consensus, SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% above-consensus estimate for second-half data-center compute supports an uplift to the total without applying 20% to all revenue; discounted FY2027 CPU sales and Rubin ramp/deployment evidence provide additional support, while the broader-period orders figure is not taken in full.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The forecast remains about $440 billion, above rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion threshold: the 20% SemiAnalysis estimate applies to second-half data-center compute, not total revenue, and supports an uplift over consensus; discounted standalone-CPU sales and stronger Rubin deployment evidence add support. Confirmed orders and the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin forecast cover broader periods, so they are not counted in full for FY2027. China exclusions, reported platform delays and AMD deployments temper the estimate but do not imply enough reduction to cross below $431.2 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e234\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e1521\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e1042\\\"]\"], [2946, \"2026-08-08\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e28; corroborated by e218, e234 and e811) is in rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.\", \"Approximately $435 billion. Starting from the roughly $393 billion reference, apply the reported 20% uplift to second-half data-center compute only\\u2014not total revenue\\u2014and add a discounted contribution from NVIDIA\\u2019s $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU target; competitive deployments and China constraints partly offset those gains.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The forecast is approximately $435 billion, above rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. The new evidence supports both sides: Vera Rubin is reported in full production and running at cloud partners, while AMD has secured sizeable competing deployments and China-related supply remains constrained. The 20% SemiAnalysis uplift applies to second-half data-center compute, not total revenue; the $20 billion CPU target is discounted because it is a new product, and the 200 MW Zankore deployment is for calendar 2027, mostly outside FY2027. These factors temper, but do not erase, the uplift over consensus. The estimate is slightly below the previous roughly $440 billion forecast, but remains in rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1219\\\", \\\"e1236\\\", \\\"e1642\\\", \\\"e1643\\\", \\\"e1157\\\"]\"], [2978, \"2026-08-09\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s approximately $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"Approximately $435 billion. Q1 reported revenue and Q2 guidance, which is close to the Q2 market estimate, anchor the forecast near consensus; the specific estimate of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above expectations supports an uplift, partly offset by China constraints and competitive risks, with only a discounted contribution from the $20 billion stand-alone CPU target.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The forecast remains about $435 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.62 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, close to the market\\u2019s Q2 estimate, so those figures anchor rather than independently lift the full-year forecast. The specific SemiAnalysis estimate of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above Wall Street\\u2019s expectations supports an uplift, while competition, China-related limits, and the lower Q3 growth expectation temper it. The $20 billion CPU forecast is discounted because the product is newly launching, and the Zankore 200 MW target is mostly outside FY2027. At about $435 billion, the implied outcome is above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary, though only narrowly.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e491\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1157\\\", \\\"e1167\\\", \\\"e1219\\\", \\\"e1236\\\", \\\"e1464\\\", \\\"e1475\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1521\\\", \\\"e1528\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e1569\\\", \\\"e1571\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e1601\\\", \\\"e1602\\\", \\\"e1603\\\", \\\"e1604\\\", \\\"e1605\\\", \\\"e1607\\\", \\\"e1608\\\", \\\"e1611\\\", \\\"e1626\\\", \\\"e1627\\\", \\\"e1628\\\", \\\"e1642\\\", \\\"e1643\\\", \\\"e1653\\\", \\\"e1654\\\", \\\"e1671\\\", \\\"e1672\\\", \\\"e1687\\\", \\\"e1693\\\", \\\"e1698\\\"]\"], [3056, \"2026-08-10\", 1, \"Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate; rung 2.\", \"About $440 billion: start from the roughly $393 billion market estimate, add the implied uplift from SemiAnalysis's 20%-above-consensus second-half data-center compute view, and include only a discounted contribution from NVIDIA's $20 billion standalone-CPU target because the product is newly launching and the target volume is not shown in hand.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The $91 billion Q2 guide and high reported/guided growth support that reference as a reasonable base, but the specific estimate that second-half data-center compute revenue will be 20% above Wall Street's expectations implies a material uplift; adding a discounted portion of NVIDIA's $20 billion standalone-CPU target puts the forecast around $440 billion. Confirmed Rubin production and the reported order pipeline support execution and demand, but do not justify counting the multi-year order and revenue figures wholly in FY2027. China-related limits, product-delay reports and competing AMD deployments temper the upside. At about $440 billion, the implied outcome is above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e1571\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e803\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e805\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e1475\\\", \\\"e1521\\\", \\\"e165\\\", \\\"e169\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e1735\\\"]\"], [3224, \"2026-08-11\", 1, \"Wall Street estimates of about $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027; rung 2.\", \"About $440 billion: start from the roughly $392\\u2013393 billion reference, apply a discounted uplift for SemiAnalysis's estimate that second-half data-center compute revenue will exceed expectations by 20%, and include only part of the $20 billion standalone-CPU target. The Q3 estimate and current-quarter guidance support the revenue trajectory; the SpaceX target is not counted as firm FY2027 NVIDIA revenue.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The Q1 result, $91 billion Q2 guide and $103.1 billion Q3 estimate support that baseline, while SemiAnalysis's quantified estimate of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above Wall Street expectations and a discounted contribution from the $20 billion standalone-CPU target imply about $440 billion. Rubin production and the order pipeline support execution; SpaceX's large capacity and spending targets add possible upside but are not sized firm NVIDIA orders for FY2027, so I do not count them in full. China exclusions and competing AMD deployments temper the upside. About $440 billion is above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1521\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e1840\\\", \\\"e1854\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e1856\\\"]\"], [3293, \"2026-08-12\", 1, \"Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e28, e218, e234, e811), which is rung 2.\", \"About $440 billion. This carries forward the quantified second-half data-center compute uplift over Wall Street expectations and discounts the $20 billion standalone-CPU target; Q1 actual revenue, Q2 guidance, and the Q3 estimate are consistent with that run-rate, while multi-year and post-deadline targets are not counted in full.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The forecast remains about $440 billion: the quantified expectation for second-half data-center compute to exceed Wall Street expectations supports an uplift over that baseline, with the $20 billion standalone-CPU target discounted rather than taken in full. Q1 revenue, Q2 guidance, and the Q3 estimate are consistent with a strong run-rate. Rubin's move from ramping to full production strengthens execution confidence, but does not by itself quantify an additional revenue uplift. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans calendar 2025\\u20132027, and the SpaceX $300 billion order estimate is tied to a capacity target by year-end 2027; neither can be counted in full for NVIDIA FY2027. China exclusions and competing AMD deployments are headwinds, but do not erase the uplift implied by the other evidence. At about $440 billion, the forecast exceeds the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e1804\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1854\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e1521\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\"]\"], [3403, \"2026-08-13\", 1, \"Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate (e811; also e234), at rung 2.\", \"About $440 billion for FY2027. Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, and the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate total about $275.7 billion through Q3; the remaining forecast and carried evidence of H2 data-center compute strength support a full-year outcome above the $393 billion reference, but the SpaceX opportunity is discounted because its capacity target is mainly after FY2027.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The forecast remains about $440 billion, above the rung-1 boundary of $431.2 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, management guided Q2 to $91 billion, and analysts estimate Q3 at $103.1 billion. Those quarterly figures support a strong run-rate, while the carried evidence that H2 data-center compute could exceed Wall Street expectations supports an outcome above the reference. The new SpaceX estimate of up to $300 billion in potential orders is not counted in full: it is conditional, and the associated 10 GW capacity target is for year-end 2027, mostly beyond NVIDIA FY2027. Confirmed Blackwell/Rubin orders span multiple products and periods, so they likewise do not translate dollar-for-dollar into FY2027 revenue. China compute is excluded from the Q2 guide, and competitive alternatives add risk, but neither overturns the above-reference forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e811\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e1475\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1854\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e1955\\\", \\\"e1956\\\"]\"], [3464, \"2026-08-14\", 1, \"Wall Street estimates of roughly $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $440 billion: carry the reported Q1 and guided Q2 growth trajectory through FY2027, incorporate the analyst Q3 estimate, and apply the 20% second-half upside only to data-center compute, with the separately forecast $20 billion standalone-CPU contribution. This places the forecast above $431.2 billion without treating multi-period orders or SpaceX\\u2019s later capacity target as FY2027 revenue.\", \"The market reference is roughly $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 2. That is a reference, not the forecast: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, and analysts expect about $103.1 billion in Q3. Carrying the strong guided growth forward, while applying the reported 20% upside expectation only to second-half data-center compute\\u2014not to total revenue\\u2014and allowing for the separately forecast $20 billion of standalone CPU sales, supports an outcome around $440 billion. That is above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and calendar years, while SpaceX\\u2019s large capacity and spending targets are not firm FY2027 NVIDIA procurement; neither is counted dollar-for-dollar. China exclusions and competing accelerator plans temper the upside, but do not outweigh the implied uplift above the boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1854\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e1734\\\", \\\"e1936\\\", \\\"e1937\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e1955\\\", \\\"e1956\\\", \\\"e1919\\\"]\"], [3561, \"2026-08-15\", 1, \"Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is about $392\\u2013393 billion, at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $435 billion for FY2027. Starting from the market's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion expectation, the strong Q1/Q2 guide and Q3 estimate support a higher run-rate; I apply the 20% second-half compute upside only to that portion of revenue and include only a discounted share of the $20 billion CPU target because shipments start in Q3 and ramp in Q4.\", \"The market reference is about $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. That remains a reference, not the forecast: the $91 billion Q2 guide and roughly $103.1 billion Q3 estimate support a strong run-rate, while SemiAnalysis's 20% upside view applies only to second-half data-center compute, not total revenue. I imply about $435 billion: carry the strong quarterly trajectory forward, apply that compute uplift only to its relevant share, and count only part of the $20 billion standalone-CPU target because Vera Rubin CPU shipments start in Q3 and ramp in Q4. Demand evidence\\u2014including CoreWeave's sold-out near-term capacity\\u2014supports the upside, while China exclusions and competing accelerators temper it. The estimate remains above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary, though with less CPU contribution than the previous approximately $440 billion estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e2016\\\", \\\"e1906\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1840\\\", \\\"e493\\\", \\\"e1955\\\", \\\"e2052\\\"]\"], [3612, \"2026-08-16\", 2, \"Wall Street estimates of about $392\\u2013393 billion for NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; this is rung 2.\", \"About $410 billion. The reported Q1, Q2 guidance, Q3 estimate, and a 12% sequential carry-forward imply about $391 billion before applying the in-force 20% upside view only to second-half data-center compute and a discounted contribution from the standalone-CPU target.\", \"The market reference is about $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 2. The quarterly run-rate is close to that level: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, $103.1 billion estimated for Q3, and about $115.5 billion in Q4 if the roughly 12% sequential growth assumption continues\\u2014about $391 billion total. Carrying forward the in-force SemiAnalysis view adds upside only to second-half data-center compute, not to all revenue; strong capacity demand and some contribution from standalone CPUs support a central estimate around $410 billion. That clears the $392 billion boundary but remains below $431.2 billion. China exclusions, reported product delays, and competition temper the upside; large customer and product targets are not treated as fully realized FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e801\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e2016\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e1475\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e1856\\\"]\"], [3767, \"2026-08-17\", 2, \"Market estimates of approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027; rung 2.\", \"About $410 billion. The reported Q1, Q2 guidance, Q3 estimate and a 12% sequential-growth Q4 assumption imply roughly $391 billion; a limited second-half data-center compute upside and discounted CPU/Rubin contributions support the higher central estimate, without applying those inputs to total revenue wholesale.\", \"The market reference is roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The quarterly path of $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, $103.1 billion expected by analysts for Q3, and about $115.5 billion in Q4 if sequential growth continues at 12% totals about $391 billion. The in-force SemiAnalysis view adds upside specifically to second-half data-center compute, while NVIDIA's CPU target and Rubin ramp provide further, discounted support; together these support a central estimate around $410 billion, not a 20% uplift to all revenue. That remains above $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. Reported rack delays and cancellations temper the outlook. The newly announced OpenAI capacity is expected to begin coming online in 2028, after FY2027, and the large SpaceX capacity target is mainly beyond this fiscal year, so neither is taken as FY2027 revenue in full.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e555\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e1856\\\", \\\"e2097\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2185\\\", \\\"e2151\\\"]\"], [3902, \"2026-08-18\", 2, \"Wall Street estimates of about $392\\u2013$393 billion for NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; this reference is rung 2.\", \"About $410 billion. Q1 reported revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 analyst expectation, and Q4 carried forward at 12% sequential growth imply about $391 billion; the in-force, specifically second-half compute upside and discounted CPU/Rubin contributions support a modest uplift, while the new 2028 OpenAI capacity is not counted as FY2027 revenue.\", \"The market reference is roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The reported Q1 result, Q2 guidance, and analyst Q3 expectation, with Q4 carried forward at the 12% sequential growth assumption described by the analyst, imply about $391 billion before further upside. The in-force SemiAnalysis estimate of 20% upside to Wall Street\\u2019s second-half data-center compute expectations, together with the discounted CPU target and the now-confirmed Rubin production ramp, supports a modest uplift to roughly $410 billion\\u2014not a 20% uplift to total revenue. New SpaceX demand signals add upside but do not establish that the estimated orders will be recognized in FY2027; the large OpenAI campus is expected to begin coming online in 2028. China revenue is excluded from Q2 guidance, so the zero-H20 evidence is a headwind already reflected in that guide. The implied figure remains above $392 billion and below the $431.2 billion boundary, so rung 2 stands.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e1804\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e956\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1734\\\", \\\"e1854\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e2097\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e1521\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e1955\\\"]\"], [4037, \"2026-08-19\", 2, \"Wall Street estimates of roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $425 billion: the Q1 result, BofA's $94\\u2013$95 billion Q2 estimate and $107\\u2013$108 billion potential Q3 guide, with 12% sequential growth carried into Q4, imply about $404 billion before a discounted uplift for stronger second-half data-center demand, CPU sales and SpaceX-related demand.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The quarterly evidence now implies a higher run-rate: using $81.6 billion for Q1, BofA's $94\\u2013$95 billion Q2 estimate, its $107\\u2013$108 billion potential Q3 guide, and carrying 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $404 billion. The previously identified second-half data-center upside and a discounted contribution from the CPU and SpaceX demand outlook lift the forecast to roughly $425 billion, still below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. SpaceX's much larger chip-spend estimates span a buildout through the end of calendar 2027, beyond NVIDIA's FY2027 end on January 31, so they are not taken in full; the OpenAI Ohio campus's first capacity is expected in 2028 and does not add materially to FY2027. The current conclusions of the AI-capex and export-policy themes support demand and do not imply a policy-driven zero or a component exceeding its whole.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2324\\\", \\\"e2326\\\", \\\"e2350\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1608\\\", \\\"e1735\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e1856\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2185\\\", \\\"e2298\\\"]\"], [4074, \"2026-08-20\", 2, \"Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the reference; it sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"The quarterly path of $81.6 billion Q1, about $94\\u2013$95 billion Q2, $107\\u2013$108 billion Q3, and roughly $120 billion Q4 using 12% sequential growth implies about $404 billion before uncertain incremental CPU and SpaceX upside. Discounting that upside for timing and execution, and weighing Morningstar's 80% growth estimate, implies roughly $420 billion for FY2027.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The quarterly path remains above that reference: Q1 was $81.6 billion, BofA expects $94\\u2013$95 billion for Q2 versus the $91 billion company guide, and sees potential Q3 guidance of $107\\u2013$108 billion. Carrying 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $404 billion before any incremental contribution from CPU or SpaceX demand; those provide upside, but their FY2027 contribution is uncertain and partly spans later periods. Morningstar's 80% growth model and the roughly $400 billion estimate are counterweights. The resulting forecast is about $420 billion, above the $392 billion boundary but below the $431.2 billion boundary, so it remains rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e1804\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2298\\\", \\\"e2324\\\", \\\"e2325\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e2335\\\", \\\"e2350\\\", \\\"e2371\\\", \\\"e1734\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e2097\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2300\\\", \\\"e2306\\\", \\\"e2372\\\", \\\"e2373\\\"]\"], [4163, \"2026-08-21\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference; that sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $434 billion: Q1\\u2019s $81.6 billion, BofA\\u2019s $94\\u2013$95 billion Q2 estimate, its $107\\u2013$108 billion potential Q3 guidance, and roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply about $404 billion before allowing roughly $20 billion for standalone CPU sales and a heavily discounted ~$10 billion FY2027 contribution from SpaceX-related demand.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The quarterly path implied by Q1 revenue, BofA\\u2019s Q2 estimate, its potential Q3 guidance, and 12% sequential growth into Q4 is about $404 billion. Allowing roughly $20 billion for forecast standalone CPU sales and only about $10 billion of incremental FY2027 SpaceX-related revenue\\u2014rather than taking the much larger, multi-period chip-spending estimate in full\\u2014puts the forecast near $434 billion. That crosses the $431.2 billion boundary into rung 1. This is an execution-sensitive forecast, not an outcome already established: the SpaceX capacity target extends to end-2027, while the OpenAI Ohio project\\u2019s first capacity is expected in 2028. China H20 sales remain a downside risk, and Morningstar\\u2019s 80% growth model is a counterweight.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e419\\\", \\\"e811\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1475\\\", \\\"e1734\\\", \\\"e1804\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2097\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2298\\\", \\\"e2327\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e2350\\\", \\\"e2371\\\"]\"], [4200, \"2026-08-22\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; rung 2 (Bullish).\", \"Approximately $420 billion: the Q1 result, Q2 guide, Q3 estimates and a continued sequential-growth path imply roughly $392\\u2013$400 billion before allowing a discounted $20\\u2013$25 billion of incremental standalone-CPU and SpaceX revenue.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits at rung 2. Using Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, the company\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide, and a Q3 estimate centered between the analyst view of about $103.1 billion and BofA\\u2019s $107\\u2013$108 billion expectation, then carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4, gives a quarterly-path estimate around $392\\u2013$400 billion. I add only about $20\\u2013$25 billion for incremental standalone-CPU and SpaceX-related revenue after discounting execution, timing and overlap with that broad revenue path; that implies roughly $420 billion. It is above the rung-2 floor but remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The reported SpaceX capex and exclusive NVIDIA hardware choice strengthen the upside case, but the 10-GW target and estimated chip spending extend beyond FY2027 or are not firm FY2027 shipment figures. The Ohio project\\u2019s first capacity is expected in 2028, so it contributes little to this due-date forecast. Export-policy themes and hyperscaler spending are supportive, while product delays and competing accelerators temper the upside.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2298\\\", \\\"e2350\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1734\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e1856\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e2371\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e2097\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1955\\\", \\\"e2300\\\"]\"], [4263, \"2026-08-23\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $430 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus an estimated $94\\u2013$95 billion Q2, $107\\u2013$108 billion Q3, and roughly $120 billion Q4 from continued sequential growth gives about $404 billion before incremental contributions. Discounted additions from standalone CPUs and SpaceX demand lift the central estimate to roughly $430 billion, while allowing for timing, overlap and execution risk.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The updated quarterly path is higher: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, about $94\\u2013$95 billion as a plausible Q2 outcome, roughly $107\\u2013$108 billion for Q3, and about $120 billion in Q4 if sequential growth continues near 12%\\u2014approximately $404 billion before incremental contributions. Discounting the $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast and the SpaceX demand estimates for timing, execution and overlap supports a central estimate near $430 billion. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin order figure spans multiple calendar years, so it cannot be treated as FY2027 revenue; likewise, SpaceX\\u2019s 10-GW target and estimated $150\\u2013$250 billion chip spend are not FY2027 shipments. These strengthen the upside case but do not make revenue of at least $431.2 billion the likeliest outcome. Competitive custom silicon and reported product delays also temper the upside.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e1734\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e1955\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2298\\\", \\\"e2300\\\", \\\"e2325\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e2350\\\", \\\"e2371\\\", \\\"e2372\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e811\\\"]\"], [4456, \"2026-08-24\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $430 billion: Q1 actual revenue, Q2 guidance with a modest beat allowance, Q3 estimates, and continued sequential growth imply roughly $394\\u2013$406 billion before discounted CPU and SpaceX additions. Those additions lift the central estimate near $430 billion, still below $431.2 billion.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. A reasonable quarterly path is about $81.6 billion in Q1, roughly $94\\u2013$95 billion for Q2, around $103\\u2013$108 billion for Q3, and about $115\\u2013$121 billion for Q4 if sequential growth continues near 12%\\u2014roughly $394\\u2013$406 billion before incremental contributions. Discounting the $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast and time- and execution-adjusting SpaceX demand puts the central outcome near $430 billion. That is bullish versus the reference but remains just below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin order figure spans multiple years, and SpaceX\\u2019s 10-GW target is for year-end 2027; neither can be counted in full as FY2027 revenue. Competing AMD and in-house chip supply also temper the upside. No dependency conclusion is supplied for this thesis.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e28\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e154\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e142\\\", \\\"e144\\\", \\\"e146\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1734\\\", \\\"e1735\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e1856\\\", \\\"e1955\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2324\\\", \\\"e2325\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e2350\\\", \\\"e2371\\\", \\\"e2372\\\", \\\"e2373\\\", \\\"e2385\\\", \\\"e2386\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e2453\\\", \\\"e2458\\\", \\\"e2459\\\", \\\"e2460\\\", \\\"e2463\\\", \\\"e2464\\\", \\\"e2522\\\", \\\"e2532\\\", \\\"e2555\\\", \\\"e2561\\\", \\\"e2562\\\"]\"], [4580, \"2026-08-25\", 2, \"LSEG analysts forecast $396 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; this is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). Other supplied Wall Street estimates around $393 billion are also in rung 2.\", \"A forward quarterly path of about $81.6 billion in Q1, $94\\u201395 billion in Q2, $107\\u2013108 billion in Q3, and roughly $120 billion in Q4 totals about $403\\u2013405 billion. Partial credit for the unshipped standalone-CPU opportunity and early SpaceX demand lifts the central estimate to about $410 billion, still within rung 2.\", \"The market reference is about $396 billion for FY2027 (LSEG), with other Wall Street estimates around $393 billion; both sit in rung 2. A forward quarterly path of roughly $81.6 billion in Q1, $94\\u201395 billion in Q2, $107\\u2013108 billion in Q3, and about $120 billion in Q4 implies roughly $403\\u2013405 billion before any additional contribution from standalone CPUs or SpaceX. Allowing some, but not all, of those unshipped or multi-period opportunities supports a central estimate near $410 billion. That is above the $392 billion floor but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The roughly $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure covers multiple calendar years and products, and SpaceX\\u2019s large buildout is a year-end 2027 target, so neither can be counted as FY2027 revenue in full. Strong current Rubin production and orders support the outlook, while export and competitive risks temper the upside.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2577\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e112\\\", \\\"e2350\\\", \\\"e2640\\\", \\\"e1806\\\", \\\"e544\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e2522\\\", \\\"e2460\\\", \\\"e1855\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e141\\\", \\\"e552\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1558\\\", \\\"e2604\\\"]\"], [5092, \"2026-08-26\", 2, \"LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion (e2577), in rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.\", \"Q1 reported $81.62 billion and Q2 reported $96.2 billion; Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion. Carrying Q3's approximately 12.3% sequential growth over Q2 into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4, or approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027 in total.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion, which sits in rung 2. The reported Q1 and Q2 results, followed by Q3 guidance, imply about $407 billion for the year: above the $392 billion rung-2 floor, but still below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The Q2 beat and higher-than-previously-expected Q3 guide lift the implied outcome above the reference, but do not move it across a rung boundary. The $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast and multi-year GPU commitments are upside inputs, but the evidence does not establish that their full amounts are incremental to the revenue path; I do not add them in full.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2577\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2686\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2776\\\"]\"], [5296, \"2026-08-27\", 2, \"LSEG\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 2.\", \"About $414 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to Q3 guidance of $108 billion, then carry Q3\\u2019s roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward to Q4\\u2019s $68 billion FY2026 comparison quarter, implying about $128.5 billion for Q4 FY2027.\", \"The market reference is LSEG\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion, which is rung 2. The latest reported quarter and Q3 guidance lift the implied outcome above that reference, but the resulting estimate remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Orders, Rubin\\u2019s production ramp and constrained demand add upside, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans calendar 2025\\u20132027 and the AWS GPU plan spans several fiscal years; neither establishes that their full value is FY2027 revenue. I therefore keep the forecast in rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2577\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2748\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2692\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3115\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2686\\\", \\\"e1050\\\", \\\"e3159\\\"]\"], [5652, \"2026-08-28\", 2, \"LSEG FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion; rung 2.\", \"About $415 billion: $81.62B Q1 actual + $96.2B Q2 actual + $108B Q3 guidance + approximately $128.8B for Q4, applying Q3\\u2019s roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate to Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68B.\", \"The market reference is LSEG\\u2019s $396 billion FY2027 estimate, in rung 2. Using the reported Q1 and Q2 revenue, NVIDIA\\u2019s $108 billion Q3 guidance, and carrying Q3\\u2019s roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate into Q4 against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion, the implied FY2027 total is about $415 billion. That is above the $392 billion rung-2 floor but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The reported 70% full-year growth figure would imply a lower total, but it conflicts with the stronger quarter-specific run-rate; even the quarterly run-rate therefore does not cross into rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2577\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e3280\\\"]\"], [5794, \"2026-08-29\", 2, \"LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2.\", \"Approximately $414 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 + $96.2 billion in Q2 + $108 billion Q3 guidance, plus an estimated $128.5 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward against the prior-year Q4 base of about $68 billion.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The implied outcome is about $414 billion, also rung 2: Q1's $81.6 billion plus reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and Q3 guidance of $108 billion, then Q4 at roughly 89% year-over-year growth carried forward from the Q3 guide. Applying that growth to the prior-year Q4 base of about $68 billion gives approximately $128.5 billion for Q4. The result is above rung 2's $392 billion floor but below rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. NVIDIA's reported Q2 beat and Q3 guidance support the run-rate; the reported 70% full-year growth outlook and supply constraints are counterweights, but the quarter-specific guidance is the stronger forecast input. Rubin purchase orders and its expected Q3 contribution support delivery of the guide, but are not added on top of it.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2577\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3115\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\"]\"], [5935, \"2026-08-30\", 2, \"LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2.\", \"About $414 billion: add Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 actual revenue of $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108 billion; carry Q3's 89% year-over-year guided growth into Q4, or about $128.5 billion on the roughly $68 billion FY2026 Q4 base.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The evidence-implied estimate is about $414 billion, also rung 2: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2's $96.2 billion and Q3 guidance of $108 billion, then Q4 at about $128.5 billion if Q3's 89% year-over-year guided growth carries forward against the roughly $68 billion FY2026 Q4 base. Q2 beat estimates and Q3 guidance is above the reported analyst expectation; those current-period outcomes support carrying the growth rate forward rather than staying at consensus. The reported 70% full-year growth outlook is a meaningful counterweight, but would imply roughly $367 billion using the FY2026 base and require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion after Q3 guidance of $108 billion. The near-term report and guidance, together with the Rubin ramp and orders, make that sharp deceleration less likely. The run-rate estimate remains above rung 2's $392 billion floor but below rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2577\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2748\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2881\\\", \\\"e2686\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3115\\\", \\\"e2776\\\", \\\"e2690\\\"]\"], [6161, \"2026-08-31\", 2, \"LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2. The additional $397 billion analyst estimate also falls in rung 2.\", \"About $414 billion: Q1 $81.6 billion + Q2 $96.2 billion + Q3 guidance midpoint $108 billion, plus an estimated Q4 of about $128.5 billion by carrying Q3's approximately 89% year-over-year guided growth forward from the prior-year Q4 base of about $68 billion.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 2; the cited analyst estimate of $397 billion is also in rung 2. The evidence-implied estimate is about $414 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 and the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, then roughly $128.5 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3's approximately 89% year-over-year guided growth forward from the prior-year Q4 base of about $68 billion. Q2 beat the midpoint of its guidance, and the current Q3 guide supports carrying growth forward rather than treating the last quarter as a flat annualized run-rate. The resulting estimate is above $392 billion but below $431.2 billion, so it remains in rung 2. NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth outlook is a counterweight, but would imply a materially lower Q4 after the first three quarters and is outweighed by the reported run-rate and current-quarter guidance. Supply constraints temper the upside; Rubin production shipments and purchase orders support execution, but the disclosed capacity and orders span periods and products and do not justify adding their full amounts to FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2577\\\", \\\"e3683\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e3200\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2690\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e2604\\\", \\\"e2300\\\", \\\"e3342\\\", \\\"e3684\\\"]\"], [6387, \"2026-09-01\", 2, \"The latest supplied market estimate is about $411 billion for FY2027 (e3837), which is rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.\", \"About $414 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1, $96.2 billion in Q2, and the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.5 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from prior-year Q4 revenue of $68 billion. The implied total is in rung 2.\", \"The current market reference is about $411 billion (e3837), in rung 2. The quarterly run-rate implies about $414 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 and the $108 billion Q3 guide, then about $128.5 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from the prior-year Q4 revenue of $68 billion. That is above $392 billion but below $431.2 billion, so it remains rung 2. The 70% full-year growth outlook is a material counterweight: using an FY2026 base of roughly $216 billion implies about $367 billion, but would leave only about $81 billion for Q4 after the first three quarters and Q3 guide. I give greater weight to the reported results and current-quarter guide than to that conflicting annual-growth figure. Supply constraints temper the upside, but the evidence does not displace the implied rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e3837\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e3517\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e2870\\\"]\"], [6412, \"2026-09-02\", 2, \"Market revenue estimates shown range from about $397 billion to $411 billion for FY2027 (e3858, e3837); both fall in rung 2.\", \"Approximately $414.6 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.8 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3\\u2019s roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from the $68 billion prior-year Q4 revenue. This is rung 2.\", \"The market reference is about $397\\u2013$411 billion (e3858, e3837), which is rung 2. The quarterly run-rate implies roughly $415 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 and the $108 billion Q3 guide, then approximately $128.8 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3\\u2019s roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from the prior-year Q4 revenue of $68 billion. That is above $392 billion but below $431.2 billion, so it remains rung 2. NVIDIA\\u2019s 70% full-year growth guidance is a counterweight, implying about $367 billion on the roughly $216 billion FY2026 base carried from the prior conclusion; that would be rung 3, but it conflicts with the reported results and current-quarter guide, which are stronger forecast inputs. Rubin production shipments and purchase orders from major customers reinforce demand, while supply constraints temper the upside; neither supplies a quantified adjustment to the run-rate estimate. The $40 billion-per-gigawatt figure is an opportunity, not a revenue forecast, so I do not add it to the estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2692\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e3733\\\", \\\"e3837\\\", \\\"e3858\\\"]\"], [6607, \"2026-09-03\", 2, \"The latest stated Wall Street consensus is $411 billion (e3837), within rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). Other cited estimates around $397 billion are also in rung 2.\", \"About $414.6 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.8 billion in Q4 by carrying the Q3 guide\\u2019s approximately 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion. This is rung 2.\", \"The market reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 revenue, which is rung 2; other cited consensus estimates are also within rung 2. The operating run-rate implies about $415 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, then about $128.8 billion in Q4 by carrying the Q3 guide\\u2019s roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion. That is above $392 billion but below $431.2 billion. The reported 70% full-year growth guidance is a counterweight, implying roughly $367 billion on an FY2026 base of about $216 billion; however, reaching that total after the Q1\\u2013Q3 figures would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion, a sharp deceleration inconsistent with the latest quarterly results and guidance. Supply constraints temper the upside, while Vera Rubin orders and shipment ramp support the run-rate. Newly quantified competing deployments weigh the other way, but do not establish a revenue reduction sufficient to cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e3837\\\", \\\"e3517\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e4059\\\", \\\"e4060\\\", \\\"e4061\\\"]\"], [6818, \"2026-09-04\", 2, \"FY2027 revenue estimates include $397 billion and about $411 billion, both in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"Using the reported $81.62 billion Q1 and $96.2 billion Q2, the $108 billion Q3 guide, and carrying approximately 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion gives Q4 FY2027 of about $128.5 billion and FY2027 total revenue of about $414.3 billion. This is rung 2, below $431.2 billion.\", \"The market reference is mixed FY2027 revenue estimates of $397 billion and about $411 billion; both sit in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The operating run-rate also points to rung 2: carrying the Q3 guide's approximately 89% year-over-year growth forward against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion implies about $128.5 billion in Q4 FY2027, and roughly $414.3 billion for the year. The 70% full-year growth indication is a counterweight, but does not outweigh the reported results and near-term guidance; the implied total remains below the rung-1 threshold.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e3837\\\", \\\"e3858\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3115\\\"]\"], [6928, \"2026-09-05\", 2, \"FY2027 revenue estimates of about $397 billion and $411 billion are the supplied market references; both sit in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $414.3 billion: carry the Q3 guide\\u2019s approximately 89% year-over-year growth into Q4, implying about $128.5 billion against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion, then add reported Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion, reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, and the $108 billion Q3 guide.\", \"The market reference is approximately $397 billion to $411 billion, which sits in rung 2. Carrying the Q3 FY2027 guide\\u2019s roughly 89% year-over-year growth into Q4 against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion implies about $128.5 billion for Q4; adding reported Q1 and Q2 revenue and the Q3 guide gives roughly $414.3 billion for FY2027. That is above the reference range\\u2019s lower end but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary, so the forecast remains rung 2. The $70% growth statements are not reconcilable with the reported first-half revenue and Q3 guidance as a full-year FY2027 growth figure, so they do not displace the quarter-by-quarter run-rate. Supply constraints and alternative-chip competition weigh against the estimate, while Rubin shipments and orders support it; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 adjustment large enough to cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e2577\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e3683\\\", \\\"e3733\\\", \\\"e3837\\\", \\\"e3890\\\", \\\"e4059\\\", \\\"e4060\\\", \\\"e4220\\\"]\"], [7116, \"2026-09-06\", 2, \"Analyst consensus is approximately $411 billion, with another supplied estimate around $397 billion; both references are in rung 2.\", \"About $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion Q1 + $96.2 billion Q2 + $108 billion Q3 guidance + approximately $128.5 billion for Q4, derived by carrying the Q3 guide's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\", \"The market reference is analyst consensus of about $411 billion (with other supplied consensus estimates around $397 billion); both sit in rung 2. The evidence-based run-rate is about $414.3 billion: Q1 $81.62 billion + Q2 $96.2 billion + Q3 guidance of $108 billion + an estimated Q4 of about $128.5 billion, carrying the 89% year-over-year growth implied by the Q3 guide forward against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion. That is above rung 2's $392 billion floor but below rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. Rubin production shipments and purchase orders, and management's expected 20% Data Center contribution in Q3, support demand; supply bottlenecks, China Data Center exclusion, and rival accelerator deployments temper the forecast but do not quantify a revision large enough to move it across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3115\\\", \\\"e4241\\\", \\\"e4248\\\", \\\"e2776\\\", \\\"e4059\\\", \\\"e4060\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\"]\"], [7206, \"2026-09-07\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus is approximately $411 billion for FY2027, placing the reference at rung 2.\", \"About $416\\u2013421 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, then an estimated roughly $129\\u2013135 billion in Q4. The baseline Q4 estimate carries the Q3 guide's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion; the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration supports a modest uplift.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion, which is rung 2. The evidence-based run-rate is roughly $416\\u2013421 billion: Q1 and Q2 reported revenue total $177.8 billion, Q3 guidance is $108 billion, and carrying the Q3 year-over-year growth rate forward to Q4's $68 billion year-ago base gives about $128.5 billion for Q4, or $414.3 billion for the year. Management's expectation that hyperscale growth accelerates in Q4 supports a modest uplift from that baseline, while supply constraints and competing accelerators limit the case for a larger one. The resulting estimate remains below rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. The reported 70% full-year growth outlook would imply about $367 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, but that would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion after the $108 billion Q3 guide; it conflicts with the quarterly run-rate and the stated Q4 acceleration, so it does not outweigh those more immediate inputs.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e4283\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e3733\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e4059\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4383\\\", \\\"e4333\\\"]\"], [7278, \"2026-09-08\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion for FY2027 (e4333), which is rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.\", \"Q1 and Q2 total $177.82 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate forward to the $68 billion prior-year Q4 base implies about $128.5 billion in Q4, or $414.3 billion for FY2027. Allowing for the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration and Rubin ramp, but tempering for supply limits, supports roughly $414\\u2013421 billion.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The operating run-rate also points to rung 2: Q1 and Q2 reported revenue total $177.82 billion, and Q3 guidance adds $108 billion. Carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward to the prior-year Q4 base of $68 billion implies about $128.5 billion for Q4 and $414.3 billion for FY2027; the expectation of accelerating hyperscale growth and the Rubin ramp support some upside from that baseline, while supply constraints and custom-chip competition temper it. That puts a reasonable implied range around $414\\u2013421 billion, above rung 3's $392 billion boundary but below rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth outlook would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, but it conflicts with the reported first two quarters and current Q3 guidance: it would require only about $81 billion in Q4. The quarterly reported results and current-quarter guidance therefore provide the more persuasive run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e3890\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e4283\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3115\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e4059\\\", \\\"e4060\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e1510\\\"]\"], [7395, \"2026-09-09\", 2, \"Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e4333) is the market reference and sits in rung 2.\", \"Q1 and Q2 reported revenue totals $177.82 billion; adding the $108 billion Q3 guide and carrying its 89% year-over-year growth rate onto the $68 billion prior-year Q4 implies about $128.5 billion for Q4 and $414.3 billion total FY2027 revenue.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 2. The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues total $177.82 billion; adding the $108 billion Q3 guide and carrying its 89% year-over-year growth rate onto the $68 billion prior-year Q4 gives about $128.5 billion for Q4 and $414.3 billion for FY2027. That is above the $392 billion rung-3 boundary but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth outlook would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, but that conflicts with the current-quarter guide and the resulting run-rate; I give the Q3 guide greater weight. Orders, capacity investment and the expected Rubin ramp support demand, while supply limits and competing accelerator deployments temper the outlook without shifting this estimate across a boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e3733\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2686\\\", \\\"e3115\\\", \\\"e4248\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e4059\\\", \\\"e4060\\\", \\\"e3684\\\"]\"], [7594, \"2026-09-10\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus is $411 billion for FY2027 (e4333), placing the reference in rung 2.\", \"About $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2, plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.5 billion in Q4 by carrying roughly 89% year-over-year growth onto Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The implied run-rate is about $414.3 billion: Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.82 billion; adding the $108 billion Q3 guide and carrying roughly 89% year-over-year growth into Q4 against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion gives about $128.5 billion for Q4. This is above the $392 billion boundary for rung 3 but below the $431.2 billion boundary for rung 1. Management's stated 70% full-year growth would imply about $367 billion (rung 3), but after the first two reported quarters and Q3 guidance it would require only about $81 billion in Q4, inconsistent with the guided quarterly trajectory. The Q2 beat, Rubin production shipments and purchase orders support the run-rate; supply constraints, China exclusion and competing accelerators temper it, but do not imply a lower total than the run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e4628\\\", \\\"e3200\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2776\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e4059\\\", \\\"e4060\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4241\\\", \\\"e4574\\\", \\\"e4575\\\", \\\"e4314\\\"]\"], [7753, \"2026-09-11\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus is approximately $411 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $414.3 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion, reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108 billion; estimate Q4 at about $128.5 billion by carrying the guided 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The reported first-half revenue plus Q3 guidance and a Q4 estimate based on carrying forward the guided 89% year-over-year growth imply about $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion + $96.2 billion + $108 billion + ($68 billion \\u00d7 1.89). That is above the $392 billion rung-3 boundary but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Management\\u2019s separate 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, in rung 3; however, that would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion after the first three quarters and conflicts with the recent quarterly trajectory, so I give greater weight to the actuals and near-term quarterly guide. Supply constraints, China\\u2019s data-center compute exclusion, and competing accelerator plans temper the upside, while Rubin shipments and hyperscaler investment support the run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e4628\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e4594\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3115\\\", \\\"e2776\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e4060\\\", \\\"e4061\\\"]\"], [7888, \"2026-09-12\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate of $411 billion (e4333) is the market reference and sits in rung 2. NVIDIA\\u2019s separate 70% full-year growth statement is a forecast input, not the market reference.\", \"About $414.3 billion: Q1 actual of $81.62 billion plus Q2 actual of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and an estimated Q4 of about $128.5 billion by carrying the Q3 guide\\u2019s 89% year-over-year growth forward from prior-year Q4 revenue of $68 billion. This falls in rung 2.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The reported Q1 and Q2 revenue, Q3 guidance, and a Q4 estimate carrying forward the Q3 guide\\u2019s 89% year-over-year growth imply about $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion + $96.2 billion + $108 billion + ($68 billion \\u00d7 1.89). That is above the $392 billion rung-3 boundary but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Reaching rung 1 would require about $145.4 billion in Q4, or roughly 114% growth over the prior-year Q4; management\\u2019s expectation of accelerating hyperscale growth and the Vera Rubin ramp are upside support, but do not quantify that outcome. Management\\u2019s separate 70% full-year growth statement implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, but would leave only about $81 billion for Q4 after the first three quarters, inconsistent with the recent quarterly trajectory. New Australian capacity plans and AWS deployments add quantified demand potential, but they are plans spanning capacity and periods\\u2014not FY2027 NVIDIA revenue in hand. Supply constraints and the China data-center compute exclusion remain offsets.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e4283\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2776\\\", \\\"e4574\\\", \\\"e4772\\\", \\\"e4575\\\", \\\"e4241\\\"]\"], [7986, \"2026-09-13\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; rung 2.\", \"About $414.3 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus an estimated $128.5 billion for Q4 by carrying Q3\\u2019s 89% year-over-year growth rate forward against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The Q1\\u2013Q3 run-rate and a Q4 carry-forward of the Q3 year-over-year growth rate imply about $414.3 billion, also rung 2. NVIDIA\\u2019s separate 70% full-year growth statement implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, but that would require only about $81 billion in Q4 after the first three quarters\\u2014well below the $108 billion Q3 guide. I give greater weight to the quarterly results and near-term guide; the implication remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Vera Rubin production shipments and its expected Q3 contribution support the trajectory, while supply bottlenecks temper the upside. The updated AI-capex and export-control themes also lean upward, but do not quantify enough additional FY2027 revenue to cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e4628\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e4770\\\", \\\"e4771\\\"]\"], [8180, \"2026-09-14\", 2, \"Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate is in rung 2. The newer Zacks consensus of 87.4% growth applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $404.5 billion, also in rung 2.\", \"About $414.6 billion: $81.62 billion (Q1) + $96.2 billion (Q2) + $108 billion (Q3 guidance) + about $128.8 billion for Q4, calculated by carrying Q3's roughly 89.4% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate, in rung 2; the newer Zacks estimate of 87.4% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $404.5 billion, also rung 2. The quarterly evidence implies about $414.6 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, then Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion carried forward at Q3's roughly 89.4% year-over-year growth. That is above the reference but remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA's 70% full-year growth statement implies about $367 billion, but conflicts with the first three quarters and Q3 guide; the near-term reported figures and guidance are the stronger forecast inputs. Rubin shipments and demand support the trajectory, while supply bottlenecks and competing accelerator plans temper it. No shown dependency supplies an additional input.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e4594\\\", \\\"e4990\\\", \\\"e4911\\\", \\\"e1510\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\"]\"], [8330, \"2026-09-15\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; rung 2 (Bullish).\", \"About $427 billion: $81.62 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2, an estimated $113.6 billion in Q3 (the $110.16 billion guide high end increased by the reported 3.1% average beat), and about $135.5 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3\\u2019s roughly 99% year-over-year growth onto Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 2. The reported first two quarters plus NVIDIA\\u2019s Q3 midpoint guide and a Q4 growth carry-forward implied about $414 billion; the newer, quantified analyst estimate of roughly $113.6 billion for Q3\\u2014based on NVIDIA\\u2019s recent guidance-beat history\\u2014raises that run-rate estimate to about $427 billion. This is above the reference but still below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, but it conflicts with the reported quarters and Q3 outlook; supply constraints temper the upside without changing the most likely rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e5053\\\", \\\"e5054\\\", \\\"e5055\\\", \\\"e5056\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e4628\\\", \\\"e5061\\\", \\\"e5067\\\", \\\"e4770\\\", \\\"e4771\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2776\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e4060\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e5071\\\"]\"], [8504, \"2026-09-16\", 2, \"Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2.\", \"Q1 and Q2 actual revenue totaled $177.82 billion. Using the analyst's $113.6 billion Q3 estimate, based on NVIDIA's recent guidance-beat record, implies about 99% Q3 year-over-year growth; carrying that growth rate onto Q4 FY2026 revenue of about $68 billion gives roughly $135.5 billion for Q4 FY2027 and about $426.9 billion for the full year.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 2. The reported quarters and a Q3 estimate above guidance imply about $427 billion for FY2027: above the reference, but still below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Strong demand and the Vera Rubin ramp support the estimate, while supply constraints and a lower full-year growth statement temper it; neither moves the implied result across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e4594\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e5054\\\", \\\"e5055\\\", \\\"e5056\\\", \\\"e2882\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e5067\\\", \\\"e3733\\\", \\\"e3890\\\", \\\"e5129\\\", \\\"e5118\\\", \\\"e5138\\\"]\"], [8609, \"2026-09-17\", 2, \"Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e4333; also e4594) is the market reference and sits in rung 2.\", \"About $404 billion: $81.62 billion Q1 plus $96.2 billion Q2, plus a $113.6 billion Q3 estimate, plus roughly $112.2 billion for Q4 (Q4 FY2026 revenue of about $68 billion grown by analysts' 65% estimate). This is an estimate-based forecast, not a claim that the FY2027 total is already established.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The evidence implies about $404 billion: Q1 and Q2 reported revenue total $177.82 billion; using the $113.6 billion Q3 estimate and applying analysts' 65% Q4 growth estimate to Q4 FY2026 revenue of about $68 billion gives approximately $112.2 billion for Q4. That is above the $392 billion rung-3 boundary but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA's reported results, Q3 outlook and the specific Q3/Q4 estimates support this run-rate. The stated 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, but would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion after the first three quarters; it conflicts with the Q4 growth estimate and management's expectation of accelerating hyperscaler growth. Supply constraints temper the upside, but do not bring the implied total below $392 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e4594\\\", \\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e5056\\\", \\\"e5072\\\", \\\"e5162\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e5061\\\", \\\"e5067\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2776\\\"]\"], [8810, \"2026-09-18\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus is $411 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.82 billion. Applying the recent average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of Q3 guidance gives about $113.6 billion for Q3; applying analysts' 65% Q4 growth estimate to FY2026 Q4 revenue of $68 billion gives about $112.2 billion, for roughly $403.6 billion total.\", \"The reference is Wall Street consensus of $411 billion, which is rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is about $404 billion, also rung 2: it is below the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion but above the rung 3 boundary of $392 billion. NVIDIA's 70% full-year growth outlook would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, but that would require a markedly lower Q4 than the 65% Q4 growth estimate; the estimate and management's stated Q4 acceleration support the higher quarterly run-rate. Supply constraints and long channel lead times temper the outlook, but no quantified reduction or cancellation changes that calculation.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e5054\\\", \\\"e5055\\\", \\\"e5056\\\", \\\"e5072\\\", \\\"e5162\\\", \\\"e3890\\\", \\\"e4283\\\", \\\"e5067\\\", \\\"e5205\\\", \\\"e5207\\\"]\"], [8928, \"2026-09-19\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus is about $411 billion, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $398 billion: add FY2027 Q1 actual revenue of $81.62 billion, Q2 actual revenue of $96.2 billion, and the Q3 guidance midpoint of $108 billion, then apply the 65% Q4 year-over-year growth expectation to an estimated FY2026 Q4 base of about $68.1 billion (about $112.4 billion).\", \"The market reference is Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion, which sits in rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is about $398 billion: it is below rung 1's $431.2 billion threshold but above rung 3's $392 billion threshold. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook points to about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, but that would require only about $81 billion in Q4 after the first three quarters' actuals and guidance\\u2014roughly 19% growth against the prior-year Q4\\u2014well below the specific 65% Q4 growth expectation. Supply constraints and competition temper the outlook, but no quantified revision or cancellation shifts the estimate below $392 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e3346\\\", \\\"e5162\\\", \\\"e5204\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e5067\\\", \\\"e3733\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e4314\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2882\\\"]\"], [9014, \"2026-09-20\", 2, \"Wall Street consensus is about $411 billion for FY2027, in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $367 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $404 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, an estimated Q3 of about $113.6 billion (the cited 3.1% historical beat applied to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance), and Q4 of about $112.2 billion (65% growth on Q4 FY2026's $68 billion actual).\", \"The market reference is Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion, which is rung 2. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion (rung 3), but the current-quarter guidance and the specific Q4 growth estimate imply a higher outcome: about $404 billion. That is above rung 3's $392 billion boundary but below rung 1's $431.2 billion threshold. Supply constraints and competition temper the upside; the evidence does not imply enough additional revenue to cross rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e111\\\", \\\"e2649\\\", \\\"e2652\\\", \\\"e5054\\\", \\\"e5055\\\", \\\"e5056\\\", \\\"e5072\\\", \\\"e2452\\\", \\\"e5162\\\", \\\"e4333\\\", \\\"e5204\\\", \\\"e3280\\\", \\\"e4273\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e5067\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e2882\\\"]\"]], [\"What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 431200000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 392000000000.0, 431200000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 352800000000.0, 392000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 313600000000.0, 352800000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 313600000000.0]]]\n[\"old-1\", \"v15\", [[972, \"2026-07-15\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility of nearly $20 billion; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to below $20 billion).\", \"Nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, based on management\\u2019s stated visibility. The figure is close to, but below, $20 billion, placing it in rung 3.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. That direct FY2027 outlook implies revenue close to, but below, the $20 billion rung-2 boundary; the $200 billion TAM statement provides upside context but does not quantify NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 revenue or share, so it does not justify crossing the boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e560\\\", \\\"e561\\\"]\"], [1139, \"2026-07-16\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to less than $20 billion).\", \"Approximately just under $20 billion: management\\u2019s direct FY2027 visibility is the quantified anchor. The planned 13,750 CPUs add potential upside, but with no deployment date or price stated, they do not support a defensible dollar adjustment.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The new 13,750-CPU deployment plan is supportive, but it has no stated date or price from which to derive additional FY2027 revenue; the first blade is scheduled only for an initial testbed. These facts do not establish an amount above the $20 billion rung-2 boundary, so the likely outcome remains just below it.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e560\\\", \\\"e561\\\", \\\"e611\\\", \\\"e618\\\"]\"], [1246, \"2026-07-19\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; that reference sits in rung 3 because it is just below $20 billion.\", \"Nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, using management\\u2019s direct FY2027 visibility statement as the forecast. The other evidence indicates potential demand and planned CPU deployments but supplies no date, price, or volume-in-hand basis for adding a quantified amount.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary and therefore in rung 3. The additional deployment and demand evidence supports the opportunity, but does not provide a dated, priced FY2027 revenue increment that would move the forecast to rung 2. The broad $200 billion TAM is market potential, not FY2027 revenue; the initial testbed and undated deployment plan do not establish enough in-year shipments to change the rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e560\\\", \\\"e561\\\", \\\"e611\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e743\\\", \\\"e744\\\"]\"], [1332, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3, below $20 billion.\", \"Just under $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, following management's FY2027-specific visibility. The undated $20 billion standalone-business target and deployments without a disclosed FY2027 revenue contribution do not establish an amount at or above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3. The FY2027-specific visibility remains the strongest quantified forecast. The separate $20 billion standalone CPU-business target has no target period, while the announced deployments have no date or disclosed revenue contribution; they do not imply enough additional FY2027 revenue to cross into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e561\\\", \\\"e755\\\", \\\"e611\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e743\\\", \\\"e560\\\", \\\"e744\\\"]\"], [1463, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue; this is just below $20 billion and therefore rung 3.\", \"Around, but below, $20 billion for FY2027: management's period-specific visibility anchors the estimate. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million Vera shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion of Vera revenue in calendar 2026, but does not revise the total FY2027 figure or establish that total revenue falls below $15 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3. Wolfe Research's estimate implies about $6.5 billion from 1.3 million Vera CPUs at approximately $5,000 each in calendar 2026, a meaningful lower cross-check, but it is an estimate for Vera shipments rather than a revised estimate of total FY2027 CPU revenue. Management's FY2027-specific visibility remains the strongest forecast input, and production, deliveries, and planned deployments support revenue arriving in the period. The separate $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period, while disclosed deployment volumes lack dates or revenue contributions sufficient to establish a crossing of the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e561\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e611\\\", \\\"e819\\\", \\\"e861\\\", \\\"e755\\\", \\\"e821\\\"]\"], [1573, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the reference and sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion).\", \"Near, but below, $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on management\\u2019s FY2027-specific visibility statement. Production and customer-deployment evidence supports delivery within the period; the separate Wolfe estimate implies about $6.5 billion from 1.3 million Vera CPUs at roughly $5,000 each, but is not a total-FY2027 revenue forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027, which sits in rung 3, just below rung 2\\u2019s $20 billion threshold. The evidence still implies revenue near, but below, $20 billion: the visibility statement is the strongest FY2027-specific forecast, while production, deliveries and planned deployments support revenue arriving in the period. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion from those shipments, but it is a calendar-2026 Vera estimate, not a revised estimate of total FY2027 CPU revenue. The $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period, and the new AMD throughput comparisons are a competitive risk without a quantified FY2027 revenue impact. Neither shifts the implied total across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e561\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e819\\\", \\\"e861\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e611\\\", \\\"e755\\\", \\\"e807\\\", \\\"e809\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\"]\"], [1685, \"2026-07-23\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the reference; it sits just below $20 billion and maps to rung 3.\", \"About $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue: NVIDIA\\u2019s forecast of $20 billion in standalone Vera CPU sales for calendar 2026 is the closest quantified forecast, and the begun production and deliveries support carrying it into the overlapping FY2027 period. That implies roughly the rung-2 boundary, not the $25 billion needed for rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion, which sits just below the $20 billion threshold in rung 2 and therefore maps to rung 3. The newer company forecast of $20 billion in standalone Vera CPU sales for calendar 2026 is a stronger, threshold-level forecast; production and customer deliveries have begun, and OpenAI plans large-quantity deployments from Q3. I therefore put FY2027 total CPU revenue at about $20 billion, crossing into rung 2, but not high enough to support rung 1. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for those shipments and tempers confidence, but it is a calendar-2026 Vera estimate rather than a revised FY2027 total-CPU forecast. The $20 billion standalone-business target has no period and is not counted as additional FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e561\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e755\\\"]\"], [1781, \"2026-07-24\", 2, \"No separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the principal reference and sits just below the $20 billion threshold, rung 3; the company\\u2019s $20 billion calendar-2026 standalone-CPU expectation sits at rung 2\\u2019s threshold.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027. The company\\u2019s calendar-2026 standalone-CPU expectation is at $20 billion, and the FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion; customer deliveries, production, and planned large-quantity deployments support a forecast at the threshold, though not materially above it.\", \"The market reference is not separately supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the clearest reference, just below $20 billion and therefore rung 3; its stated $20 billion calendar-2026 standalone-CPU expectation is at the rung 2 threshold. The implied FY2027 outcome is about $20 billion: the calendar-year expectation and FY2027 visibility are closely aligned, and production, customer deliveries, and planned large-quantity OpenAI deployments support reaching the threshold. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit shipment estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for those shipments, and AMD\\u2019s performance claims are material execution and competition risks, but neither overturns the more direct company revenue outlook. The evidence supports rung 2, narrowly, not rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e561\\\", \\\"e611\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e819\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e861\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e964\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1051\\\"]\"], [1854, \"2026-07-25\", 2, \"No separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the company reference and sits in rung 3 (below $20 billion); its separate $20 billion calendar-2026 CPU-sales target sits at rung 2.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027, based on NVIDIA\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 visibility and its $20 billion calendar-2026 CPU-sales target, with production and customer deployment evidence supporting the ramp; Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for that shipment estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3; the separate $20 billion calendar-2026 CPU-sales target is at rung 2. The implied FY2027 outcome is about $20 billion: the company\\u2019s direct visibility and calendar-year target anchor the forecast, while production, customer deliveries, and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support the ramp. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for those shipments, a material counterweight, but it does not outweigh the more direct company outlook. The estimate is near the rung boundary, but the combined evidence favors reaching $20 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [1923, \"2026-07-26\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the closest direct reference and sits in rung 3, below the $20 billion boundary. The separate $20 billion calendar-2026 CPU-sales expectation is at the rung-2 boundary.\", \"About $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue: NVIDIA\\u2019s nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility, together with its $20 billion CPU-sales expectation and the CEO\\u2019s standalone-CPU clarification, implies an outcome at or around the rung-2 threshold.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits just below $20 billion and therefore in rung 3. The implied outcome is about $20 billion, favoring rung 2: NVIDIA separately anticipates $20 billion in calendar-2026 CPU sales, and its CEO clarified that the $20 billion figure is for standalone CPU. Production, named customer deliveries, and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support the ramp. Wolfe Research\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million CPUs at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for those shipments, a material counterweight, but it does not outweigh the more direct company outlook or establish that it covers all CPU revenue. AMD\\u2019s reported performance advantages and companies designing their own CPUs are additional downside risks, but are not quantified as reductions to NVIDIA\\u2019s forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e1035\\\"]\"], [2158, \"2026-07-27\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d; because \\u201cnearly\\u201d is below $20 billion, this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027. The direct FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, while the separate $20 billion standalone CPU outlook and evidence of production, deliveries, and planned deployments support a forecast around the threshold; Wolfe Research\\u2019s shipment-and-price estimate implies roughly $6.5 billion for its forecast shipments, not necessarily the full year\\u2019s total CPU revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits just below the $20 billion boundary, in rung 3. The implied outcome is about $20 billion: management also described a $20 billion standalone CPU sales outlook, while full production, deliveries to named customers, and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support a substantial FY2027 ramp. Wolfe Research\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for those shipments, a material counterweight, but it does not establish that those are all CPU shipments or cover all FY2027 CPU revenue. The forecast therefore remains around the $20 billion threshold and, on balance, crosses into rung 2. The new production and delivery evidence strengthens the ramp case, while competitive performance and market-share risks remain downside factors.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e561\\\", \\\"e611\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e755\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e819\\\", \\\"e861\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e964\\\", \\\"e978\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1098\\\", \\\"e1099\\\"]\"], [2311, \"2026-07-28\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d; this is below $20 billion and sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $20 billion, with the balance of the evidence favoring at least $20 billion: management cited nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue and separately said it was on track for $20 billion of CPU sales in calendar 2026. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million CPUs at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for those forecast shipments, but does not establish total FY2027 CPU revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which is just below the $20 billion boundary and therefore rung 3. The forecast is around $20 billion and favors crossing that boundary: management also said it was on track for $20 billion of CPU sales in calendar 2026, while the CEO clarified that the nearly $20 billion FY2027 figure is for standalone CPU. The ramp evidence supports material sales, although Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit shipment estimate at about $5,000 per CPU implies only about $6.5 billion for those shipments, and AMD\\u2019s reported performance advantages temper the upside. The shipment estimate does not establish that it covers all FY2027 CPU revenue; on balance, the management outlook makes rung 2 the most likely outcome.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e964\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e755\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1098\\\"]\"], [2427, \"2026-07-29\", 2, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d is the reference; because it is just under $20 billion, it sits in rung 3.\", \"Around $20 billion, with a slight tilt above the boundary: management's FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, while its $20 billion calendar-2026 CPU-sales outlook and evidence of production, deliveries, and planned deployment support crossing into rung 2. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-unit forecast multiplied by about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for those forecast shipments, a significant but not conclusive counter.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits just below the $20 billion boundary, in rung 3. The most likely outcome is around $20 billion and slightly favors crossing into rung 2: management also said it was on track for $20 billion in CPU sales in calendar 2026, and the June production and delivery evidence plus OpenAI's planned Q3 deployment support a real ramp. The main counter is Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-unit shipment forecast at about $5,000 per CPU, implying roughly $6.5 billion for those shipments; that is a meaningful constraint, but it does not by itself establish total FY2027 CPU revenue, and it is outweighed by management's direct visibility and sales outlook. AMD's reported performance advantages and established market position temper the upside, but do not make a below-$20 billion outcome more likely than a slight threshold crossing.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e561\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e964\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e861\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1051\\\"]\"], [2537, \"2026-07-30\", 5, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d is the reference; because it is just under $20 billion, it sits in rung 3.\", \"About $6.5 billion, derived from Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera CPU shipment forecast for calendar 2026 multiplied by its approximately $5,000 average selling price. Calendar 2026 covers nearly all of FY2027, ending January 2027, and the evidence provides no quantified January bridge.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits just below $20 billion and therefore in rung 3. The strongest quantified shipment-based cross-check is Wolfe Research\\u2019s forecast of about 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each, implying roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026. That is a proxy for nearly all of FY2027, which ends in January 2027; the evidence gives no quantified January bridge. Management\\u2019s $20 billion outlook and the production and deployment evidence are meaningful upside, but the $20 billion figure requires roughly 4 million CPUs at that ASP, and the supplied evidence does not show that volume in hand. The implied outcome is below the $10 billion boundary, so the forecast is rung 5.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e611\\\"]\"], [2627, \"2026-07-31\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue is the reference; nearly $20 billion falls in rung 3 ($15 billion to less than $20 billion).\", \"Near, but below, $20 billion for FY2027, placing the forecast in rung 3. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera CPU shipment estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility and production/customer deployment evidence imply a substantial ramp above that estimate; the evidence does not support assuming the roughly 4 million units needed to reach $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits in rung 3, just below the $20 billion rung-2 boundary. I forecast revenue near that level but below $20 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility and the stated $20 billion standalone-CPU target are supported by Vera entering full production, deliveries to customers, and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment. However, Wolfe Research\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million 2026 shipments at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, far short of the roughly 4 million CPUs needed to generate $20 billion at that price. That shipment estimate warrants discounting the unshipped-volume portion of the target, but the production and deployment evidence supports a ramp beyond the shipment estimate alone; the evidence does not make at least $20 billion the most likely outcome or justify moving below $15 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e964\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e819\\\", \\\"e861\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e618\\\"]\"], [2676, \"2026-08-01\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d is the reference and sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, near the lower edge of rung 2. This is supported by management\\u2019s direct FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion and its separate statement that it is on track for $20 billion in CPU sales this year; the 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but does not by itself represent a full-FY2027 run-rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. I forecast about $20 billion, at the lower edge of rung 2: management also says it is on track for $20 billion in CPU sales this year and clarifies that the FY2027 visibility is for standalone CPU. Production, customer deliveries, planned large-quantity deployment and signed-up cloud deployers make that level more credible, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies only about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and competition is a material execution risk. The $6.5 billion estimate is not a full-FY2027 run-rate, and the direct FY2027 visibility plus the $20 billion sales statement support crossing the boundary, though only narrowly.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e808\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e964\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1563\\\"]\"], [2724, \"2026-08-03\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d sits just below $20 billion, in rung 3. Its newer FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion sits at rung 2.\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, using NVIDIA\\u2019s direct guidance of around $20 billion in Vera sales for this fiscal year. This is a forecast at the rung 2 floor, not an annualization of the separate calendar-2026 shipment estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d just below the $20 billion boundary and therefore rung 3; the newer guidance of \\u201caround $20 billion\\u201d sits at rung 2. I forecast approximately $20 billion, so the implied outcome reaches rung 2\\u2019s lower boundary but not rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion threshold. Production, customer deliveries, and signed-up cloud deployers support execution at that scale. The Wolfe estimate of 1.3 million CPUs at about $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that is a different period and is not carried forward as a flat FY2027 estimate. Competitive performance evidence is a downside risk, but does not outweigh the direct FY2027 guidance.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e861\\\", \\\"e819\\\"]\"], [2867, \"2026-08-06\", 2, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance is around $20 billion, at rung 2's lower boundary; its earlier visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d was just below that boundary.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, using NVIDIA's around-$20-billion FY2027 guidance rather than annualizing the Wolfe calendar-2026 shipment estimate. Production and customer/deployment evidence support execution, but do not establish a figure at or above $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2's $20 billion lower boundary; the earlier \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility was just below it. I forecast approximately $20 billion: the current-period guidance is the strongest input, and production, customer deliveries, planned Q3 deployments, and signed-up cloud customers support execution, though the disclosed deployments do not independently quantify revenue at that scale. This reaches rung 2 but does not imply the $25 billion needed for rung 1. The 1.3 million-unit, roughly $5,000 estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not FY2027 revenue carried forward flat; competitive performance claims are downside risks but do not quantify a reduction to guidance. The updated 2026 AI-capex view supports demand, without establishing a higher CPU revenue figure.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e611\\\", \\\"e861\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e1639\\\"]\"], [2906, \"2026-08-07\", 2, \"NVIDIA's guidance is around $20 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, using NVIDIA's period-matched guidance directly; the delivery and deployment evidence supports execution but does not quantify an amount above that guide.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027, at rung 2's $20 billion lower boundary. The implied outcome remains approximately $20 billion: the FY2027 guidance is the strongest and most directly period-matched input; production, customer deliveries, and planned deployments support execution, but the disclosed deployment quantities do not establish revenue above guidance. The 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not a flat FY2027 forecast, and the longer-term $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period. Nothing supplied quantifies an upside to $25 billion or a reduction below $20 billion. NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance therefore supports rung 2, not rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e755\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e1639\\\"]\"], [2972, \"2026-08-09\", 2, \"NVIDIA guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2, at its $20 billion lower boundary.\", \"Approximately $20 billion in FY2027. NVIDIA\\u2019s around-$20-billion FY2027 guidance is the direct period-matched anchor; the separately reported 1.3 million Vera units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and do not justify annualizing a flat run-rate or lifting the FY2027 estimate above guidance.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2\\u2019s $20 billion lower boundary. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion: the FY2027 guidance is the strongest period-matched input, while production, deliveries, and newly disclosed cloud commitments support execution but do not quantify revenue above guidance. The 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026; it is not a flat FY2027 forecast. No supplied quantified revision or deployment volume moves the implied outcome across the $25 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e611\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e819\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e861\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e964\\\", \\\"e978\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1098\\\", \\\"e1099\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e1589\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e1639\\\"]\"], [3220, \"2026-08-11\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2.\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, anchored to NVIDIA\\u2019s period-matched guidance and nearly $20 billion visibility. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million calendar-2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per CPU implies roughly $6.5 billion for that calendar year, not a flat annualized FY2027 forecast; the disclosed customer deployments do not quantify an increase beyond the guide.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits in rung 2. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion: the explicit FY2027 guide and management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility are the strongest period-matched inputs. Production, deliveries, and signed customer deployments support execution, but the disclosed deployments do not establish revenue above the guide. Competitive evidence and the absence of China data-center compute revenue also do not imply a quantified downward revision. The implied figure remains below rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion threshold and within rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e755\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1639\\\"]\"], [3286, \"2026-08-12\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in CPU sales; this reference sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"Roughly $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on the explicit FY2027 guide and management\\u2019s nearly-$20-billion visibility. The disclosed deployment and shipment evidence supports the guide, but does not establish revenue at or above $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits in rung 2. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion: the explicit FY2027 guide and nearly-$20-billion visibility are the strongest period-matched inputs, while signed deployments and expected second-half rack shipments support delivery but do not quantify revenue above the guide. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit shipment forecast at about $5,000 per CPU implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is an older third-party forecast for Vera shipments, not a revision to total FY2027 CPU revenue. Competitive performance claims and the absence of China data-center compute revenue are risks, but do not establish a quantified cut. The implied figure remains below rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion threshold and within rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1639\\\"]\"], [3404, \"2026-08-13\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion is the reference and sits in rung 2. No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027. The FY2027 guidance and nearly-$20-billion visibility anchor the estimate; production, customer deliveries and signed deployments support the ramp but are not sized to imply an outcome above that level.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits in rung 2; no separate consensus figure is supplied. The implied outcome remains about $20 billion: the FY2027 guidance and nearly-$20-billion visibility are consistent with that level, while full production, customer deliveries and signed deployments support a material ramp. Those deployments and expected second-half rack shipments are not sized, however, and do not imply revenue above $20 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per CPU implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a third-party estimate for a different period and does not establish a cut to FY2027. Competitive evidence is a downside risk, but supplies no quantified FY2027 revision. The evidence therefore does not move the forecast across the $20 billion or $25 billion rung boundaries. No dependency conclusions are supplied.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1099\\\", \\\"e1051\\\"]\"], [3563, \"2026-08-15\", 2, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion is the reference and sits in rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027, based on the company's guidance and nearly-$20-billion visibility. Deployment commitments and anticipated second-half rack shipments support the guided scale, but are unsized and do not substantiate a figure at or above $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits in rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence still implies about $20 billion: the FY2027 guidance and management's nearly-$20-billion visibility anchor the forecast, while signed deployments and expected second-half CPU-rack shipments support demand but are not sized enough to justify an increment. Competitive pressure and the absence of China data-center compute revenue are offsets, but do not quantify a lower FY2027 outcome. The updated hyperscaler capex themes support upside demand, but provide no basis to raise the CPU forecast to the $25 billion rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1639\\\"]\"], [3603, \"2026-08-16\", 2, \"NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027 is the reference; it sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Around $20 billion for FY2027. The direct FY2027 guidance and management's nearly-$20-billion visibility establish that level; the disclosed deployments and expected second-half rack shipments support it, but provide no quantified increment.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits in rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The implied outcome remains around $20 billion: the FY2027 guidance and management's nearly-$20-billion visibility anchor the forecast, while signed deployments and expected second-half CPU-rack shipments support delivery but are not sized enough to justify moving above that level. The announced addressable market is not a revenue forecast, and competitive pressure and no China data-center compute revenue are offsets, but none quantifies a lower FY2027 outcome. Thus the evidence does not imply crossing either the $20 billion or $25 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e1589\\\", \\\"e1639\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [3761, \"2026-08-17\", 2, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion is the reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, anchored by the company's around-$20-billion guidance and nearly-$20-billion visibility. The 1.3 million CY2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that shipment forecast, but it does not supersede the direct FY2027 guidance or imply revenue above $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion: management's FY2027 visibility and guidance anchor the forecast, while signed customer deployments and expected second-half CPU-rack shipments support execution. That points to the $20 billion boundary, not the $25 billion threshold. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million CY2026 shipment forecast at about $5,000 per CPU implies roughly $6.5 billion for that calendar-year shipment estimate, a meaningful scale check, but it does not replace the more direct FY2027 guidance. AMD's reported performance comparisons add competitive risk, while no quantified revision to the FY2027 guidance establishes a lower total.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1639\\\"]\"], [3901, \"2026-08-18\", 2, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance is around $20 billion, placing the reference in rung 2.\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, anchored by NVIDIA's around-$20-billion FY2027 Vera-sales guidance and nearly-$20-billion CPU-revenue visibility. Signed-up deployments and expected second-half CPU-rack shipments support delivery, but do not establish revenue above $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits in rung 2. The evidence still implies approximately $20 billion: the specific FY2027 guide is reinforced by management's nearly $20 billion visibility and customer/deployment evidence. Wolfe's 1.3 million CY2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per CPU implies roughly $6.5 billion for that calendar-year shipment estimate, but it is a third-party forecast for a different period and does not outweigh the FY2027 guidance. The evidence does not imply the additional $5 billion needed to reach rung 1; competitive comparisons add risk but do not quantify a revision to the guide.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e755\\\", \\\"e959\\\"]\"], [4035, \"2026-08-19\", 2, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027, anchored by the around-$20-billion Vera sales guide and clarified as standalone CPU revenue. The deployment commitments and expected second-half shipments support that figure, while Wolfe's 1.3 million CY2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that different period and tempers, but does not replace, the FY2027 guide.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guide of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2. The implied outcome remains about $20 billion: new named deployment commitments and expected second-half rack shipments support execution of the guide, but none is sized to justify the additional $5 billion needed for rung 1. Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion from 1.3 million CY2026 CPUs at roughly $5,000 each, and AMD's performance comparisons are downside risks; neither is a quantified FY2027 guide revision, so the evidence does not move the forecast below rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [4152, \"2026-08-21\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera sales for FY2027 is the reference; that corresponds to rung 2.\", \"Around $20 billion for FY2027, anchored by the company\\u2019s guide and nearly $20 billion revenue visibility. Production and planned deployments support the run-rate, but the 13,750-CPU Noetra deployment is only about $69 million at Wolfe\\u2019s roughly $5,000 ASP, and the broader commitments are unsized.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guide of around $20 billion, which is rung 2. The implied outcome remains around that level: production, planned Q3 deployments and signed customer plans support execution, while the larger server-CPU market outlook is supportive but does not size NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 revenue. Moving to rung 1 requires at least $25 billion, and no quantified revision or disclosed volume supports the additional $5 billion. Competitive performance claims and Wolfe\\u2019s estimate are risks, but do not outweigh the FY2027 guide and execution evidence enough to move below rung 2. The new evidence changes the supporting facts, not the rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e618\\\", \\\"e755\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1098\\\", \\\"e1099\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e2412\\\", \\\"e2413\\\", \\\"e2414\\\", \\\"e2415\\\"]\"], [4198, \"2026-08-22\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera sales for FY2027 (e1590), corroborated by management\\u2019s stated visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue (e899), sits at rung 2.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on the direct FY2027 sales guidance and management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility, rather than extrapolating a quarterly run-rate. The shipment estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but is a third-party estimate for a different period and does not displace the FY2027 guidance.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2\\u2019s $20 billion lower boundary. Production, deliveries and planned customer deployments support execution, while AMD\\u2019s larger server-CPU market estimate expands the potential market but does not quantify NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 share. Wolfe\\u2019s shipment estimate and competitive-performance claims temper the upside. The evidence supports about $20 billion, but supplies no quantified FY2027 revision or volume that would take revenue to the $25 billion needed for rung 1; nor does it outweigh the guidance enough to imply less than $20 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e2412\\\", \\\"e2413\\\", \\\"e2415\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [4261, \"2026-08-23\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera sales for FY2027 is the reference; that figure is at rung 2\\u2019s $20 billion lower boundary.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027, using NVIDIA\\u2019s around-$20-billion guidance and nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility. The 1.3 million-unit calendar-2026 shipment forecast at about $5,000 per CPU implies roughly $6.5 billion for that calendar year, but it is not a FY2027 estimate and does not displace the company\\u2019s FY2027 guidance.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, at rung 2\\u2019s $20 billion lower boundary. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion: full production, deliveries, customer deployments, and the company\\u2019s guidance support execution, while no quantified FY2027 revision or volume lifts the estimate to the $25 billion needed for rung 1. Competitive-performance evidence tempers the upside, but does not outweigh the guidance enough to imply less than $20 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e959\\\", \\\"e755\\\", \\\"e2414\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e2415\\\", \\\"e1051\\\", \\\"e2412\\\", \\\"e2413\\\", \\\"e1589\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1098\\\", \\\"e1099\\\"]\"], [4454, \"2026-08-24\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera sales for FY2027 is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"About $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, anchored by NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance and its nearly $20 billion visibility statement. The unsized deployment plans do not establish additional revenue sufficient to imply at least $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, at rung 2\\u2019s lower boundary. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion, not the $25 billion required for rung 1: the company\\u2019s quantified FY2027 outlook is not raised by the newly announced but unsized customer plans, while competition tempers the upside. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit forecast at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a third-party forecast for a different period and does not displace the direct FY2027 guidance. The newer customer commitments support execution, but their disclosed quantities do not justify adding enough revenue to cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e2457\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e821\\\"]\"], [4587, \"2026-08-25\", 2, \"NVIDIA's guidance is around $20 billion for FY2027, at rung 2.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027, based on NVIDIA's around-$20-billion guidance and nearly-$20-billion visibility. No disclosed amount for the newly named deployments justifies adding enough to reach $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU-revenue guidance of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2's $20 billion lower boundary. The evidence still implies about $20 billion: the quantified FY2027 outlook is reinforced by management's nearly $20 billion visibility statement, while the disclosed customer deployments are not sized to support a move to the $25 billion rung; competition is an offset, but does not outweigh the direct outlook.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e2457\\\", \\\"e618\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e1038\\\"]\"], [5094, \"2026-08-26\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU/Vera outlook is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).\", \"Around or slightly above $25 billion: roughly $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera revenue, plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on the reported trailing-12-month basis, carried forward into FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2. The evidence implies total CPU revenue around or slightly above $25 billion: the roughly $20 billion FY2027 figure is clarified as standalone CPU revenue, while NVIDIA separately reported Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that existing Grace revenue run-rate into FY2027 puts the total at the rung 1 boundary or above. This is a close forecast, not an established FY2027 result: the Grace figure is historical, and the Vera customer commitments are not sized enough to justify a higher estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\"]\"], [5299, \"2026-08-27\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the market/company reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion or more: the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, with the Grace run-rate carried into FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. The evidence implies total CPU revenue of about $25 billion or more: the Vera outlook is for standalone CPU, while NVIDIA separately reported Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that Grace run-rate into FY2027 puts the estimate at or above the $25 billion rung-1 boundary. This remains a forecast, not an established FY2027 result; deliveries, scale shipments and customer demand support the Vera outlook, but the disclosed customer quantities do not independently establish the full revenue figure.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3176\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e808\\\"]\"], [5659, \"2026-08-28\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the reference; that figure alone sits in rung 2, Bullish.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU revenue plus a separate Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion, placing the forecast in rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook, which is rung 2. For this total-CPU question, add the separate Grace CPU revenue run-rate: NVIDIA reported more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that run-rate alongside the Vera outlook implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. Vera is now shipping at scale and Oracle disclosed plans for hundreds of thousands of units, which support execution; the FY2028 more-than-doubling expectation is supportive context but does not itself raise the FY2027 estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e2697\\\"]\"], [5929, \"2026-08-30\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue at a reported trailing-12-month run-rate exceeding $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion. The Vera outlook is supported by shipments beginning at scale and customer deployment plans; carrying the separately reported Grace run-rate alongside it crosses the rung-1 boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook, which is rung 2. I treat that as distinct from Grace: management clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU, while Grace CPU revenue is separately reported. Current scale-shipping evidence and customer deployment plans support using the Vera outlook, and the reported Grace run-rate puts the implied total above $25 billion. Competitive-performance claims and Wolfe\\u2019s earlier shipment estimate temper confidence, but do not outweigh the current FY2027 outlook and newer shipment/customer evidence.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3004\\\", \\\"e3617\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e940\\\"]\"], [6169, \"2026-08-31\", 1, \"No independent consensus figure is supplied. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the reference, at rung 2.\", \"About $25 billion or more: NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook plus Grace CPU revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. The evidence implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion: the $20 billion standalone figure is separate from Grace, whose trailing-12-month revenue already exceeded $5 billion; carrying that Grace run-rate into FY2027 puts the total at or above rung 1's $25 billion threshold. Vera shipments at scale and Oracle's stated plan for hundreds of thousands of CPUs support the standalone ramp. AMD's competitive claims and Wolfe's earlier shipment estimate temper the forecast, but do not outweigh the current guidance and disclosed deployments.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [6389, \"2026-09-01\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook is the market/company reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). The company characterized the $20 billion as standalone CPU revenue.\", \"More than $25 billion for FY2027: take the approximately $20 billion standalone Vera outlook and add Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion, carrying that Grace run-rate into FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2. The evidence implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion: the $20 billion Vera/standalone CPU outlook is separate from Grace, whose trailing-12-month revenue exceeded $5 billion; carrying that Grace run-rate forward puts the combined figure above rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary. Competitive-performance claims and Wolfe\\u2019s earlier shipment estimate temper the forecast, but do not outweigh the company\\u2019s outlook and the evidence of production, shipments, and customer deployments.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e3058\\\"]\"], [6605, \"2026-09-03\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).\", \"Just over $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue, whose trailing-12-month run-rate already exceeded $5 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. The implied total is just over $25 billion: carry the $20 billion Vera outlook into FY2027 and add Grace, whose trailing-12-month CPU revenue already exceeded $5 billion; NVIDIA clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU, so these are separate contributions. Production and shipments at scale, customer demand, and quantified Oracle deployment plans support taking the Vera outlook substantially at face value. AMD's performance comparisons and Wolfe's older shipment forecast temper confidence, but do not outweigh the current outlook and deployment evidence or pull the implied total below rung 1's $25 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e1038\\\"]\"], [6822, \"2026-09-04\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera standalone-CPU outlook is the relevant company reference; $20 billion sits in rung 2. Management clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU, distinct from Grace CPU revenue.\", \"Approximately $25 billion or more for FY2027: roughly $20 billion from Vera standalone CPU sales, plus Grace CPU revenue carried at a run-rate consistent with its reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion.\", \"The market/company reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera standalone-CPU outlook, which is rung 2. The implied total is about $25 billion or more: carry the roughly $20 billion Vera outlook forward and add Grace, whose trailing-12-month CPU revenue already exceeded $5 billion. The disclosed Vera shipments and customer deployments support the outlook, while the competitive evidence tempers confidence but does not pull the implied total below the $25 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e3617\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1054\\\"]\"], [6925, \"2026-09-05\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion or more: take the roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera outlook and add Grace CPU revenue, whose trailing-12-month figure was already above $5 billion, as a baseline contribution.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. The implied total is about $25 billion or more: carry the roughly $20 billion Vera outlook forward and include Grace, whose reported trailing-12-month CPU revenue already exceeded $5 billion. The new deployment and shipment evidence supports the Vera ramp, while AMD's performance claims and Vera's stated legacy-workload trade-off are downside considerations; neither changes the implied total enough to cross below the $25 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e2457\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3004\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e3176\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [7107, \"2026-09-06\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU sales outlook is the reference; that amount is rung 2.\", \"At least approximately $25 billion: $20 billion of guided FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU sales plus more than $5 billion of Grace CPU revenue on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as a run-rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook of about $20 billion for Vera/standalone CPU sales, which sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). The implied total is at least about $25 billion: carry forward the $20 billion Vera guidance and add Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month CPU revenue of more than $5 billion. NVIDIA clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU, so Grace is additive; shipments and named deployments support the Vera ramp. The implied figure reaches rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e808\\\"]\"], [7399, \"2026-09-09\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook; this reference is in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion or more: carry forward the roughly $20 billion Vera/standalone CPU outlook and add Grace revenue at its reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. I infer total CPU revenue of about $25 billion or more by carrying that Vera outlook forward and adding Grace\\u2019s reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue; NVIDIA\\u2019s clarification that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU supports treating Grace separately. The older Wolfe estimate implies about $6.5 billion of Vera shipments at its stated ASP (1.3 million \\u00d7 $5,000), a downside signal, but it predates the refreshed company outlook and the subsequent evidence of shipments at scale and substantial customer deployments. The implied total reaches rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary, though the estimate is close to that boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e3617\\\"]\"], [7599, \"2026-09-10\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the supplied market/company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion or somewhat more: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera outlook forward and add Grace\\u2019s greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue at its reported run-rate, treating the figures as additive because NVIDIA clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2. Adding Grace\\u2019s reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue to the roughly $20 billion Vera outlook implies about $25 billion or somewhat more in total CPU revenue, just reaching rung 1\\u2019s boundary. The additive treatment is supported by NVIDIA\\u2019s clarification that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU. AMD\\u2019s performance comparisons and Wolfe\\u2019s earlier shipment estimate are downside signals, but they do not outweigh the current outlook and evidence of production, shipments, and customer demand. This is a forecast at a narrow threshold, not a claim that FY2027 revenue is already established.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [7760, \"2026-09-11\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion or somewhat more: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera outlook forward and add Grace\\u2019s reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue as a run-rate contribution. This reaches rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion threshold, though the figure is a forecast rather than an established FY2027 result.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. The forecast for total CPU revenue is about $25 billion or somewhat more: the $20 billion standalone Vera outlook plus a roughly $5 billion Grace revenue run-rate. That puts the estimate at the rung 1 boundary. Production, shipments, and disclosed customer demand support the Vera volume outlook; AMD\\u2019s performance comparisons and Wolfe\\u2019s earlier shipment estimate are downside signals, but do not outweigh NVIDIA\\u2019s current outlook and Grace\\u2019s reported revenue. This remains a forecast, not a claim that FY2027 revenue is already established.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3176\\\", \\\"e3004\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e2457\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [7887, \"2026-09-12\", 1, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU sales guidance of about $20 billion is the relevant reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $20 billion of guided FY2027 Vera revenue plus Grace revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies slightly more than $25 billion in total CPU revenue, assuming no unquantified Grace step-down. This places the forecast in rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of about $20 billion for Vera/standalone CPU sales, which sits at rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The implied total is slightly above $25 billion: about $20 billion of Vera revenue plus Grace revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried into FY2027 absent evidence of a quantified step-down. That crosses the $25 billion rung-1 boundary. The inference is supported by production at scale and disclosed customer deployments, while AMD's performance claims and the stated legacy-workload trade-off are downside risks; neither supplies a quantified revision to the FY2027 guidance or Grace run-rate. This is a forecast, not a claim that FY2027 revenue is already established.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3004\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [7991, \"2026-09-13\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU expectation is the reference and sits at rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $20 billion of Vera/standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue carried forward from NVIDIA's reported trailing-12-month run-rate of more than $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue slightly above $25 billion. That clears rung 1's $25 billion boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU expectation, which sits at rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The forecast crosses into rung 1 because that Vera figure is distinct from Grace CPU revenue, which NVIDIA reported at more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. AMD's performance comparisons and the legacy-workload trade-off are downside risks, but they do not quantify a revision to either revenue input.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e2697\\\"]\"], [8184, \"2026-09-14\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU sales expectation; this reference is in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of guided Vera/standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue at a trailing-12-month run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of about $25 billion or more. That reaches rung 1's $25 billion threshold.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU sales expectation, which sits at rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The forecast is at least about $25 billion: the $20 billion Vera expectation plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, treated as distinct from standalone Vera CPU revenue. Actual Vera shipments, shipping-at-scale statements and disclosed customer deployments support taking the Vera guide seriously. Wolfe's older 1.3 million-unit, roughly $5,000 ASP estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a downside check, but it is superseded as the stronger current forecast input by NVIDIA's guide and later shipment/deployment evidence. AMD's performance comparisons and the legacy-workload trade-off are risks but do not quantify a revenue reduction sufficient to move the forecast below the rung-1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3004\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [8328, \"2026-09-15\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation is the available reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Approximately $20 billion in total FY2027 CPU revenue: NVIDIA\\u2019s nearly $20 billion total-CPU visibility and subsequent $20 billion-level Vera outlook anchor the forecast, while the more-than-$5 billion Grace figure is trailing-12-month revenue and is not established as incremental to that total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, which sits in rung 2; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The evidence supports substantial delivery against the Vera outlook, but it does not establish a likely FY2027 total above $25 billion. NVIDIA has described nearly $20 billion as total CPU revenue and separately reiterated an approximately $20 billion Vera-sales outlook. Grace\\u2019s more-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue is material, but it is historical and does not quantify an additional FY2027 contribution on top of the company\\u2019s total-CPU outlook; legacy-workload trade-offs and AMD\\u2019s performance claims also temper an assumption that it simply carries forward as incremental revenue. Customer deliveries, shipment-at-scale statements, and deployment plans support the $20 billion-level forecast, leaving the implied total in rung 2 rather than rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1048\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e3617\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e4805\\\", \\\"e5050\\\", \\\"e806\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e2457\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3004\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e3176\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e4806\\\"]\"], [8500, \"2026-09-16\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation is the reference and sits in rung 2; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"About $20 billion in FY2027 total CPU revenue: management\\u2019s total-CPU visibility is nearly $20 billion and its Vera outlook is around $20 billion. Grace\\u2019s more-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue is not a separately stated FY2027 increment, so it is not added in full to that outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, which sits in rung 2; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The strongest direct outlook remains near $20 billion for total CPU revenue and around $20 billion for Vera, while shipments have begun and Oracle disclosed a plan for hundreds of thousands of CPUs. Grace\\u2019s more-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month figure is material, but it is historical, not a quantified FY2027 amount additive to the company\\u2019s total-CPU outlook. The customer and shipment evidence strengthens confidence in delivery, but does not support a likely total of at least $25 billion; AMD\\u2019s comparative-performance claims are a counterweight.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e899\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3004\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e5094\\\"]\"], [8615, \"2026-09-17\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the available reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $25 billion or somewhat more: the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, clarified as such, plus an approximate $5 billion Grace contribution based on its disclosed trailing-12-month revenue scale. Grace\\u2019s FY2027 contribution is not separately quantified, so the total is a forecast rather than a reported or guaranteed amount.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, which sits in rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The newer clarification that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU, together with the disclosed Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, makes total FY2027 CPU revenue of roughly $25 billion or somewhat more the most likely outcome if Grace continues at approximately its current annual scale. Vera is already shipping at scale and Oracle disclosed a deployment of hundreds of thousands of CPUs, supporting the standalone outlook. That estimate reaches rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary, though Grace\\u2019s FY2027 contribution is not separately guided and the outcome is therefore close to the boundary. AMD\\u2019s performance claims and Wolfe\\u2019s lower 2026 shipment estimate are counterweights, but do not outweigh the company\\u2019s FY2027 outlook and shipment evidence.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e808\\\", \\\"e810\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e899\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1563\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e1901\\\", \\\"e2457\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3004\\\", \\\"e3005\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e3176\\\", \\\"e3617\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e4805\\\", \\\"e4806\\\", \\\"e5049\\\", \\\"e5050\\\", \\\"e5093\\\", \\\"e5094\\\", \\\"e5095\\\", \\\"e5171\\\"]\"], [8815, \"2026-09-18\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, clarified as standalone/Vera CPU revenue, is the relevant reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion: carry forward the roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera outlook and add roughly $5 billion for Grace, using its reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion as an approximate run-rate rather than assuming a separately guided FY2027 amount.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion). The evidence implies about $25 billion in total CPU revenue: carry the roughly $20 billion Vera outlook, then add an approximately $5 billion Grace contribution based on Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue exceeding $5 billion. That is a run-rate estimate, not a separately guided FY2027 Grace figure, so the forecast is close to rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary. The approximately $20 billion estimate for the total server CPU market is a caution, but it does not outweigh NVIDIA\\u2019s product-specific outlook and the reported Grace run-rate; the shipment and competitive evidence adds execution uncertainty without shifting the most likely estimate below the boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e5094\\\", \\\"e808\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3004\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1038\\\", \\\"e1039\\\"]\"], [8927, \"2026-09-19\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook; this reference is in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion: the roughly $20 billion Vera/standalone CPU outlook plus approximately $5 billion for Grace, using NVIDIA's reported trailing-twelve-month Grace revenue as a run-rate estimate for FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. The evidence implies about $25 billion: roughly $20 billion of Vera/standalone CPU revenue plus a roughly $5 billion Grace revenue run-rate. The Grace figure is trailing-twelve-month revenue, so this is an estimate rather than a separately guided FY2027 amount; it places the forecast at rung 1's $25 billion boundary. Confirmed scale shipments and large customer deployments support delivery of the Vera outlook, while the estimated $20 billion total server CPU market and competitive-performance claims are meaningful cautions but do not outweigh the product-specific outlook and Grace run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2474\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e5049\\\", \\\"e5094\\\"]\"], [9029, \"2026-09-20\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the market reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion for FY2027: roughly $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue, with the reported more-than-$5-billion Grace trailing-12-month revenue used as an approximate annual run-rate contribution. The sum reaches rung 1's $25 billion threshold, though the Grace carry-forward and resulting boundary estimate are uncertain.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. The outcome implied is about $25 billion: roughly $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus a roughly $5 billion Grace CPU revenue run-rate. The Grace figure is trailing-twelve-month revenue, so carrying it into FY2027 is an estimate, not a separate FY2027 guide. The $20 billion standalone clarification supports adding it to the Vera outlook; Grace workload trade-offs and NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion estimate for the total server CPU market are meaningful downside checks, but the reported Grace run-rate, Vera shipments at scale, and disclosed customer deployments leave the most likely outcome at or just above rung 1's $25 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1049\\\", \\\"e1590\\\", \\\"e2694\\\", \\\"e2697\\\", \\\"e2788\\\", \\\"e3057\\\", \\\"e3058\\\", \\\"e3060\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e5049\\\", \\\"e5094\\\", \\\"e821\\\", \\\"e940\\\", \\\"e1038\\\"]\"]], [\"How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 25000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 20000000000.0, 25000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 15000000000.0, 20000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 10000000000.0, 15000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 10000000000.0]]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\narm=\\\"old-3\\\"\np=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nfor vn in [3,14]:\n v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n rows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n import json\n print(json.dumps([arm,v,rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"[\"old-3\", \"v3\", [[67, \"2026-07-02\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts expect $392 billion in FY2027; that sits at the lower boundary of rung 2.\", \"$392 billion for FY2027, using the analyst estimate as the reference because no other evidence is supplied.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street analysts\\u2019 $392 billion FY2027 estimate, which is the lower boundary of rung 2. No other evidence is supplied to move the forecast away from that reference, so the implied outcome remains $392 billion and does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\"]\"], [98, \"2026-07-03\", 1, \"Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion in NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; that reference is at rung 2, whose lower boundary is $392 billion.\", \"Applying the reported 85% year-over-year growth rate to the cited $253 billion trailing-12-month revenue base implies approximately $468 billion ($253 billion \\u00d7 1.85), above the $431.2 billion rung 1 threshold.\", \"The market reference is $392 billion for FY2027, the lower boundary of rung 2. The latest reported 85% year-over-year revenue growth is materially above the roughly 55% growth from the cited $253 billion trailing-12-month base to that reference; carrying that current growth rate forward implies about $468 billion, above rung 1's $431.2 billion threshold. The $1 trillion Blackwell and Vera Rubin sales expectation spans multiple products and periods, so it supports upside but is not treated as FY2027 total revenue; the unspecified CPU sales period and the unquantified Palantir deal do not establish additional revenue for this fiscal year.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e35\\\", \\\"e36\\\", \\\"e37\\\"]\"], [125, \"2026-07-04\", 1, \"Wall Street analysts estimate $392 billion of FY2027 revenue (e25), the rung 2 threshold/reference.\", \"Applying the reported 85% year-over-year growth (e35) to the cited $253 billion trailing-12-month revenue base (e25) implies approximately $468 billion for FY2027.\", \"The market reference is $392 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The latest reported 85% year-over-year growth, applied to the cited $253 billion trailing-12-month revenue base, implies about $468 billion; that exceeds the $431.2 billion rung 1 boundary. Wall Street's 82% FY2027 growth projection is consistent with the $392 billion reference, but the reported growth rate is modestly higher. The $1 trillion Blackwell and Vera Rubin sales expectation spans products and periods, so it is not treated as FY2027 total revenue; the unspecified-period CPU sales target and unquantified deal do not establish additional FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e35\\\", \\\"e36\\\", \\\"e37\\\", \\\"e45\\\", \\\"e46\\\", \\\"e48\\\"]\"], [147, \"2026-07-05\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s $392 billion FY2027 estimate is the market reference and sits at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $468 billion: carry the reported 85% Q1 FY2027 year-over-year growth forward on the cited $253 billion trailing-12-month revenue base ($253B \\u00d7 1.85). The second-half data-center compute projection is 20% above consensus, but its share of total FY2027 revenue is not supplied, so it is not added as a company-wide uplift.\", \"The market reference is $392 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The reported 85% year-over-year Q1 FY2027 growth, carried forward on the cited $253 billion trailing-12-month revenue base, implies about $468 billion ($253B \\u00d7 1.85), above rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion threshold. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s forecast of 20% above consensus for second-half FY2027 data-center compute adds upside, but the evidence does not give that segment\\u2019s share of full-year total revenue, so it cannot be translated into a further company-wide figure. Rubin\\u2019s planned shipments and the broader sales targets support demand but do not quantify FY2027 revenue. The implied outcome remains rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e35\\\", \\\"e48\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e36\\\", \\\"e65\\\", \\\"e46\\\", \\\"e45\\\", \\\"e67\\\"]\"], [280, \"2026-07-06\", 1, \"Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is $392 billion, placing the reference at rung 2 (Bullish).\", \"About $493 billion, derived by carrying forward the approximately 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 FY2027 guidance on the supplied $253 billion trailing-12-month revenue base ($253B \\u00d7 1.95). This exceeds the $431.2 billion rung 1 threshold.\", \"The market reference is $392 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2; the cited 82% analyst growth expectation is also below the latest company-guided Q2 growth rate. Using the supplied $253 billion trailing-12-month revenue base and carrying forward the approximately 95% year-over-year growth in NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 guidance gives an implied run-rate of about $493 billion ($253B \\u00d7 1.95), above rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. Q1's reported 85% growth supports a high-growth trajectory, while SemiAnalysis's quantified H2 data-center compute upside is supportive but cannot be translated directly into total company revenue. China-market and competition risks are counterweights, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to bring the implied figure below $431.2 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e48\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e114\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e36\\\", \\\"e46\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e158\\\", \\\"e118\\\", \\\"e119\\\", \\\"e117\\\"]\"], [353, \"2026-07-07\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s $392 billion FY2027 estimate is the reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"Using the supplied $253 billion trailing-12-month revenue base and carrying forward the approximately 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 FY2027 guidance gives a run-rate proxy of about $493 billion ($253B \\u00d7 1.95). Applying the analysts\\u2019 82% growth projection instead gives about $461 billion; both exceed rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary.\", \"The market reference is $392 billion, which is rung 2. The guided-growth run-rate proxy is above rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary, and even applying the analysts\\u2019 lower 82% growth projection to the supplied trailing-12-month base remains above that boundary. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% H2 upside is for data-center compute, not total revenue, so it supports the direction but cannot be applied as a company-wide uplift. China-market substitution and competition are risks, but the evidence does not quantify an FY2027 reduction large enough to move the implied outcome below rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e48\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e46\\\", \\\"e184\\\", \\\"e118\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e168\\\", \\\"e189\\\", \\\"e190\\\"]\"], [434, \"2026-07-08\", 1, \"Analyst expectations of about $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027; this is rung 2.\", \"The FY2026 revenue base of $215.9 billion carried forward at the Q2 FY2027 guided 95% year-over-year growth rate implies about $421 billion. The additional 20% H2 data-center compute upside is not a total-revenue uplift, but its likely contribution makes a low-to-mid-$430-billion outcome\\u2014and therefore at least $431.2 billion\\u2014more likely.\", \"The reference is about $392\\u2013393 billion, which sits in rung 2. Carrying FY2026 revenue forward at the 95% year-over-year growth rate in the Q2 FY2027 guide implies about $421 billion, below rung 1 on its own. But SemiAnalysis\\u2019s specific forecast puts second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus. That applies only to part of NVIDIA\\u2019s revenue, not the whole company; nevertheless, the required increment over the guide-based run-rate is only about $10 billion, making a total above $431.2 billion more likely than not. China substitution and competition are counterweights, but do not outweigh the current guide and H2 tracking signal.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e97\\\", \\\"e114\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e209\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e183\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e168\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e36\\\", \\\"e59\\\"]\"], [504, \"2026-07-09\", 1, \"Analysts expect about $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027; that reference is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion carried forward at the roughly 95% growth implied by the Q2 FY2027 guide implies about $421 billion. Applying the 20% above-consensus H2 signal only to data-center compute\\u2014not to total revenue\\u2014makes a total above $431.2 billion, likely in the low-$430 billions or higher, the more likely outcome.\", \"The reference is analysts\\u2019 roughly $392\\u2013393 billion estimate, which sits at rung 2. Carrying FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion forward at the roughly 95% year-over-year growth implied by the Q2 guide gives about $421 billion. The H2 SemiAnalysis signal applies only to data-center compute, not total revenue, but the prior-period evidence indicates that the uplift needed to clear $431.2 billion is about $10 billion; the above-consensus shipment tracking and new hyperscaler-sales and rack-pricing evidence make crossing that boundary more likely than not. China substitution, GPU-capacity concerns and memory costs are counterweights, but do not outweigh the growth and supply signals.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e251\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e226\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e246\\\"]\"], [562, \"2026-07-10\", 1, \"Analyst consensus of about $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027; that reference is rung 2.\", \"Approximately $440 billion: carry the roughly 95% growth implied by the Q2 guide forward from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion for a roughly $421 billion run-rate, then add a second-half uplift from SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20%-above-consensus data-center compute projection, applying it only to compute rather than total revenue.\", \"The reference is analyst consensus of about $392\\u2013393 billion, which sits at rung 2. The latest quarter and next-quarter guide imply a roughly $421 billion FY2027 run-rate if the approximately 95% guided growth is carried across FY2026 revenue, rather than annualizing one quarter flat. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus lifts that run-rate: applying the premium to the compute portion only, rather than all revenue, implies roughly $440 billion. That clears rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. The upside is supported by strong hyperscaler sales and higher expected Rubin rack pricing; China substitution and memory-cost concerns are offsets, but do not reduce the forecast below the boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e226\\\", \\\"e230\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e246\\\", \\\"e251\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e264\\\", \\\"e277\\\", \\\"e298\\\", \\\"e311\\\"]\"], [574, \"2026-07-11\", 1, \"Analyst consensus is about $392\\u2013393 billion (including $392.7 billion), placing the reference at rung 2.\", \"Roughly $440 billion: $215.9 billion of FY2026 revenue carried forward at the approximately 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance implies about $421 billion, then SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% second-half premium is applied only to the data-center compute portion. The $20 billion Vera CPU target is discounted because the evidence does not show that the volume is already in hand.\", \"The reference is analyst consensus of about $392\\u2013393 billion, which sits at rung 2. The reported FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion and Q2 FY2027 guidance of $91 billion, up about 95% year over year, support roughly $421 billion if that guided growth is carried across the FY2026 base. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus lifts the total-revenue implication to roughly $440 billion when applied to compute revenue only, not to all revenue. That clears rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s $20 billion Vera CPU sales target is an additional upside input, but is discounted because shipments and volume in hand are not established. China substitution, custom silicon and memory-cost pressures are offsets, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to put the forecast below the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e158\\\", \\\"e168\\\", \\\"e184\\\", \\\"e189\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e246\\\", \\\"e251\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e270\\\", \\\"e277\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e73\\\"]\"], [645, \"2026-07-12\", 1, \"Wall Street consensus is approximately $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $440 billion: carrying Q2 FY2027's guided roughly 95% year-over-year growth across the $215.9 billion FY2026 base implies about $421 billion, then applying SemiAnalysis's 20% above-consensus estimate only to second-half data-center compute adds roughly $19 billion.\", \"The reference is Wall Street consensus of about $392\\u2013393 billion, which sits at rung 2. The reported FY2026 revenue base of $215.9 billion and Q2 FY2027 guidance of $91 billion, up about 95% year over year, imply roughly $421 billion if that guided growth carries across the FY2026 base. SemiAnalysis's estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus adds roughly $19 billion when applied to that compute component only, implying about $440 billion overall\\u2014above rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. Strong Q1 results and shipment tracking support the high case; competition and potential demand or supply constraints are offsets, but none supplies a quantified revision large enough to bring the estimate below the boundary. The $20 billion CPU target and $1 trillion multi-year Blackwell/Rubin visibility are upside indicators, not amounts added in full: neither establishes FY2027 product volume in hand.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e168\\\", \\\"e218\\\", \\\"e229\\\", \\\"e246\\\"]\"], [732, \"2026-07-13\", 1, \"Analyst consensus of about $392\\u2013393 billion; this sits at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion carried forward at the 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance implies about $421 billion for FY2027. Applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% upside only to the second-half data-center-compute consensus component adds roughly $19 billion, implying about $440 billion total.\", \"The reference is analyst consensus of about $392\\u2013393 billion, which is rung 2. Carrying the 95% growth in Q2 guidance across FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion gives about $421 billion; SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus adds roughly $19 billion on the data-center-compute component, not on total revenue, taking the implied total to about $440 billion. That exceeds the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion. The new capex-theme update adds support, while China exclusion, memory constraints, and custom-chip competition are offsets; none provides a quantified revision that pulls the forecast below the boundary. The CPU-sales target and multi-year processor visibility are discounted because they are targets spanning products and periods, not demonstrated FY2027 volume in hand.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e208\\\", \\\"e393\\\", \\\"e392\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e417\\\", \\\"e413\\\", \\\"e303\\\", \\\"e329\\\"]\"], [858, \"2026-07-14\", 1, \"Analyst consensus is about $392\\u2013393 billion for FY2027, placing the market reference at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"Carry Q2\\u2019s guided 95% year-over-year growth across FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion to imply about $421 billion for FY2027, then add roughly $19 billion for the approximately 20% second-half FY2027 data-center compute upside to consensus, yielding about $440 billion.\", \"The reference is analyst consensus of about $392\\u2013393 billion, which sits at rung 2. The forecast remains above rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary: carrying the 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance across FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $421 billion for FY2027; the inherited SemiAnalysis estimate of second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus adds roughly $19 billion to total revenue, not 20% of total revenue, for an implied figure of about $440 billion. The Q1 result grew 85.2% year over year and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, up about 95%, supporting the strong growth run-rate. Chinese H200 purchase clearances and the updated export-controls theme add possible upside, but the reported very small number of shipments and uncertainty over Chinese import authorization do not justify a quantified increase. China substitution, custom-chip competition and memory-cost pressure are offsets, but no supplied evidence quantifies a revision large enough to pull the estimate below $431.2 billion. Multi-year platform visibility and the Vera CPU sales target are discounted because they span products or periods and do not establish FY2027 revenue in hand.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e168\\\", \\\"e246\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e481\\\", \\\"e482\\\", \\\"e483\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e499\\\"]\"], [993, \"2026-07-15\", 1, \"Analyst consensus is about $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027 (e25, e209, e221), placing the reference at rung 2.\", \"About $440 billion: $215.9 billion of FY2026 revenue carried forward at the roughly 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance implies about $421 billion; adding roughly $19 billion for SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% second-half FY2027 data-center compute premium over consensus gives approximately $440 billion (e208, e98, e60).\", \"The market reference is approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion, which sits at rung 2. The evidence-based estimate remains about $440 billion: FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion carried forward at the roughly 95% year-over-year growth rate in Q2 guidance implies about $421 billion, and the SemiAnalysis estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus adds roughly $19 billion to total revenue, as in the prior calculation. Rubin\\u2019s scheduled fall shipments and management\\u2019s supply-delivery statement support execution, while the $20 billion CPU outlook and $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility are not added in full because the evidence does not establish FY2027 shipment volume or the revenue share attributable to this fiscal year. The implied figure is above rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. China remains a two-sided factor: the policy theme now forecasts authorization, but the reported very small number of H200 shipments and NVIDIA\\u2019s zero-China Q2 guidance do not justify adding an unquantified amount.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e558\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e481\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e156\\\"]\"], [1146, \"2026-07-16\", 1, \"Analyst estimates of about $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027; this sits at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $440 billion: $215.9 billion of FY2026 revenue carried forward at the approximately 95% year-over-year growth indicated by Q2 guidance implies about $421 billion, with roughly $19 billion added for SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% above-consensus second-half FY2027 data-center compute projection.\", \"The market reference is about $392\\u2013$393 billion, which is rung 2. The evidence-implied estimate is about $440 billion: applying the roughly 95% year-over-year growth indicated by Q2 guidance to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion gives about $421 billion; the SemiAnalysis estimate for second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue implies roughly another $19 billion above consensus, taking the estimate to about $440 billion. That uplift is confined to data-center compute, a subset of total revenue, rather than applied to NVIDIA\\u2019s whole business. The new Noetra project specifies 27,500 Rubin GPUs, but gives no delivery timing or revenue amount, so it supports demand without justifying a further quantified addition. The estimate remains above the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e576\\\", \\\"e582\\\", \\\"e634\\\", \\\"e653\\\", \\\"e658\\\"]\"], [1220, \"2026-07-17\", 1, \"Analyst expectations of about $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027 (e25, e221), which is rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.\", \"About $436 billion: carrying Q2\\u2019s approximately 95% year-over-year guided growth across the remaining FY2026 base yields about $417 billion for FY2027; adding the previously derived roughly $19 billion SemiAnalysis-related uplift for second-half data-center compute yields about $436 billion.\", \"The market reference is approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion, which is rung 2. Carrying the roughly 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance forward against the remaining FY2026 quarterly base gives a FY2027 run-rate of about $417 billion: Q1 FY2026 is about $44.1 billion, Q2 about $46.7 billion, leaving about $125.2 billion for H2; growing that H2 base by 95% gives about $244.1 billion, alongside reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion and guided Q2 revenue of $91 billion. The SemiAnalysis estimate of H2 FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus was previously assessed as implying roughly $19 billion of incremental total revenue; applying that uplift gives about $436 billion. That subset-specific uplift is not applied to all revenue. The $436 billion estimate is above the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion, though only by about $4 billion. The Rubin/Noetra volume and CPU-sales visibility are supportive but lack enough timing or pricing detail to add separately without risking double-counting.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e658\\\"]\"], [1254, \"2026-07-18\", 1, \"Analyst expectations of about $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027 put the reference at rung 2.\", \"About $436 billion: the Q2-guided growth run-rate implies about $417 billion, with the previously assessed roughly $19 billion total-revenue contribution from SemiAnalysis's H2 data-center compute estimate above consensus. The newly disclosed Japan Rubin project has roughly $2.3\\u2013$2.7 billion of rack-equivalent value by the disclosed price and capacity comparables, but its FY2027 delivery share is unknown, so it is not added in full.\", \"The reference is the roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 analyst estimate, which sits at rung 2. The implied estimate remains about $436 billion: carrying the roughly 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance across the remaining FY2026 quarterly base gives about $417 billion, and SemiAnalysis's estimate of 20% above consensus for second-half FY2027 data-center compute was previously assessed as adding about $19 billion to total revenue\\u2014not 20% to all revenue. New evidence adds support but does not justify a further quantified uplift: NVIDIA reports $119 billion of supply commitments, and a Japan project specifies 27,500 Rubin GPUs. Using the disclosed $6\\u2013$7 million Rubin rack pricing and a 72-GPU rack as a rough comparator implies about $2.3\\u2013$2.7 billion of rack-equivalent value for those GPUs; however, delivery timing is unstated, and that project may overlap broader commitments and visibility, so it is not added in full to FY2027. This leaves the estimate above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Memory-supply risk is a counterweight, while the current guidance and other supplied evidence do not quantify a revision large enough to move the estimate below that boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e653\\\", \\\"e658\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e481\\\", \\\"e483\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e311\\\"]\"], [1351, \"2026-07-20\", 1, \"Analyst estimates of about $392 billion for FY2027 (also reported as $393 billion) place the market reference at rung 2.\", \"Approximately $436 billion: carrying the roughly 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance across the remaining FY2026 quarterly base implies about $417 billion, and SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate of 20% above consensus for second-half FY2027 data-center compute was assessed as adding about $19 billion to total revenue.\", \"The FY2027 analyst reference of about $392\\u2013$393 billion sits at rung 2. The implied outcome is about $436 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion; carrying the roughly 95% guided year-over-year growth across the remaining FY2026 quarterly base gives approximately $417 billion for FY2027. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s specific estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus was previously assessed as adding about $19 billion to total revenue, taking the implied result to roughly $436 billion. That is about $4.8 billion above the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion. The newly reported 2027 memory-supply constraint is a downside risk, while UAE export-tier easing and the disclosed Rubin project support demand; none supplies a quantified revision large enough to change that calculation. The CPU target and Rubin project are not added again, since the target is not evidence of full volume in hand and the project spans products whose FY2027 revenue share is unspecified.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e734\\\", \\\"e736\\\", \\\"e780\\\", \\\"e781\\\"]\"], [1471, \"2026-07-21\", 1, \"Market expectations are approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027 (e25, e818); that reference is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $436 billion: carry the roughly 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance across the remaining FY2026 quarterly base, producing an approximately $417 billion run-rate, then add about $19 billion for SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus.\", \"The reference is $392\\u2013$393 billion, which sits at rung 2. The implied estimate remains about $436 billion, $4.8 billion above the rung 1 boundary: the prior run-rate calculation was about $417 billion, with SemiAnalysis\\u2019s specific estimate of second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus adding about $19 billion. Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance support carrying strong growth forward. The newly stated 2027 memory shortage is a material downside risk, but it gives no NVIDIA-specific shipment reduction to subtract; NVIDIA\\u2019s on-time delivery statement and Wistron\\u2019s stated production ramp provide countervailing execution support. The announced UAE export-tier change is also supportive, though no firm NVIDIA purchase contract was reported.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e835\\\", \\\"e734\\\", \\\"e736\\\"]\"], [1575, \"2026-07-22\", 1, \"Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is about $393 billion (e818), in rung 2: at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.\", \"About $436 billion: carry forward the roughly $417 billion run-rate estimate based on the reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance, then add roughly $19 billion for SemiAnalysis\\u2019s forecast of second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus (e60, e140). The $20 billion CPU target is treated as additional upside but not added in full.\", \"The reference is approximately $393 billion, which sits at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The forecast remains about $436 billion: the carried run-rate estimate is about $417 billion, with SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate of second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus adding roughly $19 billion. Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance support strong growth, and Rubin production and Wistron\\u2019s manufacturing ramp support execution. The 2027 memory shortage and the absence of China data-center revenue in guidance are material risks, but no NVIDIA-specific shipment reduction is quantified; the $20 billion CPU target is not added in full because it is a target and may overlap with broader revenue expectations. At about $436 billion, the forecast is above the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e818\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e140\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e927\\\", \\\"e835\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e558\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e734\\\", \\\"e736\\\"]\"], [1697, \"2026-07-23\", 1, \"Wall Street's FY2027 estimate is about $393 billion (the ladder uses $392 billion); this reference sits at rung 2, from $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.\", \"About $436 billion: retain the carried run-rate estimate of about $417 billion and add roughly $19 billion for SemiAnalysis's estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus. Recent Q1 results and Q2 guidance support the run-rate; the newly reported competitive and memory risks do not provide a quantified NVIDIA-specific adjustment.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's FY2027 estimate of about $393 billion (the ladder's $392 billion estimate), which sits at rung 2. The forecast remains about $436 billion: the carried run-rate estimate is about $417 billion, with SemiAnalysis's estimate of second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus adding roughly $19 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion support continued growth, while on-time delivery and the manufacturing ramp support execution. New AMD deployments and claims about Huawei substitution add competitive risk, and the reported memory shortage is a supply risk, but none quantifies a reduction in NVIDIA shipments or revises NVIDIA's FY2027 outlook; they therefore do not justify a haircut large enough to cross below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e246\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e875\\\", \\\"e785\\\", \\\"e786\\\", \\\"e969\\\", \\\"e988\\\", \\\"e1005\\\", \\\"e1008\\\", \\\"e1009\\\"]\"], [1775, \"2026-07-24\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s FY2027 estimate is about $393 billion (the ladder\\u2019s $392 billion benchmark), which is rung 2.\", \"About $436 billion: carry forward the prior approximately $417 billion run-rate and add about $19 billion from SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate that second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus. This is above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $393 billion FY2027 estimate, equivalent to the ladder\\u2019s $392 billion benchmark and rung 2. The forecast remains about $436 billion: the carried run-rate is approximately $417 billion, with SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate of second-half FY2027 data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus adding about $19 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion support the run-rate. New AMD competition and memory-supply risks are meaningful, but no supplied evidence quantifies a reduction in NVIDIA shipments or revises its full-year outlook enough to pull the implied result below $431.2 billion. The result therefore remains in rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e576\\\", \\\"e582\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e246\\\", \\\"e969\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1036\\\", \\\"e987\\\", \\\"e988\\\", \\\"e734\\\", \\\"e736\\\", \\\"e1029\\\"]\"], [1836, \"2026-07-25\", 1, \"Wall Street's approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate; rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"Approximately $436 billion: FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion grown at the roughly 82% consensus rate implies about $393 billion; Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance support an approximately $417 billion run-rate, with SemiAnalysis's 20% second-half data-center compute upside adding about $19 billion.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits at rung 2. The implied forecast remains about $436 billion: FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion and the roughly 82% consensus growth benchmark imply about $393 billion; the Q1 result and Q2 guidance support a higher run-rate of approximately $417 billion, and SemiAnalysis's estimate of 20% above-consensus second-half data-center compute revenue supports roughly $19 billion of upside to that run-rate. The new evidence adds both support and risks: Rubin production and shipment plans, manufacturing expansion, and SK's supply initiative support delivery, while the 2027 memory shortage and AMD competition are headwinds. The $500 billion SK initiative spans years and includes two-way business, so it is not a $500 billion NVIDIA revenue addition; the evidence does not quantify a revenue reduction large enough to take the implied result below $431.2 billion. Thus the forecast remains in rung 1.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e481\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e835\\\", \\\"e875\\\", \\\"e927\\\", \\\"e969\\\", \\\"e1052\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1060\\\", \\\"e1061\\\", \\\"e1073\\\", \\\"e1086\\\", \\\"e1087\\\", \\\"e1088\\\"]\"], [1920, \"2026-07-26\", 1, \"Wall Street's approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate; this is rung 2.\", \"About $436 billion for FY2027. The $215.9 billion FY2026 base and approximately 82% consensus growth imply about $393 billion; Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion and Q2 guidance of approximately $91 billion support a higher run-rate, while SemiAnalysis's 20% above-consensus second-half data-center compute forecast adds upside. This remains above the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The market reference is roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The implied forecast remains about $436 billion: FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion and the approximately 82% consensus growth benchmark imply about $393 billion; the reported Q1 result and Q2 guidance support a higher run-rate, and SemiAnalysis's estimate of 20% above-consensus second-half data-center compute revenue supports further upside. New evidence adds support from higher hyperscaler capex expectations, Rubin manufacturing and deployments, and efforts to secure HBM supply. The 2027 memory shortage and announced AMD deployments are meaningful headwinds, but no supplied evidence quantifies a revision large enough to pull the forecast below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The SK initiative is multi-year and includes both parties' purchases, so its headline value is not counted as NVIDIA FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e785\\\", \\\"e786\\\", \\\"e804\\\", \\\"e835\\\", \\\"e969\\\", \\\"e1052\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1060\\\", \\\"e1061\\\", \\\"e1086\\\", \\\"e1087\\\", \\\"e1105\\\"]\"], [2159, \"2026-07-27\", 1, \"The analyst estimate is about $392 billion (with another cited average estimate of $393 billion), which is rung 2.\", \"Approximately $434 billion for FY2027: the Q1 actual and Q2 guidance support a strong growth run-rate, and SemiAnalysis's forecast of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus supports an outcome above the $392\\u2013$393 billion reference; the updated supply and competition risks warrant a modest haircut to the prior estimate.\", \"The roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion market reference sits at rung 2. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance show revenue growth around 85%\\u201395% year over year, while SemiAnalysis expects second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus. Those inputs support a FY2027 outcome modestly above the rung-1 cutoff. The memory-shortage outlook and AMD competition temper the estimate, but the supplied evidence does not quantify a revenue reduction large enough to put it below $431.2 billion; the SK initiative is multi-year and its headline value is not NVIDIA FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1059\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1132\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1053\\\", \\\"e1088\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1214\\\"]\"], [2323, \"2026-07-28\", 2, \"The supplied analyst estimate is $392 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence also includes analyst claims of 42% FY2027 revenue growth, but those are a conflicting third-party estimate rather than the stated ladder reference.\", \"Q1 revenue grew 85% year over year and Q2 guidance implies about 95% growth; carrying roughly 90%\\u201395% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies approximately $410\\u2013$421 billion in FY2027. The SemiAnalysis forecast is 20% above consensus for second-half data-center compute only, and the evidence does not quantify its contribution to total revenue.\", \"The reference is the $392 billion analyst estimate, which is rung 2. Q1 reported revenue growth of 85% and Q2 guidance of 95% year over year imply a much stronger near-term run-rate than the newer 42%-growth analyst claims; carrying roughly 90%\\u201395% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $410\\u2013$421 billion for FY2027. That is above the reference but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% upside forecast is only for second-half data-center compute revenue, so without a quantified contribution to total revenue it does not establish enough incremental uplift to cross that boundary. The forecast is tempered by the 42% analyst-growth claims, memory-supply constraints, and AMD competition.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1105\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1214\\\"]\"], [2451, \"2026-07-29\", 2, \"The $392 billion analyst estimate is the market reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"Approximately $421 billion: carry the 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance across FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion ($215.9 billion \\u00d7 1.95). This is above $392 billion but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is the $392 billion analyst estimate, which sits at rung 2. The evidence-based run-rate is higher than that reference but remains below rung 1: carrying Q2's guided 95% year-over-year growth across the FY2026 base of $215.9 billion implies about $421 billion for FY2027. Q1's reported 85% growth supports a very strong run-rate, while the quantified 42% growth estimate is a bearish counterpoint but conflicts with the reported result and near-term guidance. SemiAnalysis's 20% upside estimate applies only to second-half data-center compute, so it cannot be added wholesale to total revenue; the Blackwell/Rubin visibility figure spans multiple products and periods and is discounted. Updated capex themes add upside support, but the implied figure still falls about $10 billion short of the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1105\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e785\\\", \\\"e1140\\\"]\"], [2558, \"2026-07-30\", 2, \"The central market reference is the $392 billion FY2027 analyst estimate, which is rung 2; another supplied estimate is $393 billion, also rung 2.\", \"Applying the approximately 95% year-over-year growth implied by Q2 FY2027 guidance to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion gives approximately $421 billion for FY2027. That is above rung 2's $392 billion floor but below rung 1's $431.2 billion threshold.\", \"The $392 billion analyst reference sits at rung 2 (and the separate $393 billion estimate also sits there). Carrying the roughly 95% year-over-year growth implied by Q2 guidance across FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion gives about $421 billion for FY2027\\u2014above $392 billion but about $10 billion short of rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. Q1's 85% growth supports the strong run-rate. SemiAnalysis's 20% upside view applies only to second-half data-center compute; without its dollar base or compute's share of that period's revenue, it cannot be added wholesale or confidently shown to take the total over $431.2 billion. The roughly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility is a further upside input, but it is a product-line target and may overlap the guided run-rate, so it does not justify adding the full amount. The 42% growth estimate is a bearish counterpoint, but it conflicts with the reported Q1 growth and near-term guidance. The likely outcome therefore remains in rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1105\\\", \\\"e1204\\\", \\\"e558\\\", \\\"e576\\\"]\"], [2630, \"2026-07-31\", 2, \"The thesis's analyst estimate of $392 billion is the market reference and sits at rung 2; the separate $393 billion estimate also sits at rung 2. A reported alternative of 42% growth from FY2026 would imply about $306 billion (rung 5), but it conflicts with the 82% growth estimate and the $392\\u2013$393 billion estimates.\", \"Carrying the approximately 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance forward from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion gives about $421 billion for FY2027 ($215.9bn \\u00d7 1.95). That is above $392 billion but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The stated analyst reference of $392 billion (and the separate $393 billion estimate) is rung 2. Carrying Q2 guidance of approximately 95% year-over-year growth across FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $421 billion for FY2027, also rung 2: it is above the reference but about $10 billion below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Q1's 85% growth supports a high run-rate. SemiAnalysis's 20% upside view is confined to second-half Data Center compute and lacks a dollar base, so it cannot be added wholesale; the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility and CPU target are not sufficiently period-specific or incremental to establish a total above the boundary. A separate report of 42% consensus growth would imply about $306 billion, but conflicts with the supplied $392\\u2013$393 billion estimates and is not a quantified company revision. Memory-cost pressure is a downside risk, but no quantified production constraint or demand cancellation here changes the run-rate forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e1105\\\", \\\"e1237\\\", \\\"e1503\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e1052\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1132\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1036\\\"]\"], [2690, \"2026-08-01\", 2, \"Wall Street's roughly $392 billion FY2027 estimate (also reported as $393 billion) is the reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"About $410 billion for FY2027: applying approximately 90% growth, between Q1's reported 85% year-over-year growth and Q2's 95% year-over-year revenue guidance, to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion gives approximately $410 billion.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits at rung 2. The evidence implies about $410 billion: carrying approximately 90% growth\\u2014between Q1's reported 85% year-over-year growth and Q2's 95% year-over-year guidance\\u2014forward from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion gives about $410 billion. That is above the $392 billion rung-2 floor but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Morningstar's 80% growth view implies about $389 billion, while the 42% growth view implies about $307 billion; these are counterweights, but the latter conflicts sharply with the explicit revenue estimates and current-quarter guidance. SemiAnalysis's 20% upside applies only to second-half data-center compute, so it cannot be applied to total FY2027 revenue without a dollar base and product share. Memory-supply constraints are a downside risk, while higher hyperscaler spending, delivery performance, and export-policy themes support demand; neither shifts the central estimate across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e1502\\\", \\\"e1503\\\", \\\"e1105\\\", \\\"e1237\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1132\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1052\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e576\\\", \\\"e481\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1036\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\"]\"], [2722, \"2026-08-02\", 2, \"Wall Street's approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate; rung 2.\", \"Approximately $421 billion: FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion carried forward at the approximately 95% year-over-year growth rate in Q2 FY2027 guidance ($215.9 billion \\u00d7 1.95).\", \"The reference is Wall Street's approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. Carrying the approximately 95% year-over-year growth in the Q2 FY2027 guidance forward from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $421 billion. That is above rung 2's $392 billion floor but below rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. Q1's 85% growth and the 80% and 42% growth views temper confidence in the 95% run-rate; conversely, SemiAnalysis's 20% upside applies only to second-half data-center compute, so it cannot be applied to all FY2027 revenue. Multi-year product visibility and partnership figures are not all FY2027 NVIDIA revenue, while the memory-shortage outlook is a supply-side risk. The evidence therefore supports a result above consensus, but does not imply crossing the rung-1 threshold.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e1519\\\", \\\"e1105\\\", \\\"e1237\\\", \\\"e1502\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1052\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e785\\\", \\\"e987\\\"]\"], [2759, \"2026-08-03\", 2, \"Wall Street's approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate; rung 2.\", \"About $421 billion: FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion carried forward at the approximately 95% year-over-year growth rate in Q2 FY2027 guidance. The partial second-half data-center- compute upside view supports a higher tail but does not quantify a whole-company uplift sufficient to establish $431.2 billion or more.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, which sits at rung 2. Carrying the 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance forward from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $421 billion; Q1's 85% growth is consistent with that strong run-rate. SemiAnalysis's 20% upside concerns only second-half data-center compute, so it cannot be applied to total FY2027 revenue without overstating its effect. The higher-capex outlook and shipment tracking support upside, while the memory-supply warning and lower 42% growth view temper it. The evidence does not justify lifting the total-revenue estimate above rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary, so the forecast remains rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e818\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1105\\\", \\\"e1502\\\", \\\"e261\\\"]\"], [2791, \"2026-08-04\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (e25, e221), which is rung 2.\", \"About $421 billion: FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion carried forward at the roughly 95% year-over-year growth in Q2 guidance ($215.9B \\u00d7 1.95). That is below the $431.2 billion rung 1 boundary.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, at rung 2. The strongest forecast input is the $91 billion Q2 guide, nearly 95% above the year-ago quarter: carrying that guided growth across FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $421 billion for FY2027, below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion threshold. Q1\\u2019s 85% year-over-year growth supports a high run-rate. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% upside is limited to second-half data-center compute, and the evidence does not quantify its contribution to total revenue enough to carry the total above $431.2 billion. The memory-shortage warning and AMD deployments weigh against the run-rate; the SK Hynix supply arrangement and Rubin rollout provide offsets. The new evidence changes the supporting balance, but not the implied total or rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1519\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1140\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e835\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e481\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e969\\\", \\\"e1214\\\", \\\"e1502\\\", \\\"e1073\\\"]\"], [2836, \"2026-08-05\", 2, \"Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is about $392\\u2013$393 billion, at rung 2.\", \"About $421 billion: FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion carried forward at the approximately 95% year-over-year growth in the Q2 FY2027 guide ($215.9B \\u00d7 1.95).\", \"The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits at rung 2. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance support a high growth run-rate; carrying the approximately 95% year-over-year growth in the Q2 guide across FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $421 billion for FY2027. That is above the reference but about $10 billion below rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. SemiAnalysis's 20% upside forecast is limited to second-half data-center compute, so it cannot be applied as a 20% increase to total revenue; other unallocated product and multi-year targets likewise do not establish enough incremental FY2027 revenue to cross the boundary. Memory-supply concerns and AMD deployments temper the outlook, while supply assurances, Rubin production, and SpaceX demand are positive but not sized enough to change the total estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1105\\\", \\\"e1502\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e835\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1214\\\", \\\"e1036\\\", \\\"e1604\\\", \\\"e1605\\\"]\"], [2901, \"2026-08-06\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"Approximately $421 billion: carrying the Q2 FY2027 guide\\u2019s roughly 95% year-over-year growth forward from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies $215.9B \\u00d7 1.95. The newer demand and spending signals are upside support, but do not quantify enough incremental FY2027 total revenue to raise that estimate above $431.2 billion.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits at rung 2. The $91 billion Q2 FY2027 guide implies about 95% year-over-year growth; carrying that guided growth forward from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion gives roughly $421 billion for FY2027. Recent evidence of stronger hyperscaler spending, confirmed customer demand, and supply readiness supports the bullish direction, but the added demand signals are not sized as FY2027 NVIDIA revenue. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% upside applies only to second-half data-center compute, not total revenue, and the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans several years. The evidence therefore does not establish a sufficiently quantified uplift to take the implied total above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Memory constraints and some workload movement to AMD temper the upside.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1057\\\", \\\"e1059\\\", \\\"e1073\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e1214\\\", \\\"e1637\\\", \\\"e1639\\\", \\\"e1604\\\", \\\"e1605\\\"]\"], [2955, \"2026-08-07\", 2, \"Wall Street's approximately $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"Carrying the nearly 95% year-over-year growth in the Q2 FY2027 guide forward from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies approximately $421 billion for FY2027 ($215.9 billion \\u00d7 1.95). This is above the reference but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The $421 billion run-rate is above the $392\\u2013$393 billion reference, but remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s Q1 result and Q2 guide reinforce the high-growth run-rate, and supply readiness and elevated AI infrastructure spending support the upside. However, the 20% SemiAnalysis forecast applies only to second-half data-center compute, while announced capacity plans and product revenue visibility span multiple periods and do not state NVIDIA FY2027 revenue. The evidence does not provide a defensible total-revenue uplift of at least $10.2 billion above the run-rate; competition and the projected memory shortage also temper the upside. The forecast therefore remains at rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e785\\\", \\\"e969\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1036\\\", \\\"e1214\\\", \\\"e1637\\\", \\\"e1639\\\"]\"], [2995, \"2026-08-08\", 2, \"The market reference is the $392 billion FY2027 analyst estimate, placing the reference at rung 2. The separately supplied $393 billion estimate is also within rung 2.\", \"About $421 billion: carry forward the roughly 95% year-over-year growth in the Q2 FY2027 revenue guide against FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion ($215.9 billion \\u00d7 1.95); Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion supports the high-growth trajectory. This remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is $392 billion, which is rung 2; the supplied $393 billion estimate is also in rung 2. The implied FY2027 outcome remains about $421 billion: applying the roughly 95% year-over-year growth in NVIDIA\\u2019s Q2 guide to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion gives about $421 billion, and Q1\\u2019s $81.6 billion result is consistent with that high-growth trajectory. That is above the reference but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The new demand and capacity signals are supportive, but several are targets or plans without NVIDIA shipment volumes, revenue, or timing within FY2027; SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% upside applies only to second-half data-center compute, not total revenue. Memory constraints and competition are offsets, but do not quantify a reduction large enough to move the forecast below rung 2. The implied figure therefore does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1052\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1604\\\", \\\"e1605\\\", \\\"e1606\\\", \\\"e1637\\\", \\\"e1639\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1132\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1036\\\", \\\"e1613\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e558\\\"]\"], [3010, \"2026-08-09\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate is $392 billion, at rung 2.\", \"About $421 billion: apply the approximately 95% year-over-year growth in NVIDIA\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 FY2027 guidance to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion ($215.9B \\u00d7 1.95). Q1\\u2019s $81.6 billion revenue, up 85% year over year, is consistent with this growth run-rate.\", \"The reference is the $392 billion analyst estimate, which is rung 2. The evidence-implied outcome is above that reference but remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Recent customer and capacity signals support demand, but they do not quantify additional NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; memory-supply risks and competition are offsets, not quantified revisions to NVIDIA\\u2019s outlook.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e1604\\\", \\\"e1605\\\", \\\"e1637\\\", \\\"e1639\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1132\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1036\\\"]\"], [3071, \"2026-08-10\", 2, \"Analyst estimate of $392 billion; this is the rung-2 threshold and therefore the market reference sits at rung 2.\", \"FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion carried forward at the latest reported/guided growth rates of 85%\\u2013approximately 95% implies about $399\\u2013$421 billion for FY2027, with a midpoint near $410 billion.\", \"The reference is the $392 billion analyst estimate, which sits in rung 2. Carrying the latest reported/guided year-over-year growth rates\\u201485% in Q1 and approximately 95% in Q2\\u2014across FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies roughly $399\\u2013$421 billion, centered near $410 billion. That is above the reference but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The upside shipment tracking and demand/capacity signals support growth, while the Q2 guide excludes China and memory constraints are a counterweight; neither supplies a quantified full-year revision that moves the estimate across a rung boundary. Multi-year product visibility and unshipped-product targets are not treated as FY2027 revenue in full.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e208\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e261\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1605\\\", \\\"e1637\\\", \\\"e1723\\\"]\"], [3262, \"2026-08-11\", 2, \"The $392 billion analyst estimate is the reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"Approximately $400\\u2013$420 billion, centered near $410 billion: carrying the reported Q1 and guided Q2 growth through FY2027 revenue of $215.9 billion implies this range, with the $103.1 billion Q3 analyst expectation consistent with continued growth. This remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is the $392 billion analyst estimate, which is rung 2. The latest operating path remains consistent with revenue modestly above that estimate, but does not imply the $431.2 billion needed for rung 1. The new $103.1 billion Q3 figure is an analyst expectation, not company guidance; it reinforces the growth sequence without establishing a higher full-year run-rate. Supply, China and competition risks offset some of the demand and investment signals.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e73\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1214\\\", \\\"e1723\\\", \\\"e1836\\\", \\\"e1837\\\"]\"], [3348, \"2026-08-12\", 2, \"Wall Street\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate is about $392 billion, at rung 2. The reference identifies the market baseline; the forecast is derived from operating evidence rather than simply held at consensus.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion plus Q2 guidance of about $91 billion and Q3 expectations of about $103.1 billion imply about $392.5 billion for the year if Q4 grows at the Q2-to-Q3 sequential rate (about 13.3%), to roughly $116.8 billion. For an approximate adjustment, Q1 data-center revenue less $14.8 billion of networking revenue implies compute was about 74% of total revenue; applying that proxy to H2 run-rate revenue of about $220 billion and SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% H2 compute premium adds roughly $33 billion, giving a central estimate near $425 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $392 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence-implied estimate is about $425 billion: the Q1 result, Q2 guidance, and Q3 analyst expectation imply roughly $392.5 billion before an adjustment for SemiAnalysis\\u2019s forecast that H2 data-center compute revenue will exceed consensus by 20%. Applying that uplift to an estimated H2 compute share based on Q1 data-center revenue less networking revenue adds roughly $33 billion. This is bullish relative to the reference, but remains below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Rubin shipments, CPU expectations, and stronger AI investment support the upside, while memory constraints, China revenue excluded from Q2 guidance, and competitive alternatives temper it. The 20% forecast is a third-party view, and its estimated contribution is necessarily approximate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1683\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e311\\\", \\\"e1073\\\", \\\"e408\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e785\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1036\\\", \\\"e1054\\\"]\"], [3463, \"2026-08-13\", 1, \"The FY2027 analyst estimate is about $392 billion, placing the reference at rung 2 (Bullish).\", \"About $440 billion, with a reasonable range of roughly $435\\u2013$445 billion: carry forward the Q1 result, Q2 guidance, and Q3 expectation, then build on the prior roughly $425 billion estimate with the stronger capex outlook and upside-to-guidance evidence. The central estimate is above the rung 1 threshold of $431.2 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $392 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The prior run-rate estimate, including SemiAnalysis\\u2019s quantified H2 data-center compute upside, was about $425 billion\\u2014just below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. The updated 2026 hyperscaler capex outlook and an analyst view expecting meaningful upside to guidance add enough upside to put the central forecast around $440 billion. This crosses the boundary, though memory constraints and China revenue excluded from Q2 guidance limit confidence in the upside.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e1936\\\", \\\"e1940\\\", \\\"e1723\\\", \\\"e1957\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1132\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e311\\\"]\"], [3508, \"2026-08-14\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate is about $392 billion (e25, e818), which is rung 2.\", \"About $435 billion. The $392 billion reference and near-term guided run-rate are lifted by SemiAnalysis\\u2019s forecast that H2 FY2027 data-center compute revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20%; that uplift applies only to the compute portion, not all revenue. I temper the prior roughly $440 billion central estimate for the memory constraint and other execution risks.\", \"The market reference is $392 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The forecast is about $435 billion: the Q1 result, Q2 guidance and Q3 expectation support a run-rate near the reference, while SemiAnalysis\\u2019s quantified H2 data-center compute upside and the carried demand outlook lift the central estimate above it. That leaves roughly $4 billion of headroom over rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary, so the forecast remains rung 1, but narrowly; memory constraints, China revenue excluded from Q2 guidance and competing GPU deployments temper the upside.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e1683\\\", \\\"e1957\\\", \\\"e1946\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e484\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1036\\\"]\"], [3576, \"2026-08-15\", 1, \"The supplied FY2027 analyst estimate is $392 billion, placing the reference at rung 2.\", \"About $435 billion: carry the Q1 FY2027 result of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward with sequential growth consistent with the $103.1 billion Q3 expectation, then incorporate SemiAnalysis's 20% H2 data-center compute upside. This is above the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion.\", \"The reference is FY2027 revenue of $392 billion, which sits at rung 2. The forecast remains above rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary: the Q1 result and Q2 guidance, continued sequential growth implied by the $103.1 billion Q3 expectation, and SemiAnalysis's quantified 20% H2 data-center compute upside support an estimate around $435 billion. Higher hyperscaler spending and strong demand reinforce that outcome, while memory constraints, competing GPU deployments and the fact that the $500 billion SK Hynix figure spans several years limit confidence; they do not outweigh the forecast enough to cross back below $431.2 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e1936\\\", \\\"e2036\\\", \\\"e2034\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1132\\\", \\\"e785\\\", \\\"e1035\\\", \\\"e1214\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1999\\\", \\\"e2050\\\", \\\"e2048\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e835\\\"]\"], [3632, \"2026-08-16\", 1, \"The market reference is approximately $392B for FY2027 (with a separate average estimate of $393B), placing the reference at rung 2.\", \"A sequential-growth run-rate using Q1 actual revenue, Q2 guidance, the Q3 estimate, and roughly 12% Q4 sequential growth implies about $391B before the H2 upside. Applying SemiAnalysis's 20% above-consensus forecast only to the H2 data-center compute component implies a central FY2027 total near $433B.\", \"The reference is about $392B of FY2027 revenue (with another market estimate at $393B), which places expectations at rung 2. Carrying the reported Q1 result and Q2 guidance forward at the sequential growth implied by the Q3 estimate gives a base near $391B: $81.6B + $91B + $103.1B + about $115.5B for Q4. SemiAnalysis's estimate that H2 data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus implies a material uplift to that base; applied only to the H2 compute component, it supports a central estimate around $433B, above rung 1's $431.2B boundary. The estimate is only modestly above the boundary: e2089's sequential-growth scenario points to just under $400B, while HBM constraints remain a risk, but neither outweighs the quantified H2 compute upside and the positive near-term outlook.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e2003\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e1936\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e2051\\\"]\"], [3769, \"2026-08-17\", 1, \"Wall Street FY2027 revenue estimates of about $392B (also reported near $393B) place the reference at rung 2.\", \"The reported Q1, Q2 guidance, Q3 estimate, and roughly 12% sequential-growth carry-forward imply about $391B before the specific upside adjustment. Applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% estimate only to second-half data-center compute implies roughly $431B\\u2013$433B total FY2027 revenue.\", \"The market reference is about $392B (with other supplied estimates near $393B), which is rung 2. Carrying Q1 revenue of $81.6B, Q2 guidance of $91B, and the $103.1B Q3 estimate forward at roughly 12% sequential growth gives a baseline of about $391B. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% upside estimate applies only to second-half data-center compute, not total revenue; its implied uplift of roughly $40B puts the forecast around $431B\\u2013$433B, just over rung 1\\u2019s $431.2B boundary. The sub-$400B sequential-growth scenario and new reports of supply shortages are downside risks, but are not quantified enough to offset that compute-specific uplift; reports of customers pulling orders forward and Groq\\u2019s planned NVIDIA-powered expansion support demand.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e2143\\\", \\\"e2183\\\", \\\"e1936\\\"]\"], [3900, \"2026-08-18\", 1, \"Analyst estimates of about $392 billion (other supplied estimates are near $393 billion), placing the reference at rung 2.\", \"About $431\\u2013$433 billion: the quarterly run-rate baseline is roughly $391 billion, and the carried estimate of 20% upside on second-half Data Center compute implies roughly $40 billion of additional revenue. That puts the forecast just above the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is about $392 billion (with estimates near $393 billion), which is rung 2. The quarterly run-rate baseline is roughly $391 billion: Q1 reported revenue, Q2 guidance, the Q3 estimate, and about 12% sequential growth into Q4. The carried SemiAnalysis estimate of 20% upside applies to second-half Data Center compute\\u2014not total revenue\\u2014but its roughly $40 billion implied uplift puts the forecast around $431\\u2013$433 billion, just across rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. The sub-$400 billion sequential-growth case and supply-shortage reports are relevant downside risks, but the former repeats a scenario already considered and neither supplies a quantified revision that displaces the specific compute-tracking estimate. Customer orders being pulled forward and Groq's planned NVIDIA-powered capacity support demand. The updated high 2026 hyperscaler-capex conclusion also supports the upper-end outcome.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e2143\\\", \\\"e2183\\\", \\\"e2034\\\"]\"], [4048, \"2026-08-19\", 1, \"Analyst estimates of about $392 billion for FY2027, with estimates near $393 billion, are the market reference and sit at rung 2.\", \"Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the Q3 estimate of $103.1 billion, and about 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply a baseline around $391\\u2013$392 billion. Applying the carried estimate of 20% upside to second-half Data Center compute adds roughly $40 billion, implying total FY2027 revenue around $431\\u2013$433 billion.\", \"The reference is about $392 billion (with estimates near $393 billion), which sits at rung 2. The quarterly baseline remains near $391\\u2013$392 billion: Q1 reported revenue, Q2 guidance, the Q3 estimate, and about 12% sequential growth into Q4. The specific SemiAnalysis estimate of 20% upside to second-half Data Center compute, carried from the prior judgment, implies roughly $40 billion of additional revenue and puts the forecast around $431\\u2013$433 billion\\u2014just across rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. This is a forecast, not an established outcome. The sub-$400 billion sequential-growth case and supply constraints temper confidence, but do not displace the more specific compute-tracking signal. The export-control and AI-capex themes that bear on revenue mostly push toward the higher outcome.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e2334\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2296\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e2143\\\", \\\"e1054\\\", \\\"e2341\\\"]\"], [4101, \"2026-08-20\", 1, \"About $392 billion in FY2027 analyst estimates (with estimates near $393 billion), placing the reference at rung 2.\", \"Roughly $434\\u2013$437 billion: carry forward the prior $391\\u2013$392 billion quarterly baseline plus about $40 billion from the estimated 20% second-half Data Center compute upside, then add about $3\\u2013$4 billion for BofA\\u2019s $94\\u2013$95 billion Q2 forecast versus the $91 billion guide midpoint. The higher Q3 expectation is not added separately because it overlaps the second-half tracking uplift.\", \"The market reference is about $392 billion (with estimates near $393 billion), which is rung 2. The prior forecast\\u2019s roughly $391\\u2013$392 billion quarterly baseline plus SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimated roughly $40 billion of additional revenue from 20% second-half Data Center compute upside implied $431\\u2013$433 billion. BofA\\u2019s more specific $94\\u2013$95 billion Q2 estimate is about $3\\u2013$4 billion above the $91 billion guide midpoint, lifting the implied total to roughly $434\\u2013$437 billion; I do not add BofA\\u2019s higher Q3 guidance expectation separately because it overlaps the second-half tracking upside. That remains above rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. The supply-shortage evidence tempers confidence but does not quantify a reduction large enough to move the forecast below that boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e1957\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e2296\\\", \\\"e2329\\\", \\\"e2362\\\"]\"], [4186, \"2026-08-21\", 1, \"About $392 billion in FY2027 analyst estimates (including estimates near $393 billion), which is rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.\", \"Approximately $432\\u2013436 billion: applying the roughly $40 billion implied second-half Data Center compute upside from SemiAnalysis to the roughly $392 billion annual reference, with BofA's Q2 estimate adding about $3\\u20134 billion versus guidance, while avoiding a separate overlapping Q3 addition.\", \"The market reference is about $392 billion (rung 2). Starting from that annual estimate, SemiAnalysis's 20% upside applies only to second-half Data Center compute; the implied roughly $40 billion uplift puts revenue near $432 billion. BofA's $94\\u201395 billion Q2 forecast, versus NVIDIA's $91 billion guide, supports a further roughly $3\\u20134 billion, implying about $435\\u2013436 billion. I do not add a separate Q3 uplift because it overlaps the second-half tracking estimate. This leaves the forecast around $432\\u2013436 billion, just above rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. The lower alternative\\u201480% FY2027 growth, implying roughly $388 billion against the reference's approximately 82% growth, and a near-$400 billion sequential-growth scenario\\u2014tempers confidence but does not outweigh the specific second-half shipment/revenue tracking. The supply-shortage reports are a risk, but do not quantify a revenue reduction sufficient to cross back below the boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e818\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1957\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e2362\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e2296\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2334\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1132\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e1054\\\"]\"], [4222, \"2026-08-22\", 1, \"Wall Street's FY2027 revenue estimate is about $392 billion, placing the reference at rung 2.\", \"About $432\\u2013436 billion: apply SemiAnalysis's 20% upside to second-half FY2027 Data Center compute, implying roughly $40 billion above the $392 billion consensus, then include about $3\\u20134 billion for BofA's $94\\u201395 billion Q2 forecast versus the roughly $91 billion guide/consensus. This is a forecast, not an established result, and sits just above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"The market reference is about $392 billion, which is rung 2. The prior forecast's specific SemiAnalysis estimate implies roughly $40 billion of upside from second-half FY2027 Data Center compute versus consensus; adding the roughly $3\\u20134 billion gap between BofA's $94\\u201395 billion Q2 forecast and the $91 billion guide/consensus gives an implied range of about $432\\u2013436 billion, just above rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. The new Morningstar estimate of 80% FY2027 growth implies roughly $389 billion, a meaningful lower alternative, but it is less specific to the second-half compute trajectory and does not outweigh that signal alongside Q1's 85% growth and the Q2 guide. Supply constraints temper confidence, while NVIDIA's stated on-time delivery and the positive China-policy conclusion support execution and demand. The updated 2027 capex theme is a counterweight, but FY2027 is mostly exposed to calendar 2026 spending.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e2362\\\", \\\"e2296\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1132\\\", \\\"e59\\\", \\\"e1073\\\"]\"], [4298, \"2026-08-23\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate is $392 billion, placing the reference at rung 2.\", \"About $438\\u2013440 billion: start from the $392 billion FY2027 estimate, add roughly $40 billion implied by SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% second-half Data Center compute upside, and allow about $6\\u20138 billion for BofA\\u2019s Q2 and following-quarter estimates above the respective consensus figures.\", \"The FY2027 analyst estimate is $392 billion, which is rung 2. The evidence still implies an outcome above rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary: SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate of second-half FY2027 Data Center compute revenue 20% above consensus implies roughly $40 billion of upside, while BofA\\u2019s newer Q2 and following-quarter estimates are above the corresponding consensus figures. Those signals imply roughly $438\\u2013440 billion, though the lower Morningstar and sequential-growth estimates, and reported supply constraints, temper confidence. The positive China-policy theme supports the direction but is not treated as revenue evidence or assigned a separate dollar amount.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e2296\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2362\\\", \\\"e2480\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e522\\\", \\\"e2051\\\", \\\"e2334\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e2479\\\", \\\"e2379\\\", \\\"e2380\\\"]\"], [4510, \"2026-08-24\", 1, \"The FY2027 analyst estimate of $392 billion is the reference and sits at rung 2 (Bullish).\", \"Approximately $438\\u2013440 billion: the Q1 reported revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guide of $91 billion anchor the run-rate; above-consensus Q2/Q3 outlooks and the roughly $40 billion implied upside from SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20%-above-consensus second-half Data Center compute projection lift the estimate above $431.2 billion.\", \"The $392 billion FY2027 analyst estimate is the reference and sits at rung 2. The reported Q1 result, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and above-consensus estimates for Q2 and Q3 support a run-rate above that reference; carrying growth forward rather than annualizing Q1 flat gives a baseline around $400 billion. The additional SemiAnalysis estimate of second-half FY2027 Data Center compute revenue 20% above consensus implies roughly $40 billion of upside, putting the forecast around $438\\u2013440 billion\\u2014above rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. This remains a forecast, not an established FY total: lower growth/sequential estimates and memory constraints temper confidence, but do not pull the implied outcome below the boundary. The China-authorization theme also supports upside, while the capex themes support demand.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e1683\\\", \\\"e1957\\\", \\\"e2362\\\", \\\"e1812\\\", \\\"e2296\\\", \\\"e2089\\\", \\\"e2334\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e1132\\\", \\\"e2514\\\", \\\"e2607\\\", \\\"e2051\\\", \\\"e2582\\\", \\\"e2565\\\"]\"], [4665, \"2026-08-25\", 1, \"The latest supplied Wall Street FY2027 estimate is $396 billion (e2624), placing the reference in rung 2: $392 billion to below $431.2 billion.\", \"About $436 billion: start from the $396 billion FY2027 estimate and carry forward the existing roughly $40 billion uplift implied by SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half FY2027 Data Center compute revenue will exceed consensus by 20% (e60, e1116).\", \"The market reference is the latest supplied FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, which is rung 2. The $91 billion Q2 guide and roughly $103 billion Q3 consensus point to continued growth, while the specific SemiAnalysis forecast of second-half FY2027 Data Center compute revenue 20% above consensus supports an uplift beyond the reference. Carrying forward the existing estimate of roughly $40 billion of incremental revenue from that second-half compute outperformance implies about $436 billion, above rung 1's $431.2 billion boundary. This is a forecast, not an established result. Memory constraints and reports of sales being lost temper confidence, but are not quantified enough to erase the implied uplift; reported price increases are a partial offset.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2624\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e98\\\", \\\"e2465\\\", \\\"e2479\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e2682\\\", \\\"e2514\\\", \\\"e2650\\\"]\"], [5195, \"2026-08-26\", 1, \"The market reference is the $396 billion FY2027 analyst estimate, rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"Starting from the $396 billion FY2027 estimate, add about $4.0 billion for Q2 reported revenue above the $92.2 billion consensus and about $4.1 billion for Q3 guidance above the $103.9 billion consensus, giving roughly $404 billion. Carrying forward the prior estimate of about $40 billion of second-half Data Center compute upside implies approximately $444 billion.\", \"The reference is the $396 billion FY2027 analyst estimate, in rung 2. Updating that base for Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion versus the cited $92.2 billion consensus (+$4.0 billion), and Q3 guidance of $108 billion versus the cited $103.9 billion consensus (+$4.1 billion), gives about $404 billion. Carrying forward the prior conclusion\\u2019s roughly $40 billion estimate for second-half Data Center compute outperformance implies about $444 billion, above rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. Supply constraints temper confidence but are not quantified enough to offset that forecast; the $40 billion uplift remains within total revenue and does not exceed the whole.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2624\\\", \\\"e2622\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2862\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e1116\\\", \\\"e2921\\\", \\\"e3045\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e2754\\\"]\"], [5369, \"2026-08-27\", 1, \"The latest supplied FY2027 analyst estimate is $396 billion (e2624), in rung 2.\", \"About $444 billion: start with the $396 billion consensus, add approximately $4.0 billion for the Q2 beat, approximately $4.1 billion for Q3 guidance above consensus, and carry forward the prior conclusion's roughly $40 billion estimate for second-half Data Center compute outperformance.\", \"The reference is the $396 billion FY2027 analyst estimate (e2624), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The implied estimate is about $444 billion: carry the consensus forward, add roughly $4.0 billion for Q2's $96.2 billion result versus the $92.2 billion estimate, roughly $4.1 billion for Q3 guidance of $108 billion versus $103.9 billion consensus, and carry forward the prior conclusion's roughly $40 billion estimate for second-half Data Center compute outperformance. That is above the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion. This remains an aggressive forecast, not an already-proven FY outcome: Rubin shipments and purchase orders support the upside, while reported supply constraints and the ability to meet only about 70% of demand temper confidence. The implied uplift remains within total revenue and does not exceed the whole.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2624\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e2921\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e3045\\\"]\"], [5724, \"2026-08-28\", 2, \"FY2027 analyst consensus of $396 billion (e2624), which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $415 billion: add reported first-half revenue of $177.837 billion to the $108 billion Q3 guide, then estimate Q4 near $129 billion by carrying the guide\\u2019s roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate forward to the prior-year Q4 base of about $68.1 billion.\", \"The market reference is the $396 billion FY2027 consensus estimate (e2624), which sits in rung 2. The reported first-half total is $177.837 billion (e2754); adding the $108 billion Q3 midpoint and carrying its roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward to the comparable Q4 base of about $68.1 billion implies Q4 revenue near $129 billion and FY2027 revenue around $415 billion. That is above $392 billion but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The strongest evidence supports substantial growth: Q2 was $96.2 billion, up 18% sequentially (e2710), and Q3 guidance is $108 billion, also above the cited $103.9 billion consensus (e2862). The reported 70% full-year growth guidance would imply roughly $367 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion (e3343, e3332), but that would require a sharp Q4 slowdown despite the latest quarter and Q3 guide; I treat it as downside evidence, not the base forecast. Supply constraints and management\\u2019s estimate that current supply covers only 70% of demand temper the outlook, while Rubin shipments and purchase orders support continued growth. The H2 data-center forecast is positive but applies only to that revenue slice, so it does not justify carrying the whole-company forecast above rung 1. Themes in force that bear on revenue mostly push toward the first rung by indicating capex or policy conditions supportive of sales; they do not change the implied figure enough to cross the boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2624\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2862\\\", \\\"e2883\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e3301\\\", \\\"e2921\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3187\\\"]\"], [5859, \"2026-08-29\", 2, \"Latest supplied analyst consensus is $396 billion (e2624), rung 2; the ladder\\u2019s stated $392 billion reference is also rung 2.\", \"About $415 billion: $177.837 billion reported for H1 plus $108 billion guided for Q3, plus approximately $129 billion for Q4 by carrying Q3\\u2019s roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward to an estimated $68.1 billion FY2026 Q4 comparison. The estimate is above $392 billion and below $431.2 billion.\", \"The reference is the latest supplied FY2027 analyst consensus of $396 billion (e2624), which is rung 2; the ladder\\u2019s stated $392 billion estimate is also rung 2. The quarterly run-rate implies about $415 billion: NVIDIA reported $177.837 billion for the first half, guided Q3 to $108 billion, and applying the Q3 year-over-year growth rate of about 89% to an estimated FY2026 Q4 comparison of about $68.1 billion gives Q4 near $129 billion. That estimate remains above $392 billion but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. Management\\u2019s stated 70% full-year growth would imply roughly $367 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, a counterweight, but the reported first-half result and near-term Q3 guide support the higher quarterly run-rate. Rubin production shipments and purchase orders, plus AWS\\u2019s 2-million-GPU deployment plan, add upside but are not sized or confined to FY2027, so they do not justify taking the forecast through the rung-1 boundary. Supply constraints also limit the upside case.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2624\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e2883\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3045\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e2921\\\"]\"], [6002, \"2026-08-30\", 2, \"Latest supplied FY2027 analyst consensus is about $396 billion (e2624), placing the reference at rung 2; the ladder's stated $392 billion estimate is also rung 2.\", \"About $415 billion: the reported $177.837 billion first half plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, then Q4 estimated by carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward against the implied FY2026 Q4 base of about $68.1 billion. The 70% full-year growth guide implies about $367 billion instead, but would require Q4 revenue near $81 billion, below Q3.\", \"The market reference is the latest supplied FY2027 consensus of about $396 billion, which sits at rung 2; the ladder's $392 billion reference is also rung 2. The reported first-half revenue of $177.837 billion plus the $108 billion Q3 guide leaves the forecast dependent mainly on Q4. Carrying Q3's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward to the implied FY2026 Q4 base of about $68.1 billion gives Q4 near $129 billion and FY2027 revenue around $415 billion. That is above $392 billion but below the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The reported 70% full-year growth guide is a meaningful counterweight: applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, it implies about $367 billion, requiring Q4 revenue around $81 billion. However, the nearer-term Q3 guide and the reported run-rate point more strongly to the roughly $415 billion outcome; supply constraints add downside risk but do not, on the evidence shown, establish a Q4 contraction.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3396\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e3045\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e2921\\\", \\\"e60\\\"]\"], [6242, \"2026-08-31\", 2, \"Latest supplied FY2027 analyst consensus is about $397 billion, at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"Approximately $415 billion: $177.837 billion reported for the first half plus $108 billion Q3 guidance and an estimated $129 billion Q4, carrying roughly 89% Q3 year-over-year growth forward from an approximately $68.1 billion FY2026 Q4 base.\", \"The reference is the latest supplied FY2027 analyst consensus of about $397 billion, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The run-rate points above that reference but not to rung 1: FY2027 first-half revenue was $177.837 billion and Q3 guidance is $108 billion. Carrying the roughly 89% year-over-year growth embedded in the Q3 guide into Q4 implies about $129 billion for Q4: the FY2026 Q4 base is approximately $68.1 billion ($215.9 billion FY2026 revenue less $90.805 billion first-half revenue and $57.01 billion FY2026 Q3 revenue). That gives FY2027 revenue of roughly $415 billion, above $392 billion but below $431.2 billion. The CFO-attributed 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion on the FY2026 base, a meaningful downside counterweight, but it would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion after the $108 billion Q3 guide. The nearer-term reported run-rate and Q3 guidance therefore carry more weight. Supply bottlenecks temper the upside; Vera Rubin orders and shipment information support demand but do not quantify FY2027 revenue enough to take the higher rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e3726\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2883\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e3715\\\"]\"], [6457, \"2026-09-01\", 2, \"Latest supplied analyst expectation is about $397 billion for FY2027 (e3726), within rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"First-half revenue was $177.837 billion; the $108 billion Q3 guide implies roughly 89% year-over-year growth. Carrying that growth into Q4, whose FY2026 comparison is about $68.1 billion ($215.9 billion FY2026 revenue less $90.805 billion first-half revenue and $57.01 billion FY2026 Q3 revenue), implies roughly $129 billion for Q4 and about $415 billion for FY2027.\", \"The reference is the latest supplied analyst estimate, about $397 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The run-rate implied by reported first-half revenue and the Q3 guide is about $415 billion, also rung 2: it is above the reference but does not cross the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook would imply about $367 billion using FY2026 revenue, a meaningful lower counterweight; however, the current-quarter guide is the stronger near-term forecast input, and carrying its roughly 89% year-over-year growth into Q4 gives about $129 billion for Q4 and approximately $415 billion for FY2027. Supply constraints limit the upside case, while the SemiAnalysis forecast and Vera Rubin shipments support upside but do not establish enough total-revenue dollars to move the forecast above $431.2 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e3726\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2883\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e2941\\\"]\"], [6486, \"2026-09-02\", 2, \"FY2027 analyst estimates of about $396\\u2013397 billion (with the ladder anchored to $392 billion) sit in rung 2.\", \"Approximately $415 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus roughly $129 billion for Q4 by carrying Q3\\u2019s approximately 89% year-over-year growth forward against the prior-year Q4 base.\", \"The reference is the FY2027 analyst estimate: the ladder\\u2019s $392 billion anchor and the more recent $396\\u2013397 billion estimates both sit in rung 2. Reported first-half revenue of $177.837 billion plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, carried forward at its roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate against the prior-year Q4 base, implies about $129 billion for Q4 and approximately $415 billion for FY2027. That is above the reference but below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. Management\\u2019s 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, a meaningful lower counterweight; however, the current-quarter guide is the stronger forecast input. Supply limits temper upside. Rubin shipments and orders, the Amazon GPU deployment plan, and the China opportunity support demand, but they do not provide enough FY2027 revenue in hand to move the implied figure across the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e3726\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2883\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e2921\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e3301\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e2650\\\", \\\"e3713\\\", \\\"e3992\\\", \\\"e4010\\\"]\"], [6671, \"2026-09-03\", 2, \"Analyst expectations of $392 billion originally and approximately $396\\u2013397 billion in later estimates; each sits in rung 2.\", \"Approximately $415 billion for FY2027: $177.837 billion reported in the first half plus $108 billion guided for Q3, with roughly $129 billion implied for Q4 by carrying the Q3 guide\\u2019s approximately 89% year-over-year growth against the retained FY2026 Q4 base.\", \"The reference is the FY2027 analyst estimate: the original $392 billion estimate and the later $396\\u2013397 billion estimates are all in rung 2. The implied run-rate is about $415 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus roughly $129 billion for Q4 if the guided roughly 89% year-over-year growth rate carries forward against the retained FY2026 Q4 base. That is above the $392 billion reference but below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. Management\\u2019s 70% full-year growth statement is a lower counterweight; supply constraints also limit the upside. The new estimate that recent growth could put sales above $400 billion over a 12-month period supports the run-rate, but does not lift the FY2027 estimate across the rung 1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e2624\\\", \\\"e3726\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e3715\\\", \\\"e4047\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3045\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e3714\\\"]\"], [6880, \"2026-09-04\", 2, \"The market reference is FY2027 revenue of $392 billion in the original estimate, with later estimates of $396\\u2013397 billion; all sit in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $415 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue plus the $108 billion Q3 guide and approximately $129 billion for Q4 if the Q3 year-over-year growth rate carries forward against the FY2026 Q4 base. The stated 70% full-year growth outlook instead implies about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, a significant but less persuasive counter-signal given the reported first half and Q3 guide.\", \"The reference is the FY2027 analyst estimate: $392 billion sits in rung 2, as do the newer $396\\u2013397 billion estimates. The strongest operating run-rate calculation is about $415 billion: $177.837 billion reported for the first half, plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, plus roughly $129 billion for Q4 if the Q3 year-over-year growth rate carries forward against the FY2026 Q4 base carried from the previous conclusion. That remains below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. The main counterweight is NVIDIA\\u2019s stated 70% full-year growth outlook, which implies about $367 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion; however, that is difficult to reconcile with the reported first half and Q3 guide, so I give the near-term guide and run-rate greater weight. Supply constraints add downside risk but are not quantified enough to override that trajectory.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2624\\\", \\\"e3726\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e4047\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e733\\\", \\\"e2650\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e3715\\\"]\"], [6989, \"2026-09-05\", 2, \"Updated analyst expectations are about $396\\u2013$400 billion for FY2027: LSEG gives $396 billion, another analyst estimate is $397 billion, and Zacks\\u2019 85.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $400 billion. These references sit in rung 2.\", \"About $415 billion: add reported first-half FY2027 revenue of $177.837 billion and the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, then carry Q3\\u2019s roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward to the FY2026 Q4 base of about $68.1 billion (FY2026 revenue less its reported first half and Q3).\", \"The market reference is the updated FY2027 analyst outlook of roughly $396\\u2013$400 billion ($396 billion from LSEG, $397 billion from analysts, and 85.4% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion), all within rung 2. The operating run-rate implies about $415 billion, above that reference but still below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s 70% full-year growth outlook points lower, but the reported first half and Q3 guidance are stronger, more immediate inputs. Supply constraints temper upside; the specific SemiAnalysis H2 data-center compute projection is bullish, but the evidence does not provide the segment baseline needed to translate it reliably into total FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2624\\\", \\\"e3726\\\", \\\"e3879\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2883\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e4139\\\", \\\"e4047\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3045\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e4265\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e4261\\\"]\"], [7164, \"2026-09-06\", 2, \"Wall Street's latest supplied FY2027 revenue estimate is $411 billion (e4360), placing the reference in rung 2.\", \"About $407 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half plus $108 billion of Q3 guidance and an estimated $121 billion for Q4, carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance versus Q2.\", \"The reference is the latest supplied Wall Street FY2027 estimate of $411 billion, which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). Using the reported $177.837 billion first-half total, adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and carrying its roughly 12% sequential growth midpoint forward to Q4 implies about $121 billion for Q4 and roughly $407 billion for FY2027. That is below the reference but remains above rung 2's $392 billion floor, so it does not cross a rung boundary. The cited 70% full-year growth outlook points lower (about $367 billion on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion), but would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion after the first half and Q3 guidance; the more immediate quarterly results and guidance make that the less likely path. Supply constraints temper the upside, while the Rubin ramp and the specific H2 data-center projection support it, though neither is quantified enough to override the run-rate estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3045\\\", \\\"e4295\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e4305\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e2650\\\"]\"], [7296, \"2026-09-07\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts\\u2019 latest supplied FY2027 total-revenue estimate is $411 billion, placing the reference in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $177.837 billion reported for the first half, plus $108 billion guided for Q3, plus about $121 billion for Q4 by carrying the 12% sequential growth pace implied by Q3 guidance forward once. The separate 70% full-year growth statement implies about $367 billion from the $215.9 billion FY2026 base, but would require a sharp Q4 drop and is outweighed by the reported run-rate and near-term guidance.\", \"The reference is the latest supplied Wall Street FY2027 estimate, $411 billion, which is rung 2. The reported first-half revenue of $177.837 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion leaves about $121 billion for Q4 if the guided 12% sequential growth pace carries forward, implying about $407 billion for FY2027. That is below the reference but still above rung 2\\u2019s $392 billion floor. The separate 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, but it conflicts sharply with the reported first-half total and near-term guidance: it would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion. Near-term reported results and guidance therefore remain the stronger forecast inputs. Supply bottlenecks temper the upside; SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% upside view applies to second-half data-center compute, not total revenue, so it cannot be applied to the whole-company figure.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e60\\\"]\"], [7392, \"2026-09-08\", 2, \"Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate (e4360); rung 2.\", \"About $406.8 billion: reported first-half revenue of $177.837 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, plus approximately $121 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The reported first-half total and Q3 midpoint guidance imply about $406.8 billion for FY2027 if Q4 carries forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance: $177.837B + $108B + ($108B \\u00d7 1.12). That is below the reference but remains above rung 2's $392B floor and below rung 1's $431.2B threshold. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367B using FY2026 revenue of $215.9B, but it conflicts with actual first-half revenue and Q3 guidance, so it is not the stronger forecast input. Initial H200 deliveries add upside, but the cited $3B per 100,000 units cannot be applied without a shipment volume; supply constraints also temper the upside. Neither changes the rung boundary crossed.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e4510\\\", \\\"e2650\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e60\\\"]\"], [7515, \"2026-09-09\", 2, \"Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2.\", \"About $406.8 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, plus the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint, plus approximately $120.96 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 2. The reported first-half revenue and Q3 midpoint guidance, carried forward using the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by that guidance, imply about $406.8 billion for FY2027. That is below the reference but remains between rung 2's $392 billion floor and rung 1's $431.2 billion threshold. NVIDIA's broad 70% full-year growth statement would imply a lower figure, but it is less informative than reported results and current-quarter guidance; the supply constraints temper the extrapolation, while the H200 and capex themes support upside. Neither shifts the implied result across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e4510\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e2650\\\", \\\"e3015\\\"]\"], [7753, \"2026-09-10\", 2, \"Wall Street's latest FY2027 revenue estimate of about $411 billion is in rung 2.\", \"FY2027 revenue of about $406.8 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, plus $108 billion at the Q3 guidance midpoint, plus approximately $121.0 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\", \"The market reference is the latest Wall Street estimate of about $411 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The reported first-half total of $177.837 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and carrying the guided roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply about $406.8 billion\\u2014below the reference but still above rung 2's $392 billion floor and below rung 1's $431.2 billion threshold. The reported 70% full-year growth claim would imply about $367 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, but it conflicts with the reported first half and near-term guidance and is less specific about its period than the quarter guidance. Supply constraints temper upside; Rubin shipments and demand support it, but the supplied evidence does not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to move the forecast across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e4673\\\", \\\"e4510\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e4305\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e2650\\\", \\\"e60\\\"]\"], [7915, \"2026-09-11\", 1, \"Latest supplied Wall Street FY2027 estimate: about $411 billion, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $435 billion: the reported $177.837 billion first half plus $108 billion Q3 guidance and a 12% sequential Q4 carry imply about $406.8 billion before incremental upside. SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% above-consensus forecast is applied only to a conservatively discounted share of second-half revenue, rather than to NVIDIA\\u2019s whole revenue base, adding roughly $28 billion.\", \"The reference is the latest supplied Wall Street FY2027 estimate of about $411 billion, which is rung 2. The reported first half plus Q3 guidance and a 12% sequential carry into Q4 imply about $406.8 billion, below rung 1. But SemiAnalysis\\u2019s specific forecast that second-half data-center compute revenue would be 20% above consensus implies meaningful upside: applying a conservative, discounted uplift to that portion of the roughly $229 billion second-half run-rate gives an outcome around $435 billion. That crosses the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The uplift is uncertain and tempered by reported supply constraints, but the specific above-consensus forecast, accelerating Q4 outlook, and strong order growth make rung 1 the most likely forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e4555\\\", \\\"e4305\\\", \\\"e4606\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e3332\\\"]\"], [8046, \"2026-09-12\", 1, \"The latest supplied analyst estimate is $411 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2.\", \"The first-half reported revenue of $177.837 billion, plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion and a 12% sequential carry into Q4, implies about $406.8 billion before the SemiAnalysis forecast of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus. Applying a discounted uplift only to that portion supports an approximate FY2027 outcome of $435 billion.\", \"The market reference is the latest supplied FY2027 analyst estimate of $411 billion, which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The reported first half of $177.837 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and a 12% sequential carry into Q4 imply about $406.8 billion before the specific SemiAnalysis forecast that second-half data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus. Discounting that forecast to reflect execution and supply constraints, rather than applying it to all revenue, supports an outcome around $435 billion\\u2014above the rung-1 boundary. Recent customer-side evidence of rapid AI-capacity deployment supports demand, but does not justify taking the full SemiAnalysis uplift or adding all announced multi-year GPU plans to FY2027 revenue.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4641\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e4864\\\", \\\"e4865\\\", \\\"e4867\\\", \\\"e4819\\\"]\"], [8146, \"2026-09-13\", 1, \"Latest supplied FY2027 analyst expectation is $411 billion (e4360, e4641), in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $435 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, plus the $108 billion Q3 guide and approximately $120.96 billion for Q4 using the 12% sequential carry, gives about $406.8 billion before the discounted uplift implied by SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half data-center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus.\", \"The market reference is the latest supplied FY2027 analyst estimate of $411 billion, which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The reported first-half revenue, Q3 guide and sequential carry into Q4 imply about $406.8 billion before the SemiAnalysis forecast of second-half data-center compute revenue 20% above consensus. Discounting that forecast because it covers only data-center compute and supply remains constrained supports an outcome around $435 billion, above the rung-1 boundary. New customer-side deployment evidence strengthens the demand case, but does not quantify additional NVIDIA FY2027 revenue; I therefore do not add the full value of multi-year GPU plans or other unsized targets.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e4641\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e4819\\\", \\\"e2650\\\", \\\"e4616\\\", \\\"e4864\\\", \\\"e4865\\\", \\\"e4866\\\", \\\"e4869\\\", \\\"e4868\\\", \\\"e4619\\\"]\"], [8311, \"2026-09-14\", 1, \"The analyst reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The Zacks consensus growth estimate of 87.4%, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $404.6 billion, also rung 2; these are reference points, not the forecast conclusion.\", \"About $435 billion for FY2027. The reported first-half total plus the Q3 midpoint and a Q4 carry-forward of Q3's 12% sequential growth imply about $406.8 billion before applying a discount to SemiAnalysis's forecast that second-half data-center compute revenue would be 20% above consensus; the prior calculation put the resulting total near $435 billion.\", \"The implied forecast is about $435 billion, just above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. That forecast carries forward the prior calculation: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, $108 billion guided for Q3, and Q4 at about $121 billion if the guided 12% sequential growth carries forward, for a baseline of about $406.8 billion; the discounted SemiAnalysis forecast for second-half data-center compute lifts the estimate to roughly $435 billion. Recent customer-side evidence supports strong demand, while capacity constraints temper the upside; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 revision large enough to move the implied figure across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e4952\\\", \\\"e4958\\\", \\\"e5017\\\", \\\"e4864\\\", \\\"e4866\\\", \\\"e4873\\\"]\"], [8463, \"2026-09-15\", 1, \"Latest supplied Wall Street expectation: $411 billion, rung 2; the ladder's original $392 billion analyst estimate is also rung 2.\", \"About $445\\u2013447 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, approximately $113\\u2013114 billion for Q3 using the specific estimate and guidance-beat evidence, and about $127 billion for Q4 if the 12% sequential growth assumption carries forward yield roughly $418 billion before the discounted uplift from the second-half Data Center compute forecast. Applying that uplift only to the relevant portion of revenue puts the total above $431.2 billion.\", \"The reference is the latest supplied Wall Street expectation of $411 billion, which is rung 2; the ladder's original $392 billion estimate is also in rung 2. The reported first-half revenue and Q3 guidance establish a strong floor, while the specific estimate of $113\\u2013114 billion for Q3, supported by the reported history of beating the high end of guidance, raises the run-rate. Carrying forward the 12% sequential-growth assumption for Q4 gives about $418 billion before the discounted uplift from SemiAnalysis's forecast of second-half Data Center compute 20% above consensus; applying that forecast only to its relevant share puts the implied total at roughly $445\\u2013447 billion, above the rung-1 boundary of $431.2 billion. This remains a forecast rather than an established outcome. The stated 70% full-year growth would imply about $367 billion using FY2026 revenue, but is difficult to reconcile with first-half results, Q3 guidance, and the indicated Q4 acceleration without a sharp Q4 decline. Capacity constraints temper the upside, while early Vera Rubin shipments and hyperscaler orders support it.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e4673\\\", \\\"e4809\\\", \\\"e5017\\\", \\\"e5088\\\", \\\"e5089\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e4305\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3045\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e4819\\\", \\\"e5099\\\", \\\"e5100\\\", \\\"e5045\\\"]\"], [8633, \"2026-09-16\", 1, \"Wall Street analysts\\u2019 latest supplied FY2027 estimate is $411 billion, placing the reference at rung 2; the earlier $392 billion estimate is also rung 2.\", \"Approximately $445\\u2013447 billion: first-half revenue of $177.837 billion plus an estimated $113\\u2013114 billion Q3 and roughly $127\\u2013128 billion Q4 using 12% sequential growth yields about $418\\u2013420 billion. A discounted uplift for SemiAnalysis\\u2019s second-half Data Center compute projection, applied only to that revenue share, takes the implied total above $431.2 billion.\", \"The reference is the latest supplied Wall Street expectation of $411 billion, which is rung 2; the earlier $392 billion estimate is also rung 2. The first-half result and Q3 outlook support a run-rate above the reference, while the specific $113\\u2013114 billion Q3 estimate and the recent history of beating guidance support using more than the $108 billion guide in the run-rate. Carrying 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives roughly $418\\u2013420 billion before a discounted uplift for SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate that second-half Data Center compute revenue will be 20% above consensus. Applying that uplift only to its share of revenue implies about $445\\u2013447 billion, above the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The newer 88% FY2027 growth estimate and slower trailing growth temper the forecast, but do not outweigh the quarterly run-rate and specific second-half tracking signal. The reported supply bottlenecks are a meaningful cap on upside, not evidence that the lower rung is more likely.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e25\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e5088\\\", \\\"e5089\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e4305\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e5153\\\", \\\"e5160\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3045\\\", \\\"e4510\\\", \\\"e3332\\\"]\"], [8757, \"2026-09-17\", 1, \"The latest explicit Wall Street FY2027 estimate shown is $411 billion (e4360), rung 2. Later analyst growth estimates of 87.4%\\u201388% applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion imply about $405\\u2013406 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $445\\u2013447 billion: first-half actual revenue of $177.837 billion plus an estimated $113\\u2013114 billion Q3 and roughly 12% sequential Q4 growth implies about $418\\u2013420 billion before the second-half Data Center compute uplift. Applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% projection only to that compute share lifts the forecast above $431.2 billion.\", \"The market reference is the latest explicit Wall Street FY2027 estimate of $411 billion, rung 2; later analyst growth estimates of 87.4%\\u201388% on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion imply roughly $405\\u2013406 billion, also rung 2. The operating run-rate is higher: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion; using the analyst\\u2019s $113\\u2013114 billion Q3 estimate, informed by NVIDIA\\u2019s $108 billion guide and its recent guidance-beat history, then carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $418\\u2013420 billion before the second-half Data Center compute uplift. Applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% uplift only to that compute share, as in the prior estimate, puts the forecast around $445\\u2013447 billion. That clears the $431.2 billion rung-1 boundary. The reported 70% full-year growth characterization is a meaningful downside signal (about $367 billion on FY2026 revenue), but it would leave only about $81 billion for Q4 after first-half actuals and the Q3 midpoint, at odds with the implied sequential trajectory and management\\u2019s stated Q4 acceleration as Vera Rubin supply expands. Supply and power constraints temper the upside, but do not overturn the forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e5153\\\", \\\"e5017\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e5088\\\", \\\"e5089\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e4305\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3187\\\"]\"], [8969, \"2026-09-18\", 1, \"The latest supplied Wall Street FY2027 revenue estimate is $411 billion (e4360), which sits in rung 2; the ladder\\u2019s stated $392 billion estimate also sits in rung 2.\", \"About $445\\u2013447 billion: $177.837 billion reported for the first half, plus about $113\\u2013114 billion for Q3 (e5088), then roughly 12% sequential growth for Q4, gives about $418\\u2013420 billion before the second-half data-center-compute adjustment. Applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% uplift only to that compute portion (e60), not total revenue, gives approximately $445\\u2013447 billion.\", \"The current market reference is analysts\\u2019 FY2027 estimate of $411 billion, which is rung 2; the ladder\\u2019s original $392 billion estimate is also in rung 2. The operating estimate is about $445\\u2013447 billion: $177.837 billion reported in the first half, about $113\\u2013114 billion for Q3 using the analyst estimate above the $108 billion guide, and roughly $127 billion for Q4 carrying forward 12% sequential growth. Applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% uplift only to second-half data-center compute, rather than to total revenue, adds roughly $27\\u201328 billion to the approximately $418\\u2013420 billion run-rate estimate. This exceeds the rung-1 boundary of $431.2 billion. Supply and memory constraints temper confidence, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to move the forecast below that boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e25\\\", \\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e97\\\", \\\"e2710\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e5088\\\", \\\"e5089\\\", \\\"e5192\\\", \\\"e2941\\\", \\\"e3045\\\", \\\"e3187\\\", \\\"e5099\\\", \\\"e4952\\\", \\\"e5250\\\", \\\"e5255\\\", \\\"e5256\\\", \\\"e5100\\\", \\\"e2650\\\"]\"], [9077, \"2026-09-19\", 1, \"Analyst consensus is about $411 billion for FY2027, placing the reference at rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion).\", \"About $446 billion: $177.837 billion reported in H1, roughly $113.6 billion for Q3 using the historical beat over guidance, and about $127 billion for Q4 carrying forward 12% sequential growth, plus approximately $27\\u201328 billion from SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% uplift applied only to second-half data-center compute.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion (e5406), which is rung 2. The evidence-based run-rate remains above the rung-1 boundary: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion (e2754); applying the observed 3.1% average beat over the high end of guidance to the $108 billion Q3 guide implies roughly $113.6 billion for Q3 (e2715, e5088, e5089); carrying the 12% sequential growth pace forward implies about $127 billion for Q4 (e4190). That gives roughly $418 billion before SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% uplift to second-half data-center compute, which adds about $27\\u201328 billion when applied only to that portion, for an implied total near $446 billion (e60)\\u2014above $431.2 billion. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion from FY2026\\u2019s $215.9 billion, a bearish counter-signal, but it is less consistent with reported first-half revenue and the specific next-quarter guidance (e3332, e3343, e4510). Supply constraints and longer delivery times are real downside risks, but are not quantified as a revenue reduction sufficient to put the forecast below $431.2 billion (e5250, e5255, e5256). Growing supply commitments and Rubin production shipments/orders support the upside, but are not treated as guaranteed FY2027 revenue (e5099, e3015, e5045).\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e5406\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e5088\\\", \\\"e5089\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e3332\\\", \\\"e3343\\\", \\\"e4510\\\", \\\"e5250\\\", \\\"e5255\\\", \\\"e5256\\\", \\\"e5099\\\", \\\"e3015\\\", \\\"e5045\\\"]\"], [9183, \"2026-09-20\", 1, \"Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion for FY2027 (e4360, e5406), placing the reference in rung 2.\", \"About $446 billion: $177.837 billion in first-half revenue, approximately $113.6 billion for Q3 using the guidance midpoint and the cited average beat, and approximately $127 billion for Q4 by carrying forward 12% sequential growth, then adding about $27\\u201328 billion for the estimated 20% second-half data-center-compute uplift.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion (e4360, e5406), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). The evidence-based run-rate is about $419 billion before the additional second-half data-center-compute uplift: first-half revenue was $177.837 billion; Q3 is about $113.6 billion using the $108 billion midpoint guidance and the cited 3.1% average beat over the high end; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth pace forward gives about $127 billion for Q4. Applying SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% uplift only to second-half data-center compute adds about $27\\u201328 billion, implying roughly $446 billion and crossing the rung-1 boundary. The analyst view of 90% Q3 and 65% Q4 year-over-year growth is a counter-signal, but it does not outweigh the guided-growth run-rate and the specific second-half compute tracking. The 70% full-year growth statement would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue, but that would require a sharp Q4 decline after the reported first half and Q3 guidance, so it is inconsistent with the nearer-term run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e4360\\\", \\\"e5406\\\", \\\"e2754\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e5089\\\", \\\"e5088\\\", \\\"e4190\\\", \\\"e60\\\", \\\"e5192\\\", \\\"e4510\\\"]\"]], [\"What will NVIDIA report as total revenue for FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 431200000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 392000000000.0, 431200000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 352800000000.0, 392000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 313600000000.0, 352800000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 313600000000.0]]]\n[\"old-3\", \"v14\", [[988, \"2026-07-15\", 3, \"No market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion for FY2027 is the available reference, placing expectations in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion).\", \"Nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027, based on management's stated visibility. The unperiodized $200 billion TAM does not provide a basis to convert that figure into revenue above the $20 billion rung boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's stated visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 if, as stated, it remains below $20 billion. The $200 billion TAM indicates substantial opportunity but has no period or revenue conversion; it does not imply FY2027 revenue above $20 billion. The evidence therefore supports an outcome near the top of rung 3, without crossing the rung 2 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e557\\\", \\\"e558\\\"]\"], [1145, \"2026-07-16\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's stated visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue; this reference is in rung 3, below the $20 billion rung-2 threshold.\", \"Total FY2027 CPU revenue remains near, but below, $20 billion. The Noetra announcement specifies 13,750 Vera CPUs but no timing or revenue terms, and LANL's deployment schedule spans 2027 and 2028, so neither supports quantifying enough FY2027 revenue to move above the management visibility figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 if, as \\u201cnearly\\u201d indicates, it is below $20 billion. The newly disclosed Noetra deployment and LANL deployment plans provide evidence of Vera CPU demand, but Noetra has no timing or revenue terms and the LANL rollout spans 2027 and 2028. They do not support a defensible increment that takes FY2027 revenue to at least $20 billion, so the forecast remains near the top of rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e557\\\", \\\"e620\\\", \\\"e622\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e626\\\"]\"], [1217, \"2026-07-17\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; because \\u201cnearly\\u201d indicates below $20 billion, the reference sits in rung 3.\", \"FY2027 CPU revenue remains just under $20 billion, based on management's nearly $20 billion visibility. Noetra's 13,750-CPU plan has no timing or revenue terms, and LANL's 2027\\u20132028 deployment gives no FY2027 share, so neither supports a quantified addition taking the outcome to $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits just below $20 billion in rung 3. The Noetra plan identifies 13,750 Vera CPUs but gives no launch or shipment date or revenue terms; LANL's deployment spans 2027 and 2028 and provides no quantity. These are positive demand signals, but they do not support a defensible FY2027 revenue increment that crosses the $20 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e620\\\", \\\"e622\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e661\\\"]\"], [1251, \"2026-07-18\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue; this is just below $20 billion and sits at rung 3.\", \"Near, but below, $20 billion for FY2027, based on management's stated visibility. The disclosed Noetra CPU count and LANL deployments do not provide enough timing or revenue-per-CPU information to derive an additional FY2027 amount.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue (e558), which is just below the $20 billion boundary and therefore sits at rung 3. The announced Noetra plan's 13,750 Vera CPUs and LANL's deployments spanning 2027 and 2028 support demand, but neither gives FY2027 revenue or a price and timing from which to calculate it; the Noetra units may also already be reflected in management's visibility. The $200 billion TAM is not NVIDIA FY2027 revenue, and the broad GPU-to-CPU ratio prediction is not a quantified shipment or revenue estimate. The evidence therefore implies revenue near, but below, $20 billion and does not justify crossing into rung 2.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e557\\\", \\\"e558\\\", \\\"e620\\\", \\\"e622\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e634\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e721\\\", \\\"e728\\\", \\\"e729\\\"]\"], [1264, \"2026-07-19\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this is just below $20 billion and sits at rung 3.\", \"Near, but below, $20 billion for FY2027, based on management's stated visibility. The announced Noetra CPU quantity and LANL deployments support demand, but without revenue, pricing, or timing information they do not establish enough incremental FY2027 revenue to move the estimate above the rung 2 threshold.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3. The Noetra plan adds a concrete 13,750-CPU deployment, and LANL describes testing and deployments spanning 2027\\u20132028, but neither supplies FY2027 revenue, CPU pricing, or a delivery schedule sufficient to infer revenue above $20 billion; the Noetra units may also be included in management's visibility. The $200 billion TAM and the projected GPU-to-CPU ratio shift are not FY2027 revenue estimates. The implied outcome therefore remains near, but below, $20 billion, without crossing a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e622\\\", \\\"e620\\\", \\\"e728\\\", \\\"e557\\\", \\\"e729\\\"]\"], [1349, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this reference is rung 3 (at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion). No separate consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Near, but just below, $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on management's FY2027 visibility statement. The $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period, and the announced CPU deployments lack enough FY2027 timing and pricing information to derive additional revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3. The new $20 billion standalone-business target is not tied to FY2027, and Vera is still at the test/deployment stage; the Noetra CPU count has no completion date or revenue value, while LANL's deployment spans 2027 and 2028. These signals support substantial demand but do not establish FY2027 revenue at or above $20 billion or justify adding volume to management's visibility. The implied base case remains near, but just below, $20 billion, so it does not cross the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e620\\\", \\\"e622\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e634\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e721\\\", \\\"e728\\\", \\\"e557\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e729\\\", \\\"e749\\\", \\\"e770\\\"]\"], [1472, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027; this is below $20 billion and therefore sits in rung 3.\", \"FY2027 CPU revenue remains near, but just below, $20 billion, consistent with management\\u2019s stated visibility. Wolfe Research\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion of shipments in 2026, but it does not establish FY2027 revenue above the stated visibility or support adding the Noetra units as separate, dated revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3. The evidence supports a substantial ramp, but the $20 billion standalone-business target has no FY2027 period attached, and the Noetra deployment has no completion date or disclosed revenue value. Those signals do not justify moving the implied FY2027 outcome above the reference and across the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e863\\\"]\"], [1570, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue; \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d is below the $20 billion threshold and sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately, but below, $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, based on management\\u2019s direct visibility figure. The separate $20 billion standalone-business target has no period, while the 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion (1.3 million \\u00d7 $5,000) and does not quantify an FY2027 revision.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which is just below the $20 billion boundary and therefore sits in rung 3. The additional evidence supports that this is a real standalone CPU opportunity: Vera entered production, chips were delivered, and OpenAI plans large-scale deployment. But the shipment estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, not a quantified FY2027 revision above $20 billion. The $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period, and Noetra\\u2019s 13,750 CPUs imply only about $69 million at that cited ASP, with no deployment timing. These signals strengthen the ramp but do not move the implied FY2027 figure across the rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e622\\\", \\\"e863\\\"]\"], [1690, \"2026-07-23\", 2, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue; just below $20 billion, this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $20 billion: NVIDIA anticipates $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue in 2026 and says it is on track for $20 billion in CPU sales this year. That amount is at rung 2's $20 billion lower boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3. The newer explicit statement that NVIDIA anticipates $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue in 2026, alongside its statement that it is on track for $20 billion in CPU sales this year, shifts the most likely outcome to approximately $20 billion and across the rung 2 boundary. This is not evidence that the revenue is already realized: Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, a substantial execution risk, but it is an earlier third-party forecast and does not outweigh NVIDIA's more direct revenue visibility and on-track statements. Production, deliveries, and planned large-scale deployment support the ramp; the unsized timing of the Noetra deployment does not materially change the estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e953\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\"]\"], [1772, \"2026-07-24\", 2, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue; because that is just below $20 billion, the reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, based on management's nearly-$20-billion visibility and its statements that it was on track for $20 billion in CPU sales and anticipated $20 billion from standalone Vera CPUs in calendar 2026. The calendar-year figure is broadly aligned with, but not additive to, FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3. The implied outcome is approximately $20 billion: management also said it was on track for $20 billion in CPU sales, and the CEO clarified that the nearly-$20-billion figure is for standalone CPUs. The calendar-2026 $20 billion anticipation is not a separate additive amount, but it broadly supports that scale for FY2027. That forecast crosses the rung 2 threshold, though execution risk remains: Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion, and AMD's claimed performance advantage is a competitive risk. Those inputs do not outweigh the more direct management revenue outlook. Noetra's disclosed CPU quantity is small relative to the implied revenue, and its timing is unspecified. The $1 trillion Blackwell-and-Rubin forecast excludes standalone Vera CPU revenue, but the stated CPU upside is unquantified, so it does not justify a higher rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e953\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e863\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e622\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\"]\"], [1837, \"2026-07-25\", 2, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the reference, just below the $20 billion boundary and therefore in rung 3; the related \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d wording is at the rung 2 threshold.\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, with the base case at or just above the $20 billion rung boundary. This is anchored in NVIDIA's direct FY2027 visibility and on-track statements; the 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion but is for a different period and does not replace the FY2027 outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits just below the $20 billion boundary in rung 3; the separate wording that it is \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d places the expectation at the rung 2 threshold. The implied outcome is about $20 billion, with the direct FY2027 outlook carrying more weight than the third-party 2026 shipment estimate. That estimate implies roughly $6.5 billion (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000), but it is for calendar 2026, not the full FY2027 period. Production, June deliveries, and planned large-quantity deployment support the ramp, while AMD's claimed performance advantage is a competitive risk; neither changes the direct outlook enough to move the forecast below $20 billion. The Noetra deployment is a disclosed plan for 13,750 CPUs, but its timing is unspecified and it is only one program, so it does not establish the aggregate volume.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e953\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1042\\\"]\"], [1918, \"2026-07-26\", 2, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is just below the $20 billion boundary and sits in rung 3; the reported outlook of being on track for $20 billion sits in rung 2. The primary reference is therefore approximately $20 billion at the rung 2 boundary.\", \"About $20 billion in FY2027: the direct FY2027 visibility and on-track outlook center on that figure. Production and deployment evidence support the outlook, while the 2026 shipment estimate and Noetra plan are not added as separate full-year revenue because they cover different periods or have unspecified timing.\", \"The company reference is mixed at the $20 billion boundary: NVIDIA's CFO said it had visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d for FY2027, just below the rung 2 threshold (rung 3), while the reported \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d outlook sits at that threshold (rung 2). The implied outcome remains about $20 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Production, customer deliveries and deployment plans support that run-rate; Wolfe's 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion (1.3 million \\u00d7 $5,000), but it covers calendar 2026, not FY2027. Noetra's planned 13,750 CPUs imply roughly $69 million at that estimated ASP, with no timing given, so this is supportive but not a material addition to the full-year forecast. AMD's claimed performance advantage is a competitive risk, but it is not a quantified change to NVIDIA's FY2027 outlook. The new evidence therefore updates the supporting facts without moving the forecast below the $20 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1117\\\"]\"], [2161, \"2026-07-27\", 2, \"No external market consensus is provided. The company reference is nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, literally just below the $20 billion cutoff and thus rung 3; the reported \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d formulation is at the cutoff, rung 2.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027: NVIDIA\\u2019s visibility and on-track statements anchor the forecast, reinforced by its $20 billion calendar-2026 expectation and evidence that production and deliveries have begun. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at roughly $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a third-party estimate, not a quantified revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook.\", \"No external market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA\\u2019s \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d FY2027 visibility is just below the $20 billion rung-2 threshold (rung 3 by its literal wording), while the reported \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d outlook sits at the threshold (rung 2). The evidence still makes about $20 billion the most likely outcome: production and customer deliveries are under way, and management has also cited a $20 billion calendar-2026 expectation. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip shipment forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a material counterweight but an older third-party forecast rather than a company revision to FY2027 visibility. AMD\\u2019s reported performance advantage is a competitive risk, but it does not quantify a revenue reduction; the Noetra CPU plan is only about $69 million at Wolfe\\u2019s estimated ASP and has no stated timing. The implied figure therefore remains around the $20 billion boundary, not below it or at the $25 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e953\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e951\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e1076\\\", \\\"e1244\\\"]\"], [2325, \"2026-07-28\", 2, \"No external consensus is supplied. NVIDIA's FY2027 reference is \\u201cvisibility to nearly $20 billion,\\u201d just below $20 billion and therefore rung 3 by the literal boundaries; its separate \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d statement is exactly at the rung-2 threshold.\", \"About $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, based chiefly on NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility and on-track statements, reinforced by the reported production and customer deliveries. The specific $6.5 billion Wolfe Research calculation (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000) is a material counterweight, but does not displace the company's direct FY2027 outlook; the implied figure reaches rung 2's $20 billion floor, not rung 1's $25 billion.\", \"No external market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA's FY2027 reference is roughly $20 billion: \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d by itself is just below the rung-2 boundary, while NVIDIA's separate \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d statement sits at that boundary. The most likely outcome remains about $20 billion, at the lower edge of rung 2, rather than the $25 billion needed for rung 1. That view is supported by reported production and customer deliveries and by the company's direct revenue visibility. The specific Wolfe Research estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a substantial counterweight, but it is an earlier third-party forecast and not a quantified FY2027 company revision. The new AMD performance claims and reports of customers designing their own CPUs add competitive risk, but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's FY2027 outlook. The disclosed Noetra CPU count and customer evaluations add demand evidence but are not sized or timed enough to establish revenue materially above the stated outlook.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e863\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e951\\\", \\\"e953\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1043\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e1076\\\", \\\"e1077\\\", \\\"e1117\\\", \\\"e1181\\\", \\\"e1243\\\", \\\"e1244\\\"]\"], [2445, \"2026-07-29\", 2, \"No external market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA\\u2019s reference is nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027, with its \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d statement placing the reference at rung 2 (at the $20 billion boundary).\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027: NVIDIA says it has visibility to nearly $20 billion and is on track for $20 billion, and its CEO confirmed the figure is for standalone CPUs. The Noetra CPU quantity is only about $69 million at Wolfe Research\\u2019s $5,000 ASP and has no timing, so it supports the ramp but does not materially add to the FY2027 estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook of nearly $20 billion, with the separate \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d statement placing the reference at rung 2. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion: NVIDIA\\u2019s direct visibility statement is the strongest forecast input, and the CEO clarified that it concerns standalone CPUs. Production and customer deliveries support a ramp, while Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a meaningful execution counterweight, not a revised FY2027 forecast. Noetra\\u2019s planned 13,750 CPUs would imply about $69 million at that ASP, but the deployment has no stated timing and is not volume already in hand. The AMD performance claims add competitive risk; neither they nor the new customer and EDA announcements quantify a change to the FY2027 outlook. The implied figure remains around $20 billion, at the lower edge of rung 2, rather than crossing into rung 1 at $25 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e1243\\\", \\\"e1244\\\"]\"], [2553, \"2026-07-30\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook of nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, alongside its statement that it is on track for $20 billion; this reference sits at rung 2.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility statement and its \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d statement, with the CEO clarifying that the figure is for standalone CPUs. That is at the rung 2 boundary; the newer competitive and demand evidence does not quantify a revision across it.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook of nearly $20 billion, with the separate \\u201con track for $20 billion\\u201d statement placing the reference at rung 2. The forecast remains around $20 billion: the management visibility figure is explicitly for standalone CPUs, and no quantified revision or confirmed delay shifts it below the rung\\u2019s $20 billion floor. Production, customer deliveries, planned large-quantity deployment, and the announced Noetra deployment support a ramp, though they do not establish the full FY2027 total. As a counterweight, Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for 2026, and AMD\\u2019s performance and market-position evidence points to competitive risk; neither is a quantified FY2027 revenue revision. The 13,750-CPU Noetra deployment would be about $69 million at that ASP if all units were sold at that price, but its timing is unstated and it is not enough to move the forecast across a rung boundary. New evidence about rival CPU growth and customers designing their own CPUs adds downside risk, while evidence of higher CPU demand and greater AI infrastructure spending offsets it.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e1076\\\", \\\"e1077\\\", \\\"e1466\\\", \\\"e1468\\\", \\\"e1181\\\", \\\"e1042\\\"]\"], [2633, \"2026-07-31\", 2, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is nearly $20 billion, with a separate statement that it is on track for $20 billion. At the stated rounded target, the reference sits at rung 2 (at least $20 billion and less than $25 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, using NVIDIA's nearly-$20-billion visibility and on-track-$20-billion statements as the direct forecast, supported by production, customer deliveries, and deployment plans. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, but it is an earlier estimate and does not establish a revised FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 outlook of nearly $20 billion in CPU revenue, alongside its statement that it is on track for $20 billion; interpreted at the stated rounded target, that sits at rung 2. The implied outcome remains approximately $20 billion: production, deliveries, and planned customer deployments support a material ramp, while NVIDIA's visibility statement is the strongest direct forecast. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, and AMD's competitive evidence adds downside risk, but neither is a revised FY2027 company outlook or a quantified reason to forecast below $20 billion. The Noetra deployment is 13,750 CPUs, but no launch date is given, so it supports demand without establishing a large FY2027 revenue contribution. No dependency conclusions are supplied for this thesis.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e622\\\", \\\"e951\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1466\\\", \\\"e1468\\\", \\\"e1181\\\", \\\"e953\\\"]\"], [2685, \"2026-08-01\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook of nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, reinforced by its statement that it is on track for $20 billion; at the stated rounded target, this is rung 2.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA\\u2019s nearly-$20-billion visibility and $20-billion on-track statements. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment estimate at approximately $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for 2026, but does not by itself revise the FY2027 outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook of nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, with management also saying it is on track for $20 billion; interpreted at the stated rounded target, that sits at rung 2. The evidence still implies about $20 billion: the new cloud sign-ups add customer breadth but disclose neither deployment timing nor volume, while the AMD performance and growth evidence raises competitive risk without quantifying a revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s outlook. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, but that is not a revised FY2027 forecast and does not establish a different FY2027 total. The added evidence does not move the implied outcome across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1466\\\", \\\"e1468\\\"]\"], [2724, \"2026-08-02\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 visibility and $20 billion on-track statement; the rounded $20 billion target sits at rung 2.\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA\\u2019s stated visibility and on-track outlook. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million 2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026; the available evidence does not establish that this revises the FY2027 outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 outlook of nearly $20 billion and its statement that it is on track for $20 billion; using the stated rounded $20 billion target, that sits at rung 2. I infer roughly $20 billion, not a higher rung: management\\u2019s direct outlook remains the strongest forecast input, and the newly disclosed Noetra deployment is only 13,750 CPUs\\u2014about $69 million at Wolfe\\u2019s roughly $5,000 ASP\\u2014and has no launch date. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a meaningful execution check but not a revised FY2027 estimate; the product has entered production and customers have begun receiving or planning deployments. The named cloud sign-ups add breadth but no amounts. AMD\\u2019s performance and growth evidence is a competitive risk, but it does not quantify a revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook. The evidence therefore supports the $20 billion rung, without establishing the $25 billion needed for rung 1 or moving the implied outcome below $20 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e863\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e622\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1466\\\", \\\"e1468\\\", \\\"e951\\\", \\\"e1181\\\"]\"], [2761, \"2026-08-03\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion (with earlier visibility described as \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d) is the reference, centered at $20 billion and on rung 2.\", \"Around $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA\\u2019s direct guidance; this is rung 2. At Wolfe\\u2019s roughly $5,000 ASP, its 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment estimate implies about $6.5 billion, but it is an execution check rather than a revision to the later FY2027 guidance.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s current guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027, centered at $20 billion and therefore rung 2; its earlier \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility statement is slightly below that boundary if read literally. The implied outcome remains around $20 billion: the latest guidance is the strongest forecast input, and production, deliveries, and planned deployments support execution, but the disclosed customer quantities do not establish a path to $25 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a meaningful execution caution; it is not a quantified revision to the later FY2027 guidance. The disclosed Noetra order of 13,750 CPUs equates to about $69 million at that ASP, while the other cloud sign-ups are unsized. Neither the positive demand evidence nor the competitive caution moves the implied figure across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e1030\\\"]\"], [2837, \"2026-08-05\", 2, \"NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027 is the reference; $20 billion is rung 2 (at least $20 billion and less than $25 billion).\", \"Around $20 billion for FY2027, anchored by NVIDIA's current guidance. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, while Noetra's 13,750 CPUs imply about $69 million at that ASP; neither supplies a quantified basis to revise the FY2027 guidance above $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion: the FY2027 guidance is the strongest forecast, while production, deliveries, and additional customer deployments support execution. They do not disclose enough volume or revenue to imply $25 billion or more. As a scale check, Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026; Noetra's 13,750 CPUs imply about $69 million at that ASP. Those figures are not a quantified revision to FY2027 guidance. AMD's reported performance comparison and strong server CPU growth are competitive cautions, but likewise do not quantify a reduction in NVIDIA's FY2027 forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e558\\\", \\\"e622\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e863\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e951\\\", \\\"e953\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e1040\\\", \\\"e1041\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1043\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e1076\\\", \\\"e1077\\\", \\\"e1117\\\", \\\"e1181\\\", \\\"e1243\\\", \\\"e1244\\\", \\\"e1466\\\", \\\"e1468\\\", \\\"e1507\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e1585\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e1624\\\"]\"], [2908, \"2026-08-06\", 2, \"NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027 is the reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"Roughly $20 billion for FY2027, based on NVIDIA's around-$20-billion guidance and reiterated visibility to nearly $20 billion; the disclosed deployments support execution but do not establish additional revenue sufficient to imply $25 billion or more.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion: management reiterates that level, while production, deliveries, and customer deployments support execution but do not quantify an increase to $25 billion. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, not a quantified revision to FY2027 guidance. Competitive evidence is a downside risk, but does not quantify a reduction that would take the forecast below $20 billion.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1077\\\", \\\"e1552\\\"]\"], [2951, \"2026-08-07\", 2, \"NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027 is the market reference supplied here and sits at rung 2.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027: management's current guidance anchors the estimate, and the production, delivery and customer-deployment evidence supports execution but supplies no quantified revision. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, while Noetra's 13,750 disclosed CPUs imply about $69 million at that ASP; neither supports lifting the FY2027 estimate to $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which is rung 2. The additional evidence supports execution of that outlook but does not establish a quantified increase above it: production and customer activity are positive, while Noetra's disclosed 13,750-CPU deployment is only about $69 million at Wolfe's roughly $5,000 estimated ASP, and has no stated timing. Competitive evidence is a risk but does not quantify a cut to guidance. The evidence therefore does not move the forecast across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1032\\\"]\"], [3260, \"2026-08-11\", 5, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales; this is rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million chips in calendar 2026 multiplied by its roughly $5,000 ASP implies about $6.5 billion for 2026. Since FY2027 includes nearly all of calendar 2026 and one additional month, the estimate points to revenue below $10 billion absent unquantified incremental shipments.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. But Wolfe's specific estimate of 1.3 million Vera shipments in calendar 2026 at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that year; FY2027 overlaps nearly all of calendar 2026, with only January 2027 added. The disclosed customer deployments and commitments support demand, but give no additional unit volumes or timing that would bridge the large gap to $20 billion. The implied outcome remains below $10 billion, so it falls at rung 5.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e1624\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\"]\"], [3345, \"2026-08-12\", 5, \"NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027; this reference sits at rung 2.\", \"About $6.5 billion for FY2027: Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment forecast multiplied by its approximately $5,000 average selling price, with FY2027 overlapping almost all of calendar 2026. The disclosed deployments and commitments are not sized or timed enough to support a materially higher figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The specific Wolfe Research estimate of 1.3 million 2026 shipments at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion; calendar 2026 substantially overlaps NVIDIA FY2027. Production and initial deliveries are now confirmed, and customer deployments and commitments add demand evidence, but they do not provide enough sized volume to bridge the gap to $10 billion. For example, Noetra's 13,750-CPU deployment would be about $69 million at the cited ASP, and its timing is unspecified. Thus the implied outcome remains below $10 billion and does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e953\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e895\\\"]\"], [3454, \"2026-08-13\", 5, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027 is the reference, at rung 2 (at least $20 billion and less than $25 billion).\", \"About $6.5 billion: Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million 2026-chip shipment estimate multiplied by its roughly $5,000 ASP. Noetra\\u2019s 13,750 CPUs imply about $69 million at that ASP, but no timing is stated, so that deployment cannot be confidently added to FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits in rung 2. But the specific shipment estimate of 1.3 million CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion, and the newer customer commitments and production/deployment updates do not quantify enough additional FY2027 volume to bridge the gap to $10 billion. Noetra\\u2019s 13,750-CPU deployment would be about $69 million at that ASP, but its timing is unspecified; it does not establish a rung-crossing increase. The implied outcome therefore remains below the rung 4 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e823\\\"]\"], [3625, \"2026-08-16\", 5, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales is the reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $6.5 billion from Wolfe Research's 1.3 million 2026-chip shipment forecast multiplied by its roughly $5,000 ASP; the 13,750-unit Noetra deployment would be about $69 million at that ASP, with timing unspecified. The disclosed evidence does not quantify enough additional FY2027 units to reach $10 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The more volume-specific forecast implies a lower outcome: Wolfe Research's estimate of about 1.3 million chips in 2026 at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion; the disclosed 13,750-unit Noetra deployment would add only about $69 million at that ASP, and its timing is unspecified. Deliveries, production readiness, and additional cloud sign-ups support execution but do not quantify enough FY2027 volume to lift the implied total to the $10 billion rung-4 boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e814\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e1586\\\"]\"], [3902, \"2026-08-18\", 2, \"NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027; this reference is rung 2.\", \"Around $20 billion for FY2027, using NVIDIA's FY2027 sales guidance as the forecast and the delivery, production, and deployment evidence as support for execution. The disclosed customer deployments do not provide enough additional volume to support an estimate above $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2. The implied outcome remains near $20 billion: the direct FY2027 guide is supported by reported deliveries and full production, while cloud deployments and the SpaceX program add demand evidence without a disclosed volume that would justify a different estimate. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip forecast at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026 and is a meaningful downside check, but it is not a quantified FY2027 revision; the disclosed Noetra deployment is also too small to change the estimate materially. The implied figure is below the $25 billion rung-1 boundary and at the $20 billion rung-2 threshold.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e1624\\\"]\"], [4050, \"2026-08-19\", 2, \"NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion for FY2027; rung 2.\", \"Around $20 billion for FY2027, anchored to NVIDIA's direct guidance. Production and delivery evidence and customer commitments support execution, while Wolfe Research's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for 2026 at its stated ASP and 1.3 million-chip shipment forecast; that downside check does not quantify a revision to the FY2027 guide.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2. I forecast revenue near that figure: the guidance is the strongest direct FY2027 input, and production, deliveries, and customer commitments support execution, though they do not establish the guided revenue as already achieved. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, a material downside check, but it is not a quantified revision to the FY2027 guide. The 13,750-CPU Noetra deployment would imply about $69 million at that ASP, too small to move the total materially. AMD's reported performance advantage and the established competition are further risks, but no supplied evidence quantifies a resulting cut to NVIDIA's guidance. The implied figure remains around $20 billion: at rung 2's $20 billion floor and below rung 1's $25 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e1624\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e951\\\", \\\"e1043\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e1077\\\", \\\"e1181\\\", \\\"e1042\\\"]\"], [4113, \"2026-08-20\", 2, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion; this reference sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"Around $20 billion for FY2027, in rung 2. This follows the direct FY2027 guidance; the Wolfe Research estimate implies about $6.5 billion for 2026 (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000), but does not quantify a revision to the FY2027 guide.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2. The implied outcome remains around $20 billion: the guidance is the strongest direct forecast, and production, deliveries, planned large-volume deployment, and customer sign-ups support execution. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, a material downside check, but it is not a quantified revision to FY2027 guidance. The Noetra deployment is a stated volume but has no timing and is small at that ASP; the satellite partnership is also unsized. AMD's reported performance advantage raises competitive risk, but no supplied evidence quantifies a resulting cut to NVIDIA's guide. These inputs do not move the implied outcome across a rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e1624\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\"]\"], [4183, \"2026-08-21\", 2, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance is around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales; that reference sits at rung 2.\", \"Around $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA's stated guidance and its statement that it is on track for $20 billion. Customer deployments and production support execution, but the quantified AMD performance comparison is not tied to a revenue reduction.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2. The newer quantified AMD performance claims add competitive risk, while AMD's larger server-CPU market forecast, customer sign-ups and deployment plans support demand. None quantifies a change to NVIDIA's revenue outlook; the announced deployments are unsized or lack timing, and the available shipment estimate is for 2026 rather than a revision to FY2027 guidance. The implied outcome therefore remains around $20 billion, at the rung 2 threshold, rather than crossing into rung 1 or rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e2427\\\", \\\"e2428\\\"]\"], [4219, \"2026-08-22\", 2, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"Approximately $20 billion for FY2027, based chiefly on NVIDIA's direct guidance and nearly-$20-billion CPU-revenue visibility; no quantified revision or sufficiently sized customer commitment changes that total. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip 2026 shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies approximately $6.5 billion, but is an earlier third-party estimate and not a revised FY2027 guide.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. New evidence still implies approximately $20 billion: the guidance and nearly-$20-billion visibility remain the strongest direct forecast inputs. Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million 2026 shipments at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion, a meaningful downside signal, but it is an earlier third-party estimate rather than a revision to the FY2027 guidance. AMD's performance claims add competitive risk but do not quantify a revenue reduction. Customer commitments and deployment announcements are supportive, but mostly unsized or untimed; the disclosed Noetra CPU count, valued at Wolfe's ASP, is only about $69 million. These signals do not establish a quantified shift across a rung boundary from the roughly-$20-billion reference.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e2427\\\", \\\"e2428\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e629\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e823\\\"]\"], [4292, \"2026-08-23\", 2, \"NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027 is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027, anchored to NVIDIA's direct guidance; the production, delivery, and customer-deployment evidence supports execution but is not sized enough to revise that figure. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for 2026, a downside signal, but does not supersede the later FY2027 guidance.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which sits at rung 2. The evidence still implies about $20 billion: the direct guidance and visibility remain the strongest forecast inputs. Recent production and delivery evidence, OpenAI's planned Q3 deployment, and additional customer deployments support execution, but the disclosed Noetra quantity is worth only about $69 million at Wolfe's estimated $5,000 ASP, and most other commitments are unsized. Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip 2026 estimate implies about $6.5 billion at that ASP, while AMD's performance claims add competitive risk; neither quantifies a revision to NVIDIA's FY2027 guidance. The implied figure remains at rung 2's $20 billion threshold and does not support moving to rung 1 (at least $25 billion) or down to rung 3 (below $20 billion).\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e814\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1043\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e1077\\\", \\\"e1181\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e1624\\\", \\\"e2427\\\", \\\"e2428\\\", \\\"e2429\\\", \\\"e2430\\\", \\\"e626\\\"]\"], [4501, \"2026-08-24\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); no separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $20 billion for FY2027, anchored to NVIDIA\\u2019s around-$20-billion guidance and its stated visibility to nearly $20 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not FY2027; the disclosed Noetra deployment implies only about $69 million at that ASP and has no stated timing.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion, which is rung 2; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The newer evidence adds upside context\\u2014deployment plans and AMD\\u2019s sharply higher long-term server-CPU market estimate\\u2014but does not quantify a change to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 sales outlook. Execution evidence is supportive, while AMD\\u2019s performance claims and the 2026 shipment estimate temper the upside. The disclosed Noetra quantity is only about $69 million at Wolfe\\u2019s estimated $5,000 ASP, with no deployment timing; other customer commitments are unsized. These inputs do not make at least $25 billion more likely than the roughly $20 billion outcome, nor do they justify a forecast below the guidance level.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e2429\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e2486\\\", \\\"e2494\\\", \\\"e1624\\\", \\\"e2427\\\", \\\"e2428\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1042\\\"]\"], [4662, \"2026-08-25\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027 is the reference; it sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue: NVIDIA\\u2019s current fiscal-year guide is around $20 billion, and its clarification says the nearly $20 billion visibility figure is for standalone CPUs. The evidence supplies no quantified revision that would move this figure above $25 billion or below $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027, which sits in rung 2. The newer evidence supports real production, delivery and customer interest, but the deployments and commitments are not quantified in a way that establishes at least $25 billion of FY2027 revenue. The quantified AMD performance claims and Wolfe\\u2019s shipment estimate are downside considerations, but do not outweigh the company\\u2019s current fiscal-year guide or establish revenue below $20 billion. Thus the evidence does not imply a crossing of either adjacent rung boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e2494\\\", \\\"e1624\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e2427\\\", \\\"e2688\\\"]\"], [5194, \"2026-08-26\", 2, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales for FY2027; rung 2. No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027, using the current FY2027 guide and the company\\u2019s near-$20-billion visibility as the direct estimate. Production, shipment and customer evidence support that level, but the more-than-double statement applies to FY2028 and does not provide a quantified FY2027 uplift.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales, which sits in rung 2; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The evidence still implies roughly $20 billion for FY2027: full production, shipments underway and customer plans support the guide, while the newly stated expectation to more than double CPU revenue is for FY2028, not a quantified revision to FY2027. The evidence does not imply at least $25 billion for rung 1, nor does it outweigh the current guide enough to imply less than $20 billion for rung 3.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e2933\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\"]\"], [5371, \"2026-08-27\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance of around $20 billion in Vera CPU sales is the supplied reference; it sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue running at more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis implies total CPU revenue around or above $25 billion for FY2027, assuming the reported Grace run-rate carries forward.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 Vera/standalone CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2. For total CPU revenue, however, the evidence distinguishes standalone Vera revenue from Grace revenue: carrying the roughly $20 billion Vera guide alongside Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion implies total CPU revenue around or above $25 billion. That crosses the rung 1 boundary; the unsized AWS CPU plans do not provide a basis to add a further amount.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e2767\\\"]\"], [5726, \"2026-08-28\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the market/company reference; that figure alone sits at rung 2. The total-CPU question also includes Grace revenue.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of guided standalone CPU revenue plus Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried as an annual run-rate, implies total FY2027 CPU revenue around or above $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2 for this total-CPU question. Adding Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion as an annual run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue around or above $25 billion, crossing the rung 1 boundary. The FY2028 growth outlook and customer plans add upside, but the AWS CPU quantity has no timing and is not added separately to the FY2027 guide; AMD's performance claims are a counterweight, but do not outweigh the current-year guidance and Grace run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e3082\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e2427\\\"]\"], [5854, \"2026-08-29\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"More than $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue at a greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month run-rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2. The implied total is above $25 billion: the $20 billion standalone figure plus Grace CPU revenue running at more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. This crosses the rung 1 boundary, though the Grace component is a run-rate inference rather than FY2027 guidance. The later FY2028 outlook and customer plans support demand but do not add a separately quantified FY2027 amount; the AWS CPU quantity has no stated timing. AMD's performance claims are a competitive counterweight, but do not quantify a revenue reduction.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e2933\\\", \\\"e1030\\\"]\"], [5998, \"2026-08-30\", 1, \"NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in FY2027 standalone Vera CPU sales is the available reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). No separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\", \"Above $25 billion: add NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook to Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carrying that reported run-rate into FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's guidance of around $20 billion in FY2027 standalone Vera CPU sales, which sits at rung 2; no separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied. The implied total is above $25 billion: the roughly $20 billion standalone forecast plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. That crosses the rung 1 threshold. The Grace component is a run-rate inference, not FY2027 guidance, and the competitive evidence is a counterweight, but it does not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's outlook.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e3571\\\"]\"], [6238, \"2026-08-31\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU sales guidance is the reference; it sits at rung 2. No separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\", \"Approximately $25 billion or more: about $20 billion of guided FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as the current run-rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2; no separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied. Adding the reported Grace CPU trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion as its current run-rate implies approximately $25 billion or more in total CPU revenue, crossing the rung 1 boundary. Vera's production and shipments support the standalone outlook, while AMD's performance claims are a counterweight but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\"]\"], [6459, \"2026-09-01\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the relevant supplied reference and sits at rung 2. No separate consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\", \"About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus a roughly $5 billion-or-higher Grace CPU run-rate implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion, assuming the reported Grace run-rate carries through FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2; it is not a total-CPU figure. Adding Grace's reported trailing-12-month CPU revenue of more than $5 billion as the current run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, crossing the rung 1 boundary. The 2026 shipment estimate is a counterweight, but the later company guidance and production/shipment updates carry more weight.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e895\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e2933\\\", \\\"e814\\\"]\"], [6666, \"2026-09-03\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the reference and sits at rung 2. It is guidance for standalone CPUs, not total CPU revenue.\", \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus a continuing Grace CPU revenue base above its reported $5 billion trailing-12-month level implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion. This is an inferred run-rate, not a separately stated total-CPU forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance, which is rung 2 on this ladder; it is not a forecast of total CPU revenue. The evidence implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion if the reported Grace CPU revenue run-rate persists: roughly $20 billion from standalone CPUs plus Grace revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. That clears rung 1's $25 billion boundary. AMD's performance claims and competing market position are a meaningful counterweight, but they do not outweigh the company's FY2027 guidance, Grace revenue base, and evidence of Vera production, shipments, and customer demand.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e814\\\"]\"], [6876, \"2026-09-04\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the available reference and sits at rung 2. It is narrower than this question's total CPU revenue, which also includes Grace CPU revenue.\", \"Approximately $25 billion or slightly above: carry forward the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance and add the separately reported Grace CPU trailing-12-month revenue run-rate of more than $5 billion. This is a forecast-level estimate, not a claim that the FY2027 total is already reported.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2; it is not guidance for total CPU revenue. Adding the separate Grace CPU revenue run-rate puts the implied total around $25 billion or slightly above, so the forecast just clears rung 1's boundary. Vera production and customer activity support the standalone outlook, while AMD's performance claims and Wolfe's lower shipment estimate are counterweights but do not outweigh it.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e814\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e862\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e957\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e1624\\\", \\\"e2427\\\", \\\"e2428\\\", \\\"e2429\\\", \\\"e2486\\\", \\\"e2494\\\", \\\"e2688\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e2933\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e3082\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e3571\\\", \\\"e3921\\\", \\\"e3982\\\", \\\"e4084\\\", \\\"e4085\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e661\\\"]\"], [6982, \"2026-09-05\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); it is guidance for standalone CPUs, not total CPU revenue.\", \"Approximately $25 billion or slightly more for FY2027: roughly $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate of more than $5 billion, carried forward.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); it is not a total-CPU figure. The implied total is about $25 billion or slightly more: carry the roughly $20 billion standalone Vera outlook into FY2027 and add Grace CPU revenue at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate of more than $5 billion. That clears rung 1's $25 billion boundary, though only narrowly. Vera production, shipments, and customer plans support the standalone outlook; AMD's performance claims and Wolfe Research's lower shipment estimate are counterweights, but do not outweigh NVIDIA's guidance and disclosed Grace run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e2933\\\"]\"], [7173, \"2026-09-06\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance is the reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). It is a standalone figure, not total CPU revenue.\", \"About $25 billion or more: carry NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook forward and add Grace CPU revenue at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate of more than $5 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU guidance, which sits at rung 2; it is not guidance for total CPU revenue. Adding Grace CPU revenue at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate of more than $5 billion implies total CPU revenue of about $25 billion or more, crossing rung 1's $25 billion boundary. Wolfe Research's lower shipment estimate is for 2026, while the AMD performance claims are competitive counterweights; neither outweighs the FY2027 guidance, Grace run-rate, and evidence of Vera production and customer deployments.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e3082\\\"]\"], [7294, \"2026-09-07\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU sales guidance is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU sales plus Grace CPU revenue carried forward at its reported trailing-12-month run-rate of more than $5 billion implies roughly $25 billion or more in total CPU revenue, placing the forecast in rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU sales guidance, which sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to below $25 billion). For total CPU revenue, add Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried forward as a run-rate: that implies roughly $25 billion or more for FY2027 and crosses rung 1's $25 billion boundary. The stated demand and deployments support the forecast, while Wolfe's 2026 shipment estimate and AMD's performance claims are counterweights but do not outweigh the FY2027 guidance and Grace run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\"]\"], [7388, \"2026-09-08\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU sales guidance is the reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"Roughly $25 billion or more for FY2027: carry forward the approximately $20 billion Vera standalone forecast and add Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month CPU revenue above $5 billion as a run-rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU sales guidance, which sits at rung 2. Carrying forward the roughly $20 billion Vera standalone forecast and Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of roughly $25 billion or more, crossing rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion threshold. Production, shipment and customer-deployment evidence supports the Vera forecast; AMD\\u2019s performance claims are a counterweight but do not outweigh the guidance and run-rate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\"]\"], [7520, \"2026-09-09\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU sales guidance is the market/company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"Roughly $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue at a run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion, placing the forecast in rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU sales guidance, which sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). The evidence implies roughly $20 billion of standalone Vera revenue plus a Grace CPU revenue run-rate above $5 billion, or total CPU revenue above $25 billion; carrying the reported Grace trailing-12-month revenue forward puts the forecast over rung 1\\u2019s boundary. Vera is in full production and shipments are underway, supporting use of the company\\u2019s forecast despite execution risk. AMD\\u2019s performance claims and the third-party 2026 shipment forecast are counterweights, but do not outweigh the FY2027 guidance and current production and shipment evidence.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\"]\"], [7758, \"2026-09-10\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU sales guidance is the market/company reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"More than $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of guided FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue, plus Grace CPU revenue whose trailing-12-month figure already exceeded $5 billion and is carried forward as a run-rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU sales guidance, which sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). The forecast remains above the rung 1 boundary because the standalone Vera outlook is supplemented by Grace CPU revenue already exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis; carrying that reported run-rate forward implies total CPU revenue above $25 billion. Vera\\u2019s full production and shipments underway support the sales outlook. AMD\\u2019s performance claims are a counterweight, but do not overturn the company\\u2019s FY2027 guidance and reported Grace revenue. The new Amazon commitment and FY2028 outlook support demand, but neither quantifies an additional FY2027 amount, so they do not change the implied figure or rung.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e3921\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e2933\\\"]\"], [7911, \"2026-09-11\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference; it sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"Carry Grace\\u2019s reported more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate into FY2027 and add it to the roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU outlook, implying total CPU revenue around or above $25 billion. This reaches the rung 1 boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). The implied total is around or above $25 billion: carry Grace\\u2019s reported more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate into FY2027 and add it to the roughly $20 billion standalone outlook. That puts the forecast at the rung 1 boundary, and the most likely outcome is at least $25 billion. AMD\\u2019s performance claims are a counterweight, but they do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance or shipments; production, shipments, and customer commitments support the outlook. The FY2028 outlook and broad market-growth estimates are supportive context, not additional FY2027 revenue to add to the calculation.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e3082\\\", \\\"e2933\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e3571\\\", \\\"e3921\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e1624\\\", \\\"e2486\\\", \\\"e2494\\\", \\\"e2427\\\", \\\"e2688\\\", \\\"e4084\\\", \\\"e1075\\\"]\"], [8050, \"2026-09-12\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the relevant reference; it sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion or more: carry the reported Grace CPU trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion forward as a run-rate into FY2027 and add it to the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook. This reaches rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion minimum.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which is in rung 2. The latest company statements continue to put standalone CPU revenue around $20 billion for FY2027; NVIDIA\\u2019s clarification that this is standalone revenue matters because the question asks for total CPU revenue. Adding the reported Grace CPU trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried forward as a run-rate into FY2027, implies total CPU revenue of about $25 billion or more. That crosses rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary, so rung 1 remains the most likely outcome, although the threshold is close and not yet established as a reported FY2027 result. Customer deployments and production/shipment evidence support the outlook; AMD\\u2019s performance claims and custom-CPU competition are counterweights but do not quantify a cut to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook. The FY2028 growth outlook and broader market estimates are supportive context, not additional FY2027 revenue to add.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e4855\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e2933\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e3921\\\"]\"], [8141, \"2026-09-13\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference; it sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"At least about $25 billion: carry forward the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook and add Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis as a run-rate. This reaches the $25 billion threshold for rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion). The implied total is at least about $25 billion: carry forward the roughly $20 billion standalone Vera outlook and add Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. That reaches rung 1's $25 billion boundary. This remains a forecast rather than a reported FY2027 result; the new customer and deployment evidence is supportive but does not quantify an additional FY2027 amount, while AMD's performance claims and custom-CPU efforts are competitive risks. They do not outweigh the current outlook and Grace run-rate in the evidence shown.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e2429\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2933\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e4855\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e3082\\\", \\\"e3921\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e661\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e1042\\\"]\"], [8317, \"2026-09-14\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the supplied reference; that figure sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion for FY2027: roughly $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. This is a run-rate-based forecast, not a claim that the FY2027 total has already been reported.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. For total CPU revenue, the implied FY2027 figure is about $25 billion: carry forward the roughly $20 billion standalone Vera outlook and add Grace CPU revenue, which NVIDIA reported at more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. That places the forecast at the rung 1 threshold. Vera production and customer shipments support execution; AMD\\u2019s performance claims and competing in-house CPU designs are risks, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction to the FY2027 outlook. The FY2028 growth outlook is not used to inflate the FY2027 estimate.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e4855\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1075\\\"]\"], [8462, \"2026-09-15\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the market/company reference and sits in rung 2.\", \"Roughly $25 billion or modestly above for FY2027: the approximately $20 billion standalone Vera outlook plus Grace CPU revenue running at more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. This implies total CPU revenue at the rung-1 threshold or higher.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. The implied total is roughly $25 billion or modestly above: the $20 billion Vera outlook plus Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried as an annual run-rate contribution. That crosses the $25 billion rung-1 boundary. Vera production and customer shipments support delivery; the AMD performance claims and potential in-house CPU competition are downside risks, but do not quantify a reduction to the FY2027 outlook. The FY2028 growth outlook is not used to inflate FY2027.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1075\\\"]\"], [8625, \"2026-09-16\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook is the supplied reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion or modestly above: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera outlook forward and add Grace CPU revenue at its reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month run rate. This reaches the $25 billion threshold for rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook, which sits in rung 2. The implied total is about $25 billion or modestly above: roughly $20 billion from standalone Vera plus Grace\\u2019s greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month CPU revenue as a run-rate contribution. That reaches rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary. Full production and shipments support delivery, while the customer commitments add demand support; the supplied competitor and in-house CPU evidence is a downside risk but does not quantify a reduction to the FY2027 outlook. The FY2028 outlook is not used to inflate FY2027.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e3921\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e626\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e1075\\\"]\"], [8756, \"2026-09-17\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference and sits in rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion).\", \"About $25 billion or modestly above: approximately $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue, plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried as a run-rate contribution into FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which is rung 2. The implied total is about $25 billion or modestly above: the roughly $20 billion standalone Vera outlook plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. That reaches rung 1's $25 billion boundary. Vera is in full production and shipments are underway, while customer deployments support the outlook; the AMD performance claims and reports of in-house CPU development are downside risks but do not quantify a reduction to this implied total. The FY2028 outlook is not added to FY2027.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e2429\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e3921\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e4855\\\"]\"], [8955, \"2026-09-18\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference; it sits at rung 2 (at least $20 billion and less than $25 billion).\", \"Approximately $25 billion or slightly more for FY2027: the roughly $20 billion standalone Vera outlook plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as the current run rate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2. The implied total is just over $25 billion: about $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace revenue continuing from its reported trailing-12-month run rate of more than $5 billion. That reaches rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary. Vera is in full production and shipments are underway, supporting the outlook; AMD\\u2019s performance claims and reports of customers designing their own CPUs are downside risks but do not quantify a reduction below the boundary. The Grace contribution is the key qualification: the evidence reports its current run rate, not a separate FY2027 forecast.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1075\\\"]\"], [9076, \"2026-09-19\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the relevant market/company reference and sits at rung 2.\", \"Just above $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue for FY2027 plus Grace CPU revenue carried forward from its reported trailing-12-month run rate of more than $5 billion.\", \"The market/company reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera CPU outlook, which by itself sits at rung 2. For this question's total CPU revenue, the implied figure is just above $25 billion: carry the standalone outlook forward and add Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue run rate of more than $5 billion. The recent AMD performance claims and reports of customers developing their own CPUs are downside risks, but none quantifies a reduction to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue outlook; shipments, production, and customer commitments support the forecast. Thus the implied total remains above rung 1's $25 billion boundary.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e4855\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e814\\\"]\"], [9182, \"2026-09-20\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the relevant company reference and sits at rung 2 ($20 billion to less than $25 billion); it is not a total-CPU figure.\", \"Just above $25 billion: carry the approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook forward and add Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue run-rate of more than $5 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 2; it is not a forecast of total CPU revenue. Adding Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion as the available run-rate puts total CPU revenue just above $25 billion, crossing rung 1\\u2019s boundary. Vera is in full production, shipments are underway, and customer deployments support the $20 billion outlook. AMD\\u2019s performance claims and reports of customers designing their own CPUs are downside risks, but they do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook or establish a Grace decline.\", \"[]\", \"[\\\"e626\\\", \\\"e812\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e816\\\", \\\"e823\\\", \\\"e894\\\", \\\"e895\\\", \\\"e1030\\\", \\\"e1031\\\", \\\"e1032\\\", \\\"e1042\\\", \\\"e1075\\\", \\\"e1552\\\", \\\"e1586\\\", \\\"e1624\\\", \\\"e2427\\\", \\\"e2429\\\", \\\"e2486\\\", \\\"e2688\\\", \\\"e2767\\\", \\\"e2768\\\", \\\"e2852\\\", \\\"e2933\\\", \\\"e2949\\\", \\\"e3016\\\", \\\"e3082\\\", \\\"e3083\\\", \\\"e3302\\\", \\\"e3571\\\", \\\"e3921\\\", \\\"e4084\\\", \\\"e4855\\\", \\\"e5363\\\"]\"]], [\"What will NVIDIA report as total CPU revenue for FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 25000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 20000000000.0, 25000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 15000000000.0, 20000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 10000000000.0, 15000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 10000000000.0]]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\narm=\\\"linked-1\\\"\np=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nfor vn in [14,9,21,16]:\n v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n rows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n import json\n print(json.dumps([arm,v,rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\narm=\\\"linked-1\\\"\np=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nfor vn in [14,9,21,16]:\n v=f\\\"v{vn}\\\"\n rows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n import json\n print(json.dumps([arm,v,rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"[\"linked-1\", \"v14\", [[8743, \"2026-07-10\", 3, \"Bloomberg-compiled expectations project $393 billion of FY2027 sales; that market reference is in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $393 billion in FY2027, using Bloomberg's specific sales projection (up 82%) as the forecast input. The dependency's approximately $20 billion CPU-sales figure is part of the total and does not raise it above the whole-revenue projection.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The same quantified projection is the available forecast input; the bullish themes support demand directionally but do not quantify an adjustment that would carry the implied result across the $400 billion boundary. The dependency's approximately $20 billion CPU-sales forecast is a component of total revenue and is compatible with, not additive to, the $393 billion total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\"]\"], [8745, \"2026-07-11\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion; this is in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $393 billion for FY2027: Bloomberg\\u2019s quantified projection is the available revenue forecast, and the new observation that AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of demand has no period or revenue figure with which to adjust it. The dependency\\u2019s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is included within total revenue, not added to it.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The new demand-mix observation is directionally bullish but gives no period or revenue amount, so it does not support a quantified increase across the $400 billion boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is part of total revenue and is not added to Bloomberg\\u2019s total; the implied total remains about $393 billion, consistent with that dependency.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e213\\\"]\"], [8746, \"2026-07-12\", 3, \"Bloomberg projects $393 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 sales, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The separate consensus description, \\u201cwell over $300 billion,\\u201d is not specific enough to assign a narrower rung.\", \"About $387 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, an implied sequential increase of about 11.5%; carrying that pace forward for the remaining two quarters gives approximately $81.6B + $91B + $101.5B + $113.2B.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion); the reported description of Wall Street consensus as \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d is too broad to place more precisely. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided sequential pace through the remaining quarters gives an illustrative FY2027 total of about $387 billion. That is below Bloomberg\\u2019s estimate but remains within rung 3. Competition from custom chips and China-related demand risks, alongside reported memory constraints, temper the outlook but do not quantify a reduction that moves the forecast below $350 billion; customer and partnership announcements support demand but do not size additional FY2027 revenue. The CPU-sales dependency is part of total revenue and should not be added separately.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e213\\\", \\\"e219\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e229\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e257\\\"]\"], [8750, \"2026-07-13\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the most specific market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The broader \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d Wall Street expectation does not identify a more precise rung.\", \"Approximately $387 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided pace forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, for about $387 billion total.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3; the separate description of Wall Street expectations as \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d is too broad to place more precisely. The guided run-rate implies about $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the implied 11.5% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. Added hyperscaler-spending support and NVIDIA demand announcements are offset by memory constraints, custom-chip competition, and China-related risks, but none quantifies a change large enough to move the estimate across the $350 billion or $400 billion boundary. The dependency forecast of $18\\u201319 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to this total estimate.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e213\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e284\\\"]\"], [8752, \"2026-07-14\", 3, \"Bloomberg projects $393 billion of FY2027 sales, placing the market reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Wall Street's separate \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d expectation is less specific.\", \"About $387 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 and $91 billion guided for Q2 imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth forward gives approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, totaling about $387 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3; the separate description of Wall Street expectations as \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d is too broad to place more precisely. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided growth forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. That is below the $400 billion boundary and remains in rung 3. The disclosed AWS deployment plan and hyperscaler spending outlook support demand, while the HBM ramp slowdown and tighter China-related controls weigh the other way; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue adjustment sufficient to cross a rung boundary. NVIDIA's $18\\u201319 billion stand-alone CPU sales forecast is included within total revenue, not added on top, and is consistent with the whole-revenue estimate.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e372\\\"]\"], [8756, \"2026-07-15\", 2, \"Bloomberg projects $393 billion of FY2027 sales, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Wall Street's reported \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d expectation is too imprecise to place more narrowly.\", \"Carrying the sequential growth from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion to Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward implies about $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or roughly $387 billion for FY2027. Adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast gives about $407 billion.\", \"Bloomberg's $393 billion projection is the clearest market reference and sits in rung 3; the separate \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d expectation is too broad to locate more precisely. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply a roughly $387 billion FY2027 run-rate if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4, below the $400 billion boundary. Adding the dependency's roughly $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast, which is distinct from the existing revenue run-rate, gives about $407 billion and crosses into rung 2. This remains a forecast rather than an established result: the AWS deployment plan and $119 billion in supply-related commitments are not specified as FY2027 revenue, while HBM ramp constraints and custom-chip alternatives are downside risks.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e376\\\"]\"], [8759, \"2026-07-16\", 2, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the clearest quantified market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, with Q2's sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, implies roughly $387 billion; adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast gives about $407 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for the year if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast, treated as incremental to that run-rate, gives about $407 billion and crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. Booked supply commitments and the updated AI-capex view support the upside, while memory-supply risk and custom-chip alternatives temper confidence; neither supplies a quantified adjustment that takes the implied estimate below $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e220\\\"]\"], [8763, \"2026-07-17\", 2, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about $387 billion for FY2027 if Q2's sequential growth rate carries through Q3 and Q4. Adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate as incremental implies about $406 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for the year if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU estimate as incremental gives about $406 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and Huang's comments about orders support demand, but do not translate directly into additional FY2027 revenue. Memory-supply constraints and China-related risks temper the outlook, but are not quantified enough to pull the estimate below $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e234\\\"]\"], [8766, \"2026-07-18\", 2, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Applying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate (about 11.5%) to Q2's $91 billion guidance for Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $387 billion for FY2027. Adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives an implied total of about $406 billion, in rung 2.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for FY2027 if the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $406 billion, above rung 2's $400 billion boundary. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and management's order comments support demand but are not added directly to revenue. New evidence of HBM supply pressure and China-related restrictions is a downside risk, while the UAE export change is a partial offset; none supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue adjustment large enough to change the estimate across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [8770, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion is the market reference; it sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Using Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the 11.5% sequential increase carried forward gives approximately $101.5 billion for Q3 and $113.2 billion for Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. The approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate is included within, not added to, total company revenue.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion for FY2027, still in rung 3 and below the $400 billion boundary. The approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to this company-wide run-rate. Strong orders and the UAE export easing support the outlook, while the stated China exclusion and HBM supply concerns weigh against it; none provides a quantified FY2027 adjustment that moves the run-rate across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e376\\\"]\"], [8773, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion (also reflected in Wall Street's average estimates) is the market reference and sits in rung 3.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies 11.5% sequential growth. Carrying that growth into Q3 and Q4 yields approximately $387 billion for FY2027.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3. Using Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion gives 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion for FY2027. That is below the reference but remains above rung 3's $350 billion floor and below rung 2's $400 billion boundary. Strong orders and AWS's stated 2027\\u20132028 deployment plan support the outlook, but neither quantifies additional FY2027 revenue; China exclusions and memory-supply constraints weigh the other way. The current v8 forecast of $8\\u20139 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to the run-rate, and is consistent with total revenue remaining above it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e648\\\"]\"], [8776, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the primary market reference and sits in rung 3. Zacks' estimate of 80% year-over-year growth, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"Approximately $387 billion for FY2027: Q1 revenue was $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, an 11.5% sequential increase; extending that sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively, for roughly $387.3 billion total.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, also implies about $389 billion in rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate into Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $387 billion for FY2027. That is about $13 billion below rung 2's $400 billion boundary, while remaining above rung 3's $350 billion floor. Strong orders, AWS's planned GPU deployment, and Wistron's mass production support the outlook, but the disclosed deployment spans two fiscal years and production plans spanning several years are not FY2027 revenue in hand. China exclusions and memory constraints weigh against a higher forecast. The dependency forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not additive to, the approximately $387 billion total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e698\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e252\\\"]\"], [8780, \"2026-07-23\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350B\\u2013<$400B). Zacks' 80% growth estimate, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $388.6 billion and is also in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $387.2 billion: Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615B followed by Q2 guidance of $91.0B implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate into Q3 and Q4 gives roughly $101.5B and $113.1B, respectively.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the separate 80% growth estimate implies about $389 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, also rung 3. Independently, carrying the Q1-to-Q2 guided sequential growth rate forward gives approximately $81.6B + $91.0B + $101.5B + $113.1B = $387.2B for FY2027. That is about $12.8B below rung 2's $400B boundary and above rung 3's $350B floor. Mass production and the stated multi-year GPU and processor opportunities support upside, but do not allocate enough volume or revenue specifically to FY2027 to take the forecast across $400B; China exclusions and memory supply concerns weigh the other way. The v8 forecast of roughly $17B in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of the total run-rate. The themes provide demand or policy tailwinds, but do not quantify an FY2027 revenue revision sufficient to change the rung.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e252\\\"]\"], [8783, \"2026-07-24\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The reported 82% consensus growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion also implies approximately $393 billion, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $385.7 billion: start with reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and guided Q2 revenue of $91.0 billion, then carry the stated 11% sequential growth rate forward to Q3 ($101.0 billion) and Q4 ($112.1 billion).\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the 82% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $393 billion as well. The stronger near-term forecast input is NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 guidance of $91 billion, described as up 11% sequentially. Carrying that guided sequential growth forward gives approximately $81.615B + $91.0B + $101.0B + $112.1B = $385.7B for FY2027. Strong demand supports the run-rate, but the customer commitments span multiple periods and do not establish FY2027 revenue sufficient to cross $400 billion; memory constraints and China-related limits weigh against that move. The v8 CPU forecast of about $17 billion is part of total revenue, not an amount to add to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e882\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\"]\"], [8786, \"2026-07-25\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $385.7 billion: use reported FY2027 Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry the guided 11% sequential growth forward to approximately $101.0 billion in Q3 and $112.1 billion in Q4.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the reported 82% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion is consistent with that figure. The current-quarter $91 billion guidance, described as up 11% sequentially, implies roughly $81.615B + $91.0B + $101.0B + $112.1B = $385.7 billion if that sequential growth carries forward. New demand and production evidence leans positive, but the $1 trillion processor-sales potential spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and has no FY2027 revenue breakdown; the announced deployments and supply arrangements likewise do not quantify FY2027 revenue. The implied figure remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. The v8 CPU forecast of about $17 billion is included within total revenue, not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e284\\\"]\"], [8791, \"2026-07-26\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate (also consistent with approximately 82% growth from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion) is the market reference and sits in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"About $385.7 billion: $81.615 billion reported for Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried forward to approximately $101.0 billion in Q3 and $112.1 billion in Q4.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the separate 80%\\u201382% growth estimates also imply roughly $388\\u2013393 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate gives a lower point estimate: $81.615 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, then carrying the guided 11% sequential growth forward for Q3 and Q4 implies about $385.7 billion for FY2027. Strong orders and announced demand support the upside, while the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin figure spans multiple products and calendar years, and the memory and China evidence adds execution risk; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 uplift sufficient to take the forecast to $400 billion. The v8 estimate of $18\\u201319 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e949\\\"]\"], [8795, \"2026-07-27\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 estimate (with reported market growth estimates of 80%\\u201382%) is the reference and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $385.7 billion for FY2027: $81.615 billion reported in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, followed by Q3 and Q4 estimates of about $101.0 billion and $112.1 billion by carrying the 11% sequential growth in the Q2 outlook forward.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the reported 80%\\u201382% growth estimates also imply roughly $389\\u2013$393 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $385.7 billion: $81.615 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, then applying the guided 11% sequential growth to Q3 and Q4. Stronger AI spending, orders and supply partnerships tilt upward, but the $1 trillion product-sales forecast spans products and calendar years and supplies no FY2027 total-revenue allocation; HBM constraints and the Q2 exclusion of China compute sales are counterweights. The v8 CPU forecast of about $19 billion is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add again, and is consistent with this total. The implied figure remains below the $400 billion rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e905\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e1068\\\"]\"], [8799, \"2026-07-28\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The 80% growth consensus also implies approximately $388.6 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion.\", \"About $385.7 billion: $81.615 billion reported in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, followed by Q3 and Q4 at 11% sequential growth ($101.0 billion and $112.1 billion). This is below rung 3\\u2019s $400 billion upper boundary and above its $350 billion lower boundary.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the 80% growth consensus in e751 applied to FY2026 revenue also implies about $388.6 billion, in rung 3. The reported Q1 figure and Q2 guidance, carried forward at the guided 11% sequential growth rate, imply about $385.7 billion\\u2014still between the $350 billion and $400 billion rung boundaries. Demand commitments and the multi-year product-sales outlook support the upside, but they do not establish FY2027 total revenue at $400 billion; China restrictions and the specific HBM supply constraint are counterweights. The v8 CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not additive to, this estimate.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e906\\\"]\"], [8803, \"2026-07-29\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 estimate of $393 billion and the cited 80%\\u201382% growth expectations imply roughly $389\\u2013$393 billion; that reference is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $386 billion: take reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry the roughly 11% sequential growth in the guidance forward to about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4. The resulting FY2027 total remains within rung 3.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; the 80%\\u201382% growth estimates against FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion imply roughly $389\\u2013$393 billion, also rung 3. For the forecast, carrying Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward at roughly its 11% sequential growth rate gives about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that implies approximately $386 billion for FY2027. The updated China-authorization theme, AWS deployment plans, and demand outlook provide upside, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin forecast spans products and periods, and the $500 billion SK initiative is not an FY2027 revenue figure. The HBM ramp slowdown and China restrictions are counterweights. The dependency\\u2019s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is consistent with this estimate, not additive to it. The implied outcome remains below the $400 billion boundary, so the forecast stays in rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1263\\\"]\"], [8807, \"2026-07-30\", 3, \"Market expectations are about $393 billion in FY2027 (Bloomberg data; approximately 82% growth on FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion), which is rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"Approximately $386 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, plus $91 billion guided for Q2, then about $101 billion and $112 billion in Q3 and Q4 if Q2\\u2019s roughly 11% sequential growth carries forward.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. Carrying the Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward at its guided roughly 11% sequential growth rate implies about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that gives approximately $386 billion for the year. Strong demand indicators, price increases, and improved supply access add upside, but the cited deals and product-sales targets are not sized as FY2027 NVIDIA revenue, while memory constraints and China restrictions are counterweights. The implied figure remains below $400 billion, so it does not cross into rung 2; it is also well above rung 4\\u2019s $350 billion boundary. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU-sales forecast in v8 is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to the total, and is consistent with this forecast.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e1318\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\"]\"], [8811, \"2026-07-31\", 3, \"FY2027 consensus is about $393 billion (including Bloomberg's projection), which sits in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"The reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, carried forward at roughly 11% sequential growth, imply about $386 billion for FY2027 (Q3 about $101 billion; Q4 about $112 billion). Order visibility and pricing support upside toward roughly $393 billion, but the evidence does not imply crossing $400 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. Carrying the $91 billion Q2 guide forward at its roughly 11% sequential growth rate gives about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that implies about $386 billion for FY2027. The roughly $1 trillion of confirmed Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders through 2027, supply commitments, and reported price increases support some upside toward the reference, but those figures span products and periods and do not establish at least $400 billion of FY2027 revenue. China remains a counterweight because Q1 shipments and Q2 guidance exclude China Data Center compute revenue. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of a total that already includes it; it is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e1318\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e234\\\"]\"], [8815, \"2026-08-01\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectations of about $393 billion (with other consensus evidence indicating roughly 80\\u201382% growth) sit in rung 3.\", \"About $386 billion: carry the $91 billion Q2 guide forward at its roughly 11% sequential growth rate to estimate about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4, then add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion. The demand evidence supports upside within the rung but does not quantify a sufficient FY2027 increment to imply $400 billion or more.\", \"The Bloomberg/Wall Street reference of $393 billion sits in rung 3. The $91 billion Q2 guide, up 11% sequentially, implies roughly $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4 if that growth rate carries forward; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that gives about $386 billion for FY2027. Demand evidence supports the upper part of rung 3, but the roughly $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin order figure spans products and periods, and the deployment and customer-spending figures do not establish enough FY2027 NVIDIA revenue to cross $400 billion. China compute exclusions and memory-supply risks temper the upside. The v8 estimate of about $18 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue is consistent as a component of this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1380\\\"]\"], [8820, \"2026-08-03\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027; that reference is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Carrying the Q2 guide of $91 billion, about 11.5% above Q1's $81.6 billion, forward for the remaining quarters gives approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. Robust booked demand supports a modest lift, but after discounting orders that span products and periods, the central estimate is roughly $390 billion.\", \"The Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue reference of about $393 billion is in rung 3. The latest reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, with the Q2 sequential growth rate carried through Q3 and Q4, imply about $387 billion; strong demand evidence supports a modest lift toward roughly $390 billion, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin orders span products and periods and do not justify assigning that amount to FY2027. China restrictions and memory-supply risks temper the upside, so the forecast remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU estimate in v8 is part of NVIDIA\\u2019s total, not an amount to add again.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1309\\\", \\\"e1310\\\", \\\"e1311\\\", \\\"e1312\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e1380\\\"]\"], [8824, \"2026-08-04\", 3, \"Bloomberg and Wall Street estimates are about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference at rung 3.\", \"About $390 billion for FY2027: the reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, with Q2's 11% sequential growth carried forward through Q3 and Q4, imply about $386 billion before a modest demand-and-order uplift.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion, which is rung 3. Carrying Q2's guided 11% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives a base of about $386 billion ($81.6B + $91B + $101B + $112B); strong booked supply commitments and order visibility support a modest lift to roughly $390 billion. That remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin orders span products and periods, so they cannot be assigned in full to FY2027. Memory-supply and China-market risks temper the upside. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1376\\\"]\"], [8828, \"2026-08-05\", 3, \"Bloomberg and Wall Street estimates put FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (with estimates of 80\\u201382% growth), which is rung 3.\", \"Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Strong cloud demand and spending support a modest uplift to roughly $393 billion, partly offset by supply constraints and China exposure.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. Independently, Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the 11% sequential growth through the remaining quarters, imply about $386 billion ($81.6B + $91B + $101B + $112B). Strong cloud growth and spending support a modest uplift from that run-rate to roughly $393 billion; memory-supply constraints and China exposure limit the uplift. This remains below the $400 billion boundary. The roughly $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and periods, so it is not counted as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is included within total revenue, not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1309\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1379\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e1426\\\"]\"], [8833, \"2026-08-06\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectations are about $393 billion (also consistent with roughly 80\\u201382% growth on FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion); that reference is rung 3.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Customer spending and order/supply signals support a modest uplift, while memory and China headwinds temper it, giving an implied FY2027 total of roughly $390\\u2013395 billion.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The operating run-rate from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided 11% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4, is about $386 billion. Recent customer spending and order visibility support a modest uplift from that run-rate, but the large Blackwell/Rubin figures span products and periods and are not fully attributable to FY2027; memory constraints and China exposure offset the upside. The implied outcome is around $390\\u2013395 billion, still below the $400 billion rung boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1426\\\", \\\"e1493\\\"]\"], [8837, \"2026-08-07\", 2, \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027 (Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates), which is rung 3: $350 billion to below $400 billion.\", \"Approximately $400\\u2013410 billion, with a central estimate near $405 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion and an 11% sequential carry-forward through Q3 and Q4 implies about $386 billion; stronger order visibility and customer spending support a modest uplift, while the broad periods covered by the large product-order figures limit how much to assign to FY2027.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The operating carry-forward from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, and roughly 11% sequential growth in each remaining quarter is about $386 billion. I infer approximately $400\\u2013410 billion (central estimate about $405 billion): the customer-spending and backlog evidence, strong order visibility and reported Blackwell/Rubin orders support a modest uplift to the carry-forward, while those order figures span products and periods and should not be attributed wholly to FY2027. That uplift is enough to make crossing $400 billion slightly more likely than not, but not enough to support $450 billion. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook is already part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e1068\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1263\\\"]\"], [8841, \"2026-08-08\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Yahoo Finance market estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027, corresponding to rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $410 billion (roughly $405\\u2013415 billion): Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided sequential growth through the remaining quarters, implies a base near $386 billion; customer demand and spending evidence supports a modest uplift.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Carrying forward the guided growth from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion gives a roughly $386 billion operating base; strong customer spending and orders support a modest uplift, while supply constraints, China exposure, and spending that bypasses NVIDIA limit it. The implied estimate of about $410 billion crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but does not support $450 billion. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin orders span products and periods, and planned GPU deployments are not all FY2027 revenue.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1493\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1517\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\"]\"], [8844, \"2026-08-09\", 2, \"Wall Street's average estimate is about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the market reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $415 billion. Carrying the roughly 11% sequential increase in NVIDIA's $91 billion Q2 guide forward gives an operating base near $386 billion for FY2027; customer investment, demand visibility, and some improved supply support a modest uplift, while China exposure and memory constraints temper it.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The Q1 actual and Q2 guide imply a roughly $386 billion base if the guided 11% sequential growth is carried forward; strong customer investment and demand, plus improved supply support, justify a modest uplift to about $415 billion. China-related limits, memory constraints, and the fact that announced GPU deployments and orders span products and periods keep the forecast below $450 billion. The resulting estimate crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU-sales forecast is a component of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to the total estimate; the total remains above that dependency\\u2019s conclusion.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e669\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1517\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [8849, \"2026-08-10\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Approximately $415 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus the $91 billion Q2 guide, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion before an uplift for demand and production evidence, tempered by supply and China risks.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. Carrying NVIDIA\\u2019s Q2 guide of $91 billion and its 11% sequential growth forward gives approximately $386 billion for the four quarters, below the $400 billion boundary; the forecast remains about $415 billion after a measured uplift for strong demand and production evidence, partly offset by memory and China-related constraints. That crosses into rung 2 but remains below $450 billion. The announced $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders spans products and periods, so it supports demand but cannot be counted as FY2027 revenue in full. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is already contained within this estimate, not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e669\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1517\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1426\\\", \\\"e1318\\\"]\"], [8853, \"2026-08-11\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $410 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion and the cited Q3 estimate of $103.1 billion, with roughly 11% sequential growth carried into Q4, imply approximately $390 billion before an uplift for strong demand, booked commitments and pricing. Memory and China-related constraints temper that uplift.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The operating run-rate is close to that reference: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, and the cited Q3 analyst estimate is about $103.1 billion; carrying roughly 11% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $114 billion and approximately $390 billion for the year. Strong customer demand, booked supply commitments, rising AI infrastructure spending and pricing support a modest uplift to about $410 billion, while memory constraints and China-related restrictions cap it. That forecast crosses $400 billion but remains below $450 billion. The roughly $1 trillion of chip orders spans products and periods and is not counted as FY2027 revenue in full. The dependency's approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of, and fits within, this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e1068\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e1309\\\", \\\"e1310\\\", \\\"e1311\\\", \\\"e1312\\\", \\\"e1318\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1379\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e1426\\\", \\\"e1493\\\", \\\"e1502\\\", \\\"e1503\\\", \\\"e1505\\\", \\\"e1517\\\", \\\"e1564\\\", \\\"e1566\\\", \\\"e1660\\\", \\\"e1661\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1686\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\", \\\"e1709\\\"]\"], [8859, \"2026-08-12\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue consensus is about $393 billion, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $410 billion: Q1 actual revenue ($81.615 billion) plus Q2 guidance ($91 billion), Q3 consensus ($103.1 billion), and an estimated roughly $114 billion in Q4 from carrying approximately 11% sequential growth forward gives about $390 billion; demand, orders and pricing support a modest uplift above that run-rate.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The run-rate implied by $81.615 billion in Q1, $91 billion Q2 guidance, the $103.1 billion Q3 analyst estimate, and roughly 11% sequential growth into Q4 is about $390 billion. Strong AI infrastructure demand, booked orders, deployments and pricing support a modest uplift to around $410 billion, crossing $400 billion into rung 2. Memory constraints and China-related restrictions temper the upside; the evidence does not imply $450 billion or more. The roughly $1 trillion in Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders spans products and periods, so it is not counted in full as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA's stand-alone CPU sales forecast of about $20 billion is one component of total revenue, not an addition to the total forecast.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1764\\\", \\\"e1318\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [8863, \"2026-08-13\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 3.\", \"The Q1 actual, Q2 guidance, Q3 estimate and continued sequential growth into Q4 imply a baseline around $390 billion. Allowing a modest uplift for demand and booked orders, while discounting multi-period orders and competitive, supply and China risks, implies roughly $400\\u2013410 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue consensus of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. Carrying the reported $81.6 billion Q1, $91 billion Q2 guidance, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate and roughly 11% sequential growth into Q4 gives a baseline near $390 billion. Stronger demand and booked orders support a modest uplift: I forecast about $400\\u2013410 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. The large order and infrastructure figures span products and periods, so they do not establish FY2027 revenue at their full amounts; competitive alternatives, memory constraints and China exposure also temper the upside. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1318\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1811\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\", \\\"e1821\\\", \\\"e1822\\\", \\\"e1686\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e949\\\"]\"], [8867, \"2026-08-14\", 2, \"FY2027 market consensus is about $393 billion (Bloomberg/Wall Street), placing the reference in rung 3. The 80% consensus growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion likewise implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"About $400\\u2013410 billion, with a central estimate near $405 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate and an 11% sequential Q4 carry-forward imply a baseline near $390 billion; strong demand and booked supply support a modest uplift, not the full value of multi-period order figures.\", \"The market reference is FY2027 revenue consensus of about $393 billion, which is rung 3; the 80% growth consensus is also about $389 billion against FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The run-rate from reported Q1, Q2 guidance and the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate is about $390 billion if Q4 grows 11% sequentially from Q3. I forecast about $400\\u2013410 billion, around $405 billion: demand and booked-supply signals support a modest uplift over that run-rate, but the roughly $1 trillion order figure spans products and periods and is not FY2027 revenue guidance. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is included in total revenue, not added on top. Alternative accelerators, memory-ramp constraints and China exposure temper the upside; the implied outcome crosses $400 billion but not $450 billion.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1811\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1424\\\"]\"], [8871, \"2026-08-15\", 2, \"FY2027 revenue consensus is about $393 billion (rung 3); the 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 11% sequential growth carried into Q4 imply about $390 billion. Demand and supplier-growth evidence justify a modest uplift to roughly $400\\u2013405 billion, with the approximately $1 trillion order figure discounted because it spans products and periods.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; the 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion is about $389 billion and also falls in rung 3. A mechanical path from reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 11% sequential growth carried into Q4 gives roughly $390 billion. Strong AI-investment demand and upstream growth support a modest uplift from that baseline, while constrained HBM ramping, China exposure, and competing accelerators limit it. I therefore forecast about $400\\u2013405 billion: only a modest uplift is warranted from the roughly $1 trillion orders figure because it spans products and periods, and the forecast just crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an amount to add on top.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1686\\\", \\\"e1895\\\", \\\"e1896\\\", \\\"e1899\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\"]\"], [8875, \"2026-08-16\", 3, \"Bloomberg data and Wall Street estimates put FY2027 revenue near $393 billion; the 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion. Both figures are in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $396 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply roughly $391 billion; demand and orders support a modest uplift, but not enough to reach $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; the 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion and is also rung 3. The operating path is similar: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, analysts put Q3 near $103.1 billion, and carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $115.5 billion, or roughly $391 billion for the year. Strong demand and confirmed orders support the upper end of the high-$390-billion range, but those orders span products and periods and do not establish enough FY2027 revenue to cross $400 billion; HBM constraints and China exposure are counterweights. The roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU outlook in v8 is consistent with a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of the total-revenue path.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e1424\\\"]\"], [8879, \"2026-08-17\", 3, \"Market estimates put FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (e195, e669), rung 3: at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion. The 80% growth estimate also implies about $389 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion (e751, e577), likewise rung 3.\", \"About $391 billion on the operating path: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, $103.1 billion estimated for Q3, and approximately $115.5 billion for Q4 if sequential growth continues at roughly 12%. The demand commitments support the upper end, but their multi-year periods do not establish enough FY2027 shipments to lift this estimate to $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The forecast remains near the top of that rung, not because consensus alone keeps it there: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, and the cited Q3 estimate is $103.1 billion. Carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $115.5 billion, or roughly $391 billion for the year; the separate estimate of just under $400 billion on that growth path is consistent with the same rung. Strong demand, customer commitments and higher hyperscaler capex support the upper end, but the large commitments span products and years and do not establish enough FY2027 shipments to imply $400 billion or more. Memory and China-related constraints are counterweights. The roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not additional to, this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1493\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1982\\\", \\\"e1985\\\", \\\"e2017\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [8883, \"2026-08-18\", 3, \"Bloomberg data and Wall Street estimates put NVIDIA FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $391 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 + $91 billion guided for Q2 + the cited $103.1 billion Q3 estimate + about $115.5 billion for Q4 by carrying 12% sequential growth from Q3. The Q4 calculation and total are estimates, not reported results.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, in rung 3. The evidence-based path is about $391 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the cited Q3 estimate of $103.1 billion, and Q4 of about $115.5 billion if Q3-to-Q4 growth is 12%. That leaves the forecast just below the $400 billion rung boundary. The roughly $1 trillion in Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders through 2027 and OpenAI purchases potentially worth $600 billion through 2030 support demand, but span products and periods and do not establish enough FY2027 revenue to lift this estimate across $400 billion. Supply tightness and China-related limits are counterweights. The $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in dependency v8 is included within, and does not exceed, the total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2017\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e1814\\\"]\"], [8887, \"2026-08-19\", 2, \"Market expectations put NVIDIA FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (e195), in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"Approximately $403.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported for Q1, BofA's $94.5 billion Q2 midpoint and $107.5 billion Q3 estimate, then about $119.9 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 11.5% sequential growth implied by Q2 guidance.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The newer forecast uses $81.6 billion reported in Q1, BofA's $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate, its $107\\u2013108 billion estimate for potential Q3 guidance, and carries NVIDIA's roughly 11.5% sequential Q2 guided growth forward for Q4: approximately $81.6B + $94.5B + $107.5B + $119.9B = $403.5 billion. That narrowly crosses the $400 billion rung boundary. Q2 guidance itself remains $91 billion, and the Q3 figure is BofA's estimate rather than company guidance, so the crossing is a forecast, not an established result. The $20 billion FY2027 CPU forecast in v8 is included in\\u2014not added to\\u2014the total. Supply constraints remain a counterweight; the roughly $1 trillion of orders spans products and time and is not counted as FY2027 revenue.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2059\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e284\\\"]\"], [8892, \"2026-08-20\", 2, \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Yahoo! Finance's approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (82% growth), which sits in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"Approximately $403.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, about $94.5 billion for Q2 based on the $94\\u201395 billion estimate, about $107.5 billion for Q3 per BofA, and about $119.9 billion for Q4 by carrying roughly 11.5% sequential growth forward. The $20 billion FY2027 CPU forecast in v8 is part of this total, not additional revenue.\", \"The FY2027 revenue reference is about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. The forecast is modestly higher: Q1 was $81.6 billion; for Q2, the $91 billion company guide is the anchor, but upward revisions, the prior-quarter beat, and BofA\\u2019s $94\\u201395 billion estimate support forecasting above the guide. Using roughly $94.5 billion for Q2, BofA\\u2019s $107\\u2013108 billion Q3 estimate, and carrying approximately 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $403.5 billion for the year, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. This is a narrow forecast crossing, not an established result; the $20 billion CPU outlook in v8 is included in total revenue, not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\", \\\"e1821\\\", \\\"e1822\\\", \\\"e1895\\\", \\\"e1896\\\", \\\"e1899\\\", \\\"e1982\\\", \\\"e1983\\\", \\\"e2017\\\", \\\"e2049\\\", \\\"e2149\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2205\\\", \\\"e2206\\\"]\"], [8896, \"2026-08-21\", 2, \"Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 revenue projection is $393 billion, placing the market reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). NVIDIA\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guidance is a nearer-term reference, but by itself does not determine the FY2027 rung.\", \"About $403\\u2013404 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual, approximately $94\\u201395 billion Q2, $107\\u2013108 billion Q3, and roughly $120 billion Q4 by carrying about 11.5% sequential growth from Q3.\", \"The $393 billion FY2027 consensus reference is rung 3, but it is not the forecast once the current-quarter evidence is incorporated. Q1 was $81.6 billion; using BofA\\u2019s $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate, its $107\\u2013108 billion potential Q3 guide, and about 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 implies roughly $403\\u2013404 billion for FY2027\\u2014just over the $400 billion rung boundary. The estimate that repeating 12% sequential growth would leave revenue just under $400 billion is a meaningful countercase, so the crossing is narrow rather than secure. The roughly $20 billion Vera standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an additional amount to add.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e2244\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\"]\"], [8900, \"2026-08-22\", 2, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 revenue consensus (e195), which is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $403\\u2013404 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, plus BofA\\u2019s $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate and $107\\u2013108 billion potential Q3 guide, with about 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 (roughly $120 billion). The roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in dependency v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add again.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly $403\\u2013404 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, using BofA\\u2019s $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate and $107\\u2013108 billion potential Q3 guidance, then carrying about 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 gives approximately $120 billion for Q4. That is just above the $400 billion boundary; the company\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide and the estimate of just under $400 billion at 12% sequential growth are meaningful counterweights, but do not make the below-boundary outcome more likely.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e2148\\\"]\"], [8904, \"2026-08-23\", 2, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue consensus is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Roughly $404 billion on the base path: $81.6 billion Q1 actual, $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate, $107\\u2013108 billion potential Q3 guidance, and approximately $120 billion for Q4 carrying forward about 12% sequential growth. The separate $96 billion Q2 / $112 billion Q3 estimate supports upside, but its implied Q4 is not treated as certain.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The stronger current-quarter forecast path implies roughly $404 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, about $94\\u201395 billion expected for Q2, $107\\u2013108 billion of potential Q3 guidance, then roughly $120 billion in Q4 if sequential growth continues near 12%. A newer, more bullish estimate of $96 billion for Q2 and $112 billion for Q3 supports further upside, while the $91 billion company Q2 guide and an estimate that 12% sequential growth would leave FY2027 just under $400 billion are counterweights. On balance, the implied result is modestly above $400 billion, crossing into rung 2; the $1 trillion order-book claim spans 2026 and 2027 and is not taken in full for FY2027.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e2291\\\", \\\"e2149\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1336\\\"]\"], [8910, \"2026-08-24\", 2, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 sales consensus of $393 billion; rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $404 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, roughly $94\\u201395 billion for Q2, about $107\\u2013108 billion for Q3, and approximately $120 billion for Q4 assuming roughly 12% sequential growth from Q3.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3. The forecast is above that reference: Q1 was $81.6 billion; Q2 is estimated around $94\\u201395 billion despite the $91 billion company guide, with upward estimate revisions and a prior-quarter beat supporting some upside to guidance; BofA sees potential Q3 guidance of $107\\u2013108 billion. Carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $120 billion and a total near $404 billion, above the $400 billion rung boundary. The $98.18 billion Q2 estimate is a less corroborated upside signal, while the $1 trillion order-book figure spans 2026 and 2027 and is not counted fully as FY2027 revenue. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-sales forecast in dependency v8 is a component of, not an addition to, this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2402\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2406\\\"]\"], [8914, \"2026-08-25\", 2, \"The latest supplied FY2027 market estimate is LSEG analysts' $396 billion (e2421), which is rung 3. Bloomberg's earlier $393 billion estimate (e195) is also rung 3.\", \"About $404\\u2013405 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, an estimated $94\\u201395 billion for Q2, about $107\\u2013108 billion for Q3, and roughly $120\\u2013121 billion for Q4 by carrying approximately 12% sequential growth forward.\", \"The latest stated FY2027 market estimate is $396 billion, which is rung 3. The quarter-by-quarter forecast is modestly higher: Q1 reported $81.6 billion, Q2 is plausibly about $94\\u201395 billion despite the $91 billion guide, Q3 about $107\\u2013108 billion, and carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $120\\u2013121 billion, or roughly $404\\u2013405 billion for FY2027. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. The risk of memory constraints and competing custom chips tempers the upside; the $1 trillion order-book claims span products and periods and are not counted as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-sales forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not incremental to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2402\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e2487\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\", \\\"e2367\\\", \\\"e2443\\\", \\\"e1985\\\", \\\"e2438\\\"]\"], [8919, \"2026-08-26\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"FY2027 revenue of about $407 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2, $108 billion guided for Q3, and an estimated roughly $121 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance.\", \"The FY2027 market reference is LSEG's $396 billion estimate, which sits in rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues plus Q3 guidance imply about $407 billion for the year: carrying Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth forward gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but remains below $450 billion. Memory, power and data-center capacity constraints and the longer-dated custom-chip threat temper the forecast; NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-sales outlook is already part of total revenue, not incremental to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2760\\\"]\"], [8923, \"2026-08-27\", 2, \"LSEG FY2027 revenue estimate: $396 billion, on rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint, then approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 by carrying forward Q3\\u2019s 12.3% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is LSEG\\u2019s $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits on rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 totals plus Q3 guidance, with Q4 modeled by carrying forward Q3\\u2019s sequential growth, imply about $407 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2 but remains below $450 billion. The $1 trillion order-book figure and Rubin ramp support demand, but neither is a FY2027 recognized-revenue figure; memory, power and data-center constraints and custom-chip competition temper the upside. NVIDIA\\u2019s standalone CPU-sales estimate is part of total revenue, not incremental to it, and the dependent forecast remains consistent with a total above that component.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2521\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2979\\\"]\"], [8927, \"2026-08-28\", 2, \"LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits on rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and Q3 guidance of $108 billion total $285.8 billion. Carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3 forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.3 billion and FY2027 total revenue of about $407 billion.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 totals plus Q3 guidance imply about $407 billion: carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3 forward gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion. The stated supply constraints temper the upside, while the dependent stand-alone CPU forecast of about $20 billion is a component of, and does not exceed, the implied total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e3296\\\"]\"], [8931, \"2026-08-29\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits on rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion); Bloomberg's $393 billion projection is also below $400 billion.\", \"About $407 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to the Q3 guidance midpoint of $108 billion, then carry the roughly 12.3% Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate into Q4, implying about $121.3 billion.\", \"The implied FY2027 total is about $407 billion, which is above the $400 billion boundary for rung 2 but below the $450 billion boundary for rung 1. Supply constraints temper the extrapolation, but the current-quarter guidance is the strongest forecast input and carries the run-rate across $400 billion. The dependent stand-alone CPU forecast of about $20 billion is a component of this total and does not exceed it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e3296\\\"]\"], [8936, \"2026-08-30\", 2, \"LSEG analysts expect $396 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; Bloomberg data projects $393 billion, also rung 3.\", \"$81.6B Q1 actual + $96.2B Q2 actual + $108B Q3 guidance + about $121.3B for Q4 (carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance forward from Q3) = approximately $407B for FY2027.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, with Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate also in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The evidence-based estimate is about $407 billion: Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus Q3 guidance, then Q4 estimated by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance. That crosses the $400 billion rung boundary, but remains below $450 billion. Supply constraints temper the estimate, though the reported constraint is not quantified for the full year; the dependent stand-alone CPU forecast of about $20 billion is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2989\\\"]\"], [8941, \"2026-08-31\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 consensus of $396 billion (e2421) is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion); Bloomberg's $393 billion projection (e195) is also in rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual + $96.2 billion Q2 actual + $108 billion Q3 guidance + about $121.3 billion for Q4, carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by the Q3 guide (108 \\u00d7 1.123).\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $407.1 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 of about $121.3 billion if the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance carries forward. That crosses the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Newly detailed supply limits temper the outlook, but provide no quantified FY2027 reduction that would displace this run-rate estimate. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3094\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e3002\\\", \\\"e3278\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e2977\\\", \\\"e3178\\\", \\\"e3593\\\"]\"], [8945, \"2026-09-01\", 2, \"LSEG analysts\\u2019 FY2027 revenue consensus is $396 billion, which is rung 3 (Neutral: $350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2, $108.0 billion guided for Q3, and approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\", \"The market reference is LSEG\\u2019s $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate estimate is about $407.1 billion: reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 of about $121.3 billion if Q3\\u2019s 12.3% sequential growth carries forward. That clears the $400 billion rung boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. The reported demand-versus-supply constraint and the memory-shortage outlook temper confidence, but do not quantify a FY2027 reduction that would displace this estimate. The stand-alone CPU dependency\\u2019s approximately $20 billion forecast is consistent with the total and does not exceed it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e3732\\\", \\\"e3633\\\"]\"], [8949, \"2026-09-02\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' $396 billion FY2027 revenue consensus, in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward Q3's 12.3% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which is in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $407.1 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 of about $121.3 billion if Q3's 12.3% sequential growth carries forward. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Supply constraints and the exclusion of China Data Center compute revenue temper the upside, but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to move the implied estimate below $400 billion. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in dependency v8 is consistent with the total and does not exceed it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3094\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3482\\\", \\\"e3752\\\"]\"], [8951, \"2026-09-03\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 revenue consensus of $396 billion; this is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 plus $96.2 billion Q2, plus $108 billion Q3 guidance, plus approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 by carrying Q3's 12.3% sequential growth forward.\", \"The LSEG FY2027 consensus is $396 billion, which sits in rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its 12.3% sequential growth forward for Q4 implies about $121.3 billion, or roughly $407.1 billion for FY2027. That is above the $400 billion boundary but below $450 billion, so the forecast remains in rung 2. Supply and memory constraints temper the outlook but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to pull the implied total below $400 billion. The v8 stand-alone CPU forecast is part of total revenue and is consistent with this estimate, not additive to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\"]\"], [8955, \"2026-09-04\", 2, \"LSEG FY2027 revenue consensus of $396 billion; this is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Q1 and Q2 reported revenue sum to $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its approximately 12% sequential growth forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.3 billion. The resulting FY2027 total is approximately $407.1 billion.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its roughly 12% sequential growth forward gives about $121.3 billion for Q4, or approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Supply constraints are a downside risk, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction large enough to move the implied total below $400 billion. The v8 estimate of about $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is part of total revenue, not additive to it, and is consistent with this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\"]\"], [8959, \"2026-09-05\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' $396 billion FY2027 revenue expectation; rung 3 (Neutral: $350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"The first two reported quarters total $177.8 billion. Add Q3's $108 billion midpoint guidance and carry its roughly 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 ($108 billion \\u00d7 1.123 \\u2248 $121.3 billion), implying approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027.\", \"The reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its roughly 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4 and approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but remains below $450 billion. Capacity and memory constraints are downside risks, but the evidence does not quantify a shortfall sufficient to take the implied total below $400 billion. The v8 estimate of about $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is included in total revenue, not additive. The new report that guidance was raised for the rest of the fiscal year is not quantified, so it does not change the calculated outcome or rung.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e2521\\\"]\"], [8963, \"2026-09-06\", 2, \"The latest stated Wall Street expectation is approximately $411 billion for FY2027 (e4097), which sits at rung 2; the earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion (e2421) sits at rung 3.\", \"Q1 and Q2 reported revenue total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying the 12.3% sequential growth rate into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4 and approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027.\", \"The current market reference is the approximately $411 billion Wall Street expectation, which is rung 2; the earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion was rung 3. NVIDIA\\u2019s reported Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion, and adding Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion plus Q4 at the guided 12.3% sequential growth rate implies about $407.1 billion for FY2027. That is below the latest analyst reference but still above the $400 billion rung boundary. Supply constraints and memory availability temper the forecast, while the unquantified report that guidance was raised does not justify a larger numerical adjustment. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in v8 is part of total revenue, not additive, and is consistent with this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e2521\\\"]\"], [8968, \"2026-09-07\", 2, \"Latest cited Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectation: about $411 billion, rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). The earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $407.1 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, plus Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion, total $285.8 billion; carrying roughly 12.3% sequential growth forward gives Q4 of about $121.3 billion.\", \"The market reference is the latest cited Wall Street expectation of about $411 billion, which is rung 2; the earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion was rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate remains just above the $400 billion boundary: Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion, Q3 midpoint guidance is $108 billion, and carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth rate forward to Q4 implies about $121.3 billion, or approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027. Supply constraints temper that estimate, while the new 400,000-GPU deployment statement is not allocated to FY2027 or translated into NVIDIA revenue. Neither justifies moving below $400 billion or above $450 billion. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in dependency v8 is included within, not added to, total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4010\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4137\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e4175\\\"]\"], [8973, \"2026-09-08\", 2, \"The latest stated Wall Street expectation is about $411 billion, which sits in rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion); the earlier LSEG expectation of $396 billion sits in rung 3.\", \"Q1 and Q2 actual revenue total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion gives $285.8 billion through Q3. Carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance forward gives Q4 of about $121.3 billion and FY2027 total revenue of approximately $407.1 billion.\", \"The implied outcome is above the $400 billion rung boundary but below $450 billion, so the forecast remains rung 2. The $411 billion market expectation is a reference, not the reason to stay there. Q2 actual revenue and Q3 guidance support a roughly $407 billion run-rate; the report that NVIDIA raised guidance for the rest of the year tilts upward but gives no amount to add. Demand and pricing signals are offset by supply constraints, and the large order figure is not allocated to FY2027 revenue. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e1336\\\"]\"], [8977, \"2026-09-09\", 2, \"Market expectation: Wall Street's approximately $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, in rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6B reported in Q1 plus $96.2B in Q2 and the $108B Q3 midpoint guide, then about $121B for Q4 by carrying forward Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is approximately $411 billion, which sits in rung 2. The run-rate implied by the reported first two quarters and Q3 guidance is about $407 billion: $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B, with Q4 estimated at about $121B by carrying forward Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth. That is above the $400B boundary but below $450B. Supply and memory constraints temper the outlook, but the evidence does not make a Q4 slowdown below roughly $114B\\u2014the level needed to fall under $400B\\u2014the most likely outcome. The roughly $20B standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4120\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1336\\\"]\"], [8981, \"2026-09-10\", 2, \"Market analyst expectations of about $411 billion for FY2027 (e4097), which is rung 2. The reference is a comparison point, not the basis for holding the forecast there.\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6B in Q1 plus $96.2B in Q2 plus Q3 guidance of $108B, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3B by carrying Q3\\u2019s implied 12.3% sequential growth forward.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion, which sits in rung 2. The reported first two quarters and Q3 guidance imply $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B; carrying the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.3B, or roughly $407B for FY2027. That is below the consensus point but remains above the $400B rung boundary. Supply and memory constraints restrain the forecast, while strong demand, orders and updated AI-capex themes support it; none supplies a quantified FY2027 adjustment sufficient to move the estimate across a rung boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20B standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2521\\\"]\"], [8985, \"2026-09-11\", 2, \"Latest supplied analyst expectations put NVIDIA FY2027 revenue at about $411 billion (e4097), in rung 2.\", \"Approximately $407.1 billion: $81.6B reported in Q1 plus $96.2B reported in Q2 plus the $108B Q3 midpoint guide, with the roughly 12.3% Q3 sequential growth carried forward to an estimated $121.3B in Q4.\", \"The reference is the latest supplied analyst expectation of $411 billion, which sits in rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). The reported first two quarters and Q3 midpoint guidance imply $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B; carrying the Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3% forward for Q4 gives about $121.3B, or approximately $407.1B for FY2027. That remains in rung 2: it is about $7B above the $400B floor and about $43B short of rung 1. Strong demand, Rubin production, capacity investment, pricing plans and contracted/project demand support the run-rate, while memory, wafer, packaging, power and data-center constraints temper the upside. The announced AWS deployment and the $1T Blackwell/Rubin target span multiple periods, and the Australia figure describes infrastructure plans rather than NVIDIA revenue; they do not justify adding their full amounts to FY2027 revenue. No quantified revision in the supplied evidence moves the implied result across a boundary. NVIDIA's approximately $20B stand-alone CPU outlook in dependency v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e2521\\\", \\\"e3482\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e4325\\\", \\\"e4490\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e4513\\\"]\"], [8989, \"2026-09-12\", 2, \"Analysts expect $411 billion in FY2027; that reference is rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint guide, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% Q3 sequential growth rate.\", \"The reference is analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 expectation, which is rung 2. Independently, the reported Q1 and Q2 results plus the $108 billion Q3 guide imply about $407 billion using the Q3 sequential growth rate for Q4; that clears rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion floor but remains well below rung 1\\u2019s $450 billion threshold. Strong demand and planned pricing provide upside, but the additional evidence of persistent memory, packaging and data-center capacity constraints does not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to move the run-rate across a boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin revenue target spans products and time periods and is not evidence that the volume is in hand for this fiscal year.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e4325\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\"]\"], [8994, \"2026-09-13\", 2, \"Analysts expect $411 billion of FY2027 revenue (rung 2: $400 billion to below $450 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, with Q4 estimated at about $121 billion by carrying forward Q3\\u2019s roughly 12% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 expectation, which sits at rung 2. The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues plus the Q3 guide, carrying Q3\\u2019s approximately 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, imply about $407 billion for FY2027. That remains above rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion floor and below rung 1\\u2019s $450 billion threshold. Persistent supply and data-center bottlenecks temper the upside, while the announced pricing increase and substantial demand are not quantified as enough FY2027 revenue to cross a boundary. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add on top.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e4325\\\"]\"], [8998, \"2026-09-14\", 2, \"Analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 expectation is in rung 2. The newer Zacks consensus of 87.4% growth, applied to FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion, implies about $405 billion, also in rung 2.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 estimated by carrying Q3\\u2019s roughly 12% sequential growth forward ($121 billion) imply approximately $407 billion for FY2027.\", \"The market reference is analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 estimate, in rung 2; the newer Zacks estimate of 87.4% growth applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies roughly $405 billion, also in rung 2. Independent of those consensus figures, Q1 and Q2 reported revenue plus Q3 guidance and a Q4 carry-forward imply about $407 billion. That is above rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion floor but below rung 1\\u2019s $450 billion threshold. Strong demand and orders provide upside potential, but the evidence does not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to cross $450 billion, while reported supply and data-center bottlenecks temper the forecast. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in v8 is included in total revenue and is not added again.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\"]\"], [9002, \"2026-09-15\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 estimate is in rung 2. Zacks\\u2019 87.4% FY2027 growth estimate applied to approximately $215.9 billion of FY2026 revenue implies about $405 billion, also in rung 2.\", \"About $418 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to a Q3 estimate of roughly $113.5 billion (the midpoint of the $113\\u2013$114 billion forecast, supported by the reported guidance-beat history), then carry the 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4, giving about $127 billion.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is higher, around $418 billion, but remains below rung 1\\u2019s $450 billion boundary. It incorporates the $113\\u2013$114 billion Q3 analyst forecast and the recent history of beats over guidance, then carries the 12% sequential growth implied by management\\u2019s Q3 guide into Q4. Persistent supply and downstream data-center constraints temper the upside. NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an amount to add on top; v8\\u2019s conclusion is consistent with that treatment.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e4325\\\", \\\"e4772\\\"]\"], [9006, \"2026-09-16\", 2, \"Analysts\\u2019 FY2027 revenue expectation of about $411 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 2. Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 actual revenue totals $177.8 billion; applying the 3.1% average beat to the high end of Q3 guidance implies about $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the guide\\u2019s 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $127.2 billion. This falls within rung 2\\u2019s $400\\u2013450 billion range.\", \"The current market reference is analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 estimate, in rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies roughly $405 billion, in rung 2. My estimate is about $419 billion: actual Q1 and Q2 revenue totaled $177.8 billion, Q3 is estimated at about $113.6 billion by applying the reported 3.1% average beat to the high end of guidance, and carrying the 12% sequential growth implied by the Q3 guide into Q4 gives about $127.2 billion. Supply and data-center bottlenecks temper the upside, but do not pull the estimate below $400 billion; it remains below rung 1\\u2019s $450 billion boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $12 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is part of total revenue, not an addition to it, consistent with v8.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\"]\"], [9010, \"2026-09-17\", 2, \"Analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue implies about $405 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 total $177.8 billion; Q3 is estimated at about $113.6 billion by applying the 3.1% average beat to the high end of guidance, and Q4 at about $127.2 billion by carrying forward the guided 12% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate, which sits in rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies roughly $405 billion, in rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is about $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion; applying the reported 3.1% average beat to the high end of Q3 guidance gives about $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the guided 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $127.2 billion. Supply constraints through FY2028 temper the upside, while the specific Q3 estimate and strong demand signals support the run-rate; neither shifts the estimate across $400 billion or $450 billion. The roughly $15 billion stand-alone CPU estimate in v8 is included within total revenue, not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3633\\\"]\"], [9014, \"2026-09-18\", 2, \"The market reference is the $411 billion FY2027 analyst expectation, which sits in rung 2. Zacks\\u2019 87.4% revenue-growth consensus implies about $404.5 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $418.5 billion, or roughly $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 reported revenue totals $177.8 billion; the Q3 estimate is about $113.5 billion; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $127.1 billion.\", \"The $411 billion analyst expectation is in rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies roughly $404.5 billion, likewise rung 2. The forecast remains about $419 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 total $177.8 billion; Q3 is estimated at about $113\\u2013114 billion, consistent with the 3.1% average beat above the high end of guidance over the past four quarters; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth forward gives Q4 of about $127 billion. This remains below the $450 billion rung-1 boundary. Supply constraints temper upside, and the roughly $15 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is included in\\u2014not added to\\u2014total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\"]\"], [9018, \"2026-09-19\", 2, \"Analyst expectations of about $411 billion (e4061, e4097) are the market reference and sit at rung 2. Zacks' 87.4% FY2027 growth estimate implies about $404.5 billion from the reported FY2026 base of approximately $215.9 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 total $177.8 billion; applying the historical average 3.1% beat above the $110.16 billion high end of Q3 guidance implies about $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the guided 12% sequential growth forward implies about $127.2 billion for Q4. The sum is approximately $418.6 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 revenue, which is rung 2; the 87.4% Zacks growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $404.5 billion, also rung 2. The evidence-derived estimate is about $419 billion, still rung 2 and below the $450 billion rung-1 boundary. Near-term supply constraints and the newly quantified unfulfilled-demand estimate temper the upside, but the latter concerns AI-infrastructure demand in 2027 and does not establish additional NVIDIA FY2027 revenue. The approximately $18 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with a total above that component.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4901\\\", \\\"e4899\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e4120\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3482\\\"]\"], [9023, \"2026-09-20\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion of FY2027 revenue (rung 2). Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate against FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $404.5 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2\\u2019s $96.2 billion, an estimated Q3 of about $113.6 billion (the $110.16 billion high end of guidance adjusted by the reported 3.1% average beat), and Q4 of about $127.5 billion by carrying forward roughly 12% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion, which sits at rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies about $404.5 billion, at rung 2. The reported Q1 and Q2 figures plus Q3 guidance, adjusted for the quantified history of beating guidance and the $113\\u2013$114 billion Q3 estimate, imply about $419 billion for FY2027. Supply bottlenecks temper the upside, but this estimate remains above $400 billion and below the $450 billion boundary. The approximately $18 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with the total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4901\\\", \\\"e4902\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e4012\\\"]\"]], [\"How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 450000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 400000000000.0, 450000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 350000000000.0, 400000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 300000000000.0, 350000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 300000000000.0]]]\n[\"linked-1\", \"v9\", [[8744, \"2026-07-10\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center market consensus is supplied. The best supplied proxy is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation in v14, which is rung 3 for total revenue; at NVIDIA's Q1 Data Center share of 92%, it implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue, rung 3.\", \"About $356 billion: carrying the approximately 11.5% sequential increase from Q1 total revenue of $81.6 billion to Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward gives roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, then applying the Q1 Data Center mix of 92% gives roughly $356 billion. This is a run-rate estimate, not a flat annualization of Q1.\", \"There is no direct FY2027 Data Center consensus in the supplied evidence. As a market-reference proxy, v14 reports a Bloomberg-compiled $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation, which is in rung 3 for total revenue; applying the current 92% Data Center share puts the proxy near $362 billion, also rung 3. The operating run-rate is about $356 billion: Q1 total revenue was $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, an approximately 11.5% sequential increase; carrying that growth forward gives roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying the Q1 Data Center mix gives roughly $356 billion. Management's statement that Q2 Data Center growth will accelerate and its $1 trillion visibility across two calendar years are upside inputs, but neither supplies a quantified FY2027 segment figure sufficient to move the estimate above $400 billion. Conversely, the v14 total-revenue forecast makes a Data Center forecast above the whole-company forecast inconsistent, while memory and China substitution risks temper the outlook. The implied figure remains in rung 3, below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary and above $350 billion. The forecast moves from the previous rung 2 to rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e202\\\", \\\"e204\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e158\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\"]\"], [8747, \"2026-07-12\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus is supplied. The Bloomberg FY2027 total-revenue reference is $393 billion; applying the reported Q1 Data Center share of 92% gives a proxy of about $362 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $356 billion: v14's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue run-rate, derived by carrying the Q1-to-Q2 guided sequential growth pace forward, multiplied by the reported 92% Q1 Data Center share.\", \"The reference is a Bloomberg FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, reported in v14; with the latest 92% Data Center mix, that implies about $362 billion, in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $356 billion, also in rung 3: the strong Q1 Data Center result and Q2 total-revenue guidance support continued growth, but the supplied run-rate does not imply the $400 billion needed to move to rung 2. Management's $1 trillion visibility spans Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue across calendar 2026 and 2027, so it is not a direct FY2027 Data Center forecast and is discounted. Custom-chip competition and expected domestic substitution in China temper the upside. The implied segment revenue remains below the $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e163\\\"]\"], [8751, \"2026-07-13\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated using the 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion for Data Center; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $356 billion for FY2027. Starting from Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, applying the Q1 92% mix to the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide estimates Q2 Data Center revenue at about $83.7 billion; carrying the roughly 11.5% sequential total-revenue growth rate forward gives about $93.3 billion in Q3 and $104.1 billion in Q4.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $356 billion: Q2 total-revenue guidance of $91 billion versus Q1\\u2019s $81.6 billion implies roughly 11.5% sequential growth; applying the Q1 92% Data Center mix to Q2 and carrying that guided sequential pace through Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $83.7 billion, $93.3 billion and $104.1 billion of Data Center revenue, respectively, including Q1\\u2019s $75.25 billion. Management\\u2019s Q2 Data Center growth-acceleration statement is upside support, but gives no amount; the $91 billion total-revenue guide limits Q2 Data Center revenue to no more than that total. The resulting estimate is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary, and the dependent FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $387 billion is also a ceiling for this segment. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and is not a direct FY2027 Data Center forecast, so it is discounted. China substitution/export constraints and custom-silicon competition offset some of the strong demand and growth evidence.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e201\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e158\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e297\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\"]\"], [8753, \"2026-07-14\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated using NVIDIA's latest reported 92% Data Center share, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue. That reference is in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $356 billion for FY2027: dependency v14's approximately $387 billion total-revenue run-rate forecast, derived by carrying forward the sequential growth implied by Q1 actual revenue and Q2 guidance, multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix puts the reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. The implied Data Center outcome is about $356 billion: NVIDIA's $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide and the sequential-growth run-rate carried forward in dependency v14 imply about $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying the observed 92% Data Center mix gives roughly $356 billion. That is below the $400 billion boundary, so it remains in rung 3. Management's Q2 growth-acceleration statement and roughly $1 trillion of Blackwell/Rubin revenue visibility across calendar 2026\\u201327 support upside, but the visibility spans products and periods and is not a FY2027 Data Center figure; the $91 billion quarterly total guide also caps Q2 Data Center revenue. Competitive substitution and China-related constraints weigh the other way. The new platform-visibility fact changes the supporting facts, but does not justify moving the implied FY2027 figure across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e296\\\", \\\"e265\\\", \\\"e267\\\", \\\"e357\\\", \\\"e358\\\", \\\"e360\\\"]\"], [8758, \"2026-07-15\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion, translated using the latest 92% Data Center mix to approximately $362 billion; this is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $356 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: carry the sequential growth from Q1 total revenue of $81.6 billion to the $91 billion Q2 guide into Q3 and Q4, producing about $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, then apply the observed 92% Data Center mix. This is below $400 billion and consistent with v14\\u2019s higher total forecast because its approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU contribution is outside Data Center.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix puts the reference at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The implied Data Center forecast is about $356 billion: carrying the sequential growth from Q1 total revenue of $81.6 billion to the $91 billion Q2 guide forward gives roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion. Management\\u2019s Q2 Data Center growth-acceleration comment and multiyear platform visibility add upside, but the visibility spans calendar 2026\\u201327 and Blackwell and Rubin, so it does not establish a FY2027 Data Center amount that crosses $400 billion. Dependency v14\\u2019s roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast includes about $19.5 billion of stand-alone CPU revenue; the approximately $356 billion Data Center estimate remains below that whole and below the rung-2 boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e204\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e462\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e297\\\"]\"], [8761, \"2026-07-16\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, converted using the latest 92% Data Center mix, implies about $361 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion: carrying the $81.6 billion Q1 result and $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide forward implies roughly $387 billion for FY2027; applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, then adding v14\\u2019s approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast as incremental Data Center revenue gives about $376 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $361 billion, in rung 3. The current implied Data Center outcome is about $376 billion: carry the Q1-to-Q2 total-revenue growth implied by the $81.6 billion result and $91 billion Q2 guide through Q3 and Q4, giving roughly $387 billion for the year; apply the 92% Data Center mix (about $356 billion), then add v14\\u2019s approximately $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast as incremental Data Center revenue. This is below the $400 billion rung boundary and consistent with v14\\u2019s roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast; the added CPU revenue raises the estimate from the prior roughly $356 billion but does not change the rung. Strong demand and the Q2 acceleration comment support the run-rate, while China restrictions, competition and supply constraints temper the upside; the broad Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans products and calendar years, so it is not taken as FY2027 Data Center revenue in full.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e307\\\"]\"], [8765, \"2026-07-17\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at the 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $375 billion: the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide versus $81.6 billion in Q1 implies roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that through Q3 and Q4 gives about $387 billion for FY2027. Applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, and adding v14\\u2019s approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate yields about $375 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix puts that reference at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The current forecast is about $375 billion: carrying the Q2 total-revenue guide\\u2019s roughly 11.5% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion of FY2027 revenue; applying the 92% mix gives about $356 billion, then adding v14\\u2019s approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion. That is below the $400 billion boundary and below v14\\u2019s approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast, so it is consistent with the dependency and does not cross a rung boundary. Q1\\u2019s strong growth, Q2 acceleration guidance and multi-year platform visibility support the outlook, but the $1 trillion visibility spans products and calendar years, and the Q2 total-revenue guide constrains the near-term run-rate. China-related restrictions and proprietary-chip competition temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e297\\\"]\"], [8768, \"2026-07-18\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at the latest 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $375 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: the Q2 $91 billion total-revenue guide carried forward at the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate implies roughly $387 billion for FY2027; applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, then adding v14\\u2019s approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion.\", \"The market reference carried forward from the prior judgement is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at the latest 92% Data Center mix to about $362 billion; that sits in rung 3. The implied Data Center estimate is about $375 billion: carrying the Q2 $91 billion total-revenue guide forward at the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate implies roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, applying the 92% mix gives about $356 billion, and the approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate in v14 gives the prior method\\u2019s approximately $375 billion estimate. That is within rung 3\\u2019s $350\\u2013$400 billion range and below the $400 billion boundary; it also remains below v14\\u2019s approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast. Strong Q1 Data Center growth, Q2 acceleration guidance, platform visibility, and rising infrastructure spending support the estimate, but several inputs span products or periods and do not establish a higher FY2027 Data Center figure. China-related demand loss, competition, and memory constraints temper the upside. The new evidence changes the supporting facts, but does not move the forecast across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e297\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e462\\\", \\\"e556\\\", \\\"e558\\\", \\\"e559\\\", \\\"e604\\\"]\"], [8769, \"2026-07-19\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at the latest 92% Data Center mix to about $362 billion; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $375 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue: v14\\u2019s approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast, multiplied by the latest 92% Data Center mix, implies roughly $374 billion, rounded to about $375 billion. Noetra\\u2019s 140MW plan scales to roughly $6.9 billion at $49 billion/GW, but its unspecified delivery timing and planned Rubin deployment warrant discounting rather than adding the full amount.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is modestly higher, around $375 billion, but still below rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion boundary: Q1 results and Q2 guidance support a strong run-rate, while the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and is not FY2027 Data Center revenue. China substitution and zero H20 shipments temper the upside. The Noetra factory scales to roughly $6.9 billion at Morgan Stanley\\u2019s $49 billion/GW assumption, but its delivery date is unspecified and Rubin is a planned product, so that potential is discounted rather than counted in full.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e604\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e501\\\", \\\"e462\\\"]\"], [8772, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion, translated using the latest roughly 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $360 billion for FY2027: the current v14 forecast of approximately $387 billion total revenue multiplied by the reported 92.2% Q1 Data Center share gives about $357 billion; a modest increase to roughly 93% for management's unquantified Q2 Data Center growth acceleration gives about $360 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest roughly 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The implied outcome is about $360 billion: the current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is approximately $387 billion, and applying the reported 92.2% Q1 Data Center share gives about $357 billion; I allow a modest increase to roughly 93% for management's unquantified Q2 Data Center growth acceleration. That remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and multiple products, so it does not establish $1 trillion\\u2014or a particular share of it\\u2014as FY2027 Data Center revenue. China substitution and competition from custom chips and AMD weigh against a higher estimate; the planned Noetra Rubin factory and other demand signals provide some offset but are not sufficiently dated or sized to move the estimate across a rung boundary. The rung remains neutral, though the implied estimate is lower than the prior conclusion's roughly $375 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e204\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e538\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e636\\\"]\"], [8775, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion is the market reference; applying the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92.2% implies roughly $362 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3.\", \"About $356 billion: dependency v14 forecasts approximately $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying an estimated roughly 92% Data Center share gives about $356 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion; applying the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92.2% puts the reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. For the forecast, dependency v14 implies about $387 billion of total FY2027 revenue. Applying roughly a 92% Data Center share gives about $356 billion. Q1 Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion and management guided to further Q2 Data Center growth acceleration, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and multiple products, so it is not FY2027 Data Center revenue. The new estimate that around 90% of FY2027/FY2028 revenue will be Data Center, alongside specific AMD Helios plans, tempers the share assumption; neither changes the implied outcome enough to cross the $350 billion or $400 billion boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e604\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e630\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e638\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\"]\"], [8778, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, translated using NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly 92% Q1 Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $352 billion: v14\\u2019s approximately $387.3 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast multiplied by an estimated 91% Data Center share, between the reported 92% Q1 mix and the around-90% FY2027/FY2028 estimate.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate; applying the roughly 92% Data Center mix reported in Q1 puts the reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. The forecast is modestly below that reference: the updated total-revenue forecast from v14 is about $387.3 billion, and a roughly 91% FY2027 Data Center share implies about $352 billion. That share balances the reported 92% Q1 mix and management\\u2019s Q2 Data Center growth-acceleration outlook against the estimate that Data Center will be around 90% of FY2027/FY2028 revenue and the newly specified AMD Helios competition. The implied figure is below the reference but remains above rung 3\\u2019s $350 billion floor, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e636\\\"]\"], [8782, \"2026-07-23\", 4, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 NVIDIA total-revenue estimate is $393 billion. Applying the reported 92% Q1 Data Center share gives a Data Center reference of about $362 billion, which is rung 3.\", \"About $348.5 billion: apply the roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate to v14\\u2019s approximately $387.2 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast. This is below the $350 billion boundary and consistent with the total-revenue dependency.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, translated using the reported 92% Q1 Data Center mix to about $362 billion; that is rung 3. The forecast is lower: applying the roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate to v14\\u2019s approximately $387.2 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast implies about $348.5 billion, just below rung 3\\u2019s $350 billion floor. Q1\\u2019s 92% mix and management\\u2019s Q2 growth outlook support the segment, while the newly specified AMD Helios deployments add competitive pressure; SemiAnalysis\\u2019s Rubin ramp and efficiency analysis partly offset it. The implied segment revenue remains below v14\\u2019s total-revenue forecast, so it is consistent with the dependency. It crosses into rung 4, though only narrowly.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e630\\\", \\\"e631\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e634\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e758\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e761\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e162\\\"]\"], [8785, \"2026-07-24\", 4, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate is the market reference. Applying NVIDIA's reported Q1 Data Center mix of 92% implies about $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue, placing the reference in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $347.1 billion: take v14's approximately $385.7 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast and apply the roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate. This is below the $350 billion floor for rung 3 and is less than the whole-company forecast.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate; translating it at NVIDIA's reported 92% Q1 Data Center mix implies about $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3. The forecast implied by the evidence is lower: v14's current total-revenue forecast is approximately $385.7 billion, and applying the roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate gives about $347.1 billion, below rung 3's $350 billion floor. Q1's $75.25 billion Data Center result and management's Q2 growth outlook support continued growth, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and is not all FY2027 Data Center revenue. AMD's disclosed competing deployments and planned tens of billions of AI data-center revenue from 2027 temper the upside. The implied segment figure is below v14's total-revenue forecast, so it is consistent with the dependency, and remains in rung 4.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e204\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e296\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e630\\\", \\\"e631\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e634\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e638\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e758\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e760\\\", \\\"e761\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e852\\\", \\\"e858\\\", \\\"e870\\\", \\\"e871\\\", \\\"e880\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e902\\\"]\"], [8787, \"2026-07-25\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion is the market reference; applying the reported Q1 Data Center share of 92% gives about $361.6 billion, which is rung 3.\", \"About $357 billion: estimate Q2 Data Center revenue at roughly 92% of the $91 billion total-revenue guide, then carry the approximately 11.5% sequential growth implied by Q1 reported revenue and Q2 guidance forward through Q3 and Q4.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s reported Q1 Data Center mix of 92% implies about $361.6 billion, in rung 3. The guidance-anchored forecast is about $357 billion: infer Q2 Data Center revenue at roughly 92% of the $91 billion total-revenue guide, then carry the roughly 11.5% sequential growth implied by Q1 reported revenue and Q2 guidance through Q3 and Q4. That is above rung 3\\u2019s $350 billion floor and below $400 billion. It is consistent with v14\\u2019s $385.7 billion total-revenue forecast and does not treat the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure, which spans products and calendar years, as FY2027 Data Center revenue. AMD competition and the roughly 90% Data Center-share estimate temper the upside but do not outweigh the direct, current-quarter-guidance run-rate.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\"]\"], [8792, \"2026-07-26\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate, translated at the reported 92% Data Center mix, implies about $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue. That market reference sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $356 billion on the operating run-rate: $75.25 billion in Q1, about $83.7 billion for Q2 (92% of the $91 billion total-revenue guide), then two quarters carrying forward the approximately 11.5% sequential growth implied by that guide. The H2 outperformance view is upside evidence but lacks an absolute analyst baseline, so it does not support a defensible quantified adjustment.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate; applying the current 92% Data Center mix implies about $361.6 billion, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The operating run-rate is about $356 billion: $75.25 billion reported in Q1, then approximately $83.7 billion for Q2 using the $91 billion total-revenue guide and the Q1 92% mix, followed by Q3 and Q4 at the roughly 11.5% sequential growth implied by the Q2 total-revenue guide. Management's Q2 acceleration comment and SemiAnalysis's H2 upside view tilt higher, but neither supplies a Data Center amount that can be added reliably; the projection remains in rung 3. This is consistent with v14's $385.7 billion total-revenue forecast, since Data Center revenue cannot exceed total revenue. AMD's announced Helios demand and expected 2027 AI revenue are competitive offsets, but do not quantify an NVIDIA revenue reduction large enough to move the estimate below $350 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e828\\\"]\"], [8797, \"2026-07-27\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate, translated using the reported Data Center mix of about 92%, implies approximately $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue. That reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $357 billion for FY2027: $75.25 billion reported in Q1, about $83.9 billion estimated for Q2 using the $91 billion total-revenue guide and Q1 Data Center mix, then Q3 and Q4 estimated by carrying forward the guide-implied 11.5% sequential growth.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate; applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92% implies roughly $361.6 billion, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate is about $357 billion: start with $75.25 billion of Data Center revenue in Q1, estimate Q2 at about $83.9 billion by applying the Q1 Data Center share to the $91 billion total-revenue guide, then carry forward the guide-implied 11.5% sequential growth for Q3 and Q4. This remains within rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion) and is below the current v14 total-revenue forecast of $385.7 billion. Management\\u2019s acceleration comment and SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% H2 upside view support the higher side, but the latter has no disclosed baseline and cannot be added mechanically without conflicting with the total-revenue dependency. Competition, China\\u2019s domestic-supplier shift, and memory tightness temper that upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e758\\\"]\"], [8800, \"2026-07-28\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, translated at NVIDIA\\u2019s latest reported 92% Data Center mix, implies about $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue. That reference sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $355 billion: start with $75.25 billion of Q1 Data Center revenue, estimate Q2 at 92% of the $91 billion total-revenue guide ($83.72 billion), then carry forward 11% sequential growth for Q3 and Q4 ($92.93 billion and $103.15 billion). The total is about $355.05 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, which implies about $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue at the latest reported 92% mix; that reference is in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $355 billion, also in rung 3 and below the $400 billion boundary. Recent growth, Q2 guidance and the specific H2 upside view support the upper side, but the H2 view has no disclosed baseline to translate into dollars, while competition and export restrictions weigh against assuming a cross into rung 2. The current v14 total-revenue forecast of $385.7 billion also bounds Data Center revenue below $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e941\\\"]\"], [8804, \"2026-07-29\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, translated at NVIDIA\\u2019s latest reported 92% Data Center mix to about $362 billion; this is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $355 billion: apply the latest reported 92% Data Center mix to the current FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $386 billion, which carries Q2 guidance forward at the guided growth run-rate.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, which, at the reported 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $355 billion: the current total-revenue forecast is approximately $386 billion, and applying the latest reported 92% segment mix gives roughly $355 billion. Recent Q1 results and Q2 guidance support a strong run-rate, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans multiple products and calendar years, while AMD competition and the delayed Ohio project constrain the upside case. The implied figure remains above $350 billion and below the $400 billion rung boundary; the total-revenue forecast also caps Data Center revenue below $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1028\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e858\\\", \\\"e1125\\\", \\\"e1266\\\", \\\"e994\\\"]\"], [8808, \"2026-07-30\", 3, \"Market expectations are about $393 billion of FY2027 total revenue. Using the latest reported 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $355 billion: the current total-revenue forecast is about $386 billion, and applying the latest reported 92% Data Center share gives roughly $355 billion. This is above $350 billion and below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s latest reported 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $355 billion: the total-revenue dependency forecasts approximately $386 billion, and applying the latest reported 92% segment mix gives roughly $355 billion. Q2 guidance and management\\u2019s indication of accelerating Data Center growth support the run-rate, while the evidence does not quantify a mix change sufficient to move the estimate above $400 billion. The estimate remains above $350 billion and below $400 billion; the total-revenue forecast also caps Data Center revenue below $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e1291\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e1125\\\", \\\"e994\\\"]\"], [8813, \"2026-07-31\", 3, \"No standalone FY2027 Data Center consensus figure is supplied. The reference proxy is FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion, in rung 3; applying the latest reported 92% Data Center mix gives about $362 billion, also in rung 3.\", \"The current total-revenue dependency forecasts about $386 billion for FY2027; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s latest reported 92% Data Center share implies approximately $355 billion of Data Center revenue. That is above $350 billion and below $400 billion, so rung 3.\", \"The reference is FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3 for this thesis when mapped using NVIDIA\\u2019s latest reported 92% Data Center mix: about $362 billion. The implied estimate is lower: the current total-revenue dependency forecasts about $386 billion, and applying the 92% mix gives about $355 billion. Q2\\u2019s $91 billion total-revenue guide and management\\u2019s statement that Data Center growth will accelerate support the run-rate, while SemiAnalysis\\u2019s forecast of 20% outperformance in second-half Data Center revenue is upside evidence without a disclosed baseline from which to calculate a revised FY2027 figure. The estimate remains above $350 billion and below $400 billion; competing AMD commitments and the author\\u2019s roughly 90% mix estimate temper the upside but do not establish a move below $350 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e109\\\"]\"], [8816, \"2026-08-01\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue expectations of about $393 billion, mapped at the latest reported 92% Data Center share to approximately $362 billion; this is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $355 billion: the current total-revenue dependency forecasts approximately $386 billion for FY2027, and applying NVIDIA's latest reported 92% Data Center share gives roughly $355.1 billion. The figure is above $350 billion and below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying the current 92% Data Center mix puts the reference at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue dependency forecasts about $386 billion, implying approximately $355 billion of Data Center revenue at that 92% mix. Q1 growth, the Q2 total-revenue guide, and SemiAnalysis\\u2019s H2 upside view support demand, but the H2 estimate has no disclosed baseline to calculate an increment; the newer demand and capacity evidence likewise does not quantify enough FY2027 NVIDIA revenue to move the implied figure across $350 billion or $400 billion. Competition and supply or timing risks temper the upside. The implied figure is below the $400 billion boundary and above $350 billion, so the forecast remains rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e638\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e761\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e1028\\\", \\\"e1125\\\", \\\"e1290\\\", \\\"e1291\\\", \\\"e1362\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1379\\\", \\\"e1380\\\"]\"], [8821, \"2026-08-03\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates of about $393 billion for NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue; using the latest reported 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $351 billion: the current dependency forecast of roughly $390 billion total FY2027 revenue multiplied by the third-party estimate that around 90% of FY2027 revenue will come from Data Center.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates of about $393 billion of FY2027 total revenue. Applying the latest reported 92% Data Center mix gives about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The current total-revenue dependency forecasts roughly $390 billion; applying the specific view that Data Center will be around 90% of FY2027 revenue implies approximately $351 billion. Q1 results, management's Q2 growth guidance, and SemiAnalysis's quantified H2 upside view support the forecast, while AMD's announced deployments and other competition temper it. The implied figure is just above the $350 billion boundary, so it remains rung 3 and does not reach the $400 billion boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e638\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e880\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e1028\\\"]\"], [8825, \"2026-08-04\", 3, \"Bloomberg and Wall Street estimates are about $393 billion for total FY2027 revenue. Using the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate as a proxy gives about $354 billion, placing the reference at rung 3.\", \"About $351 billion: the current total-revenue dependency forecasts roughly $390 billion for FY2027, and applying the cited approximately 90% Data Center share gives about $351 billion. This is above the $350 billion rung boundary and below $400 billion.\", \"The reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the approximately 90% Data Center share cited for NVIDIA's FY2027/FY2028 revenue implies about $354 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3. The forecast uses the current total-revenue dependency of roughly $390 billion and the same 90% mix, implying about $351 billion\\u2014above the $350 billion boundary but below $400 billion. Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, management's Q2 revenue guide and comment that Data Center growth will accelerate, and SemiAnalysis's quantified H2 upside view support the run-rate. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans products and calendar years and is not assigned in full to FY2027; AMD deployments and China-related risks temper the forecast. The new SpaceX Rubin satellite plan adds only unquantified, conditional late-2027 demand, not enough to move the implied figure across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e1415\\\", \\\"e1416\\\", \\\"e1419\\\", \\\"e1410\\\", \\\"e1411\\\", \\\"e1412\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e289\\\"]\"], [8829, \"2026-08-05\", 3, \"The market reference is about $393 billion of FY2027 total revenue. Applying the approximately 90% Data Center share estimate for FY2027/FY2028 implies about $354 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3.\", \"About $354 billion: the total-revenue dependency forecasts roughly $393 billion for FY2027, and applying the approximately 90% Data Center share estimate gives $393 billion \\u00d7 90% \\u2248 $354 billion. Q1\\u2019s 92% actual share is supportive, but the 90% forward estimate is the basis for the FY2027 projection.\", \"The reference is approximately $393 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue; applying the cited estimate that about 90% of FY2027/FY2028 revenue will be Data Center gives roughly $354 billion, in rung 3. Q1\\u2019s 92% Data Center mix and management\\u2019s Q2 acceleration comment support that mix, while the total-revenue dependency\\u2019s forecast remains below $400 billion. New evidence of SpaceX\\u2019s planned 10 GW of compute capacity and exclusive NVIDIA use supports demand, but gives no NVIDIA revenue amount; AMD deployments, domestic Chinese chip adoption, and memory constraints offset some upside. The implied figure remains above the $350 billion boundary but does not cross $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e1438\\\", \\\"e1457\\\", \\\"e1447\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e1412\\\"]\"], [8835, \"2026-08-06\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus is stated. The available market reference is about $393 billion for NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue; applying the estimate that roughly 90% of FY2027/FY2028 revenue comes from Data Center implies about $354 billion, which is rung 3.\", \"Roughly $375\\u2013385 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue. This starts from the prior roughly $354 billion estimate, incorporates SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% second-half upside view directionally, and discounts the unquantified uplift so the segment remains below the $390\\u2013395 billion total-revenue forecast and leaves room for other segments.\", \"The available market reference is about $393 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The total-revenue dependency currently implies about $390\\u2013395 billion, so Data Center cannot exceed that whole. The quantified SemiAnalysis view that second-half Data Center revenue could beat estimates by 20% raises the segment forecast above the old roughly $354 billion calculation, but its baseline is not supplied and the uplift cannot be carried through in full without conflicting with the total-revenue constraint and leaving no room for other segments. The new SpaceX capacity disclosures support demand but do not state NVIDIA revenue or FY2027 shipment timing. On balance, roughly $375\\u2013385 billion is implied: above $350 billion, but below $400 billion, so the rung remains 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e638\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e758\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e761\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e1028\\\", \\\"e1125\\\", \\\"e1266\\\", \\\"e1290\\\", \\\"e1291\\\", \\\"e1362\\\", \\\"e1374\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1379\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1409\\\", \\\"e1411\\\", \\\"e1412\\\", \\\"e1415\\\", \\\"e1416\\\", \\\"e1417\\\", \\\"e1437\\\", \\\"e1438\\\", \\\"e1441\\\", \\\"e1442\\\", \\\"e1446\\\", \\\"e1447\\\", \\\"e1448\\\", \\\"e1449\\\", \\\"e1450\\\", \\\"e1456\\\", \\\"e1458\\\", \\\"e1467\\\", \\\"e1468\\\", \\\"e1472\\\", \\\"e1476\\\", \\\"e1477\\\", \\\"e1479\\\", \\\"e1480\\\", \\\"e1481\\\", \\\"e1482\\\", \\\"e1483\\\", \\\"e1484\\\", \\\"e1485\\\", \\\"e1486\\\", \\\"e1487\\\", \\\"e1488\\\", \\\"e1496\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\"]\"], [8838, \"2026-08-07\", 3, \"Approximately $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue, translated using the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate to about $354 billion; that reference is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $375\\u2013385 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue, with a central estimate near $380 billion. The updated $400\\u2013410 billion total-revenue forecast implies roughly $360\\u2013377 billion at a 90\\u201392% segment share, with the second-half outperformance view and demand disclosures supporting a modest uplift, subject to the total-revenue ceiling.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The updated total-revenue dependency implies approximately $400\\u2013410 billion for NVIDIA as a whole. Applying the roughly 90\\u201392% Data Center share indicated by the segment mix gives about $360\\u2013377 billion; the SemiAnalysis view that second-half Data Center revenue could beat estimates by 20%, plus demand and capacity disclosures, supports a modest uplift to roughly $375\\u2013385 billion, with a central estimate around $380 billion. That remains below the $400 billion rung boundary, and the segment forecast does not exceed the whole-company forecast. Competition and China-related risks temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e1496\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\"]\"], [8842, \"2026-08-08\", 3, \"About $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue, corresponding to an estimated Data Center reference near $354 billion using an approximately 90% segment share; this is rung 3.\", \"The current whole-company forecast of about $405\\u2013415 billion, multiplied by a 90\\u201392% Data Center share, implies roughly $365\\u2013382 billion before an uplift. Q1 growth, the SemiAnalysis second-half estimate, and customer-demand evidence support a central estimate around $390 billion, still below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast is about $405\\u2013415 billion. Applying a 90\\u201392% Data Center share gives roughly $365\\u2013382 billion; Q1\\u2019s strong growth, the SemiAnalysis estimate of a 20% second-half beat versus estimates, and evidence of additional customer demand support an uplift to a central estimate around $390 billion. That remains below the $400 billion boundary, so the forecast stays in rung 3. SpaceX\\u2019s GPU commitments support demand but do not state NVIDIA revenue, and AMD competition, China-related risks, and supply constraints temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e1496\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\"]\"], [8846, \"2026-08-09\", 3, \"Wall Street's average FY2027 total-revenue estimate is about $393 billion. Applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, which is rung 3.\", \"About $374 billion: the current dependency forecast of about $415 billion of total FY2027 revenue multiplied by the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate. The 92% share reported for Q1 would imply about $382 billion if sustained, still below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast is about $415 billion. Applying the explicit roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate gives about $374 billion; the latest Q1 mix was higher at 92%, which would imply about $382 billion if sustained. Accelerating growth guidance, the second-half estimate-beat forecast, and disclosed customer commitments support the upper part of that range, while those commitments do not all translate into NVIDIA FY2027 segment revenue, and memory constraints and competition temper the upside. Even the 92% case remains below the $400 billion rung boundary, so the forecast stays in rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e1496\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1445\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e109\\\"]\"], [8850, \"2026-08-10\", 3, \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue. Applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $378 billion: applying a 90%\\u201392% Data Center share to dependency v14's approximately $415 billion total-revenue forecast gives roughly $374\\u2013$382 billion, with the central estimate near $378 billion. The reported $75.25 billion Q1 Data Center revenue, Q2 total-revenue guide and accelerating-growth guidance support the run-rate, but do not establish a segment estimate above $400 billion.\", \"The evidence still points to the neutral band. The implied central estimate is about $378 billion, above the market-derived reference but below the $400 billion boundary. The newly disclosed SpaceX capacity agreements add customer-demand support, but they give no NVIDIA revenue amount or FY2027 deployment schedule; the $500 billion financing initiative is likewise for AI infrastructure over time, not specified FY2027 NVIDIA revenue. The $1 trillion Blackwell and Rubin figure spans products and calendar years and is not all Data Center revenue in FY2027. Strong current demand and the second-half estimate-beat view are tempered by memory constraints and competition. The implied segment amount remains below the $415 billion total-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1543\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e304\\\"]\"], [8854, \"2026-08-11\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus is about $393 billion (v14), in rung 3. Using NVIDIA\\u2019s latest 92.3% Data Center mix as a translation gives a Data Center reference of roughly $363 billion, also rung 3.\", \"Approximately $378 billion: apply the latest reported 92.3% Data Center share to v14\\u2019s approximately $410 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast ($410 billion \\u00d7 92.3% \\u2248 $378 billion). This is below the $400 billion boundary and consistent with the whole-company forecast.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion, which v14 places in rung 3; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s latest 92.3% Data Center mix gives a market-reference equivalent of about $363 billion, also rung 3. The evidence supports a higher forecast than that reference: Q1 Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion, up 92% year over year, management guided to accelerating Q2 Data Center growth, and current total-revenue guidance and the v14 forecast imply a strong full-year run-rate. But carrying the latest 92.3% mix across v14\\u2019s approximately $410 billion total-revenue forecast implies about $378 billion of Data Center revenue\\u2014above the reference, but still below the $400 billion rung boundary. Customer demand and capacity financing add upside, while memory constraints, competition, and uncertainty about how much financing converts into NVIDIA revenue temper it. The implied amount remains below the dependency\\u2019s total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1477\\\", \\\"e1479\\\", \\\"e1549\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e1710\\\", \\\"e1711\\\", \\\"e1530\\\"]\"], [8860, \"2026-08-12\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion, converted using the latest 92.3% Data Center mix to about $363 billion; this sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $378 billion: v14\\u2019s approximately $410 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast multiplied by the latest reported Data Center mix of 92.3%.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion, equivalent to roughly $363 billion of Data Center revenue at the latest reported 92.3% mix; that is rung 3. The evidence-supported total-revenue forecast in v14 is about $410 billion, which implies roughly $378 billion of Data Center revenue at the same mix. That is above the reference but remains within rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). New, quantified SpaceX capacity and spending claims add upside, but they are not firm NVIDIA procurement amounts; the $500 billion financing figure has no deployment timetable, and the dependent total-revenue forecast remains about $410 billion. The evidence therefore does not imply a move across the $400 billion boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e1530\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e1472\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1549\\\", \\\"e1609\\\", \\\"e1710\\\", \\\"e1711\\\", \\\"e1718\\\"]\"], [8864, \"2026-08-13\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion; at NVIDIA\\u2019s recent Data Center mix of about 92%, that implies roughly $362 billion of Data Center revenue, in rung 3.\", \"About $368\\u2013377 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue: the current v14 total-revenue forecast of $400\\u2013410 billion multiplied by the recent reported Data Center share of about 92%. This is a forecast allocation of the whole-company outlook, not an annualization of Q1 alone.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion. Applying NVIDIA\\u2019s recent Data Center mix of about 92% puts the segment reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is about $400\\u2013410 billion; applying the recent 92% Data Center mix implies approximately $368\\u2013377 billion, also rung 3. Q1\\u2019s reported growth and Q2 guidance support a strong trajectory, while the quantified upside from demand and financing-related announcements is not enough to take the implied segment figure above $400 billion: many announced amounts span periods or products, and several are prospective rather than firm NVIDIA revenue. Competitive, China and supply risks also temper the forecast. The implied Data Center revenue remains below the rung-2 boundary and cannot exceed the dependent whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e1477\\\", \\\"e1479\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1438\\\", \\\"e1457\\\", \\\"e1710\\\", \\\"e1711\\\", \\\"e1545\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e1472\\\"]\"], [8868, \"2026-08-14\", 3, \"FY2027 total-revenue market consensus is about $393 billion (rung 3); applying NVIDIA\\u2019s recent 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, also rung 3.\", \"About $368\\u2013377 billion, with a central estimate near $373 billion: dependency v14 forecasts total FY2027 revenue of about $400\\u2013410 billion, and the reported Q1 Data Center share is about 92% ($75.25 billion of $81.6 billion).\", \"The market reference is FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s recent 92% Data Center mix gives about $362 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is about $400\\u2013410 billion. Applying the reported 92% Data Center mix implies roughly $368\\u2013377 billion, still rung 3. Q1 growth, Q2 guidance and management\\u2019s expectation of accelerating Data Center growth support that run-rate. Multi-year or unsized demand and financing signals add upside, but do not imply enough FY2027 NVIDIA segment revenue to cross $400 billion; China\\u2019s zero-shipment quarter and growing AMD competition are offsets.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e1477\\\", \\\"e1479\\\", \\\"e1570\\\", \\\"e1438\\\", \\\"e1711\\\", \\\"e1549\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e1447\\\", \\\"e1448\\\", \\\"e638\\\"]\"], [8872, \"2026-08-15\", 3, \"FY2027 total-revenue consensus is about $393 billion. Applying the reported 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, in rung 3.\", \"The current FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14 is about $400\\u2013405 billion; applying the reported 92% Data Center mix implies approximately $368\\u2013373 billion. That remains below $400 billion and within rung 3.\", \"The market reference is FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion; applying the reported 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is about $400\\u2013405 billion. Carrying forward the observed 92% segment mix implies approximately $368\\u2013373 billion, also rung 3. Recent demand and SpaceX-related signals add upside, but their disclosed amounts cover multi-period financing, capacity or infrastructure spending\\u2014not NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center revenue dollar for dollar. At the dependency forecast, crossing into rung 2 would require Data Center to exceed roughly 98.8% of total revenue at the top of the range; the evidence does not support that mix.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1028\\\", \\\"e1125\\\", \\\"e1290\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1411\\\", \\\"e1412\\\", \\\"e1438\\\", \\\"e1477\\\", \\\"e1479\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1545\\\", \\\"e1682\\\", \\\"e1686\\\", \\\"e1711\\\", \\\"e1719\\\", \\\"e1764\\\", \\\"e1766\\\", \\\"e1841\\\", \\\"e1858\\\", \\\"e1860\\\", \\\"e1867\\\", \\\"e1868\\\", \\\"e1876\\\", \\\"e1879\\\"]\"], [8877, \"2026-08-16\", 3, \"FY2027 total-revenue estimates near $393 billion imply about $362 billion of Data Center revenue using the observed 92% mix; that reference is rung 3.\", \"About $364 billion before an upside allowance: the current v14 forecast of roughly $396 billion in total FY2027 revenue multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center mix gives about $364 billion. The SemiAnalysis forecast of H2 Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates lifts the outlook, but does not support a Data Center figure at or above the current total-revenue forecast.\", \"The market reference for Data Center is approximately $362 billion: FY2027 total-revenue estimates near $393 billion, multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center mix, place it in rung 3. The evidence supports growth above that reference, but the current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is about $396 billion. At the observed mix that implies about $364 billion; even allowing for the specific H2 upside view, the segment remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. A Data Center outcome of $400 billion or more would exceed the current forecast for NVIDIA's total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e1448\\\"]\"], [8880, \"2026-08-17\", 3, \"Market estimates put FY2027 total NVIDIA revenue at about $393 billion; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $360 billion, derived from v14's current approximately $391 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast multiplied by the 92% Data Center mix reported for Q1. The H2 view of 20% outperformance versus analyst estimates is positive but supplies no absolute figure to replace that estimate.\", \"The market reference is about $362 billion ($393 billion of FY2027 total-revenue estimates multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center mix), which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $360 billion: the current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is about $391 billion, and applying the observed 92% Data Center share gives approximately $360 billion. Q1 Data Center growth, Q2 guidance and the H2 outperformance view support growth, but the H2 view gives no absolute amount and the forecast remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. Competition and the absence of China revenue from the outlook temper the upside; the announced Ohio capacity is phased to begin in 2028, so it does not justify counting that buildout as FY2027 revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e1472\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e1953\\\"]\"], [8884, \"2026-08-18\", 3, \"About $362 billion, derived from the roughly $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate multiplied by the 92% observed Data Center mix; this sits in rung 3.\", \"About $360 billion at the baseline: the v14 forecast of roughly $391 billion total revenue multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center share. The H2 outperformance view raises the upside, but the v14 total-revenue forecast caps Data Center revenue at $391 billion, still below the $400 billion boundary.\", \"The market reference is about $362 billion (FY2027 total-revenue estimates of about $393 billion multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center mix), which is rung 3. The current baseline is about $360 billion: the v14 FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $391 billion multiplied by the 92% mix. Q1 growth, accelerating Q2 guidance, and SemiAnalysis' view that H2 Data Center revenue could exceed analyst estimates by 20% tilt the forecast upward, but no absolute H2 amount is supplied. Even a generous uplift is constrained by v14's $391 billion total-revenue forecast: Data Center revenue cannot exceed total revenue, so it cannot reach the $400 billion rung boundary. The PORTS-Pike capacity is scheduled to come online in phases beginning in 2028, so it does not justify counting that buildout as FY2027 revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e1472\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e1953\\\"]\"], [8890, \"2026-08-19\", 3, \"Market expectations are about $393 billion for FY2027 total revenue, which is rung 3 for total revenue; at the observed 92% Data Center mix, the comparable Data Center reference is about $362 billion, rung 3.\", \"About $371 billion: v14 forecasts roughly $403.5 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying NVIDIA\\u2019s observed 92% Data Center share gives approximately $403.5 billion \\u00d7 92% = $371 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix puts the reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast is about $403.5 billion (v14); applying the observed 92% mix implies about $371 billion of Data Center revenue, also rung 3. Q1\\u2019s 92% year-over-year Data Center growth, accelerating Q2 growth guidance, and SemiAnalysis\\u2019 H2 outperformance view support upside, but the latter gives no absolute H2 amount. At the current total-revenue forecast, Data Center would need to be over 99% of revenue to reach $400 billion, versus the observed 92% mix. SpaceX\\u2019s large compute target is a potential demand source, but the chip-spending estimate is not a sized NVIDIA FY2027 revenue commitment. The PORTS-Pike capacity is expected to come online in phases beginning in 2028, so it does not warrant counting that buildout in FY2027.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e1472\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e2115\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e1876\\\", \\\"e1877\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e1942\\\"]\"], [8894, \"2026-08-20\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Yahoo! Finance's approximately $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate is the reference. It is rung 3 for total revenue; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix gives approximately $362 billion, rung 3 on this ladder.\", \"The v14 total-revenue forecast of approximately $403.5 billion, applied at the observed 92% Data Center mix, implies about $371 billion for the segment. The quantified 20% H2 outperformance view is an upside risk, but its H2 base is unstated and applying it mechanically would imply about $413 billion\\u2014more than the supplied whole-company forecast.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue, which sits in rung 3; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, also rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast is about $403.5 billion (v14); at the observed 92% mix that implies about $371 billion of Data Center revenue. SemiAnalysis\\u2019 forecast that second-half Data Center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% is a meaningful upside signal, but it gives no absolute H2 base. Applying 20% to the roughly $209 billion H2 Data Center amount implied by v14 would put the segment near $413 billion\\u2014above the supplied whole-company revenue forecast. Without a corresponding change to that whole forecast or evidence that the segment\\u2019s share rises sharply from 92%, I do not carry that full uplift into this thesis. SpaceX\\u2019s chip-spending estimate and the PORTS-Pike capacity are not sized FY2027 NVIDIA revenue: the former targets end-2027 compute, while PORTS-Pike capacity is expected to come online beginning in 2028. The forecast therefore remains below the $400 billion rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e1972\\\", \\\"e1877\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e1953\\\", \\\"e2115\\\"]\"], [8898, \"2026-08-21\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus figure is supplied. The proxy market reference is the $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection in v14 times the observed 92% Data Center mix, or about $362 billion\\u2014rung 3.\", \"About $371\\u2013372 billion: v14\\u2019s roughly $403\\u2013404 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast multiplied by NVIDIA\\u2019s reported 92% Q1 FY2027 Data Center share. This falls in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"The market reference is about $362 billion: the Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion, multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center mix, places the reference in rung 3. The forecast implied by the evidence remains about $371 billion: v14 forecasts roughly $403\\u2013404 billion of total FY2027 revenue, and applying the 92% mix reported for Q1 gives about $371\\u2013372 billion. That is above $350 billion but below $400 billion, so it remains rung 3. SemiAnalysis\\u2019 20% H2 upside view and SpaceX-related demand estimates support upside risk, but do not establish an absolute FY2027 segment amount that can override the whole-company forecast; the announced Ohio capacity is expected to start coming online in 2028. Competition and component availability are counterweights. The implied segment figure is also below the v14 whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e1876\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e2146\\\"]\"], [8902, \"2026-08-22\", 3, \"About $362 billion, derived from Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center mix; this is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $371\\u2013372 billion: the current FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $403\\u2013404 billion (v14), multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center share, implies this segment amount.\", \"The reference is about $362 billion: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus, multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center mix, sits in rung 3. The forecast remains about $371\\u2013372 billion: the current whole-company forecast is approximately $403\\u2013404 billion, and applying the 92% reported mix gives that segment estimate. It is above $350 billion but below $400 billion. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility and SpaceX-related spending estimates support upside, but span multiple periods and are not FY2027 NVIDIA Data Center revenue in hand; SemiAnalysis's 20% H2 upside view has no stated absolute baseline. The $91 billion Q2 company guide is a counterweight, and the whole-company forecast leaves only about $3\\u20134 billion of headroom above a $400 billion segment result, so the evidence does not imply the near-100% segment share required to cross that boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e1942\\\"]\"], [8905, \"2026-08-23\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus, converted using the reported 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $372 billion: the current whole-company forecast of roughly $404 billion in v14 multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center share. This is a stable-mix base estimate; the new upside signals lack a quantifiable FY2027 revenue increment.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus, translated at the reported 92% Data Center share to about $362 billion; that is rung 3. The current whole-company forecast in dependency v14 is roughly $404 billion. Applying the reported 92% mix gives about $372 billion of Data Center revenue, still below the $400 billion rung-2 threshold. Moving above $400 billion would require Data Center to be about 99% of the $404 billion whole, well above the reported mix. The SemiAnalysis view of 20% H2 outperformance and the SpaceX chip-spending estimate add upside, but neither gives a usable FY2027 NVIDIA revenue increment; several large capacity plans are also timed for 2028. They do not outweigh the whole-company constraint or justify crossing a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e1876\\\", \\\"e1457\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e1953\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e2179\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e1972\\\", \\\"e2221\\\", \\\"e2222\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\"]\"], [8912, \"2026-08-24\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue consensus is $393 billion (v14); applying the reported 92% Data Center share implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue, which sits in rung 3.\", \"About $370 billion: use $75.25 billion reported in Q1 and the $85.14 billion Q2 Data Center consensus, then apply the recent 92% Data Center mix to v14's roughly $107.5 billion Q3 and $120 billion Q4 total-revenue forecast. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $393 billion, translated using NVIDIA's reported 92% Data Center mix to about $362 billion; that is rung 3. The forecast is higher, but remains below $400 billion: strong Q1 results and a $85.14 billion Q2 Data Center consensus are partly offset by competition and the fact that several large capacity plans are not expected to come online until 2028. The implied estimate therefore does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e2358\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e1953\\\", \\\"e2179\\\", \\\"e2221\\\", \\\"e2312\\\", \\\"e2313\\\"]\"], [8916, \"2026-08-25\", 3, \"The latest supplied FY2027 market estimate is $396 billion for NVIDIA total revenue. Applying the reported 92% Q1 Data Center share gives an approximate Data Center reference of $364 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $370\\u2013375 billion for FY2027: use reported Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, the roughly $85\\u201386 billion Q2 estimates, then carry approximately 14% sequential growth forward through Q3 and Q4. This remains below the dependent FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $404\\u2013405 billion.\", \"The market reference translates to about $364 billion of Data Center revenue ($396 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate \\u00d7 the reported 92% Q1 Data Center mix), which is rung 3. The quarterly run-rate implies roughly $370\\u2013375 billion: Q1 was $75.25 billion, Q2 estimates are about $85\\u201386 billion, and carrying roughly 14% sequential growth forward for Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $370 billion. That is above the reference but remains within rung 3\\u2019s $350\\u2013400 billion range. It is also below the dependent FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $404\\u2013405 billion. Competition, export-policy risks and memory constraints temper the upside; the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and periods and is not a direct FY2027 Data Center revenue estimate.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e2420\\\", \\\"e2418\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e2228\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e2358\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e994\\\"]\"], [8921, \"2026-08-26\", 3, \"About $364 billion, derived from the dependent FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $396 billion multiplied by the reported 92% Q1 Data Center mix; this is rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion for FY2027: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 (the $108 billion total-revenue guidance at the Q2 Data Center mix of about 92.5%), and about $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 total-revenue guidance.\", \"The market reference is about $364 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue: the dependent thesis\\u2019s $396 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate multiplied by NVIDIA\\u2019s reported 92% Q1 Data Center mix. That is rung 3. The reported Q2 Data Center revenue was $89 billion on $96.2 billion total revenue, a 92.5% mix. Applying that mix to the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guidance implies about $99.9 billion of Q3 Data Center revenue; carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 total-revenue guidance implies about $112.2 billion in Q4. With Q1 at $75.25 billion, that gives roughly $376 billion for FY2027\\u2014above the reference but still within rung 3. This remains below the dependent FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $407 billion. Rubin shipments and broad demand support the outlook, but the Rubin Q3 contribution is not a separate incremental revenue figure, and NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2028 supply constraint and zero-China forward outlook temper upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2510\\\"]\"], [8925, \"2026-08-27\", 3, \"The market reference is about $364 billion, derived from the LSEG FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $396 billion and NVIDIA's reported approximately 92% Data Center mix; this is rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion: add Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and Q2 revenue of $89 billion, estimate Q3 at about $99.9 billion by applying Q2's roughly 92.5% Data Center mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guidance, then carry the guidance-implied 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 for about $112.2 billion.\", \"The implied FY2027 Data Center revenue is about $376 billion, above the $364 billion reference but still below the $400 billion boundary for rung 2. The recently reported Q2 result and Q3 total-revenue guidance support strong growth, but carrying forward the guided growth does not produce enough revenue to cross that boundary. The estimate is consistent with v14's current approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast: the Data Center estimate remains below the whole-company total. Rubin shipments and purchase orders support demand, but the stated Q3 Rubin share is part of Data Center revenue, not incremental to it.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2483\\\"]\"], [8929, \"2026-08-28\", 3, \"The supplied market reference is approximately $364 billion, which sits on rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue totaled about $164.3 billion ($75.25 billion plus $89 billion). Applying the observed Q2 Data Center share of 92.5% to Q3 revenue guidance of $108 billion implies about $99.9 billion for Q3; carrying that roughly 12.2% sequential growth forward implies about $112.1 billion for Q4, or approximately $376 billion for FY2027.\", \"The implied outcome remains within rung 3: the sequential-growth carry-forward puts FY2027 Data Center revenue near $376 billion, above the $350 billion floor but below $400 billion. Q4 hyperscale reacceleration is a positive but unquantified input, so it does not support a numerical forecast above that carry-forward; the dependent whole-company forecast of about $407 billion also accommodates this segment estimate.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2572\\\"]\"], [8932, \"2026-08-29\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center annual consensus figure is supplied. The available Morningstar view says FY2027 Data Center revenue should be well over $300 billion, which only places its stated floor at rung 4; it does not specify a unique rung. The $85.14 billion consensus is for Q2, not the fiscal year. The LSEG $396 billion reference in v14 is for total FY2027 revenue, sits at rung 3 on that separate ladder, and is not a direct Data Center reference.\", \"About $376 billion: add reported Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and Q2 revenue of $89.0 billion to implied Q3 revenue of about $99.9 billion ($108 billion total guidance \\u00d7 the Q2 Data Center mix of 92.5%), then carry the roughly 12.2% sequential Data Center growth into Q4, implying about $112.1 billion. This is below the $400 billion boundary and below the dependent whole-company forecast of about $407 billion.\", \"The implied estimate remains in rung 3. The new Q2 actual and Q3 guidance give a more direct run-rate than the prior estimate; the implied Q4 carry-forward lifts the full-year figure only to about $376 billion, still below the $400 billion rung boundary. Q4 hyperscale growth is expected to reaccelerate, but no amount is given, so it does not justify adding a quantified uplift to the run-rate. The estimate is also consistent with the dependent whole-company forecast of about $407 billion: Data Center is a component and the estimate does not exceed that total.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2115\\\", \\\"e2333\\\"]\"], [8938, \"2026-08-30\", 3, \"LSEG's $396 billion and Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus (v14), translated at NVIDIA's approximately 92.5% Q2 Data Center mix (e3216), implies about $364\\u2013366 billion of Data Center revenue: rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion for FY2027: $75.25 billion Q1 actual + $89.0 billion Q2 actual + approximately $99.9 billion Q3 (92.5% of the $108 billion total-revenue guidance) + approximately $112.2 billion Q4 (carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance).\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion and Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus, both stated in v14. Applying the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% gives roughly $364\\u2013366 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, then about $99.9 billion in Q3 by applying the Q2 Data Center mix to NVIDIA's $108 billion total-revenue guidance, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by that guidance. This is above the consensus-derived reference but remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. Rubin production shipments, purchase orders, and the expected 20% Q3 Data Center contribution support the run-rate; however, Q4 reacceleration is not quantified, supply is constrained, and the large AWS GPU deployment spans 2027\\u20132028 rather than establishing enough FY2027 revenue to bridge the roughly $24 billion gap to rung 2. The estimate is consistent with v14's approximately $407 billion whole-company forecast: Data Center remains below the total.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e2839\\\"]\"], [8943, \"2026-08-31\", 3, \"LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus (v14) is the available market reference; it sits in rung 3 for total revenue. Applying the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% gives an approximate Data Center reference of $366 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion: $75.25 billion Q1 actual + $89.0 billion Q2 actual + about $99.9 billion Q3 (92.5% of the $108 billion total-revenue guide) + about $112.2 billion Q4 (carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by the Q3 total-revenue guide). This is consistent with v14's approximately $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $396 billion, which v14 places in rung 3 for total revenue. Applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% gives an approximate Data Center reference of $366 billion, also rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, then about $99.9 billion in Q3 by applying the Q2 Data Center mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guide, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by the Q3 total-revenue guide. That remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. Strong demand, Rubin shipments and the expected Q3 Rubin contribution support the run-rate, but supply constraints and competition temper upside; the estimate is consistent with v14's roughly $407.1 billion total-revenue forecast and does not exceed the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e2510\\\"]\"], [8946, \"2026-09-01\", 3, \"LSEG FY2027 total-revenue consensus is $396 billion; applying the latest reported 92.5% Data Center mix gives an approximate Data Center reference of $366 billion, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376.4 billion: $75.25 billion reported in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using Q2's 92.5% Data Center mix on the $108 billion total-revenue guide, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by that guide.\", \"The market reference is approximately $366 billion, derived by applying the latest reported Data Center mix of 92.5% to the LSEG FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $396 billion; that reference sits in rung 3. The run-rate estimate is about $376.4 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, approximately $99.9 billion in Q3 using the Q2 Data Center mix on the $108 billion total-revenue guide, and approximately $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by that guide. This is consistent with the total-revenue dependency\\u2019s approximately $407.1 billion forecast and remains below the $400 billion boundary. Rubin shipments and orders, accelerating demand, and announced future deployments support the outlook, but the multi-period deployment plans are not all FY2027 revenue; competition and supply constraints temper the upside. The evidence therefore does not move the forecast across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3224\\\", \\\"e3234\\\"]\"], [8950, \"2026-09-02\", 3, \"About $366 billion, derived from the LSEG FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $396 billion multiplied by the latest reported Data Center mix of 92.5%; this sits in rung 3.\", \"About $376.4 billion: $75.25 billion Q1 + $89.0 billion Q2 + an estimated $99.9 billion Q3 (92.5% of the $108 billion total-revenue guide) + an estimated $112.2 billion Q4 (carrying forward 12.3% sequential growth).\", \"The market reference is about $366 billion: applying the latest reported 92.5% Data Center mix to the $396 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus puts the reference in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376.4 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2; estimate Q3 at $99.9 billion by applying the reported 92.5% mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guide; then estimate Q4 at $112.2 billion by carrying forward 12.3% sequential growth. This is below $400 billion, so it remains rung 3. It is consistent with the dependency forecast of about $407.1 billion in total revenue and leaves room for non-Data-Center revenue. Rubin's expected Q3 contribution and the expected Q4 hyperscale reacceleration support the run-rate, while the no-China outlook and supply constraints temper upside; neither quantifies a change large enough to cross a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2572\\\"]\"], [8952, \"2026-09-03\", 3, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 total-revenue consensus is $396 billion. Applying the latest reported Data Center share of roughly 92.5% implies about $366.3 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3.\", \"About $376.4 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2; estimate Q3 at about $99.9 billion by applying Q2's 92.5% Data Center mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guide; estimate Q4 at about $112.2 billion by applying that mix to the dependency's approximately $121.3 billion Q4 total-revenue forecast.\", \"The market reference is about $366.3 billion: applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% to the $396 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus puts the reference in rung 3. That reference alone does not determine the forecast. Using the reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue, applying the Q2 mix to the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide, and carrying the dependency's Q4 total-revenue run-rate forward at that mix implies about $376.4 billion for FY2027. This is consistent with the total-revenue dependency and leaves about $30.7 billion for non-Data-Center revenue. The implication remains below the $400 billion boundary, so it stays in rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2562\\\"]\"], [8956, \"2026-09-04\", 3, \"LSEG FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $396 billion, translated at the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $366 billion of Data Center revenue. This is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376.4 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue sum to $164.25 billion; applying the Q2 mix to Q3's $108 billion total-revenue guide implies about $99.9 billion, and applying it to the dependency's $121.3 billion Q4 run-rate implies about $112.2 billion. The resulting FY2027 estimate is below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is approximately $366 billion: applying the latest reported Data Center mix of 92.5% to the LSEG FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $396 billion places it in rung 3. For the forecast, Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; applying the Q2 mix to NVIDIA's $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide gives about $99.9 billion, and applying that mix to the dependency's $121.3 billion Q4 run-rate gives about $112.2 billion for Q4, or approximately $376.4 billion for FY2027. That is consistent with the $407.1 billion total-revenue dependency forecast and leaves about $30.7 billion of non-Data-Center revenue. Q4 hyperscaler reacceleration and the Rubin ramp are upside risks, but no supplied amount supports lifting the implied FY2027 Data Center total to the $400 billion rung boundary; conversely, the no-China outlook limits upside. The forecast therefore remains in rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e636\\\"]\"], [8960, \"2026-09-05\", 3, \"Approximately $366 billion, derived from the LSEG FY2027 total-revenue expectation of $396 billion and the latest Data Center share of 92.5%; this reference is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376 billion: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; applying the latest 92.5% mix to Q3's $108 billion total-revenue guide implies about $99.9 billion, and applying it to the dependency's approximately $121.3 billion Q4 run-rate implies about $112.2 billion. The result is below the dependency's approximately $407.1 billion total-revenue forecast and falls in rung 3.\", \"The reference is approximately $366 billion: the prior market reference of $396 billion for FY2027 total revenue, multiplied by the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, places it in rung 3. The evidence-based forecast is about $376 billion, also rung 3. While new Rubin and hyperscaler-demand signals strengthen the upside case, the H2 outperformance estimate has no stated analyst-estimate base to translate into dollars, and the dependency forecast for total FY2027 revenue constrains the Data Center segment to be no more than the whole. The implied Data Center figure remains below $400 billion and above $350 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\"]\"], [8964, \"2026-09-06\", 3, \"Approximately $380 billion, derived from the latest supplied FY2027 total-revenue expectation of about $411 billion (v14) multiplied by the reported 92.5% Data Center share (e3216); this is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $377 billion. Applying the latest 92.5% Data Center mix to v14's $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast gives about $376.6 billion; the quarter-by-quarter run-rate is consistent with this: $75.25 billion in Q1, $89.0 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using $108 billion total-revenue guidance and the recent mix, and about $112 billion in Q4 carrying forward the dependency's guided growth.\", \"The market reference is approximately $380 billion: the latest FY2027 total-revenue expectation supplied by dependency v14 is about $411 billion, and applying NVIDIA's latest reported Data Center mix of 92.5% gives about $380 billion, which is rung 3. The evidence-based forecast is about $377 billion: use v14's $407.1 billion whole-company forecast and apply the 92.5% Data Center mix. This is consistent with $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89.0 billion in Q2, approximately $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guidance and the latest mix, and about $112 billion in Q4 carrying forward the dependency's guided-growth run-rate. It remains below $400 billion and above $350 billion, so the reference and implied outcome both sit at rung 3. Strong demand and Rubin ramp signals support the forecast, while supply constraints, no China compute revenue in the forward outlook, and competition limit the upside; none of the evidence or dependency conclusions implies a Data Center total at or above $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e2115\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2839\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e3224\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e3751\\\", \\\"e3753\\\", \\\"e3805\\\", \\\"e3839\\\", \\\"e3845\\\", \\\"e3885\\\", \\\"e3886\\\", \\\"e3918\\\", \\\"e3920\\\", \\\"e3922\\\", \\\"e3923\\\", \\\"e3924\\\", \\\"e3925\\\", \\\"e3926\\\", \\\"e3927\\\", \\\"e3975\\\", \\\"e3976\\\", \\\"e3978\\\", \\\"e4002\\\", \\\"e4041\\\", \\\"e4046\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e4073\\\", \\\"e4077\\\", \\\"e4078\\\", \\\"e4091\\\", \\\"e4093\\\", \\\"e4094\\\"]\"], [8969, \"2026-09-07\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus is supplied. The comparison reference is the latest Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue expectation of about $411 billion from v14, translated using the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% to approximately $380 billion; that is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"Approximately $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion for Q3 ($108 billion total guidance \\u00d7 92.5%), and about $112.2 billion for Q4 ($121.3 billion from v14 \\u00d7 92.5%).\", \"The market reference is about $380 billion for Data Center: the latest FY2027 total-revenue expectation supplied by v14 is about $411 billion (rung 2 on the total-revenue ladder), and applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% places the reference in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; Q3 is about $99.9 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guidance and the latest 92.5% mix; and Q4 is about $112.2 billion by applying that mix to v14's $121.3 billion Q4 total-revenue forecast. The implied FY2027 total is below $400 billion and above $350 billion, so it remains rung 3. Rubin's expected 20% Q3 contribution and Q4 acceleration support the growth outlook, but are not additional revenue on top of the segment total; AWS's 2027\\u201328 GPU plan is not allocated to FY2027. The estimate is below v14's $407.1 billion whole-company forecast, as required.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e976\\\"]\"], [8974, \"2026-09-08\", 3, \"The latest stated Wall Street expectation is about $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue. Using the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% implies approximately $380 billion of Data Center revenue, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376 billion for FY2027: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; estimate Q3 at about $99.9 billion by applying the latest 92.5% mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guide; then estimate Q4 at about $112.2 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential total-revenue growth implied by the guide and applying the same mix. This is below $400 billion and consistent with v14's approximately $407.1 billion whole-company forecast.\", \"The market reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% implies roughly $380 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3. The updated evidence-based estimate is also in rung 3. Q3 and Q4 estimates remain below the $400 billion boundary, and the dependent total-revenue forecast is about $407.1 billion, so Data Center revenue cannot exceed that whole. Strong growth, Rubin shipments, and demand support the forecast, but the unquantified Q4 acceleration does not justify adding a specific amount to the run-rate; supply limits, competition, and no China compute revenue in the outlook also temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e2115\\\"]\"], [8978, \"2026-09-09\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus figure is supplied. The market reference is approximately $411B of FY2027 total revenue; applying the latest reported Data Center share of about 92.5% gives approximately $380B, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376B: $75.25B in Q1 plus $89B in Q2, about $99.9B in Q3 (the $108B total-revenue guide at a 92.5% Data Center mix), and about $112B in Q4 (the dependent ~$121B total forecast at that mix).\", \"The market reference is approximately $411B of FY2027 total revenue; applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% implies roughly $380B of Data Center revenue, which sits in rung 3. The updated run-rate is about $376B: $75.25B in Q1, $89B in Q2, approximately $99.9B in Q3 using the $108B total-revenue guide and 92.5% mix, and about $112B in Q4 using the dependent ~$121B total-revenue forecast and the same mix. That is still within rung 3. Q4 hyperscale growth acceleration and Rubin shipments support the forecast, but do not quantify an increase sufficient to cross $400B. In the dependency forecast, Q4 total revenue is only about $121B, while FY2027 Data Center revenue would need about $135B in Q4 to reach $400B; the current mix-based estimate is well below that. Competition, the exclusion of China Data Center compute revenue from the outlook, and supply constraints also temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e4052\\\"]\"], [8983, \"2026-09-10\", 3, \"The observable market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue, which is rung 2 for total revenue. Applying the latest reported 92.5% Data Center share gives a Data Center reference of approximately $380 billion, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue: $75.25 billion in Q1, $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 (92.5% of the $108 billion total-revenue guide), and about $112.2 billion in Q4 (92.5% of the dependency's roughly $121.3 billion total-revenue forecast).\", \"The market reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue, mapped at the latest 92.5% Data Center mix to about $380 billion of Data Center revenue; that reference is in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the Q2 Data Center mix, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 using the dependency's roughly $121.3 billion total-revenue forecast and the same mix. The estimate remains below the $400 billion boundary. Rubin's expected 20% Q3 contribution is part of, not additional to, Data Center revenue; Q4 acceleration and strong customer demand support the forecast, while the no-China outlook and supply constraints temper upside. The estimate is also consistent with the dependency: approximately $376 billion of Data Center revenue is below its roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e4250\\\", \\\"e4331\\\", \\\"e4185\\\", \\\"e4324\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\"]\"], [8986, \"2026-09-11\", 3, \"Latest supplied analyst expectations put FY2027 total revenue at about $411 billion (rung 2 for total revenue). At the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5%, that implies approximately $380 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide at a 92.5% Data Center mix, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 from the dependency's $121.3 billion total-revenue forecast at the same mix. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary and is consistent with the total-revenue dependency.\", \"The reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% implies roughly $380 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the latest Data Center mix, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 using the dependency's $121.3 billion total-revenue forecast and the same mix. This is consistent with the total-revenue dependency and remains below rung 2's $400 billion floor, so it does not cross a boundary. Rubin's expected Q3 contribution is already included in the mix-based estimate, not added on top. New hyperscaler spending and demand evidence supports the run-rate, while the no-China outlook and supply constraints temper upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e976\\\"]\"], [8990, \"2026-09-12\", 3, \"Analysts' approximately $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation, translated using the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $380 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion for FY2027: $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89 billion in Q2, plus about $99.9 billion for Q3 ($108 billion total-revenue guide at a 92.5% segment mix) and about $112.2 billion for Q4 ($121.3 billion total-revenue estimate from dependency v14 at the same mix).\", \"The reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% puts the reference near $380 billion, in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the latest mix, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 using the dependency's $121.3 billion Q4 estimate and the same mix. That is above rung 4's $350 billion ceiling but below rung 2's $400 billion floor. Rubin's expected Q3 contribution and the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration support the outlook but do not provide a quantified basis to lift the segment estimate across $400 billion; competition and the absence of China compute revenue in the forward outlook temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e4534\\\"]\"], [8995, \"2026-09-13\", 3, \"Analysts expect about $411 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue (v14, rung 2 for total revenue). Applying the latest Data Center share of about 92.5% gives a segment reference of about $380 billion, rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, then approximately $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, and approximately $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3\\u2019s roughly 12% sequential total-revenue growth forward and applying the same mix. This is above rung 4\\u2019s $350 billion ceiling but below rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion floor.\", \"The reference is analysts\\u2019 approximately $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation, which is rung 2 for total revenue; applying the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% puts the segment reference near $380 billion, in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion, also rung 3. Recent customer-demand and product-ramp evidence supports the run-rate, but the Q3 Rubin share and Q4 hyperscale acceleration are not quantified as incremental revenue beyond the estimate; competition and the absence of China compute revenue in NVIDIA\\u2019s forward outlook temper the upside. The evidence does not support the roughly $24 billion increase needed to reach rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion floor.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e4550\\\", \\\"e4572\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\"]\"], [8999, \"2026-09-14\", 3, \"Analysts\\u2019 approximately $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation, translated using the recent 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $380 billion of Data Center revenue\\u2014rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion: Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion plus Q2\\u2019s $89 billion, Q3\\u2019s approximately $99.9 billion implied by the 92.5% mix on the $108 billion guide, and roughly $112 billion for Q4 using the dependency\\u2019s $121 billion total-revenue estimate and the same mix. This estimate is below the $400 billion rung-2 threshold.\", \"The market reference is about $380 billion: the analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation, multiplied by the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5%, falls in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion, also rung 3. Q2\\u2019s reported $89 billion and the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide support a strong run-rate, but carrying the Q3 total-revenue growth forward and applying the recent Data Center mix does not get close to the $400 billion rung-2 floor. Q4 acceleration and Rubin demand are upside risks, but the supplied evidence does not quantify enough additional FY2027 revenue to cross that boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e2115\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e3805\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e4185\\\", \\\"e4317\\\", \\\"e4555\\\", \\\"e4556\\\", \\\"e4557\\\", \\\"e4571\\\", \\\"e4572\\\"]\"], [9004, \"2026-09-15\", 3, \"The market reference is approximately $380 billion, derived by applying the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% to the $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation; this is rung 3.\", \"About $387 billion: the current FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $418 billion, incorporating reported Q1 and Q2 and the forecast Q3/Q4 run-rate, multiplied by the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5%.\", \"The market reference is approximately $380 billion: the $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation multiplied by the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5%, placing the reference in rung 3. The implied estimate is about $387 billion, also rung 3. Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue were $75.25 billion and $89 billion; the Q3 total-revenue guide and expected Rubin contribution support continued growth, while the dependency forecast of about $418 billion in FY2027 total revenue implies about $387 billion of Data Center revenue at the latest mix. That remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong demand and Q4 acceleration support the estimate, but no China compute revenue in the outlook and supply limits temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e127\\\"]\"], [9008, \"2026-09-16\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus figure is supplied. The stated market reference is analysts\\u2019 roughly $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation multiplied by the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5%, or approximately $380 billion; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $388 billion: the v14 FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $419 billion multiplied by the latest reported Data Center share of approximately 92.5% gives roughly $387.6 billion. This is a forecast, not a claim that the full-year result is already established.\", \"The evidence-based forecast remains in rung 3. The latest reported Data Center quarter was $89.0 billion, up 18% sequentially; management guided total Q3 revenue to $108 billion and said Rubin would account for about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue, while hyperscale growth is expected to reaccelerate in Q4. These support continued growth, but the FY2027 total-revenue dependency implies about $419 billion for the year, and applying the latest Data Center share of about 92.5% implies roughly $388 billion of Data Center revenue. That is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong demand and large customer plans support the forecast, while supply limits, no China compute revenue in the outlook, and competition temper the upside; none of the supplied evidence makes a share above roughly 95.5% of the dependency\\u2019s total-revenue forecast the most likely outcome.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e2115\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e3975\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e4534\\\", \\\"e4572\\\", \\\"e4574\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e976\\\"]\"], [9011, \"2026-09-17\", 3, \"The market reference is the approximately $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue analyst estimate (rung 2 for total revenue). Applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% gives an indicative segment reference of roughly $380 billion, which is rung 3.\", \"About $387 billion for FY2027: the current total-revenue dependency forecast is about $419 billion, and applying the recent approximately 92.5% Data Center share implies about $388 billion. The reported Q1 and Q2 segment revenues and the projected Q3/Q4 run-rate are consistent with that estimate.\", \"The market reference is the roughly $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue analyst estimate, which is rung 2 for total revenue; using the latest 92.5% Data Center mix as a proxy implies about $380 billion for this segment, rung 3. Our implied Data Center figure is about $387 billion: the current total-revenue dependency forecasts about $419 billion for FY2027, and recent Data Center mix of roughly 92.5% implies about $388 billion. The reported Q1 and Q2 segment revenues and expected Q3/Q4 growth are consistent with that run-rate. This remains below the $400 billion boundary, so the forecast stays at rung 3. Updated China-authorization and AI-capex themes modestly support the upside, while export-control risk and the absence of China compute revenue in NVIDIA\\u2019s outlook temper it; none changes the implied figure enough to cross a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e4324\\\"]\"], [9015, \"2026-09-18\", 3, \"Approximately $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus, translated using the latest 92.5% Data Center mix to about $380 billion; this proxy sits in rung 3.\", \"About $387 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: the FY2027 total-revenue forecast of roughly $419 billion, including the expected Q3/Q4 growth path, multiplied by the recent 92.5% Data Center mix.\", \"The market reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 total-revenue consensus; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% gives a proxy of roughly $380 billion, in rung 3. The implied Data Center forecast is about $387 billion: Q1 and Q2 contributed about $164 billion, and the FY2027 total-revenue forecast of roughly $419 billion, carried at the recent 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $387 billion for the segment. This is above $350 billion but remains below the $400 billion boundary. Q3 guidance, Rubin shipments and expected Q4 hyperscale acceleration support growth, while the no-China-revenue outlook tempers it; the evidence does not imply enough to cross $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e4550\\\", \\\"e4995\\\"]\"], [9020, \"2026-09-19\", 3, \"Market expectations of about $411 billion for NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue are the reference; applying the latest 92.5% Data Center mix implies about $380 billion of Data Center revenue, at rung 3.\", \"About $387 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, plus roughly $105 billion for Q3 and $118 billion for Q4, estimated by applying the latest 92.5% Data Center mix to the FY2027 total-revenue forecast\\u2019s roughly $113.6 billion Q3 and $127.2 billion Q4 estimates.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 total-revenue consensus; using the latest 92.5% Data Center mix gives a proxy of about $380 billion, which is rung 3. The evidence-derived estimate is about $387 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion of Data Center revenue in the first half; applying the total-revenue forecast\\u2019s Q3 estimate of about $113.6 billion and Q4 estimate of about $127.2 billion at a 92.5% segment mix adds about $223 billion. That remains below the $400 billion boundary. Rubin production shipments, hyperscaler orders and expected Q4 acceleration support the forecast, while the no-China outlook and supply constraints temper it.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e4995\\\", \\\"e4556\\\", \\\"e4557\\\", \\\"e4572\\\", \\\"e4640\\\"]\"], [9024, \"2026-09-20\", 3, \"Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue (v14). Applying the latest reported 92.5% Data Center mix places the reference at approximately $380 billion of Data Center revenue, rung 3.\", \"About $388 billion: v14's approximately $419 billion total-revenue forecast, based on reported first-half results, Q3 guidance adjusted for the reported beat history, and carried-forward sequential growth into Q4, multiplied by the latest 92.5% Data Center mix. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 total-revenue consensus, which the latest 92.5% Data Center mix translates to roughly $380 billion of Data Center revenue, at rung 3. The evidence supports a somewhat higher estimate, but not enough to cross $400 billion: strong demand, Rubin orders and a Q4 acceleration are balanced by supply limits and NVIDIA's no-China-revenue outlook. The updated China-authorization theme adds upside risk, but supplies no volume estimate that would move the base case across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e3805\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e4185\\\", \\\"e4250\\\", \\\"e4317\\\", \\\"e4550\\\", \\\"e4572\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e4995\\\", \\\"e4996\\\", \\\"e4982\\\"]\"]], [\"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 450000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 400000000000.0, 450000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 350000000000.0, 400000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 300000000000.0, 350000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 300000000000.0]]]\n[\"linked-1\", \"v21\", [[8794, \"2026-07-26\", 3, \"Wall Street estimates 42% FY2027 revenue growth; that is rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Approximately 42%, with an upside skew: the near-$20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue forecast and the demand-related themes support growth, but the supplied evidence does not quantify an uplift to NVIDIA\\u2019s total growth estimate.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The CPU-revenue forecast and the capex and export-control themes add upside support, but none supplies a quantified revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s total-revenue growth; the evidence therefore does not imply a figure at or above the 50% rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\"]\"], [8798, \"2026-07-27\", 3, \"Wall Street expects 42% FY2027 revenue growth for NVIDIA; this is rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Around 42% growth, with an unquantified upward skew from the possibility of additional Alphabet purchases and stronger hyperscaler spending. The evidence does not imply growth reaching 50%.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies growth around that estimate, with an upward skew: Alphabet\\u2019s higher spending could lead to more NVIDIA purchases, and the in-force capex and export-control themes also lean toward stronger demand. But the Alphabet view gives no quantified NVIDIA order or revenue increase, and the CPU forecast is only about $20 billion against NVIDIA\\u2019s much larger total-revenue base. Nothing shown supports moving the implied growth to the 50% boundary.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\"]\"], [8802, \"2026-07-28\", 3, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Roughly 44% growth, as a modest upward adjustment from the 42% reference for the positive but unquantified Alphabet demand view and the stronger capex outlook; the roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU forecast is only a small part of NVIDIA's total-revenue base.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The updated capex and export-control theme conclusions modestly affect the forecast, but the Alphabet view gives no quantified NVIDIA order or revenue increase, and the CPU forecast remains small relative to total revenue. The implied growth therefore stays in the low-to-mid 40s, below rung 2's 50% boundary and above rung 4's 35% boundary.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\"]\"], [8806, \"2026-07-29\", 3, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 total revenue growth estimate, which sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately 47% FY2027 growth: the 42% reference is raised modestly for the strong reported 85% fiscal Q1 growth and additional demand support, without extrapolating that quarter's rate across the year or treating total CPU revenue as incremental growth.\", \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate is the reference and sits in rung 3. The reported 85% year-over-year growth in fiscal Q1 is strong upside evidence, but it is one quarter's result, not a basis for carrying 85% growth through FY2027; there is no supplied guidance or quantified revision to the FY2027 forecast. The Alphabet view and the capex and export-access themes add upside, while the CPU dependency supports meaningful FY2027 revenue but does not provide a prior-year base from which to calculate incremental growth or justify adding its full revenue to the 42% estimate. I therefore imply roughly 47% growth: above the reference, but still below rung 2's 50% boundary and above rung 4's 35% boundary.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\"]\"], [8810, \"2026-07-30\", 3, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Approximately 49% FY2027 total revenue growth: above the 42% reference, reflecting the strong Q1 result and upside from the expanded cumulative chip-sales target and demand/access backdrop, while discounting the target because it is not a FY2027 revenue figure and not extrapolating Q1's 85% growth as a full-year rate.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies somewhat faster growth, but not enough to cross rung 2's 50% boundary: Q1's 85% year-over-year increase is strong upside evidence, not a full-year growth rate to carry forward unchanged, and Huang's $1 trillion cumulative chip-sales target through 2027 is not a standalone FY2027 revenue forecast. The Alphabet view and demand/access themes add upside, while the CPU dependency implies meaningful FY2027 revenue but supplies no comparable prior-year base and is part of, not additive to, total revenue. I therefore imply roughly 49% growth, still within rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1285\\\"]\"], [8814, \"2026-07-31\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 60% FY2027 revenue growth. I treat the 82% analyst estimate as an upside signal rather than consensus, discount it for the lack of later-quarter guidance, and weigh it with 85% reported Q1 growth and management's $91 billion Q2 revenue guide.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence now points materially higher: Q1 growth was 85%, management guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion, and a separate analyst estimate puts FY2027 growth at 82%. I discount that 82% forecast because it is not the consensus and later quarters are not guided here; the $1 trillion cumulative chip-sales target is not an FY2027 revenue forecast. Taken together, the evidence implies about 60% FY2027 growth\\u2014just across rung 1's 60% boundary. The Q1 figure is not annualized flat, and the CPU dependency supports meaningful revenue but is part of total revenue and supplies no comparable prior-year growth base.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\"]\"], [8818, \"2026-08-01\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3.\", \"Approximately 60% FY2027 total revenue growth, inferred from 85% Q1 year-over-year growth and the $91 billion Q2 guide (about 11.5% sequential growth), while discounting the 82% analyst estimate and not treating the cumulative $1 trillion chip-sales target as FY2027 revenue.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly 60% growth: Q1 FY2027 revenue grew 85%, and the $91 billion Q2 guide is about 11.5% above Q1, supporting a strong ongoing run-rate; I discount the separate 82% analyst estimate rather than taking it as the forecast. The $1 trillion cumulative chip-sales target is not a FY2027 total-revenue forecast. Roughly 60% reaches rung 1's 60% boundary. The CPU dependency's roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 forecast supports revenue contribution but is part of total revenue and supplies no separate growth rate, so it does not justify adding its amount to the total forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\"]\"], [8819, \"2026-08-02\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the stated reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Roughly 75\\u201380% FY2027 growth: the 82% analyst estimate is a bullish annual indicator but its period is not explicitly identified as FY2027, so it is discounted; Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst Q2/Q3 growth estimates of 96%/81% support a high-growth run-rate.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%). The forecast is materially above that reference: Q1 growth was 85%, management guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion, and analyst estimates put Q2 and Q3 growth at 96% and 81%, respectively. I read the separate 82% annual estimate as supportive but discount it because its period is not stated as FY2027. Taken together, the evidence implies roughly 75\\u201380% FY2027 growth, crossing rung 1's 60% boundary. The CPU dependency supports some revenue contribution but is part of total revenue, so its $19\\u201320 billion estimate cannot be added on top or treated as a separate growth rate.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1391\\\"]\"], [8827, \"2026-08-04\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral, 35% to below 50%).\", \"Roughly 75\\u201382% FY2027 revenue growth: the explicit 82% annual estimate is supported by 85% reported Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. This carries the current growth run-rate forward rather than annualizing Q1 flat.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%). The forecast is materially higher: NVIDIA reported 85% Q1 growth, guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion (up 11.5% sequentially from Q1), and analyst estimates put Q2 and Q3 growth at 96% and 81%; a separate analyst estimate explicitly gives 82% for FY2027. Together these support roughly 75\\u201382% FY2027 growth, above rung 1's 60% boundary. The CPU dependency's roughly $19 billion FY2027 forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top, and is consistent with the total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1391\\\"]\"], [8831, \"2026-08-05\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the reference; it falls in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 80\\u201382% FY2027 revenue growth, centered on the explicit analyst estimate of 82%. Reported Q1 growth of 85% and analyst expectations of 96% in Q2 and 81% in Q3 support that outlook; these quarterly rates are corroboration, not annualized as a flat run-rate.\", \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 estimate is the reference and sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 80\\u201382% growth: the explicit 82% FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by 85% reported Q1 growth and high Q2/Q3 growth expectations. That forecast is above rung 1\\u2019s 60% threshold. The CPU-revenue dependency\\u2019s roughly $19 billion FY2027 forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with this total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1391\\\"]\"], [8836, \"2026-08-06\", 1, \"Wall Street's stated FY2027 revenue-growth estimate of 42%, which is in rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, based on the explicit analyst estimate for FY2027, with 85% reported Q1 growth and strong Q2/Q3 growth expectations supporting that trajectory. The $91 billion Q2 guide supports the near-term revenue run-rate but is not itself a stated annual growth rate.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly 82% growth: the explicit FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by reported Q1 growth of 85%, a $91 billion Q2 management revenue guide, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That is above rung 1's 60% threshold. The roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue dependency is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1391\\\"]\"], [8840, \"2026-08-07\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3.\", \"Approximately 82% FY2027 revenue growth, based on the explicit analyst estimate and supported by 85% Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 management guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly 82% growth: the explicit FY2027 analyst estimate is consistent with 85% Q1 growth, management's $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That clears rung 1's 60% threshold. NVIDIA's roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\"]\"], [8847, \"2026-08-09\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, taking the explicit analyst estimate as the annual forecast and checking it against 85% reported Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 analyst growth estimates.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. That is a reference, not the forecast once the newer run-rate evidence is considered: the explicit FY2027 analyst estimate is 82%, with Q1 reported growth of 85%, management\\u2019s Q2 guide of $91 billion, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth supporting a high-growth trajectory. An implied outcome around 82% clears rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add; it is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1391\\\"]\"], [8852, \"2026-08-10\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Approximately 82% FY2027 total revenue growth: the explicit analyst estimate for FY2027 is corroborated by 85% reported Q1 growth and analyst estimates of 96% for Q2 and 81% for Q3, with management guiding Q2 revenue to $91 billion. This uses the full-year estimate and subsequent-quarter trajectory, not Q1 annualized flat.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The most likely outcome is materially higher: the explicit 82% FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by 85% reported Q1 growth and analyst estimates of 96% for Q2 and 81% for Q3; management's $91 billion Q2 guide is consistent with continued rapid growth. This points to roughly 82% for the year, well above rung 1's 60% threshold. The longer-term $1 trillion cumulative chip-sales target is discounted rather than treated as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA's approximately $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU revenue forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this total-revenue outlook.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\"]\"], [8857, \"2026-08-11\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference; it falls in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, using the specific FY2027 analyst estimate, supported by 85% reported Q1 growth, management\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% growth in Q2 and 81% in Q3.\", \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate is the reference and sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 82% growth: a specific FY2027 analyst estimate is consistent with reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 revenue guide, and analyst estimates of 96% growth in Q2 and 81% in Q3. That clears rung 1\\u2019s 60% threshold. New GPU rental pricing and CoreWeave demand indicators reinforce the high-growth outlook but do not quantify a different NVIDIA FY2027 growth rate. NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU revenue forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this conclusion.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1701\\\", \\\"e1702\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\"]\"], [8862, \"2026-08-12\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the reference; it sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, based on the specific analyst FY2027 estimate, with reported 85% Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 revenue guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 providing supporting run-rate evidence.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence in force instead implies about 82% growth: the specific FY2027 estimate is reinforced by reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That forecast clears rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. The CPU thesis remains a component of NVIDIA revenue, not an amount to add to total revenue, and is consistent with this conclusion. Higher GPU rental rates and CoreWeave\\u2019s reported demand/backlog support the outlook but do not independently quantify NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 growth.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1391\\\", \\\"e1701\\\", \\\"e1702\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\", \\\"e1764\\\"]\"], [8866, \"2026-08-13\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3.\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, using the specific analyst estimate as the full-year forecast and checking it against reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-supported forecast is about 82%: the specific FY2027 estimate is supported by Q1\\u2019s reported 85% year-over-year growth, management\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimates of 96% growth in Q2 and 81% in Q3. That forecast is above rung 1\\u2019s 60% threshold. CoreWeave demand evidence supports the outlook but does not independently quantify NVIDIA revenue; the CPU thesis is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and its $19\\u201320 billion forecast is consistent with this total-revenue conclusion.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1764\\\"]\"], [8870, \"2026-08-14\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, using the specific analyst estimate for the year and corroborating it with reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimated Q2 and Q3 growth of 96% and 81%. The CPU revenue forecast is included within total revenue, not added to it.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The implied forecast is about 82%: the specific FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That is above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. The CoreWeave evidence supports demand but does not independently quantify NVIDIA revenue, and NVIDIA CPU revenue is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1701\\\", \\\"e1702\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\", \\\"e1707\\\", \\\"e1764\\\"]\"], [8874, \"2026-08-15\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 growth, the specific analyst estimate in force; applying it to reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion implies roughly $393 billion of FY2027 revenue. Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst Q2/Q3 growth estimates of 96%/81% support carrying a high growth rate through the year.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 82% growth, well above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary: a specific FY2027 analyst estimate is consistent with Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. The newly reported FY2026 base and Q4 growth add context but do not overturn that forecast. NVIDIA\\u2019s expected $19\\u201320 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue is only a component of total revenue (about 5% of the implied total); it is consistent with, and must not be added to, the whole. The capex and export-policy themes in force generally support demand or avoid potential constraints, consistent with this rung.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1764\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1884\\\"]\"], [8878, \"2026-08-16\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate, cited in e966, e1148, and e1285; this is rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, implying roughly $393 billion of revenue against reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. The annual analyst estimate is supported by 85% Q1 growth, management\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 82% growth, or roughly $393 billion of FY2027 revenue on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: the specific FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by 85% reported Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That is well above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. The CPU dependency\\u2019s $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 conclusion is a component of this total, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with the implied whole.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1884\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1391\\\", \\\"e1701\\\", \\\"e1702\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\", \\\"e1707\\\", \\\"e1764\\\"]\"], [8882, \"2026-08-17\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% estimate for NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, implying roughly $393 billion of revenue from FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion ($215.94B \\u00d7 1.82). The explicit FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by reported Q1 growth, the Q2 revenue guide, and analyst Q2 and Q3 growth estimates.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 82% growth, or roughly $393 billion of FY2027 revenue on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: the specific FY2027 analyst estimate is reinforced by 85% reported Q1 growth, management\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That is above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. Recent FY2026 Q4 growth and customer-backlog evidence add support but do not justify changing the 82% implied figure. The CPU dependency\\u2019s roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 conclusion is part of total revenue, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with the implied whole.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1884\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\", \\\"e1764\\\"]\"], [8886, \"2026-08-18\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 growth, implying approximately $393 billion of revenue: $215.94 billion of FY2026 revenue multiplied by 1.82. The specific analyst estimate is consistent with the 85% Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-supported forecast remains about 82% growth, or approximately $393 billion of revenue using FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion as the base. That forecast is supported by the specific analyst estimate and the strong reported and guided quarterly run-rate; it is well above rung 1's 60% threshold. The roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue conclusion is part of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1884\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1764\\\", \\\"e2040\\\", \\\"e2041\\\", \\\"e2036\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e2061\\\"]\"], [8891, \"2026-08-19\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% estimate for NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 80% FY2027 revenue growth, implying roughly $389 billion of revenue from the FY2026 base of $215.94 billion ($215.94 billion \\u00d7 1.80). This uses Morningstar's 80% FY2027 model, supported by Q1's reported growth, Q2 guidance implying roughly 95% year-over-year growth versus the $46.7 billion prior-year quarter, and analysts' 81% Q3 estimate; the separate roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate implies about 85% growth and is consistent with the same high-growth outlook.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which is in rung 3. The evidence now supports about 80% growth, well above rung 1's 60% threshold: Morningstar models 80%, and the reported Q1 result, Q2 guidance versus the prior-year quarter, and Q3 estimate are consistent with a strong FY2027 run-rate. This forecast is also consistent with the dependency's conclusion of nearly $20 billion in FY2027 CPU revenue as one component of total revenue, not an amount to add to it.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1764\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1884\\\", \\\"e2036\\\", \\\"e2040\\\", \\\"e2041\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2154\\\"]\"], [8895, \"2026-08-20\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference; it sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 80% growth, or roughly $389 billion of FY2027 revenue, using Morningstar's 80% model against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. The separate roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate implies about 85% growth and is supportive upside rather than the central forecast.\", \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate is the reference and sits in rung 3. The evidence points materially above it: Morningstar's FY2027 model is 80%, while NVIDIA's Q2 guidance implies nearly 95% year-over-year growth and the Q3 estimate is 81%. Those forward inputs, together with the reported Q1 growth, support a full-year outcome above rung 1's 60% boundary; the 42% consensus does not keep the judgement at its rung. The forecast is consistent with the dependency's approximately $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue conclusion as one component of total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e2154\\\"]\"], [8899, \"2026-08-21\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 80\\u201385% FY2027 growth: Morningstar's 80% model implies roughly $389 billion of revenue from the FY2026 base of $215.94 billion, while the separate roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate implies about 85% growth.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly 80\\u201385% growth, or about $389\\u2013400 billion of FY2027 revenue against FY2026's $215.94 billion: Morningstar models 80%, while a separate estimate puts FY2027 revenue near $400 billion. Q1 reported growth of 85%, Q2 guidance implies nearly 95% year-over-year growth, and the cited Q3 estimate is 81%; taken together, these forward inputs support an outcome well above the 60% rung-1 boundary, rather than simply annualizing Q1. The approximately $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue forecast is consistent as one component of total revenue and does not exceed the whole.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2244\\\"]\"], [8903, \"2026-08-22\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About $389\\u2013400 billion of FY2027 total revenue, or roughly 80\\u201385% growth versus FY2026's $215.94 billion. The range is anchored by Morningstar's 80% model and the roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate, with reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance supporting strong growth rather than flat annualization of the latest quarter.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies about $389\\u2013400 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 80\\u201385% growth against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: Morningstar models 80%, and the roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate implies about 85%. Q1 growth, Q2 guidance implying nearly 100% year-over-year growth, and the cited Q3 estimate of 81% support a full-year outcome above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The new $1 trillion order-book claim spans 2026 and 2027 and does not specify NVIDIA's FY2027 share, so it reinforces demand but does not justify assigning the whole amount to this year or lifting the implied estimate. The CPU dependency's $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 forecast is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total-revenue estimate.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1884\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2244\\\"]\"], [8907, \"2026-08-23\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate, which is rung 3.\", \"About $389\\u2013400 billion of FY2027 revenue: 80% growth under Morningstar's model, rising to about 85% if revenue reaches roughly $400 billion, against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies about $389\\u2013400 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 80\\u201385% growth against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: Morningstar models 80%, while the roughly $400 billion estimate implies about 85%. Q1's 85% reported growth, Q2 guidance implying nearly 100% year-over-year growth, and the cited Q3 estimate of 81% support growth above rung 1's 60% threshold. The $1 trillion order book spans 2026 and 2027 and does not specify FY2027 revenue, so it supports demand but is not assigned in full to this year. The CPU dependency's $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 forecast is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total-revenue estimate.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e2244\\\"]\"], [8913, \"2026-08-24\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 80\\u201385% FY2027 revenue growth, or approximately $389\\u2013400 billion of revenue: Morningstar models 80%, while the roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate is about 85% above FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. Q2 guidance and the Q3 estimate support this annual outlook without treating one quarter's growth as a flat full-year run-rate.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which is rung 3. The evidence implies materially more: Morningstar models 80% growth, and the roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate implies about 85% growth against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. Q2 guidance implies roughly 95% year-over-year growth for that quarter, while the cited Q3 estimate is 81%; these are strong checks on the annual estimate, not rates to extrapolate flat across the whole year. The implied 80\\u201385% clears rung 1's 60% boundary. The CPU dependency's roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 estimate is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not greater than, this total.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2244\\\"]\"], [8917, \"2026-08-25\", 1, \"The ladder cites Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 estimate, which is rung 3. The more current explicit market reference is LSEG\\u2019s 83% estimate ($396 billion), which is rung 1.\", \"About 83% FY2027 revenue growth: the LSEG estimate of $396 billion against reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion implies approximately 83.4%. Morningstar\\u2019s 80% model and the strong quarterly guide/estimates are consistent checks, not annualized quarterly growth rates.\", \"The ladder\\u2019s cited Wall Street estimate of 42% is rung 3, but the newer LSEG FY2027 estimate is 83% on $396 billion, which is rung 1 and is the more current explicit market reference. The evidence supports a similarly high outcome rather than merely repeating that reference: $396 billion divided by FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion implies about 83.4% growth; Morningstar independently models 80%. Q1 growth of 85% and Q2 guidance imply roughly 95% year-over-year growth for that quarter, while the cited Q3 estimate is 81%, so the quarter figures support strong growth without being extrapolated flat across the year. The implied annual growth clears the 60% rung-1 boundary. The roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU estimate in v16 is a component consistent with, and below, the projected total.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2244\\\", \\\"e2419\\\", \\\"e2421\\\"]\"], [8922, \"2026-08-26\", 1, \"LSEG\\u2019s $396 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, equivalent to 83% growth, is the current quantified market reference and sits at rung 1. The older 42% Wall Street estimate sits at rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.5% growth versus FY2026\\u2019s $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2 plus $108.0 billion guided for Q3, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward Q3\\u2019s roughly 12.3% sequential growth.\", \"The reference is LSEG\\u2019s FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, or 83% growth, which is rung 1; the older 42% estimate is rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion, and Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion. Carrying the guided Q3 sequential growth of about 12.3% into Q4 implies roughly $121.3 billion for Q4, or about $407.1 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies approximately 88.5% growth, well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook in dependency v16 is a component of, and does not exceed, this total. The new Q2 result and Q3 guidance change the supporting figures and implied outcome, but not the rung.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2118\\\"]\"], [8926, \"2026-08-27\", 1, \"Latest stated market reference: LSEG estimates FY2027 revenue of $396 billion, about 83% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; that is rung 1. The older 42% Wall Street estimate is rung 3.\", \"Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion; adding guided Q3 revenue of $108.0 billion and carrying its approximately 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4 and $407.1 billion for FY2027. That is approximately 88.5% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\", \"The latest stated market reference is LSEG\\u2019s FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, equivalent to about 83% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, so the reference sits on rung 1. The older 42% Wall Street estimate sits on rung 3, but the more recent LSEG estimate is the relevant reference. For the forecast, NVIDIA reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, then guided Q3 to $108.0 billion. Carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3% into Q4 implies roughly $121.3 billion for Q4 and about $407.1 billion for FY2027 overall. Against FY2026 revenue, that is approximately 88.5% growth\\u2014well above rung 1\\u2019s 60% threshold. The $25 billion CPU-revenue forecast in dependency v16 is a component of total revenue and does not exceed this total; it is not added on top. The export-control and AI-capex themes shown generally support the higher-revenue direction, but do not change the run-rate calculation or rung.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2989\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2975\\\"]\"], [8930, \"2026-08-28\", 1, \"LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion implies about 83% growth, placing the reference at rung 1.\", \"Approximately $400 billion of FY2027 revenue, or about 85% growth over FY2026. This uses reported Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance, then estimates roughly $114 billion for Q4\\u2014slower sequential growth than the Q2-to-Q3 guided pace, partly reflecting the reported 70% full-year outlook as a downside counterweight.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies about 83% growth and sits at rung 1. The evidence implies roughly $400 billion, or about 85% growth: Q1 and Q2 revenue totaled about $177.8 billion, Q3 guidance brings the first three quarters to about $285.8 billion, and roughly $114 billion in Q4 would take the year to about $400 billion. That Q4 estimate allows for sequential growth to slow from the Q2-to-Q3 guided pace. The reported 70% full-year growth outlook is a meaningful downward counterweight, but at that full-year rate revenue would be only about $367 billion, requiring Q4 revenue of roughly $81 billion\\u2014well below Q3 guidance of $108 billion, with no evidence here of a corresponding reversal. The implied outcome remains above rung 1's 60% boundary. Dependency v16's roughly $25 billion CPU-revenue forecast is consistent with this total, but is a component and is not added again.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e2154\\\"]\"], [8934, \"2026-08-29\", 1, \"LSEG's FY2027 consensus estimate is $396 billion, or about 83% growth versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; this sits at rung 1.\", \"The reported 70% FY2027 growth outlook implies approximately $367 billion ($215.94 billion \\u00d7 1.70). Q1 and Q2 actuals plus Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion, so this outlook leaves roughly $81 billion for Q4.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, about 83% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, which is rung 1. The strongest new full-year input is the CFO's reported 70% FY2027 growth outlook: applying it to FY2026 revenue implies about $367 billion. That is lower than consensus, but remains above the rung-1 threshold of 60% (about $345.5 billion). Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion, leaving roughly $81 billion for Q4 at the 70% outlook; the annual outlook therefore moderates the prior roughly $400 billion forecast without moving it below the rung boundary. Higher third-party estimates of 80% or roughly $400 billion are supportive but do not outweigh the more direct annual outlook. The v16 dependency's roughly $25 billion CPU-revenue forecast is a component of, and is consistent with, this total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2154\\\"]\"], [8939, \"2026-08-30\", 1, \"LSEG expects $396 billion of FY2027 revenue, about 83% growth from FY2026's $215.94 billion; that market reference is rung 1.\", \"About 70% FY2027 growth, or approximately $367 billion, using the reported full-year growth outlook applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. Q1 actuals, Q2 actuals, and Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion, leaving roughly $81 billion for Q4 at that annual outcome.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, about 83% growth on FY2026 revenue, which is rung 1. I forecast about 70% growth, or roughly $367 billion: the reported 70% full-year outlook is the strongest direct annual input, while FY2027 Q1 and Q2 actuals plus Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion, leaving about $81 billion for Q4 at that annual outcome. That is a sharp slowdown from the Q3 guide, so the higher LSEG estimate and other roughly $400 billion projections are meaningful upside risks; however, they are estimates or conditional projections, not a quantified company revision to the annual outlook. The 70% forecast remains above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The v16 CPU forecast is a component of total revenue, not something to add on top of it, and is consistent with this total.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2989\\\"]\"], [8944, \"2026-08-31\", 1, \"LSEG analysts' $396 billion FY2027 estimate, about 83% growth from FY2026 revenue, sits in rung 1.\", \"About 83% growth, or roughly $395\\u2013396 billion: FY2027 Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 actual revenue of $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108 billion total about $285.8 billion; a roughly $109\\u2013110 billion Q4 would bring the year to that range.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, about 83% growth on NVIDIA's reported $215.94 billion FY2026 revenue; that is rung 1. I forecast about 83% growth as well, but not simply because it is consensus: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue plus the Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion, and an FY2027 total near $395\\u2013396 billion requires roughly $109\\u2013110 billion in Q4, close to the Q3 guide and consistent with the reported growth trajectory. The reported 70% full-year outlook would imply only about $81 billion in Q4, a sharp sequential decline from the $108 billion Q3 guide, so I give it less weight against the subsequent quarter results and guidance. The implied growth is well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. v16's CPU forecast is part of total revenue, not additive to it, and is consistent with this whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e2989\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e3481\\\"]\"], [8948, \"2026-09-01\", 1, \"LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, about 83% growth on FY2026 revenue, is the market reference and sits at rung 1. The separate Zacks estimate of 85.4% is also in rung 1.\", \"About 70% FY2027 revenue growth: applying the CFO's full-year outlook to FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion implies about $367.1 billion in FY2027 revenue. After $81.6 billion in Q1, $96.2 billion in Q2, and the $108 billion Q3 guide, that annual outcome implies about $81.3 billion in Q4.\", \"The reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, or about 83% growth on NVIDIA's FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; that sits at rung 1. I forecast about 70% growth instead, principally because the supplied CFO outlook is 70% for the full fiscal year. That implies roughly $367.1 billion of FY2027 revenue. Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus the Q3 guide total about $285.8 billion, leaving roughly $81.3 billion for Q4 under that annual outlook\\u2014a sharp sequential decline from the Q3 guide, so it is a cautious forecast against the otherwise strong quarterly trajectory. Even at 70%, implied growth remains above rung 1's 60% boundary. The v16 CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not greater than, this whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e3683\\\"]\"], [8954, \"2026-09-03\", 1, \"LSEG expects $396 billion of FY2027 revenue, about 83% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; this is rung 1. Zacks' 85.4% growth estimate is likewise rung 1.\", \"Approximately $404 billion of FY2027 revenue, or about 87% growth: Q1 and Q2 actuals plus the $108 billion Q3 guide total $285.8 billion; carrying the estimated 81% Q3 year-over-year growth into Q4 implies about $118 billion in Q4.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, or about 83% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, which is rung 1. Zacks' 85.4% estimate is also rung 1. The operating evidence points to roughly 87% growth: Q1 and Q2 revenue totaled $177.8 billion, and the $108 billion Q3 guide brings the first three quarters to $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the analyst-estimated 81% Q3 year-over-year growth to Q4 implies about $118 billion of Q4 revenue and roughly $404 billion for FY2027, or about 87% growth. The reported 70% full-year outlook would imply about $367 billion and only $81 billion in Q4 after the first three quarters, a sharp drop from the Q3 guide; it is a meaningful counterweight, but the latest quarterly actuals and guidance make that lower outcome less likely. Both the reference and implied outcome remain above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The v16 CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is consistent with this whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2989\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3620\\\"]\"], [8958, \"2026-09-04\", 1, \"The latest stated market expectations are LSEG's 83% FY2027 revenue growth to $396 billion and Zacks' 85.4%; both correspond to rung 1. The earlier 42% estimate cited in e966 corresponds to rung 3, but the later quantified estimates place the current reference at rung 1.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88% growth versus FY2026's $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint totals $285.8 billion; carrying forward the 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion in Q4.\", \"The latest specific market reference is LSEG's 83% growth forecast to $396 billion, with Zacks at 85.4%; both sit at rung 1. The operating run-rate points somewhat higher: FY2026 revenue was $215.94 billion, and FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue were $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion. Adding the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint gives $285.8 billion for the first three quarters. Carrying forward the 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives roughly $121 billion for Q4, about $407 billion for FY2027, or 88% growth. The reported 70% full-year outlook is a meaningful counterweight, but it would imply only about $81 billion in Q4 after the first three quarters, a sharp drop from Q3 guidance. The implied growth remains well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA's CPU revenue forecast is a component of, and does not exceed, this total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e3108\\\"]\"], [8962, \"2026-09-05\", 1, \"LSEG analysts expect FY2027 revenue growth of 83% to $396 billion, which is rung 1; Zacks' 85.4% estimate is also rung 1. The ladder's 42% Wall Street estimate is an older, lower reference.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.5% growth versus FY2026's $215.94 billion. This carries forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by the $108 billion Q3 guidance to estimate about $121 billion for Q4; the separate 70% full-year outlook implies a lower $367 billion but still clears rung 1.\", \"The current market reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to $396 billion (rung 1); Zacks' 85.4% estimate is also rung 1. The operating run-rate implies about $407 billion: FY2027 Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 was $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance is $108 billion, and carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion for Q4. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is approximately 88.5% growth. NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth outlook is a meaningful lower counterweight, but it still implies growth above the 60% rung boundary; the current-quarter guidance and sequential run-rate do not imply a Q4 collapse sufficient to pull the forecast below that boundary. The CPU dependency's approximately $25 billion-plus FY2027 estimate is compatible with, and far below, the implied total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e3593\\\"]\"], [8966, \"2026-09-06\", 1, \"LSEG expects 83% FY2027 revenue growth to $396 billion (rung 1); Zacks' 85.4% estimate is also rung 1.\", \"Carry forward Q3 guidance's approximately 12% sequential growth into Q4: $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B + about $121B is about $406.8B for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94B, that implies approximately 88.4% growth.\", \"The reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to $396 billion, which is rung 1; Zacks' 85.4% estimate is also rung 1. The reported Q1 and Q2 results plus Q3 guidance imply a run-rate of about $407 billion for FY2027, or roughly 88% growth. NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year outlook is a meaningful counterweight, but at $367 billion it would require Q4 revenue of about $81 billion\\u2014roughly 25% below guided Q3 revenue\\u2014despite Q3 guidance implying about 12% sequential growth. The run-rate therefore remains the more likely outcome and comfortably exceeds the 60% rung-1 boundary. The CPU dependency's forecast of just over $25 billion is consistent with, and remains below, the total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e3593\\\"]\"], [8971, \"2026-09-07\", 1, \"LSEG's estimate of 83% FY2027 revenue growth to $396 billion is the principal market reference and sits at rung 1. The cited Morningstar 80% and Zacks 85.4% estimates are also at rung 1.\", \"FY2027 Q1\\u2013Q3 reported revenue totals $285.8 billion; carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion of Q4 revenue and approximately $407 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies roughly 88% year-over-year growth.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to $396 billion, which sits at rung 1; the other cited estimates of 80% and 85.4% also sit there. The direct quarterly run-rate implies about $407 billion: FY2027 Q1\\u2013Q3 revenue totals $285.8 billion, and carrying Q3's roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121 billion for Q4, or about 88% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion. NVIDIA's 70% full-year projection is a meaningful lower-growth counterweight, but would imply about $367 billion total and only about $81 billion in Q4 after the reported first three quarters\\u2014well below Q3 guidance of $108 billion. The quarterly guidance and its carried-forward run-rate therefore remain the stronger forecast input and stay above the 60% rung boundary. The v16 CPU forecast of roughly $25 billion or somewhat more is consistent with this total-revenue forecast and remains only a component of it. The updated hyperscaler capex theme supports demand, while the other in-force export-control themes generally reduce downside policy risk; neither changes the run-rate calculation.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2975\\\", \\\"e2989\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3481\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e3683\\\", \\\"e3883\\\", \\\"e3942\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4049\\\", \\\"e4164\\\", \\\"e4165\\\"]\"], [8976, \"2026-09-08\", 1, \"LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to about $396 billion is the market reference and sits at rung 1. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth projection also sits at rung 1.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88% growth from FY2026's $215.94 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to Q3 guidance of $108 billion, then carry the implied 12% sequential growth into Q4 for about $121 billion.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to about $396 billion, which is rung 1; NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth projection is also above the rung-1 threshold. For the forecast, Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 was $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance is $108 billion. Carrying the disclosed 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion, or roughly $407 billion for FY2027 and 88% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. That run-rate is above the 60% rung boundary and supports rung 1. The 70% company projection is a meaningful lower counterweight, but it too remains within rung 1.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4226\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e3620\\\"]\"], [8980, \"2026-09-09\", 1, \"LSEG expects 83% FY2027 revenue growth to about $396 billion; that expectation sits at rung 1 (at least 60%).\", \"Carrying the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion and FY2027 revenue near $407 billion. Relative to FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies roughly 88% growth.\", \"The market reference is LSEG\\u2019s 83% FY2027 growth estimate to $396 billion, which is rung 1. The reported and guided quarterly path implies about $407 billion for FY2027: $81.6 billion in Q1, $96.2 billion in Q2, $108 billion guided for Q3, and about $121 billion in Q4 if Q3\\u2019s approximately 12% sequential growth carries forward. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is roughly 88% growth, well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s reported 70% full-year growth projection is a lower counterweight, but it also implies rung 1; reaching it after the first three quarters would require a marked Q4 slowdown. The memory-shortage comment is a risk but gives no quantified NVIDIA constraint. The CPU dependency\\u2019s forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of this total.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4274\\\"]\"], [8984, \"2026-09-10\", 1, \"LSEG's FY2027 estimate is 83% growth to $396 billion, placing the reference in rung 1.\", \"Approximately $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or about 88% growth: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, with Q4 estimated near $121 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\", \"The reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to $396 billion, which sits in rung 1. The reported and guided quarterly path implies about $407 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 was $96.2 billion, Q3 is guided at $108 billion, and carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is approximately 88% growth, comfortably above the 60% boundary. Management's 70% full-year growth outlook is a lower counterweight, implying roughly $367 billion; after the first three quarters, that would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion. The nearer-term quarterly guidance and reported run-rate make the higher outcome more likely. The CPU dependency is a component of total revenue, so its forecast is consistent with this total but is not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4274\\\", \\\"e3593\\\"]\"], [8988, \"2026-09-11\", 1, \"Latest cited analyst expectation: $411 billion of FY2027 revenue (e4348), about 90% growth versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, placing the reference at rung 1. The earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion / 83% also sits at rung 1; the ladder's cited 42% reference would be rung 3 but does not reflect these later market estimates.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88% growth: add reported Q1 and Q2 revenue ($81.6 billion and $96.2 billion), Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and a Q4 estimate of about $121 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance. This is well above the 60% rung-1 boundary.\", \"The market reference is the latest cited analyst estimate of $411 billion for FY2027; against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies about 90% growth and sits at rung 1. The older ladder reference of 42% would sit at rung 3, but it is superseded as a description of current market expectations by the later analyst estimates. The quarterly run-rate points to about $407 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue were $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance is $108 billion, and carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion. That is approximately 88% growth, well above the 60% rung boundary. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook is a meaningful lower counterweight, but applied to FY2026 revenue it implies about $367 billion and only about $81 billion in Q4 after the first three quarters\\u2014far below Q3 guidance. The latest quarterly path therefore makes the higher outcome more likely. The CPU dependency's forecast of more than $25 billion is a component of, and consistent with, the roughly $407 billion total; it cannot be added on top of total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4400\\\"]\"], [8992, \"2026-09-12\", 1, \"Latest analyst consensus is $411 billion of FY2027 revenue [e4348], about 90% growth from FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion [e1883]; this is rung 1. The ladder\\u2019s cited 42% estimate is rung 3 but is superseded as the current market reference.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88% growth: add Q1 $81.6 billion, Q2 $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and an approximately $121 billion Q4 obtained by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance. This is rung 1.\", \"The current market reference is the analyst estimate of $411 billion for FY2027 [e4348]. Against reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion [e1883], that implies about 90% growth and sits at rung 1; the ladder\\u2019s cited 42% estimate would be rung 3, but is not the latest market reference. The quarterly run-rate independently points to roughly $407 billion: FY2027 Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 was $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance is $108 billion; carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion [e2146, e2497, e2499, e3943]. That is about 88% growth, comfortably above the 60% rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s 70% full-year growth outlook is a lower counterweight, but itself implies roughly $367 billion and still clears that boundary [e4400]. The CPU dependency\\u2019s forecast is a component of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to the total [v16].\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4400\\\"]\"], [8997, \"2026-09-13\", 1, \"Latest cited analyst consensus is $411 billion of FY2027 revenue [e4348]; against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion [e1883], that implies about 90% growth and sits at rung 1. The ladder\\u2019s stated 42% estimate would sit at rung 3, but the more recent $411 billion estimate is the relevant market reference.\", \"Q1 and Q2 revenue were $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion; Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion. Carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives Q4 of about $121 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $407 billion, roughly 88% growth from FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\", \"The market reference is the latest cited analyst estimate of $411 billion for FY2027, or about 90% growth from FY2026 revenue; that sits at rung 1. The quarterly run-rate implies about $407 billion, also well above rung 1\\u2019s 60% threshold. NVIDIA\\u2019s stated 70% full-year growth outlook is a lower counterweight, but still implies growth within rung 1; the slower sequential growth embedded in Q3 guidance and the reported memory constraint do not pull the forecast below the rung boundary. The CPU dependency is a small component of total revenue and does not conflict with this whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4599\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e4274\\\", \\\"e4389\\\"]\"], [9001, \"2026-09-14\", 1, \"Latest cited market reference: Zacks consensus growth of 87.4% for FY2027, about $405 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; this is rung 1. Other analyst references include LSEG\\u2019s 83% and $396 billion and an estimate of $411 billion, also rung 1. NVIDIA\\u2019s stated 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion and is likewise rung 1.\", \"About $405\\u2013407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 87\\u201389% growth: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 actual revenue of $96.2 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion totals $285.8 billion; repeating the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion. The company\\u2019s 70% full-year outlook is a lower counterweight, but still implies growth above the 60% rung-1 threshold.\", \"The market reference is the latest cited Zacks FY2027 revenue-growth estimate of 87.4%, about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; it sits at rung 1. Other cited analyst estimates also sit at rung 1. The company\\u2019s reported 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion, which is below the market estimate but still above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. Independently, Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus the Q3 midpoint guidance total about $285.8 billion; carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion for Q4 and roughly $407 billion for FY2027, or about 88% growth. That central run-rate estimate remains well above the roughly $345.5 billion needed for 60% growth, so the deceleration in sequential growth does not cross a rung boundary. The CPU dependency is consistent with this whole-company forecast: its a little-over-$25 billion estimate is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add again.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e4348\\\"]\"], [9005, \"2026-09-15\", 1, \"Zacks' FY2027 consensus growth estimate is 87.4%, about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; it sits in rung 1. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook also sits in rung 1.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88% growth: add reported Q1 and Q2 revenue ($81.6 billion and $96.2 billion), Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and an estimated Q4 of about $121 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance; compare with FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\", \"The market reference is Zacks' 87.4% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 1; the reported 70% full-year growth outlook also sits in rung 1. The implied run-rate is about $407 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, followed by an estimated Q4 of about $121 billion if the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance carries forward. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is about 88% growth, still comfortably above rung 1's 60% boundary. The 60% FY2026\\u2013FY2029 analyst CAGR is a longer-horizon view, not a direct FY2027 estimate, and does not outweigh the current-quarter run-rate. The v16 CPU forecast of $25\\u201326 billion is a component of, and well below, the implied total; it is not added again.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4735\\\"]\"], [9009, \"2026-09-16\", 1, \"Market consensus is about 87\\u201388% FY2027 revenue growth (rung 1); the cited $411 billion estimate implies about 90% and is also rung 1. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook is rung 1 as well.\", \"Q1 and Q2 reported revenue plus Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion in Q4, or approximately $407 billion for FY2027; versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, implied growth is about 88%.\", \"The market reference is roughly 87\\u201388% FY2027 growth (rung 1); the $411 billion analyst estimate also implies about 90% against FY2026 revenue. NVIDIA's reported FY2026 growth and its stated 70% full-year outlook are likewise in rung 1. For the forecast, Q1 and Q2 revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, total $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion in Q4 and approximately $407 billion for FY2027; against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is about 88% growth, well above rung 1's 60% boundary. The 70% full-year outlook is below this quarterly run-rate implication but still in rung 1; a 70% outcome would leave only about $81 billion for Q4 after Q1\\u2013Q3, inconsistent with the guided run-rate. The v16 total-CPU forecast is only a component and is not added again; it remains well below the implied total. Demand concerns and supply constraints temper the outlook but do not move the implied result across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4274\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4822\\\"]\"], [9013, \"2026-09-17\", 1, \"Current supplied Wall Street/Zacks expectations are about 87\\u201388% FY2027 revenue growth (including 87.4% and 88%), which is rung 1. The ladder\\u2019s cited 42% benchmark would sit at rung 3, but the newer estimates in view are the relevant market reference.\", \"Q1 and Q2 revenue plus midpoint Q3 guidance totals $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion in Q4 and approximately $407 billion for FY2027, or about 88.5% growth over FY2026\\u2019s $215.94 billion.\", \"The current market reference is roughly 87\\u201388% FY2027 revenue growth, which is rung 1; the ladder\\u2019s stated 42% benchmark would be rung 3, but the supplied newer consensus estimates are much higher. Using reported Q1 and Q2 revenue and the midpoint of Q3 guidance gives $285.8 billion through Q3. Carrying forward the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion in Q4, or roughly $407 billion for FY2027 versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: approximately 88.5% growth, clearly above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. The CFO\\u2019s 70% full-year growth outlook also remains in rung 1, though it is below this quarterly run-rate implication; a 70% full-year outcome would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion after Q3 guidance of $108 billion. The Q4 analyst estimate of 65% growth is a moderating signal, but its comparison basis is unspecified and it does not imply FY2027 growth below 60%. NVIDIA\\u2019s forecast CPU revenue is a component of, and far below, the implied total-revenue figure.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4822\\\", \\\"e4863\\\"]\"], [9017, \"2026-09-18\", 1, \"Current analyst expectations are approximately 87\\u201388% FY2027 revenue growth (with estimates including 83% and about 90%); this reference is rung 1. The ladder's cited 42% Wall Street estimate is rung 3, but is not the current market reference.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.4% growth versus FY2026's $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual plus $96.2 billion Q2 actual plus the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint, then about $121 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\", \"The market reference is current analyst expectations of roughly 87\\u201388% FY2027 growth, which sits at rung 1; the ladder's cited 42% estimate would sit at rung 3, but it is not the current reference. Q1 and Q2 revenue plus the Q3 guidance midpoint total about $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $407 billion, or 88.4% growth over FY2026. The CFO's approximately 70% full-year outlook is lower but still rung 1; the Q3-guidance run-rate implies a higher figure. The reported possibility of more than $100 billion of unfulfilled AI-infrastructure demand tempers confidence, but covers broader gear and does not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA revenue. The implied outcome remains well above the 60% rung-1 boundary, so there is no rung crossing. The CPU-revenue dependency and the in-force AI-capex and export-policy themes are directionally supportive, but do not justify exceeding the total-revenue run-rate implied by the supplied quarterly figures.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4822\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4900\\\", \\\"e4901\\\", \\\"e3593\\\"]\"], [9022, \"2026-09-19\", 1, \"Current market expectations are roughly 87.4%\\u201388% FY2027 growth, rung 1; the separate $411 billion analyst estimate implies about 90.3%, also rung 1. The ladder's cited 42% estimate is rung 3 but is not the current reference.\", \"Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion. Carrying the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.0 billion of Q4 revenue and about $406.8 billion for FY2027, or 88.4% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\", \"The current market reference is roughly 87.4%\\u201388% growth (Zacks and analyst estimates), with one analyst estimate of $411 billion implying about 90.3%; these are all rung 1. The ladder's cited 42% estimate would be rung 3, but it is not the current reference. Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion; carrying Q3's 12% sequential growth forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.0 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $406.8 billion, or 88.4% growth over FY2026. NVIDIA's approximately 70% full-year outlook is a lower input, but it also remains above the rung-1 threshold; the current-quarter guidance-based run-rate supports the higher estimate. Supplier constraints and the estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled AI-infrastructure demand temper confidence but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA revenue. The CPU dependency's roughly $25 billion forecast is a component of, and does not exceed, the implied total.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4822\\\", \\\"e4901\\\", \\\"e4905\\\"]\"], [9026, \"2026-09-20\", 1, \"Current analyst estimates range from 87.4%\\u201388% growth to $411 billion of FY2027 revenue, or about 90.3% growth versus FY2026; these references sit at rung 1. NVIDIA's stated approximately 70% full-year growth outlook also sits at rung 1. The ladder's cited 42% estimate is not the current market reference.\", \"About $406.8 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.4% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: Q1 and Q2 actuals plus Q3's $108 billion midpoint total $285.8 billion, and carrying Q3's roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121 billion.\", \"The current market reference is about 87.4%\\u201390.3% growth, implying rung 1; NVIDIA's approximately 70% full-year outlook is also rung 1, though it is the lower reference. Using reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, Q1 and Q2 FY2027 revenue, and Q3 guidance of $108 billion, carrying Q3's approximately 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies Q4 revenue of about $121 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $406.8 billion, or approximately 88.4% growth. That remains well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The CFO's 70% outlook and the supply-constraint evidence temper the higher consensus/run-rate estimate, but do not imply a fall below 60%; 70% growth would imply about $367 billion for the year and a Q4 materially below Q3 guidance.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4822\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4900\\\", \\\"e4901\\\", \\\"e4905\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e4274\\\"]\"]], [\"How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 60.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 50.0, 60.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 35.0, 50.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 20.0, 35.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 20.0]]]\n[\"linked-1\", \"v16\", [[8757, \"2026-07-15\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's stated visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $19.5 billion, using management's \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility and the approximately $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in v14. This is within rung 3 and below the more-than-$20-billion threshold for rung 2.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). That figure, read as approximately $19.5 billion, implies revenue just below the rung-2 boundary of more than $20 billion; the $200 billion TAM is not a revenue target or evidence of volume in hand, so it does not justify crossing the boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e442\\\", \\\"e443\\\"]\"], [8760, \"2026-07-16\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; that reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $20 billion, just below the rung-2 threshold: carry forward management\\u2019s specific FY2027 visibility estimate, with no quantified adjustment from the unscheduled-period CPU-ratio view or initial Vera testbed delivery.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. That remains the best quantified forecast: the roughly $20 billion figure is near, but does not cross, the greater-than-$20 billion boundary for rung 2. The expectation that data-center GPU-to-CPU ratios will shrink is directionally supportive but gives no period or revenue estimate. The scheduled first Vera blade delivery is for initial testbed deployment, not evidence of commercial volume in hand. The $200 billion TAM is not a revenue forecast. NVIDIA\\u2019s total-revenue conclusion is consistent with this CPU estimate as a component of the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e442\\\", \\\"e443\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [8764, \"2026-07-17\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in FY2027 total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $19 billion in FY2027: management's direct visibility statement is nearly $20 billion, and the current total-revenue dependency uses an approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion). The evidence still implies about $19 billion: the current-year visibility is the strongest direct forecast, and the current total-revenue dependency also uses an approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate. That is consistent with CPU revenue as a component of the dependency's approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast and does not cross the greater-than-$20 billion boundary. The GPU-to-CPU ratio view is directionally supportive but gives no period or revenue estimate; the scheduled first blade delivery is for initial testbed deployment, not commercial volume in hand. The $200 billion TAM claim is not a revenue forecast, and the repeated outlet adds no new information.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e584\\\"]\"], [8767, \"2026-07-18\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"About $19 billion in FY2027, using management\\u2019s \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility and the dependency\\u2019s approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate. This is below $20 billion and within rung 3.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about $19 billion: the direct visibility statement is consistent with the approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate in the current total-revenue dependency. That is below the greater-than-$20 billion boundary for rung 2, and it remains consistent with CPU revenue as a component of the dependency\\u2019s approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast. The $200 billion TAM statements are not revenue forecasts; the GPU-to-CPU ratio view gives no FY2027 revenue estimate, and the scheduled first blade delivery is for an initial testbed, not commercial volume in hand.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e584\\\", \\\"e597\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e596\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [8771, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027: the near-$20 billion visibility statement, together with the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate, implies revenue below $20 billion. The $20 billion business target has no stated period and does not establish FY2027 volume at that level.\", \"NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility is near the top of rung 3, but it does not imply revenue above $20 billion. The unspecified-period $20 billion business target is not a FY2027 forecast, and the TAM statements are not revenue estimates. The dependency's approximately $19 billion CPU estimate remains below the rung 2 boundary and fits within its approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast. The testbed delivery is not commercial volume in hand.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\"]\"], [8774, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the stated reference and sits in rung 3 (at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027, interpreting \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d of period-specific visibility as just below $20 billion; the Wolfe estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera alone in calendar 2026 (1.3 million \\u00d7 $5,000), but does not quantify total FY2027 CPU revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits in rung 3. I infer approximately $19 billion: the visibility statement is the strongest period-specific forecast, while Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026 and is a material downside check, not an estimate of total FY2027 CPU revenue. June deliveries and OpenAI's planned large-quantity deployment support commercial traction, but volumes are not disclosed; the evidence does not support moving above $20 billion or below the rung 3 floor of $15 billion. This also fits within NVIDIA's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\"]\"], [8779, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d is the market/company reference and sits in rung 3 (at the upper boundary).\", \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027, anchored by management's \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility; the separate $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period, while 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026. The implied total is below the $20 billion rung-2 threshold and above rung 3's $15 billion floor.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits in rung 3. That period-specific forecast still implies revenue near $19 billion: the $20 billion standalone-business target has no period, and Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera shipments in calendar 2026, but neither supplies a quantified revision to the FY2027 total. Deliveries and planned deployments support execution, while the new market and competition evidence does not quantify a change sufficient to cross either the $15 billion or $20 billion boundary; this forecast also remains below NVIDIA's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\"]\"], [8781, \"2026-07-23\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue is the available reference; no separate market consensus is supplied. This reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA\\u2019s period-specific visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion.\\u201d The $20 billion standalone Vera expectation for calendar 2026 supports scale but is discounted as a different-period product target without disclosed volume in hand; the 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, not total FY2027 CPU revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The strongest period-specific implication remains roughly $19\\u201320 billion: e813 adds a $20 billion standalone Vera revenue expectation for calendar 2026, but that is a product target for a different period, not evidence that more than $20 billion is in hand for FY2027. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, tempering that target; it is not total CPU revenue and does not displace the direct FY2027 visibility. Deliveries and planned large deployments support execution, but the evidence does not imply a total above the rung-2 boundary of $20 billion. NVIDIA\\u2019s total FY2027 revenue forecast in v14 is ample room for this CPU estimate, so the component does not exceed its whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\"]\"], [8784, \"2026-07-24\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management's nearly $20 billion visibility, with the CEO clarifying it is for standalone CPU sales. The separate $20 billion calendar-2026 Vera target is tempered by Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip (roughly $6.5 billion), and the delivery and deployment evidence is not quantified into additional FY2027 revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d FY2027 total CPU revenue visibility, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). The evidence still implies approximately $19\\u201320 billion, not more than $20 billion: the CEO clarifies that the FY2027 figure is for standalone CPU sales, while the $20 billion Vera target for calendar 2026 is not the same as demonstrated FY2027 revenue. Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, tempering the target; deliveries and planned deployments support execution but do not quantify additional FY2027 revenue. This remains below the rung-2 threshold of more than $20 billion. The estimate is consistent with v14's approximately $385.7 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component and does not exceed the whole. Recent clarification of the standalone scope and delivery/deployment evidence updates the supporting facts, but does not move the forecast across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\"]\"], [8788, \"2026-07-25\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue is the market/company reference; that figure sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion: management's FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, while Wolfe Research's 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers, but does not displace, that broader visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). The evidence still implies revenue near, but not above, $20 billion: the specific shipment-and-price estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, tempering the much higher management visibility, while deliveries and planned deployments support execution. AMD's performance claims add competitive risk but do not quantify a revenue reduction or customer shift. Nothing in the evidence supports crossing the rung-2 boundary of more than $20 billion. NVIDIA's total-revenue forecast in v14 is ample headroom and is consistent with CPU revenue at this level.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8793, \"2026-07-26\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Near, but not above, $20 billion in FY2027. The company\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility is the strongest direct forecast; the estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 and tempers, but does not replace, that total-FY forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The implied outcome remains near, but not above, $20 billion: NVIDIA\\u2019s current-year visibility is the strongest direct forecast, while Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion of Vera revenue in calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in the higher figure. June deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution, but no stated customer volume or quantified revision supports revenue above $20 billion. The AMD performance and market-share evidence adds competitive risk without quantifying a revenue reduction or customer shift. NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue forecast is consistent with this CPU outcome and imposes no tighter constraint.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\"]\"], [8796, \"2026-07-27\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (at or below $20 billion, above $15 billion).\", \"About $20 billion, or slightly below: management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, and its standalone-CPU statements point to approximately that scale; the 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, but is not additive to the total-revenue visibility. This is well below NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $385.7 billion total-revenue forecast in v14 and remains at rung 3\\u2019s upper boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still points to revenue around, but not demonstrably above, $20 billion: the $20 billion standalone-CPU statements are targets or visibility, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, not an additional amount to add to management\\u2019s total. Deliveries, planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment, and the updated AI-capex outlook support execution; AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence adds risk but does not quantify a revenue reduction. The implied level remains at rung 3\\u2019s upper boundary, not above it. NVIDIA\\u2019s total-revenue forecast in v14 is consistent with this CPU contribution.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e794\\\"]\"], [8801, \"2026-07-28\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the available reference; it sits at the upper end of rung 3. No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Around $20 billion, most likely just below $20 billion. Management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility anchors the estimate; Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not an additional amount to add to total CPU revenue. The implied CPU revenue is well below v14\\u2019s approximately $385.7 billion total-revenue forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive); no separate consensus figure is supplied. The evidence still implies revenue around, and most likely just below, $20 billion: Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a Vera estimate rather than an additional amount to add to management\\u2019s total. Deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution, while AMD\\u2019s reported performance and share gains are a competitive counterweight but do not quantify a revenue reduction. The implied outcome remains within rung 3, and is consistent with NVIDIA\\u2019s much larger total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8805, \"2026-07-29\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). No separate consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19 billion to just under $20 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility is the strongest direct forecast, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 and is not additive to the total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive); no separate consensus figure is supplied. The new evidence still points to revenue near but most likely just below $20 billion: the CFO\\u2019s statement reiterates that visibility, while Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, not $6.5 billion to add to management\\u2019s total. Deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution, while the AMD performance and market-share evidence is a competitive counterweight without a quantified revenue impact. The implied figure remains within rung 3 and is consistent with NVIDIA\\u2019s broader FY2027 revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [8809, \"2026-07-30\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the supplied reference; that is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). No separate consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion, centered just below $20 billion, based primarily on management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility statement. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that overlaps the FY2027 period and is not added to management\\u2019s total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits at rung 3. The evidence still implies revenue near, but most likely just below, $20 billion: the new AMD performance comparisons add a competitive counterweight, while NVIDIA\\u2019s delivery and deployment evidence and the stronger infrastructure-demand view support execution. None quantifies a change to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 CPU revenue, so the evidence does not move the forecast across a rung boundary. This remains consistent with v14\\u2019s roughly $386 billion total-revenue forecast: the CPU business is a component well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8812, \"2026-07-31\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3. No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19.5 billion, or near but most likely just below $20 billion. This follows management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 visibility, supported by early delivery and deployment evidence but tempered by competitive evidence and the lower, period-mismatched Wolfe shipment estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive); no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. NVIDIA\\u2019s statement that the nearly $20 billion is for standalone CPU sales supports applying that reference to this thesis. Deliveries, OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployments and HPE\\u2019s planned fall availability support execution, while AMD\\u2019s performance comparisons and incumbent market position are counterweights. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate implies about $6.5 billion (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000) in calendar 2026, but it is a third-party estimate for a different period and does not revise NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 visibility. The evidence therefore implies revenue near, but most likely just below, $20 billion; it does not cross a rung boundary. That remains consistent with v14\\u2019s approximately $386 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8817, \"2026-08-01\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue is the reference and sits in rung 3. No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Roughly $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, most likely just below $20 billion. This uses management's nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility as the direct anchor; deliveries and planned deployments support execution, while Wolfe's 1.3 million \\u00d7 about $5,000 estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 but does not independently establish a higher FY2027 figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; that sits at rung 3, whose range includes $20 billion. The CEO's clarification that this is standalone CPU revenue makes it applicable to this thesis. The implied outcome remains roughly $19\\u201320 billion, most likely just below $20 billion: the visibility statement anchors the forecast, while Vera deliveries, OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment and HPE's fall availability support the ramp. Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000), but is not an FY2027 forecast; the newer AMD performance comparisons are a counterweight, not a quantified FY2027 revenue revision. NVIDIA's total-revenue forecast of about $386 billion leaves ample room for CPU revenue at this level, so the dependency is consistent. The implied figure does not cross the rung-2 boundary, which requires more than $20 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8823, \"2026-08-03\", 3, \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027, clarified by the CEO as standalone CPU sales. This reference sits in rung 3 (at or below $20 billion and at least $15 billion); no separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027. NVIDIA's direct visibility anchors the forecast just below $20 billion; rollout evidence supports reaching close to it, while Wolfe's 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and AMD competition temper the estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, with the CEO clarifying that it is standalone CPU revenue; nearly $20 billion sits in rung 3. I forecast roughly $19 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility statement remains the strongest annual input, supported by customer deliveries, OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment and HPE's fall availability. Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies approximately $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a material counterweight, while AMD's share and performance evidence adds execution risk; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 revision that outweighs NVIDIA's direct visibility and rollout evidence. The estimate remains below $20 billion but above rung 4's $15 billion boundary. The total-revenue dependency's roughly $390 billion FY2027 forecast leaves ample headroom for this CPU contribution.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8826, \"2026-08-04\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference, and it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive). No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"About $19 billion in FY2027: start from NVIDIA's nearly $20 billion visibility and retain it as the leading annual input, with customer deliveries and the planned Q3 OpenAI deployment supporting the ramp, while competition and Wolfe Research's roughly $6.5 billion calendar-2026 Vera estimate temper the forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. The direct visibility statement remains the strongest annual input, and customer deliveries plus OpenAI's planned Q3 deployment support the rollout. Wolfe Research's estimate implies about $6.5 billion from 1.3 million Vera CPUs at roughly $5,000 each in calendar 2026, while AMD's competitive evidence adds execution risk, but neither provides a quantified FY2027 revision that outweighs NVIDIA's visibility. The implied figure remains below $20 billion and above $15 billion, so it does not cross a boundary. At roughly $19 billion, CPU revenue is also comfortably below the dependent thesis's roughly $390 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8830, \"2026-08-05\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the available reference; it falls in rung 3. No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $19 billion in FY2027, derived from management\\u2019s direct \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d annual visibility, with customer deliveries and planned OpenAI deployment supporting the ramp. Wolfe\\u2019s shipment-and-price estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but the newer competitive evidence does not quantify a FY2027 reduction to the direct visibility figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. Deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-scale Q3 deployment support meaningful sales, while Wolfe\\u2019s roughly 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each imply only about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and AMD\\u2019s reported performance advantages add execution risk. Those competitive facts do not quantify a FY2027 revenue reduction that outweighs NVIDIA\\u2019s direct visibility, so the forecast remains around $19 billion\\u2014below the greater-than-$20 billion boundary. This is also consistent with dependent thesis v14\\u2019s roughly $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component and remains well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8834, \"2026-08-06\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference is in rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion, anchored on management's direct FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion. Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but is a shipment estimate for a period that does not override management's FY2027 visibility; the implied total remains at or below $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. The direct visibility supports an implied outcome of roughly $19\\u201320 billion, but not an outcome above $20 billion. Q3 plans for large OpenAI deployments and customer deliveries support the ramp, while Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and AMD's reported performance and share gains add execution risk. Those signals do not establish a quantified reduction that outweighs management's FY2027 visibility. The outcome remains below the rung-2 boundary, and it is consistent with dependency v14: CPU revenue is a component of NVIDIA's roughly $390\\u2013395 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8839, \"2026-08-07\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this reference is in rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion inclusive). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027. Management's direct visibility anchors the forecast near $20 billion; Wolfe Research's 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a meaningful execution-risk check but not a quantified revision to the FY2027 visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 because that rung includes $20 billion but rung 2 requires more than $20 billion. I infer approximately $19\\u201320 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility remains the strongest forecast input, while Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and highlights execution and ramp risk. June deliveries, planned large OpenAI deployments from Q3, and the additional AMD performance comparisons do not provide a quantified change to FY2027 revenue that would move the forecast across a rung boundary. This component-level estimate is consistent with dependency v14's roughly $400\\u2013410 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8843, \"2026-08-08\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility of nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 (at most $20 billion). No separate market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion for FY2027. I use management\\u2019s direct nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility as the primary forecast; disclosed deliveries and planned large OpenAI deployments support execution but are not sized enough to imply revenue above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3: rung 2 requires revenue greater than $20 billion. I infer about $19\\u201320 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility remains the strongest forecast input; deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployment support execution, but no disclosed deployment volume or quantified revision lifts the estimate above $20 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is an execution caution, not a replacement for the FY2027 visibility figure. AMD performance comparisons and market-share reports are competitive risks but do not quantify a revenue reduction. This estimate is consistent with dependency v14\\u2019s roughly $405\\u2013415 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: the CPU component is well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8845, \"2026-08-09\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the supplied reference; it sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027. The direct FY2027 visibility remains the strongest input; deliveries and planned large Q3 deployments support realization, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million CPUs at about $5,000 each imply about $6.5 billion in calendar 2026 and the new AMD comparisons temper upside without quantifying a FY2027 reduction.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; rung 2 requires more than $20 billion. The evidence still implies roughly $19\\u201320 billion: deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployment support execution, but no disclosed volume or quantified FY2027 revision lifts the estimate above $20 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a useful execution check but not a replacement for the direct FY2027 visibility. New AMD comparisons add specific competitive risk, but do not quantify a revenue reduction sufficient to move the implied figure across a rung boundary. The estimate is consistent with dependency v14\\u2019s roughly $415 billion total-revenue forecast; CPU revenue is only a portion of that total.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8851, \"2026-08-10\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, centered just below $20 billion. The direct FY2027 visibility remains the anchor; deliveries and planned Q3 deployment support execution, while the $6.5 billion implied by Wolfe's calendar-2026 shipment and price estimate is only a partial-period execution check.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; rung 2 requires more than $20 billion. The evidence still implies approximately $19\\u201320 billion: reported deliveries and OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment support execution, while Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 as an execution check, not a substitute FY2027 forecast. The separate $20 billion business target has no specified period, and the AMD performance comparisons add competitive risk without quantifying a FY2027 revenue reduction. The implied figure does not cross a rung boundary and is consistent with dependency v14's approximately $415 billion total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8856, \"2026-08-11\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based chiefly on management's direct visibility statement, with the CEO confirming it is standalone CPU revenue. The 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is an execution check, not a flat annualized FY2027 estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies roughly $19\\u201320 billion: the FY2027 visibility statement is the strongest direct input, and the CEO confirms it is standalone CPU revenue. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million units at about $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, useful as an execution check but not a flat annualized FY2027 forecast. Deliveries, OpenAI's planned Q3 deployment, and HPE's Vera-powered server support execution; AMD's performance comparisons add competitive risk but do not quantify a revenue reduction. The separate $20 billion business target has no specified period, so it does not establish revenue above $20 billion in FY2027. The implied outcome does not cross into rung 2. It is consistent with dependency v14's total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is well below the whole. Recent product-launch and competitive details, and the updated AI-capex theme, change the supporting context but not the implied $19\\u201320 billion outcome or rung.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\"]\"], [8861, \"2026-08-12\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management\\u2019s period-specific visibility statement. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026 and serve as an execution check, not a flat annualized FY2027 forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. That remains the strongest period-specific input. The implied outcome remains about $19\\u201320 billion: the separate $20 billion standalone-business target has no specified period, while Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, an execution check rather than a flat annualized FY2027 forecast. June deliveries, OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-scale Q3 deployment, and HPE\\u2019s planned fall availability support execution; AMD\\u2019s quantified performance comparisons are a competitive risk but do not establish a revenue reduction. The evidence does not imply revenue above $20 billion, the boundary for rung 2. The estimate is also consistent with v14\\u2019s roughly $410 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue remains a small part of the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8865, \"2026-08-13\", 3, \"No separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the relevant company reference, and it sits in rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion inclusive).\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility is period-specific, and deliveries plus planned large-scale Q3 deployment support that forecast. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million calendar-2026 shipments at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for that calendar year, but do not establish a FY2027 figure or justify flat annualization.\", \"The company\\u2019s period-specific visibility is still the strongest forecast input and implies revenue near, but not demonstrably above, $20 billion. Deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-scale Q3 deployment support execution, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026\\u2014not a FY2027 total or a valid flat annualization. AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence is a risk but does not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 revenue. The estimate therefore remains around $19\\u201320 billion, within rung 3; rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. This is consistent with v14\\u2019s roughly $400\\u2013410 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e443\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8869, \"2026-08-14\", 3, \"No independent market consensus for NVIDIA CPU revenue is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the relevant period-specific reference, at rung 3.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d period-specific visibility anchors the estimate just below or around $20 billion. Deliveries and planned deployments support execution, but the $6.5 billion implied by Wolfe\\u2019s 2026 unit-and-price forecast is for calendar 2026 and is not extrapolated flat into FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3; the supplied evidence does not give an independent market consensus. The period-specific visibility remains the strongest forecast input. Deliveries to customers and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-scale Q3 deployment support execution, but do not quantify revenue above $20 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not FY2027 revenue, so it is not carried forward as a flat annualized run-rate. The newer AMD performance comparisons sharpen the competitive risk but do not quantify a reduction in NVIDIA revenue. The implied outcome remains about $19\\u201320 billion, within rung 3; rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. This is consistent with v14\\u2019s $400\\u2013410 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8873, \"2026-08-15\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this places the reference in rung 3. No independent market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management\\u2019s period-specific visibility, with customer deliveries and planned OpenAI deployment supporting execution. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at roughly $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a downside check, but does not displace the company\\u2019s FY2027 outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no independent market consensus is supplied. The evidence still points to revenue around $19\\u201320 billion: deliveries and planned large-scale OpenAI deployment support execution, while the specific Wolfe estimate of about 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a meaningful downside check. That estimate is not a revised FY2027 company outlook, and the other competitive evidence does not quantify a reduction in NVIDIA revenue. The updated hyperscaler-spending theme is supportive, but does not size an increase above $20 billion. The implied outcome therefore remains within rung 3; rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8876, \"2026-08-16\", 3, \"No independent market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the reference, and it sits in rung 3.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s period-specific visibility anchors the estimate near $20 billion, while customer deliveries and planned Q3 deployment support execution without establishing a figure above that level. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a downside check rather than a replacement for the FY2027 visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue; that is rung 3, since rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. The implied outcome remains about $19\\u201320 billion: the FY2027 visibility is the strongest period-specific input, while the separate $20 billion business target has no specified period. Deliveries to named customers and OpenAI\\u2019s planned Q3 deployment support execution, but do not quantify revenue above the visibility figure. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a significant downside check, but it does not revise the FY2027 guidance; competitive evidence also weighs against an uplift. The implied figure is below the rung-2 boundary. It is consistent with dependency v14\\u2019s roughly $396 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue at about $20 billion is well below total revenue. Higher hyperscaler capex in themes v5 and v6 is supportive, but does not size an increase above $20 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8881, \"2026-08-17\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027. The period-specific visibility is the main forecast input; the $20 billion Vera target is not evidence of revenue above $20 billion, and Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 as a downside cross-check rather than a replacement FY2027 forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 because rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. The forecast remains near $19\\u201320 billion: management\\u2019s period-specific visibility is the strongest input, and the stated $20 billion Vera target does not establish revenue above that level. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a downside check, while deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned Q3 ramp support execution but are not sized enough to lift the forecast. New AMD performance comparisons add competitive downside but do not quantify a change to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 revenue outlook. The implied outcome remains within rung 3, below the greater-than-$20 billion boundary for rung 2. NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue conclusion is consistent with this CPU contribution as a part of the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e443\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8885, \"2026-08-18\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue; this reference is in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). No separate market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management\\u2019s period-specific visibility to nearly $20 billion. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a partial-period cross-check rather than an additive FY2027 estimate; the implied figure remains at or below $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 because rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. The implied outcome remains near, but not above, $20 billion: the Wolfe estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, but that is a partial-period estimate and does not replace the stronger FY2027 visibility. June deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned Q3 large-volume deployment support execution, but are not sized enough to lift the total above the guidance-based figure. AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence is a downside risk without a quantified revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $391 billion overall FY2027 revenue forecast is consistent with, and does not constrain, a CPU figure near $20 billion. The AI-capex themes support demand but do not supply a quantified CPU-revenue increment across the greater-than-$20-billion boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8889, \"2026-08-19\", 3, \"No independent market-consensus figure is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the operative reference; it sits in rung 3.\", \"Near, but not above, $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA\\u2019s period-specific visibility. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that Vera shipment estimate does not replace the stronger total-FY2027 company outlook.\", \"The supplied evidence gives no independent market-consensus figure; the operative reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion inclusive). I forecast revenue near, but not above, $20 billion: that visibility is the strongest period-specific input, and there is no quantified upward revision. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a third-party estimate for Vera shipments rather than a revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s total-FY2027 visibility. June deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-volume Q3 deployment support execution, but do not quantify enough additional volume to move the forecast above $20 billion. AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence is a downside risk, not a quantified NVIDIA revenue revision. NVIDIA\\u2019s total-revenue dependency forecast is about $403.5 billion, so a CPU forecast near $20 billion is consistent with the whole and does not exceed it.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8893, \"2026-08-20\", 3, \"No independent market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue is the operative reference, and it sits in rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion, inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, centered just below $20 billion: the period-specific management visibility remains the strongest forecast input, while the cited shipments and deployment plans support execution without quantifying a higher total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue; no independent market-consensus figure is supplied. That reference sits in rung 3. June deliveries, OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment, and further product availability support execution, but they do not quantify an upward revision. Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a useful but narrower product-and-period estimate rather than a revision to FY2027 total CPU revenue. AMD's competitive performance claims add execution risk but do not quantify a change to NVIDIA's revenue outlook. I therefore forecast approximately $19\\u201320 billion, centered just below $20 billion, which remains in rung 3. The dependency's approximately $403.5 billion total-revenue forecast is compatible with this CPU contribution; it treats the CPU figure as part of total revenue, not additional revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8897, \"2026-08-21\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this is rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion).\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, centered just below $20 billion. The management visibility is directly for FY2027; the $6.5 billion derived from Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 each is for Vera in calendar 2026, not total CPU revenue for the full FY2027 period.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The forecast remains about $19\\u201320 billion, centered just below $20 billion: the stated visibility is for the question's period, while the Vera shipment estimate of about 1.3 million chips at roughly $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 only and does not cover all CPU revenue or the full FY2027 period. Deliveries, OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment, and HPE's expected fall availability support execution; AMD's larger server-CPU market estimate supports demand, while its performance claims are a competitive counterweight. None quantifies a change to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue visibility, so the implied figure does not cross the $20 billion rung boundary. The v14 total-revenue forecast of about $403\\u2013404 billion is consistent with, and easily accommodates, CPU revenue near $20 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\"]\"], [8901, \"2026-08-22\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this is rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, centered just below $20 billion. The 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at roughly $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, and delivery and deployment evidence supports a ramp, but there is no quantified revision that lifts the FY2027 total above management's nearly-$20-billion visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The forecast remains about $19\\u201320 billion: Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment estimate at roughly $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while deliveries and OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment support a ramp; neither quantifies a higher FY2027 total. AMD's larger market estimates strengthen the demand backdrop, but its performance and market-share claims are counterweights and do not quantify NVIDIA's share. The implied outcome remains below the $20 billion boundary, and the component remains well below NVIDIA's approximately $403\\u2013404 billion total-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2262\\\"]\"], [8906, \"2026-08-23\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s stated visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, anchored by management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while the deployment evidence supports a ramp but does not establish a higher FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). The evidence still implies a total around $19\\u201320 billion: the $20 billion visibility is the strongest company-specific forecast, while Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and provides support for a ramp, not a separate FY2027 total. OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployment and AMD\\u2019s higher server-CPU market forecasts support demand, but they do not quantify NVIDIA revenue above $20 billion; AMD\\u2019s competitive performance and market-share evidence is a counterweight. The implied figure does not cross the greater-than-$20-billion boundary for rung 2 and remains far below NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $404 billion total-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\"]\"], [8911, \"2026-08-24\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the supplied reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility is the strongest period-specific forecast. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion in CY2026, supporting a ramp but not establishing a FY2027 total above $20 billion; the announced customer deployments are not sized.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence still implies roughly $19\\u201320 billion, not a forecast above $20 billion. Recent reports that Vera chips were delivered to customers and that OpenAI plans large Q3 deployments add commercial support, but those deployments are unsized. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit CY2026 estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for that calendar year, not a FY2027 total; at that price, $20 billion would require about 4 million units. Management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility remains the strongest direct forecast, while AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence tempers upside. The NVIDIA-wide FY2027 revenue conclusion is consistent with a roughly $20 billion CPU contribution but does not imply a higher CPU figure. The evidence therefore does not cross the greater-than-$20-billion boundary into rung 2.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2262\\\", \\\"e2294\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2353\\\", \\\"e2405\\\"]\"], [8915, \"2026-08-25\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility/expectation of about $20 billion in total CPU revenue; that reference sits at rung 3.\", \"Roughly $19\\u201320 billion for FY2027. Management\\u2019s direct FY2027 figure anchors the estimate; Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and the disclosed but unsized customer plans do not justify extrapolating above the $20 billion management figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility/expectation of about $20 billion in CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). The evidence still implies roughly $19\\u201320 billion: management\\u2019s FY2027 figure remains the strongest direct forecast, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and does not establish a higher FY2027 total. Customer deliveries, OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployment, and SpaceXAI\\u2019s announced use support adoption, but are not sized enough to lift the forecast above $20 billion. AMD\\u2019s larger market outlook supports demand, while its reported performance advantages add competitive risk. The implied figure remains within rung 3, and is consistent with NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $404\\u2013405 billion total-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2439\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e2294\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2479\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2405\\\"]\"], [8920, \"2026-08-26\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the reference; it sits at rung 3, whose range is $15 billion to $20 billion inclusive.\", \"Approximately $20 billion in FY2027. I carry forward management\\u2019s FY2027 outlook: the new more-than-double statement applies to FY2028, while the Grace figure is trailing-12-month revenue and the AWS CPU purchases have no specified period or exact quantity.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at the top of rung 3 (at most $20 billion). The new Q2 commentary strengthens the demand and supply case, but does not revise the FY2027 figure: the more-than-double statement is for FY2028, AWS\\u2019s \\u201cmillions of CPUs\\u201d has no purchase period or exact quantity, and Grace\\u2019s over-$5 billion figure is trailing-12-month revenue with no bridge showing it is additive to the FY2027 outlook. The evidence therefore still implies roughly $20 billion for FY2027, not a figure above $20 billion that would cross into rung 2. This is consistent with v14\\u2019s approximately $407 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast, which already includes CPU revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2439\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2794\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8924, \"2026-08-27\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the reference, on rung 3 (at the top boundary). Jensen Huang\\u2019s clarification that this is standalone CPU sales affects how it informs total CPU revenue.\", \"About $25 billion: roughly $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus an approximately $5 billion annual Grace CPU run-rate based on the disclosed TTM figure. That implies revenue above $20 billion but does not make revenue above $25 billion most likely.\", \"The market reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at the top of rung 3. However, Jensen Huang clarified that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales, while NVIDIA separately reported more than $5 billion of Grace CPU revenue on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that Grace run-rate into FY2027 implies total CPU revenue of about $25 billion alongside the roughly $20 billion standalone outlook: above $20 billion, but not enough to make revenue above $25 billion the most likely outcome. That places the forecast on rung 2. The separate Grace contribution is an inference from the disclosed product scope and run-rate, not an explicit company forecast of a combined total. This remains well below v14\\u2019s approximately $407 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2544\\\"]\"], [8928, \"2026-08-28\", 2, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 outlook is approximately $20 billion in CPU revenue (with the CEO clarifying that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales); this reference sits at rung 3. No separate consensus figure is supplied.\", \"About $25 billion total: approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace's more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue, carried forward as a run-rate. That implies rung 2; the evidence does not make a total above $25 billion the most likely outcome.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at rung 3; the CFO described nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue visibility, while the CEO clarified that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales. The implied total is about $25 billion: carry the approximately $20 billion standalone outlook and add Grace's disclosed trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, already a run-rate rather than a last-quarter annualization. The evidence supports meaningful Vera shipments and demand, but does not make revenue above $25 billion the most likely FY2027 outcome. This stays at rung 2, whose upper boundary includes $25 billion. v14's approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast is consistent with CPU revenue at this level; CPU remains a component of the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8933, \"2026-08-29\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue outlook is the reference; at $20 billion, it sits on rung 3 (Neutral: $15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $25 billion or slightly more: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera outlook forward and add Grace CPU revenue at its disclosed trailing-12-month run rate of more than $5 billion. The resulting forecast is just over the rung 1 threshold of $25 billion.\", \"The market/company reference is NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion). The implied total is modestly above $25 billion: carry the roughly $20 billion Vera standalone outlook forward and add Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Vera is now in full production with shipments underway, and the AWS adoption adds execution support; this makes the standalone target more credible and the combined total more likely to edge across the $25 billion boundary. The FY2028 more-than-doubling outlook supports the direction but is not counted as FY2027 revenue. This remains a close call, not a forecast materially above the boundary; v14's total-revenue forecast is consistent with this CPU component.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e668\\\"]\"], [8937, \"2026-08-30\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is about $20 billion, at rung 3. Management clarified that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales.\", \"Approximately $25 billion plus: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera outlook alongside Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. This narrowly exceeds the $25 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook of about $20 billion, which sits at rung 3. The standalone Vera target is not taken as certain merely because it is guidance, but full production, shipments to lead partners, and named customer deployments make achieving close to that target more likely; Grace revenue is separate from the standalone target. The Wolfe Research estimate of about 1.3 million Vera chips at roughly $5,000 each for calendar 2026 and reported AMD performance advantages temper the forecast, but do not outweigh the FY2027 outlook and execution evidence. Adding the Grace run-rate puts the most likely total just over $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. This is consistent with v14's approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a plausible part of, and does not exceed, the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e897\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8942, \"2026-08-31\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the relevant reference and sits at rung 3 (greater than $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion, assuming that Grace run-rate carries through FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion\\u2013$20 billion). I forecast total CPU revenue above $25 billion: the evidence identifies the $20 billion target as standalone CPU revenue, while Grace CPU revenue was already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that Grace run-rate into FY2027 puts total CPU revenue just above the rung-1 boundary. Vera is in production and shipping to named partners, supporting the standalone target, though the shipment and competitive evidence tempers confidence in the amount above the threshold. This is consistent with v14\\u2019s approximately $407.1 billion total-revenue forecast: the CPU estimate is a subset of total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2262\\\", \\\"e2294\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2353\\\", \\\"e2405\\\", \\\"e2439\\\", \\\"e2479\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2794\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e3175\\\", \\\"e3379\\\", \\\"e3381\\\", \\\"e3382\\\", \\\"e3414\\\", \\\"e3415\\\", \\\"e3416\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e3596\\\"]\"], [8947, \"2026-09-01\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference and sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. That clears rung 1\\u2019s greater-than-$25-billion boundary, though the amount above it is uncertain because quantified Vera shipment tracking implies materially less standalone revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. The forecast is higher because Grace CPU revenue was already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, while Vera is now in full production and shipping to lead partners; adding the Grace run-rate to the standalone outlook puts total CPU revenue just above $25 billion. This is a narrow crossing: Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit, approximately $5,000-per-chip forecast implies about $6.5 billion of Vera shipments in calendar 2026, well below the company outlook, so the outlook is not fully validated by quantified volumes. The later production and customer evidence supports the forecast, but does not eliminate that execution risk. The estimate remains consistent with v14\\u2019s approximately $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a subset.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e897\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\"]\"], [8953, \"2026-09-03\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 outlook is about $20 billion for standalone CPU revenue; that reference is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $25 billion-plus for FY2027: about $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue, with Grace contributing on a run-rate basis after exceeding $5 billion in trailing-12-month revenue during FY2027. This is a forecast, not a proved FY2027 total, and it narrowly clears the rung 1 boundary of more than $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook of about $20 billion, which sits in rung 3. For total CPU revenue, the implied forecast remains narrowly above $25 billion: the standalone Vera outlook is about $20 billion, and Grace had already generated more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027. The updated production and customer evidence supports delivery of the Vera outlook, though it does not quantify shipments enough to eliminate execution risk; the analyst view that CPU revenue reaches about 5% of total revenue by 2028 and the competitive evidence temper the estimate but do not outweigh management\\u2019s outlook and the Grace run-rate. The implied total is consistent with v14\\u2019s approximately $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\"]\"], [8957, \"2026-09-04\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 outlook is about $20 billion for standalone CPU revenue; as a total-revenue reference at about $20 billion, it sits at rung 3. The market's broader CPU-demand estimates are not company revenue guidance.\", \"About $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus more than $5 billion of Grace CPU revenue on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. This crosses the rung 1 boundary; the unsized customer deployments are not counted as additional revenue.\", \"The company reference is approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3 if treated as the total. The forecast for total CPU revenue is higher: NVIDIA's standalone Vera outlook is about $20 billion, while its separately identified Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027, implying a total just over $25 billion. Vera is in production and shipments are underway, supporting use of the current-year outlook; the disclosed customer deployments reinforce demand but are not sized enough to add a separate amount. Competitive evidence and the analyst view of CPU revenue as about 5% of total sales by 2028 temper the forecast, but do not outweigh the current-year management outlook and Grace revenue. This is consistent with v14's approximately $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\"]\"], [8961, \"2026-09-05\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion is rung 3. It is for standalone Vera CPU revenue, not the total CPU revenue asked here.\", \"Approximately $25 billion-plus for FY2027: combine the approximately $20 billion standalone Vera outlook with Grace CPU revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, using that reported run-rate as the estimate for Grace's FY2027 contribution.\", \"The company reference is about $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3. The implied total is just over $25 billion: add that standalone outlook to Grace CPU revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Vera shipments are underway and management says it is in full production, supporting the outlook; the Grace run-rate is the narrow margin over the rung-1 boundary. AMD competition tempers confidence, but the evidence does not make a total at or below $25 billion more likely. The implied total is well below NVIDIA's roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast in v14, so it is consistent with that dependency.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e3419\\\"]\"], [8965, \"2026-09-06\", 1, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation is about $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue; that figure alone is rung 3 on this total-CPU-revenue ladder.\", \"About $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. That is above the rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation, which is rung 3 for total CPU revenue. I read the CEO's clarification that the $20 billion is standalone Vera CPU sales alongside Grace, rather than adding Grace to an already-total figure: the $20 billion Vera target plus Grace revenue above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. Vera shipments and production are underway and customer deployments support the ramp; Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit, roughly $5,000 ASP estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in achieving the full target, but does not outweigh the current company outlook and deployment evidence. The implied figure crosses the rung-1 boundary. It is also far below NVIDIA's approximately $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14, so it is consistent with that dependency.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e884\\\"]\"], [8970, \"2026-09-07\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation is the relevant company reference; taken alone as total CPU revenue, it sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $25 billion or somewhat more: the roughly $20 billion standalone CPU expectation plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, treated as a run-rate into FY2027. This is an estimate, not a reported FY2027 total, and it lies just above the rung-1 boundary of $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation, which sits at rung 3 if read as total CPU revenue. The CEO clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU sales; adding Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried forward as a run-rate, implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of roughly $25 billion or somewhat more. That crosses the rung-1 boundary. The inference is not certain: Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit calendar-2026 shipment forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion of Vera revenue and tempers confidence in the full target. But more recent evidence says Vera is in full production, shipments are underway, and AWS adopted Vera alongside a 2-million-GPU agreement; NVIDIA also cited customer demand and improving supply. The FY2028 more-than-doubling outlook supports momentum but does not itself increase the FY2027 estimate. The implied CPU figure remains well below NVIDIA's approximately $407 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast in dependency v14, so it is consistent with the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8975, \"2026-09-08\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation; that reference sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $25 billion and slightly above: the $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation plus Grace CPU revenue exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as an annual run-rate. This is above the $25 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation, which sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion). The implied total is higher: add the $20 billion standalone Vera expectation to Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried forward as an annual run-rate, implying total CPU revenue slightly above $25 billion. That crosses the rung-1 boundary. The estimate is a forecast rather than an already-reported FY2027 result, but Vera is in production with shipments underway and disclosed customer deployments. The implied CPU revenue is well below NVIDIA's roughly $407 billion total FY2027 revenue forecast, so it is consistent with dependency v14. Competitive evidence and the limited shipment estimate temper confidence, but do not outweigh the company outlook and reported Grace run-rate.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8979, \"2026-09-09\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation is the reference; it sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace\\u2019s more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate yields total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, implying rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, which sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion). The evidence implies a higher total: the $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation is for Vera, while Grace separately had more than $5 billion of trailing-12-month revenue as of the Q2 FY2027 call. Carrying Grace\\u2019s reported TTM run-rate through FY2027 gives total CPU revenue above $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. The 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment forecast at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in execution, but it is a dated shipment forecast rather than a revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance; shipments and deployments are underway. The FY2028 more-than-double outlook and improving supply outlook are supportive but do not change the FY2027 calculation. The implied total is far below NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $407 billion total FY2027 revenue forecast, so it is consistent with dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8982, \"2026-09-10\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's roughly $20B FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation is the available reference and sits in rung 3 ($15B\\u2013$20B). The nearly $20B figure was clarified as standalone CPU sales; no separate market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $20B of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace's more-than-$5B trailing-12-month revenue run-rate carried through FY2027 implies total CPU revenue above $25B. That crosses the rung-1 boundary.\", \"The available reference is NVIDIA management's roughly $20B FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, which sits in rung 3 (at most $20B). The implied total is higher: management specified that the approximately $20B FY2027 expectation was for standalone CPU sales, while Grace CPU revenue had already exceeded $5B on a trailing-12-month basis; carrying that Grace run-rate through FY2027 gives total CPU revenue above $25B, crossing into rung 1. Vera shipments are underway and management cited improving supply, supporting the standalone outlook. Wolfe Research's 1.3M-unit CY2026 shipment forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5B for that period and tempers execution confidence, but it is a third-party, calendar-year forecast rather than a revision to management's FY2027 guidance. The FY2028 more-than-double outlook is supportive context, not added to FY2027 revenue. The dependency's roughly $407B FY2027 total-revenue forecast is consistent with this CPU estimate; CPU revenue remains well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8987, \"2026-09-11\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue expectation is the reference; it sits at rung 3 (at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue with a trailing-12-month run-rate already above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, in rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue expectation, which sits at rung 3 (at most $20 billion). The implied total is above $25 billion: add the roughly $20 billion standalone outlook to Grace CPU revenue already exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Vera's production and shipments, plus the improving supply outlook, support delivery of the standalone forecast; the lower Wolfe shipment estimate for calendar 2026 tempers confidence but does not replace the FY2027 guidance or cover the full fiscal year. NVIDIA's total FY2027 revenue forecast in v14 is large enough to accommodate this CPU estimate.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [8991, \"2026-09-12\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue expectation is the available reference; treating that as the total-revenue reference places it at rung 3 (at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $25 billion-plus: carry NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook forward and add the continuing Grace CPU contribution, whose trailing-12-month revenue had already exceeded $5 billion. The latter is a run-rate input, not a claim that a single trailing-12-month figure by itself proves FY2027 revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue expectation, which is rung 3 if read as the total-revenue figure. The CEO clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU, while Grace CPU revenue had already exceeded a $5 billion trailing-12-month run rate; carrying that Grace contribution alongside the standalone outlook implies total FY2027 CPU revenue modestly above $25 billion. The 1.3 million Vera shipments at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and temper execution confidence, but do not cover the full fiscal-year outlook; shipments and deployments are underway. AMD\\u2019s performance claims add competitive risk, but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 forecast. The implied total clears rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary, and the approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast in v14 is large enough to accommodate it.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\"]\"], [8996, \"2026-09-13\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 expectation is about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales (rung 3 if read as total CPU revenue). Since the CEO clarified that this is standalone and Grace is separate, the relevant total-CPU reference, adding the reported Grace run rate, sits just above $25 billion at rung 1.\", \"About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue carried at its reported trailing-12-month run rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion. That is above rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion threshold; it remains well below the approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"The company\\u2019s FY2027 headline expectation is about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales, which would be rung 3 if treated as the answer to total CPU revenue. But Jensen Huang clarified that the figure is for standalone CPU, while Grace CPU revenue is separate; NVIDIA later reported Grace revenue above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run rate. Carrying that run rate alongside the roughly $20 billion standalone expectation implies total CPU revenue just above $25 billion, crossing rung 1\\u2019s boundary. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera shipment forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in the standalone ramp, but it is an earlier third-party forecast, not a revision of NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook. Full production and shipments underway support execution; AMD\\u2019s competitive claims are a risk but do not quantify a cut to NVIDIA\\u2019s outlook. This CPU estimate remains a small part of the approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [9000, \"2026-09-14\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU revenue expectation is the market/company reference; it sits in rung 3. It is a stand-alone figure, not total CPU revenue.\", \"A little over $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of stand-alone CPU revenue plus a roughly $5 billion-or-more Grace contribution, using Grace's reported above-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue as the available scale indicator. The TTM amount is not a guaranteed FY2027 Grace figure, so the estimate is close to the boundary rather than a precise point forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU revenue expectation, which sits in rung 3. For total CPU revenue, that standalone figure is joined by Grace: its trailing-12-month revenue was already above $5 billion as of the Q2 FY2027 call. That supports a forecast just over $25 billion, though the margin is not robust because the TTM period partly predates FY2027 and Vera is ramping while Grace may transition. Vera's full production, shipments and named customer deployments support the standalone ramp; AMD's performance claims are a competitive risk but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's forecast. The implied figure is consistent with v14's approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue remains a small part of the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [9003, \"2026-09-15\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation is the closest supplied reference; it sits in rung 3. No separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\", \"About $25\\u201326 billion: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a roughly $5 billion-or-more Grace contribution, using Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue as the run-rate indication. This is marginally above the $25 billion rung-1 boundary, though the TTM period partly predates FY2027.\", \"The closest market reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation, which is rung 3; the evidence supplies no separate market consensus for total CPU revenue. Adding Grace, whose trailing-12-month revenue was already above $5 billion by Q2 FY2027, implies total FY2027 CPU revenue around $25\\u201326 billion, just over rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary. That margin is narrow: the Grace trailing period partly predates FY2027, and Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies only about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026. But NVIDIA\\u2019s $20 billion FY2027 visibility, deliveries and rollout support the higher standalone forecast, while the AMD performance and market-share evidence does not quantify a reduction to it. The implied figure is a small part of the current dependency forecast of about $418 billion in total FY2027 revenue, so it is consistent with v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\"]\"], [9007, \"2026-09-16\", 1, \"No separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied. NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation of about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales is the reference; that figure is in rung 3.\", \"About $25\\u201326 billion: carry Grace's more-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue into FY2027 and add NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion standalone CPU expectation. The estimate is narrowly above the $25 billion cutoff for rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation of about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales; there is no separate total-CPU market consensus shown, and $20 billion sits in rung 3. The implied total is about $25\\u201326 billion: the $20 billion Vera/standalone expectation plus Grace's trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried as a run-rate into FY2027. That is narrowly above rung 1's $25 billion boundary. Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and AMD's performance claims plus the roughly $20 billion server-CPU demand estimate temper confidence; none is a quantified revision or cancellation that displaces NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation. Deliveries, broad deployment plans and full production support execution. The implied total is far below NVIDIA's approximately $419 billion FY2027 company-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e4796\\\", \\\"e4797\\\"]\"], [9012, \"2026-09-17\", 1, \"NVIDIA's expectation of about $20 billion in FY2027 standalone CPU revenue; exactly $20 billion is rung 3.\", \"About $25 billion-plus: add NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation to Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried as a run-rate through FY2027. That clears the rung 1 boundary of more than $25 billion, narrowly.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's expectation of about $20 billion in FY2027 standalone CPU revenue, which is rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion, inclusive). For total CPU revenue, I carry Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion as a run-rate through FY2027 and add it to the $20 billion standalone figure, implying just over $25 billion and crossing into rung 1. Vera shipments and customer deployments support execution, though the unshipped volume implied by the full-year target remains uncertain. AMD's performance and share gains, analyst estimates, and the roughly $20 billion server-CPU-market estimate temper confidence but do not quantify a cut to NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation. The implied CPU total is consistent with v14's roughly $419 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue remains a subset of total revenue. The AI-capex themes support demand, but are not themselves evidence of CPU sales.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e4796\\\", \\\"e4797\\\"]\"], [9016, \"2026-09-18\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation is the reference and sits at rung 3 (Neutral, $15\\u201320 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried as a FY2027 run-rate, implies slightly more than $25 billion in total CPU revenue\\u2014rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation, which is rung 3. For total CPU revenue, adding Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion to the approximately $20 billion standalone expectation implies just over $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in the scale of the standalone forecast; however, NVIDIA later said Vera was in full production, cited customer demand and improving supply, and reiterated the FY2027 expectation. AMD's competitive claims and the approximately $20 billion server-CPU-market estimate are risks but do not quantify a cut to NVIDIA's FY2027 forecast. This component forecast is consistent with the dependency's approximately $419 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e4796\\\", \\\"e4797\\\"]\"], [9019, \"2026-09-19\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation is the supplied reference; at exactly $20 billion it sits at rung 3. The supplied evidence does not give a separate market-consensus figure.\", \"About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue carried forward at its reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies just over $25 billion in total FY2027 CPU revenue. That clears the rung-1 threshold narrowly; the Vera shipment estimate of about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 is a material execution risk, but not a quantified revision to NVIDIA's expectation.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation of about $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3. The forecast for total CPU revenue adds Grace: carrying its reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue forward at roughly that run-rate alongside the $20 billion standalone expectation implies just over $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. This is a narrow crossing, not an established result: Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera shipments at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and temper confidence in the standalone forecast. But NVIDIA says Vera is in production and shipping to customers, and its FY2027 expectation remains supported by customer demand; AMD's performance claims do not quantify a revenue reduction. The implied total is well below the approximately $419 billion FY2027 company-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e5047\\\", \\\"e5048\\\", \\\"e5049\\\", \\\"e5050\\\", \\\"e5051\\\", \\\"e5052\\\", \\\"e5053\\\", \\\"e5054\\\"]\"], [9025, \"2026-09-20\", 1, \"NVIDIA's stated expectation of nearly $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue for FY2027; that reference is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue carried forward at its reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion.\", \"NVIDIA's stated FY2027 expectation of nearly $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue is the reference and sits at rung 3. Adding Grace at its reported trailing-12-month revenue run-rate of more than $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. Wolfe's lower Vera shipment estimate and AMD's performance claims temper confidence, but neither supplies a quantified revision to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue expectation; reported production and customer shipments support execution. The implied CPU total remains well below NVIDIA's roughly $419 billion total-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e5049\\\", \\\"e5050\\\", \\\"e5051\\\", \\\"e5052\\\", \\\"e5053\\\", \\\"e5054\\\"]\"]], [\"How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 25000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 20000000000.0, 25000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 15000000000.0, 20000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 10000000000.0, 15000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 10000000000.0]]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nv=\\\"v9\\\"\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(base/\\\"linked-1-copy\\\"/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nrows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>8737 order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,)).fetchall()\nprint(json.dumps([rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
