{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nv=\\\"v21\\\"\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(base/\\\"linked-1-copy\\\"/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nrows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>8737 order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,)).fetchall()\nprint(json.dumps([rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nv=\\\"v16\\\"\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(base/\\\"linked-1-copy\\\"/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nrows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>8737 order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,)).fetchall()\nprint(json.dumps([rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nv=\\\"v21\\\"\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(base/\\\"linked-1-copy\\\"/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nrows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>8737 order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,)).fetchall()\nprint(json.dumps([rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"[[[8794, \"2026-07-26\", 3, \"Wall Street estimates 42% FY2027 revenue growth; that is rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Approximately 42%, with an upside skew: the near-$20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue forecast and the demand-related themes support growth, but the supplied evidence does not quantify an uplift to NVIDIA\\u2019s total growth estimate.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The CPU-revenue forecast and the capex and export-control themes add upside support, but none supplies a quantified revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s total-revenue growth; the evidence therefore does not imply a figure at or above the 50% rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\"]\"], [8798, \"2026-07-27\", 3, \"Wall Street expects 42% FY2027 revenue growth for NVIDIA; this is rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Around 42% growth, with an unquantified upward skew from the possibility of additional Alphabet purchases and stronger hyperscaler spending. The evidence does not imply growth reaching 50%.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies growth around that estimate, with an upward skew: Alphabet\\u2019s higher spending could lead to more NVIDIA purchases, and the in-force capex and export-control themes also lean toward stronger demand. But the Alphabet view gives no quantified NVIDIA order or revenue increase, and the CPU forecast is only about $20 billion against NVIDIA\\u2019s much larger total-revenue base. Nothing shown supports moving the implied growth to the 50% boundary.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\"]\"], [8802, \"2026-07-28\", 3, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Roughly 44% growth, as a modest upward adjustment from the 42% reference for the positive but unquantified Alphabet demand view and the stronger capex outlook; the roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU forecast is only a small part of NVIDIA's total-revenue base.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The updated capex and export-control theme conclusions modestly affect the forecast, but the Alphabet view gives no quantified NVIDIA order or revenue increase, and the CPU forecast remains small relative to total revenue. The implied growth therefore stays in the low-to-mid 40s, below rung 2's 50% boundary and above rung 4's 35% boundary.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\"]\"], [8806, \"2026-07-29\", 3, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 total revenue growth estimate, which sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately 47% FY2027 growth: the 42% reference is raised modestly for the strong reported 85% fiscal Q1 growth and additional demand support, without extrapolating that quarter's rate across the year or treating total CPU revenue as incremental growth.\", \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate is the reference and sits in rung 3. The reported 85% year-over-year growth in fiscal Q1 is strong upside evidence, but it is one quarter's result, not a basis for carrying 85% growth through FY2027; there is no supplied guidance or quantified revision to the FY2027 forecast. The Alphabet view and the capex and export-access themes add upside, while the CPU dependency supports meaningful FY2027 revenue but does not provide a prior-year base from which to calculate incremental growth or justify adding its full revenue to the 42% estimate. I therefore imply roughly 47% growth: above the reference, but still below rung 2's 50% boundary and above rung 4's 35% boundary.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\"]\"], [8810, \"2026-07-30\", 3, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Approximately 49% FY2027 total revenue growth: above the 42% reference, reflecting the strong Q1 result and upside from the expanded cumulative chip-sales target and demand/access backdrop, while discounting the target because it is not a FY2027 revenue figure and not extrapolating Q1's 85% growth as a full-year rate.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies somewhat faster growth, but not enough to cross rung 2's 50% boundary: Q1's 85% year-over-year increase is strong upside evidence, not a full-year growth rate to carry forward unchanged, and Huang's $1 trillion cumulative chip-sales target through 2027 is not a standalone FY2027 revenue forecast. The Alphabet view and demand/access themes add upside, while the CPU dependency implies meaningful FY2027 revenue but supplies no comparable prior-year base and is part of, not additive to, total revenue. I therefore imply roughly 49% growth, still within rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1285\\\"]\"], [8814, \"2026-07-31\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 60% FY2027 revenue growth. I treat the 82% analyst estimate as an upside signal rather than consensus, discount it for the lack of later-quarter guidance, and weigh it with 85% reported Q1 growth and management's $91 billion Q2 revenue guide.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence now points materially higher: Q1 growth was 85%, management guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion, and a separate analyst estimate puts FY2027 growth at 82%. I discount that 82% forecast because it is not the consensus and later quarters are not guided here; the $1 trillion cumulative chip-sales target is not an FY2027 revenue forecast. Taken together, the evidence implies about 60% FY2027 growth\\u2014just across rung 1's 60% boundary. The Q1 figure is not annualized flat, and the CPU dependency supports meaningful revenue but is part of total revenue and supplies no comparable prior-year growth base.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\"]\"], [8818, \"2026-08-01\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3.\", \"Approximately 60% FY2027 total revenue growth, inferred from 85% Q1 year-over-year growth and the $91 billion Q2 guide (about 11.5% sequential growth), while discounting the 82% analyst estimate and not treating the cumulative $1 trillion chip-sales target as FY2027 revenue.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly 60% growth: Q1 FY2027 revenue grew 85%, and the $91 billion Q2 guide is about 11.5% above Q1, supporting a strong ongoing run-rate; I discount the separate 82% analyst estimate rather than taking it as the forecast. The $1 trillion cumulative chip-sales target is not a FY2027 total-revenue forecast. Roughly 60% reaches rung 1's 60% boundary. The CPU dependency's roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 forecast supports revenue contribution but is part of total revenue and supplies no separate growth rate, so it does not justify adding its amount to the total forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\"]\"], [8819, \"2026-08-02\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the stated reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Roughly 75\\u201380% FY2027 growth: the 82% analyst estimate is a bullish annual indicator but its period is not explicitly identified as FY2027, so it is discounted; Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst Q2/Q3 growth estimates of 96%/81% support a high-growth run-rate.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%). The forecast is materially above that reference: Q1 growth was 85%, management guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion, and analyst estimates put Q2 and Q3 growth at 96% and 81%, respectively. I read the separate 82% annual estimate as supportive but discount it because its period is not stated as FY2027. Taken together, the evidence implies roughly 75\\u201380% FY2027 growth, crossing rung 1's 60% boundary. The CPU dependency supports some revenue contribution but is part of total revenue, so its $19\\u201320 billion estimate cannot be added on top or treated as a separate growth rate.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1391\\\"]\"], [8827, \"2026-08-04\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral, 35% to below 50%).\", \"Roughly 75\\u201382% FY2027 revenue growth: the explicit 82% annual estimate is supported by 85% reported Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. This carries the current growth run-rate forward rather than annualizing Q1 flat.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%). The forecast is materially higher: NVIDIA reported 85% Q1 growth, guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion (up 11.5% sequentially from Q1), and analyst estimates put Q2 and Q3 growth at 96% and 81%; a separate analyst estimate explicitly gives 82% for FY2027. Together these support roughly 75\\u201382% FY2027 growth, above rung 1's 60% boundary. The CPU dependency's roughly $19 billion FY2027 forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top, and is consistent with the total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1391\\\"]\"], [8831, \"2026-08-05\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the reference; it falls in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 80\\u201382% FY2027 revenue growth, centered on the explicit analyst estimate of 82%. Reported Q1 growth of 85% and analyst expectations of 96% in Q2 and 81% in Q3 support that outlook; these quarterly rates are corroboration, not annualized as a flat run-rate.\", \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 estimate is the reference and sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 80\\u201382% growth: the explicit 82% FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by 85% reported Q1 growth and high Q2/Q3 growth expectations. That forecast is above rung 1\\u2019s 60% threshold. The CPU-revenue dependency\\u2019s roughly $19 billion FY2027 forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with this total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1391\\\"]\"], [8836, \"2026-08-06\", 1, \"Wall Street's stated FY2027 revenue-growth estimate of 42%, which is in rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, based on the explicit analyst estimate for FY2027, with 85% reported Q1 growth and strong Q2/Q3 growth expectations supporting that trajectory. The $91 billion Q2 guide supports the near-term revenue run-rate but is not itself a stated annual growth rate.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly 82% growth: the explicit FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by reported Q1 growth of 85%, a $91 billion Q2 management revenue guide, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That is above rung 1's 60% threshold. The roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue dependency is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1391\\\"]\"], [8840, \"2026-08-07\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3.\", \"Approximately 82% FY2027 revenue growth, based on the explicit analyst estimate and supported by 85% Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 management guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly 82% growth: the explicit FY2027 analyst estimate is consistent with 85% Q1 growth, management's $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That clears rung 1's 60% threshold. NVIDIA's roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\"]\"], [8847, \"2026-08-09\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, taking the explicit analyst estimate as the annual forecast and checking it against 85% reported Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 analyst growth estimates.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. That is a reference, not the forecast once the newer run-rate evidence is considered: the explicit FY2027 analyst estimate is 82%, with Q1 reported growth of 85%, management\\u2019s Q2 guide of $91 billion, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth supporting a high-growth trajectory. An implied outcome around 82% clears rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add; it is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1391\\\"]\"], [8852, \"2026-08-10\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"Approximately 82% FY2027 total revenue growth: the explicit analyst estimate for FY2027 is corroborated by 85% reported Q1 growth and analyst estimates of 96% for Q2 and 81% for Q3, with management guiding Q2 revenue to $91 billion. This uses the full-year estimate and subsequent-quarter trajectory, not Q1 annualized flat.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The most likely outcome is materially higher: the explicit 82% FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by 85% reported Q1 growth and analyst estimates of 96% for Q2 and 81% for Q3; management's $91 billion Q2 guide is consistent with continued rapid growth. This points to roughly 82% for the year, well above rung 1's 60% threshold. The longer-term $1 trillion cumulative chip-sales target is discounted rather than treated as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA's approximately $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU revenue forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this total-revenue outlook.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\"]\"], [8857, \"2026-08-11\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference; it falls in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, using the specific FY2027 analyst estimate, supported by 85% reported Q1 growth, management\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% growth in Q2 and 81% in Q3.\", \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate is the reference and sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 82% growth: a specific FY2027 analyst estimate is consistent with reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 revenue guide, and analyst estimates of 96% growth in Q2 and 81% in Q3. That clears rung 1\\u2019s 60% threshold. New GPU rental pricing and CoreWeave demand indicators reinforce the high-growth outlook but do not quantify a different NVIDIA FY2027 growth rate. NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU revenue forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this conclusion.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1701\\\", \\\"e1702\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\"]\"], [8862, \"2026-08-12\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the reference; it sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, based on the specific analyst FY2027 estimate, with reported 85% Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 revenue guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 providing supporting run-rate evidence.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence in force instead implies about 82% growth: the specific FY2027 estimate is reinforced by reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That forecast clears rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. The CPU thesis remains a component of NVIDIA revenue, not an amount to add to total revenue, and is consistent with this conclusion. Higher GPU rental rates and CoreWeave\\u2019s reported demand/backlog support the outlook but do not independently quantify NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 growth.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1391\\\", \\\"e1701\\\", \\\"e1702\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\", \\\"e1764\\\"]\"], [8866, \"2026-08-13\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3.\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, using the specific analyst estimate as the full-year forecast and checking it against reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-supported forecast is about 82%: the specific FY2027 estimate is supported by Q1\\u2019s reported 85% year-over-year growth, management\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimates of 96% growth in Q2 and 81% in Q3. That forecast is above rung 1\\u2019s 60% threshold. CoreWeave demand evidence supports the outlook but does not independently quantify NVIDIA revenue; the CPU thesis is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add, and its $19\\u201320 billion forecast is consistent with this total-revenue conclusion.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1764\\\"]\"], [8870, \"2026-08-14\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, using the specific analyst estimate for the year and corroborating it with reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimated Q2 and Q3 growth of 96% and 81%. The CPU revenue forecast is included within total revenue, not added to it.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The implied forecast is about 82%: the specific FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by reported Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That is above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. The CoreWeave evidence supports demand but does not independently quantify NVIDIA revenue, and NVIDIA CPU revenue is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1701\\\", \\\"e1702\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\", \\\"e1707\\\", \\\"e1764\\\"]\"], [8874, \"2026-08-15\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 growth, the specific analyst estimate in force; applying it to reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion implies roughly $393 billion of FY2027 revenue. Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst Q2/Q3 growth estimates of 96%/81% support carrying a high growth rate through the year.\", \"The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 82% growth, well above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary: a specific FY2027 analyst estimate is consistent with Q1 growth of 85%, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. The newly reported FY2026 base and Q4 growth add context but do not overturn that forecast. NVIDIA\\u2019s expected $19\\u201320 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue is only a component of total revenue (about 5% of the implied total); it is consistent with, and must not be added to, the whole. The capex and export-policy themes in force generally support demand or avoid potential constraints, consistent with this rung.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1764\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1884\\\"]\"], [8878, \"2026-08-16\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate, cited in e966, e1148, and e1285; this is rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, implying roughly $393 billion of revenue against reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. The annual analyst estimate is supported by 85% Q1 growth, management\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 82% growth, or roughly $393 billion of FY2027 revenue on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: the specific FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by 85% reported Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That is well above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. The CPU dependency\\u2019s $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 conclusion is a component of this total, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with the implied whole.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1884\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1369\\\", \\\"e1384\\\", \\\"e1389\\\", \\\"e1391\\\", \\\"e1701\\\", \\\"e1702\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\", \\\"e1707\\\", \\\"e1764\\\"]\"], [8882, \"2026-08-17\", 1, \"Wall Street\\u2019s 42% estimate for NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 revenue growth, implying roughly $393 billion of revenue from FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion ($215.94B \\u00d7 1.82). The explicit FY2027 analyst estimate is supported by reported Q1 growth, the Q2 revenue guide, and analyst Q2 and Q3 growth estimates.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about 82% growth, or roughly $393 billion of FY2027 revenue on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: the specific FY2027 analyst estimate is reinforced by 85% reported Q1 growth, management\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth. That is above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. Recent FY2026 Q4 growth and customer-backlog evidence add support but do not justify changing the 82% implied figure. The CPU dependency\\u2019s roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 conclusion is part of total revenue, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with the implied whole.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1884\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\", \\\"e1764\\\"]\"], [8886, \"2026-08-18\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate; rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 82% FY2027 growth, implying approximately $393 billion of revenue: $215.94 billion of FY2026 revenue multiplied by 1.82. The specific analyst estimate is consistent with the 85% Q1 growth, the $91 billion Q2 guide, and analyst estimates of 96% Q2 and 81% Q3 growth.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-supported forecast remains about 82% growth, or approximately $393 billion of revenue using FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion as the base. That forecast is supported by the specific analyst estimate and the strong reported and guided quarterly run-rate; it is well above rung 1's 60% threshold. The roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue conclusion is part of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with this total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1884\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1764\\\", \\\"e2040\\\", \\\"e2041\\\", \\\"e2036\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e2061\\\"]\"], [8891, \"2026-08-19\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% estimate for NVIDIA FY2027 revenue growth is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 80% FY2027 revenue growth, implying roughly $389 billion of revenue from the FY2026 base of $215.94 billion ($215.94 billion \\u00d7 1.80). This uses Morningstar's 80% FY2027 model, supported by Q1's reported growth, Q2 guidance implying roughly 95% year-over-year growth versus the $46.7 billion prior-year quarter, and analysts' 81% Q3 estimate; the separate roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate implies about 85% growth and is consistent with the same high-growth outlook.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which is in rung 3. The evidence now supports about 80% growth, well above rung 1's 60% threshold: Morningstar models 80%, and the reported Q1 result, Q2 guidance versus the prior-year quarter, and Q3 estimate are consistent with a strong FY2027 run-rate. This forecast is also consistent with the dependency's conclusion of nearly $20 billion in FY2027 CPU revenue as one component of total revenue, not an amount to add to it.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1097\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1764\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1884\\\", \\\"e2036\\\", \\\"e2040\\\", \\\"e2041\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2154\\\"]\"], [8895, \"2026-08-20\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference; it sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 80% growth, or roughly $389 billion of FY2027 revenue, using Morningstar's 80% model against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. The separate roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate implies about 85% growth and is supportive upside rather than the central forecast.\", \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate is the reference and sits in rung 3. The evidence points materially above it: Morningstar's FY2027 model is 80%, while NVIDIA's Q2 guidance implies nearly 95% year-over-year growth and the Q3 estimate is 81%. Those forward inputs, together with the reported Q1 growth, support a full-year outcome above rung 1's 60% boundary; the 42% consensus does not keep the judgement at its rung. The forecast is consistent with the dependency's approximately $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue conclusion as one component of total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1148\\\", \\\"e1285\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e2154\\\"]\"], [8899, \"2026-08-21\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (35% to below 50%).\", \"About 80\\u201385% FY2027 growth: Morningstar's 80% model implies roughly $389 billion of revenue from the FY2026 base of $215.94 billion, while the separate roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate implies about 85% growth.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly 80\\u201385% growth, or about $389\\u2013400 billion of FY2027 revenue against FY2026's $215.94 billion: Morningstar models 80%, while a separate estimate puts FY2027 revenue near $400 billion. Q1 reported growth of 85%, Q2 guidance implies nearly 95% year-over-year growth, and the cited Q3 estimate is 81%; taken together, these forward inputs support an outcome well above the 60% rung-1 boundary, rather than simply annualizing Q1. The approximately $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue forecast is consistent as one component of total revenue and does not exceed the whole.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2244\\\"]\"], [8903, \"2026-08-22\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About $389\\u2013400 billion of FY2027 total revenue, or roughly 80\\u201385% growth versus FY2026's $215.94 billion. The range is anchored by Morningstar's 80% model and the roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate, with reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance supporting strong growth rather than flat annualization of the latest quarter.\", \"The reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies about $389\\u2013400 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 80\\u201385% growth against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: Morningstar models 80%, and the roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate implies about 85%. Q1 growth, Q2 guidance implying nearly 100% year-over-year growth, and the cited Q3 estimate of 81% support a full-year outcome above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The new $1 trillion order-book claim spans 2026 and 2027 and does not specify NVIDIA's FY2027 share, so it reinforces demand but does not justify assigning the whole amount to this year or lifting the implied estimate. The CPU dependency's $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 forecast is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total-revenue estimate.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1249\\\", \\\"e1278\\\", \\\"e1280\\\", \\\"e1333\\\", \\\"e1348\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e1388\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1884\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2244\\\"]\"], [8907, \"2026-08-23\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate, which is rung 3.\", \"About $389\\u2013400 billion of FY2027 revenue: 80% growth under Morningstar's model, rising to about 85% if revenue reaches roughly $400 billion, against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies about $389\\u2013400 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 80\\u201385% growth against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: Morningstar models 80%, while the roughly $400 billion estimate implies about 85%. Q1's 85% reported growth, Q2 guidance implying nearly 100% year-over-year growth, and the cited Q3 estimate of 81% support growth above rung 1's 60% threshold. The $1 trillion order book spans 2026 and 2027 and does not specify FY2027 revenue, so it supports demand but is not assigned in full to this year. The CPU dependency's $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 forecast is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total-revenue estimate.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e2244\\\"]\"], [8913, \"2026-08-24\", 1, \"Wall Street's 42% FY2027 revenue-growth estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (Neutral).\", \"About 80\\u201385% FY2027 revenue growth, or approximately $389\\u2013400 billion of revenue: Morningstar models 80%, while the roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate is about 85% above FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. Q2 guidance and the Q3 estimate support this annual outlook without treating one quarter's growth as a flat full-year run-rate.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street's 42% FY2027 growth estimate, which is rung 3. The evidence implies materially more: Morningstar models 80% growth, and the roughly $400 billion FY2027 estimate implies about 85% growth against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. Q2 guidance implies roughly 95% year-over-year growth for that quarter, while the cited Q3 estimate is 81%; these are strong checks on the annual estimate, not rates to extrapolate flat across the whole year. The implied 80\\u201385% clears rung 1's 60% boundary. The CPU dependency's roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 estimate is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not greater than, this total.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e1350\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2244\\\"]\"], [8917, \"2026-08-25\", 1, \"The ladder cites Wall Street\\u2019s 42% FY2027 estimate, which is rung 3. The more current explicit market reference is LSEG\\u2019s 83% estimate ($396 billion), which is rung 1.\", \"About 83% FY2027 revenue growth: the LSEG estimate of $396 billion against reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion implies approximately 83.4%. Morningstar\\u2019s 80% model and the strong quarterly guide/estimates are consistent checks, not annualized quarterly growth rates.\", \"The ladder\\u2019s cited Wall Street estimate of 42% is rung 3, but the newer LSEG FY2027 estimate is 83% on $396 billion, which is rung 1 and is the more current explicit market reference. The evidence supports a similarly high outcome rather than merely repeating that reference: $396 billion divided by FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion implies about 83.4% growth; Morningstar independently models 80%. Q1 growth of 85% and Q2 guidance imply roughly 95% year-over-year growth for that quarter, while the cited Q3 estimate is 81%, so the quarter figures support strong growth without being extrapolated flat across the year. The implied annual growth clears the 60% rung-1 boundary. The roughly $19\\u201320 billion FY2027 CPU estimate in v16 is a component consistent with, and below, the projected total.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e966\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2142\\\", \\\"e2145\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2244\\\", \\\"e2419\\\", \\\"e2421\\\"]\"], [8922, \"2026-08-26\", 1, \"LSEG\\u2019s $396 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, equivalent to 83% growth, is the current quantified market reference and sits at rung 1. The older 42% Wall Street estimate sits at rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.5% growth versus FY2026\\u2019s $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2 plus $108.0 billion guided for Q3, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward Q3\\u2019s roughly 12.3% sequential growth.\", \"The reference is LSEG\\u2019s FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, or 83% growth, which is rung 1; the older 42% estimate is rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion, and Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion. Carrying the guided Q3 sequential growth of about 12.3% into Q4 implies roughly $121.3 billion for Q4, or about $407.1 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies approximately 88.5% growth, well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook in dependency v16 is a component of, and does not exceed, this total. The new Q2 result and Q3 guidance change the supporting figures and implied outcome, but not the rung.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2118\\\"]\"], [8926, \"2026-08-27\", 1, \"Latest stated market reference: LSEG estimates FY2027 revenue of $396 billion, about 83% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; that is rung 1. The older 42% Wall Street estimate is rung 3.\", \"Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion; adding guided Q3 revenue of $108.0 billion and carrying its approximately 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4 and $407.1 billion for FY2027. That is approximately 88.5% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\", \"The latest stated market reference is LSEG\\u2019s FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, equivalent to about 83% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, so the reference sits on rung 1. The older 42% Wall Street estimate sits on rung 3, but the more recent LSEG estimate is the relevant reference. For the forecast, NVIDIA reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, then guided Q3 to $108.0 billion. Carrying the Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3% into Q4 implies roughly $121.3 billion for Q4 and about $407.1 billion for FY2027 overall. Against FY2026 revenue, that is approximately 88.5% growth\\u2014well above rung 1\\u2019s 60% threshold. The $25 billion CPU-revenue forecast in dependency v16 is a component of total revenue and does not exceed this total; it is not added on top. The export-control and AI-capex themes shown generally support the higher-revenue direction, but do not change the run-rate calculation or rung.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2989\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2975\\\"]\"], [8930, \"2026-08-28\", 1, \"LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion implies about 83% growth, placing the reference at rung 1.\", \"Approximately $400 billion of FY2027 revenue, or about 85% growth over FY2026. This uses reported Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance, then estimates roughly $114 billion for Q4\\u2014slower sequential growth than the Q2-to-Q3 guided pace, partly reflecting the reported 70% full-year outlook as a downside counterweight.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies about 83% growth and sits at rung 1. The evidence implies roughly $400 billion, or about 85% growth: Q1 and Q2 revenue totaled about $177.8 billion, Q3 guidance brings the first three quarters to about $285.8 billion, and roughly $114 billion in Q4 would take the year to about $400 billion. That Q4 estimate allows for sequential growth to slow from the Q2-to-Q3 guided pace. The reported 70% full-year growth outlook is a meaningful downward counterweight, but at that full-year rate revenue would be only about $367 billion, requiring Q4 revenue of roughly $81 billion\\u2014well below Q3 guidance of $108 billion, with no evidence here of a corresponding reversal. The implied outcome remains above rung 1's 60% boundary. Dependency v16's roughly $25 billion CPU-revenue forecast is consistent with this total, but is a component and is not added again.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e2154\\\"]\"], [8934, \"2026-08-29\", 1, \"LSEG's FY2027 consensus estimate is $396 billion, or about 83% growth versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; this sits at rung 1.\", \"The reported 70% FY2027 growth outlook implies approximately $367 billion ($215.94 billion \\u00d7 1.70). Q1 and Q2 actuals plus Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion, so this outlook leaves roughly $81 billion for Q4.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, about 83% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, which is rung 1. The strongest new full-year input is the CFO's reported 70% FY2027 growth outlook: applying it to FY2026 revenue implies about $367 billion. That is lower than consensus, but remains above the rung-1 threshold of 60% (about $345.5 billion). Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion, leaving roughly $81 billion for Q4 at the 70% outlook; the annual outlook therefore moderates the prior roughly $400 billion forecast without moving it below the rung boundary. Higher third-party estimates of 80% or roughly $400 billion are supportive but do not outweigh the more direct annual outlook. The v16 dependency's roughly $25 billion CPU-revenue forecast is a component of, and is consistent with, this total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2154\\\"]\"], [8939, \"2026-08-30\", 1, \"LSEG expects $396 billion of FY2027 revenue, about 83% growth from FY2026's $215.94 billion; that market reference is rung 1.\", \"About 70% FY2027 growth, or approximately $367 billion, using the reported full-year growth outlook applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. Q1 actuals, Q2 actuals, and Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion, leaving roughly $81 billion for Q4 at that annual outcome.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, about 83% growth on FY2026 revenue, which is rung 1. I forecast about 70% growth, or roughly $367 billion: the reported 70% full-year outlook is the strongest direct annual input, while FY2027 Q1 and Q2 actuals plus Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion, leaving about $81 billion for Q4 at that annual outcome. That is a sharp slowdown from the Q3 guide, so the higher LSEG estimate and other roughly $400 billion projections are meaningful upside risks; however, they are estimates or conditional projections, not a quantified company revision to the annual outlook. The 70% forecast remains above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The v16 CPU forecast is a component of total revenue, not something to add on top of it, and is consistent with this total.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2989\\\"]\"], [8944, \"2026-08-31\", 1, \"LSEG analysts' $396 billion FY2027 estimate, about 83% growth from FY2026 revenue, sits in rung 1.\", \"About 83% growth, or roughly $395\\u2013396 billion: FY2027 Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 actual revenue of $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance of $108 billion total about $285.8 billion; a roughly $109\\u2013110 billion Q4 would bring the year to that range.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, about 83% growth on NVIDIA's reported $215.94 billion FY2026 revenue; that is rung 1. I forecast about 83% growth as well, but not simply because it is consensus: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue plus the Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion, and an FY2027 total near $395\\u2013396 billion requires roughly $109\\u2013110 billion in Q4, close to the Q3 guide and consistent with the reported growth trajectory. The reported 70% full-year outlook would imply only about $81 billion in Q4, a sharp sequential decline from the $108 billion Q3 guide, so I give it less weight against the subsequent quarter results and guidance. The implied growth is well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. v16's CPU forecast is part of total revenue, not additive to it, and is consistent with this whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e2989\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e3481\\\"]\"], [8948, \"2026-09-01\", 1, \"LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, about 83% growth on FY2026 revenue, is the market reference and sits at rung 1. The separate Zacks estimate of 85.4% is also in rung 1.\", \"About 70% FY2027 revenue growth: applying the CFO's full-year outlook to FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion implies about $367.1 billion in FY2027 revenue. After $81.6 billion in Q1, $96.2 billion in Q2, and the $108 billion Q3 guide, that annual outcome implies about $81.3 billion in Q4.\", \"The reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, or about 83% growth on NVIDIA's FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; that sits at rung 1. I forecast about 70% growth instead, principally because the supplied CFO outlook is 70% for the full fiscal year. That implies roughly $367.1 billion of FY2027 revenue. Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus the Q3 guide total about $285.8 billion, leaving roughly $81.3 billion for Q4 under that annual outlook\\u2014a sharp sequential decline from the Q3 guide, so it is a cautious forecast against the otherwise strong quarterly trajectory. Even at 70%, implied growth remains above rung 1's 60% boundary. The v16 CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not greater than, this whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e3683\\\"]\"], [8954, \"2026-09-03\", 1, \"LSEG expects $396 billion of FY2027 revenue, about 83% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; this is rung 1. Zacks' 85.4% growth estimate is likewise rung 1.\", \"Approximately $404 billion of FY2027 revenue, or about 87% growth: Q1 and Q2 actuals plus the $108 billion Q3 guide total $285.8 billion; carrying the estimated 81% Q3 year-over-year growth into Q4 implies about $118 billion in Q4.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, or about 83% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, which is rung 1. Zacks' 85.4% estimate is also rung 1. The operating evidence points to roughly 87% growth: Q1 and Q2 revenue totaled $177.8 billion, and the $108 billion Q3 guide brings the first three quarters to $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the analyst-estimated 81% Q3 year-over-year growth to Q4 implies about $118 billion of Q4 revenue and roughly $404 billion for FY2027, or about 87% growth. The reported 70% full-year outlook would imply about $367 billion and only $81 billion in Q4 after the first three quarters, a sharp drop from the Q3 guide; it is a meaningful counterweight, but the latest quarterly actuals and guidance make that lower outcome less likely. Both the reference and implied outcome remain above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The v16 CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is consistent with this whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2989\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3620\\\"]\"], [8958, \"2026-09-04\", 1, \"The latest stated market expectations are LSEG's 83% FY2027 revenue growth to $396 billion and Zacks' 85.4%; both correspond to rung 1. The earlier 42% estimate cited in e966 corresponds to rung 3, but the later quantified estimates place the current reference at rung 1.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88% growth versus FY2026's $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint totals $285.8 billion; carrying forward the 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion in Q4.\", \"The latest specific market reference is LSEG's 83% growth forecast to $396 billion, with Zacks at 85.4%; both sit at rung 1. The operating run-rate points somewhat higher: FY2026 revenue was $215.94 billion, and FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue were $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion. Adding the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint gives $285.8 billion for the first three quarters. Carrying forward the 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives roughly $121 billion for Q4, about $407 billion for FY2027, or 88% growth. The reported 70% full-year outlook is a meaningful counterweight, but it would imply only about $81 billion in Q4 after the first three quarters, a sharp drop from Q3 guidance. The implied growth remains well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA's CPU revenue forecast is a component of, and does not exceed, this total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e3108\\\"]\"], [8962, \"2026-09-05\", 1, \"LSEG analysts expect FY2027 revenue growth of 83% to $396 billion, which is rung 1; Zacks' 85.4% estimate is also rung 1. The ladder's 42% Wall Street estimate is an older, lower reference.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.5% growth versus FY2026's $215.94 billion. This carries forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by the $108 billion Q3 guidance to estimate about $121 billion for Q4; the separate 70% full-year outlook implies a lower $367 billion but still clears rung 1.\", \"The current market reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to $396 billion (rung 1); Zacks' 85.4% estimate is also rung 1. The operating run-rate implies about $407 billion: FY2027 Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 was $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance is $108 billion, and carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion for Q4. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is approximately 88.5% growth. NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth outlook is a meaningful lower counterweight, but it still implies growth above the 60% rung boundary; the current-quarter guidance and sequential run-rate do not imply a Q4 collapse sufficient to pull the forecast below that boundary. The CPU dependency's approximately $25 billion-plus FY2027 estimate is compatible with, and far below, the implied total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e3593\\\"]\"], [8966, \"2026-09-06\", 1, \"LSEG expects 83% FY2027 revenue growth to $396 billion (rung 1); Zacks' 85.4% estimate is also rung 1.\", \"Carry forward Q3 guidance's approximately 12% sequential growth into Q4: $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B + about $121B is about $406.8B for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94B, that implies approximately 88.4% growth.\", \"The reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to $396 billion, which is rung 1; Zacks' 85.4% estimate is also rung 1. The reported Q1 and Q2 results plus Q3 guidance imply a run-rate of about $407 billion for FY2027, or roughly 88% growth. NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year outlook is a meaningful counterweight, but at $367 billion it would require Q4 revenue of about $81 billion\\u2014roughly 25% below guided Q3 revenue\\u2014despite Q3 guidance implying about 12% sequential growth. The run-rate therefore remains the more likely outcome and comfortably exceeds the 60% rung-1 boundary. The CPU dependency's forecast of just over $25 billion is consistent with, and remains below, the total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e3593\\\"]\"], [8971, \"2026-09-07\", 1, \"LSEG's estimate of 83% FY2027 revenue growth to $396 billion is the principal market reference and sits at rung 1. The cited Morningstar 80% and Zacks 85.4% estimates are also at rung 1.\", \"FY2027 Q1\\u2013Q3 reported revenue totals $285.8 billion; carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion of Q4 revenue and approximately $407 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies roughly 88% year-over-year growth.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to $396 billion, which sits at rung 1; the other cited estimates of 80% and 85.4% also sit there. The direct quarterly run-rate implies about $407 billion: FY2027 Q1\\u2013Q3 revenue totals $285.8 billion, and carrying Q3's roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121 billion for Q4, or about 88% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion. NVIDIA's 70% full-year projection is a meaningful lower-growth counterweight, but would imply about $367 billion total and only about $81 billion in Q4 after the reported first three quarters\\u2014well below Q3 guidance of $108 billion. The quarterly guidance and its carried-forward run-rate therefore remain the stronger forecast input and stay above the 60% rung boundary. The v16 CPU forecast of roughly $25 billion or somewhat more is consistent with this total-revenue forecast and remains only a component of it. The updated hyperscaler capex theme supports demand, while the other in-force export-control themes generally reduce downside policy risk; neither changes the run-rate calculation.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1390\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2154\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2975\\\", \\\"e2989\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3481\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3620\\\", \\\"e3683\\\", \\\"e3883\\\", \\\"e3942\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4049\\\", \\\"e4164\\\", \\\"e4165\\\"]\"], [8976, \"2026-09-08\", 1, \"LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to about $396 billion is the market reference and sits at rung 1. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth projection also sits at rung 1.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88% growth from FY2026's $215.94 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to Q3 guidance of $108 billion, then carry the implied 12% sequential growth into Q4 for about $121 billion.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to about $396 billion, which is rung 1; NVIDIA's reported 70% full-year growth projection is also above the rung-1 threshold. For the forecast, Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 was $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance is $108 billion. Carrying the disclosed 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion, or roughly $407 billion for FY2027 and 88% growth on FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion. That run-rate is above the 60% rung boundary and supports rung 1. The 70% company projection is a meaningful lower counterweight, but it too remains within rung 1.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4226\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e3620\\\"]\"], [8980, \"2026-09-09\", 1, \"LSEG expects 83% FY2027 revenue growth to about $396 billion; that expectation sits at rung 1 (at least 60%).\", \"Carrying the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion and FY2027 revenue near $407 billion. Relative to FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies roughly 88% growth.\", \"The market reference is LSEG\\u2019s 83% FY2027 growth estimate to $396 billion, which is rung 1. The reported and guided quarterly path implies about $407 billion for FY2027: $81.6 billion in Q1, $96.2 billion in Q2, $108 billion guided for Q3, and about $121 billion in Q4 if Q3\\u2019s approximately 12% sequential growth carries forward. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is roughly 88% growth, well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s reported 70% full-year growth projection is a lower counterweight, but it also implies rung 1; reaching it after the first three quarters would require a marked Q4 slowdown. The memory-shortage comment is a risk but gives no quantified NVIDIA constraint. The CPU dependency\\u2019s forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of this total.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2118\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4274\\\"]\"], [8984, \"2026-09-10\", 1, \"LSEG's FY2027 estimate is 83% growth to $396 billion, placing the reference in rung 1.\", \"Approximately $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or about 88% growth: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint, with Q4 estimated near $121 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\", \"The reference is LSEG's 83% FY2027 growth estimate to $396 billion, which sits in rung 1. The reported and guided quarterly path implies about $407 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 was $96.2 billion, Q3 is guided at $108 billion, and carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is approximately 88% growth, comfortably above the 60% boundary. Management's 70% full-year growth outlook is a lower counterweight, implying roughly $367 billion; after the first three quarters, that would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion. The nearer-term quarterly guidance and reported run-rate make the higher outcome more likely. The CPU dependency is a component of total revenue, so its forecast is consistent with this total but is not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4274\\\", \\\"e3593\\\"]\"], [8988, \"2026-09-11\", 1, \"Latest cited analyst expectation: $411 billion of FY2027 revenue (e4348), about 90% growth versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, placing the reference at rung 1. The earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion / 83% also sits at rung 1; the ladder's cited 42% reference would be rung 3 but does not reflect these later market estimates.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88% growth: add reported Q1 and Q2 revenue ($81.6 billion and $96.2 billion), Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and a Q4 estimate of about $121 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance. This is well above the 60% rung-1 boundary.\", \"The market reference is the latest cited analyst estimate of $411 billion for FY2027; against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies about 90% growth and sits at rung 1. The older ladder reference of 42% would sit at rung 3, but it is superseded as a description of current market expectations by the later analyst estimates. The quarterly run-rate points to about $407 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue were $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance is $108 billion, and carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion. That is approximately 88% growth, well above the 60% rung boundary. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook is a meaningful lower counterweight, but applied to FY2026 revenue it implies about $367 billion and only about $81 billion in Q4 after the first three quarters\\u2014far below Q3 guidance. The latest quarterly path therefore makes the higher outcome more likely. The CPU dependency's forecast of more than $25 billion is a component of, and consistent with, the roughly $407 billion total; it cannot be added on top of total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4400\\\"]\"], [8992, \"2026-09-12\", 1, \"Latest analyst consensus is $411 billion of FY2027 revenue [e4348], about 90% growth from FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion [e1883]; this is rung 1. The ladder\\u2019s cited 42% estimate is rung 3 but is superseded as the current market reference.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88% growth: add Q1 $81.6 billion, Q2 $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and an approximately $121 billion Q4 obtained by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance. This is rung 1.\", \"The current market reference is the analyst estimate of $411 billion for FY2027 [e4348]. Against reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion [e1883], that implies about 90% growth and sits at rung 1; the ladder\\u2019s cited 42% estimate would be rung 3, but is not the latest market reference. The quarterly run-rate independently points to roughly $407 billion: FY2027 Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 was $96.2 billion, and Q3 guidance is $108 billion; carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion [e2146, e2497, e2499, e3943]. That is about 88% growth, comfortably above the 60% rung-1 boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s 70% full-year growth outlook is a lower counterweight, but itself implies roughly $367 billion and still clears that boundary [e4400]. The CPU dependency\\u2019s forecast is a component of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to the total [v16].\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4400\\\"]\"], [8997, \"2026-09-13\", 1, \"Latest cited analyst consensus is $411 billion of FY2027 revenue [e4348]; against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion [e1883], that implies about 90% growth and sits at rung 1. The ladder\\u2019s stated 42% estimate would sit at rung 3, but the more recent $411 billion estimate is the relevant market reference.\", \"Q1 and Q2 revenue were $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion; Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion. Carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives Q4 of about $121 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $407 billion, roughly 88% growth from FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\", \"The market reference is the latest cited analyst estimate of $411 billion for FY2027, or about 90% growth from FY2026 revenue; that sits at rung 1. The quarterly run-rate implies about $407 billion, also well above rung 1\\u2019s 60% threshold. NVIDIA\\u2019s stated 70% full-year growth outlook is a lower counterweight, but still implies growth within rung 1; the slower sequential growth embedded in Q3 guidance and the reported memory constraint do not pull the forecast below the rung boundary. The CPU dependency is a small component of total revenue and does not conflict with this whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4599\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e4274\\\", \\\"e4389\\\"]\"], [9001, \"2026-09-14\", 1, \"Latest cited market reference: Zacks consensus growth of 87.4% for FY2027, about $405 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; this is rung 1. Other analyst references include LSEG\\u2019s 83% and $396 billion and an estimate of $411 billion, also rung 1. NVIDIA\\u2019s stated 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion and is likewise rung 1.\", \"About $405\\u2013407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 87\\u201389% growth: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 actual revenue of $96.2 billion plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion totals $285.8 billion; repeating the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion. The company\\u2019s 70% full-year outlook is a lower counterweight, but still implies growth above the 60% rung-1 threshold.\", \"The market reference is the latest cited Zacks FY2027 revenue-growth estimate of 87.4%, about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; it sits at rung 1. Other cited analyst estimates also sit at rung 1. The company\\u2019s reported 70% full-year growth outlook implies about $367 billion, which is below the market estimate but still above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. Independently, Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus the Q3 midpoint guidance total about $285.8 billion; carrying the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121 billion for Q4 and roughly $407 billion for FY2027, or about 88% growth. That central run-rate estimate remains well above the roughly $345.5 billion needed for 60% growth, so the deceleration in sequential growth does not cross a rung boundary. The CPU dependency is consistent with this whole-company forecast: its a little-over-$25 billion estimate is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add again.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e4348\\\"]\"], [9005, \"2026-09-15\", 1, \"Zacks' FY2027 consensus growth estimate is 87.4%, about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion; it sits in rung 1. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook also sits in rung 1.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or roughly 88% growth: add reported Q1 and Q2 revenue ($81.6 billion and $96.2 billion), Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and an estimated Q4 of about $121 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance; compare with FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\", \"The market reference is Zacks' 87.4% FY2027 growth estimate, which sits in rung 1; the reported 70% full-year growth outlook also sits in rung 1. The implied run-rate is about $407 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, followed by an estimated Q4 of about $121 billion if the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance carries forward. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is about 88% growth, still comfortably above rung 1's 60% boundary. The 60% FY2026\\u2013FY2029 analyst CAGR is a longer-horizon view, not a direct FY2027 estimate, and does not outweigh the current-quarter run-rate. The v16 CPU forecast of $25\\u201326 billion is a component of, and well below, the implied total; it is not added again.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4735\\\"]\"], [9009, \"2026-09-16\", 1, \"Market consensus is about 87\\u201388% FY2027 revenue growth (rung 1); the cited $411 billion estimate implies about 90% and is also rung 1. NVIDIA's stated 70% full-year growth outlook is rung 1 as well.\", \"Q1 and Q2 reported revenue plus Q3 guidance total about $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion in Q4, or approximately $407 billion for FY2027; versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, implied growth is about 88%.\", \"The market reference is roughly 87\\u201388% FY2027 growth (rung 1); the $411 billion analyst estimate also implies about 90% against FY2026 revenue. NVIDIA's reported FY2026 growth and its stated 70% full-year outlook are likewise in rung 1. For the forecast, Q1 and Q2 revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, total $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion in Q4 and approximately $407 billion for FY2027; against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that is about 88% growth, well above rung 1's 60% boundary. The 70% full-year outlook is below this quarterly run-rate implication but still in rung 1; a 70% outcome would leave only about $81 billion for Q4 after Q1\\u2013Q3, inconsistent with the guided run-rate. The v16 total-CPU forecast is only a component and is not added again; it remains well below the implied total. Demand concerns and supply constraints temper the outlook but do not move the implied result across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4274\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4822\\\"]\"], [9013, \"2026-09-17\", 1, \"Current supplied Wall Street/Zacks expectations are about 87\\u201388% FY2027 revenue growth (including 87.4% and 88%), which is rung 1. The ladder\\u2019s cited 42% benchmark would sit at rung 3, but the newer estimates in view are the relevant market reference.\", \"Q1 and Q2 revenue plus midpoint Q3 guidance totals $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion in Q4 and approximately $407 billion for FY2027, or about 88.5% growth over FY2026\\u2019s $215.94 billion.\", \"The current market reference is roughly 87\\u201388% FY2027 revenue growth, which is rung 1; the ladder\\u2019s stated 42% benchmark would be rung 3, but the supplied newer consensus estimates are much higher. Using reported Q1 and Q2 revenue and the midpoint of Q3 guidance gives $285.8 billion through Q3. Carrying forward the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives about $121 billion in Q4, or roughly $407 billion for FY2027 versus FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion: approximately 88.5% growth, clearly above rung 1\\u2019s 60% boundary. The CFO\\u2019s 70% full-year growth outlook also remains in rung 1, though it is below this quarterly run-rate implication; a 70% full-year outcome would require Q4 revenue of only about $81 billion after Q3 guidance of $108 billion. The Q4 analyst estimate of 65% growth is a moderating signal, but its comparison basis is unspecified and it does not imply FY2027 growth below 60%. NVIDIA\\u2019s forecast CPU revenue is a component of, and far below, the implied total-revenue figure.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4822\\\", \\\"e4863\\\"]\"], [9017, \"2026-09-18\", 1, \"Current analyst expectations are approximately 87\\u201388% FY2027 revenue growth (with estimates including 83% and about 90%); this reference is rung 1. The ladder's cited 42% Wall Street estimate is rung 3, but is not the current market reference.\", \"About $407 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.4% growth versus FY2026's $215.94 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual plus $96.2 billion Q2 actual plus the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint, then about $121 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\", \"The market reference is current analyst expectations of roughly 87\\u201388% FY2027 growth, which sits at rung 1; the ladder's cited 42% estimate would sit at rung 3, but it is not the current reference. Q1 and Q2 revenue plus the Q3 guidance midpoint total about $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the roughly 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $407 billion, or 88.4% growth over FY2026. The CFO's approximately 70% full-year outlook is lower but still rung 1; the Q3-guidance run-rate implies a higher figure. The reported possibility of more than $100 billion of unfulfilled AI-infrastructure demand tempers confidence, but covers broader gear and does not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA revenue. The implied outcome remains well above the 60% rung-1 boundary, so there is no rung crossing. The CPU-revenue dependency and the in-force AI-capex and export-policy themes are directionally supportive, but do not justify exceeding the total-revenue run-rate implied by the supplied quarterly figures.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4822\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4900\\\", \\\"e4901\\\", \\\"e3593\\\"]\"], [9022, \"2026-09-19\", 1, \"Current market expectations are roughly 87.4%\\u201388% FY2027 growth, rung 1; the separate $411 billion analyst estimate implies about 90.3%, also rung 1. The ladder's cited 42% estimate is rung 3 but is not the current reference.\", \"Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion. Carrying the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.0 billion of Q4 revenue and about $406.8 billion for FY2027, or 88.4% growth over FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion.\", \"The current market reference is roughly 87.4%\\u201388% growth (Zacks and analyst estimates), with one analyst estimate of $411 billion implying about 90.3%; these are all rung 1. The ladder's cited 42% estimate would be rung 3, but it is not the current reference. Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion; carrying Q3's 12% sequential growth forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.0 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $406.8 billion, or 88.4% growth over FY2026. NVIDIA's approximately 70% full-year outlook is a lower input, but it also remains above the rung-1 threshold; the current-quarter guidance-based run-rate supports the higher estimate. Supplier constraints and the estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled AI-infrastructure demand temper confidence but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA revenue. The CPU dependency's roughly $25 billion forecast is a component of, and does not exceed, the implied total.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4400\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4822\\\", \\\"e4901\\\", \\\"e4905\\\"]\"], [9026, \"2026-09-20\", 1, \"Current analyst estimates range from 87.4%\\u201388% growth to $411 billion of FY2027 revenue, or about 90.3% growth versus FY2026; these references sit at rung 1. NVIDIA's stated approximately 70% full-year growth outlook also sits at rung 1. The ladder's cited 42% estimate is not the current market reference.\", \"About $406.8 billion of FY2027 revenue, or approximately 88.4% growth over FY2026's $215.94 billion: Q1 and Q2 actuals plus Q3's $108 billion midpoint total $285.8 billion, and carrying Q3's roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121 billion.\", \"The current market reference is about 87.4%\\u201390.3% growth, implying rung 1; NVIDIA's approximately 70% full-year outlook is also rung 1, though it is the lower reference. Using reported FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, Q1 and Q2 FY2027 revenue, and Q3 guidance of $108 billion, carrying Q3's approximately 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies Q4 revenue of about $121 billion and FY2027 revenue of about $406.8 billion, or approximately 88.4% growth. That remains well above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The CFO's 70% outlook and the supply-constraint evidence temper the higher consensus/run-rate estimate, but do not imply a fall below 60%; 70% growth would imply about $367 billion for the year and a Q4 materially below Q3 guidance.\", \"[\\\"v16\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e1883\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4348\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4822\\\", \\\"e3108\\\", \\\"e4900\\\", \\\"e4901\\\", \\\"e4905\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e4274\\\"]\"]], [\"How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 60.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 50.0, 60.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 35.0, 50.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 20.0, 35.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 20.0]]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nv=\\\"v14\\\"\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(base/\\\"linked-1-copy\\\"/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nrows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>8737 order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,)).fetchall()\nprint(json.dumps([rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"[[[8743, \"2026-07-10\", 3, \"Bloomberg-compiled expectations project $393 billion of FY2027 sales; that market reference is in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $393 billion in FY2027, using Bloomberg's specific sales projection (up 82%) as the forecast input. The dependency's approximately $20 billion CPU-sales figure is part of the total and does not raise it above the whole-revenue projection.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The same quantified projection is the available forecast input; the bullish themes support demand directionally but do not quantify an adjustment that would carry the implied result across the $400 billion boundary. The dependency's approximately $20 billion CPU-sales forecast is a component of total revenue and is compatible with, not additive to, the $393 billion total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\"]\"], [8745, \"2026-07-11\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 sales projection of $393 billion; this is in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $393 billion for FY2027: Bloomberg\\u2019s quantified projection is the available revenue forecast, and the new observation that AI laboratories account for roughly 20% of demand has no period or revenue figure with which to adjust it. The dependency\\u2019s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is included within total revenue, not added to it.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The new demand-mix observation is directionally bullish but gives no period or revenue amount, so it does not support a quantified increase across the $400 billion boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is part of total revenue and is not added to Bloomberg\\u2019s total; the implied total remains about $393 billion, consistent with that dependency.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e213\\\"]\"], [8746, \"2026-07-12\", 3, \"Bloomberg projects $393 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 sales, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The separate consensus description, \\u201cwell over $300 billion,\\u201d is not specific enough to assign a narrower rung.\", \"About $387 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, an implied sequential increase of about 11.5%; carrying that pace forward for the remaining two quarters gives approximately $81.6B + $91B + $101.5B + $113.2B.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 sales projection, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion); the reported description of Wall Street consensus as \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d is too broad to place more precisely. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided sequential pace through the remaining quarters gives an illustrative FY2027 total of about $387 billion. That is below Bloomberg\\u2019s estimate but remains within rung 3. Competition from custom chips and China-related demand risks, alongside reported memory constraints, temper the outlook but do not quantify a reduction that moves the forecast below $350 billion; customer and partnership announcements support demand but do not size additional FY2027 revenue. The CPU-sales dependency is part of total revenue and should not be added separately.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e213\\\", \\\"e219\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e229\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e257\\\"]\"], [8750, \"2026-07-13\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the most specific market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The broader \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d Wall Street expectation does not identify a more precise rung.\", \"Approximately $387 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided pace forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, for about $387 billion total.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3; the separate description of Wall Street expectations as \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d is too broad to place more precisely. The guided run-rate implies about $387 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the implied 11.5% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively. Added hyperscaler-spending support and NVIDIA demand announcements are offset by memory constraints, custom-chip competition, and China-related risks, but none quantifies a change large enough to move the estimate across the $350 billion or $400 billion boundary. The dependency forecast of $18\\u201319 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to this total estimate.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e213\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e284\\\"]\"], [8752, \"2026-07-14\", 3, \"Bloomberg projects $393 billion of FY2027 sales, placing the market reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Wall Street's separate \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d expectation is less specific.\", \"About $387 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 and $91 billion guided for Q2 imply roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth forward gives approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, totaling about $387 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3; the separate description of Wall Street expectations as \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d is too broad to place more precisely. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that guided growth forward gives roughly $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. That is below the $400 billion boundary and remains in rung 3. The disclosed AWS deployment plan and hyperscaler spending outlook support demand, while the HBM ramp slowdown and tighter China-related controls weigh the other way; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue adjustment sufficient to cross a rung boundary. NVIDIA's $18\\u201319 billion stand-alone CPU sales forecast is included within total revenue, not added on top, and is consistent with the whole-revenue estimate.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e372\\\"]\"], [8756, \"2026-07-15\", 2, \"Bloomberg projects $393 billion of FY2027 sales, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). Wall Street's reported \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d expectation is too imprecise to place more narrowly.\", \"Carrying the sequential growth from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion to Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward implies about $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or roughly $387 billion for FY2027. Adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast gives about $407 billion.\", \"Bloomberg's $393 billion projection is the clearest market reference and sits in rung 3; the separate \\u201cwell over $300 billion\\u201d expectation is too broad to locate more precisely. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply a roughly $387 billion FY2027 run-rate if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4, below the $400 billion boundary. Adding the dependency's roughly $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast, which is distinct from the existing revenue run-rate, gives about $407 billion and crosses into rung 2. This remains a forecast rather than an established result: the AWS deployment plan and $119 billion in supply-related commitments are not specified as FY2027 revenue, while HBM ramp constraints and custom-chip alternatives are downside risks.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e376\\\"]\"], [8759, \"2026-07-16\", 2, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the clearest quantified market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, with Q2's sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, implies roughly $387 billion; adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast gives about $407 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for the year if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast, treated as incremental to that run-rate, gives about $407 billion and crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. Booked supply commitments and the updated AI-capex view support the upside, while memory-supply risk and custom-chip alternatives temper confidence; neither supplies a quantified adjustment that takes the implied estimate below $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e220\\\"]\"], [8763, \"2026-07-17\", 2, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies about $387 billion for FY2027 if Q2's sequential growth rate carries through Q3 and Q4. Adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate as incremental implies about $406 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for the year if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU estimate as incremental gives about $406 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and Huang's comments about orders support demand, but do not translate directly into additional FY2027 revenue. Memory-supply constraints and China-related risks temper the outlook, but are not quantified enough to pull the estimate below $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e234\\\"]\"], [8766, \"2026-07-18\", 2, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Applying the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate (about 11.5%) to Q2's $91 billion guidance for Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $387 billion for FY2027. Adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives an implied total of about $406 billion, in rung 2.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for FY2027 if the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $406 billion, above rung 2's $400 billion boundary. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and management's order comments support demand but are not added directly to revenue. New evidence of HBM supply pressure and China-related restrictions is a downside risk, while the UAE export change is a partial offset; none supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue adjustment large enough to change the estimate across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [8770, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion is the market reference; it sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Using Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the 11.5% sequential increase carried forward gives approximately $101.5 billion for Q3 and $113.2 billion for Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. The approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate is included within, not added to, total company revenue.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion for FY2027, still in rung 3 and below the $400 billion boundary. The approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to this company-wide run-rate. Strong orders and the UAE export easing support the outlook, while the stated China exclusion and HBM supply concerns weigh against it; none provides a quantified FY2027 adjustment that moves the run-rate across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e376\\\"]\"], [8773, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue projection of $393 billion (also reflected in Wall Street's average estimates) is the market reference and sits in rung 3.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion followed by Q2 guidance of $91 billion implies 11.5% sequential growth. Carrying that growth into Q3 and Q4 yields approximately $387 billion for FY2027.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3. Using Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion gives 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that growth through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion for FY2027. That is below the reference but remains above rung 3's $350 billion floor and below rung 2's $400 billion boundary. Strong orders and AWS's stated 2027\\u20132028 deployment plan support the outlook, but neither quantifies additional FY2027 revenue; China exclusions and memory-supply constraints weigh the other way. The current v8 forecast of $8\\u20139 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to the run-rate, and is consistent with total revenue remaining above it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e220\\\", \\\"e221\\\", \\\"e222\\\", \\\"e648\\\"]\"], [8776, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the primary market reference and sits in rung 3. Zacks' estimate of 80% year-over-year growth, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"Approximately $387 billion for FY2027: Q1 revenue was $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, an 11.5% sequential increase; extending that sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives about $101.5 billion and $113.2 billion, respectively, for roughly $387.3 billion total.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 3; Zacks' 80% growth estimate, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, also implies about $389 billion in rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate into Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $387 billion for FY2027. That is about $13 billion below rung 2's $400 billion boundary, while remaining above rung 3's $350 billion floor. Strong orders, AWS's planned GPU deployment, and Wistron's mass production support the outlook, but the disclosed deployment spans two fiscal years and production plans spanning several years are not FY2027 revenue in hand. China exclusions and memory constraints weigh against a higher forecast. The dependency forecast of roughly $17 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not additive to, the approximately $387 billion total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e698\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e252\\\"]\"], [8780, \"2026-07-23\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350B\\u2013<$400B). Zacks' 80% growth estimate, applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, implies about $388.6 billion and is also in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $387.2 billion: Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615B followed by Q2 guidance of $91.0B implies about 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that rate into Q3 and Q4 gives roughly $101.5B and $113.1B, respectively.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the separate 80% growth estimate implies about $389 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, also rung 3. Independently, carrying the Q1-to-Q2 guided sequential growth rate forward gives approximately $81.6B + $91.0B + $101.5B + $113.1B = $387.2B for FY2027. That is about $12.8B below rung 2's $400B boundary and above rung 3's $350B floor. Mass production and the stated multi-year GPU and processor opportunities support upside, but do not allocate enough volume or revenue specifically to FY2027 to take the forecast across $400B; China exclusions and memory supply concerns weigh the other way. The v8 forecast of roughly $17B in stand-alone CPU sales is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of the total run-rate. The themes provide demand or policy tailwinds, but do not quantify an FY2027 revenue revision sufficient to change the rung.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e252\\\"]\"], [8783, \"2026-07-24\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The reported 82% consensus growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion also implies approximately $393 billion, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $385.7 billion: start with reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion and guided Q2 revenue of $91.0 billion, then carry the stated 11% sequential growth rate forward to Q3 ($101.0 billion) and Q4 ($112.1 billion).\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the 82% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $393 billion as well. The stronger near-term forecast input is NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 guidance of $91 billion, described as up 11% sequentially. Carrying that guided sequential growth forward gives approximately $81.615B + $91.0B + $101.0B + $112.1B = $385.7B for FY2027. Strong demand supports the run-rate, but the customer commitments span multiple periods and do not establish FY2027 revenue sufficient to cross $400 billion; memory constraints and China-related limits weigh against that move. The v8 CPU forecast of about $17 billion is part of total revenue, not an amount to add to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e882\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\"]\"], [8786, \"2026-07-25\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $385.7 billion: use reported FY2027 Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry the guided 11% sequential growth forward to approximately $101.0 billion in Q3 and $112.1 billion in Q4.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the reported 82% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion is consistent with that figure. The current-quarter $91 billion guidance, described as up 11% sequentially, implies roughly $81.615B + $91.0B + $101.0B + $112.1B = $385.7 billion if that sequential growth carries forward. New demand and production evidence leans positive, but the $1 trillion processor-sales potential spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and has no FY2027 revenue breakdown; the announced deployments and supply arrangements likewise do not quantify FY2027 revenue. The implied figure remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. The v8 CPU forecast of about $17 billion is included within total revenue, not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e284\\\"]\"], [8791, \"2026-07-26\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate (also consistent with approximately 82% growth from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion) is the market reference and sits in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"About $385.7 billion: $81.615 billion reported for Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried forward to approximately $101.0 billion in Q3 and $112.1 billion in Q4.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the separate 80%\\u201382% growth estimates also imply roughly $388\\u2013393 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate gives a lower point estimate: $81.615 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, then carrying the guided 11% sequential growth forward for Q3 and Q4 implies about $385.7 billion for FY2027. Strong orders and announced demand support the upside, while the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin figure spans multiple products and calendar years, and the memory and China evidence adds execution risk; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 uplift sufficient to take the forecast to $400 billion. The v8 estimate of $18\\u201319 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e949\\\"]\"], [8795, \"2026-07-27\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 estimate (with reported market growth estimates of 80%\\u201382%) is the reference and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $385.7 billion for FY2027: $81.615 billion reported in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, followed by Q3 and Q4 estimates of about $101.0 billion and $112.1 billion by carrying the 11% sequential growth in the Q2 outlook forward.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the reported 80%\\u201382% growth estimates also imply roughly $389\\u2013$393 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $385.7 billion: $81.615 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, then applying the guided 11% sequential growth to Q3 and Q4. Stronger AI spending, orders and supply partnerships tilt upward, but the $1 trillion product-sales forecast spans products and calendar years and supplies no FY2027 total-revenue allocation; HBM constraints and the Q2 exclusion of China compute sales are counterweights. The v8 CPU forecast of about $19 billion is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add again, and is consistent with this total. The implied figure remains below the $400 billion rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e905\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e1068\\\"]\"], [8799, \"2026-07-28\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 revenue estimate of $393 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The 80% growth consensus also implies approximately $388.6 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion.\", \"About $385.7 billion: $81.615 billion reported in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, followed by Q3 and Q4 at 11% sequential growth ($101.0 billion and $112.1 billion). This is below rung 3\\u2019s $400 billion upper boundary and above its $350 billion lower boundary.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3; the 80% growth consensus in e751 applied to FY2026 revenue also implies about $388.6 billion, in rung 3. The reported Q1 figure and Q2 guidance, carried forward at the guided 11% sequential growth rate, imply about $385.7 billion\\u2014still between the $350 billion and $400 billion rung boundaries. Demand commitments and the multi-year product-sales outlook support the upside, but they do not establish FY2027 total revenue at $400 billion; China restrictions and the specific HBM supply constraint are counterweights. The v8 CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not additive to, this estimate.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e906\\\"]\"], [8803, \"2026-07-29\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 estimate of $393 billion and the cited 80%\\u201382% growth expectations imply roughly $389\\u2013$393 billion; that reference is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $386 billion: take reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, then carry the roughly 11% sequential growth in the guidance forward to about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4. The resulting FY2027 total remains within rung 3.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; the 80%\\u201382% growth estimates against FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion imply roughly $389\\u2013$393 billion, also rung 3. For the forecast, carrying Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward at roughly its 11% sequential growth rate gives about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that implies approximately $386 billion for FY2027. The updated China-authorization theme, AWS deployment plans, and demand outlook provide upside, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin forecast spans products and periods, and the $500 billion SK initiative is not an FY2027 revenue figure. The HBM ramp slowdown and China restrictions are counterweights. The dependency\\u2019s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is consistent with this estimate, not additive to it. The implied outcome remains below the $400 billion boundary, so the forecast stays in rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1263\\\"]\"], [8807, \"2026-07-30\", 3, \"Market expectations are about $393 billion in FY2027 (Bloomberg data; approximately 82% growth on FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion), which is rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"Approximately $386 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, plus $91 billion guided for Q2, then about $101 billion and $112 billion in Q3 and Q4 if Q2\\u2019s roughly 11% sequential growth carries forward.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. Carrying the Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward at its guided roughly 11% sequential growth rate implies about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that gives approximately $386 billion for the year. Strong demand indicators, price increases, and improved supply access add upside, but the cited deals and product-sales targets are not sized as FY2027 NVIDIA revenue, while memory constraints and China restrictions are counterweights. The implied figure remains below $400 billion, so it does not cross into rung 2; it is also well above rung 4\\u2019s $350 billion boundary. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU-sales forecast in v8 is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add to the total, and is consistent with this forecast.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e1318\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e975\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\"]\"], [8811, \"2026-07-31\", 3, \"FY2027 consensus is about $393 billion (including Bloomberg's projection), which sits in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"The reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, carried forward at roughly 11% sequential growth, imply about $386 billion for FY2027 (Q3 about $101 billion; Q4 about $112 billion). Order visibility and pricing support upside toward roughly $393 billion, but the evidence does not imply crossing $400 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. Carrying the $91 billion Q2 guide forward at its roughly 11% sequential growth rate gives about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that implies about $386 billion for FY2027. The roughly $1 trillion of confirmed Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders through 2027, supply commitments, and reported price increases support some upside toward the reference, but those figures span products and periods and do not establish at least $400 billion of FY2027 revenue. China remains a counterweight because Q1 shipments and Q2 guidance exclude China Data Center compute revenue. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of a total that already includes it; it is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e1318\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e234\\\"]\"], [8815, \"2026-08-01\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectations of about $393 billion (with other consensus evidence indicating roughly 80\\u201382% growth) sit in rung 3.\", \"About $386 billion: carry the $91 billion Q2 guide forward at its roughly 11% sequential growth rate to estimate about $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4, then add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion. The demand evidence supports upside within the rung but does not quantify a sufficient FY2027 increment to imply $400 billion or more.\", \"The Bloomberg/Wall Street reference of $393 billion sits in rung 3. The $91 billion Q2 guide, up 11% sequentially, implies roughly $101 billion in Q3 and $112 billion in Q4 if that growth rate carries forward; with reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, that gives about $386 billion for FY2027. Demand evidence supports the upper part of rung 3, but the roughly $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin order figure spans products and periods, and the deployment and customer-spending figures do not establish enough FY2027 NVIDIA revenue to cross $400 billion. China compute exclusions and memory-supply risks temper the upside. The v8 estimate of about $18 billion in stand-alone CPU revenue is consistent as a component of this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1380\\\"]\"], [8820, \"2026-08-03\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027; that reference is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Carrying the Q2 guide of $91 billion, about 11.5% above Q1's $81.6 billion, forward for the remaining quarters gives approximately $101.5 billion in Q3 and $113.2 billion in Q4, or about $387 billion for FY2027. Robust booked demand supports a modest lift, but after discounting orders that span products and periods, the central estimate is roughly $390 billion.\", \"The Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue reference of about $393 billion is in rung 3. The latest reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, with the Q2 sequential growth rate carried through Q3 and Q4, imply about $387 billion; strong demand evidence supports a modest lift toward roughly $390 billion, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin orders span products and periods and do not justify assigning that amount to FY2027. China restrictions and memory-supply risks temper the upside, so the forecast remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU estimate in v8 is part of NVIDIA\\u2019s total, not an amount to add again.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1309\\\", \\\"e1310\\\", \\\"e1311\\\", \\\"e1312\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e1380\\\"]\"], [8824, \"2026-08-04\", 3, \"Bloomberg and Wall Street estimates are about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference at rung 3.\", \"About $390 billion for FY2027: the reported $81.6 billion Q1 and $91 billion Q2 guide, with Q2's 11% sequential growth carried forward through Q3 and Q4, imply about $386 billion before a modest demand-and-order uplift.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion, which is rung 3. Carrying Q2's guided 11% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 gives a base of about $386 billion ($81.6B + $91B + $101B + $112B); strong booked supply commitments and order visibility support a modest lift to roughly $390 billion. That remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin orders span products and periods, so they cannot be assigned in full to FY2027. Memory-supply and China-market risks temper the upside. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1376\\\"]\"], [8828, \"2026-08-05\", 3, \"Bloomberg and Wall Street estimates put FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (with estimates of 80\\u201382% growth), which is rung 3.\", \"Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Strong cloud demand and spending support a modest uplift to roughly $393 billion, partly offset by supply constraints and China exposure.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. Independently, Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the 11% sequential growth through the remaining quarters, imply about $386 billion ($81.6B + $91B + $101B + $112B). Strong cloud growth and spending support a modest uplift from that run-rate to roughly $393 billion; memory-supply constraints and China exposure limit the uplift. This remains below the $400 billion boundary. The roughly $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and periods, so it is not counted as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is included within total revenue, not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1309\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1379\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e735\\\", \\\"e1426\\\"]\"], [8833, \"2026-08-06\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectations are about $393 billion (also consistent with roughly 80\\u201382% growth on FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion); that reference is rung 3.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried through Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion. Customer spending and order/supply signals support a modest uplift, while memory and China headwinds temper it, giving an implied FY2027 total of roughly $390\\u2013395 billion.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The operating run-rate from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided 11% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4, is about $386 billion. Recent customer spending and order visibility support a modest uplift from that run-rate, but the large Blackwell/Rubin figures span products and periods and are not fully attributable to FY2027; memory constraints and China exposure offset the upside. The implied outcome is around $390\\u2013395 billion, still below the $400 billion rung boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1426\\\", \\\"e1493\\\"]\"], [8837, \"2026-08-07\", 2, \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027 (Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates), which is rung 3: $350 billion to below $400 billion.\", \"Approximately $400\\u2013410 billion, with a central estimate near $405 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion and an 11% sequential carry-forward through Q3 and Q4 implies about $386 billion; stronger order visibility and customer spending support a modest uplift, while the broad periods covered by the large product-order figures limit how much to assign to FY2027.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The operating carry-forward from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, and roughly 11% sequential growth in each remaining quarter is about $386 billion. I infer approximately $400\\u2013410 billion (central estimate about $405 billion): the customer-spending and backlog evidence, strong order visibility and reported Blackwell/Rubin orders support a modest uplift to the carry-forward, while those order figures span products and periods and should not be attributed wholly to FY2027. That uplift is enough to make crossing $400 billion slightly more likely than not, but not enough to support $450 billion. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook is already part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e1068\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1371\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1263\\\"]\"], [8841, \"2026-08-08\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Yahoo Finance market estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027, corresponding to rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $410 billion (roughly $405\\u2013415 billion): Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided sequential growth through the remaining quarters, implies a base near $386 billion; customer demand and spending evidence supports a modest uplift.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Carrying forward the guided growth from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion gives a roughly $386 billion operating base; strong customer spending and orders support a modest uplift, while supply constraints, China exposure, and spending that bypasses NVIDIA limit it. The implied estimate of about $410 billion crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but does not support $450 billion. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin orders span products and periods, and planned GPU deployments are not all FY2027 revenue.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1493\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1517\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e376\\\", \\\"e379\\\"]\"], [8844, \"2026-08-09\", 2, \"Wall Street's average estimate is about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the market reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $415 billion. Carrying the roughly 11% sequential increase in NVIDIA's $91 billion Q2 guide forward gives an operating base near $386 billion for FY2027; customer investment, demand visibility, and some improved supply support a modest uplift, while China exposure and memory constraints temper it.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The Q1 actual and Q2 guide imply a roughly $386 billion base if the guided 11% sequential growth is carried forward; strong customer investment and demand, plus improved supply support, justify a modest uplift to about $415 billion. China-related limits, memory constraints, and the fact that announced GPU deployments and orders span products and periods keep the forecast below $450 billion. The resulting estimate crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU-sales forecast is a component of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to the total estimate; the total remains above that dependency\\u2019s conclusion.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e669\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1517\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [8849, \"2026-08-10\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Approximately $415 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus the $91 billion Q2 guide, with the guided 11% sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, implies about $386 billion before an uplift for demand and production evidence, tempered by supply and China risks.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. Carrying NVIDIA\\u2019s Q2 guide of $91 billion and its 11% sequential growth forward gives approximately $386 billion for the four quarters, below the $400 billion boundary; the forecast remains about $415 billion after a measured uplift for strong demand and production evidence, partly offset by memory and China-related constraints. That crosses into rung 2 but remains below $450 billion. The announced $1 trillion of Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders spans products and periods, so it supports demand but cannot be counted as FY2027 revenue in full. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of total revenue and is already contained within this estimate, not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e669\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1517\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1426\\\", \\\"e1318\\\"]\"], [8853, \"2026-08-11\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $410 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion and the cited Q3 estimate of $103.1 billion, with roughly 11% sequential growth carried into Q4, imply approximately $390 billion before an uplift for strong demand, booked commitments and pricing. Memory and China-related constraints temper that uplift.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The operating run-rate is close to that reference: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, and the cited Q3 analyst estimate is about $103.1 billion; carrying roughly 11% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $114 billion and approximately $390 billion for the year. Strong customer demand, booked supply commitments, rising AI infrastructure spending and pricing support a modest uplift to about $410 billion, while memory constraints and China-related restrictions cap it. That forecast crosses $400 billion but remains below $450 billion. The roughly $1 trillion of chip orders spans products and periods and is not counted as FY2027 revenue in full. The dependency's approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is a component of, and fits within, this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e949\\\", \\\"e1068\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e1309\\\", \\\"e1310\\\", \\\"e1311\\\", \\\"e1312\\\", \\\"e1318\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1379\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e1426\\\", \\\"e1493\\\", \\\"e1502\\\", \\\"e1503\\\", \\\"e1505\\\", \\\"e1517\\\", \\\"e1564\\\", \\\"e1566\\\", \\\"e1660\\\", \\\"e1661\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1686\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1705\\\", \\\"e1706\\\", \\\"e1709\\\"]\"], [8859, \"2026-08-12\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue consensus is about $393 billion, placing the reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $410 billion: Q1 actual revenue ($81.615 billion) plus Q2 guidance ($91 billion), Q3 consensus ($103.1 billion), and an estimated roughly $114 billion in Q4 from carrying approximately 11% sequential growth forward gives about $390 billion; demand, orders and pricing support a modest uplift above that run-rate.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus of about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The run-rate implied by $81.615 billion in Q1, $91 billion Q2 guidance, the $103.1 billion Q3 analyst estimate, and roughly 11% sequential growth into Q4 is about $390 billion. Strong AI infrastructure demand, booked orders, deployments and pricing support a modest uplift to around $410 billion, crossing $400 billion into rung 2. Memory constraints and China-related restrictions temper the upside; the evidence does not imply $450 billion or more. The roughly $1 trillion in Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders spans products and periods, so it is not counted in full as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA's stand-alone CPU sales forecast of about $20 billion is one component of total revenue, not an addition to the total forecast.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e458\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1764\\\", \\\"e1318\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [8863, \"2026-08-13\", 2, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street consensus is about $393 billion for FY2027, placing the reference in rung 3.\", \"The Q1 actual, Q2 guidance, Q3 estimate and continued sequential growth into Q4 imply a baseline around $390 billion. Allowing a modest uplift for demand and booked orders, while discounting multi-period orders and competitive, supply and China risks, implies roughly $400\\u2013410 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 revenue consensus of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. Carrying the reported $81.6 billion Q1, $91 billion Q2 guidance, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate and roughly 11% sequential growth into Q4 gives a baseline near $390 billion. Stronger demand and booked orders support a modest uplift: I forecast about $400\\u2013410 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. The large order and infrastructure figures span products and periods, so they do not establish FY2027 revenue at their full amounts; competitive alternatives, memory constraints and China exposure also temper the upside. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an additional amount to add to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e460\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1318\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1811\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\", \\\"e1821\\\", \\\"e1822\\\", \\\"e1686\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e949\\\"]\"], [8867, \"2026-08-14\", 2, \"FY2027 market consensus is about $393 billion (Bloomberg/Wall Street), placing the reference in rung 3. The 80% consensus growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion likewise implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"About $400\\u2013410 billion, with a central estimate near $405 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate and an 11% sequential Q4 carry-forward imply a baseline near $390 billion; strong demand and booked supply support a modest uplift, not the full value of multi-period order figures.\", \"The market reference is FY2027 revenue consensus of about $393 billion, which is rung 3; the 80% growth consensus is also about $389 billion against FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The run-rate from reported Q1, Q2 guidance and the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate is about $390 billion if Q4 grows 11% sequentially from Q3. I forecast about $400\\u2013410 billion, around $405 billion: demand and booked-supply signals support a modest uplift over that run-rate, but the roughly $1 trillion order figure spans products and periods and is not FY2027 revenue guidance. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is included in total revenue, not added on top. Alternative accelerators, memory-ramp constraints and China exposure temper the upside; the implied outcome crosses $400 billion but not $450 billion.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e878\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1811\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1424\\\"]\"], [8871, \"2026-08-15\", 2, \"FY2027 revenue consensus is about $393 billion (rung 3); the 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion, also rung 3.\", \"Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 11% sequential growth carried into Q4 imply about $390 billion. Demand and supplier-growth evidence justify a modest uplift to roughly $400\\u2013405 billion, with the approximately $1 trillion order figure discounted because it spans products and periods.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; the 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion is about $389 billion and also falls in rung 3. A mechanical path from reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 11% sequential growth carried into Q4 gives roughly $390 billion. Strong AI-investment demand and upstream growth support a modest uplift from that baseline, while constrained HBM ramping, China exposure, and competing accelerators limit it. I therefore forecast about $400\\u2013405 billion: only a modest uplift is warranted from the roughly $1 trillion orders figure because it spans products and periods, and the forecast just crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an amount to add on top.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e456\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1686\\\", \\\"e1895\\\", \\\"e1896\\\", \\\"e1899\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1424\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\"]\"], [8875, \"2026-08-16\", 3, \"Bloomberg data and Wall Street estimates put FY2027 revenue near $393 billion; the 80% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion. Both figures are in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $396 billion. Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the $103.1 billion Q3 estimate, and 12% sequential growth into Q4 imply roughly $391 billion; demand and orders support a modest uplift, but not enough to reach $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; the 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion implies about $389 billion and is also rung 3. The operating path is similar: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, analysts put Q3 near $103.1 billion, and carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $115.5 billion, or roughly $391 billion for the year. Strong demand and confirmed orders support the upper end of the high-$390-billion range, but those orders span products and periods and do not establish enough FY2027 revenue to cross $400 billion; HBM constraints and China exposure are counterweights. The roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU outlook in v8 is consistent with a component of total revenue, not an amount to add on top of the total-revenue path.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e234\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e883\\\", \\\"e1263\\\", \\\"e1424\\\"]\"], [8879, \"2026-08-17\", 3, \"Market estimates put FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (e195, e669), rung 3: at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion. The 80% growth estimate also implies about $389 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion (e751, e577), likewise rung 3.\", \"About $391 billion on the operating path: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, $91 billion guided for Q2, $103.1 billion estimated for Q3, and approximately $115.5 billion for Q4 if sequential growth continues at roughly 12%. The demand commitments support the upper end, but their multi-year periods do not establish enough FY2027 shipments to lift this estimate to $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The forecast remains near the top of that rung, not because consensus alone keeps it there: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, Q2 guidance is $91 billion, and the cited Q3 estimate is $103.1 billion. Carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $115.5 billion, or roughly $391 billion for the year; the separate estimate of just under $400 billion on that growth path is consistent with the same rung. Strong demand, customer commitments and higher hyperscaler capex support the upper end, but the large commitments span products and years and do not establish enough FY2027 shipments to imply $400 billion or more. Memory and China-related constraints are counterweights. The roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is a component of total revenue and is consistent with, not additional to, this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e669\\\", \\\"e751\\\", \\\"e817\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e877\\\", \\\"e924\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e1493\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1982\\\", \\\"e1985\\\", \\\"e2017\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e599\\\"]\"], [8883, \"2026-08-18\", 3, \"Bloomberg data and Wall Street estimates put NVIDIA FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $391 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 + $91 billion guided for Q2 + the cited $103.1 billion Q3 estimate + about $115.5 billion for Q4 by carrying 12% sequential growth from Q3. The Q4 calculation and total are estimates, not reported results.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, in rung 3. The evidence-based path is about $391 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, the cited Q3 estimate of $103.1 billion, and Q4 of about $115.5 billion if Q3-to-Q4 growth is 12%. That leaves the forecast just below the $400 billion rung boundary. The roughly $1 trillion in Blackwell and Vera Rubin orders through 2027 and OpenAI purchases potentially worth $600 billion through 2030 support demand, but span products and periods and do not establish enough FY2027 revenue to lift this estimate across $400 billion. Supply tightness and China-related limits are counterweights. The $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in dependency v8 is included within, and does not exceed, the total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2017\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e1814\\\"]\"], [8887, \"2026-08-19\", 2, \"Market expectations put NVIDIA FY2027 revenue at about $393 billion (e195), in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"Approximately $403.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported for Q1, BofA's $94.5 billion Q2 midpoint and $107.5 billion Q3 estimate, then about $119.9 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 11.5% sequential growth implied by Q2 guidance.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The newer forecast uses $81.6 billion reported in Q1, BofA's $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate, its $107\\u2013108 billion estimate for potential Q3 guidance, and carries NVIDIA's roughly 11.5% sequential Q2 guided growth forward for Q4: approximately $81.6B + $94.5B + $107.5B + $119.9B = $403.5 billion. That narrowly crosses the $400 billion rung boundary. Q2 guidance itself remains $91 billion, and the Q3 figure is BofA's estimate rather than company guidance, so the crossing is a forecast, not an established result. The $20 billion FY2027 CPU forecast in v8 is included in\\u2014not added to\\u2014the total. Supply constraints remain a counterweight; the roughly $1 trillion of orders spans products and time and is not counted as FY2027 revenue.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e577\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2059\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e284\\\"]\"], [8892, \"2026-08-20\", 2, \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Yahoo! Finance's approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate (82% growth), which sits in rung 3: $350 billion to less than $400 billion.\", \"Approximately $403.5 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, about $94.5 billion for Q2 based on the $94\\u201395 billion estimate, about $107.5 billion for Q3 per BofA, and about $119.9 billion for Q4 by carrying roughly 11.5% sequential growth forward. The $20 billion FY2027 CPU forecast in v8 is part of this total, not additional revenue.\", \"The FY2027 revenue reference is about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3. The forecast is modestly higher: Q1 was $81.6 billion; for Q2, the $91 billion company guide is the anchor, but upward revisions, the prior-quarter beat, and BofA\\u2019s $94\\u201395 billion estimate support forecasting above the guide. Using roughly $94.5 billion for Q2, BofA\\u2019s $107\\u2013108 billion Q3 estimate, and carrying approximately 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $403.5 billion for the year, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. This is a narrow forecast crossing, not an established result; the $20 billion CPU outlook in v8 is included in total revenue, not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e599\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\", \\\"e1821\\\", \\\"e1822\\\", \\\"e1895\\\", \\\"e1896\\\", \\\"e1899\\\", \\\"e1982\\\", \\\"e1983\\\", \\\"e2017\\\", \\\"e2049\\\", \\\"e2149\\\", \\\"e2151\\\", \\\"e2205\\\", \\\"e2206\\\"]\"], [8896, \"2026-08-21\", 2, \"Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 revenue projection is $393 billion, placing the market reference in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). NVIDIA\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guidance is a nearer-term reference, but by itself does not determine the FY2027 rung.\", \"About $403\\u2013404 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual, approximately $94\\u201395 billion Q2, $107\\u2013108 billion Q3, and roughly $120 billion Q4 by carrying about 11.5% sequential growth from Q3.\", \"The $393 billion FY2027 consensus reference is rung 3, but it is not the forecast once the current-quarter evidence is incorporated. Q1 was $81.6 billion; using BofA\\u2019s $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate, its $107\\u2013108 billion potential Q3 guide, and about 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 implies roughly $403\\u2013404 billion for FY2027\\u2014just over the $400 billion rung boundary. The estimate that repeating 12% sequential growth would leave revenue just under $400 billion is a meaningful countercase, so the crossing is narrow rather than secure. The roughly $20 billion Vera standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an additional amount to add.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e240\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e2244\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\"]\"], [8900, \"2026-08-22\", 2, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 revenue consensus (e195), which is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $403\\u2013404 billion: Q1 actual revenue of $81.6 billion, plus BofA\\u2019s $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate and $107\\u2013108 billion potential Q3 guide, with about 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 (roughly $120 billion). The roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in dependency v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add again.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies roughly $403\\u2013404 billion: starting with Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, using BofA\\u2019s $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate and $107\\u2013108 billion potential Q3 guidance, then carrying about 11.5% sequential growth into Q4 gives approximately $120 billion for Q4. That is just above the $400 billion boundary; the company\\u2019s $91 billion Q2 guide and the estimate of just under $400 billion at 12% sequential growth are meaningful counterweights, but do not make the below-boundary outcome more likely.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e2148\\\"]\"], [8904, \"2026-08-23\", 2, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 revenue consensus is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Roughly $404 billion on the base path: $81.6 billion Q1 actual, $94\\u201395 billion Q2 estimate, $107\\u2013108 billion potential Q3 guidance, and approximately $120 billion for Q4 carrying forward about 12% sequential growth. The separate $96 billion Q2 / $112 billion Q3 estimate supports upside, but its implied Q4 is not treated as certain.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The stronger current-quarter forecast path implies roughly $404 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, about $94\\u201395 billion expected for Q2, $107\\u2013108 billion of potential Q3 guidance, then roughly $120 billion in Q4 if sequential growth continues near 12%. A newer, more bullish estimate of $96 billion for Q2 and $112 billion for Q3 supports further upside, while the $91 billion company Q2 guide and an estimate that 12% sequential growth would leave FY2027 just under $400 billion are counterweights. On balance, the implied result is modestly above $400 billion, crossing into rung 2; the $1 trillion order-book claim spans 2026 and 2027 and is not taken in full for FY2027.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e851\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e2291\\\", \\\"e2149\\\", \\\"e1696\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1336\\\"]\"], [8910, \"2026-08-24\", 2, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 sales consensus of $393 billion; rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $404 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1, roughly $94\\u201395 billion for Q2, about $107\\u2013108 billion for Q3, and approximately $120 billion for Q4 assuming roughly 12% sequential growth from Q3.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits in rung 3. The forecast is above that reference: Q1 was $81.6 billion; Q2 is estimated around $94\\u201395 billion despite the $91 billion company guide, with upward estimate revisions and a prior-quarter beat supporting some upside to guidance; BofA sees potential Q3 guidance of $107\\u2013108 billion. Carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $120 billion and a total near $404 billion, above the $400 billion rung boundary. The $98.18 billion Q2 estimate is a less corroborated upside signal, while the $1 trillion order-book figure spans 2026 and 2027 and is not counted fully as FY2027 revenue. The approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-sales forecast in dependency v8 is a component of, not an addition to, this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2402\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2406\\\"]\"], [8914, \"2026-08-25\", 2, \"The latest supplied FY2027 market estimate is LSEG analysts' $396 billion (e2421), which is rung 3. Bloomberg's earlier $393 billion estimate (e195) is also rung 3.\", \"About $404\\u2013405 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion, an estimated $94\\u201395 billion for Q2, about $107\\u2013108 billion for Q3, and roughly $120\\u2013121 billion for Q4 by carrying approximately 12% sequential growth forward.\", \"The latest stated FY2027 market estimate is $396 billion, which is rung 3. The quarter-by-quarter forecast is modestly higher: Q1 reported $81.6 billion, Q2 is plausibly about $94\\u201395 billion despite the $91 billion guide, Q3 about $107\\u2013108 billion, and carrying roughly 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $120\\u2013121 billion, or roughly $404\\u2013405 billion for FY2027. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but not the $450 billion boundary. The risk of memory constraints and competing custom chips tempers the upside; the $1 trillion order-book claims span products and periods and are not counted as FY2027 revenue. NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-sales forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not incremental to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2147\\\", \\\"e2148\\\", \\\"e2402\\\", \\\"e2171\\\", \\\"e1662\\\", \\\"e2487\\\", \\\"e1932\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e252\\\", \\\"e253\\\", \\\"e231\\\", \\\"e232\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e1816\\\", \\\"e2367\\\", \\\"e2443\\\", \\\"e1985\\\", \\\"e2438\\\"]\"], [8919, \"2026-08-26\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"FY2027 revenue of about $407 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2, $108 billion guided for Q3, and an estimated roughly $121 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance.\", \"The FY2027 market reference is LSEG's $396 billion estimate, which sits in rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues plus Q3 guidance imply about $407 billion for the year: carrying Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth forward gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but remains below $450 billion. Memory, power and data-center capacity constraints and the longer-dated custom-chip threat temper the forecast; NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-sales outlook is already part of total revenue, not incremental to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2760\\\"]\"], [8923, \"2026-08-27\", 2, \"LSEG FY2027 revenue estimate: $396 billion, on rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint, then approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 by carrying forward Q3\\u2019s 12.3% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is LSEG\\u2019s $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which sits on rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 totals plus Q3 guidance, with Q4 modeled by carrying forward Q3\\u2019s sequential growth, imply about $407 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2 but remains below $450 billion. The $1 trillion order-book figure and Rubin ramp support demand, but neither is a FY2027 recognized-revenue figure; memory, power and data-center constraints and custom-chip competition temper the upside. NVIDIA\\u2019s standalone CPU-sales estimate is part of total revenue, not incremental to it, and the dependent forecast remains consistent with a total above that component.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2521\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e2759\\\", \\\"e2979\\\"]\"], [8927, \"2026-08-28\", 2, \"LSEG's FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits on rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion and Q3 guidance of $108 billion total $285.8 billion. Carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3 forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.3 billion and FY2027 total revenue of about $407 billion.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 totals plus Q3 guidance imply about $407 billion: carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth from Q2 to Q3 forward gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion. The stated supply constraints temper the upside, while the dependent stand-alone CPU forecast of about $20 billion is a component of, and does not exceed, the implied total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e3296\\\"]\"], [8931, \"2026-08-29\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 estimate of $396 billion is the market reference and sits on rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion); Bloomberg's $393 billion projection is also below $400 billion.\", \"About $407 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to the Q3 guidance midpoint of $108 billion, then carry the roughly 12.3% Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth rate into Q4, implying about $121.3 billion.\", \"The implied FY2027 total is about $407 billion, which is above the $400 billion boundary for rung 2 but below the $450 billion boundary for rung 1. Supply constraints temper the extrapolation, but the current-quarter guidance is the strongest forecast input and carries the run-rate across $400 billion. The dependent stand-alone CPU forecast of about $20 billion is a component of this total and does not exceed it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e3296\\\"]\"], [8936, \"2026-08-30\", 2, \"LSEG analysts expect $396 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; Bloomberg data projects $393 billion, also rung 3.\", \"$81.6B Q1 actual + $96.2B Q2 actual + $108B Q3 guidance + about $121.3B for Q4 (carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance forward from Q3) = approximately $407B for FY2027.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, with Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate also in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion). The evidence-based estimate is about $407 billion: Q1 and Q2 actual revenue plus Q3 guidance, then Q4 estimated by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance. That crosses the $400 billion rung boundary, but remains below $450 billion. Supply constraints temper the estimate, though the reported constraint is not quantified for the full year; the dependent stand-alone CPU forecast of about $20 billion is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2989\\\"]\"], [8941, \"2026-08-31\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 consensus of $396 billion (e2421) is the market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion); Bloomberg's $393 billion projection (e195) is also in rung 3.\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 actual + $96.2 billion Q2 actual + $108 billion Q3 guidance + about $121.3 billion for Q4, carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by the Q3 guide (108 \\u00d7 1.123).\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3; Bloomberg's $393 billion estimate is also in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $407.1 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 of about $121.3 billion if the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance carries forward. That crosses the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Newly detailed supply limits temper the outlook, but provide no quantified FY2027 reduction that would displace this run-rate estimate. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is consistent with, and does not exceed, the total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3094\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e3002\\\", \\\"e3278\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e195\\\", \\\"e1814\\\", \\\"e2977\\\", \\\"e3178\\\", \\\"e3593\\\"]\"], [8945, \"2026-09-01\", 2, \"LSEG analysts\\u2019 FY2027 revenue consensus is $396 billion, which is rung 3 (Neutral: $350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2, $108.0 billion guided for Q3, and approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance.\", \"The market reference is LSEG\\u2019s $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate estimate is about $407.1 billion: reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 of about $121.3 billion if Q3\\u2019s 12.3% sequential growth carries forward. That clears the $400 billion rung boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. The reported demand-versus-supply constraint and the memory-shortage outlook temper confidence, but do not quantify a FY2027 reduction that would displace this estimate. The stand-alone CPU dependency\\u2019s approximately $20 billion forecast is consistent with the total and does not exceed it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2723\\\", \\\"e3732\\\", \\\"e3633\\\"]\"], [8949, \"2026-09-02\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' $396 billion FY2027 revenue consensus, in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus Q3 guidance of $108 billion, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward Q3's 12.3% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which is in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $407.1 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 of about $121.3 billion if Q3's 12.3% sequential growth carries forward. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Supply constraints and the exclusion of China Data Center compute revenue temper the upside, but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to move the implied estimate below $400 billion. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in dependency v8 is consistent with the total and does not exceed it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3094\\\", \\\"e284\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3482\\\", \\\"e3752\\\"]\"], [8951, \"2026-09-03\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 revenue consensus of $396 billion; this is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"About $407.1 billion: $81.6 billion Q1 plus $96.2 billion Q2, plus $108 billion Q3 guidance, plus approximately $121.3 billion for Q4 by carrying Q3's 12.3% sequential growth forward.\", \"The LSEG FY2027 consensus is $396 billion, which sits in rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its 12.3% sequential growth forward for Q4 implies about $121.3 billion, or roughly $407.1 billion for FY2027. That is above the $400 billion boundary but below $450 billion, so the forecast remains in rung 2. Supply and memory constraints temper the outlook but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to pull the implied total below $400 billion. The v8 stand-alone CPU forecast is part of total revenue and is consistent with this estimate, not additive to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\"]\"], [8955, \"2026-09-04\", 2, \"LSEG FY2027 revenue consensus of $396 billion; this is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"Q1 and Q2 reported revenue sum to $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its approximately 12% sequential growth forward implies Q4 revenue of about $121.3 billion. The resulting FY2027 total is approximately $407.1 billion.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its roughly 12% sequential growth forward gives about $121.3 billion for Q4, or approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027. That clears the $400 billion boundary but remains below $450 billion, so the forecast is rung 2. Supply constraints are a downside risk, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction large enough to move the implied total below $400 billion. The v8 estimate of about $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is part of total revenue, not additive to it, and is consistent with this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\"]\"], [8959, \"2026-09-05\", 2, \"LSEG analysts' $396 billion FY2027 revenue expectation; rung 3 (Neutral: $350 billion to less than $400 billion).\", \"The first two reported quarters total $177.8 billion. Add Q3's $108 billion midpoint guidance and carry its roughly 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 ($108 billion \\u00d7 1.123 \\u2248 $121.3 billion), implying approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027.\", \"The reference is LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The reported first two quarters total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying its roughly 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4 and approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but remains below $450 billion. Capacity and memory constraints are downside risks, but the evidence does not quantify a shortfall sufficient to take the implied total below $400 billion. The v8 estimate of about $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales is included in total revenue, not additive. The new report that guidance was raised for the rest of the fiscal year is not quantified, so it does not change the calculated outcome or rung.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e2521\\\"]\"], [8963, \"2026-09-06\", 2, \"The latest stated Wall Street expectation is approximately $411 billion for FY2027 (e4097), which sits at rung 2; the earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion (e2421) sits at rung 3.\", \"Q1 and Q2 reported revenue total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 guidance of $108 billion and carrying the 12.3% sequential growth rate into Q4 implies about $121.3 billion for Q4 and approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027.\", \"The current market reference is the approximately $411 billion Wall Street expectation, which is rung 2; the earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion was rung 3. NVIDIA\\u2019s reported Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion, and adding Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion plus Q4 at the guided 12.3% sequential growth rate implies about $407.1 billion for FY2027. That is below the latest analyst reference but still above the $400 billion rung boundary. Supply constraints and memory availability temper the forecast, while the unquantified report that guidance was raised does not justify a larger numerical adjustment. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in v8 is part of total revenue, not additive, and is consistent with this total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e2521\\\"]\"], [8968, \"2026-09-07\", 2, \"Latest cited Wall Street FY2027 revenue expectation: about $411 billion, rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). The earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $407.1 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 revenue of $81.6 billion and $96.2 billion, plus Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion, total $285.8 billion; carrying roughly 12.3% sequential growth forward gives Q4 of about $121.3 billion.\", \"The market reference is the latest cited Wall Street expectation of about $411 billion, which is rung 2; the earlier LSEG estimate of $396 billion was rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate remains just above the $400 billion boundary: Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion, Q3 midpoint guidance is $108 billion, and carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth rate forward to Q4 implies about $121.3 billion, or approximately $407.1 billion for FY2027. Supply constraints temper that estimate, while the new 400,000-GPU deployment statement is not allocated to FY2027 or translated into NVIDIA revenue. Neither justifies moving below $400 billion or above $450 billion. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in dependency v8 is included within, not added to, total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4010\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4137\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e4175\\\"]\"], [8973, \"2026-09-08\", 2, \"The latest stated Wall Street expectation is about $411 billion, which sits in rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion); the earlier LSEG expectation of $396 billion sits in rung 3.\", \"Q1 and Q2 actual revenue total $177.8 billion; adding Q3 midpoint guidance of $108 billion gives $285.8 billion through Q3. Carrying the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance forward gives Q4 of about $121.3 billion and FY2027 total revenue of approximately $407.1 billion.\", \"The implied outcome is above the $400 billion rung boundary but below $450 billion, so the forecast remains rung 2. The $411 billion market expectation is a reference, not the reason to stay there. Q2 actual revenue and Q3 guidance support a roughly $407 billion run-rate; the report that NVIDIA raised guidance for the rest of the year tilts upward but gives no amount to add. Demand and pricing signals are offset by supply constraints, and the large order figure is not allocated to FY2027 revenue. The roughly $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e2421\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e1336\\\"]\"], [8977, \"2026-09-09\", 2, \"Market expectation: Wall Street's approximately $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, in rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6B reported in Q1 plus $96.2B in Q2 and the $108B Q3 midpoint guide, then about $121B for Q4 by carrying forward Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is approximately $411 billion, which sits in rung 2. The run-rate implied by the reported first two quarters and Q3 guidance is about $407 billion: $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B, with Q4 estimated at about $121B by carrying forward Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth. That is above the $400B boundary but below $450B. Supply and memory constraints temper the outlook, but the evidence does not make a Q4 slowdown below roughly $114B\\u2014the level needed to fall under $400B\\u2014the most likely outcome. The roughly $20B standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4006\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4120\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e1336\\\"]\"], [8981, \"2026-09-10\", 2, \"Market analyst expectations of about $411 billion for FY2027 (e4097), which is rung 2. The reference is a comparison point, not the basis for holding the forecast there.\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6B in Q1 plus $96.2B in Q2 plus Q3 guidance of $108B, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3B by carrying Q3\\u2019s implied 12.3% sequential growth forward.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion, which sits in rung 2. The reported first two quarters and Q3 guidance imply $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B; carrying the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives about $121.3B, or roughly $407B for FY2027. That is below the consensus point but remains above the $400B rung boundary. Supply and memory constraints restrain the forecast, while strong demand, orders and updated AI-capex themes support it; none supplies a quantified FY2027 adjustment sufficient to move the estimate across a rung boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20B standalone-CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2521\\\"]\"], [8985, \"2026-09-11\", 2, \"Latest supplied analyst expectations put NVIDIA FY2027 revenue at about $411 billion (e4097), in rung 2.\", \"Approximately $407.1 billion: $81.6B reported in Q1 plus $96.2B reported in Q2 plus the $108B Q3 midpoint guide, with the roughly 12.3% Q3 sequential growth carried forward to an estimated $121.3B in Q4.\", \"The reference is the latest supplied analyst expectation of $411 billion, which sits in rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion). The reported first two quarters and Q3 midpoint guidance imply $81.6B + $96.2B + $108B; carrying the Q3 sequential growth rate of about 12.3% forward for Q4 gives about $121.3B, or approximately $407.1B for FY2027. That remains in rung 2: it is about $7B above the $400B floor and about $43B short of rung 1. Strong demand, Rubin production, capacity investment, pricing plans and contracted/project demand support the run-rate, while memory, wafer, packaging, power and data-center constraints temper the upside. The announced AWS deployment and the $1T Blackwell/Rubin target span multiple periods, and the Australia figure describes infrastructure plans rather than NVIDIA revenue; they do not justify adding their full amounts to FY2027 revenue. No quantified revision in the supplied evidence moves the implied result across a boundary. NVIDIA's approximately $20B stand-alone CPU outlook in dependency v8 is part of total revenue, not an addition to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e2521\\\", \\\"e3482\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e4325\\\", \\\"e4490\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e4513\\\"]\"], [8989, \"2026-09-12\", 2, \"Analysts expect $411 billion in FY2027; that reference is rung 2 ($400 billion to less than $450 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 midpoint guide, with Q4 estimated at about $121.3 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% Q3 sequential growth rate.\", \"The reference is analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 expectation, which is rung 2. Independently, the reported Q1 and Q2 results plus the $108 billion Q3 guide imply about $407 billion using the Q3 sequential growth rate for Q4; that clears rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion floor but remains well below rung 1\\u2019s $450 billion threshold. Strong demand and planned pricing provide upside, but the additional evidence of persistent memory, packaging and data-center capacity constraints does not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to move the run-rate across a boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin revenue target spans products and time periods and is not evidence that the volume is in hand for this fiscal year.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e4325\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\"]\"], [8994, \"2026-09-13\", 2, \"Analysts expect $411 billion of FY2027 revenue (rung 2: $400 billion to below $450 billion).\", \"About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $96.2 billion in Q2 plus the $108 billion Q3 guide, with Q4 estimated at about $121 billion by carrying forward Q3\\u2019s roughly 12% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 expectation, which sits at rung 2. The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues plus the Q3 guide, carrying Q3\\u2019s approximately 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, imply about $407 billion for FY2027. That remains above rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion floor and below rung 1\\u2019s $450 billion threshold. Persistent supply and data-center bottlenecks temper the upside, while the announced pricing increase and substantial demand are not quantified as enough FY2027 revenue to cross a boundary. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add on top.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e4325\\\"]\"], [8998, \"2026-09-14\", 2, \"Analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 expectation is in rung 2. The newer Zacks consensus of 87.4% growth, applied to FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion, implies about $405 billion, also in rung 2.\", \"Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, Q3 guidance of $108 billion, and Q4 estimated by carrying Q3\\u2019s roughly 12% sequential growth forward ($121 billion) imply approximately $407 billion for FY2027.\", \"The market reference is analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 estimate, in rung 2; the newer Zacks estimate of 87.4% growth applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies roughly $405 billion, also in rung 2. Independent of those consensus figures, Q1 and Q2 reported revenue plus Q3 guidance and a Q4 carry-forward imply about $407 billion. That is above rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion floor but below rung 1\\u2019s $450 billion threshold. Strong demand and orders provide upside potential, but the evidence does not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to cross $450 billion, while reported supply and data-center bottlenecks temper the forecast. The approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook in v8 is included in total revenue and is not added again.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\"]\"], [9002, \"2026-09-15\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 estimate is in rung 2. Zacks\\u2019 87.4% FY2027 growth estimate applied to approximately $215.9 billion of FY2026 revenue implies about $405 billion, also in rung 2.\", \"About $418 billion: add reported Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion to a Q3 estimate of roughly $113.5 billion (the midpoint of the $113\\u2013$114 billion forecast, supported by the reported guidance-beat history), then carry the 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance into Q4, giving about $127 billion.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 estimate, which is in rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is higher, around $418 billion, but remains below rung 1\\u2019s $450 billion boundary. It incorporates the $113\\u2013$114 billion Q3 analyst forecast and the recent history of beats over guidance, then carries the 12% sequential growth implied by management\\u2019s Q3 guide into Q4. Persistent supply and downstream data-center constraints temper the upside. NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion stand-alone CPU outlook is part of total revenue, not an amount to add on top; v8\\u2019s conclusion is consistent with that treatment.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e4325\\\", \\\"e4772\\\"]\"], [9006, \"2026-09-16\", 2, \"Analysts\\u2019 FY2027 revenue expectation of about $411 billion is the market reference and sits in rung 2. Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $405 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 actual revenue totals $177.8 billion; applying the 3.1% average beat to the high end of Q3 guidance implies about $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the guide\\u2019s 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $127.2 billion. This falls within rung 2\\u2019s $400\\u2013450 billion range.\", \"The current market reference is analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 estimate, in rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies roughly $405 billion, in rung 2. My estimate is about $419 billion: actual Q1 and Q2 revenue totaled $177.8 billion, Q3 is estimated at about $113.6 billion by applying the reported 3.1% average beat to the high end of guidance, and carrying the 12% sequential growth implied by the Q3 guide into Q4 gives about $127.2 billion. Supply and data-center bottlenecks temper the upside, but do not pull the estimate below $400 billion; it remains below rung 1\\u2019s $450 billion boundary. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $12 billion stand-alone CPU forecast is part of total revenue, not an addition to it, consistent with v8.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\"]\"], [9010, \"2026-09-17\", 2, \"Analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 revenue estimate is the market reference and sits in rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate on FY2026 revenue implies about $405 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 total $177.8 billion; Q3 is estimated at about $113.6 billion by applying the 3.1% average beat to the high end of guidance, and Q4 at about $127.2 billion by carrying forward the guided 12% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is the $411 billion FY2027 analyst estimate, which sits in rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies roughly $405 billion, in rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is about $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.8 billion; applying the reported 3.1% average beat to the high end of Q3 guidance gives about $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the guided 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $127.2 billion. Supply constraints through FY2028 temper the upside, while the specific Q3 estimate and strong demand signals support the run-rate; neither shifts the estimate across $400 billion or $450 billion. The roughly $15 billion stand-alone CPU estimate in v8 is included within total revenue, not added to it.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4012\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3633\\\"]\"], [9014, \"2026-09-18\", 2, \"The market reference is the $411 billion FY2027 analyst expectation, which sits in rung 2. Zacks\\u2019 87.4% revenue-growth consensus implies about $404.5 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $418.5 billion, or roughly $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 reported revenue totals $177.8 billion; the Q3 estimate is about $113.5 billion; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth into Q4 implies about $127.1 billion.\", \"The $411 billion analyst expectation is in rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies roughly $404.5 billion, likewise rung 2. The forecast remains about $419 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 total $177.8 billion; Q3 is estimated at about $113\\u2013114 billion, consistent with the 3.1% average beat above the high end of guidance over the past four quarters; carrying the guided 12% sequential growth forward gives Q4 of about $127 billion. This remains below the $450 billion rung-1 boundary. Supply constraints temper upside, and the roughly $15 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is included in\\u2014not added to\\u2014total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\"]\"], [9018, \"2026-09-19\", 2, \"Analyst expectations of about $411 billion (e4061, e4097) are the market reference and sit at rung 2. Zacks' 87.4% FY2027 growth estimate implies about $404.5 billion from the reported FY2026 base of approximately $215.9 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: Q1 and Q2 total $177.8 billion; applying the historical average 3.1% beat above the $110.16 billion high end of Q3 guidance implies about $113.6 billion for Q3, and carrying the guided 12% sequential growth forward implies about $127.2 billion for Q4. The sum is approximately $418.6 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 revenue, which is rung 2; the 87.4% Zacks growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $404.5 billion, also rung 2. The evidence-derived estimate is about $419 billion, still rung 2 and below the $450 billion rung-1 boundary. Near-term supply constraints and the newly quantified unfulfilled-demand estimate temper the upside, but the latter concerns AI-infrastructure demand in 2027 and does not establish additional NVIDIA FY2027 revenue. The approximately $18 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add to it, and is consistent with a total above that component.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4901\\\", \\\"e4899\\\", \\\"e2951\\\", \\\"e4120\\\", \\\"e3633\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e3593\\\", \\\"e3482\\\"]\"], [9023, \"2026-09-20\", 2, \"Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion of FY2027 revenue (rung 2). Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate against FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion implies about $404.5 billion, also rung 2.\", \"About $419 billion: Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2\\u2019s $96.2 billion, an estimated Q3 of about $113.6 billion (the $110.16 billion high end of guidance adjusted by the reported 3.1% average beat), and Q4 of about $127.5 billion by carrying forward roughly 12% sequential growth.\", \"The market reference is Wall Street consensus of about $411 billion, which sits at rung 2; Zacks\\u2019 87.4% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue of about $215.9 billion also implies about $404.5 billion, at rung 2. The reported Q1 and Q2 figures plus Q3 guidance, adjusted for the quantified history of beating guidance and the $113\\u2013$114 billion Q3 estimate, imply about $419 billion for FY2027. Supply bottlenecks temper the upside, but this estimate remains above $400 billion and below the $450 billion boundary. The approximately $18 billion stand-alone CPU forecast in v8 is part of total revenue, not an amount to add, and is consistent with the total.\", \"[\\\"v8\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e238\\\", \\\"e2497\\\", \\\"e2499\\\", \\\"e3943\\\", \\\"e4061\\\", \\\"e4097\\\", \\\"e4377\\\", \\\"e4702\\\", \\\"e4761\\\", \\\"e4762\\\", \\\"e4769\\\", \\\"e4901\\\", \\\"e4902\\\", \\\"e2705\\\", \\\"e4511\\\", \\\"e4512\\\", \\\"e1336\\\", \\\"e321\\\", \\\"e906\\\", \\\"e4012\\\"]\"]], [\"How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 450000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 400000000000.0, 450000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 350000000000.0, 400000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 300000000000.0, 350000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 300000000000.0]]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nv=\\\"v9\\\"\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(base/\\\"linked-1-copy\\\"/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nrows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>8737 order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,)).fetchall()\nprint(json.dumps([rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"[[[8744, \"2026-07-10\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center market consensus is supplied. The best supplied proxy is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation in v14, which is rung 3 for total revenue; at NVIDIA's Q1 Data Center share of 92%, it implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue, rung 3.\", \"About $356 billion: carrying the approximately 11.5% sequential increase from Q1 total revenue of $81.6 billion to Q2 guidance of $91 billion forward gives roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, then applying the Q1 Data Center mix of 92% gives roughly $356 billion. This is a run-rate estimate, not a flat annualization of Q1.\", \"There is no direct FY2027 Data Center consensus in the supplied evidence. As a market-reference proxy, v14 reports a Bloomberg-compiled $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation, which is in rung 3 for total revenue; applying the current 92% Data Center share puts the proxy near $362 billion, also rung 3. The operating run-rate is about $356 billion: Q1 total revenue was $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance is $91 billion, an approximately 11.5% sequential increase; carrying that growth forward gives roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying the Q1 Data Center mix gives roughly $356 billion. Management's statement that Q2 Data Center growth will accelerate and its $1 trillion visibility across two calendar years are upside inputs, but neither supplies a quantified FY2027 segment figure sufficient to move the estimate above $400 billion. Conversely, the v14 total-revenue forecast makes a Data Center forecast above the whole-company forecast inconsistent, while memory and China substitution risks temper the outlook. The implied figure remains in rung 3, below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary and above $350 billion. The forecast moves from the previous rung 2 to rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e202\\\", \\\"e204\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e158\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\"]\"], [8747, \"2026-07-12\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus is supplied. The Bloomberg FY2027 total-revenue reference is $393 billion; applying the reported Q1 Data Center share of 92% gives a proxy of about $362 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $356 billion: v14's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue run-rate, derived by carrying the Q1-to-Q2 guided sequential growth pace forward, multiplied by the reported 92% Q1 Data Center share.\", \"The reference is a Bloomberg FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, reported in v14; with the latest 92% Data Center mix, that implies about $362 billion, in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $356 billion, also in rung 3: the strong Q1 Data Center result and Q2 total-revenue guidance support continued growth, but the supplied run-rate does not imply the $400 billion needed to move to rung 2. Management's $1 trillion visibility spans Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue across calendar 2026 and 2027, so it is not a direct FY2027 Data Center forecast and is discounted. Custom-chip competition and expected domestic substitution in China temper the upside. The implied segment revenue remains below the $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e163\\\"]\"], [8751, \"2026-07-13\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated using the 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion for Data Center; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $356 billion for FY2027. Starting from Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, applying the Q1 92% mix to the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide estimates Q2 Data Center revenue at about $83.7 billion; carrying the roughly 11.5% sequential total-revenue growth rate forward gives about $93.3 billion in Q3 and $104.1 billion in Q4.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $356 billion: Q2 total-revenue guidance of $91 billion versus Q1\\u2019s $81.6 billion implies roughly 11.5% sequential growth; applying the Q1 92% Data Center mix to Q2 and carrying that guided sequential pace through Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $83.7 billion, $93.3 billion and $104.1 billion of Data Center revenue, respectively, including Q1\\u2019s $75.25 billion. Management\\u2019s Q2 Data Center growth-acceleration statement is upside support, but gives no amount; the $91 billion total-revenue guide limits Q2 Data Center revenue to no more than that total. The resulting estimate is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary, and the dependent FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $387 billion is also a ceiling for this segment. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and is not a direct FY2027 Data Center forecast, so it is discounted. China substitution/export constraints and custom-silicon competition offset some of the strong demand and growth evidence.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e201\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e158\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e297\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\"]\"], [8753, \"2026-07-14\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated using NVIDIA's latest reported 92% Data Center share, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue. That reference is in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $356 billion for FY2027: dependency v14's approximately $387 billion total-revenue run-rate forecast, derived by carrying forward the sequential growth implied by Q1 actual revenue and Q2 guidance, multiplied by the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92%.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix puts the reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. The implied Data Center outcome is about $356 billion: NVIDIA's $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide and the sequential-growth run-rate carried forward in dependency v14 imply about $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying the observed 92% Data Center mix gives roughly $356 billion. That is below the $400 billion boundary, so it remains in rung 3. Management's Q2 growth-acceleration statement and roughly $1 trillion of Blackwell/Rubin revenue visibility across calendar 2026\\u201327 support upside, but the visibility spans products and periods and is not a FY2027 Data Center figure; the $91 billion quarterly total guide also caps Q2 Data Center revenue. Competitive substitution and China-related constraints weigh the other way. The new platform-visibility fact changes the supporting facts, but does not justify moving the implied FY2027 figure across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e296\\\", \\\"e265\\\", \\\"e267\\\", \\\"e357\\\", \\\"e358\\\", \\\"e360\\\"]\"], [8758, \"2026-07-15\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion, translated using the latest 92% Data Center mix to approximately $362 billion; this is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $356 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: carry the sequential growth from Q1 total revenue of $81.6 billion to the $91 billion Q2 guide into Q3 and Q4, producing about $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, then apply the observed 92% Data Center mix. This is below $400 billion and consistent with v14\\u2019s higher total forecast because its approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU contribution is outside Data Center.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix puts the reference at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The implied Data Center forecast is about $356 billion: carrying the sequential growth from Q1 total revenue of $81.6 billion to the $91 billion Q2 guide forward gives roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion. Management\\u2019s Q2 Data Center growth-acceleration comment and multiyear platform visibility add upside, but the visibility spans calendar 2026\\u201327 and Blackwell and Rubin, so it does not establish a FY2027 Data Center amount that crosses $400 billion. Dependency v14\\u2019s roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast includes about $19.5 billion of stand-alone CPU revenue; the approximately $356 billion Data Center estimate remains below that whole and below the rung-2 boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e204\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e462\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e297\\\"]\"], [8761, \"2026-07-16\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, converted using the latest 92% Data Center mix, implies about $361 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion: carrying the $81.6 billion Q1 result and $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide forward implies roughly $387 billion for FY2027; applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, then adding v14\\u2019s approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast as incremental Data Center revenue gives about $376 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $361 billion, in rung 3. The current implied Data Center outcome is about $376 billion: carry the Q1-to-Q2 total-revenue growth implied by the $81.6 billion result and $91 billion Q2 guide through Q3 and Q4, giving roughly $387 billion for the year; apply the 92% Data Center mix (about $356 billion), then add v14\\u2019s approximately $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast as incremental Data Center revenue. This is below the $400 billion rung boundary and consistent with v14\\u2019s roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast; the added CPU revenue raises the estimate from the prior roughly $356 billion but does not change the rung. Strong demand and the Q2 acceleration comment support the run-rate, while China restrictions, competition and supply constraints temper the upside; the broad Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans products and calendar years, so it is not taken as FY2027 Data Center revenue in full.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e307\\\"]\"], [8765, \"2026-07-17\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at the 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $375 billion: the $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guide versus $81.6 billion in Q1 implies roughly 11.5% sequential growth; carrying that through Q3 and Q4 gives about $387 billion for FY2027. Applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, and adding v14\\u2019s approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate yields about $375 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix puts that reference at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The current forecast is about $375 billion: carrying the Q2 total-revenue guide\\u2019s roughly 11.5% sequential growth through Q3 and Q4 implies about $387 billion of FY2027 revenue; applying the 92% mix gives about $356 billion, then adding v14\\u2019s approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion. That is below the $400 billion boundary and below v14\\u2019s approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast, so it is consistent with the dependency and does not cross a rung boundary. Q1\\u2019s strong growth, Q2 acceleration guidance and multi-year platform visibility support the outlook, but the $1 trillion visibility spans products and calendar years, and the Q2 total-revenue guide constrains the near-term run-rate. China-related restrictions and proprietary-chip competition temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e297\\\"]\"], [8768, \"2026-07-18\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at the latest 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $375 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: the Q2 $91 billion total-revenue guide carried forward at the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate implies roughly $387 billion for FY2027; applying the 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion, then adding v14\\u2019s approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $375 billion.\", \"The market reference carried forward from the prior judgement is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at the latest 92% Data Center mix to about $362 billion; that sits in rung 3. The implied Data Center estimate is about $375 billion: carrying the Q2 $91 billion total-revenue guide forward at the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate implies roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, applying the 92% mix gives about $356 billion, and the approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate in v14 gives the prior method\\u2019s approximately $375 billion estimate. That is within rung 3\\u2019s $350\\u2013$400 billion range and below the $400 billion boundary; it also remains below v14\\u2019s approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast. Strong Q1 Data Center growth, Q2 acceleration guidance, platform visibility, and rising infrastructure spending support the estimate, but several inputs span products or periods and do not establish a higher FY2027 Data Center figure. China-related demand loss, competition, and memory constraints temper the upside. The new evidence changes the supporting facts, but does not move the forecast across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e297\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e462\\\", \\\"e556\\\", \\\"e558\\\", \\\"e559\\\", \\\"e604\\\"]\"], [8769, \"2026-07-19\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection, translated at the latest 92% Data Center mix to about $362 billion; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $375 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue: v14\\u2019s approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast, multiplied by the latest 92% Data Center mix, implies roughly $374 billion, rounded to about $375 billion. Noetra\\u2019s 140MW plan scales to roughly $6.9 billion at $49 billion/GW, but its unspecified delivery timing and planned Rubin deployment warrant discounting rather than adding the full amount.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is modestly higher, around $375 billion, but still below rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion boundary: Q1 results and Q2 guidance support a strong run-rate, while the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and is not FY2027 Data Center revenue. China substitution and zero H20 shipments temper the upside. The Noetra factory scales to roughly $6.9 billion at Morgan Stanley\\u2019s $49 billion/GW assumption, but its delivery date is unspecified and Rubin is a planned product, so that potential is discounted rather than counted in full.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e604\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e501\\\", \\\"e462\\\"]\"], [8772, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion, translated using the latest roughly 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $360 billion for FY2027: the current v14 forecast of approximately $387 billion total revenue multiplied by the reported 92.2% Q1 Data Center share gives about $357 billion; a modest increase to roughly 93% for management's unquantified Q2 Data Center growth acceleration gives about $360 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the latest roughly 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The implied outcome is about $360 billion: the current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is approximately $387 billion, and applying the reported 92.2% Q1 Data Center share gives about $357 billion; I allow a modest increase to roughly 93% for management's unquantified Q2 Data Center growth acceleration. That remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and multiple products, so it does not establish $1 trillion\\u2014or a particular share of it\\u2014as FY2027 Data Center revenue. China substitution and competition from custom chips and AMD weigh against a higher estimate; the planned Noetra Rubin factory and other demand signals provide some offset but are not sufficiently dated or sized to move the estimate across a rung boundary. The rung remains neutral, though the implied estimate is lower than the prior conclusion's roughly $375 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e204\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e538\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e636\\\"]\"], [8775, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion is the market reference; applying the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92.2% implies roughly $362 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3.\", \"About $356 billion: dependency v14 forecasts approximately $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying an estimated roughly 92% Data Center share gives about $356 billion.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion; applying the reported Q1 Data Center mix of about 92.2% puts the reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. For the forecast, dependency v14 implies about $387 billion of total FY2027 revenue. Applying roughly a 92% Data Center share gives about $356 billion. Q1 Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion and management guided to further Q2 Data Center growth acceleration, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and multiple products, so it is not FY2027 Data Center revenue. The new estimate that around 90% of FY2027/FY2028 revenue will be Data Center, alongside specific AMD Helios plans, tempers the share assumption; neither changes the implied outcome enough to cross the $350 billion or $400 billion boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e604\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e630\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e638\\\", \\\"e459\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\"]\"], [8778, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, translated using NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly 92% Q1 Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $352 billion: v14\\u2019s approximately $387.3 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast multiplied by an estimated 91% Data Center share, between the reported 92% Q1 mix and the around-90% FY2027/FY2028 estimate.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate; applying the roughly 92% Data Center mix reported in Q1 puts the reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. The forecast is modestly below that reference: the updated total-revenue forecast from v14 is about $387.3 billion, and a roughly 91% FY2027 Data Center share implies about $352 billion. That share balances the reported 92% Q1 mix and management\\u2019s Q2 Data Center growth-acceleration outlook against the estimate that Data Center will be around 90% of FY2027/FY2028 revenue and the newly specified AMD Helios competition. The implied figure is below the reference but remains above rung 3\\u2019s $350 billion floor, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e636\\\"]\"], [8782, \"2026-07-23\", 4, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 NVIDIA total-revenue estimate is $393 billion. Applying the reported 92% Q1 Data Center share gives a Data Center reference of about $362 billion, which is rung 3.\", \"About $348.5 billion: apply the roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate to v14\\u2019s approximately $387.2 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast. This is below the $350 billion boundary and consistent with the total-revenue dependency.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, translated using the reported 92% Q1 Data Center mix to about $362 billion; that is rung 3. The forecast is lower: applying the roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate to v14\\u2019s approximately $387.2 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast implies about $348.5 billion, just below rung 3\\u2019s $350 billion floor. Q1\\u2019s 92% mix and management\\u2019s Q2 growth outlook support the segment, while the newly specified AMD Helios deployments add competitive pressure; SemiAnalysis\\u2019s Rubin ramp and efficiency analysis partly offset it. The implied segment revenue remains below v14\\u2019s total-revenue forecast, so it is consistent with the dependency. It crosses into rung 4, though only narrowly.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e630\\\", \\\"e631\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e634\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e758\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e761\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e162\\\"]\"], [8785, \"2026-07-24\", 4, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate is the market reference. Applying NVIDIA's reported Q1 Data Center mix of 92% implies about $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue, placing the reference in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $347.1 billion: take v14's approximately $385.7 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast and apply the roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate. This is below the $350 billion floor for rung 3 and is less than the whole-company forecast.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate; translating it at NVIDIA's reported 92% Q1 Data Center mix implies about $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3. The forecast implied by the evidence is lower: v14's current total-revenue forecast is approximately $385.7 billion, and applying the roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate gives about $347.1 billion, below rung 3's $350 billion floor. Q1's $75.25 billion Data Center result and management's Q2 growth outlook support continued growth, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans calendar 2026 and 2027 and is not all FY2027 Data Center revenue. AMD's disclosed competing deployments and planned tens of billions of AI data-center revenue from 2027 temper the upside. The implied segment figure is below v14's total-revenue forecast, so it is consistent with the dependency, and remains in rung 4.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e204\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e296\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e630\\\", \\\"e631\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e634\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e638\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e758\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e760\\\", \\\"e761\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e852\\\", \\\"e858\\\", \\\"e870\\\", \\\"e871\\\", \\\"e880\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e902\\\"]\"], [8787, \"2026-07-25\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion is the market reference; applying the reported Q1 Data Center share of 92% gives about $361.6 billion, which is rung 3.\", \"About $357 billion: estimate Q2 Data Center revenue at roughly 92% of the $91 billion total-revenue guide, then carry the approximately 11.5% sequential growth implied by Q1 reported revenue and Q2 guidance forward through Q3 and Q4.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s reported Q1 Data Center mix of 92% implies about $361.6 billion, in rung 3. The guidance-anchored forecast is about $357 billion: infer Q2 Data Center revenue at roughly 92% of the $91 billion total-revenue guide, then carry the roughly 11.5% sequential growth implied by Q1 reported revenue and Q2 guidance through Q3 and Q4. That is above rung 3\\u2019s $350 billion floor and below $400 billion. It is consistent with v14\\u2019s $385.7 billion total-revenue forecast and does not treat the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure, which spans products and calendar years, as FY2027 Data Center revenue. AMD competition and the roughly 90% Data Center-share estimate temper the upside but do not outweigh the direct, current-quarter-guidance run-rate.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\"]\"], [8792, \"2026-07-26\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate, translated at the reported 92% Data Center mix, implies about $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue. That market reference sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $356 billion on the operating run-rate: $75.25 billion in Q1, about $83.7 billion for Q2 (92% of the $91 billion total-revenue guide), then two quarters carrying forward the approximately 11.5% sequential growth implied by that guide. The H2 outperformance view is upside evidence but lacks an absolute analyst baseline, so it does not support a defensible quantified adjustment.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate; applying the current 92% Data Center mix implies about $361.6 billion, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The operating run-rate is about $356 billion: $75.25 billion reported in Q1, then approximately $83.7 billion for Q2 using the $91 billion total-revenue guide and the Q1 92% mix, followed by Q3 and Q4 at the roughly 11.5% sequential growth implied by the Q2 total-revenue guide. Management's Q2 acceleration comment and SemiAnalysis's H2 upside view tilt higher, but neither supplies a Data Center amount that can be added reliably; the projection remains in rung 3. This is consistent with v14's $385.7 billion total-revenue forecast, since Data Center revenue cannot exceed total revenue. AMD's announced Helios demand and expected 2027 AI revenue are competitive offsets, but do not quantify an NVIDIA revenue reduction large enough to move the estimate below $350 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e828\\\"]\"], [8797, \"2026-07-27\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate, translated using the reported Data Center mix of about 92%, implies approximately $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue. That reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $357 billion for FY2027: $75.25 billion reported in Q1, about $83.9 billion estimated for Q2 using the $91 billion total-revenue guide and Q1 Data Center mix, then Q3 and Q4 estimated by carrying forward the guide-implied 11.5% sequential growth.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate; applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92% implies roughly $361.6 billion, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-based run-rate is about $357 billion: start with $75.25 billion of Data Center revenue in Q1, estimate Q2 at about $83.9 billion by applying the Q1 Data Center share to the $91 billion total-revenue guide, then carry forward the guide-implied 11.5% sequential growth for Q3 and Q4. This remains within rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion) and is below the current v14 total-revenue forecast of $385.7 billion. Management\\u2019s acceleration comment and SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% H2 upside view support the higher side, but the latter has no disclosed baseline and cannot be added mechanically without conflicting with the total-revenue dependency. Competition, China\\u2019s domestic-supplier shift, and memory tightness temper that upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e758\\\"]\"], [8800, \"2026-07-28\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, translated at NVIDIA\\u2019s latest reported 92% Data Center mix, implies about $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue. That reference sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $355 billion: start with $75.25 billion of Q1 Data Center revenue, estimate Q2 at 92% of the $91 billion total-revenue guide ($83.72 billion), then carry forward 11% sequential growth for Q3 and Q4 ($92.93 billion and $103.15 billion). The total is about $355.05 billion.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, which implies about $361.6 billion of Data Center revenue at the latest reported 92% mix; that reference is in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $355 billion, also in rung 3 and below the $400 billion boundary. Recent growth, Q2 guidance and the specific H2 upside view support the upper side, but the H2 view has no disclosed baseline to translate into dollars, while competition and export restrictions weigh against assuming a cross into rung 2. The current v14 total-revenue forecast of $385.7 billion also bounds Data Center revenue below $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e941\\\"]\"], [8804, \"2026-07-29\", 3, \"Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, translated at NVIDIA\\u2019s latest reported 92% Data Center mix to about $362 billion; this is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $355 billion: apply the latest reported 92% Data Center mix to the current FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $386 billion, which carries Q2 guidance forward at the guided growth run-rate.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $393 billion, which, at the reported 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $355 billion: the current total-revenue forecast is approximately $386 billion, and applying the latest reported 92% segment mix gives roughly $355 billion. Recent Q1 results and Q2 guidance support a strong run-rate, but the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans multiple products and calendar years, while AMD competition and the delayed Ohio project constrain the upside case. The implied figure remains above $350 billion and below the $400 billion rung boundary; the total-revenue forecast also caps Data Center revenue below $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1028\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e858\\\", \\\"e1125\\\", \\\"e1266\\\", \\\"e994\\\"]\"], [8808, \"2026-07-30\", 3, \"Market expectations are about $393 billion of FY2027 total revenue. Using the latest reported 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $355 billion: the current total-revenue forecast is about $386 billion, and applying the latest reported 92% Data Center share gives roughly $355 billion. This is above $350 billion and below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s latest reported 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $355 billion: the total-revenue dependency forecasts approximately $386 billion, and applying the latest reported 92% segment mix gives roughly $355 billion. Q2 guidance and management\\u2019s indication of accelerating Data Center growth support the run-rate, while the evidence does not quantify a mix change sufficient to move the estimate above $400 billion. The estimate remains above $350 billion and below $400 billion; the total-revenue forecast also caps Data Center revenue below $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e1291\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e1125\\\", \\\"e994\\\"]\"], [8813, \"2026-07-31\", 3, \"No standalone FY2027 Data Center consensus figure is supplied. The reference proxy is FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion, in rung 3; applying the latest reported 92% Data Center mix gives about $362 billion, also in rung 3.\", \"The current total-revenue dependency forecasts about $386 billion for FY2027; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s latest reported 92% Data Center share implies approximately $355 billion of Data Center revenue. That is above $350 billion and below $400 billion, so rung 3.\", \"The reference is FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion, which sits in rung 3 for this thesis when mapped using NVIDIA\\u2019s latest reported 92% Data Center mix: about $362 billion. The implied estimate is lower: the current total-revenue dependency forecasts about $386 billion, and applying the 92% mix gives about $355 billion. Q2\\u2019s $91 billion total-revenue guide and management\\u2019s statement that Data Center growth will accelerate support the run-rate, while SemiAnalysis\\u2019s forecast of 20% outperformance in second-half Data Center revenue is upside evidence without a disclosed baseline from which to calculate a revised FY2027 figure. The estimate remains above $350 billion and below $400 billion; competing AMD commitments and the author\\u2019s roughly 90% mix estimate temper the upside but do not establish a move below $350 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e109\\\"]\"], [8816, \"2026-08-01\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue expectations of about $393 billion, mapped at the latest reported 92% Data Center share to approximately $362 billion; this is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $355 billion: the current total-revenue dependency forecasts approximately $386 billion for FY2027, and applying NVIDIA's latest reported 92% Data Center share gives roughly $355.1 billion. The figure is above $350 billion and below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying the current 92% Data Center mix puts the reference at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue dependency forecasts about $386 billion, implying approximately $355 billion of Data Center revenue at that 92% mix. Q1 growth, the Q2 total-revenue guide, and SemiAnalysis\\u2019s H2 upside view support demand, but the H2 estimate has no disclosed baseline to calculate an increment; the newer demand and capacity evidence likewise does not quantify enough FY2027 NVIDIA revenue to move the implied figure across $350 billion or $400 billion. Competition and supply or timing risks temper the upside. The implied figure is below the $400 billion boundary and above $350 billion, so the forecast remains rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e638\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e761\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e1028\\\", \\\"e1125\\\", \\\"e1290\\\", \\\"e1291\\\", \\\"e1362\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1379\\\", \\\"e1380\\\"]\"], [8821, \"2026-08-03\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates of about $393 billion for NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue; using the latest reported 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $351 billion: the current dependency forecast of roughly $390 billion total FY2027 revenue multiplied by the third-party estimate that around 90% of FY2027 revenue will come from Data Center.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street estimates of about $393 billion of FY2027 total revenue. Applying the latest reported 92% Data Center mix gives about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The current total-revenue dependency forecasts roughly $390 billion; applying the specific view that Data Center will be around 90% of FY2027 revenue implies approximately $351 billion. Q1 results, management's Q2 growth guidance, and SemiAnalysis's quantified H2 upside view support the forecast, while AMD's announced deployments and other competition temper it. The implied figure is just above the $350 billion boundary, so it remains rung 3 and does not reach the $400 billion boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e638\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e880\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e1028\\\"]\"], [8825, \"2026-08-04\", 3, \"Bloomberg and Wall Street estimates are about $393 billion for total FY2027 revenue. Using the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate as a proxy gives about $354 billion, placing the reference at rung 3.\", \"About $351 billion: the current total-revenue dependency forecasts roughly $390 billion for FY2027, and applying the cited approximately 90% Data Center share gives about $351 billion. This is above the $350 billion rung boundary and below $400 billion.\", \"The reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the approximately 90% Data Center share cited for NVIDIA's FY2027/FY2028 revenue implies about $354 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3. The forecast uses the current total-revenue dependency of roughly $390 billion and the same 90% mix, implying about $351 billion\\u2014above the $350 billion boundary but below $400 billion. Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, management's Q2 revenue guide and comment that Data Center growth will accelerate, and SemiAnalysis's quantified H2 upside view support the run-rate. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility spans products and calendar years and is not assigned in full to FY2027; AMD deployments and China-related risks temper the forecast. The new SpaceX Rubin satellite plan adds only unquantified, conditional late-2027 demand, not enough to move the implied figure across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e1415\\\", \\\"e1416\\\", \\\"e1419\\\", \\\"e1410\\\", \\\"e1411\\\", \\\"e1412\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e289\\\"]\"], [8829, \"2026-08-05\", 3, \"The market reference is about $393 billion of FY2027 total revenue. Applying the approximately 90% Data Center share estimate for FY2027/FY2028 implies about $354 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3.\", \"About $354 billion: the total-revenue dependency forecasts roughly $393 billion for FY2027, and applying the approximately 90% Data Center share estimate gives $393 billion \\u00d7 90% \\u2248 $354 billion. Q1\\u2019s 92% actual share is supportive, but the 90% forward estimate is the basis for the FY2027 projection.\", \"The reference is approximately $393 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue; applying the cited estimate that about 90% of FY2027/FY2028 revenue will be Data Center gives roughly $354 billion, in rung 3. Q1\\u2019s 92% Data Center mix and management\\u2019s Q2 acceleration comment support that mix, while the total-revenue dependency\\u2019s forecast remains below $400 billion. New evidence of SpaceX\\u2019s planned 10 GW of compute capacity and exclusive NVIDIA use supports demand, but gives no NVIDIA revenue amount; AMD deployments, domestic Chinese chip adoption, and memory constraints offset some upside. The implied figure remains above the $350 billion boundary but does not cross $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e1438\\\", \\\"e1457\\\", \\\"e1447\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e1412\\\"]\"], [8835, \"2026-08-06\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus is stated. The available market reference is about $393 billion for NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue; applying the estimate that roughly 90% of FY2027/FY2028 revenue comes from Data Center implies about $354 billion, which is rung 3.\", \"Roughly $375\\u2013385 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue. This starts from the prior roughly $354 billion estimate, incorporates SemiAnalysis\\u2019s 20% second-half upside view directionally, and discounts the unquantified uplift so the segment remains below the $390\\u2013395 billion total-revenue forecast and leaves room for other segments.\", \"The available market reference is about $393 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The total-revenue dependency currently implies about $390\\u2013395 billion, so Data Center cannot exceed that whole. The quantified SemiAnalysis view that second-half Data Center revenue could beat estimates by 20% raises the segment forecast above the old roughly $354 billion calculation, but its baseline is not supplied and the uplift cannot be carried through in full without conflicting with the total-revenue constraint and leaving no room for other segments. The new SpaceX capacity disclosures support demand but do not state NVIDIA revenue or FY2027 shipment timing. On balance, roughly $375\\u2013385 billion is implied: above $350 billion, but below $400 billion, so the rung remains 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e156\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e638\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e758\\\", \\\"e759\\\", \\\"e761\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e1028\\\", \\\"e1125\\\", \\\"e1266\\\", \\\"e1290\\\", \\\"e1291\\\", \\\"e1362\\\", \\\"e1374\\\", \\\"e1376\\\", \\\"e1377\\\", \\\"e1378\\\", \\\"e1379\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1409\\\", \\\"e1411\\\", \\\"e1412\\\", \\\"e1415\\\", \\\"e1416\\\", \\\"e1417\\\", \\\"e1437\\\", \\\"e1438\\\", \\\"e1441\\\", \\\"e1442\\\", \\\"e1446\\\", \\\"e1447\\\", \\\"e1448\\\", \\\"e1449\\\", \\\"e1450\\\", \\\"e1456\\\", \\\"e1458\\\", \\\"e1467\\\", \\\"e1468\\\", \\\"e1472\\\", \\\"e1476\\\", \\\"e1477\\\", \\\"e1479\\\", \\\"e1480\\\", \\\"e1481\\\", \\\"e1482\\\", \\\"e1483\\\", \\\"e1484\\\", \\\"e1485\\\", \\\"e1486\\\", \\\"e1487\\\", \\\"e1488\\\", \\\"e1496\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\"]\"], [8838, \"2026-08-07\", 3, \"Approximately $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue, translated using the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate to about $354 billion; that reference is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $375\\u2013385 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue, with a central estimate near $380 billion. The updated $400\\u2013410 billion total-revenue forecast implies roughly $360\\u2013377 billion at a 90\\u201392% segment share, with the second-half outperformance view and demand disclosures supporting a modest uplift, subject to the total-revenue ceiling.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The updated total-revenue dependency implies approximately $400\\u2013410 billion for NVIDIA as a whole. Applying the roughly 90\\u201392% Data Center share indicated by the segment mix gives about $360\\u2013377 billion; the SemiAnalysis view that second-half Data Center revenue could beat estimates by 20%, plus demand and capacity disclosures, supports a modest uplift to roughly $375\\u2013385 billion, with a central estimate around $380 billion. That remains below the $400 billion rung boundary, and the segment forecast does not exceed the whole-company forecast. Competition and China-related risks temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e1496\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\"]\"], [8842, \"2026-08-08\", 3, \"About $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue, corresponding to an estimated Data Center reference near $354 billion using an approximately 90% segment share; this is rung 3.\", \"The current whole-company forecast of about $405\\u2013415 billion, multiplied by a 90\\u201392% Data Center share, implies roughly $365\\u2013382 billion before an uplift. Q1 growth, the SemiAnalysis second-half estimate, and customer-demand evidence support a central estimate around $390 billion, still below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast is about $405\\u2013415 billion. Applying a 90\\u201392% Data Center share gives roughly $365\\u2013382 billion; Q1\\u2019s strong growth, the SemiAnalysis estimate of a 20% second-half beat versus estimates, and evidence of additional customer demand support an uplift to a central estimate around $390 billion. That remains below the $400 billion boundary, so the forecast stays in rung 3. SpaceX\\u2019s GPU commitments support demand but do not state NVIDIA revenue, and AMD competition, China-related risks, and supply constraints temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e1496\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\"]\"], [8846, \"2026-08-09\", 3, \"Wall Street's average FY2027 total-revenue estimate is about $393 billion. Applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, which is rung 3.\", \"About $374 billion: the current dependency forecast of about $415 billion of total FY2027 revenue multiplied by the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate. The 92% share reported for Q1 would imply about $382 billion if sustained, still below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast is about $415 billion. Applying the explicit roughly 90% FY2027/FY2028 Data Center share estimate gives about $374 billion; the latest Q1 mix was higher at 92%, which would imply about $382 billion if sustained. Accelerating growth guidance, the second-half estimate-beat forecast, and disclosed customer commitments support the upper part of that range, while those commitments do not all translate into NVIDIA FY2027 segment revenue, and memory constraints and competition temper the upside. Even the 92% case remains below the $400 billion rung boundary, so the forecast stays in rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e1496\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1445\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e117\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e109\\\"]\"], [8850, \"2026-08-10\", 3, \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue. Applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $378 billion: applying a 90%\\u201392% Data Center share to dependency v14's approximately $415 billion total-revenue forecast gives roughly $374\\u2013$382 billion, with the central estimate near $378 billion. The reported $75.25 billion Q1 Data Center revenue, Q2 total-revenue guide and accelerating-growth guidance support the run-rate, but do not establish a segment estimate above $400 billion.\", \"The evidence still points to the neutral band. The implied central estimate is about $378 billion, above the market-derived reference but below the $400 billion boundary. The newly disclosed SpaceX capacity agreements add customer-demand support, but they give no NVIDIA revenue amount or FY2027 deployment schedule; the $500 billion financing initiative is likewise for AI infrastructure over time, not specified FY2027 NVIDIA revenue. The $1 trillion Blackwell and Rubin figure spans products and calendar years and is not all Data Center revenue in FY2027. Strong current demand and the second-half estimate-beat view are tempered by memory constraints and competition. The implied segment amount remains below the $415 billion total-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e670\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1543\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e304\\\"]\"], [8854, \"2026-08-11\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus is about $393 billion (v14), in rung 3. Using NVIDIA\\u2019s latest 92.3% Data Center mix as a translation gives a Data Center reference of roughly $363 billion, also rung 3.\", \"Approximately $378 billion: apply the latest reported 92.3% Data Center share to v14\\u2019s approximately $410 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast ($410 billion \\u00d7 92.3% \\u2248 $378 billion). This is below the $400 billion boundary and consistent with the whole-company forecast.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion, which v14 places in rung 3; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s latest 92.3% Data Center mix gives a market-reference equivalent of about $363 billion, also rung 3. The evidence supports a higher forecast than that reference: Q1 Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion, up 92% year over year, management guided to accelerating Q2 Data Center growth, and current total-revenue guidance and the v14 forecast imply a strong full-year run-rate. But carrying the latest 92.3% mix across v14\\u2019s approximately $410 billion total-revenue forecast implies about $378 billion of Data Center revenue\\u2014above the reference, but still below the $400 billion rung boundary. Customer demand and capacity financing add upside, while memory constraints, competition, and uncertainty about how much financing converts into NVIDIA revenue temper it. The implied amount remains below the dependency\\u2019s total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1477\\\", \\\"e1479\\\", \\\"e1549\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e1710\\\", \\\"e1711\\\", \\\"e1530\\\"]\"], [8860, \"2026-08-12\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion, converted using the latest 92.3% Data Center mix to about $363 billion; this sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"About $378 billion: v14\\u2019s approximately $410 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast multiplied by the latest reported Data Center mix of 92.3%.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion, equivalent to roughly $363 billion of Data Center revenue at the latest reported 92.3% mix; that is rung 3. The evidence-supported total-revenue forecast in v14 is about $410 billion, which implies roughly $378 billion of Data Center revenue at the same mix. That is above the reference but remains within rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). New, quantified SpaceX capacity and spending claims add upside, but they are not firm NVIDIA procurement amounts; the $500 billion financing figure has no deployment timetable, and the dependent total-revenue forecast remains about $410 billion. The evidence therefore does not imply a move across the $400 billion boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e1530\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e1472\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1549\\\", \\\"e1609\\\", \\\"e1710\\\", \\\"e1711\\\", \\\"e1718\\\"]\"], [8864, \"2026-08-13\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion; at NVIDIA\\u2019s recent Data Center mix of about 92%, that implies roughly $362 billion of Data Center revenue, in rung 3.\", \"About $368\\u2013377 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue: the current v14 total-revenue forecast of $400\\u2013410 billion multiplied by the recent reported Data Center share of about 92%. This is a forecast allocation of the whole-company outlook, not an annualization of Q1 alone.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg/Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion. Applying NVIDIA\\u2019s recent Data Center mix of about 92% puts the segment reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is about $400\\u2013410 billion; applying the recent 92% Data Center mix implies approximately $368\\u2013377 billion, also rung 3. Q1\\u2019s reported growth and Q2 guidance support a strong trajectory, while the quantified upside from demand and financing-related announcements is not enough to take the implied segment figure above $400 billion: many announced amounts span periods or products, and several are prospective rather than firm NVIDIA revenue. Competitive, China and supply risks also temper the forecast. The implied Data Center revenue remains below the rung-2 boundary and cannot exceed the dependent whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e1477\\\", \\\"e1479\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1438\\\", \\\"e1457\\\", \\\"e1710\\\", \\\"e1711\\\", \\\"e1545\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e1472\\\"]\"], [8868, \"2026-08-14\", 3, \"FY2027 total-revenue market consensus is about $393 billion (rung 3); applying NVIDIA\\u2019s recent 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, also rung 3.\", \"About $368\\u2013377 billion, with a central estimate near $373 billion: dependency v14 forecasts total FY2027 revenue of about $400\\u2013410 billion, and the reported Q1 Data Center share is about 92% ($75.25 billion of $81.6 billion).\", \"The market reference is FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s recent 92% Data Center mix gives about $362 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is about $400\\u2013410 billion. Applying the reported 92% Data Center mix implies roughly $368\\u2013377 billion, still rung 3. Q1 growth, Q2 guidance and management\\u2019s expectation of accelerating Data Center growth support that run-rate. Multi-year or unsized demand and financing signals add upside, but do not imply enough FY2027 NVIDIA segment revenue to cross $400 billion; China\\u2019s zero-shipment quarter and growing AMD competition are offsets.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e307\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e1477\\\", \\\"e1479\\\", \\\"e1570\\\", \\\"e1438\\\", \\\"e1711\\\", \\\"e1549\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e1447\\\", \\\"e1448\\\", \\\"e638\\\"]\"], [8872, \"2026-08-15\", 3, \"FY2027 total-revenue consensus is about $393 billion. Applying the reported 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, in rung 3.\", \"The current FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14 is about $400\\u2013405 billion; applying the reported 92% Data Center mix implies approximately $368\\u2013373 billion. That remains below $400 billion and within rung 3.\", \"The market reference is FY2027 total-revenue consensus of about $393 billion; applying the reported 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, which is rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is about $400\\u2013405 billion. Carrying forward the observed 92% segment mix implies approximately $368\\u2013373 billion, also rung 3. Recent demand and SpaceX-related signals add upside, but their disclosed amounts cover multi-period financing, capacity or infrastructure spending\\u2014not NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center revenue dollar for dollar. At the dependency forecast, crossing into rung 2 would require Data Center to exceed roughly 98.8% of total revenue at the top of the range; the evidence does not support that mix.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e294\\\", \\\"e633\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e941\\\", \\\"e1028\\\", \\\"e1125\\\", \\\"e1290\\\", \\\"e1380\\\", \\\"e1411\\\", \\\"e1412\\\", \\\"e1438\\\", \\\"e1477\\\", \\\"e1479\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1545\\\", \\\"e1682\\\", \\\"e1686\\\", \\\"e1711\\\", \\\"e1719\\\", \\\"e1764\\\", \\\"e1766\\\", \\\"e1841\\\", \\\"e1858\\\", \\\"e1860\\\", \\\"e1867\\\", \\\"e1868\\\", \\\"e1876\\\", \\\"e1879\\\"]\"], [8877, \"2026-08-16\", 3, \"FY2027 total-revenue estimates near $393 billion imply about $362 billion of Data Center revenue using the observed 92% mix; that reference is rung 3.\", \"About $364 billion before an upside allowance: the current v14 forecast of roughly $396 billion in total FY2027 revenue multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center mix gives about $364 billion. The SemiAnalysis forecast of H2 Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates lifts the outlook, but does not support a Data Center figure at or above the current total-revenue forecast.\", \"The market reference for Data Center is approximately $362 billion: FY2027 total-revenue estimates near $393 billion, multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center mix, place it in rung 3. The evidence supports growth above that reference, but the current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is about $396 billion. At the observed mix that implies about $364 billion; even allowing for the specific H2 upside view, the segment remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. A Data Center outcome of $400 billion or more would exceed the current forecast for NVIDIA's total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e227\\\", \\\"e289\\\", \\\"e1448\\\"]\"], [8880, \"2026-08-17\", 3, \"Market estimates put FY2027 total NVIDIA revenue at about $393 billion; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $360 billion, derived from v14's current approximately $391 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast multiplied by the 92% Data Center mix reported for Q1. The H2 view of 20% outperformance versus analyst estimates is positive but supplies no absolute figure to replace that estimate.\", \"The market reference is about $362 billion ($393 billion of FY2027 total-revenue estimates multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center mix), which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $360 billion: the current total-revenue forecast in dependency v14 is about $391 billion, and applying the observed 92% Data Center share gives approximately $360 billion. Q1 Data Center growth, Q2 guidance and the H2 outperformance view support growth, but the H2 view gives no absolute amount and the forecast remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. Competition and the absence of China revenue from the outlook temper the upside; the announced Ohio capacity is phased to begin in 2028, so it does not justify counting that buildout as FY2027 revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e1472\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e828\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e1953\\\"]\"], [8884, \"2026-08-18\", 3, \"About $362 billion, derived from the roughly $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate multiplied by the 92% observed Data Center mix; this sits in rung 3.\", \"About $360 billion at the baseline: the v14 forecast of roughly $391 billion total revenue multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center share. The H2 outperformance view raises the upside, but the v14 total-revenue forecast caps Data Center revenue at $391 billion, still below the $400 billion boundary.\", \"The market reference is about $362 billion (FY2027 total-revenue estimates of about $393 billion multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center mix), which is rung 3. The current baseline is about $360 billion: the v14 FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $391 billion multiplied by the 92% mix. Q1 growth, accelerating Q2 guidance, and SemiAnalysis' view that H2 Data Center revenue could exceed analyst estimates by 20% tilt the forecast upward, but no absolute H2 amount is supplied. Even a generous uplift is constrained by v14's $391 billion total-revenue forecast: Data Center revenue cannot exceed total revenue, so it cannot reach the $400 billion rung boundary. The PORTS-Pike capacity is scheduled to come online in phases beginning in 2028, so it does not justify counting that buildout as FY2027 revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e1472\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e1953\\\"]\"], [8890, \"2026-08-19\", 3, \"Market expectations are about $393 billion for FY2027 total revenue, which is rung 3 for total revenue; at the observed 92% Data Center mix, the comparable Data Center reference is about $362 billion, rung 3.\", \"About $371 billion: v14 forecasts roughly $403.5 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying NVIDIA\\u2019s observed 92% Data Center share gives approximately $403.5 billion \\u00d7 92% = $371 billion.\", \"The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix puts the reference near $362 billion, in rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast is about $403.5 billion (v14); applying the observed 92% mix implies about $371 billion of Data Center revenue, also rung 3. Q1\\u2019s 92% year-over-year Data Center growth, accelerating Q2 growth guidance, and SemiAnalysis\\u2019 H2 outperformance view support upside, but the latter gives no absolute H2 amount. At the current total-revenue forecast, Data Center would need to be over 99% of revenue to reach $400 billion, versus the observed 92% mix. SpaceX\\u2019s large compute target is a potential demand source, but the chip-spending estimate is not a sized NVIDIA FY2027 revenue commitment. The PORTS-Pike capacity is expected to come online in phases beginning in 2028, so it does not warrant counting that buildout in FY2027.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e1472\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e2115\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e1876\\\", \\\"e1877\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e1942\\\"]\"], [8894, \"2026-08-20\", 3, \"Bloomberg/Yahoo! Finance's approximately $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate is the reference. It is rung 3 for total revenue; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix gives approximately $362 billion, rung 3 on this ladder.\", \"The v14 total-revenue forecast of approximately $403.5 billion, applied at the observed 92% Data Center mix, implies about $371 billion for the segment. The quantified 20% H2 outperformance view is an upside risk, but its H2 base is unstated and applying it mechanically would imply about $413 billion\\u2014more than the supplied whole-company forecast.\", \"The market reference is approximately $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue, which sits in rung 3; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix implies about $362 billion, also rung 3. The current total-revenue forecast is about $403.5 billion (v14); at the observed 92% mix that implies about $371 billion of Data Center revenue. SemiAnalysis\\u2019 forecast that second-half Data Center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% is a meaningful upside signal, but it gives no absolute H2 base. Applying 20% to the roughly $209 billion H2 Data Center amount implied by v14 would put the segment near $413 billion\\u2014above the supplied whole-company revenue forecast. Without a corresponding change to that whole forecast or evidence that the segment\\u2019s share rises sharply from 92%, I do not carry that full uplift into this thesis. SpaceX\\u2019s chip-spending estimate and the PORTS-Pike capacity are not sized FY2027 NVIDIA revenue: the former targets end-2027 compute, while PORTS-Pike capacity is expected to come online beginning in 2028. The forecast therefore remains below the $400 billion rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2146\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e1972\\\", \\\"e1877\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e1953\\\", \\\"e2115\\\"]\"], [8898, \"2026-08-21\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus figure is supplied. The proxy market reference is the $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection in v14 times the observed 92% Data Center mix, or about $362 billion\\u2014rung 3.\", \"About $371\\u2013372 billion: v14\\u2019s roughly $403\\u2013404 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast multiplied by NVIDIA\\u2019s reported 92% Q1 FY2027 Data Center share. This falls in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"The market reference is about $362 billion: the Bloomberg-compiled FY2027 total-revenue projection of $393 billion, multiplied by the observed 92% Data Center mix, places the reference in rung 3. The forecast implied by the evidence remains about $371 billion: v14 forecasts roughly $403\\u2013404 billion of total FY2027 revenue, and applying the 92% mix reported for Q1 gives about $371\\u2013372 billion. That is above $350 billion but below $400 billion, so it remains rung 3. SemiAnalysis\\u2019 20% H2 upside view and SpaceX-related demand estimates support upside risk, but do not establish an absolute FY2027 segment amount that can override the whole-company forecast; the announced Ohio capacity is expected to start coming online in 2028. Competition and component availability are counterweights. The implied segment figure is also below the v14 whole-company forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e1876\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e2146\\\"]\"], [8902, \"2026-08-22\", 3, \"About $362 billion, derived from Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center mix; this is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $371\\u2013372 billion: the current FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $403\\u2013404 billion (v14), multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center share, implies this segment amount.\", \"The reference is about $362 billion: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus, multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center mix, sits in rung 3. The forecast remains about $371\\u2013372 billion: the current whole-company forecast is approximately $403\\u2013404 billion, and applying the 92% reported mix gives that segment estimate. It is above $350 billion but below $400 billion. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin visibility and SpaceX-related spending estimates support upside, but span multiple periods and are not FY2027 NVIDIA Data Center revenue in hand; SemiAnalysis's 20% H2 upside view has no stated absolute baseline. The $91 billion Q2 company guide is a counterweight, and the whole-company forecast leaves only about $3\\u20134 billion of headroom above a $400 billion segment result, so the evidence does not imply the near-100% segment share required to cross that boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e1942\\\"]\"], [8905, \"2026-08-23\", 3, \"Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus, converted using the reported 92% Data Center mix, implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $372 billion: the current whole-company forecast of roughly $404 billion in v14 multiplied by the reported 92% Data Center share. This is a stable-mix base estimate; the new upside signals lack a quantifiable FY2027 revenue increment.\", \"The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus, translated at the reported 92% Data Center share to about $362 billion; that is rung 3. The current whole-company forecast in dependency v14 is roughly $404 billion. Applying the reported 92% mix gives about $372 billion of Data Center revenue, still below the $400 billion rung-2 threshold. Moving above $400 billion would require Data Center to be about 99% of the $404 billion whole, well above the reported mix. The SemiAnalysis view of 20% H2 outperformance and the SpaceX chip-spending estimate add upside, but neither gives a usable FY2027 NVIDIA revenue increment; several large capacity plans are also timed for 2028. They do not outweigh the whole-company constraint or justify crossing a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e1876\\\", \\\"e1457\\\", \\\"e1497\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e1953\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e2179\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e1972\\\", \\\"e2221\\\", \\\"e2222\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\"]\"], [8912, \"2026-08-24\", 3, \"Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue consensus is $393 billion (v14); applying the reported 92% Data Center share implies about $362 billion of Data Center revenue, which sits in rung 3.\", \"About $370 billion: use $75.25 billion reported in Q1 and the $85.14 billion Q2 Data Center consensus, then apply the recent 92% Data Center mix to v14's roughly $107.5 billion Q3 and $120 billion Q4 total-revenue forecast. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary.\", \"The reference is Bloomberg's FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $393 billion, translated using NVIDIA's reported 92% Data Center mix to about $362 billion; that is rung 3. The forecast is higher, but remains below $400 billion: strong Q1 results and a $85.14 billion Q2 Data Center consensus are partly offset by competition and the fact that several large capacity plans are not expected to come online until 2028. The implied estimate therefore does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e255\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e171\\\", \\\"e2358\\\", \\\"e1498\\\", \\\"e2153\\\", \\\"e994\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e891\\\", \\\"e1942\\\", \\\"e1953\\\", \\\"e2179\\\", \\\"e2221\\\", \\\"e2312\\\", \\\"e2313\\\"]\"], [8916, \"2026-08-25\", 3, \"The latest supplied FY2027 market estimate is $396 billion for NVIDIA total revenue. Applying the reported 92% Q1 Data Center share gives an approximate Data Center reference of $364 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $370\\u2013375 billion for FY2027: use reported Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, the roughly $85\\u201386 billion Q2 estimates, then carry approximately 14% sequential growth forward through Q3 and Q4. This remains below the dependent FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $404\\u2013405 billion.\", \"The market reference translates to about $364 billion of Data Center revenue ($396 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate \\u00d7 the reported 92% Q1 Data Center mix), which is rung 3. The quarterly run-rate implies roughly $370\\u2013375 billion: Q1 was $75.25 billion, Q2 estimates are about $85\\u201386 billion, and carrying roughly 14% sequential growth forward for Q3 and Q4 gives approximately $370 billion. That is above the reference but remains within rung 3\\u2019s $350\\u2013400 billion range. It is also below the dependent FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $404\\u2013405 billion. Competition, export-policy risks and memory constraints temper the upside; the $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and periods and is not a direct FY2027 Data Center revenue estimate.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e2420\\\", \\\"e2418\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e2228\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e116\\\", \\\"e2358\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e994\\\"]\"], [8921, \"2026-08-26\", 3, \"About $364 billion, derived from the dependent FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $396 billion multiplied by the reported 92% Q1 Data Center mix; this is rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion for FY2027: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 (the $108 billion total-revenue guidance at the Q2 Data Center mix of about 92.5%), and about $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 total-revenue guidance.\", \"The market reference is about $364 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue: the dependent thesis\\u2019s $396 billion FY2027 total-revenue estimate multiplied by NVIDIA\\u2019s reported 92% Q1 Data Center mix. That is rung 3. The reported Q2 Data Center revenue was $89 billion on $96.2 billion total revenue, a 92.5% mix. Applying that mix to the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guidance implies about $99.9 billion of Q3 Data Center revenue; carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 total-revenue guidance implies about $112.2 billion in Q4. With Q1 at $75.25 billion, that gives roughly $376 billion for FY2027\\u2014above the reference but still within rung 3. This remains below the dependent FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $407 billion. Rubin shipments and broad demand support the outlook, but the Rubin Q3 contribution is not a separate incremental revenue figure, and NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2028 supply constraint and zero-China forward outlook temper upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2510\\\"]\"], [8925, \"2026-08-27\", 3, \"The market reference is about $364 billion, derived from the LSEG FY2027 total-revenue estimate of $396 billion and NVIDIA's reported approximately 92% Data Center mix; this is rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion: add Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and Q2 revenue of $89 billion, estimate Q3 at about $99.9 billion by applying Q2's roughly 92.5% Data Center mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guidance, then carry the guidance-implied 12.3% sequential growth into Q4 for about $112.2 billion.\", \"The implied FY2027 Data Center revenue is about $376 billion, above the $364 billion reference but still below the $400 billion boundary for rung 2. The recently reported Q2 result and Q3 total-revenue guidance support strong growth, but carrying forward the guided growth does not produce enough revenue to cross that boundary. The estimate is consistent with v14's current approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast: the Data Center estimate remains below the whole-company total. Rubin shipments and purchase orders support demand, but the stated Q3 Rubin share is part of Data Center revenue, not incremental to it.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2483\\\"]\"], [8929, \"2026-08-28\", 3, \"The supplied market reference is approximately $364 billion, which sits on rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue totaled about $164.3 billion ($75.25 billion plus $89 billion). Applying the observed Q2 Data Center share of 92.5% to Q3 revenue guidance of $108 billion implies about $99.9 billion for Q3; carrying that roughly 12.2% sequential growth forward implies about $112.1 billion for Q4, or approximately $376 billion for FY2027.\", \"The implied outcome remains within rung 3: the sequential-growth carry-forward puts FY2027 Data Center revenue near $376 billion, above the $350 billion floor but below $400 billion. Q4 hyperscale reacceleration is a positive but unquantified input, so it does not support a numerical forecast above that carry-forward; the dependent whole-company forecast of about $407 billion also accommodates this segment estimate.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2572\\\"]\"], [8932, \"2026-08-29\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center annual consensus figure is supplied. The available Morningstar view says FY2027 Data Center revenue should be well over $300 billion, which only places its stated floor at rung 4; it does not specify a unique rung. The $85.14 billion consensus is for Q2, not the fiscal year. The LSEG $396 billion reference in v14 is for total FY2027 revenue, sits at rung 3 on that separate ladder, and is not a direct Data Center reference.\", \"About $376 billion: add reported Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion and Q2 revenue of $89.0 billion to implied Q3 revenue of about $99.9 billion ($108 billion total guidance \\u00d7 the Q2 Data Center mix of 92.5%), then carry the roughly 12.2% sequential Data Center growth into Q4, implying about $112.1 billion. This is below the $400 billion boundary and below the dependent whole-company forecast of about $407 billion.\", \"The implied estimate remains in rung 3. The new Q2 actual and Q3 guidance give a more direct run-rate than the prior estimate; the implied Q4 carry-forward lifts the full-year figure only to about $376 billion, still below the $400 billion rung boundary. Q4 hyperscale growth is expected to reaccelerate, but no amount is given, so it does not justify adding a quantified uplift to the run-rate. The estimate is also consistent with the dependent whole-company forecast of about $407 billion: Data Center is a component and the estimate does not exceed that total.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2115\\\", \\\"e2333\\\"]\"], [8938, \"2026-08-30\", 3, \"LSEG's $396 billion and Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus (v14), translated at NVIDIA's approximately 92.5% Q2 Data Center mix (e3216), implies about $364\\u2013366 billion of Data Center revenue: rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion for FY2027: $75.25 billion Q1 actual + $89.0 billion Q2 actual + approximately $99.9 billion Q3 (92.5% of the $108 billion total-revenue guidance) + approximately $112.2 billion Q4 (carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance).\", \"The market reference is LSEG's $396 billion and Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus, both stated in v14. Applying the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% gives roughly $364\\u2013366 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, then about $99.9 billion in Q3 by applying the Q2 Data Center mix to NVIDIA's $108 billion total-revenue guidance, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by that guidance. This is above the consensus-derived reference but remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. Rubin production shipments, purchase orders, and the expected 20% Q3 Data Center contribution support the run-rate; however, Q4 reacceleration is not quantified, supply is constrained, and the large AWS GPU deployment spans 2027\\u20132028 rather than establishing enough FY2027 revenue to bridge the roughly $24 billion gap to rung 2. The estimate is consistent with v14's approximately $407 billion whole-company forecast: Data Center remains below the total.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e2839\\\"]\"], [8943, \"2026-08-31\", 3, \"LSEG's $396 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus (v14) is the available market reference; it sits in rung 3 for total revenue. Applying the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% gives an approximate Data Center reference of $366 billion, in rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion: $75.25 billion Q1 actual + $89.0 billion Q2 actual + about $99.9 billion Q3 (92.5% of the $108 billion total-revenue guide) + about $112.2 billion Q4 (carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by the Q3 total-revenue guide). This is consistent with v14's approximately $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $396 billion, which v14 places in rung 3 for total revenue. Applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% gives an approximate Data Center reference of $366 billion, also rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, then about $99.9 billion in Q3 by applying the Q2 Data Center mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guide, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by the Q3 total-revenue guide. That remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. Strong demand, Rubin shipments and the expected Q3 Rubin contribution support the run-rate, but supply constraints and competition temper upside; the estimate is consistent with v14's roughly $407.1 billion total-revenue forecast and does not exceed the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e2510\\\"]\"], [8946, \"2026-09-01\", 3, \"LSEG FY2027 total-revenue consensus is $396 billion; applying the latest reported 92.5% Data Center mix gives an approximate Data Center reference of $366 billion, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376.4 billion: $75.25 billion reported in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using Q2's 92.5% Data Center mix on the $108 billion total-revenue guide, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by that guide.\", \"The market reference is approximately $366 billion, derived by applying the latest reported Data Center mix of 92.5% to the LSEG FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $396 billion; that reference sits in rung 3. The run-rate estimate is about $376.4 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, approximately $99.9 billion in Q3 using the Q2 Data Center mix on the $108 billion total-revenue guide, and approximately $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying forward the 12.3% sequential growth implied by that guide. This is consistent with the total-revenue dependency\\u2019s approximately $407.1 billion forecast and remains below the $400 billion boundary. Rubin shipments and orders, accelerating demand, and announced future deployments support the outlook, but the multi-period deployment plans are not all FY2027 revenue; competition and supply constraints temper the upside. The evidence therefore does not move the forecast across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3224\\\", \\\"e3234\\\"]\"], [8950, \"2026-09-02\", 3, \"About $366 billion, derived from the LSEG FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $396 billion multiplied by the latest reported Data Center mix of 92.5%; this sits in rung 3.\", \"About $376.4 billion: $75.25 billion Q1 + $89.0 billion Q2 + an estimated $99.9 billion Q3 (92.5% of the $108 billion total-revenue guide) + an estimated $112.2 billion Q4 (carrying forward 12.3% sequential growth).\", \"The market reference is about $366 billion: applying the latest reported 92.5% Data Center mix to the $396 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus puts the reference in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376.4 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2; estimate Q3 at $99.9 billion by applying the reported 92.5% mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guide; then estimate Q4 at $112.2 billion by carrying forward 12.3% sequential growth. This is below $400 billion, so it remains rung 3. It is consistent with the dependency forecast of about $407.1 billion in total revenue and leaves room for non-Data-Center revenue. Rubin's expected Q3 contribution and the expected Q4 hyperscale reacceleration support the run-rate, while the no-China outlook and supply constraints temper upside; neither quantifies a change large enough to cross a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2572\\\"]\"], [8952, \"2026-09-03\", 3, \"LSEG analysts' FY2027 total-revenue consensus is $396 billion. Applying the latest reported Data Center share of roughly 92.5% implies about $366.3 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3.\", \"About $376.4 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2; estimate Q3 at about $99.9 billion by applying Q2's 92.5% Data Center mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guide; estimate Q4 at about $112.2 billion by applying that mix to the dependency's approximately $121.3 billion Q4 total-revenue forecast.\", \"The market reference is about $366.3 billion: applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% to the $396 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus puts the reference in rung 3. That reference alone does not determine the forecast. Using the reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue, applying the Q2 mix to the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide, and carrying the dependency's Q4 total-revenue run-rate forward at that mix implies about $376.4 billion for FY2027. This is consistent with the total-revenue dependency and leaves about $30.7 billion for non-Data-Center revenue. The implication remains below the $400 billion boundary, so it stays in rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2562\\\"]\"], [8956, \"2026-09-04\", 3, \"LSEG FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $396 billion, translated at the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $366 billion of Data Center revenue. This is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376.4 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue sum to $164.25 billion; applying the Q2 mix to Q3's $108 billion total-revenue guide implies about $99.9 billion, and applying it to the dependency's $121.3 billion Q4 run-rate implies about $112.2 billion. The resulting FY2027 estimate is below $400 billion.\", \"The market reference is approximately $366 billion: applying the latest reported Data Center mix of 92.5% to the LSEG FY2027 total-revenue consensus of $396 billion places it in rung 3. For the forecast, Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; applying the Q2 mix to NVIDIA's $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide gives about $99.9 billion, and applying that mix to the dependency's $121.3 billion Q4 run-rate gives about $112.2 billion for Q4, or approximately $376.4 billion for FY2027. That is consistent with the $407.1 billion total-revenue dependency forecast and leaves about $30.7 billion of non-Data-Center revenue. Q4 hyperscaler reacceleration and the Rubin ramp are upside risks, but no supplied amount supports lifting the implied FY2027 Data Center total to the $400 billion rung boundary; conversely, the no-China outlook limits upside. The forecast therefore remains in rung 3.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e636\\\"]\"], [8960, \"2026-09-05\", 3, \"Approximately $366 billion, derived from the LSEG FY2027 total-revenue expectation of $396 billion and the latest Data Center share of 92.5%; this reference is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376 billion: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; applying the latest 92.5% mix to Q3's $108 billion total-revenue guide implies about $99.9 billion, and applying it to the dependency's approximately $121.3 billion Q4 run-rate implies about $112.2 billion. The result is below the dependency's approximately $407.1 billion total-revenue forecast and falls in rung 3.\", \"The reference is approximately $366 billion: the prior market reference of $396 billion for FY2027 total revenue, multiplied by the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, places it in rung 3. The evidence-based forecast is about $376 billion, also rung 3. While new Rubin and hyperscaler-demand signals strengthen the upside case, the H2 outperformance estimate has no stated analyst-estimate base to translate into dollars, and the dependency forecast for total FY2027 revenue constrains the Data Center segment to be no more than the whole. The implied Data Center figure remains below $400 billion and above $350 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\"]\"], [8964, \"2026-09-06\", 3, \"Approximately $380 billion, derived from the latest supplied FY2027 total-revenue expectation of about $411 billion (v14) multiplied by the reported 92.5% Data Center share (e3216); this is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $377 billion. Applying the latest 92.5% Data Center mix to v14's $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast gives about $376.6 billion; the quarter-by-quarter run-rate is consistent with this: $75.25 billion in Q1, $89.0 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using $108 billion total-revenue guidance and the recent mix, and about $112 billion in Q4 carrying forward the dependency's guided growth.\", \"The market reference is approximately $380 billion: the latest FY2027 total-revenue expectation supplied by dependency v14 is about $411 billion, and applying NVIDIA's latest reported Data Center mix of 92.5% gives about $380 billion, which is rung 3. The evidence-based forecast is about $377 billion: use v14's $407.1 billion whole-company forecast and apply the 92.5% Data Center mix. This is consistent with $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89.0 billion in Q2, approximately $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guidance and the latest mix, and about $112 billion in Q4 carrying forward the dependency's guided-growth run-rate. It remains below $400 billion and above $350 billion, so the reference and implied outcome both sit at rung 3. Strong demand and Rubin ramp signals support the forecast, while supply constraints, no China compute revenue in the forward outlook, and competition limit the upside; none of the evidence or dependency conclusions implies a Data Center total at or above $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e170\\\", \\\"e304\\\", \\\"e636\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e2115\\\", \\\"e2333\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2839\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e3224\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e3751\\\", \\\"e3753\\\", \\\"e3805\\\", \\\"e3839\\\", \\\"e3845\\\", \\\"e3885\\\", \\\"e3886\\\", \\\"e3918\\\", \\\"e3920\\\", \\\"e3922\\\", \\\"e3923\\\", \\\"e3924\\\", \\\"e3925\\\", \\\"e3926\\\", \\\"e3927\\\", \\\"e3975\\\", \\\"e3976\\\", \\\"e3978\\\", \\\"e4002\\\", \\\"e4041\\\", \\\"e4046\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e4073\\\", \\\"e4077\\\", \\\"e4078\\\", \\\"e4091\\\", \\\"e4093\\\", \\\"e4094\\\"]\"], [8969, \"2026-09-07\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus is supplied. The comparison reference is the latest Wall Street FY2027 total-revenue expectation of about $411 billion from v14, translated using the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% to approximately $380 billion; that is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\", \"Approximately $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89.0 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion for Q3 ($108 billion total guidance \\u00d7 92.5%), and about $112.2 billion for Q4 ($121.3 billion from v14 \\u00d7 92.5%).\", \"The market reference is about $380 billion for Data Center: the latest FY2027 total-revenue expectation supplied by v14 is about $411 billion (rung 2 on the total-revenue ladder), and applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% places the reference in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; Q3 is about $99.9 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guidance and the latest 92.5% mix; and Q4 is about $112.2 billion by applying that mix to v14's $121.3 billion Q4 total-revenue forecast. The implied FY2027 total is below $400 billion and above $350 billion, so it remains rung 3. Rubin's expected 20% Q3 contribution and Q4 acceleration support the growth outlook, but are not additional revenue on top of the segment total; AWS's 2027\\u201328 GPU plan is not allocated to FY2027. The estimate is below v14's $407.1 billion whole-company forecast, as required.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e976\\\"]\"], [8974, \"2026-09-08\", 3, \"The latest stated Wall Street expectation is about $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue. Using the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% implies approximately $380 billion of Data Center revenue, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376 billion for FY2027: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; estimate Q3 at about $99.9 billion by applying the latest 92.5% mix to the $108 billion total-revenue guide; then estimate Q4 at about $112.2 billion by carrying forward the roughly 12.3% sequential total-revenue growth implied by the guide and applying the same mix. This is below $400 billion and consistent with v14's approximately $407.1 billion whole-company forecast.\", \"The market reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% implies roughly $380 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3. The updated evidence-based estimate is also in rung 3. Q3 and Q4 estimates remain below the $400 billion boundary, and the dependent total-revenue forecast is about $407.1 billion, so Data Center revenue cannot exceed that whole. Strong growth, Rubin shipments, and demand support the forecast, but the unquantified Q4 acceleration does not justify adding a specific amount to the run-rate; supply limits, competition, and no China compute revenue in the outlook also temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e2115\\\"]\"], [8978, \"2026-09-09\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus figure is supplied. The market reference is approximately $411B of FY2027 total revenue; applying the latest reported Data Center share of about 92.5% gives approximately $380B, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376B: $75.25B in Q1 plus $89B in Q2, about $99.9B in Q3 (the $108B total-revenue guide at a 92.5% Data Center mix), and about $112B in Q4 (the dependent ~$121B total forecast at that mix).\", \"The market reference is approximately $411B of FY2027 total revenue; applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% implies roughly $380B of Data Center revenue, which sits in rung 3. The updated run-rate is about $376B: $75.25B in Q1, $89B in Q2, approximately $99.9B in Q3 using the $108B total-revenue guide and 92.5% mix, and about $112B in Q4 using the dependent ~$121B total-revenue forecast and the same mix. That is still within rung 3. Q4 hyperscale growth acceleration and Rubin shipments support the forecast, but do not quantify an increase sufficient to cross $400B. In the dependency forecast, Q4 total revenue is only about $121B, while FY2027 Data Center revenue would need about $135B in Q4 to reach $400B; the current mix-based estimate is well below that. Competition, the exclusion of China Data Center compute revenue from the outlook, and supply constraints also temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e4052\\\"]\"], [8983, \"2026-09-10\", 3, \"The observable market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue, which is rung 2 for total revenue. Applying the latest reported 92.5% Data Center share gives a Data Center reference of approximately $380 billion, in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue: $75.25 billion in Q1, $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 (92.5% of the $108 billion total-revenue guide), and about $112.2 billion in Q4 (92.5% of the dependency's roughly $121.3 billion total-revenue forecast).\", \"The market reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue, mapped at the latest 92.5% Data Center mix to about $380 billion of Data Center revenue; that reference is in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the Q2 Data Center mix, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 using the dependency's roughly $121.3 billion total-revenue forecast and the same mix. The estimate remains below the $400 billion boundary. Rubin's expected 20% Q3 contribution is part of, not additional to, Data Center revenue; Q4 acceleration and strong customer demand support the forecast, while the no-China outlook and supply constraints temper upside. The estimate is also consistent with the dependency: approximately $376 billion of Data Center revenue is below its roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e4250\\\", \\\"e4331\\\", \\\"e4185\\\", \\\"e4324\\\", \\\"e976\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\"]\"], [8986, \"2026-09-11\", 3, \"Latest supplied analyst expectations put FY2027 total revenue at about $411 billion (rung 2 for total revenue). At the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5%, that implies approximately $380 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3.\", \"Approximately $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide at a 92.5% Data Center mix, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 from the dependency's $121.3 billion total-revenue forecast at the same mix. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary and is consistent with the total-revenue dependency.\", \"The reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% implies roughly $380 billion of Data Center revenue, which is rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the latest Data Center mix, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 using the dependency's $121.3 billion total-revenue forecast and the same mix. This is consistent with the total-revenue dependency and remains below rung 2's $400 billion floor, so it does not cross a boundary. Rubin's expected Q3 contribution is already included in the mix-based estimate, not added on top. New hyperscaler spending and demand evidence supports the run-rate, while the no-China outlook and supply constraints temper upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2693\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e976\\\"]\"], [8990, \"2026-09-12\", 3, \"Analysts' approximately $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation, translated using the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $380 billion of Data Center revenue and sits in rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion for FY2027: $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89 billion in Q2, plus about $99.9 billion for Q3 ($108 billion total-revenue guide at a 92.5% segment mix) and about $112.2 billion for Q4 ($121.3 billion total-revenue estimate from dependency v14 at the same mix).\", \"The reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue; applying the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% puts the reference near $380 billion, in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, about $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the latest mix, and about $112.2 billion in Q4 using the dependency's $121.3 billion Q4 estimate and the same mix. That is above rung 4's $350 billion ceiling but below rung 2's $400 billion floor. Rubin's expected Q3 contribution and the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration support the outlook but do not provide a quantified basis to lift the segment estimate across $400 billion; competition and the absence of China compute revenue in the forward outlook temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e4534\\\"]\"], [8995, \"2026-09-13\", 3, \"Analysts expect about $411 billion of NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue (v14, rung 2 for total revenue). Applying the latest Data Center share of about 92.5% gives a segment reference of about $380 billion, rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion: $75.25 billion in Q1 plus $89 billion in Q2, then approximately $99.9 billion in Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the latest 92.5% Data Center mix, and approximately $112.2 billion in Q4 by carrying Q3\\u2019s roughly 12% sequential total-revenue growth forward and applying the same mix. This is above rung 4\\u2019s $350 billion ceiling but below rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion floor.\", \"The reference is analysts\\u2019 approximately $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation, which is rung 2 for total revenue; applying the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% puts the segment reference near $380 billion, in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion, also rung 3. Recent customer-demand and product-ramp evidence supports the run-rate, but the Q3 Rubin share and Q4 hyperscale acceleration are not quantified as incremental revenue beyond the estimate; competition and the absence of China compute revenue in NVIDIA\\u2019s forward outlook temper the upside. The evidence does not support the roughly $24 billion increase needed to reach rung 2\\u2019s $400 billion floor.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e4550\\\", \\\"e4572\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\"]\"], [8999, \"2026-09-14\", 3, \"Analysts\\u2019 approximately $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation, translated using the recent 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $380 billion of Data Center revenue\\u2014rung 3.\", \"About $376 billion: Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion plus Q2\\u2019s $89 billion, Q3\\u2019s approximately $99.9 billion implied by the 92.5% mix on the $108 billion guide, and roughly $112 billion for Q4 using the dependency\\u2019s $121 billion total-revenue estimate and the same mix. This estimate is below the $400 billion rung-2 threshold.\", \"The market reference is about $380 billion: the analysts\\u2019 $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation, multiplied by the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5%, falls in rung 3. The evidence-based estimate is about $376 billion, also rung 3. Q2\\u2019s reported $89 billion and the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide support a strong run-rate, but carrying the Q3 total-revenue growth forward and applying the recent Data Center mix does not get close to the $400 billion rung-2 floor. Q4 acceleration and Rubin demand are upside risks, but the supplied evidence does not quantify enough additional FY2027 revenue to cross that boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e2115\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e3805\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e4185\\\", \\\"e4317\\\", \\\"e4555\\\", \\\"e4556\\\", \\\"e4557\\\", \\\"e4571\\\", \\\"e4572\\\"]\"], [9004, \"2026-09-15\", 3, \"The market reference is approximately $380 billion, derived by applying the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% to the $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation; this is rung 3.\", \"About $387 billion: the current FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $418 billion, incorporating reported Q1 and Q2 and the forecast Q3/Q4 run-rate, multiplied by the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5%.\", \"The market reference is approximately $380 billion: the $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation multiplied by the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5%, placing the reference in rung 3. The implied estimate is about $387 billion, also rung 3. Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue were $75.25 billion and $89 billion; the Q3 total-revenue guide and expected Rubin contribution support continued growth, while the dependency forecast of about $418 billion in FY2027 total revenue implies about $387 billion of Data Center revenue at the latest mix. That remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong demand and Q4 acceleration support the estimate, but no China compute revenue in the outlook and supply limits temper the upside.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e127\\\"]\"], [9008, \"2026-09-16\", 3, \"No direct FY2027 Data Center consensus figure is supplied. The stated market reference is analysts\\u2019 roughly $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue expectation multiplied by the latest Data Center mix of about 92.5%, or approximately $380 billion; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $388 billion: the v14 FY2027 total-revenue forecast of about $419 billion multiplied by the latest reported Data Center share of approximately 92.5% gives roughly $387.6 billion. This is a forecast, not a claim that the full-year result is already established.\", \"The evidence-based forecast remains in rung 3. The latest reported Data Center quarter was $89.0 billion, up 18% sequentially; management guided total Q3 revenue to $108 billion and said Rubin would account for about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue, while hyperscale growth is expected to reaccelerate in Q4. These support continued growth, but the FY2027 total-revenue dependency implies about $419 billion for the year, and applying the latest Data Center share of about 92.5% implies roughly $388 billion of Data Center revenue. That is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong demand and large customer plans support the forecast, while supply limits, no China compute revenue in the outlook, and competition temper the upside; none of the supplied evidence makes a share above roughly 95.5% of the dependency\\u2019s total-revenue forecast the most likely outcome.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e2115\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e2870\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e3975\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e4534\\\", \\\"e4572\\\", \\\"e4574\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e976\\\"]\"], [9011, \"2026-09-17\", 3, \"The market reference is the approximately $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue analyst estimate (rung 2 for total revenue). Applying the latest reported Data Center mix of about 92.5% gives an indicative segment reference of roughly $380 billion, which is rung 3.\", \"About $387 billion for FY2027: the current total-revenue dependency forecast is about $419 billion, and applying the recent approximately 92.5% Data Center share implies about $388 billion. The reported Q1 and Q2 segment revenues and the projected Q3/Q4 run-rate are consistent with that estimate.\", \"The market reference is the roughly $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue analyst estimate, which is rung 2 for total revenue; using the latest 92.5% Data Center mix as a proxy implies about $380 billion for this segment, rung 3. Our implied Data Center figure is about $387 billion: the current total-revenue dependency forecasts about $419 billion for FY2027, and recent Data Center mix of roughly 92.5% implies about $388 billion. The reported Q1 and Q2 segment revenues and expected Q3/Q4 growth are consistent with that run-rate. This remains below the $400 billion boundary, so the forecast stays at rung 3. Updated China-authorization and AI-capex themes modestly support the upside, while export-control risk and the absence of China compute revenue in NVIDIA\\u2019s outlook temper it; none changes the implied figure enough to cross a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e2483\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e4324\\\"]\"], [9015, \"2026-09-18\", 3, \"Approximately $411 billion FY2027 total-revenue consensus, translated using the latest 92.5% Data Center mix to about $380 billion; this proxy sits in rung 3.\", \"About $387 billion for FY2027 Data Center revenue: the FY2027 total-revenue forecast of roughly $419 billion, including the expected Q3/Q4 growth path, multiplied by the recent 92.5% Data Center mix.\", \"The market reference is approximately $411 billion of FY2027 total-revenue consensus; applying NVIDIA\\u2019s latest Data Center mix of about 92.5% gives a proxy of roughly $380 billion, in rung 3. The implied Data Center forecast is about $387 billion: Q1 and Q2 contributed about $164 billion, and the FY2027 total-revenue forecast of roughly $419 billion, carried at the recent 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $387 billion for the segment. This is above $350 billion but remains below the $400 billion boundary. Q3 guidance, Rubin shipments and expected Q4 hyperscale acceleration support growth, while the no-China-revenue outlook tempers it; the evidence does not imply enough to cross $400 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e4550\\\", \\\"e4995\\\"]\"], [9020, \"2026-09-19\", 3, \"Market expectations of about $411 billion for NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue are the reference; applying the latest 92.5% Data Center mix implies about $380 billion of Data Center revenue, at rung 3.\", \"About $387 billion: $164 billion reported in the first half, plus roughly $105 billion for Q3 and $118 billion for Q4, estimated by applying the latest 92.5% Data Center mix to the FY2027 total-revenue forecast\\u2019s roughly $113.6 billion Q3 and $127.2 billion Q4 estimates.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 total-revenue consensus; using the latest 92.5% Data Center mix gives a proxy of about $380 billion, which is rung 3. The evidence-derived estimate is about $387 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion of Data Center revenue in the first half; applying the total-revenue forecast\\u2019s Q3 estimate of about $113.6 billion and Q4 estimate of about $127.2 billion at a 92.5% segment mix adds about $223 billion. That remains below the $400 billion boundary. Rubin production shipments, hyperscaler orders and expected Q4 acceleration support the forecast, while the no-China outlook and supply constraints temper it.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3219\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e4995\\\", \\\"e4556\\\", \\\"e4557\\\", \\\"e4572\\\", \\\"e4640\\\"]\"], [9024, \"2026-09-20\", 3, \"Wall Street analysts expect about $411 billion of FY2027 total revenue (v14). Applying the latest reported 92.5% Data Center mix places the reference at approximately $380 billion of Data Center revenue, rung 3.\", \"About $388 billion: v14's approximately $419 billion total-revenue forecast, based on reported first-half results, Q3 guidance adjusted for the reported beat history, and carried-forward sequential growth into Q4, multiplied by the latest 92.5% Data Center mix. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary.\", \"The market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 total-revenue consensus, which the latest 92.5% Data Center mix translates to roughly $380 billion of Data Center revenue, at rung 3. The evidence supports a somewhat higher estimate, but not enough to cross $400 billion: strong demand, Rubin orders and a Q4 acceleration are balanced by supply limits and NVIDIA's no-China-revenue outlook. The updated China-authorization theme adds upside risk, but supplies no volume estimate that would move the base case across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e74\\\", \\\"e109\\\", \\\"e127\\\", \\\"e153\\\", \\\"e162\\\", \\\"e163\\\", \\\"e2510\\\", \\\"e2535\\\", \\\"e2541\\\", \\\"e2562\\\", \\\"e2567\\\", \\\"e2572\\\", \\\"e2574\\\", \\\"e2848\\\", \\\"e3216\\\", \\\"e3222\\\", \\\"e3234\\\", \\\"e3805\\\", \\\"e4052\\\", \\\"e4185\\\", \\\"e4250\\\", \\\"e4317\\\", \\\"e4550\\\", \\\"e4572\\\", \\\"e4640\\\", \\\"e4995\\\", \\\"e4996\\\", \\\"e4982\\\"]\"]], [\"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 450000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 400000000000.0, 450000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 350000000000.0, 400000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 300000000000.0, 350000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 300000000000.0]]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nv=\\\"v16\\\"\nconn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(base/\\\"linked-1-copy\\\"/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\nrows=conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,reference,implied_outcome,reasons,cited_views_json,cited_evidence_json from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>8737 order by seq,judgement_id\\\",(v,)).fetchall()\nprint(json.dumps([rows,conn.execute(\\\"select question from views where view_id=?\\\",(v,)).fetchone(),conn.execute(\\\"select position,label,lo,hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position\\\",(v,)).fetchall()]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"[[[8757, \"2026-07-15\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's stated visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $19.5 billion, using management's \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility and the approximately $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast in v14. This is within rung 3 and below the more-than-$20-billion threshold for rung 2.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). That figure, read as approximately $19.5 billion, implies revenue just below the rung-2 boundary of more than $20 billion; the $200 billion TAM is not a revenue target or evidence of volume in hand, so it does not justify crossing the boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e442\\\", \\\"e443\\\"]\"], [8760, \"2026-07-16\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; that reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $20 billion, just below the rung-2 threshold: carry forward management\\u2019s specific FY2027 visibility estimate, with no quantified adjustment from the unscheduled-period CPU-ratio view or initial Vera testbed delivery.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. That remains the best quantified forecast: the roughly $20 billion figure is near, but does not cross, the greater-than-$20 billion boundary for rung 2. The expectation that data-center GPU-to-CPU ratios will shrink is directionally supportive but gives no period or revenue estimate. The scheduled first Vera blade delivery is for initial testbed deployment, not evidence of commercial volume in hand. The $200 billion TAM is not a revenue forecast. NVIDIA\\u2019s total-revenue conclusion is consistent with this CPU estimate as a component of the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e442\\\", \\\"e443\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [8764, \"2026-07-17\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in FY2027 total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $19 billion in FY2027: management's direct visibility statement is nearly $20 billion, and the current total-revenue dependency uses an approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion). The evidence still implies about $19 billion: the current-year visibility is the strongest direct forecast, and the current total-revenue dependency also uses an approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate. That is consistent with CPU revenue as a component of the dependency's approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast and does not cross the greater-than-$20 billion boundary. The GPU-to-CPU ratio view is directionally supportive but gives no period or revenue estimate; the scheduled first blade delivery is for initial testbed deployment, not commercial volume in hand. The $200 billion TAM claim is not a revenue forecast, and the repeated outlet adds no new information.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e584\\\"]\"], [8767, \"2026-07-18\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"About $19 billion in FY2027, using management\\u2019s \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility and the dependency\\u2019s approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate. This is below $20 billion and within rung 3.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The evidence implies about $19 billion: the direct visibility statement is consistent with the approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate in the current total-revenue dependency. That is below the greater-than-$20 billion boundary for rung 2, and it remains consistent with CPU revenue as a component of the dependency\\u2019s approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast. The $200 billion TAM statements are not revenue forecasts; the GPU-to-CPU ratio view gives no FY2027 revenue estimate, and the scheduled first blade delivery is for an initial testbed, not commercial volume in hand.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e584\\\", \\\"e597\\\", \\\"e475\\\", \\\"e596\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [8771, \"2026-07-20\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027: the near-$20 billion visibility statement, together with the dependency's approximately $19 billion stand-alone CPU estimate, implies revenue below $20 billion. The $20 billion business target has no stated period and does not establish FY2027 volume at that level.\", \"NVIDIA's FY2027 visibility is near the top of rung 3, but it does not imply revenue above $20 billion. The unspecified-period $20 billion business target is not a FY2027 forecast, and the TAM statements are not revenue estimates. The dependency's approximately $19 billion CPU estimate remains below the rung 2 boundary and fits within its approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast. The testbed delivery is not commercial volume in hand.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e442\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\"]\"], [8774, \"2026-07-21\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the stated reference and sits in rung 3 (at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027, interpreting \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d of period-specific visibility as just below $20 billion; the Wolfe estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera alone in calendar 2026 (1.3 million \\u00d7 $5,000), but does not quantify total FY2027 CPU revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits in rung 3. I infer approximately $19 billion: the visibility statement is the strongest period-specific forecast, while Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026 and is a material downside check, not an estimate of total FY2027 CPU revenue. June deliveries and OpenAI's planned large-quantity deployment support commercial traction, but volumes are not disclosed; the evidence does not support moving above $20 billion or below the rung 3 floor of $15 billion. This also fits within NVIDIA's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\"]\"], [8779, \"2026-07-22\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d is the market/company reference and sits in rung 3 (at the upper boundary).\", \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027, anchored by management's \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d visibility; the separate $20 billion standalone-business target has no stated period, while 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for that product in calendar 2026. The implied total is below the $20 billion rung-2 threshold and above rung 3's $15 billion floor.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion,\\u201d which sits in rung 3. That period-specific forecast still implies revenue near $19 billion: the $20 billion standalone-business target has no period, and Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera shipments in calendar 2026, but neither supplies a quantified revision to the FY2027 total. Deliveries and planned deployments support execution, while the new market and competition evidence does not quantify a change sufficient to cross either the $15 billion or $20 billion boundary; this forecast also remains below NVIDIA's approximately $387 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\"]\"], [8781, \"2026-07-23\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue is the available reference; no separate market consensus is supplied. This reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA\\u2019s period-specific visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion.\\u201d The $20 billion standalone Vera expectation for calendar 2026 supports scale but is discounted as a different-period product target without disclosed volume in hand; the 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, not total FY2027 CPU revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3. The strongest period-specific implication remains roughly $19\\u201320 billion: e813 adds a $20 billion standalone Vera revenue expectation for calendar 2026, but that is a product target for a different period, not evidence that more than $20 billion is in hand for FY2027. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, tempering that target; it is not total CPU revenue and does not displace the direct FY2027 visibility. Deliveries and planned large deployments support execution, but the evidence does not imply a total above the rung-2 boundary of $20 billion. NVIDIA\\u2019s total FY2027 revenue forecast in v14 is ample room for this CPU estimate, so the component does not exceed its whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\"]\"], [8784, \"2026-07-24\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management's nearly $20 billion visibility, with the CEO clarifying it is for standalone CPU sales. The separate $20 billion calendar-2026 Vera target is tempered by Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip (roughly $6.5 billion), and the delivery and deployment evidence is not quantified into additional FY2027 revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d FY2027 total CPU revenue visibility, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). The evidence still implies approximately $19\\u201320 billion, not more than $20 billion: the CEO clarifies that the FY2027 figure is for standalone CPU sales, while the $20 billion Vera target for calendar 2026 is not the same as demonstrated FY2027 revenue. Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, tempering the target; deliveries and planned deployments support execution but do not quantify additional FY2027 revenue. This remains below the rung-2 threshold of more than $20 billion. The estimate is consistent with v14's approximately $385.7 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component and does not exceed the whole. Recent clarification of the standalone scope and delivery/deployment evidence updates the supporting facts, but does not move the forecast across a rung boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\"]\"], [8788, \"2026-07-25\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue is the market/company reference; that figure sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion: management's FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, while Wolfe Research's 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers, but does not displace, that broader visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). The evidence still implies revenue near, but not above, $20 billion: the specific shipment-and-price estimate implies about $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, tempering the much higher management visibility, while deliveries and planned deployments support execution. AMD's performance claims add competitive risk but do not quantify a revenue reduction or customer shift. Nothing in the evidence supports crossing the rung-2 boundary of more than $20 billion. NVIDIA's total-revenue forecast in v14 is ample headroom and is consistent with CPU revenue at this level.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8793, \"2026-07-26\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Near, but not above, $20 billion in FY2027. The company\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility is the strongest direct forecast; the estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 and tempers, but does not replace, that total-FY forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The implied outcome remains near, but not above, $20 billion: NVIDIA\\u2019s current-year visibility is the strongest direct forecast, while Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion of Vera revenue in calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in the higher figure. June deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution, but no stated customer volume or quantified revision supports revenue above $20 billion. The AMD performance and market-share evidence adds competitive risk without quantifying a revenue reduction or customer shift. NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue forecast is consistent with this CPU outcome and imposes no tighter constraint.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\"]\"], [8796, \"2026-07-27\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the market reference and sits in rung 3 (at or below $20 billion, above $15 billion).\", \"About $20 billion, or slightly below: management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility is nearly $20 billion, and its standalone-CPU statements point to approximately that scale; the 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, but is not additive to the total-revenue visibility. This is well below NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $385.7 billion total-revenue forecast in v14 and remains at rung 3\\u2019s upper boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still points to revenue around, but not demonstrably above, $20 billion: the $20 billion standalone-CPU statements are targets or visibility, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, not an additional amount to add to management\\u2019s total. Deliveries, planned large-quantity OpenAI deployment, and the updated AI-capex outlook support execution; AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence adds risk but does not quantify a revenue reduction. The implied level remains at rung 3\\u2019s upper boundary, not above it. NVIDIA\\u2019s total-revenue forecast in v14 is consistent with this CPU contribution.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e794\\\"]\"], [8801, \"2026-07-28\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the available reference; it sits at the upper end of rung 3. No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Around $20 billion, most likely just below $20 billion. Management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility anchors the estimate; Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not an additional amount to add to total CPU revenue. The implied CPU revenue is well below v14\\u2019s approximately $385.7 billion total-revenue forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive); no separate consensus figure is supplied. The evidence still implies revenue around, and most likely just below, $20 billion: Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a Vera estimate rather than an additional amount to add to management\\u2019s total. Deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution, while AMD\\u2019s reported performance and share gains are a competitive counterweight but do not quantify a revenue reduction. The implied outcome remains within rung 3, and is consistent with NVIDIA\\u2019s much larger total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8805, \"2026-07-29\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). No separate consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19 billion to just under $20 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility is the strongest direct forecast, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026 and is not additive to the total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive); no separate consensus figure is supplied. The new evidence still points to revenue near but most likely just below $20 billion: the CFO\\u2019s statement reiterates that visibility, while Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for Vera in calendar 2026, not $6.5 billion to add to management\\u2019s total. Deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-quantity deployment support execution, while the AMD performance and market-share evidence is a competitive counterweight without a quantified revenue impact. The implied figure remains within rung 3 and is consistent with NVIDIA\\u2019s broader FY2027 revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e489\\\"]\"], [8809, \"2026-07-30\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the supplied reference; that is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive). No separate consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion, centered just below $20 billion, based primarily on management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility statement. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that overlaps the FY2027 period and is not added to management\\u2019s total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits at rung 3. The evidence still implies revenue near, but most likely just below, $20 billion: the new AMD performance comparisons add a competitive counterweight, while NVIDIA\\u2019s delivery and deployment evidence and the stronger infrastructure-demand view support execution. None quantifies a change to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 CPU revenue, so the evidence does not move the forecast across a rung boundary. This remains consistent with v14\\u2019s roughly $386 billion total-revenue forecast: the CPU business is a component well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8812, \"2026-07-31\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3. No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19.5 billion, or near but most likely just below $20 billion. This follows management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 visibility, supported by early delivery and deployment evidence but tempered by competitive evidence and the lower, period-mismatched Wolfe shipment estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive); no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. NVIDIA\\u2019s statement that the nearly $20 billion is for standalone CPU sales supports applying that reference to this thesis. Deliveries, OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployments and HPE\\u2019s planned fall availability support execution, while AMD\\u2019s performance comparisons and incumbent market position are counterweights. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate implies about $6.5 billion (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000) in calendar 2026, but it is a third-party estimate for a different period and does not revise NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 visibility. The evidence therefore implies revenue near, but most likely just below, $20 billion; it does not cross a rung boundary. That remains consistent with v14\\u2019s approximately $386 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8817, \"2026-08-01\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue is the reference and sits in rung 3. No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Roughly $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, most likely just below $20 billion. This uses management's nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility as the direct anchor; deliveries and planned deployments support execution, while Wolfe's 1.3 million \\u00d7 about $5,000 estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 but does not independently establish a higher FY2027 figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; that sits at rung 3, whose range includes $20 billion. The CEO's clarification that this is standalone CPU revenue makes it applicable to this thesis. The implied outcome remains roughly $19\\u201320 billion, most likely just below $20 billion: the visibility statement anchors the forecast, while Vera deliveries, OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment and HPE's fall availability support the ramp. Wolfe's estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 (1.3 million chips \\u00d7 about $5,000), but is not an FY2027 forecast; the newer AMD performance comparisons are a counterweight, not a quantified FY2027 revenue revision. NVIDIA's total-revenue forecast of about $386 billion leaves ample room for CPU revenue at this level, so the dependency is consistent. The implied figure does not cross the rung-2 boundary, which requires more than $20 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8823, \"2026-08-03\", 3, \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027, clarified by the CEO as standalone CPU sales. This reference sits in rung 3 (at or below $20 billion and at least $15 billion); no separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19 billion in FY2027. NVIDIA's direct visibility anchors the forecast just below $20 billion; rollout evidence supports reaching close to it, while Wolfe's 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and AMD competition temper the estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, with the CEO clarifying that it is standalone CPU revenue; nearly $20 billion sits in rung 3. I forecast roughly $19 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility statement remains the strongest annual input, supported by customer deliveries, OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment and HPE's fall availability. Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies approximately $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a material counterweight, while AMD's share and performance evidence adds execution risk; neither supplies a quantified FY2027 revision that outweighs NVIDIA's direct visibility and rollout evidence. The estimate remains below $20 billion but above rung 4's $15 billion boundary. The total-revenue dependency's roughly $390 billion FY2027 forecast leaves ample headroom for this CPU contribution.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8826, \"2026-08-04\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference, and it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive). No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\", \"About $19 billion in FY2027: start from NVIDIA's nearly $20 billion visibility and retain it as the leading annual input, with customer deliveries and the planned Q3 OpenAI deployment supporting the ramp, while competition and Wolfe Research's roughly $6.5 billion calendar-2026 Vera estimate temper the forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. The direct visibility statement remains the strongest annual input, and customer deliveries plus OpenAI's planned Q3 deployment support the rollout. Wolfe Research's estimate implies about $6.5 billion from 1.3 million Vera CPUs at roughly $5,000 each in calendar 2026, while AMD's competitive evidence adds execution risk, but neither provides a quantified FY2027 revision that outweighs NVIDIA's visibility. The implied figure remains below $20 billion and above $15 billion, so it does not cross a boundary. At roughly $19 billion, CPU revenue is also comfortably below the dependent thesis's roughly $390 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8830, \"2026-08-05\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the available reference; it falls in rung 3. No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $19 billion in FY2027, derived from management\\u2019s direct \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d annual visibility, with customer deliveries and planned OpenAI deployment supporting the ramp. Wolfe\\u2019s shipment-and-price estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but the newer competitive evidence does not quantify a FY2027 reduction to the direct visibility figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. Deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-scale Q3 deployment support meaningful sales, while Wolfe\\u2019s roughly 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each imply only about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and AMD\\u2019s reported performance advantages add execution risk. Those competitive facts do not quantify a FY2027 revenue reduction that outweighs NVIDIA\\u2019s direct visibility, so the forecast remains around $19 billion\\u2014below the greater-than-$20 billion boundary. This is also consistent with dependent thesis v14\\u2019s roughly $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component and remains well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8834, \"2026-08-06\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference is in rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion, anchored on management's direct FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion. Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but is a shipment estimate for a period that does not override management's FY2027 visibility; the implied total remains at or below $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. The direct visibility supports an implied outcome of roughly $19\\u201320 billion, but not an outcome above $20 billion. Q3 plans for large OpenAI deployments and customer deliveries support the ramp, while Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and AMD's reported performance and share gains add execution risk. Those signals do not establish a quantified reduction that outweighs management's FY2027 visibility. The outcome remains below the rung-2 boundary, and it is consistent with dependency v14: CPU revenue is a component of NVIDIA's roughly $390\\u2013395 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8839, \"2026-08-07\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; this reference is in rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion inclusive). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027. Management's direct visibility anchors the forecast near $20 billion; Wolfe Research's 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a meaningful execution-risk check but not a quantified revision to the FY2027 visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 because that rung includes $20 billion but rung 2 requires more than $20 billion. I infer approximately $19\\u201320 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility remains the strongest forecast input, while Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and highlights execution and ramp risk. June deliveries, planned large OpenAI deployments from Q3, and the additional AMD performance comparisons do not provide a quantified change to FY2027 revenue that would move the forecast across a rung boundary. This component-level estimate is consistent with dependency v14's roughly $400\\u2013410 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8843, \"2026-08-08\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility of nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 (at most $20 billion). No separate market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion for FY2027. I use management\\u2019s direct nearly-$20-billion FY2027 visibility as the primary forecast; disclosed deliveries and planned large OpenAI deployments support execution but are not sized enough to imply revenue above $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3: rung 2 requires revenue greater than $20 billion. I infer about $19\\u201320 billion: the direct FY2027 visibility remains the strongest forecast input; deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployment support execution, but no disclosed deployment volume or quantified revision lifts the estimate above $20 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera CPUs at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is an execution caution, not a replacement for the FY2027 visibility figure. AMD performance comparisons and market-share reports are competitive risks but do not quantify a revenue reduction. This estimate is consistent with dependency v14\\u2019s roughly $405\\u2013415 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: the CPU component is well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8845, \"2026-08-09\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the supplied reference; it sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027. The direct FY2027 visibility remains the strongest input; deliveries and planned large Q3 deployments support realization, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million CPUs at about $5,000 each imply about $6.5 billion in calendar 2026 and the new AMD comparisons temper upside without quantifying a FY2027 reduction.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; rung 2 requires more than $20 billion. The evidence still implies roughly $19\\u201320 billion: deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployment support execution, but no disclosed volume or quantified FY2027 revision lifts the estimate above $20 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a useful execution check but not a replacement for the direct FY2027 visibility. New AMD comparisons add specific competitive risk, but do not quantify a revenue reduction sufficient to move the implied figure across a rung boundary. The estimate is consistent with dependency v14\\u2019s roughly $415 billion total-revenue forecast; CPU revenue is only a portion of that total.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8851, \"2026-08-10\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, centered just below $20 billion. The direct FY2027 visibility remains the anchor; deliveries and planned Q3 deployment support execution, while the $6.5 billion implied by Wolfe's calendar-2026 shipment and price estimate is only a partial-period execution check.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; rung 2 requires more than $20 billion. The evidence still implies approximately $19\\u201320 billion: reported deliveries and OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment support execution, while Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 as an execution check, not a substitute FY2027 forecast. The separate $20 billion business target has no specified period, and the AMD performance comparisons add competitive risk without quantifying a FY2027 revenue reduction. The implied figure does not cross a rung boundary and is consistent with dependency v14's approximately $415 billion total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8856, \"2026-08-11\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based chiefly on management's direct visibility statement, with the CEO confirming it is standalone CPU revenue. The 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is an execution check, not a flat annualized FY2027 estimate.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies roughly $19\\u201320 billion: the FY2027 visibility statement is the strongest direct input, and the CEO confirms it is standalone CPU revenue. Wolfe Research's 1.3 million units at about $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, useful as an execution check but not a flat annualized FY2027 forecast. Deliveries, OpenAI's planned Q3 deployment, and HPE's Vera-powered server support execution; AMD's performance comparisons add competitive risk but do not quantify a revenue reduction. The separate $20 billion business target has no specified period, so it does not establish revenue above $20 billion in FY2027. The implied outcome does not cross into rung 2. It is consistent with dependency v14's total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is well below the whole. Recent product-launch and competitive details, and the updated AI-capex theme, change the supporting context but not the implied $19\\u201320 billion outcome or rung.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\"]\"], [8861, \"2026-08-12\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this reference sits in rung 3.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management\\u2019s period-specific visibility statement. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026 and serve as an execution check, not a flat annualized FY2027 forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. That remains the strongest period-specific input. The implied outcome remains about $19\\u201320 billion: the separate $20 billion standalone-business target has no specified period, while Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, an execution check rather than a flat annualized FY2027 forecast. June deliveries, OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-scale Q3 deployment, and HPE\\u2019s planned fall availability support execution; AMD\\u2019s quantified performance comparisons are a competitive risk but do not establish a revenue reduction. The evidence does not imply revenue above $20 billion, the boundary for rung 2. The estimate is also consistent with v14\\u2019s roughly $410 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue remains a small part of the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8865, \"2026-08-13\", 3, \"No separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the relevant company reference, and it sits in rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion inclusive).\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility is period-specific, and deliveries plus planned large-scale Q3 deployment support that forecast. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million calendar-2026 shipments at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for that calendar year, but do not establish a FY2027 figure or justify flat annualization.\", \"The company\\u2019s period-specific visibility is still the strongest forecast input and implies revenue near, but not demonstrably above, $20 billion. Deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-scale Q3 deployment support execution, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026\\u2014not a FY2027 total or a valid flat annualization. AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence is a risk but does not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 revenue. The estimate therefore remains around $19\\u201320 billion, within rung 3; rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. This is consistent with v14\\u2019s roughly $400\\u2013410 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e443\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8869, \"2026-08-14\", 3, \"No independent market consensus for NVIDIA CPU revenue is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the relevant period-specific reference, at rung 3.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d period-specific visibility anchors the estimate just below or around $20 billion. Deliveries and planned deployments support execution, but the $6.5 billion implied by Wolfe\\u2019s 2026 unit-and-price forecast is for calendar 2026 and is not extrapolated flat into FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3; the supplied evidence does not give an independent market consensus. The period-specific visibility remains the strongest forecast input. Deliveries to customers and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-scale Q3 deployment support execution, but do not quantify revenue above $20 billion. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, not FY2027 revenue, so it is not carried forward as a flat annualized run-rate. The newer AMD performance comparisons sharpen the competitive risk but do not quantify a reduction in NVIDIA revenue. The implied outcome remains about $19\\u201320 billion, within rung 3; rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. This is consistent with v14\\u2019s $400\\u2013410 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a component well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8873, \"2026-08-15\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d in total CPU revenue; this places the reference in rung 3. No independent market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management\\u2019s period-specific visibility, with customer deliveries and planned OpenAI deployment supporting execution. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at roughly $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a downside check, but does not displace the company\\u2019s FY2027 outlook.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to \\u201cnearly $20 billion\\u201d of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no independent market consensus is supplied. The evidence still points to revenue around $19\\u201320 billion: deliveries and planned large-scale OpenAI deployment support execution, while the specific Wolfe estimate of about 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a meaningful downside check. That estimate is not a revised FY2027 company outlook, and the other competitive evidence does not quantify a reduction in NVIDIA revenue. The updated hyperscaler-spending theme is supportive, but does not size an increase above $20 billion. The implied outcome therefore remains within rung 3; rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8876, \"2026-08-16\", 3, \"No independent market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion is the reference, and it sits in rung 3.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s period-specific visibility anchors the estimate near $20 billion, while customer deliveries and planned Q3 deployment support execution without establishing a figure above that level. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a downside check rather than a replacement for the FY2027 visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue; that is rung 3, since rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. The implied outcome remains about $19\\u201320 billion: the FY2027 visibility is the strongest period-specific input, while the separate $20 billion business target has no specified period. Deliveries to named customers and OpenAI\\u2019s planned Q3 deployment support execution, but do not quantify revenue above the visibility figure. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and is a significant downside check, but it does not revise the FY2027 guidance; competitive evidence also weighs against an uplift. The implied figure is below the rung-2 boundary. It is consistent with dependency v14\\u2019s roughly $396 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue at about $20 billion is well below total revenue. Higher hyperscaler capex in themes v5 and v6 is supportive, but does not size an increase above $20 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8881, \"2026-08-17\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue; this reference sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027. The period-specific visibility is the main forecast input; the $20 billion Vera target is not evidence of revenue above $20 billion, and Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 as a downside cross-check rather than a replacement FY2027 forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue, which sits in rung 3 because rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. The forecast remains near $19\\u201320 billion: management\\u2019s period-specific visibility is the strongest input, and the stated $20 billion Vera target does not establish revenue above that level. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a downside check, while deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned Q3 ramp support execution but are not sized enough to lift the forecast. New AMD performance comparisons add competitive downside but do not quantify a change to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 revenue outlook. The implied outcome remains within rung 3, below the greater-than-$20 billion boundary for rung 2. NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 total-revenue conclusion is consistent with this CPU contribution as a part of the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e443\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8885, \"2026-08-18\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue; this reference is in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). No separate market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, based primarily on management\\u2019s period-specific visibility to nearly $20 billion. Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a partial-period cross-check rather than an additive FY2027 estimate; the implied figure remains at or below $20 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 because rung 2 requires revenue strictly above $20 billion. The implied outcome remains near, but not above, $20 billion: the Wolfe estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 implies roughly $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, but that is a partial-period estimate and does not replace the stronger FY2027 visibility. June deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned Q3 large-volume deployment support execution, but are not sized enough to lift the total above the guidance-based figure. AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence is a downside risk without a quantified revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook. NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $391 billion overall FY2027 revenue forecast is consistent with, and does not constrain, a CPU figure near $20 billion. The AI-capex themes support demand but do not supply a quantified CPU-revenue increment across the greater-than-$20-billion boundary.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\"]\"], [8889, \"2026-08-19\", 3, \"No independent market-consensus figure is supplied. NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027 is the operative reference; it sits in rung 3.\", \"Near, but not above, $20 billion for FY2027, based primarily on NVIDIA\\u2019s period-specific visibility. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but that Vera shipment estimate does not replace the stronger total-FY2027 company outlook.\", \"The supplied evidence gives no independent market-consensus figure; the operative reference is NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion inclusive). I forecast revenue near, but not above, $20 billion: that visibility is the strongest period-specific input, and there is no quantified upward revision. Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, but it is a third-party estimate for Vera shipments rather than a revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s total-FY2027 visibility. June deliveries and OpenAI\\u2019s planned large-volume Q3 deployment support execution, but do not quantify enough additional volume to move the forecast above $20 billion. AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence is a downside risk, not a quantified NVIDIA revenue revision. NVIDIA\\u2019s total-revenue dependency forecast is about $403.5 billion, so a CPU forecast near $20 billion is consistent with the whole and does not exceed it.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8893, \"2026-08-20\", 3, \"No independent market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue is the operative reference, and it sits in rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion, inclusive).\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, centered just below $20 billion: the period-specific management visibility remains the strongest forecast input, while the cited shipments and deployment plans support execution without quantifying a higher total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue; no independent market-consensus figure is supplied. That reference sits in rung 3. June deliveries, OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment, and further product availability support execution, but they do not quantify an upward revision. Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, a useful but narrower product-and-period estimate rather than a revision to FY2027 total CPU revenue. AMD's competitive performance claims add execution risk but do not quantify a change to NVIDIA's revenue outlook. I therefore forecast approximately $19\\u201320 billion, centered just below $20 billion, which remains in rung 3. The dependency's approximately $403.5 billion total-revenue forecast is compatible with this CPU contribution; it treats the CPU figure as part of total revenue, not additional revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\"]\"], [8897, \"2026-08-21\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this is rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion).\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, centered just below $20 billion. The management visibility is directly for FY2027; the $6.5 billion derived from Wolfe's 1.3 million-chip estimate at about $5,000 each is for Vera in calendar 2026, not total CPU revenue for the full FY2027 period.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The forecast remains about $19\\u201320 billion, centered just below $20 billion: the stated visibility is for the question's period, while the Vera shipment estimate of about 1.3 million chips at roughly $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 only and does not cover all CPU revenue or the full FY2027 period. Deliveries, OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment, and HPE's expected fall availability support execution; AMD's larger server-CPU market estimate supports demand, while its performance claims are a competitive counterweight. None quantifies a change to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue visibility, so the implied figure does not cross the $20 billion rung boundary. The v14 total-revenue forecast of about $403\\u2013404 billion is consistent with, and easily accommodates, CPU revenue near $20 billion.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\"]\"], [8901, \"2026-08-22\", 3, \"NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027; this is rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, centered just below $20 billion. The 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at roughly $5,000 implies about $6.5 billion in calendar 2026, and delivery and deployment evidence supports a ramp, but there is no quantified revision that lifts the FY2027 total above management's nearly-$20-billion visibility.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3. The forecast remains about $19\\u201320 billion: Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment estimate at roughly $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while deliveries and OpenAI's planned large Q3 deployment support a ramp; neither quantifies a higher FY2027 total. AMD's larger market estimates strengthen the demand backdrop, but its performance and market-share claims are counterweights and do not quantify NVIDIA's share. The implied outcome remains below the $20 billion boundary, and the component remains well below NVIDIA's approximately $403\\u2013404 billion total-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2262\\\"]\"], [8906, \"2026-08-23\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s stated visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027; that reference sits in rung 3.\", \"Approximately $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027, anchored by management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while the deployment evidence supports a ramp but does not establish a higher FY2027 total.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). The evidence still implies a total around $19\\u201320 billion: the $20 billion visibility is the strongest company-specific forecast, while Wolfe\\u2019s estimate of 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and provides support for a ramp, not a separate FY2027 total. OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployment and AMD\\u2019s higher server-CPU market forecasts support demand, but they do not quantify NVIDIA revenue above $20 billion; AMD\\u2019s competitive performance and market-share evidence is a counterweight. The implied figure does not cross the greater-than-$20-billion boundary for rung 2 and remains far below NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $404 billion total-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\"]\"], [8911, \"2026-08-24\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027 is the supplied reference; it sits in rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive). No separate market consensus is supplied.\", \"About $19\\u201320 billion in FY2027: management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion visibility is the strongest period-specific forecast. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million chips at about $5,000 imply roughly $6.5 billion in CY2026, supporting a ramp but not establishing a FY2027 total above $20 billion; the announced customer deployments are not sized.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s visibility to nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue in FY2027, which sits in rung 3; no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence still implies roughly $19\\u201320 billion, not a forecast above $20 billion. Recent reports that Vera chips were delivered to customers and that OpenAI plans large Q3 deployments add commercial support, but those deployments are unsized. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit CY2026 estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for that calendar year, not a FY2027 total; at that price, $20 billion would require about 4 million units. Management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility remains the strongest direct forecast, while AMD\\u2019s competitive evidence tempers upside. The NVIDIA-wide FY2027 revenue conclusion is consistent with a roughly $20 billion CPU contribution but does not imply a higher CPU figure. The evidence therefore does not cross the greater-than-$20-billion boundary into rung 2.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2262\\\", \\\"e2294\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2353\\\", \\\"e2405\\\"]\"], [8915, \"2026-08-25\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility/expectation of about $20 billion in total CPU revenue; that reference sits at rung 3.\", \"Roughly $19\\u201320 billion for FY2027. Management\\u2019s direct FY2027 figure anchors the estimate; Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million units at about $5,000 each implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and the disclosed but unsized customer plans do not justify extrapolating above the $20 billion management figure.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility/expectation of about $20 billion in CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). The evidence still implies roughly $19\\u201320 billion: management\\u2019s FY2027 figure remains the strongest direct forecast, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and does not establish a higher FY2027 total. Customer deliveries, OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployment, and SpaceXAI\\u2019s announced use support adoption, but are not sized enough to lift the forecast above $20 billion. AMD\\u2019s larger market outlook supports demand, while its reported performance advantages add competitive risk. The implied figure remains within rung 3, and is consistent with NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $404\\u2013405 billion total-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2439\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e2294\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2479\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2405\\\"]\"], [8920, \"2026-08-26\", 3, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the reference; it sits at rung 3, whose range is $15 billion to $20 billion inclusive.\", \"Approximately $20 billion in FY2027. I carry forward management\\u2019s FY2027 outlook: the new more-than-double statement applies to FY2028, while the Grace figure is trailing-12-month revenue and the AWS CPU purchases have no specified period or exact quantity.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at the top of rung 3 (at most $20 billion). The new Q2 commentary strengthens the demand and supply case, but does not revise the FY2027 figure: the more-than-double statement is for FY2028, AWS\\u2019s \\u201cmillions of CPUs\\u201d has no purchase period or exact quantity, and Grace\\u2019s over-$5 billion figure is trailing-12-month revenue with no bridge showing it is additive to the FY2027 outlook. The evidence therefore still implies roughly $20 billion for FY2027, not a figure above $20 billion that would cross into rung 2. This is consistent with v14\\u2019s approximately $407 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast, which already includes CPU revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2439\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2794\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8924, \"2026-08-27\", 2, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is the reference, on rung 3 (at the top boundary). Jensen Huang\\u2019s clarification that this is standalone CPU sales affects how it informs total CPU revenue.\", \"About $25 billion: roughly $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus an approximately $5 billion annual Grace CPU run-rate based on the disclosed TTM figure. That implies revenue above $20 billion but does not make revenue above $25 billion most likely.\", \"The market reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s nearly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at the top of rung 3. However, Jensen Huang clarified that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales, while NVIDIA separately reported more than $5 billion of Grace CPU revenue on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that Grace run-rate into FY2027 implies total CPU revenue of about $25 billion alongside the roughly $20 billion standalone outlook: above $20 billion, but not enough to make revenue above $25 billion the most likely outcome. That places the forecast on rung 2. The separate Grace contribution is an inference from the disclosed product scope and run-rate, not an explicit company forecast of a combined total. This remains well below v14\\u2019s approximately $407 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2544\\\"]\"], [8928, \"2026-08-28\", 2, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 outlook is approximately $20 billion in CPU revenue (with the CEO clarifying that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales); this reference sits at rung 3. No separate consensus figure is supplied.\", \"About $25 billion total: approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace's more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue, carried forward as a run-rate. That implies rung 2; the evidence does not make a total above $25 billion the most likely outcome.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at rung 3; the CFO described nearly $20 billion of total CPU revenue visibility, while the CEO clarified that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales. The implied total is about $25 billion: carry the approximately $20 billion standalone outlook and add Grace's disclosed trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, already a run-rate rather than a last-quarter annualization. The evidence supports meaningful Vera shipments and demand, but does not make revenue above $25 billion the most likely FY2027 outcome. This stays at rung 2, whose upper boundary includes $25 billion. v14's approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast is consistent with CPU revenue at this level; CPU remains a component of the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e1018\\\"]\"], [8933, \"2026-08-29\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue outlook is the reference; at $20 billion, it sits on rung 3 (Neutral: $15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $25 billion or slightly more: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera outlook forward and add Grace CPU revenue at its disclosed trailing-12-month run rate of more than $5 billion. The resulting forecast is just over the rung 1 threshold of $25 billion.\", \"The market/company reference is NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue outlook, which sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion). The implied total is modestly above $25 billion: carry the roughly $20 billion Vera standalone outlook forward and add Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Vera is now in full production with shipments underway, and the AWS adoption adds execution support; this makes the standalone target more credible and the combined total more likely to edge across the $25 billion boundary. The FY2028 more-than-doubling outlook supports the direction but is not counted as FY2027 revenue. This remains a close call, not a forecast materially above the boundary; v14's total-revenue forecast is consistent with this CPU component.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e668\\\"]\"], [8937, \"2026-08-30\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook is about $20 billion, at rung 3. Management clarified that the $20 billion refers to standalone CPU sales.\", \"Approximately $25 billion plus: carry the roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone Vera outlook alongside Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. This narrowly exceeds the $25 billion rung-1 boundary.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's FY2027 CPU-revenue outlook of about $20 billion, which sits at rung 3. The standalone Vera target is not taken as certain merely because it is guidance, but full production, shipments to lead partners, and named customer deployments make achieving close to that target more likely; Grace revenue is separate from the standalone target. The Wolfe Research estimate of about 1.3 million Vera chips at roughly $5,000 each for calendar 2026 and reported AMD performance advantages temper the forecast, but do not outweigh the FY2027 outlook and execution evidence. Adding the Grace run-rate puts the most likely total just over $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. This is consistent with v14's approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a plausible part of, and does not exceed, the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e897\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8942, \"2026-08-31\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the relevant reference and sits at rung 3 (greater than $15 billion and at most $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion (reported on a trailing-12-month basis) implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion, assuming that Grace run-rate carries through FY2027.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits in rung 3 ($15 billion\\u2013$20 billion). I forecast total CPU revenue above $25 billion: the evidence identifies the $20 billion target as standalone CPU revenue, while Grace CPU revenue was already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Carrying that Grace run-rate into FY2027 puts total CPU revenue just above the rung-1 boundary. Vera is in production and shipping to named partners, supporting the standalone target, though the shipment and competitive evidence tempers confidence in the amount above the threshold. This is consistent with v14\\u2019s approximately $407.1 billion total-revenue forecast: the CPU estimate is a subset of total revenue.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e443\\\", \\\"e489\\\", \\\"e612\\\", \\\"e664\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e694\\\", \\\"e715\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e793\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e813\\\", \\\"e814\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e1018\\\", \\\"e1338\\\", \\\"e1473\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2240\\\", \\\"e2262\\\", \\\"e2294\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2353\\\", \\\"e2405\\\", \\\"e2439\\\", \\\"e2479\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2636\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2715\\\", \\\"e2730\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e2794\\\", \\\"e2948\\\", \\\"e3007\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3126\\\", \\\"e3175\\\", \\\"e3379\\\", \\\"e3381\\\", \\\"e3382\\\", \\\"e3414\\\", \\\"e3415\\\", \\\"e3416\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e3596\\\"]\"], [8947, \"2026-09-01\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook is the reference and sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion, inclusive).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a Grace CPU run-rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. That clears rung 1\\u2019s greater-than-$25-billion boundary, though the amount above it is uncertain because quantified Vera shipment tracking implies materially less standalone revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. The forecast is higher because Grace CPU revenue was already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, while Vera is now in full production and shipping to lead partners; adding the Grace run-rate to the standalone outlook puts total CPU revenue just above $25 billion. This is a narrow crossing: Wolfe Research\\u2019s 1.3 million-unit, approximately $5,000-per-chip forecast implies about $6.5 billion of Vera shipments in calendar 2026, well below the company outlook, so the outlook is not fully validated by quantified volumes. The later production and customer evidence supports the forecast, but does not eliminate that execution risk. The estimate remains consistent with v14\\u2019s approximately $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is a subset.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e897\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\"]\"], [8953, \"2026-09-03\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 outlook is about $20 billion for standalone CPU revenue; that reference is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $25 billion-plus for FY2027: about $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue, with Grace contributing on a run-rate basis after exceeding $5 billion in trailing-12-month revenue during FY2027. This is a forecast, not a proved FY2027 total, and it narrowly clears the rung 1 boundary of more than $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 standalone-CPU outlook of about $20 billion, which sits in rung 3. For total CPU revenue, the implied forecast remains narrowly above $25 billion: the standalone Vera outlook is about $20 billion, and Grace had already generated more than $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027. The updated production and customer evidence supports delivery of the Vera outlook, though it does not quantify shipments enough to eliminate execution risk; the analyst view that CPU revenue reaches about 5% of total revenue by 2028 and the competitive evidence temper the estimate but do not outweigh management\\u2019s outlook and the Grace run-rate. The implied total is consistent with v14\\u2019s approximately $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\"]\"], [8957, \"2026-09-04\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's FY2027 outlook is about $20 billion for standalone CPU revenue; as a total-revenue reference at about $20 billion, it sits at rung 3. The market's broader CPU-demand estimates are not company revenue guidance.\", \"About $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus more than $5 billion of Grace CPU revenue on a trailing-12-month basis implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. This crosses the rung 1 boundary; the unsized customer deployments are not counted as additional revenue.\", \"The company reference is approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3 if treated as the total. The forecast for total CPU revenue is higher: NVIDIA's standalone Vera outlook is about $20 billion, while its separately identified Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027, implying a total just over $25 billion. Vera is in production and shipments are underway, supporting use of the current-year outlook; the disclosed customer deployments reinforce demand but are not sized enough to add a separate amount. Competitive evidence and the analyst view of CPU revenue as about 5% of total sales by 2028 temper the forecast, but do not outweigh the current-year management outlook and Grace revenue. This is consistent with v14's approximately $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue is well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\"]\"], [8961, \"2026-09-05\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook is the reference; $20 billion is rung 3. It is for standalone Vera CPU revenue, not the total CPU revenue asked here.\", \"Approximately $25 billion-plus for FY2027: combine the approximately $20 billion standalone Vera outlook with Grace CPU revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, using that reported run-rate as the estimate for Grace's FY2027 contribution.\", \"The company reference is about $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3. The implied total is just over $25 billion: add that standalone outlook to Grace CPU revenue already above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Vera shipments are underway and management says it is in full production, supporting the outlook; the Grace run-rate is the narrow margin over the rung-1 boundary. AMD competition tempers confidence, but the evidence does not make a total at or below $25 billion more likely. The implied total is well below NVIDIA's roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast in v14, so it is consistent with that dependency.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e3419\\\"]\"], [8965, \"2026-09-06\", 1, \"NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation is about $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue; that figure alone is rung 3 on this total-CPU-revenue ladder.\", \"About $20 billion of standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. That is above the rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation, which is rung 3 for total CPU revenue. I read the CEO's clarification that the $20 billion is standalone Vera CPU sales alongside Grace, rather than adding Grace to an already-total figure: the $20 billion Vera target plus Grace revenue above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just over $25 billion. Vera shipments and production are underway and customer deployments support the ramp; Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit, roughly $5,000 ASP estimate implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in achieving the full target, but does not outweigh the current company outlook and deployment evidence. The implied figure crosses the rung-1 boundary. It is also far below NVIDIA's approximately $407.1 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast in v14, so it is consistent with that dependency.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e884\\\"]\"], [8970, \"2026-09-07\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation is the relevant company reference; taken alone as total CPU revenue, it sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion inclusive).\", \"Approximately $25 billion or somewhat more: the roughly $20 billion standalone CPU expectation plus Grace CPU revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, treated as a run-rate into FY2027. This is an estimate, not a reported FY2027 total, and it lies just above the rung-1 boundary of $25 billion.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation, which sits at rung 3 if read as total CPU revenue. The CEO clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU sales; adding Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried forward as a run-rate, implies total FY2027 CPU revenue of roughly $25 billion or somewhat more. That crosses the rung-1 boundary. The inference is not certain: Wolfe's 1.3 million-unit calendar-2026 shipment forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion of Vera revenue and tempers confidence in the full target. But more recent evidence says Vera is in full production, shipments are underway, and AWS adopted Vera alongside a 2-million-GPU agreement; NVIDIA also cited customer demand and improving supply. The FY2028 more-than-doubling outlook supports momentum but does not itself increase the FY2027 estimate. The implied CPU figure remains well below NVIDIA's approximately $407 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast in dependency v14, so it is consistent with the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8975, \"2026-09-08\", 1, \"NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation; that reference sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $25 billion and slightly above: the $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation plus Grace CPU revenue exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried forward as an annual run-rate. This is above the $25 billion rung-1 threshold.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation, which sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion). The implied total is higher: add the $20 billion standalone Vera expectation to Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried forward as an annual run-rate, implying total CPU revenue slightly above $25 billion. That crosses the rung-1 boundary. The estimate is a forecast rather than an already-reported FY2027 result, but Vera is in production with shipments underway and disclosed customer deployments. The implied CPU revenue is well below NVIDIA's roughly $407 billion total FY2027 revenue forecast, so it is consistent with dependency v14. Competitive evidence and the limited shipment estimate temper confidence, but do not outweigh the company outlook and reported Grace run-rate.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8979, \"2026-09-09\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation is the reference; it sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera CPU revenue plus Grace\\u2019s more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue run-rate yields total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, implying rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, which sits at rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion). The evidence implies a higher total: the $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation is for Vera, while Grace separately had more than $5 billion of trailing-12-month revenue as of the Q2 FY2027 call. Carrying Grace\\u2019s reported TTM run-rate through FY2027 gives total CPU revenue above $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. The 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment forecast at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in execution, but it is a dated shipment forecast rather than a revision to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 guidance; shipments and deployments are underway. The FY2028 more-than-double outlook and improving supply outlook are supportive but do not change the FY2027 calculation. The implied total is far below NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $407 billion total FY2027 revenue forecast, so it is consistent with dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e2660\\\"]\"], [8982, \"2026-09-10\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's roughly $20B FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation is the available reference and sits in rung 3 ($15B\\u2013$20B). The nearly $20B figure was clarified as standalone CPU sales; no separate market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"Approximately $20B of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace's more-than-$5B trailing-12-month revenue run-rate carried through FY2027 implies total CPU revenue above $25B. That crosses the rung-1 boundary.\", \"The available reference is NVIDIA management's roughly $20B FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, which sits in rung 3 (at most $20B). The implied total is higher: management specified that the approximately $20B FY2027 expectation was for standalone CPU sales, while Grace CPU revenue had already exceeded $5B on a trailing-12-month basis; carrying that Grace run-rate through FY2027 gives total CPU revenue above $25B, crossing into rung 1. Vera shipments are underway and management cited improving supply, supporting the standalone outlook. Wolfe Research's 1.3M-unit CY2026 shipment forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5B for that period and tempers execution confidence, but it is a third-party, calendar-year forecast rather than a revision to management's FY2027 guidance. The FY2028 more-than-double outlook is supportive context, not added to FY2027 revenue. The dependency's roughly $407B FY2027 total-revenue forecast is consistent with this CPU estimate; CPU revenue remains well below the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\"]\"], [8987, \"2026-09-11\", 1, \"NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue expectation is the reference; it sits at rung 3 (at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue with a trailing-12-month run-rate already above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue above $25 billion, in rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA management's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue expectation, which sits at rung 3 (at most $20 billion). The implied total is above $25 billion: add the roughly $20 billion standalone outlook to Grace CPU revenue already exceeding $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis. Vera's production and shipments, plus the improving supply outlook, support delivery of the standalone forecast; the lower Wolfe shipment estimate for calendar 2026 tempers confidence but does not replace the FY2027 guidance or cover the full fiscal year. NVIDIA's total FY2027 revenue forecast in v14 is large enough to accommodate this CPU estimate.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e666\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [8991, \"2026-09-12\", 1, \"NVIDIA management\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue expectation is the available reference; treating that as the total-revenue reference places it at rung 3 (at most $20 billion).\", \"Approximately $25 billion-plus: carry NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook forward and add the continuing Grace CPU contribution, whose trailing-12-month revenue had already exceeded $5 billion. The latter is a run-rate input, not a claim that a single trailing-12-month figure by itself proves FY2027 revenue.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-revenue expectation, which is rung 3 if read as the total-revenue figure. The CEO clarified that the $20 billion is for standalone CPU, while Grace CPU revenue had already exceeded a $5 billion trailing-12-month run rate; carrying that Grace contribution alongside the standalone outlook implies total FY2027 CPU revenue modestly above $25 billion. The 1.3 million Vera shipments at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and temper execution confidence, but do not cover the full fiscal-year outlook; shipments and deployments are underway. AMD\\u2019s performance claims add competitive risk, but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 forecast. The implied total clears rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary, and the approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast in v14 is large enough to accommodate it.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\"]\"], [8996, \"2026-09-13\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 expectation is about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales (rung 3 if read as total CPU revenue). Since the CEO clarified that this is standalone and Grace is separate, the relevant total-CPU reference, adding the reported Grace run rate, sits just above $25 billion at rung 1.\", \"About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace CPU revenue carried at its reported trailing-12-month run rate above $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion. That is above rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion threshold; it remains well below the approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"The company\\u2019s FY2027 headline expectation is about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales, which would be rung 3 if treated as the answer to total CPU revenue. But Jensen Huang clarified that the figure is for standalone CPU, while Grace CPU revenue is separate; NVIDIA later reported Grace revenue above a $5 billion trailing-12-month run rate. Carrying that run rate alongside the roughly $20 billion standalone expectation implies total CPU revenue just above $25 billion, crossing rung 1\\u2019s boundary. Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera shipment forecast at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in the standalone ramp, but it is an earlier third-party forecast, not a revision of NVIDIA\\u2019s FY2027 outlook. Full production and shipments underway support execution; AMD\\u2019s competitive claims are a risk but do not quantify a cut to NVIDIA\\u2019s outlook. This CPU estimate remains a small part of the approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast in v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [9000, \"2026-09-14\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU revenue expectation is the market/company reference; it sits in rung 3. It is a stand-alone figure, not total CPU revenue.\", \"A little over $25 billion: approximately $20 billion of stand-alone CPU revenue plus a roughly $5 billion-or-more Grace contribution, using Grace's reported above-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue as the available scale indicator. The TTM amount is not a guaranteed FY2027 Grace figure, so the estimate is close to the boundary rather than a precise point forecast.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU revenue expectation, which sits in rung 3. For total CPU revenue, that standalone figure is joined by Grace: its trailing-12-month revenue was already above $5 billion as of the Q2 FY2027 call. That supports a forecast just over $25 billion, though the margin is not robust because the TTM period partly predates FY2027 and Vera is ramping while Grace may transition. Vera's full production, shipments and named customer deployments support the standalone ramp; AMD's performance claims are a competitive risk but do not quantify a reduction to NVIDIA's forecast. The implied figure is consistent with v14's approximately $407 billion total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue remains a small part of the whole.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\"]\"], [9003, \"2026-09-15\", 1, \"NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation is the closest supplied reference; it sits in rung 3. No separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied.\", \"About $25\\u201326 billion: approximately $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus a roughly $5 billion-or-more Grace contribution, using Grace\\u2019s reported trailing-12-month revenue as the run-rate indication. This is marginally above the $25 billion rung-1 boundary, though the TTM period partly predates FY2027.\", \"The closest market reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU revenue expectation, which is rung 3; the evidence supplies no separate market consensus for total CPU revenue. Adding Grace, whose trailing-12-month revenue was already above $5 billion by Q2 FY2027, implies total FY2027 CPU revenue around $25\\u201326 billion, just over rung 1\\u2019s $25 billion boundary. That margin is narrow: the Grace trailing period partly predates FY2027, and Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 each implies only about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026. But NVIDIA\\u2019s $20 billion FY2027 visibility, deliveries and rollout support the higher standalone forecast, while the AMD performance and market-share evidence does not quantify a reduction to it. The implied figure is a small part of the current dependency forecast of about $418 billion in total FY2027 revenue, so it is consistent with v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\"]\"], [9007, \"2026-09-16\", 1, \"No separate market consensus for total CPU revenue is supplied. NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation of about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales is the reference; that figure is in rung 3.\", \"About $25\\u201326 billion: carry Grace's more-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue into FY2027 and add NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion standalone CPU expectation. The estimate is narrowly above the $25 billion cutoff for rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation of about $20 billion for standalone CPU sales; there is no separate total-CPU market consensus shown, and $20 billion sits in rung 3. The implied total is about $25\\u201326 billion: the $20 billion Vera/standalone expectation plus Grace's trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried as a run-rate into FY2027. That is narrowly above rung 1's $25 billion boundary. Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera-chip estimate at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, and AMD's performance claims plus the roughly $20 billion server-CPU demand estimate temper confidence; none is a quantified revision or cancellation that displaces NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation. Deliveries, broad deployment plans and full production support execution. The implied total is far below NVIDIA's approximately $419 billion FY2027 company-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e4796\\\", \\\"e4797\\\"]\"], [9012, \"2026-09-17\", 1, \"NVIDIA's expectation of about $20 billion in FY2027 standalone CPU revenue; exactly $20 billion is rung 3.\", \"About $25 billion-plus: add NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation to Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion, carried as a run-rate through FY2027. That clears the rung 1 boundary of more than $25 billion, narrowly.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's expectation of about $20 billion in FY2027 standalone CPU revenue, which is rung 3 ($15\\u201320 billion, inclusive). For total CPU revenue, I carry Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion as a run-rate through FY2027 and add it to the $20 billion standalone figure, implying just over $25 billion and crossing into rung 1. Vera shipments and customer deployments support execution, though the unshipped volume implied by the full-year target remains uncertain. AMD's performance and share gains, analyst estimates, and the roughly $20 billion server-CPU-market estimate temper confidence but do not quantify a cut to NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation. The implied CPU total is consistent with v14's roughly $419 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast: CPU revenue remains a subset of total revenue. The AI-capex themes support demand, but are not themselves evidence of CPU sales.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e4796\\\", \\\"e4797\\\"]\"], [9016, \"2026-09-18\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation is the reference and sits at rung 3 (Neutral, $15\\u201320 billion).\", \"Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue above $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis, carried as a FY2027 run-rate, implies slightly more than $25 billion in total CPU revenue\\u2014rung 1.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation, which is rung 3. For total CPU revenue, adding Grace's reported trailing-12-month revenue of more than $5 billion to the approximately $20 billion standalone expectation implies just over $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and tempers confidence in the scale of the standalone forecast; however, NVIDIA later said Vera was in full production, cited customer demand and improving supply, and reiterated the FY2027 expectation. AMD's competitive claims and the approximately $20 billion server-CPU-market estimate are risks but do not quantify a cut to NVIDIA's FY2027 forecast. This component forecast is consistent with the dependency's approximately $419 billion FY2027 total-revenue forecast.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e667\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e794\\\", \\\"e884\\\", \\\"e885\\\", \\\"e886\\\", \\\"e887\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2239\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e3419\\\", \\\"e4395\\\", \\\"e4397\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e4796\\\", \\\"e4797\\\"]\"], [9019, \"2026-09-19\", 1, \"NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU expectation is the supplied reference; at exactly $20 billion it sits at rung 3. The supplied evidence does not give a separate market-consensus figure.\", \"About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue carried forward at its reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies just over $25 billion in total FY2027 CPU revenue. That clears the rung-1 threshold narrowly; the Vera shipment estimate of about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 is a material execution risk, but not a quantified revision to NVIDIA's expectation.\", \"The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 expectation of about $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3. The forecast for total CPU revenue adds Grace: carrying its reported greater-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue forward at roughly that run-rate alongside the $20 billion standalone expectation implies just over $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. This is a narrow crossing, not an established result: Wolfe's 1.3 million Vera shipments at about $5,000 each imply roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and temper confidence in the standalone forecast. But NVIDIA says Vera is in production and shipping to customers, and its FY2027 expectation remains supported by customer demand; AMD's performance claims do not quantify a revenue reduction. The implied total is well below the approximately $419 billion FY2027 company-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e723\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e2735\\\", \\\"e3073\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e5047\\\", \\\"e5048\\\", \\\"e5049\\\", \\\"e5050\\\", \\\"e5051\\\", \\\"e5052\\\", \\\"e5053\\\", \\\"e5054\\\"]\"], [9025, \"2026-09-20\", 1, \"NVIDIA's stated expectation of nearly $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue for FY2027; that reference is rung 3 ($15 billion to $20 billion).\", \"About $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus Grace revenue carried forward at its reported greater-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month run-rate implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion.\", \"NVIDIA's stated FY2027 expectation of nearly $20 billion in standalone CPU revenue is the reference and sits at rung 3. Adding Grace at its reported trailing-12-month revenue run-rate of more than $5 billion implies total FY2027 CPU revenue just above $25 billion, crossing the rung-1 boundary. Wolfe's lower Vera shipment estimate and AMD's performance claims temper confidence, but neither supplies a quantified revision to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue expectation; reported production and customer shipments support execution. The implied CPU total remains well below NVIDIA's roughly $419 billion total-revenue forecast in dependency v14.\", \"[\\\"v14\\\"]\", \"[\\\"e666\\\", \\\"e668\\\", \\\"e724\\\", \\\"e896\\\", \\\"e897\\\", \\\"e2311\\\", \\\"e2542\\\", \\\"e2543\\\", \\\"e2544\\\", \\\"e2659\\\", \\\"e2660\\\", \\\"e3087\\\", \\\"e4398\\\", \\\"e4758\\\", \\\"e5049\\\", \\\"e5050\\\", \\\"e5051\\\", \\\"e5052\\\", \\\"e5053\\\", \\\"e5054\\\"]\"]], [\"How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\"], [[1, \"Strongly bullish\", 25000000000.0, null], [2, \"Bullish\", 20000000000.0, 25000000000.0], [3, \"Neutral\", 15000000000.0, 20000000000.0], [4, \"Bearish\", 10000000000.0, 15000000000.0], [5, \"Strongly bearish\", null, 10000000000.0]]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nfor a in [\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"old-2\\\",\\\"old-3\\\",\\\"linked-1\\\",\\\"control-1\\\"]:\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n conn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n if a==\\\"old-2\\\":\n  for row in conn.execute(\\\"select * from evidence where evidence_id in (\\\\x27e2542\\\\x27,\\\\x27e896\\\\x27,\\\\x27e897\\\\x27,\\\\x27e51\\\\x27)\\\"):\n   print(row)\n print(a)\n for r in conn.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,original_day,quote from evidence where quote like \\\\x27%SemiAnalysis%20%%\\\\x27 or quote like \\\\x27%Wall Street%20%data center compute%\\\\x27 limit 12\\\"): print(r)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nfor a in [\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"old-2\\\",\\\"old-3\\\",\\\"linked-1\\\",\\\"control-1\\\"]:\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n conn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n if a==\\\"old-2\\\":\n  for row in conn.execute(\\\"select * from evidence where evidence_id in (\\\\x27e2542\\\\x27,\\\\x27e896\\\\x27,\\\\x27e897\\\\x27,\\\\x27e51\\\\x27)\\\"):\n   print(row)\n print(a)\n for r in conn.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,original_day,quote from evidence where quote like \\\\x27%SemiAnalysis%20%%\\\\x27 or quote like \\\\x27%Wall Street%20%data center compute%\\\\x27 limit 12\\\"): print(r)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"old-1\n('e61', '', \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\")\n('e141', '', \"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\")\n('e149', '', 'SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028.')\n('e154', '', 'SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.')\n('e190', '', 'Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028.')\n('e339', '', \"A December 2024 SemiAnalysis benchmark study found that AMD 's MI300X, despite higher marketed performance numbers, delivered 14% slower real-world results than Nvidia 's H100 and H200 in key training benchmarks.\")\n('e362', '', 'SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.')\n('e496', '', 'reports circulated from industry research firm SemiAnalysis suggesting that Nvidia’s upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028.')\n('e576', '', '“Vera Rubin is already in production. Giant amounts of production incoming,” Huang told reporters, rejecting delay concerns and dismissing a SemiAnalysis post that suggested a specialized circuit board issue could push the next-generation AI server rack into 2028.')\n('e1135', '', \"Even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\")\n('e1856', '', 'Research firm SemiAnalysis estimates the buildout at $50 billion per gigawatt annually, or $300 billion to $500 billion in 2027 capital spending alone, and considers the target achievable .')\n('e2542', 'Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 has been a great success, with revenue on a trailing 12-month basis, exceeding $5 billion.', 'NVIDIA Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis as of the Q2 FY2027 call.', 'Colette Kress', 'NVIDIA', '2026-08-26', 'party_statement', 'Trailing 12 months', 1072, 'truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26')\n('e51', 'Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.', 'NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.', 'fool.com (analyst)', 'NVIDIA', '', 'party_statement', 'FY2027', 49, 'gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348')\n('e896', 'Colette Kress, Executive Vice President and CFO, said Vera “opens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA, a market we have never addressed before,” adding: “We have visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, setting us up to become the world-leading CPU supplier.”', 'NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera opens a $200 billion market for NVIDIA and that the company has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, FY2027.', 'tikr.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress', 'Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO', '2026-05-20', 'party_statement', '2026-05-20 earnings call', 504, 'gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b')\n('e897', 'CEO Jensen Huang: “The $20 billion is for standalone CPU.”', 'NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the nearly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility for FY2027 is for standalone CPU sales.', 'tikr.com (analyst), quoting Jensen Huang', 'Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO', '2026-05-20', 'party_statement', '2026-05-20 earnings call', 504, 'gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b')\nold-2\n('e81', '', \"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\")\n('e88', '', \"Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\")\n('e93', '', 'Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year, from 2027 to 2028.')\n('e120', '', 'Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028.')\n('e250', '', 'SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.')\n('e366', '', 'reports circulated from industry research firm SemiAnalysis suggesting that Nvidia’s upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028')\n('e457', '', '“Vera Rubin is already in production. Giant amounts of production incoming,” Huang told reporters, rejecting delay concerns and dismissing a SemiAnalysis post that suggested a specialized circuit board issue could push the next-generation AI server rack into 2028.')\n('e972', '', \"Earlier this month, semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia ( NVDA -4.40% ) was facing major setbacks with its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale solution. The server cabinet, designed to house Nvidia's Rubin Ultra architecture, had been delayed over 12 months to 2028, according to SemiAnalysis.\")\n('e976', '', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\")\n('e1711', '', 'Research firm SemiAnalysis estimates the buildout at $50 billion per gigawatt annually, or $300 billion to $500 billion in 2027 capital spending alone, and considers the target achievable .')\nold-3\n('e60', '', \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\")\n('e127', '', \"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\")\n('e132', '', \"Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\")\n('e137', '', 'Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028.')\n('e140', '', 'SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.')\n('e180', '', 'Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028.')\n('e360', '', 'SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.')\n('e486', '', 'Over the weekend, reports circulated from industry research firm SemiAnalysis suggesting that Nvidia’s upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028.')\n('e580', '', '“Vera Rubin is already in production. Giant amounts of production incoming,” Huang told reporters, rejecting delay concerns and dismissing a SemiAnalysis post that suggested a specialized circuit board issue could push the next-generation AI server rack into 2028.')\n('e1110', '', \"Earlier this month, semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia ( NVDA -4.40% ) was facing major setbacks with its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale solution. The server cabinet, designed to house Nvidia's Rubin Ultra architecture, had been delayed over 12 months to 2028, according to SemiAnalysis.\")\n('e1116', '', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\")\n('e1864', '', 'Research firm SemiAnalysis estimates the buildout at $50 billion per gigawatt annually, or $300 billion to $500 billion in 2027 capital spending alone, and considers the target achievable .')\nlinked-1\n('e81', '', \"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\")\n('e88', '', \"Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\")\n('e93', '', 'Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year, from 2027 to 2028.')\n('e120', '', 'Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028.')\n('e250', '', 'SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.')\n('e366', '', 'reports circulated from industry research firm SemiAnalysis suggesting that Nvidia’s upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028')\n('e457', '', '“Vera Rubin is already in production. Giant amounts of production incoming,” Huang told reporters, rejecting delay concerns and dismissing a SemiAnalysis post that suggested a specialized circuit board issue could push the next-generation AI server rack into 2028.')\n('e972', '', \"Earlier this month, semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia ( NVDA -4.40% ) was facing major setbacks with its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale solution. The server cabinet, designed to house Nvidia's Rubin Ultra architecture, had been delayed over 12 months to 2028, according to SemiAnalysis.\")\n('e976', '', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\")\n('e1711', '', 'Research firm SemiAnalysis estimates the buildout at $50 billion per gigawatt annually, or $300 billion to $500 billion in 2027 capital spending alone, and considers the target achievable .')\ncontrol-1\n('e81', '', \"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\")\n('e88', '', \"Supply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane.\")\n('e93', '', 'Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year, from 2027 to 2028.')\n('e120', '', 'Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028.')\n('e250', '', 'SemiAnalysis reported that its Kyber NVL144 next-gen AI rack system could be delayed until 2028 due to troubles with manufacturing a crucial circuit board.')\n('e366', '', 'reports circulated from industry research firm SemiAnalysis suggesting that Nvidia’s upcoming Kyber NVL144 architecture could face delays extending into 2028')\n('e457', '', '“Vera Rubin is already in production. Giant amounts of production incoming,” Huang told reporters, rejecting delay concerns and dismissing a SemiAnalysis post that suggested a specialized circuit board issue could push the next-generation AI server rack into 2028.')\n('e972', '', \"Earlier this month, semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia ( NVDA -4.40% ) was facing major setbacks with its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale solution. The server cabinet, designed to house Nvidia's Rubin Ultra architecture, had been delayed over 12 months to 2028, according to SemiAnalysis.\")\n('e976', '', \"And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\")\n('e1711', '', 'Research firm SemiAnalysis estimates the buildout at $50 billion per gigawatt annually, or $300 bil