{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '30,110p;150,322p'; rg -n 'reference|consensus|quantified revision|accounting identities|part cannot|D537|D198' docs/prd/06-views.md docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -60\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '175,201p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; tail -8 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md; ls -l poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-{1,2,3}/prod/archive.sqlite\"","aggregated_output":"  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n| 34 | 11-05 | 第三版的登记测试：和第 8 轮一样，只换这一句；读 132 次（0.078 美元）、两人分类（第二位盲分）、读到数的 7 道题三遍重判、当天对照（108 次，0.268 美元） | **三条全过**：v10、v12 各 9/9；判对的题一道没变错（只有 v22 第 2 遍反过来对照 0/3、起点组 3/3，理由是在 41% 和 32% 两个增长估计间摇摆，和第 13 轮方向相反，与起点数无关）；46 个数全是自己的量（2 条是标明的二季度指引），两人分类全一致；v19、v20 的错数全没了，其余 15 道白卷、没冒出新错。没盖住：资本开支那一行试发时漏过 1 次；指引也被写成\"事实\"；一路判下来的效果和跨题效果要端到端才量得到 | 交 Alex：要不要做进生产、跑 14 天三遍端到端 |\n| 35 | 11-05 | 独立读者挑战\"现在做进生产、跑 14 天三遍\"的建议 | 核实属实的：资本开支那一行在新句子下 12 次读漏 3 次（旧句子 9/9，模型两次都怪\"更大的行\"半句）；句子照 5 月财报改、又只在 5 月财报上量，没有留出的财报；做进生产前的记录写法（同一个数挂几道题、指引不该写成\"事实\"、第二个提示词）要 Alex 定；分类和判断同时跑、说明里还引着旧句子，照实记下 | 建议撤回；下一步：修掉资本开支漏读（改成\"写完逐条复核\"），在 5 月和留出的 8 月两份财报上各测一遍，约 0.5 美元 |\n| 36 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好开始做到production 上，完整的更新所有系统设计等\"；同一个数挂几道题，他选\"存一条，挂到每道读到它的题\"（D364）。做进生产前先修资本开支漏读：第四版把\"是不是这道题的量\"挪到最后逐条复核（并删掉别的期间），文件规则补\"有申报的只读申报的\"；Codex 审 3 轮（共 6 条严重，都改）；试发 7 道题过；然后 5 月登记读法、8 月留出读法 | **没过**：5 月\"不能删掉该留的\"——v22 只留住 1/3、资本开支 3/6 读；8 月资本开支二季度 0/3（选了上半年、引用跳格被拒）。机理：复核里\"别的期间删掉\"让全年题删掉最近一季；\"最近一期\"在本季和年初至今两栏间没说清。好消息：8 月 v9 找到了新披露的担保 1,085 亿和承诺 560 亿 | 停，交 Alex：第三版是唯一过了 5 月三条的版本 |\n| 37 | 11-05 | Alex：\"为什么没过？…你确定是正确的rejection 吗？你又下去看trace吗？\"；\"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信下一步你要跑什么才能收敛\"。普查全部 7 次读法、606 次文件读（`census_all_reads.py`，$0） | 第三版 0 个错数、156 次读漏 3 次（全是资本开支）；第二版 2 个错数；第四版漏 24 次（5 月）、16 次（8 月）。我自己判错的：6 个对的数被我的引用检查拒（漏星号、跳格），8 月\"只认本季\"规则错，第 13 轮 3 条\"有争议\"的数是标准答案自己用的行，第三版全过后又改第四版 | 收敛方案：提示词定为第三版不再改；修引用检查（$0 重放全部 606 次读核对）；在 8 月财报上用第三版读一次（约 8 美分，只有\"收错数\"能叫停）；然后做进生产 |\n| 38 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好，按这三步做，你不要再搬石头砸自己脚了，你要收敛上生产\"。第 1 步：引用检查搬进正式代码 `src/hivemind/baseline.py`，加\"行名是报告的、数在同一行就存原文那一整行\"的修复（漏脚注记号、跳格）；对照 36 例全对，三个弄坏的版本各抓出 1 个错；重放全部存下的读法，改变的正好是那 6 个被拒的对数。第 2 步：第三版加修好的检查读 8 月留出财报 132 次（0.096 美元），两人分类 | 第 1 步：只改了那 6 条。第 2 步过：44 个数 0 个错（3 条\"剩余资本开支承诺 70 亿\"两人不一致，对着原文定为\"自己的量，别的期间\"）；v10 3/3、v12 2/3 | 读法定为第三版加修好的检查，不再改；第 3 步写正式代码 |\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff  336748544 Nov 18 01:20 poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-1/prod/archive.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff  282230784 Nov 18 01:20 poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod/archive.sqlite\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 lchingen  staff  297226240 Nov 18 01:20 poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-3/prod/archive.sqlite\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '30,110p;150,322p'; rg -n 'reference|consensus|quantified revision|accounting identities|part cannot|D537|D198' docs/prd/06-views.md docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -60\"","aggregated_output":"    30\tSUPPORTED_VERSIONS = ('judging-v7', 'judging-v8', 'judging-v12')   # D354: the frozen judging prompt; D694: v8 = v7's text with the fuller dependency block; D712/D713: v12 = v7's text and request with closed id lists\n    31\tFULL_DEPENDENCY_BLOCK = ('judging-v8',)\n    32\tCLOSED_LISTS = ('judging-v12',)   # the answer's id fields take the ids the request lists (D713)\n    33\tREADINGS = 5                      # a piece is shown in the thesis's first five judgements after it arrives\n    34\tBASIS = 10                        # and while one of the last ten judgements cited it\n    35\tTHEME_REASONS_CHARS = 300\n    36\tUPDATED_MARK = 'updated since this thesis was last judged'\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\tdef require_supported(prompt_version):\n    40\t    \"\"\"Refuses a judging prompt version this code does not build, before any request is made.\"\"\"\n    41\t    if prompt_version not in SUPPORTED_VERSIONS:\n    42\t        raise ValueError(f'judging {prompt_version!r} is not built; this code builds only {\", \".join(SUPPORTED_VERSIONS)} (D354)')\n    43\t\n    44\t\n    45\tdef evidence_in_view(conn, view_id):\n    46\t    \"\"\"(the pieces a judgement of this thesis reads, how many pieces in force are not shown): the arrivals (linked after the\n    47\t    READINGS-th most recent judgement, or all when there are fewer) and the basis (cited by one of the last BASIS judgements),\n    48\t    both among the evidence in force.\"\"\"\n    49\t    in_force = store.evidence_in_force(conn, view_id)\n    50\t    recent = store.recent_judgements(conn, view_id, BASIS)\n    51\t    arrivals_after = recent[READINGS - 1]['seq'] if len(recent) >= READINGS else -1\n    52\t    basis = {evidence_id for j in recent for evidence_id in j['cites']}\n    53\t    linked_at = {r['evidence_id']: r['seq'] for r in conn.execute('select evidence_id, seq from evidence_links where view_id=?', (view_id,))}\n    54\t    shown = [p for p in in_force if linked_at.get(p['evidence_id'], -1) > arrivals_after or p['evidence_id'] in basis]\n    55\t    return shown, len(in_force) - len(shown)\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\tdef schema_for(prompt_version, lists=None):\n    59\t    \"\"\"The answer's format. Under judging-v12 (D713) its four id fields take the ids the request lists (`lists`, from\n    60\t    `request_parts`): one shared enum of the evidence ids for cites and push ids, the dependency ids for cites_views, the theme ids\n    61\t    for theme pushes; a field with no id allowed is an array with maxItems 0. Other versions ignore `lists`.\"\"\"\n    62\t    schema = _open_format(prompt_version)\n    63\t    if prompt_version not in CLOSED_LISTS:\n    64\t        return schema\n    65\t    if lists is None:\n    66\t        raise ValueError(f'{prompt_version} builds its answer format from the ids its request lists')\n    67\t    properties = schema['properties']\n    68\t    evidence, dependencies, themes = (sorted(set(lists[key])) for key in ('evidence', 'dependencies', 'themes'))\n    69\t    if evidence:\n    70\t        schema['$defs'] = {'evidence_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence}}\n    71\t        properties['cites'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'$ref': '#/$defs/evidence_id'}}\n    72\t        push_item = properties['pushes']['items']\n    73\t        properties['pushes'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': dict(push_item, properties=dict(push_item['properties'], evidence_id={'$ref': '#/$defs/evidence_id'}))}\n    74\t    else:\n    75\t        properties['cites'] = dict(properties['cites'], maxItems=0)\n    76\t        properties['pushes'] = dict(properties['pushes'], maxItems=0)\n    77\t    if dependencies:\n    78\t        properties['cites_views'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string', 'enum': dependencies}}\n    79\t    else:\n    80\t        properties['cites_views'] = dict(properties['cites_views'], maxItems=0)\n    81\t    theme_item = properties['theme_pushes']['items']\n    82\t    if themes:\n    83\t        properties['theme_pushes'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': dict(theme_item, properties=dict(theme_item['properties'], view_id={'type': 'string', 'enum': themes}))}\n    84\t    else:\n    85\t        properties['theme_pushes'] = dict(properties['theme_pushes'], maxItems=0)\n    86\t    return schema\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\tdef _open_format(prompt_version):\n    90\t    require_supported(prompt_version)\n    91\t    pushes = PUSHES\n    92\t    schema = {\n    93\t        'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False,\n    94\t        'required': ['rung', 'reasons', 'cites', 'cites_views', 'pushes', 'changed', 'why_changed'],\n    95\t        'properties': {\n    96\t            'rung': {'type': 'integer'}, 'reasons': {'type': 'string'},\n    97\t            'cites': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}}, 'cites_views': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}},\n    98\t            'pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['evidence_id', 'push'],\n    99\t                                                  'properties': {'evidence_id': {'type': 'string'}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': pushes}}}},\n   100\t            'changed': {'type': 'boolean'}, 'why_changed': {'type': 'string'},\n   101\t        },\n   102\t    }\n   103\t    schema['required'].append('theme_pushes')\n   104\t    schema['properties']['theme_pushes'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['view_id', 'push'],\n   105\t                                                                       'properties': {'view_id': {'type': 'string'}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': pushes}}}}\n   106\t    schema['required'][1:1] = STANCE_FIELDS           # after rung: the reference and the implied outcome are written before the reasons\n   107\t    schema['properties'].update({field: {'type': 'string'} for field in STANCE_FIELDS})\n   108\t    return schema\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   150\t        if not progressed:                 # a cycle slipped through: keep going in the given order\n   151\t            ordered.extend(remaining)\n   152\t            break\n   153\t    return ordered\n   154\t\n   155\t\n   156\tdef _indented(text, indent='        '):\n   157\t    \"\"\"A stored value inside a block: every continuation line indented, so no physical line can read as a header or a listed id.\"\"\"\n   158\t    return str(text).replace('\\r\\n', '\\n').replace('\\n', '\\n' + indent)\n   159\t\n   160\t\n   161\tdef _dependency_lines_full(conn, target, d, state, rung_label, tv, flag):\n   162\t    \"\"\"judging-v8 (D694, design §3): the read judgement's comparison point and the system's forecast in full, labelled as ours, beside\n   163\t    the reasons excerpt; the read question not judged yet shows only that.\"\"\"\n   164\t    if not state:\n   165\t        return [f'- {target} ({flag}; {d[\"direction\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now not judged yet']\n   166\t    return [f'- {target} ({flag}; {d[\"direction\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"]} {rung_label}, judged {state[\"publication_day\"]}',\n   167\t            f'    Comparison point stated by that judgement: {_indented(state.get(\"reference\") or \"not stated\")}',\n   168\t            f'    The system\\'s forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): {_indented(state.get(\"implied_outcome\") or \"not stated\")}',\n   169\t            f'    Reasons (excerpt): {_indented((state.get(\"reasons\") or \"\")[:600])}']\n   170\t\n   171\t\n   172\tdef _lines_english(conn, view_id, v, rungs, pieces, deps, last, themes=(), hidden=0, full_dependencies=False):\n   173\t    \"\"\"hidden: how many pieces in force are not shown. full_dependencies: the judging-v8 dependency block.\"\"\"\n   174\t    lines = [f'Thesis {view_id} ({v[\"object_id\"]}, {v[\"kind\"]}, focus \"{v[\"looks_at\"]}\", {v[\"year\"] or \"\"}, due {v[\"due_day\"]}): {v[\"question\"]}',\n   175\t             'Ladder: ' + ' / '.join(f'rung {r[\"position\"]} {r[\"label\"]}: {r[\"condition\"]}' for r in rungs), '',\n   176\t             f'[Evidence in view under this thesis, {len(pieces)} items]']\n   177\t    if hidden:\n   178\t        lines.append(f'[Not shown: {hidden} more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]')\n   179\t    for p in pieces:\n   180\t        src = p['sources'][0] if p['sources'] else {}\n   181\t        lines.append(f'- {p[\"evidence_id\"]} [{p[\"tier\"]}; {p[\"speaker\"]}; original source {p[\"original_who\"]} {p[\"original_day\"]}; from {src.get(\"input_id\")} {src.get(\"publication_day\")}] {p[\"one_line\"]}\\n    Quote: {p[\"quote\"][:400]}')\n   182\t        if p.get('replaced'):\n   183\t            lines.append(f'    It replaces {p[\"replaced\"][\"evidence_id\"]} ({p[\"replaced\"][\"original_day\"]}): {p[\"replaced\"][\"one_line\"]}')\n   184\t        if p.get('same_as'):\n   185\t            lines.append(f'    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as {\", \".join(p[\"same_as\"])}; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.')\n   186\t        if p.get('revises'):\n   187\t            lines.append(f'    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of {p[\"revises\"][\"evidence_id\"]} ({p[\"revises\"][\"original_day\"]}); both are in force: {p[\"revises\"][\"one_line\"]}')\n   188\t    if deps:\n   189\t        lines.append('\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]')\n   190\t        for d in deps:\n   191\t            target = d['resolved_to']\n   192\t            if not target:\n   193\t                lines.append(f'- the {d[\"wait_year\"]} thesis on chain {d[\"wait_chain_view_id\"]}: not yet created ({d[\"direction\"]})')\n   194\t                continue\n   195\t            tv = store.view(conn, target)\n   196\t            state = store.current_state(conn, target)\n   197\t            flag = 'historical only' if d['resolution'] == 'history_only' else ('closed' if not store.is_open(conn, target) else 'open')\n   198\t            rung_label = store.rungs_of(conn, target)[state['rung'] - 1]['label'] if state else 'not judged yet'\n   199\t            if full_dependencies:\n   200\t                lines.extend(_dependency_lines_full(conn, target, d, state, rung_label, tv, flag))\n   201\t                continue\n   202\t            lines.append(f'- {target} ({flag}; {d[\"direction\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"] if state else \"-\"} {rung_label}; reasons: {(state or {}).get(\"reasons\", \"\")[:600]}')\n   203\t    if themes:\n   204\t        lines.append(f'\\n[Themes in force, {len(themes)}]')\n   205\t        for theme in themes:\n   206\t            tv, state = theme['view'], theme['state']\n   207\t            mark = f'; {UPDATED_MARK}' if theme['updated'] else ''\n   208\t            lines.append(f'- {theme[\"view_id\"]} ({tv[\"object_id\"]}, due {tv[\"due_day\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"]} {theme[\"rung_label\"]}; '\n   209\t                         f'judged {state[\"publication_day\"]}{mark}; reasons: {(state[\"reasons\"] or \"\")[:THEME_REASONS_CHARS]}')\n   210\t    if last:\n   211\t        lines.append(f'\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung {last[\"rung\"]}; reasons: {last[\"reasons\"][:800]}')\n   212\t    else:\n   213\t        lines.append('\\n[Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.')\n   214\t    lines.append('\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; '\n   215\t                 + ('cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above' if deps else 'cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis')\n   216\t                 + ('; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views' if themes else '') + '.')\n   217\t    return lines\n   218\t\n   219\t\n   220\tdef build_prompt(conn, view_id, prompt_version):\n   221\t    system, user, _ = request_parts(conn, view_id, prompt_version)\n   222\t    return system, user\n   223\t\n   224\t\n   225\tdef request_parts(conn, view_id, prompt_version):\n   226\t    \"\"\"(system text, user text, the ids the request lists), all from the same rows at the same ledger state: the evidence pieces it\n   227\t    prints, the dependencies it gives an entry (resolved ones; a dependency still waiting has no id to cite) and the themes it prints.\"\"\"\n   228\t    require_supported(prompt_version)\n   229\t    v = store.view(conn, view_id)\n   230\t    rungs = store.rungs_of(conn, view_id)\n   231\t    pieces, hidden = evidence_in_view(conn, view_id)\n   232\t    deps = store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id)\n   233\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n   234\t    themes = themes_in_force(conn, view_id)\n   235\t    lines = _lines_english(conn, view_id, v, rungs, pieces, deps, last, themes, hidden=hidden,\n   236\t                           full_dependencies=prompt_version in FULL_DEPENDENCY_BLOCK)\n   237\t    lists = {'evidence': sorted({p['evidence_id'] for p in pieces}), 'dependencies': sorted({d['resolved_to'] for d in deps if d['resolved_to']}),\n   238\t             'themes': sorted({theme['view_id'] for theme in themes})}\n   239\t    with open(config.prompt_path(prompt_version + '.txt'), encoding='utf-8') as handle:\n   240\t        return handle.read(), '\\n'.join(lines), lists\n   241\t\n   242\t\n   243\tdef retry_note(errors):\n   244\t    return '\\n\\nThe code returned the previous answer because: ' + '; '.join(errors) + '\\nCorrect it and answer again, citing only the listed ids.'\n   245\t\n   246\t\n   247\tdef themes_shown_in(request_text):\n   248\t    \"\"\"The theme ids of the block a request carried: the ids the answer may name, whatever the ledger says now.\"\"\"\n   249\t    block = request_text.split('\\n[Themes in force, ', 1)\n   250\t    return set(re.findall(r'^- (v\\d+) \\(', block[1].split('\\n\\n[', 1)[0], re.M)) if len(block) == 2 else set()\n   251\t\n   252\t\n   253\tdef evidence_shown_in(request_text):\n   254\t    \"\"\"The evidence ids the request listed: the ids the answer may cite and push.\"\"\"\n   255\t    block = request_text.split('\\n[Evidence in ', 1)\n   256\t    return set(re.findall(r'^- (e\\d+) \\[', block[1].split('\\n\\n[', 1)[0], re.M)) if len(block) == 2 else set()\n   257\t\n   258\t\n   259\tdef check_answer(conn, view_id, data, request_text=None, lists=None):\n   260\t    \"\"\"request_text: the user message that was sent, so theme and evidence ids are checked against what it carried (a theme may have\n   261\t    closed or opened since); without it, against what the ledger would show now. lists (judging-v12, D713): the ids the request\n   262\t    listed, from the rows that rendered it, the same lists its answer format closed; membership is checked against them.\"\"\"\n   263\t    errors = []\n   264\t    if lists is not None:\n   265\t        shown = set(lists['themes'])\n   266\t    else:\n   267\t        shown = themes_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {theme['view_id'] for theme in themes_in_force(conn, view_id)}\n   268\t    named = []\n   269\t    for entry in data.get('theme_pushes', []):\n   270\t        if entry['view_id'] in named:\n   271\t            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is named twice in theme_pushes')\n   272\t        elif entry['view_id'] not in shown:\n   273\t            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is not a theme in force shown to {view_id}')\n   274\t        named.append(entry['view_id'])\n   275\t    # the closed set is what the request showed (D198), read from the text that was sent, else computed from the ledger\n   276\t    if lists is not None:\n   277\t        allowed, deps = set(lists['evidence']), set(lists['dependencies'])\n   278\t    else:\n   279\t        allowed = evidence_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {p['evidence_id'] for p in evidence_in_view(conn, view_id)[0]}\n   280\t        deps = {d['resolved_to'] for d in store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id) if d['resolved_to']}\n   281\t    not_allowed = lambda e: f'{e} is not among the evidence shown to {view_id}'\n   282\t    bad_push = lambda e: f'push on {e} which was not shown to {view_id}'\n   283\t    rungs = store.rungs_of(conn, view_id)\n   284\t    if not 1 <= data['rung'] <= len(rungs):\n   285\t        errors.append(f'rung {data[\"rung\"]} outside 1..{len(rungs)}')\n   286\t    for cited in data['cites']:\n   287\t        if cited not in allowed:\n   288\t            errors.append(not_allowed(cited))\n   289\t    for cited in data['cites_views']:\n   290\t        if cited not in deps:\n   291\t            errors.append(f'{cited} is not a dependency of {view_id}')\n   292\t    for push in data['pushes']:\n   293\t        if push['evidence_id'] not in allowed:\n   294\t            errors.append(bad_push(push['evidence_id']))\n   295\t    for field in STANCE_FIELDS:\n   296\t        if not str(data.get(field) or '').strip():\n   297\t            errors.append(f'{field} is empty: state what the market expects and the figure or date the evidence implies')\n   298\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n   299\t    if last is None and not data['changed']:\n   300\t        errors.append('first judgement must say changed')\n   301\t    if last is not None and last['rung'] != data['rung'] and not data['changed']:\n   302\t        errors.append(f'rung moved from {last[\"rung\"]} to {data[\"rung\"]} but changed is false')\n   303\t    return errors\n   304\t\n   305\t\n   306\tdef _record(conn, cfg, view_id, seq, item, answer):\n   307\t    data = vocabulary.judgement_answer(answer.data)\n   308\t    judgement_id = store.insert(conn, 'judgements', view_id=view_id, seq=seq, input_id=item['input_id'], rung=data['rung'],\n   309\t                                reasons=data['reasons'], cited_evidence_json=json.dumps(data['cites']), cited_views_json=json.dumps(data['cites_views']),\n   310\t                                pushes_json=json.dumps(data['pushes'], ensure_ascii=False), changed=1 if data['changed'] else 0,\n   311\t                                theme_pushes_json=json.dumps(data.get('theme_pushes', []), ensure_ascii=False),\n   312\t                                reference=data.get('reference'), implied_outcome=data.get('implied_outcome'),\n   313\t                                why_changed=data['why_changed'], config_checksum=cfg.checksum, call_id=answer.call_id,\n   314\t                                request_sha256=answer.request_sha256, publication_day=item['publication_day'])\n   315\t    conn.commit()\n   316\t    return {'view_id': view_id, 'judgement_id': judgement_id, 'rung': data['rung'], 'changed': data['changed'], 'cost': answer.cost_usd, 'cached': answer.cached}\n   317\t\n   318\t\n   319\tJUDGE_WORKERS = 32     # a day's round is up to 40 judgements of about 16 s each in two dependency levels (themes, then assets); 32 at a time makes a round about two calls long (2026-11-02)\n   320\t\n   321\t\n   322\tdef upstream(conn, view_id):\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9318:- **Decided (consensus; D439's limits):**\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9334:     - **Today:** will he mark the deck, and may D658 wait until his marks (recommended yes to both)? If the reference is to continue, he\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9335:       chooses between completing the approved luna-question reference and a separately reviewed design for astra's questions.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9388:  2. May D658's new reference wait?\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9395:  - Question 1 goes to a council, which determines it and records its consensus with his words, as an exercise of the authority he gave. The\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9398:## D665 · 2026-11-17 · Council (D664, Alex's \"run your council to determine\"): the six lines stop blocking release; one product comparison against the running pipeline decides readiness, its verdict written before the first send (council consensus)\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9428:     - D431's frozen-reference reading of 07-01 to 07-14 is still produced and printed beside the comparison, not deciding. D665 supersedes D431\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9517:- **Recorded** after consensus: Codex READY at round 10 (`review-codex-10.md`); the Claude reviewer READY at round 7. Rounds 8–10 changed only the record's wording and the corpus-copy rule Codex raised. The Claude reviewer's build-level notes are carried in `freeze-review-notes.md`.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9526:    - the three-month runs (v29-3m, v34-3m) and the run3 family were served as the product, but their traces drove changes: D151, D198,\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9792:      - a key defined by the Step 2 reference's question 2 and made by two readers plus a third;\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9821:- **Round 8: the council's consensus on Step 2's line 3, a hard check** (Codex in `tools-review-codex-8.md`, and a Claude reader;\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9828:    analyst would want to keep\" (the Step 2 reference's question 2; PRD §6, \"details a later report adds, such as a guidance\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:9871:- **Round 10: the council's consensus is that the §1.6 tools may read the run's outputs.**\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10007:## D676 · 2026-11-19 · The release comparison: the new system loses for two known mechanisms; keep the old system; the outcome was computable before the run (council consensus)\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10035:- **Decided (council consensus):**\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10042:## D677 · 2026-11-19 · The release comparison's goal loop is closed; the old system stays; no paid work continues (council consensus, D439/D544)\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10061:## D678 · 2026-11-19 · Merge design (council consensus): the old system opens questions, the clean system reads and organises evidence, the same judge answers; a 12-article test awaits Alex's approval\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10065:- **Council:** Claude's proposal (written first), Codex's cold independent design and one challenge round, which ended in consensus.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10143:  - 24 answers rejected by both readers. In about 16 of them the answer leaves its own reference's rung on a weak step: overlapping\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10145:  - The judge writes the rung first and the reference and implied outcome last, in all 48 stored answers read. `schema_for` appends\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10152:- Council thread `01a1f7a7…`, rounds A to G (`docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/council-*-codex.md`), consensus at E for the sends and at G for\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10164:  - It is confounded: in 34 of 48 requests, the previous conclusion also carries hidden pieces (D198).\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10173:- **For Alex after the separation:** whether to change how the previous conclusion is presented (within D198), or what it carries\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10174:  (a change to D198).\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10188:- **Decided (council H, consensus):**\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10191:  - one recommendation goes to Alex: keep the previous conclusion and its reasons intact (what D198 carries), and change only its\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10212:## D685 · 2026-11-20 · Under D684 (councils I–K): the fresh test cannot be fed; H1 fails its diagnostic; the judge line pauses within D198; phase 2 (opening) starts\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10223:- **Reflection council K** (a cold read, consensus with Claude's reflection apart from one point):\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10224:  - Four angles on the judge have failed. Every remaining lever changes D198.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10227:    - D198 stays in place, and its options are not taken to Alex yet;\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10233:  - Claude's reflection had proposed taking D198's options to Alex now. Council K: not before the census shows what the hybrid can\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10294:  - What binds: the list counts finer than D248/D388 on 9 matters, the library's NVIDIA-only subject on 5, and D198 for the judge's 2.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10319:- **Decided (council N, under D543):** the judge line and the c8 reading line close for now. Nothing is sent, and D198 is not changed.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10325:    - Every remaining lever changes D198, and every big numeric bet on this corpus has been read by the design.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10332:    - Matched supported controls, and a source-checked summary of the omitted facts worth testing (D198 option 2), are also needed.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10333:    - Then the D198 choice and its price go to Alex. A paid test needs his approval and funding.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10366:    and whether the judge carries its previous conclusion (D198). \"One bet, one question\" (D248) was not offered for change, because\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10379:- **Council Q** (`docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/RESULT.md`, consensus, $0): an analyst would use the\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10391:  The census of that stretch (D537) found the useful pair to be Data Center revenue reading total revenue.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10396:- **Next:** council round 3 on the probe's design (`dependency-probe/design.md`), then the send (18 requests, cap $0.40) on consensus.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10409:  self-made $414B reference.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10410:- **Decided (council consensus under D691):** council Q's PARTLY and the deferral of the daily wrapper stand. The dependency design\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10412:  before any build or send. The self-made reference is traced apart on stored answers, at $0.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10414:## D693 · 2026-11-21 · Under Alex's A (D691): the design for linking related questions reaches council consensus at $0; three choices go to Alex before any build or send; the self-made reference is traced\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10419:  round 2 DISAGREE with five changes, round 3 DISAGREE on one, all taken; consensus): for the product (poc-v118, no seed edges) a\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10429:- **The self-made reference** (`docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/self-reference/RESULT.md`, `reference_census.py`, $0): 44 of\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10430:  2,375 number judgements (1.9%) present the system's own earlier figure as the reference, 33 of them on the FY2027 Data Center question\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10433:  revenue only. Its own fix (a sourced reference) returns to Alex separately, after the link is measured.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10434:- **Decided (council consensus; Alex's choices pending):** nothing is built or sent. To Alex: (a) a chosen reading link leaves filing\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10448:- **Decided (Alex):** A. poc-v118 stays the product and the control; the linking version is a new configuration. The sourced-reference\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10503:  decision that makes a linking version the product. The sourced-reference change (D693) waits for a longer linked run.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10509:  still contradict each other, which links the matcher chose and whether any is wrong, whether the self-made market reference becomes\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10570:  either question could read the other, the one less anchored by guidance, consensus or a reported figure for the quantity and full\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10573:  this probe tests under D703; D537 decided only the Data Center case.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10674:- **Council round 37** (`round37-brief.md`, `codex-r37.md`, `claude-r37.md`, `round37b-brief.md`, `codex-r37b.md`), consensus A:\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10737:  - **T4:** the largest request in the five archives is 464 enum values counted once (915 per reference); in the sealed D358 run, 676\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10738:    (1,323 per reference).\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10749:  - provider acceptance of the format and its counting of a referenced enum;\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10780:  request itself names in \"same claim as …\" notes, which is a latent invitation. e393 is the $393 billion reference written as an id.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10804:  answers #2 and #3 name Bloomberg for the $393 billion reference while citing only e669 (Wall Street's average via Yahoo Finance);\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; from collections import defaultdict\nd=defaultdict(list)\nfor r in json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/readings-claude.json\\\")): d[r[\\\"store\\\"],r[\\\"view\\\"],r[\\\"class\\\"],r[\\\"rung_decided_by_it\\\"]].append((r[\\\"call_id\\\"],r[\\\"day\\\"][5:]))\nfor k,v in d.items(): print(k,len(v),v)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; from collections import defaultdict\nd=defaultdict(list)\nfor r in json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/readings-claude.json\\\")): d[r[\\\"store\\\"],r[\\\"view\\\"],r[\\\"class\\\"],r[\\\"rung_decided_by_it\\\"]].append((r[\\\"call_id\\\"],r[\\\"day\\\"][5:]))\nfor k,v in d.items(): print(k,len(v),v)'\"","aggregated_output":"('old-1', 'v4', 'added', 'no') 17 [(354, '07-07'), (1236, '07-18'), (1250, '07-19'), (1569, '07-22'), (1691, '07-23'), (1782, '07-24'), (2313, '07-28'), (2727, '08-03'), (3612, '08-16'), (3767, '08-17'), (3902, '08-18'), (4037, '08-19'), (4074, '08-20'), (4200, '08-22'), (4263, '08-23'), (4456, '08-24'), (4580, '08-25')]\n('old-1', 'v4', 'added', 'certain') 4 [(978, '07-15'), (1142, '07-16'), (1208, '07-17'), (4163, '08-21')]\n('old-1', 'v4', 'added', 'possible') 11 [(2539, '07-30'), (2631, '07-31'), (2870, '08-06'), (2907, '08-07'), (2946, '08-08'), (2978, '08-09'), (3056, '08-10'), (3224, '08-11'), (3293, '08-12'), (3464, '08-14'), (3561, '08-15')]\n('old-3', 'v3', 'upside only', 'no') 2 [(574, '07-11'), (1575, '07-22')]\n('linked-1', 'v14', 'added', 'certain') 4 [(8756, '07-15'), (8759, '07-16'), (8763, '07-17'), (8766, '07-18')]\n('linked-1', 'v9', 'copied', 'no') 4 [(8761, '07-16'), (8765, '07-17'), (8768, '07-18'), (8769, '07-19')]\n('linked-1', 'v21', 'upside only', 'no') 1 [(8794, '07-26')]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '89,110p;156,243p;259,321p'; rg -n '\"'^## D(186|191|198|260|537)'\"\\\\b' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; rg -n 'Judg|reference|dependencies|ladder' docs/prd/06-views.md | tail -45\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '89,110p;156,243p;259,321p'; rg -n '\"'^## D(186|191|198|260|537)'\"\\\\b' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; rg -n 'Judg|reference|dependencies|ladder' docs/prd/06-views.md | tail -45\"","aggregated_output":"    89\tdef _open_format(prompt_version):\n    90\t    require_supported(prompt_version)\n    91\t    pushes = PUSHES\n    92\t    schema = {\n    93\t        'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False,\n    94\t        'required': ['rung', 'reasons', 'cites', 'cites_views', 'pushes', 'changed', 'why_changed'],\n    95\t        'properties': {\n    96\t            'rung': {'type': 'integer'}, 'reasons': {'type': 'string'},\n    97\t            'cites': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}}, 'cites_views': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}},\n    98\t            'pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['evidence_id', 'push'],\n    99\t                                                  'properties': {'evidence_id': {'type': 'string'}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': pushes}}}},\n   100\t            'changed': {'type': 'boolean'}, 'why_changed': {'type': 'string'},\n   101\t        },\n   102\t    }\n   103\t    schema['required'].append('theme_pushes')\n   104\t    schema['properties']['theme_pushes'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['view_id', 'push'],\n   105\t                                                                       'properties': {'view_id': {'type': 'string'}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': pushes}}}}\n   106\t    schema['required'][1:1] = STANCE_FIELDS           # after rung: the reference and the implied outcome are written before the reasons\n   107\t    schema['properties'].update({field: {'type': 'string'} for field in STANCE_FIELDS})\n   108\t    return schema\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   156\tdef _indented(text, indent='        '):\n   157\t    \"\"\"A stored value inside a block: every continuation line indented, so no physical line can read as a header or a listed id.\"\"\"\n   158\t    return str(text).replace('\\r\\n', '\\n').replace('\\n', '\\n' + indent)\n   159\t\n   160\t\n   161\tdef _dependency_lines_full(conn, target, d, state, rung_label, tv, flag):\n   162\t    \"\"\"judging-v8 (D694, design §3): the read judgement's comparison point and the system's forecast in full, labelled as ours, beside\n   163\t    the reasons excerpt; the read question not judged yet shows only that.\"\"\"\n   164\t    if not state:\n   165\t        return [f'- {target} ({flag}; {d[\"direction\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now not judged yet']\n   166\t    return [f'- {target} ({flag}; {d[\"direction\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"]} {rung_label}, judged {state[\"publication_day\"]}',\n   167\t            f'    Comparison point stated by that judgement: {_indented(state.get(\"reference\") or \"not stated\")}',\n   168\t            f'    The system\\'s forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): {_indented(state.get(\"implied_outcome\") or \"not stated\")}',\n   169\t            f'    Reasons (excerpt): {_indented((state.get(\"reasons\") or \"\")[:600])}']\n   170\t\n   171\t\n   172\tdef _lines_english(conn, view_id, v, rungs, pieces, deps, last, themes=(), hidden=0, full_dependencies=False):\n   173\t    \"\"\"hidden: how many pieces in force are not shown. full_dependencies: the judging-v8 dependency block.\"\"\"\n   174\t    lines = [f'Thesis {view_id} ({v[\"object_id\"]}, {v[\"kind\"]}, focus \"{v[\"looks_at\"]}\", {v[\"year\"] or \"\"}, due {v[\"due_day\"]}): {v[\"question\"]}',\n   175\t             'Ladder: ' + ' / '.join(f'rung {r[\"position\"]} {r[\"label\"]}: {r[\"condition\"]}' for r in rungs), '',\n   176\t             f'[Evidence in view under this thesis, {len(pieces)} items]']\n   177\t    if hidden:\n   178\t        lines.append(f'[Not shown: {hidden} more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]')\n   179\t    for p in pieces:\n   180\t        src = p['sources'][0] if p['sources'] else {}\n   181\t        lines.append(f'- {p[\"evidence_id\"]} [{p[\"tier\"]}; {p[\"speaker\"]}; original source {p[\"original_who\"]} {p[\"original_day\"]}; from {src.get(\"input_id\")} {src.get(\"publication_day\")}] {p[\"one_line\"]}\\n    Quote: {p[\"quote\"][:400]}')\n   182\t        if p.get('replaced'):\n   183\t            lines.append(f'    It replaces {p[\"replaced\"][\"evidence_id\"]} ({p[\"replaced\"][\"original_day\"]}): {p[\"replaced\"][\"one_line\"]}')\n   184\t        if p.get('same_as'):\n   185\t            lines.append(f'    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as {\", \".join(p[\"same_as\"])}; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.')\n   186\t        if p.get('revises'):\n   187\t            lines.append(f'    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of {p[\"revises\"][\"evidence_id\"]} ({p[\"revises\"][\"original_day\"]}); both are in force: {p[\"revises\"][\"one_line\"]}')\n   188\t    if deps:\n   189\t        lines.append('\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]')\n   190\t        for d in deps:\n   191\t            target = d['resolved_to']\n   192\t            if not target:\n   193\t                lines.append(f'- the {d[\"wait_year\"]} thesis on chain {d[\"wait_chain_view_id\"]}: not yet created ({d[\"direction\"]})')\n   194\t                continue\n   195\t            tv = store.view(conn, target)\n   196\t            state = store.current_state(conn, target)\n   197\t            flag = 'historical only' if d['resolution'] == 'history_only' else ('closed' if not store.is_open(conn, target) else 'open')\n   198\t            rung_label = store.rungs_of(conn, target)[state['rung'] - 1]['label'] if state else 'not judged yet'\n   199\t            if full_dependencies:\n   200\t                lines.extend(_dependency_lines_full(conn, target, d, state, rung_label, tv, flag))\n   201\t                continue\n   202\t            lines.append(f'- {target} ({flag}; {d[\"direction\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"] if state else \"-\"} {rung_label}; reasons: {(state or {}).get(\"reasons\", \"\")[:600]}')\n   203\t    if themes:\n   204\t        lines.append(f'\\n[Themes in force, {len(themes)}]')\n   205\t        for theme in themes:\n   206\t            tv, state = theme['view'], theme['state']\n   207\t            mark = f'; {UPDATED_MARK}' if theme['updated'] else ''\n   208\t            lines.append(f'- {theme[\"view_id\"]} ({tv[\"object_id\"]}, due {tv[\"due_day\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"]} {theme[\"rung_label\"]}; '\n   209\t                         f'judged {state[\"publication_day\"]}{mark}; reasons: {(state[\"reasons\"] or \"\")[:THEME_REASONS_CHARS]}')\n   210\t    if last:\n   211\t        lines.append(f'\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung {last[\"rung\"]}; reasons: {last[\"reasons\"][:800]}')\n   212\t    else:\n   213\t        lines.append('\\n[Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.')\n   214\t    lines.append('\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; '\n   215\t                 + ('cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above' if deps else 'cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis')\n   216\t                 + ('; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views' if themes else '') + '.')\n   217\t    return lines\n   218\t\n   219\t\n   220\tdef build_prompt(conn, view_id, prompt_version):\n   221\t    system, user, _ = request_parts(conn, view_id, prompt_version)\n   222\t    return system, user\n   223\t\n   224\t\n   225\tdef request_parts(conn, view_id, prompt_version):\n   226\t    \"\"\"(system text, user text, the ids the request lists), all from the same rows at the same ledger state: the evidence pieces it\n   227\t    prints, the dependencies it gives an entry (resolved ones; a dependency still waiting has no id to cite) and the themes it prints.\"\"\"\n   228\t    require_supported(prompt_version)\n   229\t    v = store.view(conn, view_id)\n   230\t    rungs = store.rungs_of(conn, view_id)\n   231\t    pieces, hidden = evidence_in_view(conn, view_id)\n   232\t    deps = store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id)\n   233\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n   234\t    themes = themes_in_force(conn, view_id)\n   235\t    lines = _lines_english(conn, view_id, v, rungs, pieces, deps, last, themes, hidden=hidden,\n   236\t                           full_dependencies=prompt_version in FULL_DEPENDENCY_BLOCK)\n   237\t    lists = {'evidence': sorted({p['evidence_id'] for p in pieces}), 'dependencies': sorted({d['resolved_to'] for d in deps if d['resolved_to']}),\n   238\t             'themes': sorted({theme['view_id'] for theme in themes})}\n   239\t    with open(config.prompt_path(prompt_version + '.txt'), encoding='utf-8') as handle:\n   240\t        return handle.read(), '\\n'.join(lines), lists\n   241\t\n   242\t\n   243\tdef retry_note(errors):\n   259\tdef check_answer(conn, view_id, data, request_text=None, lists=None):\n   260\t    \"\"\"request_text: the user message that was sent, so theme and evidence ids are checked against what it carried (a theme may have\n   261\t    closed or opened since); without it, against what the ledger would show now. lists (judging-v12, D713): the ids the request\n   262\t    listed, from the rows that rendered it, the same lists its answer format closed; membership is checked against them.\"\"\"\n   263\t    errors = []\n   264\t    if lists is not None:\n   265\t        shown = set(lists['themes'])\n   266\t    else:\n   267\t        shown = themes_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {theme['view_id'] for theme in themes_in_force(conn, view_id)}\n   268\t    named = []\n   269\t    for entry in data.get('theme_pushes', []):\n   270\t        if entry['view_id'] in named:\n   271\t            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is named twice in theme_pushes')\n   272\t        elif entry['view_id'] not in shown:\n   273\t            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is not a theme in force shown to {view_id}')\n   274\t        named.append(entry['view_id'])\n   275\t    # the closed set is what the request showed (D198), read from the text that was sent, else computed from the ledger\n   276\t    if lists is not None:\n   277\t        allowed, deps = set(lists['evidence']), set(lists['dependencies'])\n   278\t    else:\n   279\t        allowed = evidence_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {p['evidence_id'] for p in evidence_in_view(conn, view_id)[0]}\n   280\t        deps = {d['resolved_to'] for d in store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id) if d['resolved_to']}\n   281\t    not_allowed = lambda e: f'{e} is not among the evidence shown to {view_id}'\n   282\t    bad_push = lambda e: f'push on {e} which was not shown to {view_id}'\n   283\t    rungs = store.rungs_of(conn, view_id)\n   284\t    if not 1 <= data['rung'] <= len(rungs):\n   285\t        errors.append(f'rung {data[\"rung\"]} outside 1..{len(rungs)}')\n   286\t    for cited in data['cites']:\n   287\t        if cited not in allowed:\n   288\t            errors.append(not_allowed(cited))\n   289\t    for cited in data['cites_views']:\n   290\t        if cited not in deps:\n   291\t            errors.append(f'{cited} is not a dependency of {view_id}')\n   292\t    for push in data['pushes']:\n   293\t        if push['evidence_id'] not in allowed:\n   294\t            errors.append(bad_push(push['evidence_id']))\n   295\t    for field in STANCE_FIELDS:\n   296\t        if not str(data.get(field) or '').strip():\n   297\t            errors.append(f'{field} is empty: state what the market expects and the figure or date the evidence implies')\n   298\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n   299\t    if last is None and not data['changed']:\n   300\t        errors.append('first judgement must say changed')\n   301\t    if last is not None and last['rung'] != data['rung'] and not data['changed']:\n   302\t        errors.append(f'rung moved from {last[\"rung\"]} to {data[\"rung\"]} but changed is false')\n   303\t    return errors\n   304\t\n   305\t\n   306\tdef _record(conn, cfg, view_id, seq, item, answer):\n   307\t    data = vocabulary.judgement_answer(answer.data)\n   308\t    judgement_id = store.insert(conn, 'judgements', view_id=view_id, seq=seq, input_id=item['input_id'], rung=data['rung'],\n   309\t                                reasons=data['reasons'], cited_evidence_json=json.dumps(data['cites']), cited_views_json=json.dumps(data['cites_views']),\n   310\t                                pushes_json=json.dumps(data['pushes'], ensure_ascii=False), changed=1 if data['changed'] else 0,\n   311\t                                theme_pushes_json=json.dumps(data.get('theme_pushes', []), ensure_ascii=False),\n   312\t                                reference=data.get('reference'), implied_outcome=data.get('implied_outcome'),\n   313\t                                why_changed=data['why_changed'], config_checksum=cfg.checksum, call_id=answer.call_id,\n   314\t                                request_sha256=answer.request_sha256, publication_day=item['publication_day'])\n   315\t    conn.commit()\n   316\t    return {'view_id': view_id, 'judgement_id': judgement_id, 'rung': data['rung'], 'changed': data['changed'], 'cost': answer.cost_usd, 'cached': answer.cached}\n   317\t\n   318\t\n   319\tJUDGE_WORKERS = 32     # a day's round is up to 40 judgements of about 16 s each in two dependency levels (themes, then assets); 32 at a time makes a round about two calls long (2026-11-02)\n   320\t\n   321\t\n2476:## D186 · 2026-10-26 · Asset-thesis rungs describe business outcomes; the favourable end is registered per thesis\n2503:## D191 · 2026-10-26 · Theme theses reach every company; no named dependant is required to open one\n2559:## D198 · 2026-10-27 · A judgement reads the evidence in view, not everything in force\n3049:## D260 · 2026-11-02 · A judgement is a forecast against a reference: the rung the evidence in force makes most likely, judged against what the market expects; the answer states the reference and the implied outcome (judging-v6)\n6457:## D537 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency-selection census has one clear case and negative controls; before any send, the edge's effect on filing and judging is proved on stored data (council consensus, reflection round 28)\n37:│       ├─ State → The thesis's current ladder rung (Evidence pushes towards either end; the model forecasts the rung most likely at the due date, against a reference (D260). A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it.)\n39:│       ├─ Dependency → Another thesis (Direction specifies which end of the other thesis is bullish for an asset thesis, or whether the two ladders have the same or opposite orientation for a theme thesis.)\n59:<!-- generated: ladder -->\n61:Two kinds of ladder (Illustrative ranges)\n76:1. A ladder has five rungs. Asset theses run from strongly bullish to strongly bearish, describing business impact; theme theses name five ordered outcomes without good/bad labels. Use two rungs for yes/no rather than forcing five: bullish/bearish for assets; named outcomes for themes, such as signed by the deadline/not signed. Decided for the next extraction version (D186, D368): asset rungs state the business outcome against guidance (above, near, below) and each thesis names its favourable end.\n78:3. One ladder concerns one thing. Separate numeric and event questions and connect them with dependencies. Likewise, separate components that can move in opposite directions, such as H20 and Blackwell chip classes.\n79:4. The extraction model writes the candidate's ladder. A new thesis adopts it unchanged until expiry. A merged candidate uses the existing thesis's ladder and due date.\n82:<!-- /generated: ladder -->\n89:| State | Our judgement of the thesis's current rung; evidence pushes toward either end | The model judges the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, against a reference (below). It reads the evidence in view, the arrivals since its fifth most recent judgement and the basis its last ten judgements cited, the request naming how many more pieces stay in force (D198); a revision is shown beside the piece it revises and a repeat beside the piece the matcher read it as, both in force (D202, D204); plus current dependency states and reasons and, for an asset thesis, the theme block. Do not increment or decrement the previous score. Cite evidence and compare with the conclusion before this round to state whether it changed. Do not rejudge if only source references were added and dependency conclusions did not change. | After the 8-K: strongly bearish |\n92:- Code checks cited IDs against the request that was sent: evidence must be among the pieces shown to this thesis (D198); cited theses must be its dependencies. Accept only if all checks pass. Otherwise record the reason and ask for one corrected judgement. If that also fails, use normal failure handling: retry from this step, then human review after repeated failure. This checks references, not whether the judgement is correct, like verifying a cited page exists.\n95:- The rung is a forecast against a reference (D260): the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, judged against what the market expects (consensus, guidance, the stated plan or deadline). The judgement names the reference and its rung and the outcome the evidence implies (a figure or date derived from reported figures, guidance and quantified revisions), and moves when the implied outcome crosses a rung boundary. A consensus figure alone keeps the judgement at the reference's rung and is not the reason to stay once other evidence has arrived; \"not established\" is confidence, not a reason to stay. From judging-v6 the reference and the implied outcome are required fields of the answer, stored beside the rung; an empty one is refused.\n116:<!-- generated: dependencies -->\n118:B depends on A | What kind of thesis is A? (For an asset thesis B, state which end of A is bullish for B. For a theme thesis B, state whether their ladders have the same or opposite orientation.)\n133:- Rejudge an open thesis when its inputs change: new evidence, replacement, a new link to existing evidence, a dependency declaring a changed conclusion, or (for an asset thesis) a theme thesis declaring one. Additional source references alone do not count. Binding an awaited A or moving to its successor also changes B's inputs. Closed theses do not update. A memo-derived thesis is first judged when evidence first links to it; before that, it and its dependants see “not yet judged”.\n134:- The matching call chooses a new thesis's dependencies, stating which conclusions it relies on and each direction (D51). Choose only among searched theses, extraction block-5 theses and candidates opened from the same item; no extra search. Jev, the default matcher, is not asked for them, so new theses do not receive dependencies through the default matcher; building the choice on the Jev path is decided (D368). The historical seed questions are retired (D370). A historical seed's dependency arrow means \"supports\" (D368, by D208): for filing, the supporting (driver) thesis is the narrower one; for judging, the supported (root) thesis reads the supporting theses' conclusions, so drivers and themes are judged before their roots. The judging code reads the stored edge the other way today; the fix is to be built and measured (D368). Completed events are evidence, not dependencies: today's oil price influencing OPEC output is evidence for the OPEC-output thesis.\n136:<!-- /generated: dependencies -->\n143:- Keep ladder orientation across succession: a strongly bullish first rung remains strongly bullish, although thresholds can change. Dependencies retain their direction; reversing the ends would break it.\n166:  ↓ 2 Receive evidence (Code): Retries do not duplicate evidence. A revised input updates only its own source reference. A revised retelling does not alter the original evidence. If the revision is from the original source, compare its statements normally: a changed statement is stored as a revision of the old one, both in force (D202).\n168:  ↓ 4 Match (Model): The matcher does not read the original item. Jev answers, with a score, each candidate's relation to every thesis on its list (shown with its subject, question, focus, ladder, due date and open/closed state) and each piece of evidence's relation to every stored piece on its list under each linked thesis; code turns the answers into the destinations below and owns identity on typed keys (D155, D169, D197). When a candidate's list holds a thesis of its own subject and measure, the collision judge, one generative call, answers the analyst's question for those theses under D248's test, and its verdict replaces Jev's (D190, D258). Jev is not asked for dependencies. Evidence linked to candidates waits for their destinations, then searches all linked existing theses together and is compared once more. Evidence linked only to new theses is new evidence.\n169:  ↓ 5 Validate (Code): Destination IDs must be in the shown lists or the relevant thesis's evidence. Merge only into open theses. New subjects must resolve within the universe using classification's identifier rules; otherwise skip. Dependencies must reference shown theses or an explicit awaited chain/year and remain acyclic. Return invalid answers for correction.\n171:  ↓ 7 Run the day's rejudgement round (Model): Once a day, after the day's last item (D63), rejudge only open theses whose inputs changed that day: new or newly linked evidence (a repeat or a revision is a new piece), or a changed dependency conclusion, newly created awaited thesis or successor. Judge dependencies first, then theme theses before asset theses, and each thesis once per round; each judgement reads the evidence in view (D198). Extra source references alone do not trigger it. Memo-derived theses without evidence stay not yet judged. Each judgement declares whether its conclusion changed; changed conclusions propagate to dependants, and a theme's to every open asset thesis (D195). The round's queue is read from the ledger, so a resumed round recovers what an earlier commit queued.\n180:| Merge into existing | The candidate concerns the same focus or a part of it, such as H20 China revenue within China revenue. Merge only into open theses; closed theses can only be succeeded. | Compare its evidence within the target thesis and preserve grouping under this section's rules. Use the existing ladder and due date. |\n182:| New thesis | Distinct or merely similar: Blackwell export licences and H20 export licences are different questions | Create a thesis with its ladder and due date fixed from now on |\n240:Jev judges step 4, and the collision judge, a generative model, answers for a candidate whose list holds a thesis of its own subject and measure (D169, D190, D258). Retain every decision and have people label code-refused cases and periodic random samples: candidate destinations, dependencies and evidence decisions. When sufficient labels exist, train a faster smaller model to take over. Details remain deferred.\n263:| Decision | Each candidate's destination and reason; new-thesis dependencies and directions; evidence repeat/replacement/new decisions; validation results and refusal reasons |\n306:| Source | Upstream ID, category, publication day and location. A number repeated in text and tables within one item adds references to the same piece; a retelling or repeated statement in another item is its own piece, with the pair recorded as the same claim (D204). |\n318:- One queue writes each thesis: the theme's queue for theme theses; the company, cryptocurrency or commodity queue for asset theses. Within an item, write themes before assets. Rejudgement order follows dependencies under the round rule. Other queues send requests, such as dependency changes or replacement links, to the owning queue with the item's sequence number.\n320:- A day's items form one round: each item's evidence links, thesis creation, succession and dependencies are written in sequence order, and after the day's last item the round rejudges in dependency order (D63). Dependants read conclusions freshly judged in that round. Judge each thesis once. A widely depended-on thesis such as the Fed rate triggers each dependant once only if it declares its conclusion changed; otherwise propagation stops.\n321:- Append-only records: questions, ladders and due dates are immutable. Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labels append records with sequence numbers.\n323:- A revised input changes only its own source reference. Revising a retelling does not change original evidence. For an original-source revision, a changed statement is stored as a revision of the old one, both in force; nothing is retired (D202). Rejudge each open thesis linked to affected statements once. A retelling-reference change alone does not trigger rejudgement. Keep old judgement records unchanged.\n343:- Every thesis has an immutable question, ladder and due date; five rungs or two for yes/no, with exhaustive, non-overlapping, verifiable outcomes.\n346:- Every comparison records shown input, raw response, Config version and decision maker. Reject destinations outside the shown lists or relevant evidence, and dependencies not shown or explicitly awaiting a chain/year, including cycles.\n349:- Rejudge only open theses with changed inputs: new or newly linked evidence (repeats and revisions are new pieces), or changed dependency conclusions. Memo-derived theses without evidence remain unjudged. Cite evidence and relevant dependency IDs, and declare conclusion change. First judgement and rung changes must declare a change. The rung is a forecast of the most likely outcome at the due date, against a reference (D260).\n350:- Choose dependencies during matching from shown theses or explicitly awaited chain/years (D51; decided, not built on the default Jev path, which asks for none). Numeric dependencies read the specified year; event dependencies follow succession. No cycles.\n351:- Judge dependencies before dependants and each thesis once per day's round (D63).\n353:- Append knowledge in arrival order. Store a late older statement as one more piece with its true date and no revision (D202); accept newly learned matters with their true dates. A revised retelling changes only its own source reference. An original-source revision's changed statements are stored as revisions, both in force; nothing is retired (D202).\n368:1. Proposal, not decided (D356): daily pairwise duplicate checks among open theses of a subject; merge the later thesis into the earlier, move links and dependencies, check cycles and rejudge. Add separate duplicate-check settings to Config.\n376:- D38–D40: question/ladder/due-date structure, state, evidence links/tiers, dependencies/succession, round-based intake, history and matching labels.\n377:- D41/D42: align frozen modules with the rebuilt library, separate evidence/candidates, compare within-thesis evidence, and permit binary ladders; the extraction request they described (with dependencies) became open theses with their takes lines (D211, D264). D375 supersedes D251's day-start timing with the latest committed questions before each item.\n379:- D46/D49: one state, concrete ladders, ordinary ingestion of decisive evidence, years based on publication day, top-N whole-library search.\n380:- D47: consistent terms, evidence comparison after candidate resolution, dependency references in judgement reasons and Reuters example correction.\n395:- D149 requirement: organise theses by the continuing business process they discuss, called a research group. Vera Rubin readiness and customer deployment can share a group while shipment dates, SK factory launch and OpenAI deployment retain separate questions, ladders and due dates. A group may start with one thesis; a thesis may belong to several groups. A group has no state or ladder, creates no automatic dependency and is not an overarching investment thesis for an agent.\n397:- The full-text call shows no list of stored groups; an open thesis's takes line names the groups it belongs to (D264). During that call, extraction declares named and scoped groups. A candidate that tracks an ongoing matter (a product rollout and its deployments, a deal, a policy process, an investment or demand cycle) cites one or more groups it declared, without repeats; a company's own reported or guided financial lines (total or segment revenue, margins, expenses, tax rate, capex) belong to the company and cite none unless the line itself tracks such a matter (D165). Code validates the references before writing, folds a declared group into a stored one by its names (D166, D257), and creates a declared group only when a written thesis that tracks a matter cites it, so a company's lines alone never found a group.\n398:- Matching still decides thesis/evidence identity and dependencies; it does not reread association fields to regroup. After creation, succession or merging, the writer resolves candidate IDs to final thesis IDs and local group IDs to stored group IDs, then stores membership. Candidates failing source checks or ultimately skipped do not become members. Do not create empty groups for unused declarations or reject other valid memberships because of them.\n402:- Historical implementation reference: D147's isolated grouping prototype and its field names are preserved in the research records. D165 and D169 subsequently integrated grouping into the default pipeline. See docs/poc/pipeline-plan.md for the current implementation and measured limits; the old prototype does not establish large-library recall.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nfor a,ids in [(\\\"old-1\\\",[354,978,3464,4163]),(\\\"old-2\\\",[1788,5474]),(\\\"old-3\\\",[3348,4101,5195,5369]),(\\\"linked-1\\\",[8752,8756,8759,8763,8766,8761,8769,8770,8842,8920,8925])]:\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n conn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n cols=[x[1] for x in conn.execute(\\\"pragma table_info(model_calls)\\\")]\n if a==\\\"old-1\\\": print(cols)\n for id in ids:\n  row=conn.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n  if row:\n   x=json.loads(row[0])\n   print(json.dumps([a,id,x]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nfor a,ids in [(\\\"old-1\\\",[354,978,3464,4163]),(\\\"old-2\\\",[1788,5474]),(\\\"old-3\\\",[3348,4101,5195,5369]),(\\\"linked-1\\\",[8752,8756,8759,8763,8766,8761,8769,8770,8842,8920,8925])]:\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n conn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n cols=[x[1] for x in conn.execute(\\\"pragma table_info(model_calls)\\\")]\n if a==\\\"old-1\\\": print(cols)\n for id in ids:\n  row=conn.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()\n  if row:\n   x=json.loads(row[0])\n   print(json.dumps([a,id,x]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"['call_id', 'purpose', 'config_version', 'config_checksum', 'model', 'request_sha256', 'request_json', 'response_json', 'answer_json', 'prompt_chars', 'input_tokens', 'output_tokens', 'cost_usd', 'seconds', 'created_at']\n[\"old-1\", 354, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 88 items]\\n- e27 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang expects $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e35; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e28 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027, compared with trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion, and $554 billion in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e51, e107; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e35 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] CEO Jensen Huang said in March that Blackwell and Vera Rubin chip sales are expected to total $1 trillion from then through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e36 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA management said it expects nearly $20 billion in CPU sales; the item gives no period for the estimate.\\n    Quote: Management said they think they can generate nearly $20 billion of CPU sales, making it an instant industry leader.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e65; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e37 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Cerebras ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] Cerebras claims that its existing chips are 15 times faster than some NVIDIA models.\\n    Quote: There are competitors like Cerebras and Space Exploration Technologies Corp , which have promised to make more powerful GPUs than Nvidia, and Cerebras already has chips it claims are 15 times faster than some of Nvidia's models.\\n- e38 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Palantir ; from gnews:bdcb0ed0921c2e9ae241083c 2026-07-03] Palantir is inking a deal to use NVIDIA's Nemotron to build government AI models; the item gives no deal date or scale.\\n    Quote: With Palantir now inking a deal to use Nvidia's Nemotron to build models for the government\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e39 [third_party_view; Jim Cramer, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com; original source Jim Cramer ; from gnews:a96a4cbf6aae35d01d0d285b 2026-07-03] Jim Cramer said Google, Amazon and Tesla feel NVIDIA charges too much; he gave no period or amount.\\n    Quote: the company has some powerful detractors, mainly clients that feel they charge too much, like Google and Amazon and Tesla.\\n- e40 [third_party_view; Jim Cramer, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com; original source Jim Cramer ; from gnews:a96a4cbf6aae35d01d0d285b 2026-07-03] Jim Cramer said Google and Amazon claim their chips are better than NVIDIA\\u2019s, while also using NVIDIA products.\\n    Quote: The first two claim that they have better chips than NVIDIA, even as they use NVIDIA.\\n- e44 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e45 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies is constructing a DSX-aligned campus in Batam expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e25; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e48 [party_statement; bgr.com; original source Josh Parker, NVIDIA head of corporate sustainability ; from gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a 2026-07-04] NVIDIA corporate sustainability head Josh Parker said AI workload demand is not easing and that compute demand driving data-center construction is growing faster than almost any other infrastructure-investment category; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: AI workloads are not getting lighter. The compute demand driving data center construction is growing faster than almost any other category of infrastructure investment.\\n- e51 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Wall Street analysts project NVIDIA revenue growth of 82% in FY2027 and 41% in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e28; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e61 [third_party_view; CNBC, reporting SemiAnalysis\\u2019s projection; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis projected NVIDIA data-center compute revenue in the second half of FY2027 to be 20% above Wall Street consensus.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e62 [fact; wccftech.com; original source U.S. and European retailers (not individually named) ; from gnews:3faf8050f6d3b8ddee1d18c2 2026-07-05] U.S. and European retailers are restocking NVIDIA\\u2019s GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB, with prices starting at $329 and \\u20ac333, respectively.\\n    Quote: So as of right now, both US and European retailers are restocking the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB with prices starting at $329 and \\u20ac333, respectively.\\n- e63 [third_party_view; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source fool.com analyst Adria Cimino 2026-07-05; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author reports that AMD\\u2019s share of the CPU market has risen from about 17% in 2016 to 38% in 2026.\\n    Quote: AMD has gained ground, progressing from a low of about 17% back in 2016 to 38% today.\\n- e64 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA plans to launch its first stand-alone CPU as part of the Vera Rubin platform in fall 2026.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\\n- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e67 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source Singaporean prosecutors ; from gnews:d1a7a7f18dce5c8ae5e4d0ae 2026-07-05] Singaporean prosecutors filed additional money-laundering charges against key suspect Alan Wei in an AI-server fraud case involving possible diversion of servers with NVIDIA chips.\\n    Quote: Singaporean prosecutors filed additional charges including money laundering against a key suspect in an artificial intelligence (AI) server fraud case\\n- e68 [party_statement; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source Singapore police 2026-07-01; from gnews:d1a7a7f18dce5c8ae5e4d0ae 2026-07-05] Singapore police said Aperia executives falsely represented that an Aperia company would be the end-user of servers supplied between November 2023 and February 2025, in a case involving possible diversion of servers containing NVIDIA chips.\\n    Quote: Between November 2023 and February 2025, three Aperia executives - Wei, Aaron Woon Guo Jie and Jenny Lim - falsely told the suppliers that one of the Aperia Group's companies would be the end-user of the servers, according to a police statement published on July 1.\\n- e69 [rumour; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source sg.finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:d1a7a7f18dce5c8ae5e4d0ae 2026-07-05] The outlet reported that US authorities suspect a company involved in Thailand's national AI efforts of helping smuggle billions of dollars' worth of Super Micro servers containing advanced NVIDIA chips to customers in China, including Alibaba.\\n    Quote: US authorities also suspect a company behind Thailand's national AI efforts of helping to smuggle billions of dollars worth of Super Micro servers containing advanced Nvidia chips to several customers in China, including Alibaba Group Holding Ltd.\\n- e74 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis estimates NVIDIA's second-half 2026 shipments and revenue above general-market estimates, based on its supply-chain tracking.\\n    Quote: Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\\n- e92 [third_party_view; marketscale.com; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley estimates that a Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, versus about $4 million for a GB300 NVL72 rack.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\\n- e93 [third_party_view; marketscale.com; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\n    Quote: The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\n- e94 [third_party_view; marketscale.com; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in a Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, up 435% from the prior generation, including roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\\n- e95 [third_party_view; marketscale.com; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley estimates memory's share of the bill of materials rises from 5\\u201310% on GB200 to 25\\u201330% on VR200, while the GPU share falls from about 63% to 51%.\\n    Quote: As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\\n- e97 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4a892d75e355bee00aa3c572 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $81.6 billion in calendar Q1 2026, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: During the first quarter of calendar 2026, Nvidia generated record revenue of $81.6 billion -- up 85% year over year.\\n- e98 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4a892d75e355bee00aa3c572 2026-07-06] NVIDIA's data center segment revenue was $75.2 billion in calendar Q1 2026, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: The company's core data center segment grew even faster, at 92% year over year, to $75.2 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113, e132; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e106 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA revenue to grow 82% for the rest of 2026.\\n    Quote: For the rest of this year, they expect 82% growth.\\n- e108 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Nvidia and Palantir Technologies ; from gnews:0e3cbf453958ce83fabf7a65 2026-07-06] Nvidia and Palantir announced a collaboration to bring open AI models into classified environments; no amount or deployment date was specified.\\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) and Palantir Technologies ( PLTR +1.34% ) announced a collaboration designed to bring powerful open AI models into highly sensitive, classified environments.\\n- e111 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion, while non-GAAP earnings per share jumped 140% to $1.87.\\n- e112 [party_statement; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA guided fiscal Q2 2027 revenue to approximately $91 billion, nearly 95% above the year-earlier quarter.\\n    Quote: For the second quarter of fiscal 2027, NVIDIA projects revenues of approximately $91 billion, representing nearly 95% year-over-year growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e113 [fact; Danielle Liverance, 247WallSt; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year, and data center revenue of $75.2 billion, up 92%.\\n    Quote: Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e98, e125; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e115 [third_party_view; Danielle Liverance, 247WallSt; original source Danielle Liverance, 247WallSt 2026-07-06; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] The author estimated NVIDIA's share of the AI chip market at roughly 81%.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market\\n- e116 [party_statement; Danielle Liverance, 247WallSt; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% higher year over year.\\n    Quote: Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e117 [party_statement; Danielle Liverance, 247WallSt, quoting Lisa Su; original source Lisa Su, AMD ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] AMD CEO Lisa Su said leading-customer forecasts for MI450 exceeded AMD's initial expectations.\\n    Quote: Lisa Su noted \\u201cleading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations\\u201d on MI450.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e193; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e118 [fact; Danielle Liverance, 247WallSt; original source Micron ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Micron reported Q3 revenue of $41.46 billion, up 345.7% year over year.\\n    Quote: Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\\n- e119 [party_statement; Danielle Liverance, 247WallSt; original source Micron ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Micron forecast an adjusted gross margin of 86% next quarter.\\n    Quote: Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved.\\n- e125 [fact; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, and data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\\n    Quote: Q1 FY27 revenue came in at $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113, e155; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e126 [third_party_view; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan) 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author estimates that hyperscalers account for about 50% of NVIDIA's data center revenue and are funding alternatives including Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\\n    Quote: About 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\\n- e127 [party_statement; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91.0 billion and non-GAAP gross margin to 75%.\\n    Quote: Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e112; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e128 [fact; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA networking revenue was $14.8 billion, up 199% year over year; the author says this indicates its business extends beyond GPUs into networking products.\\n    Quote: Networking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion, evidence the moat extends past GPUs into InfiniBand, Spectrum-X and NVLink.\\n- e129 [party_statement; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source Amazon ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] Amazon disclosed that its Trainium business is now worth multiple billions of dollars.\\n    Quote: Amazon has disclosed Trainium is now a multi-billion-dollar business\\n- e130 [party_statement; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author says NVIDIA's China data center compute revenue is effectively zero; CFO Colette Kress described the future China market as close to $50 billion and said losing it would be material.\\n    Quote: China data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia\\u2019s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at \\u201cclose to about $50 billion in the future,\\u201d would be material.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e210; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e131 [third_party_view; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan) 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author cites $119 billion of NVIDIA supply commitments as adding risk if hyperscaler orders slow.\\n    Quote: Supply commitments of $119 billion compound demand risk if hyperscaler orders slow\\n- e132 [third_party_view; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan) 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author cites NVIDIA data center revenue growth of 92%, gross margins of 75%, and quarterly free cash flow of $48.55 billion.\\n    Quote: A company compounding data center revenue at 92% with 75% gross margins and $48.55 billion of quarterly free cash flow is a durable franchise.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e98; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e133 [third_party_view; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan) 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author estimates that six companies generate roughly half of NVIDIA's data center revenue and are actively engineering alternatives to reduce their reliance on NVIDIA products.\\n    Quote: Roughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA\\u2019s price list\\n- e135 [fact; Autonocion.com; original source NVIDIA and Valar Atomics 2026-07-01; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] NVIDIA and Valar Atomics announced an agreement to explore nuclear-powered AI systems, including a 30-megawatt closed-loop AI factory in Utah; the factory is under study, with no construction schedule reported.\\n    Quote: Alongside the demo, the two companies announced an agreement to jointly explore nuclear-powered AI systems. The concrete version under study is a 30-megawatt closed-loop AI factory in Utah, which Valar\\u2019s own announcement says \\u201cconsumes no water from the local community.\\u201d\\n- e136 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com; original source Bleeding Edge ; from gnews:c2924b0380602940d68de7ff 2026-07-06] Bleeding Edge launched an AI Factory and neocloud offering in Quer\\u00e9taro, Mexico.\\n    Quote: Bleeding Edge has launched an AI Factory in Quer\\u00e9taro, Mexico, bringing online its neocloud offering.\\n- e137 [party_statement; datacenterdynamics.com; original source Bleeding Edge ; from gnews:c2924b0380602940d68de7ff 2026-07-06] Bleeding Edge claimed its neocloud was the first in Mexico and Latin America based on Nvidia Blackwell technology.\\n    Quote: The company claims it is the first neocloud in Mexico and Latin America based on Nvidia Blackwell technology.\\n- e138 [party_statement; datacenterdynamics.com; original source Bleeding Edge ; from gnews:c2924b0380602940d68de7ff 2026-07-06] Bleeding Edge said AI-solution companies had contracted the neocloud platform\\u2019s entire initial capacity.\\n    Quote: According to Bleeding Edge, the platform\\u2019s entire initial capacity has already been contracted by companies dedicated to developing AI solutions.\\n- e139 [fact; datacenterdynamics.com; original source Bleeding Edge ; from gnews:c2924b0380602940d68de7ff 2026-07-06] Bleeding Edge\\u2019s neocloud platform incorporated Nvidia Blackwell B300 systems.\\n    Quote: The neocloud platform incorporates Nvidia Blackwell B300 systems and a software layer developed in-house for automation, observability, monitoring, and resource management, with the goal of optimizing workload performance.\\n- e140 [party_statement; datacenterdynamics.com; original source Bleeding Edge ; from gnews:c2924b0380602940d68de7ff 2026-07-06] Bleeding Edge plans to expand neocloud and AI Factory capacity in the coming months by adding Nvidia systems and deploying infrastructure in other markets.\\n    Quote: Bleeding Edge plans to expand both the capacity of its neocloud and its AI Factories in the coming months by incorporating new Nvidia systems and deploying infrastructure in other strategic markets.\\n- e141 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 AI server rack had been pushed back more than twelve months, to 2028, because of manufacturing problems.\\n    Quote: Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99, e157; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e142 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA had scrapped its planned NVL72x2 design, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back.\\n    Quote: A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e101, e151; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e143 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that cloud providers and large data center operators opposed the NVL72x2 design because of its form factor and operational overhead.\\n    Quote: Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e101, e152; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e144 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA canceled the four-compute-die Rubin Ultra version, leaving a two-die version with roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Quote: The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103, e147; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e145 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology would not arrive until the Feynman generation, two generations later.\\n    Quote: A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e163; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e146 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA planned to sell more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor to make up for the shortfall.\\n    Quote: Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\n- e154 [third_party_view; tikr.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis expects NVIDIA data-center compute revenue in the second half of FY2027 to be 20% above Wall Street\\u2019s current expectations.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia\\u2019s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street\\u2019s current expectations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e155 [fact; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported $82 billion in total revenue and $75 billion in data-center revenue in its most recent quarter, up 85% year over year in total revenue.\\n    Quote: In the most recent quarter, the company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, with data center revenue alone hitting $75 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e125; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e165 [fact; Business Times, reporting Bloomberg Intelligence's survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Chinese executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\\nThis is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7).\\n- e166 [fact; Business Times, reporting Bloomberg Intelligence's survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] 80% of surveyed Chinese executives said their total infrastructure spending is running over budget this year, mostly because of high AI-project costs.\\n    Quote: In addition, 80 per cent of executives said their total infrastructure spending is running over-budget this year, mostly because of the high cost of AI-related projects.\\n- e167 [fact; Business Times, reporting Bloomberg Intelligence's survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] A large pool of surveyed Chinese executives was evaluating AI accelerators made by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies.\\n    Quote: AI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\\n- e168 [third_party_view; Bloomberg Intelligence report, quoted by Business Times; original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg Intelligence said China\\u2019s substitution of locally made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress and is likely to benefit Huawei and Hygon; its survey covered 60 executives.\\n    Quote: \\u201cChina\\u2019s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic makers such as Huawei and Hygon,\\u201d said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\\n- e169 [third_party_view; Business Times, reporting Bloomberg Intelligence's survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg Intelligence's survey said Nvidia's market share in China is expected to shrink as H20 chips become harder to find, Beijing urges firms not to use them, and local suppliers take over.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia\\u2019s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm\\u2019s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n- e170 [fact; Business Times, reporting Bloomberg; original source Chinese government ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] China is allocating roughly 2 trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres over the next five years as part of a government-led AI expansion initiative.\\n    Quote: China is allocating roughly two trillion yuan (US$294 billion) to build data centres across the country in the next five years, a government-led initiative to expand the use of AI in sectors from healthcare to city management.\\n- e171 [third_party_view; Business Times, reporting Bloomberg News; original source Bloomberg News ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg News reported that domestic companies will supply at least 80% of core technologies, including chips, for China's data-centre initiative.\\n    Quote: At least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\n- e172 [third_party_view; Business Times, reporting Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg said a global memory-chip shortage is likely to cap growth at Chinese AI firms such as SMIC, while ChangXin Memory Technologies is poised to benefit.\\n    Quote: But a global memory chip shortage is likely to cap the growth of Chinese AI firms like Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corporation, with ChangXin Memory Technologies poised to reap the benefits.\\n- e174 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA and Hugging Face 2026-07-07; from nvblog:hugging-face-lerobot-models-frameworks-open-robotics 2026-07-07] NVIDIA and Hugging Face are bringing Isaac GR00T 1.7 and Isaac Teleop into LeRobot, with Cosmos 3 integration planned soon.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA and Hugging Face are collaborating to bring the NVIDIA Isaac GR00T 1.7 open, reasoning vision language action (VLA) model for humanoid robots and the NVIDIA Isaac Teleop framework to LeRobot \\u2014 Hugging Face\\u2019s open source library for robotics \\u2014 with NVIDIA Cosmos 3 , a frontier model for physical AI, planned soon.\\n- e175 [fact; Cindy Goh, in an NVIDIA blog; original source SoftBank and NVIDIA ; from gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08 2026-07-07] SoftBank and NVIDIA reported a completed outdoor trial of 16-layer massive MU-MIMO on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform, delivering roughly 3x the spectral efficiency of a conventional 4-layer baseline.\\n    Quote: SoftBank and NVIDIA recently reported stable outdoor 16-layer massive MU-MIMO operation on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform. The trial delivered roughly 3x the spectral efficiency of a conventional 4-layer baseline.\\n- e176 [party_statement; Cindy Goh, in an NVIDIA blog; original source NVIDIA and Nokia 2026-07-07; from gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08 2026-07-07] NVIDIA is working with Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia\\u2019s anyRAN software for operators\\u2019 AI-native 5G-Advanced networks.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is working with industry leaders like Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia\\u2019s anyRAN software, enabling operators to deploy AI-native 5G-Advanced networks ready for 6G evolution.\\n- e177 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Palantir ; from gnews:1a116fecd5de1747cc5a06b1 2026-07-07] Palantir announced it is combining its intelligence engine with NVIDIA's Nemotron models to deliver AI for U.S. government agencies and major infrastructure operators.\\n    Quote: Earlier this week, Palantir announced that it is combining its new intelligence engine and Nvidia's Nemotron open AI models to deliver mission-focused AI for government agencies and major infrastructure operators in the U.S.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e38; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e185 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The author 2026-07-07; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] The author forecasts that TSMC will report strong earnings and provide great forward guidance, including on AI semiconductor sales.\\n    Quote: I think both of these companies will report strong earnings with great forward guidance\\n- e186 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SpaceX ; from gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9 2026-07-07] SpaceX plans to use Nvidia chips in its AI1 orbital data-center satellites, with Nvidia set to be an initial hardware supplier; no timing or quantity is given.\\n    Quote: That involvement with SpaceX will expand further, as Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers. SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as data centers in space is called AI1 and will use Nvidia chips.\\n- e187 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9 2026-07-07] Nvidia announced plans to launch its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module at an unspecified future date.\\n    Quote: Also, Nvidia announced in March that it will be launching its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module at some point in the future.\\n- e188 [third_party_view; fool.com (Keithen Drury); original source Keithen Drury 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author expects memory-chip supply to remain low for at least the next year and a half, with new industry capacity not online until 2027 or later and prices potentially rising further.\\n    Quote: As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\\nAs mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\\n- e193 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Lisa Su, AMD CEO ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] AMD CEO Lisa Su said customer engagement for MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening and leading-customer forecasts exceed AMD\\u2019s initial expectations.\\n    Quote: CEO Lisa Su has said \\u201ccustomer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations.\\u201d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e117; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e194 [fact; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Broadcom reported Q2 AI semiconductor revenue of $10.8 billion, up 143% year over year.\\n    Quote: Q2 AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion, +143% YoY,\\n- e195 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion.\\n    Quote: with Q3 guided to $16.0 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e116; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e196 [third_party_view; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts a 27% compound annual growth rate for the custom ASIC market through 2033.\\n    Quote: Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts 27% CAGR for the custom ASIC market through 2033.\\n- e197 [fact; 24/7 Wall St.; original source Google ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google Cloud revenue rose 63%, and its backlog nearly doubled to more than $460 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion,\\n- e204 [fact; Nebius; original source LangChain 2026-07-07; from gnews:022516991251df7f1af4d7cb 2026-07-07] LangChain released a Deep Agents profile tuned for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra; Nebius reports that it achieved top agent accuracy among open models at roughly 10x lower cost than closed alternatives.\\n    Quote: Today, LangChain released a Deep Agents profile tuned for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra, achieving top agent accuracy among open models at roughly 10x lower cost than closed alternatives, with no changes to the model itself.\\n- e205 [fact; Nebius; original source Nebius and LangChain 2026-07-07; from gnews:022516991251df7f1af4d7cb 2026-07-07] Nebius and LangChain announced a partnership to integrate Nebius Token Factory with LangChain\\u2019s Deep Agents.\\n    Quote: Nebius and LangChain have partnered to integrate Nebius Token Factory with LangChain\\u2019s Deep Agents.\\n- e206 [rumour; idnfinancials.com; original source Three unnamed sources familiar with DeepSeek's plans, as reported by Reuters ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Reuters, citing three unnamed sources, reported that DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce reliance on Nvidia and Huawei products used for its AI models.\\n    Quote: DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\\n- e207 [third_party_view; idnfinancials.com; original source idnfinancials.com 2026-07-07; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] The article reported that Huawei controls around half of China's AI chip market, valued at approximately US$50 billion.\\n    Quote: Huawei has expanded its domestic market share and now controls around half of China's AI chip market, which is valued at approximately US$50 billion.\\n- e208 [fact; idnfinancials.com; original source DeepSeek; Huawei ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] DeepSeek launched its V4 model in April, optimized for Huawei Ascend chips; Huawei said V4-Flash training also used Ascend processors.\\n    Quote: In April, DeepSeek launched its V4 model, which had been optimised for Huawei Ascend chips, while Huawei revealed that training for the V4-Flash model also used Ascend processors.\\n- e209 [third_party_view; idnfinancials.com; original source idnfinancials.com 2026-07-07; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] The article reported a surge in Chinese technology companies' demand for Huawei Ascend 950 chips following the V4 launch.\\n    Quote: The launch also drove a surge in demand for Huawei's Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies.\\n- e210 [third_party_view; Richard Windsor, quoted by idnfinancials.com; original source Richard Windsor of Radio Free Mobile ; from gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce 2026-07-07] Analyst Richard Windsor said NVIDIA's position in China is effectively zero and will remain so, and that DeepSeek is unlikely to sell chips outside China without access to advanced manufacturing technology; he said DeepSeek's chip development would not affect NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: \\u201cNvidia's position in China is effectively at zero and will remain that way. DeepSeek has almost no chance of selling chips outside China unless it gains access to advanced manufacturing technology,\\u201d said analyst Richard Windsor of Radio Free Mobile, adding that the development would not affect the chipmaker.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 2]\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-07; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is around $650 billion for the four largest hyperscalers' 2026 data-center capex, which is rung 3. The evidence still implies roughly that amount: the $765 billion estimate is for broader AI infrastructure and cannot be applied in full, while Google's guidance of up to $190 billion is \\n- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-29) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s 2027 forecast of more than $1 trillion, which sits in rung 3. The implied outcome remains around $1 trillion and within rung 3: the financing constraint is a downside risk, but it gives no quantified reduction, while the supplied forecasts do not imply spending a\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The market reference is Wall Street's $392 billion FY2027 estimate, the lower boundary of rung 2. Q1 reported revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of about $91 billion, with 95% year-over-year Q2 growth, support carrying strong growth into the forecast period. SemiAnalysis's 20% upside applies only to second-half data-center compute, not all FY2027 revenue: treating H2 as roughly half the year and compute as a subset implies a gross total-revenue uplift below roughly 10%, or less than the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold. The resulting central estimate is in the low $400 billions, around $420 billion. Product-transition delay/cancellation reports and the China substitution evidence temper that upside, but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to put the forecast below $392 billion.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"old-1\", 978, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 205 items]\\n[Not shown: 99 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e27 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang expects $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang has laid out expectations for $1 trillion in orders for Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips through 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e35, e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e28 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027, compared with trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion, and $554 billion in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e51, e107, e218, e432; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e35 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] CEO Jensen Huang said in March that Blackwell and Vera Rubin chip sales are expected to total $1 trillion from then through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: In March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27, e431; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e36 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] NVIDIA management said it expects nearly $20 billion in CPU sales; the item gives no period for the estimate.\\n    Quote: Management said they think they can generate nearly $20 billion of CPU sales, making it an instant industry leader.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e65; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e37 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Cerebras ; from gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1 2026-07-03] Cerebras claims that its existing chips are 15 times faster than some NVIDIA models.\\n    Quote: There are competitors like Cerebras and Space Exploration Technologies Corp , which have promised to make more powerful GPUs than Nvidia, and Cerebras already has chips it claims are 15 times faster than some of Nvidia's models.\\n- e38 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Palantir ; from gnews:bdcb0ed0921c2e9ae241083c 2026-07-03] Palantir is inking a deal to use NVIDIA's Nemotron to build government AI models; the item gives no deal date or scale.\\n    Quote: With Palantir now inking a deal to use Nvidia's Nemotron to build models for the government\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e39 [third_party_view; Jim Cramer, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com; original source Jim Cramer ; from gnews:a96a4cbf6aae35d01d0d285b 2026-07-03] Jim Cramer said Google, Amazon and Tesla feel NVIDIA charges too much; he gave no period or amount.\\n    Quote: the company has some powerful detractors, mainly clients that feel they charge too much, like Google and Amazon and Tesla.\\n- e40 [third_party_view; Jim Cramer, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com; original source Jim Cramer ; from gnews:a96a4cbf6aae35d01d0d285b 2026-07-03] Jim Cramer said Google and Amazon claim their chips are better than NVIDIA\\u2019s, while also using NVIDIA products.\\n    Quote: The first two claim that they have better chips than NVIDIA, even as they use NVIDIA.\\n- e44 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e15, e223; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e45 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies is constructing a DSX-aligned campus in Batam expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e25, e224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e48 [party_statement; bgr.com; original source Josh Parker, NVIDIA head of corporate sustainability ; from gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a 2026-07-04] NVIDIA corporate sustainability head Josh Parker said AI workload demand is not easing and that compute demand driving data-center construction is growing faster than almost any other infrastructure-investment category; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: AI workloads are not getting lighter. The compute demand driving data center construction is growing faster than almost any other category of infrastructure investment.\\n- e51 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Wall Street analysts project NVIDIA revenue growth of 82% in FY2027 and 41% in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e28, e234; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e61 [third_party_view; CNBC, reporting SemiAnalysis\\u2019s projection; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis projected NVIDIA data-center compute revenue in the second half of FY2027 to be 20% above Wall Street consensus.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e62 [fact; wccftech.com; original source U.S. and European retailers (not individually named) ; from gnews:3faf8050f6d3b8ddee1d18c2 2026-07-05] U.S. and European retailers are restocking NVIDIA\\u2019s GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB, with prices starting at $329 and \\u20ac333, respectively.\\n    Quote: So as of right now, both US and European retailers are restocking the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 12 GB with prices starting at $329 and \\u20ac333, respectively.\\n- e63 [third_party_view; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source fool.com analyst Adria Cimino 2026-07-05; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author reports that AMD\\u2019s share of the CPU market has risen from about 17% in 2016 to 38% in 2026.\\n    Quote: AMD has gained ground, progressing from a low of about 17% back in 2016 to 38% today.\\n- e64 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA plans to launch its first stand-alone CPU as part of the Vera Rubin platform in fall 2026.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\\n- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36, e544; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e67 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source Singaporean prosecutors ; from gnews:d1a7a7f18dce5c8ae5e4d0ae 2026-07-05] Singaporean prosecutors filed additional money-laundering charges against key suspect Alan Wei in an AI-server fraud case involving possible diversion of servers with NVIDIA chips.\\n    Quote: Singaporean prosecutors filed additional charges including money laundering against a key suspect in an artificial intelligence (AI) server fraud case\\n- e68 [party_statement; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source Singapore police 2026-07-01; from gnews:d1a7a7f18dce5c8ae5e4d0ae 2026-07-05] Singapore police said Aperia executives falsely represented that an Aperia company would be the end-user of servers supplied between November 2023 and February 2025, in a case involving possible diversion of servers containing NVIDIA chips.\\n    Quote: Between November 2023 and February 2025, three Aperia executives - Wei, Aaron Woon Guo Jie and Jenny Lim - falsely told the suppliers that one of the Aperia Group's companies would be the end-user of the servers, according to a police statement published on July 1.\\n- e69 [rumour; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source sg.finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:d1a7a7f18dce5c8ae5e4d0ae 2026-07-05] The outlet reported that US authorities suspect a company involved in Thailand's national AI efforts of helping smuggle billions of dollars' worth of Super Micro servers containing advanced NVIDIA chips to customers in China, including Alibaba.\\n    Quote: US authorities also suspect a company behind Thailand's national AI efforts of helping to smuggle billions of dollars worth of Super Micro servers containing advanced Nvidia chips to several customers in China, including Alibaba Group Holding Ltd.\\n- e74 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis estimates NVIDIA's second-half 2026 shipments and revenue above general-market estimates, based on its supply-chain tracking.\\n    Quote: Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\\n- e97 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4a892d75e355bee00aa3c572 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $81.6 billion in calendar Q1 2026, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: During the first quarter of calendar 2026, Nvidia generated record revenue of $81.6 billion -- up 85% year over year.\\n- e111 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion, while non-GAAP earnings per share jumped 140% to $1.87.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e273, e274, e326, e478; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e112 [party_statement; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA guided fiscal Q2 2027 revenue to approximately $91 billion, nearly 95% above the year-earlier quarter.\\n    Quote: For the second quarter of fiscal 2027, NVIDIA projects revenues of approximately $91 billion, representing nearly 95% year-over-year growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127, e308, e351; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e126 [third_party_view; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan) 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author estimates that hyperscalers account for about 50% of NVIDIA's data center revenue and are funding alternatives including Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\\n    Quote: About 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\\n- e127 [party_statement; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91.0 billion and non-GAAP gross margin to 75%.\\n    Quote: Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e112, e269, e413; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e130 [party_statement; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author says NVIDIA's China data center compute revenue is effectively zero; CFO Colette Kress described the future China market as close to $50 billion and said losing it would be material.\\n    Quote: China data center compute revenue is effectively zero, and Colette Kress (Nvidia\\u2019s CFO) said losing that market, which NVIDIA sizes at \\u201cclose to about $50 billion in the future,\\u201d would be material.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e210; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e133 [third_party_view; 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan); original source 247Wall St. (Omor Ibne Ehsan) 2026-07-06; from gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3 2026-07-06] The author estimates that six companies generate roughly half of NVIDIA's data center revenue and are actively engineering alternatives to reduce their reliance on NVIDIA products.\\n    Quote: Roughly half of that data center revenue comes from six companies actively engineering their way off NVIDIA\\u2019s price list\\n- e141 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 AI server rack had been pushed back more than twelve months, to 2028, because of manufacturing problems.\\n    Quote: Asian tech stocks slid after