 billion, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Networking was the sleeper hit at $14.8 billion (+199% YoY), driven by InfiniBand, Spectrum-X, and NVLink.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e405; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e477 [party_statement; 247wallst.com; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said AI-factory buildout was accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\n    Quote: Jensen Huang framed the moment bluntly: \\u201cThe buildout of AI factories , the largest infrastructure expansion in human history, is accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\u201d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e569; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e478 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 non-GAAP EPS of $1.87, above expectations.\\n    Quote: Non-GAAP EPS of $1.87 beat expectations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e111; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e479 [party_statement; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA guided to Q2 revenue of $91 billion, assuming zero Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA guided Q2 to $91 billion in revenue, which assumes zero China Data Center compute.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e351, e581; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e480 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA\\u2019s Q1 FY27 revenue hit $81.61B, up 85.2% YoY\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e407, e567; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e484 [fact; Rest of World; original source Saudi Arabia 2026-06-01; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Saudi Arabia agreed on June 1, 2026, to use self-driving taxis powered by Nvidia technology, including its onboard computer and driving software.\\n    Quote: Saudi Arabia agreed on June 1 to use self-driving taxis that run on Nvidia technology \\u2014 from the computer inside each car to the software that steers it.\\n- e491 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. government 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] The U.S. government approved ZTE and Maginfra to buy NVIDIA H200 chips, Reuters reported on 2026-07-14.\\n    Quote: Today, Reuters reports that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra have received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-gen H200 \\\"Hopper\\\" chips.\\n- e493 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. trade official 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official said at a congressional hearing on 2026-07-14 that shipments against licenses for H200 chips and equivalents had been very few and involved a very small quantity of chips.\\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \\\"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It\\u2019s a very small quantity of chips\\\" during a congressional hearing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e524; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e499 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe, reporting Airlangga Hartarto\\u2019s remarks; original source Airlangga Hartarto, Indonesia\\u2019s Chief Economic Affairs Minister ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Indonesia\\u2019s Chief Economic Affairs Minister Airlangga Hartarto said investors plan about 1.3 GW of additional data center capacity, alongside roughly 580 MW currently operating.\\n    Quote: Chief Economic Affairs Minister Airlangga Hartarto said investors are planning data center projects with a combined capacity of about 1.3 gigawatts (GW), on top of the roughly 580 megawatts (MW) currently in operation.\\n- e500 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe, reporting Airlangga Hartarto\\u2019s remarks; original source Airlangga Hartarto, Indonesia\\u2019s Chief Economic Affairs Minister ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Airlangga Hartarto said Indonesia\\u2019s pipeline of additional data center projects, including Batam, represents around $15 billion to $20 billion in investment.\\n    Quote: The additional 1.3 GW that is in the pipeline, including the Batam project, represents investments of around $15 billion (Rp 271 trillion) to $20 billion\\n- e501 [fact; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies and DayOne ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus Technologies and DayOne are developing a 360 MW Nvidia DSX AI Factory Campus in Batam.\\n    Quote: The Australian AI infrastructure company is partnering with Singapore-based data center operator DayOne to develop the Nvidia DSX AI Factory Campus, a 360-megawatt AI data center complex that is expected to become one of Southeast Asia's largest AI computing hubs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e55; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e502 [third_party_view; Jakarta Globe, relaying The Straits Times\\u2019 report; original source The Straits Times ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] The Straits Times projected that the Batam campus could generate up to $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n    Quote: According to The Straits Times , the project is projected to generate as much as $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e503 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of NVIDIA\\u2019s latest-generation GPUs in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 to provide AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Quote: Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e518 [fact; Sakana AI; original source Sakana AI 2026-07-15; from gnews:68d900bceeb241f931ab1f2e 2026-07-15] Sakana AI is integrating NVIDIA\\u2019s Nemotron open-model family as specialized agents in its Fugu orchestration system; the integration is part of an expanded collaboration.\\n    Quote: As part of the expanded collaboration, Sakana is integrating Nemotron, an open model family with open weights and tooling, as specialized agents within Sakana Fugu.\\n- e520 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db8d58512ec8bb97266d4b48 2026-07-15] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $82 billion in fiscal 2027 Q1, up 85% year over year and marking its 14th consecutive quarter of sequential growth.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's total revenue increased 85% YOY to $82 billion in Q1, marking its 14th-straight quarter of sequential revenue growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e155; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e524 [party_statement; Jeffrey Kessler, quoted by Reuters and reported by Benzinga; original source Jeffrey Kessler 2026-07-14; from gnews:6c62a57b64473d2ac15a53ad 2026-07-15] Commerce Under Secretary Jeffrey Kessler said H200 exports to China so far have been minimal.\\n    Quote: There have been minimal exports of any H200s to China so far\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e493; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e525 [fact; Benzinga; original source Benzinga 2026-07-15; from gnews:6c62a57b64473d2ac15a53ad 2026-07-15] Recent U.S. approvals cleared around 10 Chinese firms, including units of ZTE, Alibaba, Tencent and ByteDance, to receive advanced AI hardware from Nvidia and AMD.\\n    Quote: recent U.S. approvals have cleared around 10 Chinese firms to receive advanced AI hardware from Nvidia and Advanced Micro Devices Inc. (NASDAQ: AMD ). Major tech conglomerates\\u2014including a unit of telecom equipment manufacturer ZTE Corp. (OTC: ZTCOF ), alongside Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. ADR (NYSE: BABA ), Tencent Holdings Ltd. (OTC: TCEHY ), and ByteDance \\u2014are among the entities cleared to purcha\\n- e527 [third_party_view; Benzinga, reporting KeyBanc's outlook; original source KeyBanc ; from gnews:6c62a57b64473d2ac15a53ad 2026-07-15] Nvidia is expected to ship more B300 GPUs instead of R200 models to mitigate the reported Rubin delays.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is expected to ship more B300 GPUs in place of its R200 models\\n- e528 [fact; Light Reading; original source Nokia 2026-07-15; from gnews:d8ce94ed4295ce1fcaeed4cc 2026-07-15] Nokia announced Nvidia-based RAN hardware products that it said would be available from 2027.\\n    Quote: Nokia's vision, outlined during an exclusive interview with Light Reading, is that the Nvidia-based hardware products announced today and available from next year will potentially last customers deep into the 6G era.\\n- e529 [fact; Light Reading; original source Nokia 2026-07-15; from gnews:d8ce94ed4295ce1fcaeed4cc 2026-07-15] Nokia plans pilots of three sets of Nvidia-based RAN products toward the end of 2026 and commercial rollouts in 2027.\\n    Quote: As promised, Nokia is launching three broad sets of products, with pilots expected toward the end of 2026 and commercial rollouts anticipated in 2027.\\n- e530 [party_statement; Light Reading, quoting Mark Atkinson; original source Mark Atkinson, Nokia head of RAN 2026-07-15; from gnews:d8ce94ed4295ce1fcaeed4cc 2026-07-15] Nokia's RAN head said the company was making a final pivot from custom-silicon to merchant-silicon baseband products, including Nvidia-based products.\\n    Quote: We are making now the final pivot from a custom silicon baseband strategy to a merchant silicon baseband strategy\\n- e538 [fact; Reuters; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-16; from gnews:0ce55967fc1dd27daa9fa6c8 2026-07-15] NVIDIA said it was partnering with Japanese companies including Fanuc and Yaskawa Electric to advance robotics and AI development; the item dates the announcement to Thursday, July 16.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said on Thursday it was partnering with Japanese companies including Fanuc and \\u200cYaskawa Electric to advance the development of robotics \\u200cand AI .\\n- e539 [party_statement; Reuters; original source ASML 2026-07-15; from gnews:0ce55967fc1dd27daa9fa6c8 2026-07-15] ASML raised its sales forecast and pledged capacity expansion on Wednesday, July 15, amid investor focus on the AI investment cycle.\\n    Quote: Investors are weighing \\u200bthe strength of the AI investment cycle, with \\u200cchipmaking equipment maker ASML on \\u200bWednesday raising \\u200bits sales forecast and pledging capacity expansion.\\n- e540 [third_party_view; Reuters; original source Reuters 2026-07-15; from gnews:0ce55967fc1dd27daa9fa6c8 2026-07-15] Reuters said TSMC was expected to post a fifth consecutive quarter of record earnings on Thursday, July 16, attributing the expected result to the AI boom.\\n    Quote: TSMC, the world's leading contract chipmaker, is expected to post a fifth \\u200bconsecutive quarter of \\u200crecord earnings on Thursday due to the AI \\u200bboom.\\n- e544 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] NVIDIA forecast $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027 and said it was on track to dominate the stand-alone CPU market.\\n    Quote: In its latest earnings report, it predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year and said it was on track to dominate this market.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e65; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e545 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Cerebras Systems ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] Cerebras recently signed a compute deal with OpenAI.\\n    Quote: And the company recently signed key deals with OpenAI for compute\\n- e546 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Cerebras Systems and Amazon's cloud unit ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] Cerebras recently signed a deal with Amazon\\u2019s cloud unit to make Cerebras WSE systems more broadly available.\\n    Quote: with Amazon 's cloud unit to make its WSE systems more broadly available.\\n- e547 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Cerebras Systems ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] Cerebras reported first-quarter revenue of $193 million, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: first-quarter revenue soaring 92% to $193 million.\\n- e548 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts (not named) ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] Analysts forecast that the AI market will exceed $3 trillion in the early 2030s.\\n    Quote: Analysts predict the AI market will reach beyond $3 trillion in the early part of the next decade\\n- e551 [rumour; Tom's Hardware; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 2026-07-15] SemiAnalysis said customers rejected NVIDIA's planned NVL72x2 alternative design and its operational requirements.\\n    Quote: However, SemiAnalysis says customers rejected the unusual design and operational requirements\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e143; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e552 [rumour; Tom's Hardware; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 2026-07-15] SemiAnalysis claimed NVIDIA's planned NVL576 rack-scale system had been postponed or would ship in relatively small quantities because of ongoing co-packaged-optics challenges.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the planned NVL576 rack scale solution that was supposed to combine eight Oberon racks interconnected using co-packaged optics between NVSwitches has also been postponed, or shipped in relatively small quantities because of 'ongoing CPO challenges,' SemiAnalysis claims.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e160; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e554 [rumour; Tom's Hardware, citing another report; original source Unspecified report cited by Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 2026-07-15] A report said NVIDIA had replaced the quad-compute-chiplet Rubin Ultra design with a dual-compute-chiplet design projected to deliver 2\\u00d7 lower performance.\\n    Quote: The reported Kyber delay comes on the heels of another report saying that Nvidia had canceled quad-compute-chiplet version of its Rubin Ultra in favor or a dual-compute-chiplet design that is projected to deliver 2X lower performance.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e144; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e555 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-07-15; from gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 2026-07-15] Tom's Hardware judged that the reported Kyber delay and NVL72x2 cancellation could leave NVIDIA offering only 72-way scale-up systems until sometime in 2028, potentially giving AMD and Google more competitive scale-up systems in 2027\\u20132028.\\n    Quote: With Kyber NVL144 delayed and NVL72x2 cancelled, Nvidia will only be able to offer 72-way scale-up systems till sometimes in 2028, meaning that AMD and Google may end up with more competitive scale-up systems in 2027 \\u2013 2028.\\n- e556 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 2026-07-15] AMD's planned Mega Pod, based on Verano CPUs and Instinct MI500-series accelerators, was expected to contain up to 256 accelerators.\\n    Quote: AMD's Mega Pod based on the Verano CPUs and Instinct MI500-series accelerators, is expected to pack up to 256 accelerators .\\n- e557 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 2026-07-15] Tom's Hardware reported that Google's TPU 8i could provide roughly 1,024\\u20131,152 accelerators per low-latency domain and TPU 8t up to 9,600 chip packages per domain.\\n    Quote: Google's TPU 8i can provide roughly 1,024\\u20131,152 accelerators within one low-latency domain , whereas the TPU 8t goes much further and can get to 9,600 chip packages per domain.\\n- e565 [rumour; Tweaktown, relaying a Financial Times report; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:96607f3f5939f53a4b3cdea7 2026-07-15] NVIDIA reportedly cut its authorized AI chip customers in Singapore, Malaysia, and Japan by more than half, introducing a vetted whitelist and stricter compliance checks to prevent diversion to China; excluded firms, many of them neocloud providers, can reapply.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA drastically reduced authorized AI chip customers in Singapore, Malaysia, and Japan by over half, introducing a vetted whitelist and stricter compliance checks to prevent high-end accelerators being diverted to China; excluded firms can reapply, and many affected were neo-cloud AI providers amid concerns over a large GPU black market.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e467; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e567 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] NVIDIA reported quarterly revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.23% year over year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA posted $81.61 billion in quarterly revenue on 85.23% growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e480; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e569 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted by 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said AI factory construction was accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\n    Quote: Jensen Huang framed the moment plainly: \\u201cThe buildout of AI factories, the largest infrastructure expansion in human history, is accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\u201d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e477; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e570 [party_statement; Lisa Su, quoted by 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Lisa Su ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] AMD CEO Lisa Su said customer engagement for MI450 Series and Helios was strengthening and leading customer forecasts exceeded AMD\\u2019s initial expectations.\\n    Quote: Lisa Su highlighted that \\u201cCustomer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations.\\u201d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e193; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e571 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Meta ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] Meta committed to deploy up to 6 gigawatts of AMD Instinct GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meta committed to up to 6 gigawatts of Instinct GPU deployment.\\n- e573 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] NVIDIA guided to Q2 revenue of $91.0 billion.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA guided Q2 revenue to $91.0 billion\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e470; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e574 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source AMD ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] AMD guided to approximately $11.20 billion in revenue and approximately 56% gross margin for Q2.\\n    Quote: AMD guided to roughly $11.20 billion at ~56% gross margin.\\n- e575 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source OpenAI and Meta ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] OpenAI and Meta signed major deals with both NVIDIA and AMD during the same quarter.\\n    Quote: OpenAI and Meta just signed major deals with both NVIDIA and AMD in the same quarter\\n- e577 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, quoting Huang; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had more orders at the time of the remarks than when he last discussed orders at GTC.\\n    Quote: \\u201cWe have more orders today than we did at the last time I spoke about orders at GTC\\u201d\\n- e578 [fact; 247wallst.com, citing NVIDIA's Q1 FY2027 results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year; Data Center revenue was $75.246 billion and Networking revenue rose 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia\\u2019s Q1 FY2027 revenue hit $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center alone contributing $75.246 billion and Networking revenue rising 199% YoY.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e405; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e581 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, summarizing NVIDIA guidance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] NVIDIA guided to Q2 revenue of $91.0 billion, excluding China Data Center compute sales.\\n    Quote: is guiding for $91.0 billion in Q2 revenue, a forecast that excludes any China Data Center compute sales.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e479; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e584 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing reported June revenue of NT$442.68 billion, up 67.9% year over year.\\n    Quote: June revenue jumped 67.9% YoY to NT$442.68 billion\\n- e585 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] TSMC is adding three advanced packaging facilities at Chiayi Science Park Phase II to relieve CoWoS bottlenecks.\\n    Quote: TSMC is adding three new advanced packaging facilities in Chiayi Science Park Phase II to relieve CoWoS bottlenecks .\\n\\n[Themes in force, 3]\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-15; reasons: The reference is about $650 billion for the four largest hyperscalers' 2026 data-center capex, which is rung 3. The newer Google and Meta figures are total-capex guidance, not disclosed data-center allocations; the $765 billion and roughly $800 billion estimates cover broader AI infrastructure, so t\\n- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-29) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-15; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 3 ($900 billion to below $1.1 trillion). The other specific forecast also says spending will exceed $1 trillion, while the Morgan Stanley-attributed estimate adds a capacity figure but no higher dollar\\n- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 Yes; judged 2026-07-15; reasons: The reference is no Chinese authorization by the deadline, rung 1: no consensus or stated Chinese deadline plan is supplied, and the prior judgement identified the status quo as its reference. The more specific reports say selected AI firms were told they would be permitted to buy some H200s, subjec\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is Wall Street\\u2019s approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, which sits in rung 2. The evidence continues to imply an outcome near $430 billion: SemiAnalysis\\u2019s estimate is 20% above consensus for second-half data-center compute, not for all revenue, and the prior calculation attributes roughly $37 billion of upside to that portion. That leaves the forecast just below rung 1\\u2019s $431.2 billion boundary. Q1 actual revenue and Q2 guidance support a strong run-rate, while the newly specified product delays and cancellations, China substitution, and limited licensed H200 shipments do not establish a quantified revenue reduction sufficient to move the estimate across a boundary. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin figure spans products and calendar years and is not FY2027 total r\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"old-1\", 3464, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 173 items]\\n[Not shown: 785 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e28 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027, compared with trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion, and $554 billion in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e51, e107, e218, e432; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36, e544; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e111 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion, while non-GAAP earnings per share jumped 140% to $1.87.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e273, e274, e326, e478, e1401, e1425; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e112 [party_statement; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA guided fiscal Q2 2027 revenue to approximately $91 billion, nearly 95% above the year-earlier quarter.\\n    Quote: For the second quarter of fiscal 2027, NVIDIA projects revenues of approximately $91 billion, representing nearly 95% year-over-year growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127, e308, e351, e739, e1265, e1929; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e141 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 AI server rack had been pushed back more than twelve months, to 2028, because of manufacturing problems.\\n    Quote: Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99, e157; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e142 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA had scrapped its planned NVL72x2 design, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back.\\n    Quote: A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e101, e151; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e144 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA canceled the four-compute-die Rubin Ultra version, leaving a two-die version with roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Quote: The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103, e147, e554; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e146 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA planned to sell more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor to make up for the shortfall.\\n    Quote: Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\n- e154 [third_party_view; tikr.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis expects NVIDIA data-center compute revenue in the second half of FY2027 to be 20% above Wall Street\\u2019s current expectations.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia\\u2019s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street\\u2019s current expectations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e165 [fact; Business Times, reporting Bloomberg Intelligence's survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Chinese executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\\nThis is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e235; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e169 [third_party_view; Business Times, reporting Bloomberg Intelligence's survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg Intelligence's survey said Nvidia's market share in China is expected to shrink as H20 chips become harder to find, Beijing urges firms not to use them, and local suppliers take over.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia\\u2019s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm\\u2019s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use , become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e238; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e171 [third_party_view; Business Times, reporting Bloomberg News; original source Bloomberg News ; from gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b 2026-07-06] Bloomberg News reported that domestic companies will supply at least 80% of core technologies, including chips, for China's data-centre initiative.\\n    Quote: At least 80 per cent of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e240; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e218 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Analysts ; from gnews:b40db2c507df72ec016e99e6 2026-07-08] Analysts expect NVIDIA total revenue of $392.7 billion in FY2027.\\n    Quote: And analysts are anticipating further gains of $392.7 billion in its current fiscal year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e28; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e234 [third_party_view; tradingview.com; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:a09988fd9c5c1a739a14f873 2026-07-08] Bloomberg-tracked estimates project NVIDIA FY2027 profit of $228 billion and total revenue of $393 billion, growth of 90% and 82%, respectively.\\n    Quote: The company is projected to generate $228 billion in profit on $393 billion in sales in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31, representing expected growth of 90% and 82%, respectively\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e51, e299, e705, e811; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e419 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped zero H20 chips to China in its spring quarter, compared with $4.6 billion of H20 shipments a year earlier.\\n    Quote: Yet zero H20 chips shipped to China this quarter, versus $4.6 billion a year ago.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e638, e1314; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e491 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. government 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] The U.S. government approved ZTE and Maginfra to buy NVIDIA H200 chips, Reuters reported on 2026-07-14.\\n    Quote: Today, Reuters reports that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra have received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-gen H200 \\\"Hopper\\\" chips.\\n- e493 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. trade official 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official said at a congressional hearing on 2026-07-14 that shipments against licenses for H200 chips and equivalents had been very few and involved a very small quantity of chips.\\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \\\"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It\\u2019s a very small quantity of chips\\\" during a congressional hearing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e524; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e544 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] NVIDIA forecast $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027 and said it was on track to dominate the stand-alone CPU market.\\n    Quote: In its latest earnings report, it predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year and said it was on track to dominate this market.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e65; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e552 [rumour; Tom's Hardware; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 2026-07-15] SemiAnalysis claimed NVIDIA's planned NVL576 rack-scale system had been postponed or would ship in relatively small quantities because of ongoing co-packaged-optics challenges.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the planned NVL576 rack scale solution that was supposed to combine eight Oberon racks interconnected using co-packaged optics between NVSwitches has also been postponed, or shipped in relatively small quantities because of 'ongoing CPO challenges,' SemiAnalysis claims.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e160; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e555 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-07-15; from gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 2026-07-15] Tom's Hardware judged that the reported Kyber delay and NVL72x2 cancellation could leave NVIDIA offering only 72-way scale-up systems until sometime in 2028, potentially giving AMD and Google more competitive scale-up systems in 2027\\u20132028.\\n    Quote: With Kyber NVL144 delayed and NVL72x2 cancelled, Nvidia will only be able to offer 72-way scale-up systems till sometimes in 2028, meaning that AMD and Google may end up with more competitive scale-up systems in 2027 \\u2013 2028.\\n- e801 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-21; from nvblog:vera-rubin 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said Vera Rubin NVL72 production is ramping up, with racks running at CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin NVL72 production is ramping up with racks running at partners CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e936, e937; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e738 (2026-07-18); both are in force: The author said NVIDIA\\u2019s Vera Rubin platform was slated to enter production in the second half of 2026.\\n- e803 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:vera-rubin 2026-07-21] CoreWeave became the first AI cloud to bring up and validate NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72, and began sharing performance results from live hardware.\\n    Quote: After months of co-engineering work, CoreWeave became the first AI cloud to bring up and validate Vera Rubin NVL72 \\u2014 and it is now sharing the first measured performance numbers from live hardware.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1902; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e804 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Google Cloud ; from nvblog:vera-rubin 2026-07-21] Google Cloud\\u2019s first A5X instance powered by NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 is up and running for London startup Ineffable Intelligence.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 is powering Google Cloud\\u2019s first A5X instance, now up and running for London startup Ineffable Intelligence.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e867; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e805 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Nebius ; from nvblog:vera-rubin 2026-07-21] Nebius received its first NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 system at its Finland AI Factory and said it plans to deploy many more systems globally.\\n    Quote: The company recently received its first Vera Rubin NVL72 system at its Finland AI Factory, the first of many systems that will be deployed globally.\\n- e811 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:0993b79d5b6b817b8d65b011 2026-07-21] Yahoo! Finance's Wall Street average estimate put NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue at $393 billion.\\n    Quote: According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e234; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e956 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1d24faffe6c3a2b74f2bfc8b 2026-07-23] NVIDIA said Vera Rubin NVL72 entered full production in June 2026 and was being deployed across Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and CoreWeave.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said Vera Rubin NVL72 entered full production in June and is already being deployed across cloud providers including Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and CoreWeave.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e801 (2026-07-21); both are in force: NVIDIA said Vera Rubin NVL72 production is ramping up, with racks running at CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius.\\n- e1016 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation and SK Group 2026-07-24; from nvidianews:sk-group-and-nvidia-expand-strategic-partnership-across-ai-factories-and-next-generation-memory 2026-07-24] NVIDIA and SK Group described their AI initiative as worth more than $500 billion, encompassing an SK Telecom AI factory of up to 2 gigawatts and an SK hynix AI memory partnership.\\n    Quote: $500-Billion-Plus NVIDIA-SK AI Initiative Spans SK Telecom\\u2019s AI Factory of up to 2 Gigawatts and SK hynix\\u2019s Long-Term AI Memory Partnership\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1026, e1064, e1100, e1106, e1275; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1040 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source OpenAI ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 GW of GPUs, a planned deployment that could affect NVIDIA's competitive position in AI accelerators.\\n    Quote: OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 gigawatts of GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e914, e1243; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1041 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Meta ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] Meta signed for a deployment of up to 6 GW of AMD Instinct GPUs, a competing accelerator commitment relevant to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue outlook.\\n    Quote: Meta signed for up to 6 gigawatts of Instinct GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e571; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1042 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Oracle ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] Oracle is standing up a 50,000-GPU AMD Helios cluster, a competing AI-compute deployment relevant to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue outlook.\\n    Quote: Oracle is standing up a 50,000-GPU Helios cluster.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1244; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1050 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA guided to $1 trillion in combined Blackwell and Rubin revenue from calendar 2025 through calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has guided to $1 trillion in combined Blackwell and Rubin revenue from 2025 through calendar 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e431, e1095; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1051 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO 2026-05-20; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said standalone Vera CPU revenue was excluded from the $1 trillion Blackwell and Rubin revenue forecast and expected it to be the second-largest source of upside to that forecast.\\n    Quote: we didn\\u2019t include any Vera CPU, standalone CPU in that number. And so I expect that to be the second largest\\u201d source of upside above the $1 trillion, behind only share gains in frontier AI models.\\n- e1057 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst), reporting Colette Kress\\u2019s statement; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera Rubin\\u2019s seven-chip architecture would start shipping in Q3 and ramp in Q4.\\n    Quote: The second is rising content per rack as Vera Rubin\\u2019s seven-chip architecture, which Kress said starts shipping in Q3 and ramps into Q4, replaces narrower Blackwell configurations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1110 [party_statement; Business Times; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:99a1ba68b933cb3eeb687120 2026-07-25] Jensen Huang said the more-than-US$500 billion SK initiative figure includes NVIDIA memory-chip purchases and SK Group purchases of NVIDIA supercomputers.\\n    Quote: The figure includes money that Nvidia will spend buying memory chips, as well as purchases by SK Group of Nvidia\\u2019s supercomputers, Huang said in the interview.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e1100 (); both are in force: NVIDIA and SK Group signed letters of intent this week for a strategic relationship valued at more than $500 billion; the item does not specify the period covered or NVIDIA\\u2019s share of that value.\\n- e1157 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source Bernstein analysts ; from gnews:c871b9605dd781113ba40cef 2026-07-27] Bernstein analysts said the SK Group initiative's $500 billion-plus headline figure may overstate its near-term revenue impact for Nvidia.\\n    Quote: While the read is highly constructive for SK Hynix, Bernstein warns the $500B+ headline figure may overstate the near-term revenue impact for Nvidia.\\n- e1167 [rumour; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com 2026-07-27; from gnews:e7777109769b68857021b63b 2026-07-27] NVIDIA is reportedly in preliminary discussions to finance approximately $350 billion of OpenAI's NVIDIA-processor purchases for the Ohio project; the negotiations could change or collapse.\\n    Quote: The chipmaker is separately discussing financing approximately $350 billion of OpenAI's purchases of NVIDIA processors for the project, although the negotiations remain preliminary and could change or collapse.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1113, e1184, e1201, e1234, e1251; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1219 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Turing ; from gnews:ef88f177ac9be042f169ec16 2026-07-27] Turing is migrating 10% of its AI training workloads from NVIDIA to AMD.\\n    Quote: Lastly, the autonomous driving company Turing is migrating 10% of its AI training workloads from Nvidia to AMD.\\n- e1236 [party_statement; qz.com; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:3561dee8ab82ef4e42d8e315 2026-07-27] OpenAI\\u2019s projected computing-infrastructure spending through 2030 has risen to around $750 billion from roughly $600 billion earlier in 2026.\\n    Quote: OpenAI has raised its projected spending on computing infrastructure to around $750 billion through 2030 , up from roughly $600 billion earlier this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1257; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1464 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Meta ; from gnews:2636e625ce993ef397c7a7d9 2026-07-30] Meta committed to 6 gigawatts of AMD Instinct GPU capacity, including a custom MI450 chip.\\n    Quote: Meta committed to 6 gigawatts of AMD Instinct GPUs including a custom MI450 chip\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e914, e1558; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1475 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:30e3129696abb67c369bdbf5 2026-07-30] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 revenue guidance assumes no H20 shipments to China.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance assumes no H20 shipments to China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e673; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1510 [third_party_view; insidermonkey.com; original source insidermonkey.com 2026-07-31; from gnews:39deea97b690ac7b56dada0b 2026-07-31] NVIDIA has roughly $1 trillion in confirmed Blackwell and Vera Rubin chip orders through 2027.\\n    Quote: The company already has roughly $1 trillion in confirmed orders for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips through 2027,\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1521 [party_statement; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b72a0b11ab85854e09955d1 2026-07-31] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 guidance excludes Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e997; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1528 [party_statement; Zacks.com analyst; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:6f53d290d7aa54066ad3f160 2026-07-31] NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q2 2027 total revenue to $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Quote: Management further expects AI demand to remain robust, as they project NVIDIA\\u2019s fiscal second-quarter 2027 revenues to come in at $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e308, e1804; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1558 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source AMD and Meta ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] AMD and Meta agreed to deploy up to 6 GW of AMD Instinct GPUs, with the first 1 GW using MI450.\\n    Quote: AMD locked in a Meta partnership to deploy up to 6 gigawatts of Instinct GPUs, with the first 1-GW on MI450.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1464; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1569 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:0b1b72dac911085466f3a3b1 2026-08-02] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA revenue to grow 96% year over year in FY2027 Q2, ending in late July.\\n    Quote: For the second quarter of fiscal year 2027 (ending in late July), Wall Street analysts expect an impressive 96% growth rate.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e343, e1921; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1571 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts ; from gnews:0b1b72dac911085466f3a3b1 2026-08-02] Analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue will grow 81% year over year in FY2027 Q3.\\n    Quote: Right now, analysts estimate 81% revenue growth for Q3.\\n- e1586 [fact; english.cw.com.tw; original source Anthropic co-founder Tom Brown ; from gnews:62ea477f766b5061a81b39e8 2026-08-03] Anthropic co-founder Tom Brown announced that Anthropic plans to deploy 2 GW of AMD Helios racks; he made the announcement at last week\\u2019s Advancing AI 2026 conference.\\n    Quote: Anthropic co-founder Tom Brown announced on stage that the company would deploy 2 GW of Helios racks.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1005; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1601 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Wistron and NVIDIA 2026-08-05; from nvblog:nvidia-and-partners-build-in-america-for-america 2026-08-05] Wistron\\u2019s Fort Worth facility is already producing NVIDIA GB300 Grace Blackwell Ultra Superchips and is preparing to produce Vera Rubin Superchips; the facility is part of a combined $700 million U.S. advanced-manufacturing commitment that has created more than 500 jobs, with jobs expected to reach 1,000 by year-end.\\n    Quote: The facility is already producing the NVIDIA GB300 Grace Blackwell Ultra Superchip and preparing to produce the NVIDIA Vera Rubin Superchip, part of a combined $700 million commitment to U.S. advanced manufacturing that has created more than 500 jobs, expanding to 1,000 jobs by the end of the year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e845; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1602 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Coherent 2026-08-05; from nvblog:nvidia-and-partners-build-in-america-for-america 2026-08-05] Coherent broke ground in August 2026 on an expanded Sherman, Texas, facility to scale what it calls the world\\u2019s first volume-production 6-inch indium phosphide fab; the project is expected to create 1,000 jobs.\\n    Quote: In Sherman, Texas, Coherent , a key networking supplier across NVIDIA\\u2019s AI stack, broke ground this month on its expanded facility, scaling what it calls the world\\u2019s first volume production 6-inch indium phosphide fab. This is part of a project expected to create 1,000 jobs.\\n- e1603 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Corning and Lumentum 2026-08-05; from nvblog:nvidia-and-partners-build-in-america-for-america 2026-08-05] Corning is expanding U.S. manufacturing of advanced optical connectivity solutions in North Carolina and Texas, building new facilities and opening more than 3,000 jobs; Lumentum is advancing U.S. manufacturing and deepening its R&D collaboration with NVIDIA on optics technologies.\\n    Quote: In North Carolina and Texas, Corning is expanding its U.S.-based manufacturing of advanced optical connectivity solutions needed to power next-generation AI, building new facilities and opening more than 3,000 jobs. Also in North Carolina, Lumentum is advancing U.S.-based manufacturing and deepening its R&D collaboration with NVIDIA in state-of-the-art optics technologies.\\n- e1604 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Foxconn 2026-08-05; from nvblog:nvidia-and-partners-build-in-america-for-america 2026-08-05] Foxconn is building a factory in Houston to manufacture NVIDIA AI systems, including GB300 tray modules.\\n    Quote: In Houston, Texas, Foxconn is building a state-of-the-art factory for manufacturing NVIDIA AI systems, including NVIDIA GB300 tray modules.\\n- e1605 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-05; from nvblog:nvidia-and-partners-build-in-america-for-america 2026-08-05] NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the United States with TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron, Corning, Lumentum, Coherent and Amkor.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the U.S. with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron, Corning, Lumentum, Coherent and Amkor.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e933; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1607 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source SpaceX CEO Elon Musk ; from gnews:baaf41a9a32da4e5efcfd131 2026-08-05] SpaceX plans to build its AI services exclusively on Nvidia systems going forward.\\n    Quote: CEO Elon Musk told investors during the company's first-ever earnings call that going forward, SpaceX will build its AI services exclusively on Nvidia's systems.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1612; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e186 (); both are in force: SpaceX plans to use Nvidia chips in its AI1 orbital data-center satellites, with Nvidia set to be an initial hardware supplier; no timing or quantity is given.\\n- e1608 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source SpaceX CEO Elon Musk ; from gnews:baaf41a9a32da4e5efcfd131 2026-08-05] SpaceX expects to have more than 2 GW of compute capacity by the end of 2026.\\n    Quote: According to the CEO, SpaceX expects to end the year with more than 2 gigawatts of compute\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2010; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1611 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:baaf41a9a32da4e5efcfd131 2026-08-05] SpaceX entered into multibillion-dollar data-center capacity leasing agreements with Google and Anthropic for their AI services.\\n    Quote: SpaceX entered into multibillion-dollar leasing agreements with Google and Anthropic that will see the companies rent out SpaceX's data center capacity to power their AI services.\\n- e1626 [fact; CNBC; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:4a98f08dff6ec85e82597c02 2026-08-05] SpaceX reported capital expenditures of $18.4 billion, six times the prior level, with most spending going toward AI; the period is not specified.\\n    Quote: SpaceX shares sank 13.6% after a surge in AI spending rattled investors and clouded an otherwise expectation-beating quarter. Capital expenditures jumped sixfold to $18.4 billion, with the majority going toward AI.\\n- e1627 [party_statement; CNBC, quoting Elon Musk; original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:4a98f08dff6ec85e82597c02 2026-08-05] Elon Musk said SpaceX would use only NVIDIA processors for its AI infrastructure; no timeline or amount was specified.\\n    Quote: Musk's pledge that SpaceX would only use Nvidia processors for its AI infrastructure sent the chipmaker's shares up more than 3%. \\\"We're exclusive to Nvidia,\\\" he said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1857; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e186 (); both are in force: SpaceX plans to use Nvidia chips in its AI1 orbital data-center satellites, with Nvidia set to be an initial hardware supplier; no timing or quantity is given.\\n- e1628 [party_statement; CNBC; original source AMD CEO Lisa Su ; from gnews:4a98f08dff6ec85e82597c02 2026-08-05] AMD CEO Lisa Su gave guidance for data center growth of well over 100% for the period covered by the company's second-quarter guidance; the item does not specify the forecast period more precisely.\\n    Quote: AMD's stock closed 7% lower despite strong second-quarter earnings and guidance for data center growth \\\"well over 100%.\\\"\\n- e1642 [party_statement; Nokia; original source Zankore by Indosat 2026-08-06; from gnews:211ef1e93536bca4527f679e 2026-08-06] Zankore by Indosat set a deployment target of 1 GW of NVIDIA DSX AI Factory capacity; no deadline was specified.\\n    Quote: With a deployment target of 1 gigawatt (GW) of NVIDIA DSX AI Factory capacity, Zankore by Indosat lays the foundation for one of the region\\u2019s largest AI infrastructure platforms which will enable the next phase of agentic and enterprise AI adoption at scale.\\n- e1643 [party_statement; Nokia; original source Zankore by Indosat 2026-08-06; from gnews:211ef1e93536bca4527f679e 2026-08-06] Zankore plans to deliver approximately 200 MW of AI capacity in the first half of 2027, powered by NVIDIA GB300 NVL72.\\n    Quote: Zankore will deliver approximately 200 megawatts (MW) of AI capacity in the first half of 2027, powered by NVIDIA GB300 NVL72.\\n- e1653 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:cd5ac64487d5b4078b36f355 2026-08-06] Wall Street analysts\\u2019 average Q2 2026 revenue estimate for NVIDIA was $91.9 billion.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts are aware of this game, and have an average projection of $91.9 billion -- which is within Nvidia's 2% margin of error for its guidance.\\n- e1654 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool; original source Keithen Drury 2026-08-06; from gnews:cd5ac64487d5b4078b36f355 2026-08-06] Keithen Drury forecast NVIDIA Q2 2026 revenue of nearly $95 billion, which would double year over year.\\n    Quote: Still, I wouldn't be surprised if Nvidia reports revenue of nearly $95 billion, allowing it to say that it doubled its revenue year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e707; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1671 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:51e243641cee0eb43681f82b 2026-08-07] AMD acquired Taalas, giving AMD access to a chip design approach that embeds AI model weights in silicon for AI inference.\\n    Quote: The acquisition gives AMD access to a chip design approach that embeds AI model weights directly into silicon, reducing reliance on external memory and potentially improving speed and energy efficiency.\\n- e1672 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:51e243641cee0eb43681f82b 2026-08-07] AMD plans to incorporate Taalas technology with Instinct accelerators and EPYC processors in its Helios rack-scale systems.\\n    Quote: AMD plans to incorporate Taalas technology into its broader AI infrastructure strategy, combining it with Instinct accelerators and EPYC processors within its Helios rack-scale systems.\\n- e1687 [party_statement; Hock Tan, quoted by 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Hock Tan ; from gnews:1e5ae2752ae901af3268b2a3 2026-08-08] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan forecast Q3 AI semiconductor revenue of $16.0 billion, growth of more than 200% year over year.\\n    Quote: \\u201cin Q3 we expect semiconductor revenue from AI to grow over 200 percent year-over-year to $16.0 billion.\\u201d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e116, e1705; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1693 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:0214359f2efcb2f2aad79168 2026-08-08] Micron said HBM4 was in volume production, including supply to a lead accelerator customer.\\n    Quote: HBM4 in volume production.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e325; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1698 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:0214359f2efcb2f2aad79168 2026-08-08] Micron targets volume production of HBM4E on 1-gamma DRAM in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: HBM4E on 1-gamma DRAM is targeted for volume production in calendar 2027\\n- e1712 [third_party_view; Jon Gray, quoted by CNBC; original source Jon Gray, Blackstone President 2026-08-10; from gnews:820e72d2fe11d8aad54f380c 2026-08-10] Blackstone President Jon Gray said AI demand exceeded supply and AI use at Blackstone portfolio companies had surged sevenfold in 2026.\\n    Quote: Demand for AI is outstripping supply, with use at Blackstone portfolio companies surging sevenfold this year, Gray said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1789; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1733 [fact; stockstory.org; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:66195b46efbce26c584fbead 2026-08-10] Microsoft is developing its Maia 300 AI chip, which StockStory says could reduce NVIDIA's market share.\\n    Quote: Separately, Microsoft's development of its own Maia 300 AI chip presents a significant competitive threat, potentially reducing Nvidia's market share.\\n- e1734 [fact; stockstory.org; original source SpaceX; Elon Musk confirmed the partnership 2026-08-05; from gnews:66195b46efbce26c584fbead 2026-08-10] SpaceX announced it will exclusively use NVIDIA hardware for future AI infrastructure, including its Starmind orbital compute program; Elon Musk confirmed the partnership and named NVIDIA's Vera Rubin architecture as its hardware choice.\\n    Quote: The previous big move we wrote about was 5 days ago when the stock gained 3.5% on the news that SpaceX announced it will exclusively use the chipmaker's hardware for its future artificial intelligence infrastructure, including its ambitious new Starmind orbital compute program. During SpaceX's first earnings call as a public company, CEO Elon Musk confirmed the strategic partnership, publicly decl\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1669; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1735 [third_party_view; stockstory.org; original source JPMorgan ; from gnews:66195b46efbce26c584fbead 2026-08-10] JPMorgan estimated that SpaceX alone would account for a \\u201cvery significant percentage\\u201d of NVIDIA's total GPU shipments in 2027.\\n    Quote: Reinforcing the bullish outlook, JPMorgan noted that SpaceX alone is expected to absorb a \\\"very significant percentage\\\" of Nvidia's total GPU shipments in 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1625; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1736 [fact; stockstory.org; original source Hon Hai Precision Industry Co. (Foxconn) ; from gnews:66195b46efbce26c584fbead 2026-08-10] NVIDIA manufacturing partner Foxconn reported that monthly sales increased 54.2%; the item does not specify the month.\\n    Quote: Further signaling the sustained, relentless demand across Nvidia's supply chain, its primary manufacturing partner, Hon Hai Precision Industry Co. (Foxconn), reported a staggering 54.2% increase in monthly sales.\\n- e1737 [fact; BBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-10; from gnews:04a61e80230f06330016870f 2026-08-10] NVIDIA teamed up with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to raise $500 billion for AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has teamed up with some of Wall Street's largest banks and investors to raise $500bn (\\u00a3370bn) in capital for artificial intelligence infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1741; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1738 [fact; BBC; original source Anthropic, Macquarie Asset Management and GIC ; from gnews:04a61e80230f06330016870f 2026-08-10] Anthropic recently entered a deal with Macquarie Asset Management and GIC to invest in AI infrastructure; the item gives no deal amount.\\n    Quote: Anthropic also recently entered into a deal with Macquarie Asset Management and GIC, a sovereign wealth fund in Singapore, for its own investment in AI infrastructure.\\n- e1739 [third_party_view; BBC; original source BBC 2026-08-10; from gnews:04a61e80230f06330016870f 2026-08-10] Companies using NVIDIA GPUs, including major hyperscalers and AI firms, collectively spent more than $1 trillion on AI projects and infrastructure over three years; the BBC reports that more spending is expected.\\n    Quote: Such companies have collectively spent over $1tn in just three years on AI projects and infrastructure, with much more spending expected.\\n- e1740 [party_statement; BBC, reporting Anthropic's statement; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:04a61e80230f06330016870f 2026-08-10] Anthropic said demand for Claude requires significant new compute and that more financing was needed; it did not state the deal size.\\n    Quote: The company did not specify the size of the deal, but said more financing was needed as its popular chatbot Claude had become so popular that the \\\"demand requires significant new compute\\\".\\n- e1741 [party_statement; Nvidia, as reported by straitstimes.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-10; from gnews:deb37e6ad15fb74d545ca368 2026-08-10] NVIDIA said on August 10, 2026, that it partnered with six financial institutions to launch compute-financing platforms targeting more than US$500 billion in third-party capital for AI infrastructure; individual commitments and deployment timing were not disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said on Aug 10 it has partnered six major financial institutions to launch compute financing platforms aimed at raising more than US$500 billion (S$640 billion) in third-party capital for AI infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1737, e1764, e1817; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1742 [third_party_view; straitstimes.com; original source straitstimes.com 2026-08-10; from gnews:deb37e6ad15fb74d545ca368 2026-08-10] The article said Big Tech companies\\u2019 combined AI spending is set to exceed US$730 billion in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: Big Tech companies have signalled that spending on AI will \\u200bnot slow down, with combined outlays set to surpass US$730 billion in 2026.\\n- e1756 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Perplexity ; from nvblog:local-ai-open-source-models-agents-nemotron 2026-08-11] Perplexity introduced Portable Computer, a local agent app optimized to run on NVIDIA DGX Spark.\\n    Quote: Perplexity\\u2019s new local agent app \\u2014 Portable Computer \\u2014 lets users run a powerful personal AI agent on NVIDIA DGX Spark\\n- e1757 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Meta ; from nvblog:local-ai-open-source-models-agents-nemotron 2026-08-11] Meta released Muse Glimmer, a 30-billion-parameter open-weight model optimized for NVIDIA GeForce RTX PCs, DGX Spark, DGX Station and Jetson.\\n    Quote: Today, Meta released Muse Glimmer, a 30-billion-parameter, dense, open weight model with a 120K+ context window. It\\u2019s purpose-built for coding and local agentic AI.\\nOptimized for NVIDIA GeForce RTX PCs, NVIDIA DGX Spark, DGX Station and NVIDIA Jetson\\n- e1758 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Poolside AI ; from nvblog:local-ai-open-source-models-agents-nemotron 2026-08-11] Poolside AI launched Laguna S 2.1, a 118-billion-parameter open-weight coding model with an NVFP4 checkpoint that runs locally on one NVIDIA DGX Spark.\\n    Quote: Poolside AI launched Laguna S 2.1 , a 118-billion-parameter open weight agentic coding model that works through hours-long tasks. An NVFP4 checkpoint enables developers to run the model locally on a single NVIDIA DGX Spark with lower compute and memory requirements, without sacrificing accuracy.\\n- e1761 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-11; from nvblog:nvidia-ai-factory-compute 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported that one-year H100 rental pricing rose from about $1.70 to about $2.35 per GPU-hour between October 2025 and March 2026.\\n    Quote: One-year H100 rental pricing rose from about $1.70 per GPU-hour in October 2025 to about $2.35 per GPU-hour in March 2026.\\n- e1762 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-11; from nvblog:nvidia-ai-factory-compute 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported that cross-provider on-demand median GPU pricing rose from roughly $2.00 per GPU-hour in October 2025 to $2.70 in June 2026.\\n    Quote: Cross-provider on-demand median pricing rose from roughly $2.00 per GPU-hour in October 2025 to $2.70 in June 2026.\\n- e1763 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-11; from nvblog:nvidia-ai-factory-compute 2026-08-11] NVIDIA said reported B200 cloud rates were approximately $5.30\\u2013$7.05 per GPU-hour, a premium for Blackwell capacity.\\n    Quote: Blackwell capacity commands a premium, with reported B200 cloud rates spanning approximately $5.30 to $7.05 per GPU-hour.\\n- e1764 [fact; Latham & Watkins; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:fadabf850d186187869d38fb 2026-08-11] NVIDIA announced partnerships with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to establish independent compute-financing platforms intended to mobilize more than US$500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has announced strategic partnerships to establish independent compute financing platforms with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR to mobilize over US$500 billion of third\\u2011party capital for the buildout of AI infrastructure over time.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1741, e1908; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1768 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-08-11; from gnews:2e751a0d48924fef38cdaace 2026-08-11] Nvidia released its open-source Nemotron 3.5 Lightning AI model, which the company says can run on a single GPU.\\n    Quote: Nvidia released Nemotron 3.5 Lightning, an open-source model that the company says is \\\"lightweight\\\" and can run on a single GPU on a laptop or desktop.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1814; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1769 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-08-11; from gnews:2e751a0d48924fef38cdaace 2026-08-11] Nvidia made the Nemotron 3.5 Lightning model free for companies to download, use and modify without permission or payment.\\n    Quote: The new Nemotron offering is free for companies to download, use and modify without getting permission or paying Nvidia.\\n- e1770 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:2e751a0d48924fef38cdaace 2026-08-11] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said in an interview last month that free AI should benefit hardware and chip sales.\\n    Quote: \\\"Free AI should be great for hardware,\\\" Huang told Axios in an interview last month. \\\"Free AI should be great for chips.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1483; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1771 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-08-11; from gnews:2e751a0d48924fef38cdaace 2026-08-11] Nvidia said CrowdStrike, CodeRabbit and Harvey have tested and customized Nemotron 3.5 Lightning.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said companies including CrowdStrike , CodeRabbit and Harvey have tested and customized its latest model.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1815; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1802 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $81.6 billion in FY2027 Q1, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia generated $81.6 billion in total revenue during its fiscal 2027 first quarter (ended May 28), an 85% increase from the year-ago period.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e712; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1804 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] NVIDIA guided to FY2027 Q2 total revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Quote: According to the company's own guidance, the upcoming second-quarter report on Aug. 26 could show $91 billion in total revenue (plus or minus 2%),\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1528; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1805 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The average analyst ; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] The average analyst estimates NVIDIA FY2027 Q2 total revenue of $91.8 billion.\\n    Quote: the average analyst expects $91.8 billion in second-quarter revenue.\\n- e1806 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts ; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] Analysts expect NVIDIA to guide to approximately $103.1 billion in FY2027 Q3 total revenue.\\n    Quote: Analysts believe the company will forecast around $103.1 billion in total revenue for the fiscal 2027 third quarter,\\n- e1810 [third_party_view; Network World; original source Mark Tauschek, distinguished analyst at Info-Tech Research Group ; from gnews:b60bc5a0f82bac5679239ec6 2026-08-11] Mark Tauschek projected that enterprise costs would rise 15%\\u201320% because of the proposed Nvidia financing pool; the item gives no forecast period.\\n    Quote: In fact, for enterprises, Tauschek projected a 15%-20% cost hike.\\n- e1811 [third_party_view; Sanchit Vir Gogia, as quoted by Network World; original source Sanchit Vir Gogia, chief analyst at Greyhound Research ; from gnews:b60bc5a0f82bac5679239ec6 2026-08-11] Sanchit Vir Gogia estimated AI chip prices would stay roughly flat for another year, with good pricing increasingly requiring long-term customer commitments and shrinking commitment discounts.\\n    Quote: He estimated that AI chip prices would be roughly the same for another year, and increasingly only at a good price if customers sign a long-term commitment. \\u201cThe discount for committing is shrinking, not growing,\\u201d he said.\\n- e1812 [third_party_view; Mark Tauschek, as quoted by Network World; original source Mark Tauschek, distinguished analyst at Info-Tech Research Group ; from gnews:b60bc5a0f82bac5679239ec6 2026-08-11] Mark Tauschek said new fabs would take years to build and predicted that the proposed Nvidia fund would probably worsen the chip shortage.\\n    Quote: \\u201cIt will also take years to build new chip fabs. [The proposed Nvidia fund] will probably exacerbate the shortage.\\u201d\\n- e1813 [third_party_view; Justin Greis, as quoted by Network World; original source Justin Greis, CEO of Acceligence ; from gnews:b60bc5a0f82bac5679239ec6 2026-08-11] Justin Greis predicted that the financing would accelerate AI infrastructure creation but that enterprise costs would remain elevated and availability constrained for 12\\u201318 months.\\n    Quote: \\u201cMy view is that this financing will accelerate the creation of AI infrastructure, but it will not provide meaningful near-term price relief for most enterprises. In fact, I think the next 12-18 months could remain a period of elevated costs and constrained availability as the market absorbs this new wave of investment,\\u201d Greis said.\\n- e1814 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-11; from gnews:0e014dc5547e8f83afbc385f 2026-08-11] NVIDIA released its free, open-source Nemotron 3.5 Lightning model, designed for autonomous-agent workloads, on August 11, 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia $NVDA released Nemotron 3.5 Lightning on Tuesday, a free, open-source AI model the company says can run on a single graphics processing unit on a PC and is designed for autonomous agent workloads.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1768; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1815 [party_statement; qz.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-11; from gnews:0e014dc5547e8f83afbc385f 2026-08-11] CrowdStrike, CodeRabbit, and Harvey have tested and customized NVIDIA\\u2019s Nemotron 3.5 Lightning model; no date or period was given.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said companies including CrowdStrike $CRWD , CodeRabbit, and Harvey have tested and customized the model.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1771; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1816 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-11; from gnews:0e014dc5547e8f83afbc385f 2026-08-11] NVIDIA released NeMo Switchyard, an open-source library for routing agent-workflow requests among models, alongside Nemotron 3.5 Lightning on August 11, 2026.\\n    Quote: Alongside Nemotron 3.5 Lightning, Nvidia released NeMo Switchyard, an open-source model routing library that automatically directs requests to the most suitable and cost-efficient model for each step of an agent workflow.\\n- e1817 [fact; CoinDesk; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-10; from gnews:55bdb3e767f79bead8c93322 2026-08-11] NVIDIA said it signed MoUs with Apollo Global Management, Blackstone, BlackRock, Brookfield Asset Management, Goldman Sachs and KKR to establish financing platforms that could eventually access more than $500 billion in third-party capital.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said Monday it has signed memorandums of understanding with six Wall Street heavyweights \\u2013 Apollo Global Management, Blackstone, BlackRock, Brookfield Asset Management, Goldman Sachs and KKR \\u2013 to set up financing platforms that could eventually tap more than $500 billion in third\\u2011party capital.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1741; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1830 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois; original source Alex Sirois 2026-08-11; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] Alex Sirois estimates NVIDIA has more than 80% market share in high-end AI accelerators; the period is not specified.\\n    Quote: Nvidia commands over 80% market share in high-end AI accelerators.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e438; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1831 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.23% year over year.\\n    Quote: Q1 FY27 revenue landed at $81.615 billion, up 85.23% year over year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e474; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1833 [party_statement; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] NVIDIA management guided Q2 FY2027 total revenue to $91 billion.\\n    Quote: Management guided Q2 FY27 to $91 billion\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e316; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1835 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] OpenAI committed to 10 gigawatts of NVIDIA systems; the timing of the commitment is not specified.\\n    Quote: OpenAI committed to 10 gigawatts of Nvidia systems\\n- e1836 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois; original source Meta ; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] Meta signed a multigenerational deal covering millions of NVIDIA Blackwell and Rubin GPUs; the signing date is not specified.\\n    Quote: Meta signed a multigenerational deal across millions of Blackwell and Rubin GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1477; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1837 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois; original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] CoreWeave is targeting more than 5 gigawatts of AI-factory capacity by 2030.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave is targeting 5+ gigawatts of AI factories by 2030.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e683; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1840 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois; original source Alex Sirois 2026-08-11; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 revenue guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue; the author estimates this removes roughly $4.6 billion in H20 sales per quarter.\\n    Quote: Nvidia\\u2019s Q2 guide excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China, erasing what used to be roughly $4.6 billion per quarter in H20 sales.\\n- e1851 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.61 billion.\\n    Quote: $81.61 billion in revenue,\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e762; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1853 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, reporting NVIDIA's earnings-call guidance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] NVIDIA guided to Q2 revenue of about $91 billion.\\n    Quote: Q2 guidance of $91 billion, give or take.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e763; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1854 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, summarizing Musk's first earnings call and its own estimate; original source Elon Musk (capacity and supplier plan); 247Wallst.com (potential orders estimate) ; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] The article says Elon Musk plans to scale SpaceX data center capacity to 10 GW by 2027 using NVIDIA hardware exclusively, which the article estimates could generate $300 billion in orders.\\n    Quote: Musk's plan to scale SpaceX data centers to 10 gigawatts by 2027, exclusively with NVDA hardware, could generate $300 billion in new orders.\\n- e1855 [party_statement; 247Wallst.com, reporting Elon Musk's remarks; original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] Elon Musk said SpaceX aims to increase data center capacity from roughly 1.4 GW to 10 GW by year-end 2027.\\n    Quote: On SpaceX\\u2019s first earnings call, Elon Musk told investors he wants to scale data center capacity from roughly 1.4 gigawatts today to 10 gigawatts by year-end 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1609; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, reporting SemiAnalysis's estimate; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] SemiAnalysis estimates SpaceX's data center buildout could cost $300 billion\\u2013$500 billion in 2027 capital spending, based on $50 billion per gigawatt annually, and considers the 10 GW target achievable.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis estimates the buildout at $50 billion per gigawatt annually, or $300 billion to $500 billion in 2027 capital spending alone, and considers the target achievable .\\n- e1857 [party_statement; 247Wallst.com, reporting Musk's remarks and SemiAnalysis's view; original source Elon Musk (supplier choice); SemiAnalysis (financing-support interpretation) ; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] Musk said SpaceX would work exclusively with NVIDIA; SemiAnalysis links the supplier choice to expected vendor-financing support.\\n    Quote: Here\\u2019s the rub: Musk said SpaceX would work exclusively with NVIDIA, a decision SemiAnalysis ties to expected vendor-financing support.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1627; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1859 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com; original source 247Wallst.com 2026-08-11; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] The author estimates that $150 billion\\u2013$200 billion in incremental SpaceX orders could support a $500 NVIDIA share price; the author cites NVIDIA Q2 revenue guidance of $91 billion, up roughly 34% year over year.\\n    Quote: Layer even $150 billion to $200 billion in incremental SpaceX orders onto NVIDIA\\u2019s accelerating trajectory, including Q2 guidance of $91 billion, up roughly 34% year over year, and the path to a $500 share price becomes a math problem.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e739; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1860 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com; original source 247Wallst.com 2026-08-11; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] The author says SpaceX's proposed capital spending, comparable to AWS and Google combined despite SpaceX being less profitable, creates customer-concentration risk for NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: A single customer proposing capex on par with AWS and Google combined, at a company far less profitable than either, is exactly the kind of concentration risk bears have salivated over.\\n- e1862 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com; original source 247Wallst.com 2026-08-11; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] The author says NVIDIA's Q2 guidance assumed zero China Data Center compute and that AMD is pursuing the same hyperscalers with its MI450 roadmap.\\n    Quote: Bears also point to zero China Data Center compute in the Q2 guide and the reality that AMD ( NASDAQ:AMD ) is courting the same hyperscalers with its MI450 roadmap.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e406; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1864 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com; original source 247Wallst.com 2026-08-11; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] The author says that if SpaceX allocates any part of its projected $300 billion\\u2013$500 billion in 2027 capital spending to NVIDIA, revenue estimates could rise and a $500 share price could become plausible within 12\\u201318 months.\\n    Quote: If SpaceX directs even a fraction of its projected $300 billion to $500 billion in 2027 capital spending toward NVIDIA, with support from the Apollo and BlackRock financing platform, revenue estimates get rewritten higher and a $500 share price becomes a serious 12- to 18-month conversation.\\n- e1865 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com; original source 247Wallst.com 2026-08-11; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] The author identifies firm SpaceX procurement commitments and NVIDIA Data Center growth above 60% into FY2028 as bullish confirmation conditions.\\n    Quote: Bullish confirmation would require firm procurement commitments for the SpaceX buildout and continued NVIDIA Data Center growth above 60% into fiscal 2028.\\n- e1874 [fact; Network World; original source NVIDIA 2026-06-22; from gnews:9b084fc0f1583da0df01c45c 2026-08-12] NVIDIA formally launched its Vera Rubin CPU-and-GPU platform on June 22, 2026, for AI and high-performance-computing infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Nvidia unveils Vera Rubin platform targeting AI, HPC infrastructure\\nJune 22, 2026 : Nvidia has formally launched the Vera Rubin platform , a combination CPU and GPU platform billed as a major step forward in the convergence of artificial intelligence and high-performance computing (HPC) for scientific research.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e742; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1875 [fact; Network World; original source Dell and Super Micro 2026-06-22; from gnews:9b084fc0f1583da0df01c45c 2026-08-12] Dell and Super Micro unveiled AI servers based on NVIDIA\\u2019s Vera Rubin platform on June 22, 2026; Dell\\u2019s PowerEdge XE8812 uses Vera Rubin NVL4 and supports up to 144 GPUs per rack.\\n    Quote: Dell and Super Micro each unveiled new AI servers as part of Nvidia\\u2019s Vera Rubin rollout. The Dell PowerEdge XE8812, with its core Nvidia Vera Rubin NVL4 architecture, scales up to 144 GPUs per rack and will be at the heart of the Dell AI Factory with Nvidia preconfigured package of server, storage, networking, and software infrastructure.\\n- e1876 [fact; Network World; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-22; from gnews:9b084fc0f1583da0df01c45c 2026-08-12] NVIDIA introduced its next-generation Spectrum-X Ethernet networking platform on July 22, 2026, targeting AI factories designed to connect millions of GPUs.\\n    Quote: Nvidia unveils Spectrum-X networking platform designed to connect millions of GPUs\\nJuly 22, 2026: Nvidia has introduced its next-generation Spectrum-X Ethernet networking platform , positioning it as a key building block for the next wave of \\u201cgigascale\\u201d AI factories designed to connect millions of GPUs while reducing power consumption and operational costs.\\n- e1879 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307 2026-08-12] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya says NVIDIA has about 70% industry share and expects the Vera Rubin product cycle to help cement it; the item gives no period for the share estimate.\\n    Quote: However, we remain positive on potential with Nvidia's next-gen Vera Rubin product cycle in further cementing the company's about 70% industry share.\\n- e1883 [fact; Fortune, reporting NVIDIA\\u2019s announcement; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-10; from gnews:c24969942ce8886f9557c571 2026-08-12] NVIDIA announced partnerships with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to create AI-infrastructure financing platforms intended to mobilize more than $500 billion, largely from third-party investors.\\n    Quote: On Monday, Nvidia announced partnerships with Apollo , BlackRock , Blackstone , Brookfield , Goldman Sachs , and KKR to create financing platforms intended to mobilize more than $500 billion for AI infrastructure. The money will largely come from \\u201cthird-party investors,\\u201d allowing Nvidia customers to finance chips and data centers while keeping Nvidia\\u2019s own risk limited and off the balance sheet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1721; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1884 [third_party_view; Fortune; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:c24969942ce8886f9557c571 2026-08-12] Goldman Sachs estimates AI-related financing accounts for nearly one-quarter of gross U.S. investment-grade issuance and AI investment is approaching $600 billion in 2026.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates AI-related financing now accounts for nearly one-quarter of all gross U.S. investment-grade issuance, while AI investment itself is approaching $600 billion this year.\\n- e1888 [third_party_view; Fortune; original source Ben Thompson ; from gnews:c24969942ce8886f9557c571 2026-08-12] Ben Thompson characterized NVIDIA\\u2019s use of its profits to reduce customers\\u2019 cost of capital and ease financing for NVIDIA-based data centers as, in a certain sense, a price cut.\\n    Quote: Ben Thompson, who writes the technology strategy publication Stratechery , calls that \\u201cin a certain sense, a price cut\\u201d: Nvidia is using its own profits to reduce customers\\u2019 cost of capital and make Nvidia-based data centers easier to finance.\\n- e1890 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:59a5ada9c38635f7607e3e4e 2026-08-12] NVIDIA reported quarterly revenue of $81 billion, up 85% year over year; the quarter is not specified.\\n    Quote: The company reported $81 billion in quarterly revenue, an 85% increase from a year earlier\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398, e1892; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1891 [party_statement; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:59a5ada9c38635f7607e3e4e 2026-08-12] NVIDIA\\u2019s current-quarter revenue forecast is $91 billion; the quarter is not specified.\\n    Quote: while its current-quarter forecast calls for $91 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1265, e1893; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1892 [fact; Douglas A. McIntyre; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e374df62e211739d07419f77 2026-08-12] NVIDIA reported quarterly revenue of $81 billion, up 85% year over year; the item does not identify the quarter.\\n    Quote: In its most recent quarter, it had revenue of $81 billion, which grew 85% year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1890; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1893 [party_statement; Douglas A. McIntyre; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e374df62e211739d07419f77 2026-08-12] NVIDIA guided revenue to $91 billion for the current quarter; the item does not identify the quarter.\\n    Quote: And, it said in the current quarter, revenue will be $91 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1891; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1897 [fact; Tom's Hardware, reporting by Hassam Nasir; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:946c90e22055765847d93eae 2026-08-12] NVIDIA raised the US Marketplace listed price of the RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell workstation GPU to $16,000 in August 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has once again silently raised the price of its RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell on its US Marketplace website to $16,000.\\n- e1903 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source CoreWeave 2026-08-10; from gnews:011203b517e045ffe773b1fb 2026-08-12] CoreWeave CEO Mike Intrator disclosed that CoreWeave signed a contract for NVIDIA A100 GPUs running into 2029.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave signed a contract for Nvidia A100 GPUs that runs into 2029, nine years after the Ampere-based SKU debuted, CEO Mike Intrator disclosed on the company's second-quarter earnings call on Monday.\\n- e1904 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source CoreWeave 2026-08-10; from gnews:011203b517e045ffe773b1fb 2026-08-12] CoreWeave reported second-quarter revenue of $2.58 billion, up 112% year over year, and a $104 billion revenue backlog excluding more than $25 billion of new commitments booked since July.\\n    Quote: The company reported $2.58 billion in quarterly revenue, up 112% year over year, and a $104 billion revenue backlog that excludes more than $25 billion in new commitments booked since July.\\n- e1905 [party_statement; Mike Intrator, as quoted by Tom's Hardware; original source Mike Intrator, CoreWeave CEO 2026-08-10; from gnews:011203b517e045ffe773b1fb 2026-08-12] CoreWeave CEO Mike Intrator said pricing for prior-generation GPU SKUs was at or above levels from years earlier.\\n    Quote: \\u201cpricing for prior generation SKUs is at or above where it was years ago.\\u201d\\n- e1906 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source CoreWeave 2026-08-10; from gnews:011203b517e045ffe773b1fb 2026-08-12] CoreWeave's contracted power was 3.7 GW in Q2 and 4.2 GW on 2026-08-10, compared with 1.5 GW online; customer commitments covered nearly three times its deliverable capacity.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave's contracted power grew to 3.7 GW in Q2 and stood at 4.2 GW as of Monday, against just 1.5 GW online, so customer commitments already cover nearly triple the capacity the company can deliver.\\n- e1907 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware; original source Nitin Agrawal, CoreWeave CFO 2026-08-10; from gnews:011203b517e045ffe773b1fb 2026-08-12] CoreWeave CFO Nitin Agrawal said a very limited share of its fleet was coming up for renewal and older-generation GPU average selling prices were at or above year-earlier levels.\\n    Quote: CFO Nitin Agrawal added on the call that capacity coming up for renewal represents a very limited share of CoreWeave's fleet, with older-generation ASPs at or above levels from a year ago.\\n- e1908 [fact; FinTech Magazine; original source NVIDIA, KKR, Goldman Sachs, Brookfield, Blackstone, BlackRock and Apollo ; from gnews:c56a4f66f2f22be6ae12da82 2026-08-13] NVIDIA and KKR, Goldman Sachs, Brookfield, Blackstone, BlackRock and Apollo signed MOUs to establish separate financing platforms for NVIDIA customers; the platforms are intended to unlock more than US$500 billion in third-party funding for AI data centers, chip deployments and related infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Under the signed Memorandums of Understanding (MOUs) , each institution will separately underwrite, structure and syndicate capital pools, offering competitive debt and hybrid financing terms to NVIDIA\\u2019s enterprise, lab and cloud customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1919 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance, reporting Goldman Sachs analyst James Schneider\\u2019s note; original source James Schneider, Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:4ec81c2c385eafc1ab9156b8 2026-08-13] Goldman Sachs analyst James Schneider expected NVIDIA to deliver a solid quarter with meaningful upside to guidance, citing tight GPU supply and demand.\\n    Quote: \\\"We expect a solid quarter [for Nvidia] with meaningful upside to guidance supported by tight GPU supply/demand trends, but think the bar for the stock is elevated given its +12% move in two weeks,\\\" Goldman Sachs analyst James Schneider cautioned in a new note.\\n- e1920 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance, quoting Goldman Sachs analyst James Schneider; original source James Schneider, Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:4ec81c2c385eafc1ab9156b8 2026-08-13] Goldman Sachs analyst James Schneider said expectations for NVIDIA were elevated, citing positive 2026 U.S. hyperscaler capex revisions and commentary on near-term GPU capacity shortages.\\n    Quote: \\\"We believe expectations are elevated given strong demand datapoints, including positive 2026 capex revisions from US hyperscalers and commentary on near-term GPU capacity shortages.\\\"\\n- e1921 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance; original source Yahoo Finance 2026-08-13; from gnews:4ec81c2c385eafc1ab9156b8 2026-08-13] Yahoo Finance consensus estimates forecast NVIDIA revenue of about $92 billion for its second quarter of fiscal 2027, up about 96% year over year.\\n    Quote: Consensus estimates as compiled on Yahoo Finance forecast revenue near $92 billion \\u2014 a massive ~96% year-over-year jump.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1569, e1930; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1928 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] NVIDIA reported FY2027 first-quarter revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85% year over year; the quarter ended April 26, 2026.\\n    Quote: Revenue in its fiscal 2027 first quarter (which ended April 26) was $81.61 billion, up 85% from a year earlier.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1929 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] NVIDIA projected FY2027 second-quarter revenue of $91 billion, up 94% year over year from $46.7 billion.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's projected fiscal 2027 Q2 revenue is $91 billion, a 94% gain from a year ago, when revenue was $46.7 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e112; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1930 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts surveyed by Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Yahoo! Finance-surveyed analysts estimated NVIDIA FY2027 second-quarter revenue at $91.85 billion, up 96.5% year over year.\\n    Quote: Analysts surveyed by Yahoo! Finance are expecting even more, with the consensus revenue estimate of $91.85 billion, up 96.5% from a year ago.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1921; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1932 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1258c8b56ae0bb231116e1e5 2026-08-13] The author reports NVIDIA Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.23% year over year.\\n    Quote: Q1 FY2027 revenue landed at $81.615 billion, up 85.23% year over year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e474; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1936 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1258c8b56ae0bb231116e1e5 2026-08-13] The author reports NVIDIA made no H20 shipments to China in Q1 FY2027.\\n    Quote: Q1 saw no H20 shipments to China\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e638; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1937 [party_statement; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1258c8b56ae0bb231116e1e5 2026-08-13] The author reports NVIDIA guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, assuming zero China Data Center compute revenue.\\n    Quote: Q2 guidance of $91.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, explicitly assumes zero China Data Center compute.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e479; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1940 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois; original source Alex Sirois 2026-08-13; from gnews:1258c8b56ae0bb231116e1e5 2026-08-13] The author estimates reasoning models use 100 to 1,000 times as many tokens as a one-shot query, supporting the author's view that agentic AI increases compute demand.\\n    Quote: Reasoning models chew through a hundred to a thousand times more tokens than a one-shot query.\\n- e1941 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:772d816faae6d31e5ae347e3 2026-08-13] NVIDIA reported FY2027 first-quarter total revenue of US$81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: In the first quarter of its fiscal year ending January 2027 (1QFY2027), NVIDIA's revenue jumped 85% year-on-year (YoY) to US$81.6 billion\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e893; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1943 [third_party_view; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source Industry sources ; from gnews:772d816faae6d31e5ae347e3 2026-08-13] Industry sources estimate TSMC holds 90% of manufacturing share for leading-edge logic chips.\\n    Quote: Industry sources place TSMC's share of manufacturing leading-edge logic chips at an impressive 90%, meaning there is very little effective competition.\\n- e1944 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source TSMC ; from gnews:772d816faae6d31e5ae347e3 2026-08-13] TSMC reported calendar 2026 second-quarter revenue of US$40.2 billion, up 36%, and net income of US$22.37 billion, up 77.8%.\\n    Quote: In the second quarter of 2026 (2Q2026), TSMC's revenue rose 36% to US$40.2 billion, while net income surged 77.8% to US$22.37 billion, supported by its leading-edge process technologies.\\n- e1945 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:772d816faae6d31e5ae347e3 2026-08-13] SK Hynix reported calendar 2026 second-quarter revenue of KRW79.32 trillion, up 257% year over year, driven by rising memory prices including HBM.\\n    Quote: SK Hynix's revenue skyrocketed 257% YoY to KRW79.32 trillion in 2Q2026, driven by an overall surge in memory prices, including HBM.\\n- e1948 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source uncoveralpha.com 2026-08-13; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] The author estimates NVIDIA\\u2019s share of the merchant AI-chip market for training workloads is above 90%, with no period specified.\\n    Quote: In training, Nvidia\\u2019s share of the merchant market is still estimated to be north of 90%;\\n- e1949 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] Microsoft processed more than 100 trillion tokens in a quarter in 2025, up fivefold year over year; the quarter is unspecified.\\n    Quote: Microsoft processed over 100 trillion tokens in a quarter in 2025, up 5x year over year.\\n- e1950 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source Google ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] Google cited processing of 22 billion tokens per minute across Alphabet\\u2019s APIs; no date is given.\\n    Quote: Google cited 22 billion tokens processed per minute across Alphabet\\u2019s APIs.\\n- e1951 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source uncoveralpha.com 2026-08-13; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] The author says Google produces more than 3 million TPUs annually; no period is specified.\\n    Quote: Google produces +3 million TPUs annually.\\n- e1952 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source Google and Broadcom ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] Google and Broadcom expanded their TPU-capacity arrangement in April 2026 to add roughly 3.5 GW of next-generation TPU capacity starting in 2027.\\n    Quote: In April this year, that was expanded with Broadcom to roughly 3.5 additional gigawatts of next-gen TPU capacity starting 2027.\\n- e1953 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source uncoveralpha.com 2026-08-13; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] The author says Amazon\\u2019s Trainium and Graviton chips business has annualized revenue above $25 billion and is growing at a triple-digit rate; no measurement date is specified.\\n    Quote: Amazon\\u2019s chips business (Trainium + Graviton) now runs at over $25B in annualized revenue, growing triple digits.\\n- e1954 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] Amazon\\u2019s Project Rainier Anthropic cluster is scaling from more than 500,000 Trainium chips toward 1 million chips; no completion date is given.\\n    Quote: Project Rainier - the Anthropic cluster is scaling from 500,000+ Trainium chips toward 1M.\\n- e1955 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source The Information; the \\u201cgigawatts\\u2019 worth\\u201d remark is attributed to an Azure Maia manager ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] The Information reported that Microsoft may unveil Maia 300 as soon as September 2026, is negotiating with TSMC for capacity for more than 300,000 chips for delivery in 2027, and has an eventual target above 1 million units; an Azure Maia manager described a potential \\u201cgigawatts\\u2019 worth\\u201d of Maia.\\n    Quote: The Information reported that Microsoft will unveil Maia 300 as soon as September and is negotiating with TSMC for capacity for more than 300,000 chips for delivery in 2027, with an eventual target of over 1 million units and, per an Azure Maia manager, \\u201cgigawatts\\u2019 worth\\u201d of Maia.\\n- e1956 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source Unspecified reports; Microsoft for the reported customer outreach ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] Reports cited by the author put Maia\\u2019s targeted cost advantage at 30\\u201340% versus NVIDIA chips; Microsoft is courting Anthropic as an anchor customer.\\n    Quote: Reports mention a targeted cost advantage of 30-40% over Nvidia chips, and Microsoft is actively courting Anthropic as an anchor customer for it.\\n- e1957 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source Amazon, Google, Microsoft, and Meta ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] The author says Amazon, Google, Microsoft, and Meta framed hundreds of billions of dollars in future AI spending as optional on their Q2 earnings calls; the amount and period are unspecified.\\n    Quote: On the Q2 calls, all three hyperscalers and Meta described their CapEx in a way that Nvidia surely doesn\\u2019t like. They framed hundreds of billions in future AI spending as an option rather than a must.\\n- e1958 [party_statement; uncoveralpha.com; original source Amy Hood, Microsoft ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] Microsoft CFO Amy Hood said that if demand changes, the company can slow purchases of the largest component of its data-center investment; the item presents this as referring to chips.\\n    Quote: \\u201cif the demand environment changes, you just slow down what is, in fact, the largest component\\u201d\\n(Amy Hood, Microsoft)\\n- e1959 [party_statement; uncoveralpha.com; original source Anat Ashkenazi, Google ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] Google\\u2019s Anat Ashkenazi said Google will continue investing as long as it sees an attractive return; the investment amount and period are unspecified.\\n    Quote: \\u201cGoogle will keep investing as long as we see an attractive return on that investment\\u201d\\n(Google\\u2019s Anat Ashkenazil).\\n- e1968 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source uncoveralpha.com 2026-08-13; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] The author judges that NVIDIA\\u2019s moves could contribute to an AI bubble and an overbuild of compute capacity.\\n    Quote: These moves by Nvidia are important because they do change the short-term nature of the compute ecosystem and could help create a real AI bubble and an overbuild in compute capacity.\\n- e1976 [fact; Computer Weekly; original source Zayo ; from gnews:0738c693650b928a54b7abc0 2026-08-13] Zayo plans to build more than 8,000 miles of long-haul fibre, including six new routes, and overbuild its network in 10 high-demand markets as part of its collaboration with NVIDIA; no completion period was stated.\\n    Quote: In practical terms, the collaboration will see Zayo building more than 8,000 miles of long-haul fibre across what it considers the fastest-growing AI corridor and significantly expanding capacity across its existing infrastructure.\\n- e1981 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Komdigi, Indosat Ooredoo Hutchison, NVIDIA and Universitas Gadjah Mada 2026-08-14; from nvblog:ugm-indosat-nvidia-ai-technology-center 2026-08-14] Komdigi, Indosat, NVIDIA and Universitas Gadjah Mada launched Indonesia\\u2019s first university-based AI technology center in Yogyakarta this week.\\n    Quote: This week, the Ministry of Communication and Digital Affairs (Komdigi), Indosat Ooredoo Hutchison (Indosat or IOH), NVIDIA and Universitas Gadjah Mada (UGM) launched the UGM Indosat NVIDIA AI Technology Center (NVAITC) in Yogyakarta \\u2014 the country\\u2019s first university-based AI technology center.\\n- e1990 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:21e48426197a853da4ea7af0 2026-08-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal first-quarter total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) reported record financial results for its fiscal first quarter, which provide provide further financial context for Cramer's bullish view of the broader AI infrastructure buildout.\\nThe company delivered total revenue of $81.6 billion, an 85% increase compared to the prior year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1666; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1995 [third_party_view; Jim Cramer, on Mad Money; original source Jim Cramer 2026-08-12; from gnews:21e48426197a853da4ea7af0 2026-08-14] Jim Cramer said CoreWeave\\u2019s results showed that its NVIDIA-chip data centers had a useful life longer than three to five years.\\n    Quote: And finally, CoreWeave reported a monster quarter, demonstrating that their strategy of building data centers embedding NVIDIA chips\\u2026 a longer shelf life than 3 to 5 years, was more than paying off.\\n- e1996 [third_party_view; Jim Cramer, on Mad Money; original source Jim Cramer 2026-08-12; from gnews:21e48426197a853da4ea7af0 2026-08-14] Jim Cramer said nine-year-old NVIDIA GPUs could be as valuable as, or more valuable than, when they were built.\\n    Quote: He gave us genuine proof that older NVIDIA GPUs are as valuable or even more valuable than when they were built, even ones that came out of the foundry 9 years ago.\\n- e1999 [third_party_view; es.tradingview.com; original source es.tradingview.com 2026-08-14; from gnews:d2f8f21b70ab607dade514a7 2026-08-14] The article expects AMD's Helios rack systems to reduce overall GPU demand, citing AMD's inference position and NVIDIA supply constraints.\\n    Quote: AMD\\u2019s Helios rack systems are expected to cut into overall GPU demand, given AMD's strengths in inference and the supply constraints currently limiting NVIDIA amid surging global demand.\\n- e2000 [third_party_view; es.tradingview.com; original source es.tradingview.com 2026-08-14; from gnews:d2f8f21b70ab607dade514a7 2026-08-14] The article forecasts inference infrastructure value to grow at a double-digit compound annual rate through the middle of the next decade and exceed twice the model-training market's peak.\\n    Quote: The value of the inference industry infrastructure alone is forecast to grow at a solid double-digit compound annual growth rate through the middle of the next decade, more than doubling the model training market at its peak.\\n- e2004 [fact; How-To Geek; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c0d25f56474b9282cb9442b4 2026-08-14] NVIDIA officially moved its native GeForce Now app for Linux out of beta, expanding the service to Linux users.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's native GeForce Now app for Linux has officially left beta, giving the open-source OS a major, refined cloud streaming option beyond the web browser.\\n- e2010 [party_statement; The article; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:523fd45e2f88220fdc75964f 2026-08-14] SpaceX has discussed targets to expand computing capacity beyond 2 GW by the end of 2026 and to nearly 10 GW in 2027.\\n    Quote: SpaceX has discussed rapidly expanding its computing capacity, with targets exceeding 2 gigawatts by the end of 2026 and approaching 10 gigawatts the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1608; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 7]\\n- v16 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: The reference is passage by December 31, 2026, which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. RASA had passed the House but was still awaiting Senate action on July 23, leaving about five months for passage. The reported GB300 access in Thailand adds policy urgency, but supplies no evide\\n- v22 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan\\u2019s Ministry of Economic Affairs adopt performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The reference is rung 2: no formal adoption by the deadline is indicated in the available plan or timeline. The consultations and reported consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry set no timeline; that evidence does not make formal adoption by January 31, 2027 the mor\\n- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan adopt a criminal ban on all AI chip exports to China by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: The reference is rung 1: the supplied evidence says Taiwan is considering a ban, not that it has adopted one. That consideration, in the context of an investigation into suspected smuggling, raises the prospect of action, but the evidence gives no adopted measure, legislative stage, or timetable. Th\\n- v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms\\u2019 access to advanced U.S. chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-07; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or Commerce completion deadline is supplied, and the stated status is a formal investigation, not a scheduled conclusion. The evidence makes non-conclusion by December 31, 2026 more likely: Commerce is still examining access through computing capacit\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-08-13; reasons: The original stated plan of around $650 billion sits in rung 3. The more current four-company tracker near $745 billion and the indication of more than $730 billion in 2026 AI-infrastructure spending place the best available expectation in rung 4. The tracker implies spending above $700 billion but \\n- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-29) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-08-13; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s forecast of more than $1 trillion for 2027, which sits in rung 3; other estimates around or above $1 trillion are consistent with that reference. Recent evidence of optional future spending and companies\\u2019 ability to slow purchases if demand changes tempers the up\\n- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 Yes; judged 2026-07-31; reasons: The reference is no authorization by January 31, 2027 (rung 1): no market consensus or Chinese official authorization deadline is supplied. The forecast nevertheless favors a limited yes. Reports specifically say Chinese officials told Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek they would be permitted to buy s\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The forecast remains about $440 billion, above the rung-1 boundary of $431.2 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, management guided Q2 to $91 billion, and analysts estimate Q3 at $103.1 billion. Those quarterly figures support a strong run-rate, while the carried evidence that H2 data-center compute could exceed Wall Street expectations supports an outcome above the reference. The new SpaceX estimate of up to $300 billion in potential orders is not counted in full: it is conditional, and the associated 10 GW capacity target is for year-end 2027, mostly beyond NVIDIA FY2027. Confirmed Blackwell/Rubin orders span multiple products and periods, so they likewise do not translate dollar-for-dollar into F\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"old-1\", 4163, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 146 items]\\n[Not shown: 949 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e28 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue of $392 billion in FY2027, compared with trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion, and $554 billion in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e51, e107, e218, e432; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36, e544; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e111 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion, while non-GAAP earnings per share jumped 140% to $1.87.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e273, e274, e326, e478, e1401, e1425; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e112 [party_statement; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA guided fiscal Q2 2027 revenue to approximately $91 billion, nearly 95% above the year-earlier quarter.\\n    Quote: For the second quarter of fiscal 2027, NVIDIA projects revenues of approximately $91 billion, representing nearly 95% year-over-year growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127, e308, e351, e739, e1265, e1929, e2054; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e141 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 AI server rack had been pushed back more than twelve months, to 2028, because of manufacturing problems.\\n    Quote: Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99, e157; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e142 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA had scrapped its planned NVL72x2 design, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back.\\n    Quote: A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e101, e151; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e144 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA canceled the four-compute-die Rubin Ultra version, leaving a two-die version with roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Quote: The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103, e147, e554; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e146 [rumour; the-decoder.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA planned to sell more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor to make up for the shortfall.\\n    Quote: Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\n- e154 [third_party_view; tikr.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis expects NVIDIA data-center compute revenue in the second half of FY2027 to be 20% above Wall Street\\u2019s current expectations.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia\\u2019s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street\\u2019s current expectations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e218 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Analysts ; from gnews:b40db2c507df72ec016e99e6 2026-07-08] Analysts expect NVIDIA total revenue of $392.7 billion in FY2027.\\n    Quote: And analysts are anticipating further gains of $392.7 billion in its current fiscal year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e28; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e234 [third_party_view; tradingview.com; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:a09988fd9c5c1a739a14f873 2026-07-08] Bloomberg-tracked estimates project NVIDIA FY2027 profit of $228 billion and total revenue of $393 billion, growth of 90% and 82%, respectively.\\n    Quote: The company is projected to generate $228 billion in profit on $393 billion in sales in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31, representing expected growth of 90% and 82%, respectively\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e51, e299, e705, e811; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e419 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped zero H20 chips to China in its spring quarter, compared with $4.6 billion of H20 shipments a year earlier.\\n    Quote: Yet zero H20 chips shipped to China this quarter, versus $4.6 billion a year ago.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e638, e1314; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e493 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. trade official 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official said at a congressional hearing on 2026-07-14 that shipments against licenses for H200 chips and equivalents had been very few and involved a very small quantity of chips.\\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \\\"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It\\u2019s a very small quantity of chips\\\" during a congressional hearing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e524; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e544 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] NVIDIA forecast $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027 and said it was on track to dominate the stand-alone CPU market.\\n    Quote: In its latest earnings report, it predicted $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year and said it was on track to dominate this market.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e65; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e552 [rumour; Tom's Hardware; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 2026-07-15] SemiAnalysis claimed NVIDIA's planned NVL576 rack-scale system had been postponed or would ship in relatively small quantities because of ongoing co-packaged-optics challenges.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the planned NVL576 rack scale solution that was supposed to combine eight Oberon racks interconnected using co-packaged optics between NVSwitches has also been postponed, or shipped in relatively small quantities because of 'ongoing CPO challenges,' SemiAnalysis claims.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e160; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e555 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-07-15; from gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 2026-07-15] Tom's Hardware judged that the reported Kyber delay and NVL72x2 cancellation could leave NVIDIA offering only 72-way scale-up systems until sometime in 2028, potentially giving AMD and Google more competitive scale-up systems in 2027\\u20132028.\\n    Quote: With Kyber NVL144 delayed and NVL72x2 cancelled, Nvidia will only be able to offer 72-way scale-up systems till sometimes in 2028, meaning that AMD and Google may end up with more competitive scale-up systems in 2027 \\u2013 2028.\\n- e801 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-21; from nvblog:vera-rubin 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said Vera Rubin NVL72 production is ramping up, with racks running at CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin NVL72 production is ramping up with racks running at partners CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e936, e937; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e738 (2026-07-18); both are in force: The author said NVIDIA\\u2019s Vera Rubin platform was slated to enter production in the second half of 2026.\\n- e811 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:0993b79d5b6b817b8d65b011 2026-07-21] Yahoo! Finance's Wall Street average estimate put NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue at $393 billion.\\n    Quote: According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e234; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e956 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1d24faffe6c3a2b74f2bfc8b 2026-07-23] NVIDIA said Vera Rubin NVL72 entered full production in June 2026 and was being deployed across Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and CoreWeave.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said Vera Rubin NVL72 entered full production in June and is already being deployed across cloud providers including Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and CoreWeave.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e801 (2026-07-21); both are in force: NVIDIA said Vera Rubin NVL72 production is ramping up, with racks running at CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius.\\n- e1040 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source OpenAI ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 GW of GPUs, a planned deployment that could affect NVIDIA's competitive position in AI accelerators.\\n    Quote: OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 gigawatts of GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e914, e1243; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1041 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Meta ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] Meta signed for a deployment of up to 6 GW of AMD Instinct GPUs, a competing accelerator commitment relevant to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue outlook.\\n    Quote: Meta signed for up to 6 gigawatts of Instinct GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e571; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1042 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Oracle ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] Oracle is standing up a 50,000-GPU AMD Helios cluster, a competing AI-compute deployment relevant to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue outlook.\\n    Quote: Oracle is standing up a 50,000-GPU Helios cluster.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1244; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1050 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA guided to $1 trillion in combined Blackwell and Rubin revenue from calendar 2025 through calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has guided to $1 trillion in combined Blackwell and Rubin revenue from 2025 through calendar 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e431, e1095; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1051 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO 2026-05-20; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said standalone Vera CPU revenue was excluded from the $1 trillion Blackwell and Rubin revenue forecast and expected it to be the second-largest source of upside to that forecast.\\n    Quote: we didn\\u2019t include any Vera CPU, standalone CPU in that number. And so I expect that to be the second largest\\u201d source of upside above the $1 trillion, behind only share gains in frontier AI models.\\n- e1057 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst), reporting Colette Kress\\u2019s statement; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera Rubin\\u2019s seven-chip architecture would start shipping in Q3 and ramp in Q4.\\n    Quote: The second is rising content per rack as Vera Rubin\\u2019s seven-chip architecture, which Kress said starts shipping in Q3 and ramps into Q4, replaces narrower Blackwell configurations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e96; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1475 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:30e3129696abb67c369bdbf5 2026-07-30] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 revenue guidance assumes no H20 shipments to China.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance assumes no H20 shipments to China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e673; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1510 [third_party_view; insidermonkey.com; original source insidermonkey.com 2026-07-31; from gnews:39deea97b690ac7b56dada0b 2026-07-31] NVIDIA has roughly $1 trillion in confirmed Blackwell and Vera Rubin chip orders through 2027.\\n    Quote: The company already has roughly $1 trillion in confirmed orders for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips through 2027,\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1521 [party_statement; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b72a0b11ab85854e09955d1 2026-07-31] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 guidance excludes Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e997; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1558 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source AMD and Meta ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] AMD and Meta agreed to deploy up to 6 GW of AMD Instinct GPUs, with the first 1 GW using MI450.\\n    Quote: AMD locked in a Meta partnership to deploy up to 6 gigawatts of Instinct GPUs, with the first 1-GW on MI450.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1464, e2402; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1608 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source SpaceX CEO Elon Musk ; from gnews:baaf41a9a32da4e5efcfd131 2026-08-05] SpaceX expects to have more than 2 GW of compute capacity by the end of 2026.\\n    Quote: According to the CEO, SpaceX expects to end the year with more than 2 gigawatts of compute\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2010; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1734 [fact; stockstory.org; original source SpaceX; Elon Musk confirmed the partnership 2026-08-05; from gnews:66195b46efbce26c584fbead 2026-08-10] SpaceX announced it will exclusively use NVIDIA hardware for future AI infrastructure, including its Starmind orbital compute program; Elon Musk confirmed the partnership and named NVIDIA's Vera Rubin architecture as its hardware choice.\\n    Quote: The previous big move we wrote about was 5 days ago when the stock gained 3.5% on the news that SpaceX announced it will exclusively use the chipmaker's hardware for its future artificial intelligence infrastructure, including its ambitious new Starmind orbital compute program. During SpaceX's first earnings call as a public company, CEO Elon Musk confirmed the strategic partnership, publicly decl\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1669, e2265; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1735 [third_party_view; stockstory.org; original source JPMorgan ; from gnews:66195b46efbce26c584fbead 2026-08-10] JPMorgan estimated that SpaceX alone would account for a \\u201cvery significant percentage\\u201d of NVIDIA's total GPU shipments in 2027.\\n    Quote: Reinforcing the bullish outlook, JPMorgan noted that SpaceX alone is expected to absorb a \\\"very significant percentage\\\" of Nvidia's total GPU shipments in 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1625; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1804 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] NVIDIA guided to FY2027 Q2 total revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Quote: According to the company's own guidance, the upcoming second-quarter report on Aug. 26 could show $91 billion in total revenue (plus or minus 2%),\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1528, e2321; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1806 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts ; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] Analysts expect NVIDIA to guide to approximately $103.1 billion in FY2027 Q3 total revenue.\\n    Quote: Analysts believe the company will forecast around $103.1 billion in total revenue for the fiscal 2027 third quarter,\\n- e1840 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois; original source Alex Sirois 2026-08-11; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] NVIDIA's Q2 FY2027 revenue guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue; the author estimates this removes roughly $4.6 billion in H20 sales per quarter.\\n    Quote: Nvidia\\u2019s Q2 guide excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China, erasing what used to be roughly $4.6 billion per quarter in H20 sales.\\n- e1854 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, summarizing Musk's first earnings call and its own estimate; original source Elon Musk (capacity and supplier plan); 247Wallst.com (potential orders estimate) ; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] The article says Elon Musk plans to scale SpaceX data center capacity to 10 GW by 2027 using NVIDIA hardware exclusively, which the article estimates could generate $300 billion in orders.\\n    Quote: Musk's plan to scale SpaceX data centers to 10 gigawatts by 2027, exclusively with NVDA hardware, could generate $300 billion in new orders.\\n- e1855 [party_statement; 247Wallst.com, reporting Elon Musk's remarks; original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] Elon Musk said SpaceX aims to increase data center capacity from roughly 1.4 GW to 10 GW by year-end 2027.\\n    Quote: On SpaceX\\u2019s first earnings call, Elon Musk told investors he wants to scale data center capacity from roughly 1.4 gigawatts today to 10 gigawatts by year-end 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1609, e2332; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, reporting SemiAnalysis's estimate; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:484913e67532d3a806abf6a1 2026-08-11] SemiAnalysis estimates SpaceX's data center buildout could cost $300 billion\\u2013$500 billion in 2027 capital spending, based on $50 billion per gigawatt annually, and considers the 10 GW target achievable.\\n    Quote: Research firm SemiAnalysis estimates the buildout at $50 billion per gigawatt annually, or $300 billion to $500 billion in 2027 capital spending alone, and considers the target achievable .\\n- e1906 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source CoreWeave 2026-08-10; from gnews:011203b517e045ffe773b1fb 2026-08-12] CoreWeave's contracted power was 3.7 GW in Q2 and 4.2 GW on 2026-08-10, compared with 1.5 GW online; customer commitments covered nearly three times its deliverable capacity.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave's contracted power grew to 3.7 GW in Q2 and stood at 4.2 GW as of Monday, against just 1.5 GW online, so customer commitments already cover nearly triple the capacity the company can deliver.\\n- e1919 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance, reporting Goldman Sachs analyst James Schneider\\u2019s note; original source James Schneider, Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:4ec81c2c385eafc1ab9156b8 2026-08-13] Goldman Sachs analyst James Schneider expected NVIDIA to deliver a solid quarter with meaningful upside to guidance, citing tight GPU supply and demand.\\n    Quote: \\\"We expect a solid quarter [for Nvidia] with meaningful upside to guidance supported by tight GPU supply/demand trends, but think the bar for the stock is elevated given its +12% move in two weeks,\\\" Goldman Sachs analyst James Schneider cautioned in a new note.\\n- e1936 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1258c8b56ae0bb231116e1e5 2026-08-13] The author reports NVIDIA made no H20 shipments to China in Q1 FY2027.\\n    Quote: Q1 saw no H20 shipments to China\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e638; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1937 [party_statement; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1258c8b56ae0bb231116e1e5 2026-08-13] The author reports NVIDIA guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to $91.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, assuming zero China Data Center compute revenue.\\n    Quote: Q2 guidance of $91.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, explicitly assumes zero China Data Center compute.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e479; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1955 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source The Information; the \\u201cgigawatts\\u2019 worth\\u201d remark is attributed to an Azure Maia manager ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] The Information reported that Microsoft may unveil Maia 300 as soon as September 2026, is negotiating with TSMC for capacity for more than 300,000 chips for delivery in 2027, and has an eventual target above 1 million units; an Azure Maia manager described a potential \\u201cgigawatts\\u2019 worth\\u201d of Maia.\\n    Quote: The Information reported that Microsoft will unveil Maia 300 as soon as September and is negotiating with TSMC for capacity for more than 300,000 chips for delivery in 2027, with an eventual target of over 1 million units and, per an Azure Maia manager, \\u201cgigawatts\\u2019 worth\\u201d of Maia.\\n- e1956 [third_party_view; uncoveralpha.com; original source Unspecified reports; Microsoft for the reported customer outreach ; from gnews:dcc5556122de8bb36a458f29 2026-08-13] Reports cited by the author put Maia\\u2019s targeted cost advantage at 30\\u201340% versus NVIDIA chips; Microsoft is courting Anthropic as an anchor customer.\\n    Quote: Reports mention a targeted cost advantage of 30-40% over Nvidia chips, and Microsoft is actively courting Anthropic as an anchor customer for it.\\n- e2016 [party_statement; SiliconANGLE / Breaking Analysis, reporting CoreWeave management; original source CoreWeave management ; from gnews:a66e734cdc6c8f0170fe8471 2026-08-15] CoreWeave management said its near-term capacity was effectively sold out, with multiple buyers competing for each GPU brought online.\\n    Quote: Management says near-term capacity is effectively sold out, with multiple buyers competing for each GPU brought online.\\n- e2052 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:5e3fbb0b291e2c881ef57c38 2026-08-15] AMD attributed a 107% year-over-year increase in second-quarter data-center revenue to robust demand for its server CPUs and GPUs.\\n    Quote: AMD attributed its impressive performance to robust demand for its server CPUs and GPUs, resulting in a 107% year-over-year increase in data center revenue in Q2.\\n- e2089 [third_party_view; Howard Smith, Motley Fool; original source Howard Smith, Motley Fool 2026-08-16; from gnews:1b27a1a0652e204c0c5402a4 2026-08-16] Howard Smith estimated NVIDIA FY2027 revenue would be just under $400 billion if quarterly revenue continued rising 12% sequentially.\\n    Quote: Even if sales continue to increase 12% sequentially quarter to quarter, Nvidia will still report revenue just shy of $400 billion this fiscal year.\\n- e2095 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-17; from nvidianews:nvidia-guarantees-sb-energy-s-ports-pike-technology-campus-in-ohio-to-exclusively-host-nvidia-ai-compute 2026-08-17] NVIDIA said OpenAI would be the customer for 8 IT-GW of capacity at PORTS-Pike.\\n    Quote: OpenAI will be the customer for 8-IT GW.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2117, e2273; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2096 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-17; from nvidianews:nvidia-guarantees-sb-energy-s-ports-pike-technology-campus-in-ohio-to-exclusively-host-nvidia-ai-compute 2026-08-17] NVIDIA said SB Energy would build, own and operate the PORTS-Pike data center under a 20-year lease to OpenAI.\\n    Quote: SB Energy will build, own and operate the data center under a 20-year lease to OpenAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e1372 (); both are in force: Unnamed sources said a prospective NVIDIA-OpenAI deal would help OpenAI lease a $500 billion, 10-GW data center hub being developed by SB Energy in Ohio.\\n- e2097 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-17; from nvidianews:nvidia-guarantees-sb-energy-s-ports-pike-technology-campus-in-ohio-to-exclusively-host-nvidia-ai-compute 2026-08-17] NVIDIA said planned PORTS-Pike capacity was expected to come online in phases beginning in 2028.\\n    Quote: The planned capacity is expected to come online in phases beginning in 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2098 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source SB Energy and SoftBank Group 2026-08-17; from nvidianews:nvidia-guarantees-sb-energy-s-ports-pike-technology-campus-in-ohio-to-exclusively-host-nvidia-ai-compute 2026-08-17] SB Energy and SoftBank said they would build at least 10 GW of new power generation to support 8 IT-GW of AI factory capacity and invest at least $4.2 billion in regional grid infrastructure for the PORTS-Pike project.\\n    Quote: In support of the surrounding Ohio community, SB Energy and SoftBank will build at least 10 gigawatts of new energy generation, which results in 8 IT-GW of AI factory capacity, and invest at least $4.2 billion in new regional grid infrastructure through an innovative partnership with AEP Ohio designed to protect ratepayers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2128, e2158, e2209, e2219, e2275; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2115 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-17; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000069:nvda-20260817.htm 2026-08-17] OpenAI plans to use Portsmouth Site capacity supporting approximately 4.25 GW of IT load to deploy NVIDIA\\u2019s full-stack DSX AI factory platform, subject to limited exceptions.\\n    Quote: OpenAI, as the tenant, will utilize capacity at the Portsmouth Site supporting approximately 4.25 gigawatts of IT load to deploy NVIDIA\\u2019s full-stack DSX AI factory platform, subject to limited exceptions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2102, e2217; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2123 [fact; AWS; original source Amazon Web Services 2026-08-17; from gnews:c526ed1239763219a9dbedd0 2026-08-17] Amazon made NVIDIA Nemotron 3.5 Lightning deployable through SageMaker JumpStart, integrating the model into its cloud service.\\n    Quote: With this launch, you can deploy Nemotron 3.5 Lightning from Amazon SageMaker JumpStart without configuring the serving infrastructure yourself.\\n- e2125 [fact; Rich Duprey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:127ca150cda794237261a87c 2026-08-17] NVIDIA invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy for a data center intended for OpenAI to fill with NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Nvidia invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy, funding the data center OpenAI will fill with Nvidia GPUs and booking revenue twice on one deal.\\n- e2126 [party_statement; Rich Duprey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:127ca150cda794237261a87c 2026-08-17] NVIDIA plans to invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy and secure up to 8 GW of AI computing capacity at the PORTS-Pike Technology Campus.\\n    Quote: Nvidia will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy and lock in up to 8 gigawatts of AI computing capacity at the PORTS-Pike Technology Campus in Pike County, Ohio.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2116; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2127 [fact; Rich Duprey; original source SB Energy ; from gnews:127ca150cda794237261a87c 2026-08-17] SB Energy plans an initial 4.25 GW data-center phase, which it will build, own, and operate under a 20-year lease to OpenAI.\\n    Quote: The initial phase covers 4.25 GW, with SB Energy building, owning, and operating the facility under a 20-year lease to OpenAI.\\n- e2128 [fact; Rich Duprey; original source SB Energy and SoftBank ; from gnews:127ca150cda794237261a87c 2026-08-17] SB Energy and SoftBank are committing $4.2 billion to regional grid infrastructure as part of a plan for at least 10 GW of new power generation.\\n    Quote: SB Energy and SoftBank are also committing $4.2 billion to regional grid infrastructure, part of a plan for at least 10 GW of new power generation.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2098; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2139 [party_statement; TechCrunch; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-17; from gnews:9dc5caf38ae16c329a830342 2026-08-17] NVIDIA will be the sole compute-infrastructure supplier for OpenAI\\u2019s Ports-Pike data center near Cincinnati, Ohio.\\n    Quote: The investment ensures that Nvidia will be the sole supplier of compute infrastructure at OpenAI\\u2019s Ports-Pike data center near Cincinnati, Ohio.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2105, e2147; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2140 [fact; TechCrunch; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9dc5caf38ae16c329a830342 2026-08-17] NVIDIA will provide up to $105 billion in credit to help build OpenAI\\u2019s Ports-Pike data center, which could scale from an initial 4.25 GW to 8 GW.\\n    Quote: Nvidia will also provide up to $105 billion in credit to help build the facility, which could scale from an initial 4.25 gigawatts to 8 gigawatts in size, according to documents the company filed with the SEC.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2116, e2145, e2213; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2141 [fact; TechCrunch; original source SB Energy ; from gnews:9dc5caf38ae16c329a830342 2026-08-17] SB Energy plans to build a 9.2 GW natural gas power plant at the data-center site on U.S. Department of Energy-owned land.\\n    Quote: The data center and power developer will build a 9.2 gigawatt natural gas power plant on the site, which is land owned by the U.S. Department of Energy.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2142 [third_party_view; TechCrunch; original source TechCrunch 2026-08-17; from gnews:9dc5caf38ae16c329a830342 2026-08-17] The planned natural gas power plant is expected to cost $33 billion.\\n    Quote: The power plant is expected to cost $33 billion .\\n- e2146 [fact; nypost.com; original source OpenAI 2026-08-17; from gnews:759117b9a2981f9f0c2a2f84 2026-08-17] OpenAI signed a 20-year deal for an Ohio data center expected ultimately to provide about 8 GW of computing capacity.\\n    Quote: OpenAI has inked a 20-year deal for the site, which will ultimately provide about eight gigawatts of computing capacity that will help support products like ChatGPT, the companies said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2185, e2215; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e1720 (); both are in force: SiliconANGLE relayed an unconfirmed Bloomberg report that NVIDIA was discussing $250 billion of financing for the lease and debt of OpenAI\\u2019s 10-gigawatt Ohio data center and separately discussing $350 billion to finance chip purchases; no agreement was confirmed.\\n- e2147 [fact; nypost.com; original source NVIDIA and OpenAI 2026-08-17; from gnews:759117b9a2981f9f0c2a2f84 2026-08-17] NVIDIA will be the exclusive computer-chip provider for OpenAI\\u2019s Pike County, Ohio data center.\\n    Quote: Nvidia will serve as the exclusive computer chip provider for the facility, to be located in Ohio\\u2019s Pike County.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2148 [party_statement; nypost.com; original source NVIDIA and OpenAI 2026-08-17; from gnews:759117b9a2981f9f0c2a2f84 2026-08-17] The first 800 MW of computing capacity at the Ohio data center is expected to be available for use by 2028.\\n    Quote: The first 800 megawatts of \\u201ccompute\\u201d is expected to be available for use by 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1290, e2167; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2151 [party_statement; nypost.com; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO 2026-08-17; from gnews:759117b9a2981f9f0c2a2f84 2026-08-17] Jensen Huang said OpenAI\\u2019s commitments to buy NVIDIA hardware and compute capacity will reach $600 billion by 2030.\\n    Quote: In all, OpenAI\\u2019s commitments to buy Nvidia hardware and compute capacity will reach $600 billion by 2030, according to Huang.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2104, e2186; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2183 [party_statement; TechCrunch; original source Groq ; from gnews:f7751a62e0c6a2e9c6c7752c 2026-08-17] Groq said the new funding will support customers seeking medium- and larger-sized clusters of NVIDIA accelerated computing for training and inference.\\n    Quote: Groq says the fresh funds will support \\u201cthose seeking usage of medium and larger sized clusters of Nvidia accelerated computing for training and inference.\\u201d\\n- e2185 [fact; theedgemalaysia.com; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:ca2bdbbe1e7d537be3bbedf6 2026-08-17] OpenAI agreed to secure up to roughly 8 GW of computing capacity from the Pike County complex, with the first 800 MW expected online by 2028.\\n    Quote: The ChatGPT maker has entered into an agreement to secure up to roughly eight gigawatts of computing capacity from the Pike County complex, with the first 800 megawatts expected to come online by 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2146; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2186 [party_statement; theedgemalaysia.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-17; from gnews:ca2bdbbe1e7d537be3bbedf6 2026-08-17] NVIDIA said the purchases covered by its OpenAI commitment could represent about US$600 billion of revenue through 2030.\\n    Quote: In all, the purchases could represent about US$600 billion of revenue for Nvidia through 2030.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2151, e2348; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2192 [party_statement; theedgemalaysia.com; original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:ca2bdbbe1e7d537be3bbedf6 2026-08-17] SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said he had decided to use NVIDIA products exclusively in a large data-centre expansion.\\n    Quote: Musk, who serves as SpaceX\\u2019s CEO, said earlier this month that he\\u2019s decided to exclusively use Nvidia products in a massive data centre expansion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1627; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2193 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-17; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] NVIDIA said on August 17, 2026, that it will provide up to $105 billion in support for an OpenAI data center in Ohio.\\n    Quote: Following last week's pact with Wall Street firms to pursue $500 billion worth of financing for Nvidia's graphics processing units, Nvidia said Monday that it's providing up to $105 billion for a giant OpenAI data center in Ohio, offering a backstop of sorts should the ChatGPT creator see its fortunes turn.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2145; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2199 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source Cantor analysts 2026-08-17; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] Cantor analysts said the latest NVIDIA agreement signals that the current AI investment cycle will be prolonged and durable.\\n    Quote: In a note to clients Monday, analysts at Cantor brushed off concerns that Nvidia is effectively buying revenue through its financial maneuvering. They reiterated their buy rating and said the latest agreement is a \\\"clear signal that the current AI investment cycle will be elongated and durable.\\\"\\n- e2200 [fact; CNBC; original source Google ; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] Google began recognizing revenue from TPU system sales in the second quarter, and its cloud unit grew 82%.\\n    Quote: In the second quarter, Google began recognizing revenue from tensor processing unit system sales, contributing to the cloud unit's 82% growth .\\n- e2201 [fact; CNBC; original source AMD ; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] AMD reported more than 100% growth in its data center business.\\n    Quote: AMD, meanwhile, reported more than 100% growth in its data center business\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e1615 (2026-08-04); both are in force: AMD guided to Data Center segment growth well over 100%; the article does not specify the period in this sentence.\\n- e2202 [party_statement; CNBC; original source AMD ; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] AMD expects to ship its first rack-scale system, Helios, in 2026.\\n    Quote: the company expects its first rack-scale system, called Helios, to ship later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e970; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2204 [fact; CNBC; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] Anthropic told investors that its annualized revenue run rate reached $65 billion in July 2026, seven times its level a year earlier.\\n    Quote: Anthropic told investors over the weekend that its annualized revenue run rate hit $65 billion in July, up sevenfold from a year earlier.\\n- e2205 [fact; CNBC; original source CNBC ; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] OpenAI's revenue run rate recently reached $40 billion.\\n    Quote: OpenAI's run rate recently reached $40 billion.\\n- e2206 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source Matthew Vegari, Clearwater Analytics ; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] Matthew Vegari said the view that the AI trade is a fragile, circular structure is somewhat misguided given current market dynamics.\\n    Quote: Matthew Vegari, head of research at Clearwater Analytics, said in an email that, based on the market dynamics, the \\\"narrative around the AI trade's circuitous, 'house of cards' structure strikes us as somewhat misguided.\\\"\\n- e2207 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source Matthew Vegari, Clearwater Analytics ; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] Matthew Vegari said the AI market may eventually face overcapacity, but is not at that point now.\\n    Quote: \\\"We might one day be at overcapacity,\\\" he wrote. \\\"But that day isn't today.\\\"\\n- e2212 [fact; blog.mozilla.org; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-18; from gnews:8cad4fb3d33f40c2d7f17ecb 2026-08-18] NVIDIA officially added Firefox on Windows to the supported browsers for GeForce NOW on 2026-08-18.\\n    Quote: Today, NVIDIA made it official, opening up GeForce RTX-powered play to Firefox on Windows.\\n- e2213 [fact; Datacentre Magazine, reporting; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b5ba89bf5c874701f209a987 2026-08-18] NVIDIA plans to supply the exclusive AI compute infrastructure and provide up to US$105 billion in credit support for the initial 4.25 IT-GW buildout at the PORTS-Pike campus.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA will supply the exclusive AI compute infrastructure while providing up to US$105bn in credit support for the land, power and shell buildout associated with the initial 4.25 IT-GW.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2140, e2270; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2215 [fact; Datacentre Magazine, reporting; original source SB Energy and OpenAI ; from gnews:b5ba89bf5c874701f209a987 2026-08-18] SB Energy plans to build, own and operate the PORTS-Pike campus under a 20-year lease with OpenAI, which has secured approximately 8 IT-GW of capacity.\\n    Quote: SB Energy will build, own and operate the campus under a 20-year lease with OpenAI, which has secured approximately 8GW-IT of capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2146, e2272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2217 [fact; Datacentre Magazine, reporting; original source SB Energy ; from gnews:b5ba89bf5c874701f209a987 2026-08-18] The initial PORTS-Pike deployment is planned to support 4.25 IT-GW of NVIDIA compute using NVIDIA\\u2019s DSX AI factory platform.\\n    Quote: Its initial deployment is planned to support 4.25 IT-GW of NVIDIA compute using the company\\u2019s DSX AI factory platform, bringing together GPUs, CPUs, networking, facilities and software.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2115, e2274; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2218 [fact; Datacentre Magazine, reporting; original source NVIDIA and OpenAI ; from gnews:b5ba89bf5c874701f209a987 2026-08-18] NVIDIA and OpenAI plan to collaborate on design, testing and commissioning at the PORTS-Pike data center.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA and OpenAI will collaborate on design, testing and commissioning at the data centre.\\n- e2219 [fact; Datacentre Magazine, reporting; original source SB Energy, SoftBank and OpenAI ; from gnews:b5ba89bf5c874701f209a987 2026-08-18] SB Energy and SoftBank plan to build at least 10 GW of new energy generation to enable 8 IT-GW of AI factory capacity, which OpenAI has secured.\\n    Quote: SB Energy and SoftBank will build at least 10GW of new energy generation, enabling 8 IT-GW of AI factory capacity, all of which OpenAI has already secured.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2098, e2276; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2220 [party_statement; Datacentre Magazine, reporting; original source SB Energy and SoftBank ; from gnews:b5ba89bf5c874701f209a987 2026-08-18] The first 800 MW of power for the campus is expected to become available in 2028, largely using existing AEP infrastructure.\\n    Quote: The first 800MW is expected to become available in 2028, largely using existing AEP infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1290, e2243, e2277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2223 [fact; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:017a192de3e167df97420b36 2026-08-18] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% quarter over quarter.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported revenues of $81.6 billion in the fiscal first quarter of 2027, up 85% year over year and 20% quarter over quarter, according to the company's press release.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e399; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2225 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:017a192de3e167df97420b36 2026-08-18] NVIDIA expects fiscal Q2 2027 total revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Quote: Revenues are expected to reach $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, driven by strong AI demand.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1528; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2228 [third_party_view; fool.com analyst; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:d254893c47e85a5d182918d7 2026-08-18] The author says Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA to grow faster than AMD this year; no growth rates are provided.\\n    Quote: The market is essentially saying that it prefers AMD's outlook to Nvidia's, which doesn't make sense considering that Wall Street analysts expect faster growth this year from Nvidia versus AMD.\\n- e2257 [third_party_view; TechRepublic; original source TechRepublic 2026-08-18; from gnews:5d2d15afbe1559d0a5f8d604 2026-08-18] TechRepublic said SB Energy is expected to buy more NVIDIA chips in the near future to outfit the Ohio facility.\\n    Quote: It would also be another deal in which Nvidia invests in a company that is expected to buy more of its chips in the near future to outfit the facility.\\n- e2264 [third_party_view; TechRepublic; original source TechRepublic 2026-08-18; from gnews:5d2d15afbe1559d0a5f8d604 2026-08-18] TechRepublic said custom chips built by hyperscalers and AI labs are often deployed alongside NVIDIA GPUs rather than replacing them.\\n    Quote: Efforts by hyperscalers and AI labs to build custom chips, often AI accelerators, still frequently see those chips deployed alongside Nvidia GPUs in server racks rather than replacing them entirely.\\n- e2265 [fact; TechRepublic; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:5d2d15afbe1559d0a5f8d604 2026-08-18] SpaceX decided to build its infrastructure around NVIDIA hardware and go \\u201call-in\\u201d on NVIDIA chips.\\n    Quote: We saw this most recently with SpaceX\\u2019s decision to go \\u201call-in\\u201d on Nvidia hardware and build its infrastructure around the company\\u2019s chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1734, e2331; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2266 [third_party_view; TechRepublic; original source TechRepublic 2026-08-18; from gnews:5d2d15afbe1559d0a5f8d604 2026-08-18] TechRepublic reported that NVIDIA has more than 85% market share for AI chips used in data centers.\\n    Quote: chipmaker remains by far the most popular supplier of GPUs for data centers, with a market share of more than 85 percent for AI chips\\n- e2267 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-08-18; from gnews:1aa13bc66527f127bb16b011 2026-08-18] Current estimates cited by the author put NVIDIA fiscal second-quarter sales at nearly $91.9 billion, up 96% year over year.\\n    Quote: Based on current estimates, Nvidia is expected to deliver nearly $91.9 billion in fiscal second-quarter sales (up 96% from the year-ago quarter)\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1921; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2272 [fact; aimagazine.com; original source SB Energy ; from gnews:21e5146ed930213ec6022bde 2026-08-18] SB Energy plans to construct, own and operate the PORTS-Pike facility under a 20-year lease agreement.\\n    Quote: Under the terms of a 20-year lease agreement, SB Energy will construct, own and operate the facility.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2215; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2273 [fact; aimagazine.com; original source OpenAI and NVIDIA ; from gnews:21e5146ed930213ec6022bde 2026-08-18] OpenAI has committed to approximately 8 GW-IT of campus capacity, and NVIDIA is to be the exclusive provider of AI compute infrastructure.\\n    Quote: OpenAI has committed to approximately 8 GW-IT of the total capacity, with NVIDIA serving as the exclusive provider of AI compute infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2095; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2274 [fact; aimagazine.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:21e5146ed930213ec6022bde 2026-08-18] The initial PORTS-Pike rollout is scheduled to accommodate 4.25 IT-GW of NVIDIA compute through its DSX AI factory platform.\\n    Quote: The initial rollout is scheduled to accommodate 4.25 IT-GW of NVIDIA compute through the company's DSX AI factory platform, which integrates GPUs, CPUs, networking infrastructure, facilities and software systems.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2217; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2275 [fact; aimagazine.com; original source AEP Ohio ; from gnews:21e5146ed930213ec6022bde 2026-08-18] AEP Ohio plans to deploy at least US$4.2 billion of new regional grid infrastructure through its partnership with SB Energy and SoftBank.\\n    Quote: AEP Ohio is planning to deploy at least US$4.2bn worth of new regional grid infrastructure through its partnership with SB Energy and SoftBank .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2098; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2276 [fact; aimagazine.com; original source SB Energy and SoftBank ; from gnews:21e5146ed930213ec6022bde 2026-08-18] SB Energy and SoftBank plan to construct at least 10 GW of new energy-generation capacity to support the 8 IT-GW AI factory infrastructure secured by OpenAI.\\n    Quote: SB Energy and SoftBank plan to construct at least 10GW of new energy generation capacity, supporting the 8 IT-GW of AI factory infrastructure that OpenAI has secured.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2219; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2277 [fact; aimagazine.com; original source SB Energy and SoftBank ; from gnews:21e5146ed930213ec6022bde 2026-08-18] Initial power delivery of 800 MW for the campus is anticipated in 2028, primarily using existing AEP infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Initial power delivery of 800MW is anticipated in 2028, primarily utilising existing AEP infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2220; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2294 [third_party_view; Morningstar (analyst); original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar sees upside in NVIDIA shares conditional on the company staying on track to meet its near- and medium-term revenue targets.\\n    Quote: We still see upside in Nvidia stock as long as it remains on pace to hit its near- and medium-term revenue targets.\\n- e2296 [party_statement; Morningstar (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] NVIDIA disclosed at its March 2026 GTC conference that it had high confidence in cumulative Blackwell and Rubin product revenue of $1 trillion from the start of 2025 through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia disclosed at its March 2026 GTC conference that it has high confidence in $1 trillion of cumulative revenue for its Blackwell and Rubin products from the start of calendar 2025 to the end of calendar 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1095; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2298 [third_party_view; Morningstar (analyst); original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar models NVIDIA total revenue growth of 80% in FY2027, based on its expectation of accelerating industry AI capital spending in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: Given the acceleration in AI capex we expect within the industry in calendar 2026, we model 80% total revenue growth for Nvidia in fiscal 2027.\\n- e2299 [third_party_view; Morningstar (analyst); original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar says NVIDIA\\u2019s AI spending is concentrated among a handful of customers that have incentives to optimize or reduce their investments over time.\\n    Quote: but such spending comes from a handful of customers, and they all have an incentive to eventually optimize, if not reduce, their investments over time.\\n- e2300 [third_party_view; Morningstar (analyst); original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar expects technology leaders to use in-house chips for at least some AI workloads, citing Google TPUs and Amazon Trainium and Inferentia as chips designed for AI workloads.\\n    Quote: Within these AI buildouts, we also think tech leaders will turn to in-house chips for at least a portion of their workloads. Google\\u2019s TPUs and Amazon\\u2019s Trainium and Inferentia chips were designed with AI workloads in mind.\\n- e2301 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Stifel analyst Ruben Roy 2026-08-19; from gnews:f2d0b1e9af505f284de48a0b 2026-08-19] Stifel analyst Ruben Roy said cloud-service-provider capital spending had been raised meaningfully; Foxconn\\u2019s cloud and networking segment exceeded 50% of revenue for the first time and its full-year AI rack shipments were guided to more than double; and Super Micro booked over $60 billion of new orders in one quarter.\\n    Quote: Earnings season has consistently reinforced the demand case that we made in our broader coverage preview as CSP capex was raised meaningfully, Foxconn's cloud and networking segment crossed 50% of revenue for the first time with full-year AI rack shipments guided to more than double, and Super Micro booked over $60 billion of new orders in a single quarter.\\n- e2306 [third_party_view; Eagle Capital Management, as quoted by Insider Monkey; original source Eagle Capital Management ; from gnews:1e0b901518eaf682a7393126 2026-08-19] Eagle Capital Management said NVIDIA\\u2019s largest customers\\u2019 internal silicon programs, including Google TPUs and Amazon Trainium, are likely to reduce NVIDIA\\u2019s market share over time.\\n    Quote: its largest customers have internal silicon programs that are likely to reduce NVIDIA's share over time. Google TPUs and Amazon's Trainium chips are the most competitive, but Microsoft, Meta, OpenAl, and Anthropic all have programs in various stages.\\n- e2307 [third_party_view; Eagle Capital Management, as quoted by Insider Monkey; original source Eagle Capital Management ; from gnews:1e0b901518eaf682a7393126 2026-08-19] Eagle Capital Management said the current scale of the market allows cloud providers to amortize custom-silicon R&D across a larger base.\\n    Quote: At today's scale, R&D can be amortized across the larger base.\\n- e2309 [third_party_view; Eagle Capital Management, as quoted by Insider Monkey; original source Eagle Capital Management ; from gnews:1e0b901518eaf682a7393126 2026-08-19] Eagle Capital Management said competing custom-silicon programs could offer customers better price/performance than NVIDIA even if they are not as capable.\\n    Quote: Custom programs don't have to be as good as NVIDIA to offer better price/performance to the end customer.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e259; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2310 [third_party_view; Eagle Capital Management, as quoted by Insider Monkey; original source Eagle Capital Management ; from gnews:1e0b901518eaf682a7393126 2026-08-19] Eagle Capital Management said NVIDIA\\u2019s chip pricing makes neocloud operators high-cost and less likely to succeed against vertically integrated hyperscalers over the long term.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is investing in the neocloud industry to support demand for its chips and weaken hyperscalers' position. Ironically, by selling its chips at high prices, it makes the neoclouds high-cost operators, and therefore less likely to succeed against vertically integrated hyperscalers over the long term.\\n- e2312 [fact; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e0b901518eaf682a7393126 2026-08-19] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $82 billion in fiscal Q1 2027, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: In the first quarter of fiscal year 2027, NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA) reported revenues of $82 billion, achieving an impressive 85% year-over-year growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e770; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2321 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] NVIDIA guided to fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue of $91.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Quote: The company's own guidance, issued back in May, calls for revenue of $91.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1804; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2322 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] Yahoo! Finance's average of 42 analyst estimates put NVIDIA fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue near $91.9 billion and adjusted EPS near $2.08.\\n    Quote: Wall Street's average estimate sits near $91.9 billion, based on the 42 analyst estimates compiled by Yahoo! Finance, with adjusted earnings pegged near $2.08 per share.\\n- e2323 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Daniel Sparks 2026-08-19; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] Daniel Sparks estimated that repeating NVIDIA's prior-quarter outperformance would put fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue near $95 billion.\\n    Quote: A repeat of last quarter's outperformance would put revenue near $95 billion, well above both numbers in the headline.\\n- e2324 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue of $81.6 billion, about $2 billion above the top of its $78.0 billion, plus-or-minus-2%, guidance range.\\n    Quote: In May, Nvidia reported $81.6 billion of fiscal first-quarter revenue -- about $2 billion above the top of the $78.0 billion, plus-or-minus-2%, range it had guided to three months earlier.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e349; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2325 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue growth of 85% year over year and 20% sequentially; Data Center revenue grew 92% to $75.2 billion.\\n    Quote: Growth ran 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, with data center revenue climbing 92% to $75.2 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e313; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2326 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] NVIDIA's adjusted EPS exceeded analyst estimates by 3% to 6% in each of the past four quarters; Yahoo! Finance recorded five upward and no downward analyst revisions for the current quarter over the past month.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's adjusted earnings per share have topped analysts' estimates by 3% to 6% in each of the past four quarters, and analysts have been nudging this quarter's numbers higher into the report -- five upward revisions over the past month, and none down, per Yahoo! Finance.\\n- e2327 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter outlook assumed no Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: The company said it is not assuming any data center compute revenue from China in its outlook\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e317; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2329 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] NVIDIA reported revenue growth of 85% in the first quarter.\\n    Quote: The company reported 85% revenue growth in the first quarter\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1395; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2330 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] The author expects NVIDIA revenue growth to reach 97% in the second quarter.\\n    Quote: revenue growth is expected to accelerate to 97% in the second quarter\\n- e2331 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source SpaceX ; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] SpaceX said it would build its AI infrastructure exclusively on NVIDIA chips.\\n    Quote: SpaceX ( SPCX -9.14% ) , flush with cash after its recent IPO, said it would build its AI infrastructure exclusively on Nvidia chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2265; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2332 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source SpaceX ; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] SpaceX is targeting 10 GW of compute capacity by the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: SpaceX is targeting 10GW of compute by the end of 2027\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1855; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2333 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] The author estimates that SpaceX will spend $150 billion\\u2013$250 billion on chips for its planned 10 GW of compute by the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: meaning it will spend an estimated $150 billion-$250 billion chips.\\n- e2334 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang set a $1 trillion total-revenue target for 2026\\u20132027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has given $1 trillion in total revenue as a target for 2026-2027\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2335 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] The author estimates NVIDIA revenue at roughly $400 billion this year and $600 billion in FY2028.\\n    Quote: implying the company could hit roughly $400 billion in revenue this year and $600 billion in fiscal 2028, which ends in Jan. 2028.\\n- e2336 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] The author estimates that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, and Meta will spend roughly $700 billion on AI chips this year, supporting NVIDIA demand.\\n    Quote: Nvidia will also benefit from soaring capex spend among the four big hyperscalers, Amazon , Alphabet , Microsoft , and Meta Platforms , who are expected to spend roughly $700 billion on AI chips this year\\n- e2347 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA ; from gnews:1dcfc890ed3dff2c543fd41d 2026-08-19] BofA estimated that the initial 4.25 GW Ohio project could require about 1.5 million GPUs and generate $150\\u2013200 billion of NVIDIA revenue per hardware generation; the item gives no period for a generation.\\n    Quote: BofA estimates the initial 4.25GW project alone could require roughly 1.5 million GPUs and generate around $150\\u2013200B of Nvidia revenue per hardware generation.\\n- e2348 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA ; from gnews:1dcfc890ed3dff2c543fd41d 2026-08-19] BofA estimated that NVIDIA could generate about $600 billion of revenue through 2030 from OpenAI\\u2019s broader commitments.\\n    Quote: Including OpenAI\\u2019s broader commitments, BofA sees roughly $600B of potential Nvidia revenue through 2030.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2186; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2350 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source BofA ; from gnews:1dcfc890ed3dff2c543fd41d 2026-08-19] BofA expected NVIDIA revenue of about $94\\u201395 billion for the quarter associated with its August 26 earnings report, versus NVIDIA\\u2019s $91 billion guide, and anticipated potential guidance of $107\\u2013108 billion for the following quarter.\\n    Quote: BofA expects around $94\\u201395B of revenue , above Nvidia\\u2019s $91B guide, and sees potential next-quarter guidance of $107\\u2013108B , also above current sell-side expectations.\\n- e2355 [third_party_view; The Verge reporter; original source The Verge ; from gnews:9d8a9b8bf88a2be11484a45e 2026-08-19] An unnamed cloud service provider nearly doubled rental prices for NVIDIA Blackwell B200 chips for one customer at contract renewal.\\n    Quote: One cloud service provider nearly doubled its prices on Nvidia Blackwell B200 chips for one rental customer during its contract renewal.\\n- e2370 [third_party_view; David Engle, MarketWise; original source David Engle, MarketWise 2026-08-20; from gnews:7bc637ed19fc86da45832cc8 2026-08-20] The author estimates that AI and data centers account for more than 90% of Nvidia\\u2019s revenue.\\n    Quote: AI and data centers now represent more than 90% of Nvidia\\u2019s revenue.\\n- e2371 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SpaceX ; from gnews:1f9046fdf6a6b54d6290613f 2026-08-20] SpaceX reported AI capital expenditures of $23.55 billion in the first six months of 2026, up from $3.32 billion in the same period of 2025.\\n    Quote: Through the first six months of 2026, AI capex has vaulted from $3.32 billion last year to $23.55 billion this year.\\n- e2372 [rumour; Investing.com; original source The Information, reporting an unnamed source ; from gnews:c258a90ec294e1838a69819d 2026-08-20] NVIDIA reportedly plans to begin small-batch shipments of a newly tailored AI chip to Chinese customers by the end of 2026.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) plans to begin small-batch shipments of a newly tailored artificial intelligence chip to Chinese customers by the end of the year, according to a report from The Information.\\n- e2373 [rumour; Investing.com; original source The Information, reporting an unnamed source ; from gnews:c258a90ec294e1838a69819d 2026-08-20] Several Chinese firms have reportedly placed orders for NVIDIA's newly tailored AI chip.\\n    Quote: Several Chinese firms have already placed orders for the device\\n- e2376 [rumour; Investing.com; original source The Information, reporting an unnamed source ; from gnews:c258a90ec294e1838a69819d 2026-08-20] NVIDIA is reportedly developing two lower-power gaming chips tailored for the Chinese market.\\n    Quote: separately developing two lower-power gaming chips tailored for the market.\\n- e2384 [fact; home.saxo; original source Cerebras 2026-08-18; from gnews:331b5397b4e060571d6d8f12 2026-08-20] Cerebras unveiled new AI-inference hardware on 2026-08-18, positioning it to compete in the AI accelerator market.\\n    Quote: Cerebras unveiled new hardware on 18 August designed to make artificial intelligence (AI) inference dramatically faster.\\n- e2385 [fact; home.saxo; original source Google and Marvell Technology 2026-08-19; from gnews:331b5397b4e060571d6d8f12 2026-08-20] Google expanded its custom-chip relationship with Marvell on 2026-08-19; the deal could generate up to USD 120 billion of Marvell revenue through fiscal 2033 if performance targets are met.\\n    Quote: One day later, Google expanded its custom-chip relationship with Marvell Technology, potentially creating up to 120 billion USD of revenue for Marvell through fiscal 2033 if performance targets are reached.\\n- e2386 [fact; home.saxo; original source Broadcom and Google ; from gnews:331b5397b4e060571d6d8f12 2026-08-20] Broadcom agreed to develop future Google custom AI chips through 2031, adding another supplier relationship in the market for custom AI processors.\\n    Quote: Broadcom separately agreed to develop future Google custom AI chips through 2031.\\n- e2388 [third_party_view; Monta HONG, CFA, moomoo Options Strategist, reporting Jefferies' estimate; original source Jefferies ; from gnews:423200474bc9afd76b6b84d2 2026-08-21] Jefferies modeled NVIDIA quarterly revenue of approximately $95 billion, about $3 billion above consensus.\\n    Quote: Jefferies is modeling a record ~$95B quarter, about $3B above consensus.\\n- e2406 [third_party_view; fool.com (author); original source Bank of America ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] Bank of America reported that the four largest U.S. cloud service providers had a combined revenue backlog of $2.3 trillion at the end of the second quarter.\\n    Quote: Bank of America notes that the top four cloud service providers in the U.S. ended the second quarter with an enormous backlog of $2.3 trillion.\\n- e2407 [third_party_view; fool.com (author); original source Vivek Arya, Bank of America ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said Microsoft, Oracle, Amazon and Alphabet need to invest aggressively in cloud-computing capacity to serve their backlogs.\\n    Quote: Analyst Vivek Arya points out that companies such as Microsoft , Oracle , Amazon , and Alphabet 's Google need to invest aggressively in cloud computing capacity to meet their backlogs.\\n- e2408 [party_statement; fool.com (author); original source Oracle and Microsoft ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] Oracle estimated it would recognize 12% of its backlog as revenue over the next year, while Microsoft expected to recognize 30% over the same period.\\n    Quote: Oracle, for instance, estimates that it can convert only 12% of its backlog into revenue over the next year, while Microsoft expects to recognize 30% of the backlog as revenue over the same period.\\n- e2409 [party_statement; fool.com (author); original source Advanced Micro Devices ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] AMD said on its recent earnings call that the AI-compute market could grow at a 40% CAGR and reach nearly $2 trillion in revenue by 2030.\\n    Quote: Not surprisingly, Nvidia's peer, Advanced Micro Devices , noted on its recent earnings call that the total addressable market (TAM) for AI compute is poised to grow at a 40% compound annual growth rate (CAGR) over the long run, generating nearly $2 trillion in revenue by 2030.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2049; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2410 [party_statement; fool.com (author); original source Advanced Micro Devices ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] AMD raised its long-term revenue growth expectation above its earlier estimate of 35%.\\n    Quote: What's more, AMD now expects to clock faster growth than its earlier long-term revenue growth estimate of 35%.\\n- e2411 [third_party_view; fool.com (author); original source fool.com (author) 2026-08-21; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] The author estimated NVIDIA's share of the AI-accelerator market at 80% to 90%.\\n    Quote: Given that Nvidia is a much bigger player than AMD, with an estimated 80% to 90% share of the AI accelerator market, it is poised to win big from the lucrative opportunity over here.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e464; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2416 [party_statement; fool.com (author); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] NVIDIA said its order book was worth $1 trillion for 2026 and 2027.\\n    Quote: The company has already noted that it has an order book worth a whopping $1 trillion for 2026 and 2027 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2422 [third_party_view; Victoria Scholar, Head of Investment, interactive investor; original source Victoria Scholar ; from gnews:93e9c78870df34aee36685cf 2026-08-21] interactive investor expects NVIDIA to report about $91 billion of revenue for fiscal 2027 second quarter.\\n    Quote: The company at the heart of the AI infrastructure roll-out is expected to report revenue of around $91 billion\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1431; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 7]\\n- v16 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: No market consensus is supplied. The deadline reference is passage by December 31, 2026 (rung 1); the alternative is that the Senate has not passed RASA by then (rung 2). RASA had passed the House but was still awaiting Senate action on July 23, and the August 19 report says it had still not passed.\\n- v22 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan\\u2019s Ministry of Economic Affairs adopt performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The reference is rung 2: no formal adoption by the deadline is indicated in the available plan or timeline. The consultations and reported consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry set no timeline; that evidence does not make formal adoption by January 31, 2027 the mor\\n- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan adopt a criminal ban on all AI chip exports to China by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: The reference is rung 1: the supplied evidence says Taiwan is considering a ban, not that it has adopted one. That consideration, in the context of an investigation into suspected smuggling, raises the prospect of action, but the evidence gives no adopted measure, legislative stage, or timetable. Th\\n- v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms\\u2019 access to advanced U.S. chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-07; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or Commerce completion deadline is supplied, and the stated status is a formal investigation, not a scheduled conclusion. The evidence makes non-conclusion by December 31, 2026 more likely: Commerce is still examining access through computing capacit\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is roughly $745 billion for the four largest hyperscalers, which is rung 4. The best direct four-company indicator remains tracking near $745 billion (e1847), consistent with the indication of more than $730 billion for those four (e1927). Amazon's raised projection and Alphabet's rais\\n- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-29) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is Bank of America's reported 2027 analyst consensus of $1.08 trillion, which is rung 3. The evidence still implies about $1.08 trillion: the NVIDIA and other forecasts above $1 trillion, stronger demand and financing evidence support a large build, while spending flexibility, financin\\n- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 2 Yes; judged 2026-08-21; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The observable status-quo reference is no confirmed Chinese authorization, which sits at rung 1; no explicit market consensus or Chinese deadline is supplied. The evidence points beyond that reference: reports describe deliveries of roughly 10,000 H200s each to ByteDance and Tencent, resumed server \\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392\\u2013$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The quarterly path remains above that reference: Q1 was $81.6 billion, BofA expects $94\\u2013$95 billion for Q2 versus the $91 billion company guide, and sees potential Q3 guidance of $107\\u2013$108 billion. Carrying 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $404 billion before any incremental contribution from CPU or SpaceX demand; those provide upside, but their FY2027 contribution is uncertain and partly spans later periods. Morningstar's 80% growth model and the roughly $400 billion estimate are counterweights. The resulting forecast is about $420 billion, above the $392 billion boundary but below the $431.2 billion boundary, so it remains rung 2.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"old-2\", 1788, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is less than $300 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 98 items]\\n[Not shown: 166 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e195 [third_party_view; Jeran Wittenstein, citing data compiled by Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:49f225cd79fb22132f3b9ee4 2026-07-10] Bloomberg data project NVIDIA FY2027 sales of $393 billion, up 82%, and profits of $228 billion, up 90%.\\n    Quote: The chipmaker is projected to deliver $228 billion in profits on sales of $393 billion in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31. That would represent an expansion of 90% and 82%, respectively, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e572, e669, e800; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e220 [fact; qz.com; original source OpenAI and Broadcom ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] OpenAI\\u2019s announced partnership with Broadcom involves co-developing custom AI chips designed to draw up to 10 gigawatts of power.\\n    Quote: Its partnership with Broadcom $AVGO , announced in October 2025, involves co-developing custom AI chips that will draw up to 10 gigawatts of power, or as much electricity as 10 large nuclear reactors generate.\\n- e222 [fact; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:2beadbce40c7f927f33d9fc5 2026-07-12] Amazon committed two gigawatts of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI.\\n    Quote: Amazon has also committed two gigawatts of Trainium computing capacity to OpenAI, putting its chips inside two of the industry's most prominent AI labs.\\n- e231 [rumour; tech-insider.org; original source CNBC (as retold by tech-insider.org) ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron reportedly sold all their 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, with hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon buying the supply.\\n    Quote: The root cause sits one layer below Nvidia entirely. Hyperscalers including Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon are buying up high-bandwidth memory as fast as Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron can produce it, and all three manufacturers have reportedly sold out their entire 2026 HBM output under long-term contracts, according to CNBC\\u2019s reporting from January 2026.\\n- e232 [third_party_view; tech-insider.org; original source Micron and Kearney\\u2019s PERLab, as reported by tech-insider.org ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] Micron suggested memory supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027 and may return gradually to balance by 2028; Kearney\\u2019s PERLab reportedly projected the shortage could last into 2030.\\n    Quote: Micron has since suggested supply may not meaningfully normalize until 2027, with a gradual return to balance by 2028, while other forecasters take a bleaker view: an analysis from Kearney\\u2019s PERLab reportedly projects the shortage could persist into 2030 .\\n- e234 [party_statement; tech-insider.org; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs during 2027\\u20132028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\\n- e238 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: For the first quarter of fiscal 2027, revenue jumped 85% year-over-year (YOY) to $81.6 billion, easily beating analyst expectations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e223, e248, e268, e967; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e240 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management forecast approximately $91 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, assuming no China data-center sales.\\n    Quote: Management also issued strong guidance, forecasting approximately $91 billion in Q2 revenue despite assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e554, e851; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e252 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source Chinese technology companies surveyed by Bloomberg ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] A Bloomberg survey reported that Chinese technology companies expect to allocate about 46% of their AI chip spending to domestic suppliers over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\\n    Quote: A new Bloomberg survey showed Chinese technology companies expect to allocate roughly 46% of their AI chip spending to domestic suppliers over the next year, up from about 30% currently.\\n- e255 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management guided FY2027 Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, assuming no China Data Center revenue.\\n    Quote: Looking ahead, management guided Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202, e271, e292, e332, e350, e768; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e284 [fact; Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped no H20 compute products to China in Q1 FY2027 and excluded Data Center compute revenue from China from its Q2 FY2027 guidance.\\n    Quote: There were no H20 compute products shipped to China in Q1 FY27, and the Q2 FY27 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n- e321 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:c73b90b63a733c6ab712fcdd 2026-07-14] SK Hynix is slowing its HBM4 ramp and redirecting capacity to conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above HBM margins.\\n    Quote: SK Hynix is deliberately slowing its HBM4 ramp to redirect capacity into conventional DRAM, where shortages have pushed operating margins above HBM's.\\n- e376 [rumour; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Financial Times 2026-07-14; from gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd 2026-07-14] The Financial Times reported that NVIDIA more than halved its list of Asian customers authorised to buy AI chips after creating a new compliance-checked whitelist to prevent shipments reaching China.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new \\u201cwhite list\\u201d of companies that have passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e337; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e408 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-15; from gnews:4e8785bda74ec6e3d7abe4ef 2026-07-15] NVIDIA says it maintained delivery performance while coordinating wafer-to-server supply plans across GPUs, switches, NICs, and cables amid unprecedented demand growth.\\n    Quote: Orchestrated wafer-to-server supply plans across GPUs, switches, NICs, and cables\\u2014keeping delivery performance intact through unprecedented demand growth.\\n- e456 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] NVIDIA guided Q2 revenue to $91.0 billion and reported $119.0 billion in booked supply commitments.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA guided Q2 revenue to $91.0 billion with $119.0 billion in supply commitments already booked.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e349, e460; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e459 [party_statement; Gerelyn Terzo, reporting Huang's remarks; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had more orders than at his previous GTC remarks and expected to keep its supply chain busy for several more years.\\n    Quote: He added that \\u201cWe have more orders today than we did at the last time I spoke about orders at GTC\\u201d and that NVIDIA will \\u201ckeep our supply chain quite busy for several many more years coming.\\u201d\\n- e577 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c03377d9fbbf30b7a0933cd5 2026-07-17] NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of close to $215.9 billion, up 65.5% year over year.\\n    Quote: In FY 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, a significant jump of 65.5% over the previous year.\\n- e599 [fact; tikr.com; original source US Commerce Department 2026-07-10; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] On July 10, 2026, the US Commerce Department moved the UAE into its highest-trust export tier, easing export authorization for NVIDIA Blackwell processors to UAE entities.\\n    Quote: On July 10, the US Commerce Department reclassified the United Arab Emirates into its highest-trust export tier, clearing the way for UAE entities to buy NVIDIA\\u2019s most advanced Blackwell processors without the months-long, shipment-by-shipment license reviews that had governed Gulf sales.\\n- e621 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:24da4cc269dbf1a4b9fdf76b 2026-07-20] NVIDIA is planning for no revenue contribution from China's data center market.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is planning for no contribution from China\\u2019s data center market\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e441; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e669 [third_party_view; Anthony Di Pizio, Motley Fool; original source Wall Street's average estimates, provided by Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:0993b79d5b6b817b8d65b011 2026-07-21] Wall Street's average estimates put NVIDIA total revenue at $393 billion in FY2027 and $559 billion in FY2028.\\n    Quote: According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year, and then to $559 billion in fiscal 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e698 [party_statement; Dallas Innovates, reporting NVIDIA\\u2019s statement; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:49d513bf1eb19cbade4a0607 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said it plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the United States over the next several years with named partners including Wistron, TSMC, Foxconn, Corning, Lumentum, Coherent and Amkor.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA said it plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the U.S. over the next several years with partners including Wistron, TSMC in Phoenix, Foxconn in Houston, Corning, Lumentum, Coherent and Amkor.\\n- e735 [fact; focustaiwan.tw; original source Wistron ; from gnews:5d3afe42b4344647c2ba51bc 2026-07-22] Wistron\\u2019s first U.S. manufacturing facility, D1 in Fort Worth, has begun mass-producing NVIDIA\\u2019s GB300 Grace Blackwell Ultra Superchip.\\n    Quote: Wistron's D1 facility is its first manufacturing base in the U.S. and has begun mass-producing Nvidia's GB300 Grace Blackwell Ultra Superchip.\\n- e746 [third_party_view; Bruegel; original source Bruegel 2026-07-22; from gnews:2a37a1e51a1917e7c0b5d693 2026-07-22] Bruegel estimates that Nvidia accounts for roughly half of the world's installed AI chip stock and up to two thirds of installed AI computing capacity; no period is specified.\\n    Quote: Nvidia accounts for roughly half of the world's installed AI chip stock and as much as two thirds of installed computing capacity\\n- e747 [third_party_view; Bruegel; original source Bruegel 2026-07-22; from gnews:2a37a1e51a1917e7c0b5d693 2026-07-22] Bruegel estimates that Huawei's processors use two to four times as much power as Nvidia's for equivalent processing output; no period is specified.\\n    Quote: Huawei compensates for this by packing processors together, resulting in an estimated two to four times more power usage for equivalent processing output.\\n- e748 [third_party_view; Bruegel; original source Bruegel 2026-07-22; from gnews:2a37a1e51a1917e7c0b5d693 2026-07-22] Bruegel says Huawei is reinvesting its sharply rising AI chip revenue this year into R&D; the revenue amount is not stated.\\n    Quote: revenue that is reinvested back into R&D.\\n- e749 [fact; Zacks.com analyst; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:43105a934c3a4e2d21029e9c 2026-07-22] NVIDIA reported first-quarter FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: First-quarter revenues soared 85% year over year to $81.6 billion\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e579, e974; the argumen