026, with additional Groq chips planned annually.\\n    Quote: In March, Nvidia announced it would roll out Groq-based chips before the end of the year, alongside new GPUs and CPUs, with more Groq chips coming on an annual cadence.\\n- e834 [rumour; wccftech.com; original source Benchlife ; from gnews:8fed36a218d0b0149b373475 2026-07-23] Benchlife reported that NVIDIA notified its board partners of price increases for both GDDR6 and GDDR7 memory kits; the report gives no date for the increases.\\n    Quote: Benchlife says that NVIDIA has sent the price increase notice to its board partners, and this includes both GDDR6 and GDDR7 memory kits.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e837; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e835 [third_party_view; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com 2026-07-23; from gnews:8fed36a218d0b0149b373475 2026-07-23] wccftech.com reported that upcoming RTX 50 SUPER cards are expected to have a larger price increase, citing 3 GB GDDR7 memory prices at three times those of 2 GB modules; no price amount or date was given.\\n    Quote: On the other hand, the upcoming RTX 50 SUPER cards are going to bring a much larger price hike, given that the prices of 3 GB GDDR7 memory is 3X higher than 2 GB modules .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e839; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e837 [rumour; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:546daa557a27073326935caa 2026-07-23] NVIDIA reportedly notified add-in card partners of higher prices for GPU kits with GDDR7 and GDDR6 memory; the increase amount and affected models were not disclosed.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has reportedly informed its add-in card partners of higher prices for GPU kits supplied with graphics memory. The adjustment covers GeForce products using both GDDR7 and GDDR6 memory, according to BenchLife.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e834; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e838 [third_party_view; videocardz.com; original source BenchLife ; from gnews:546daa557a27073326935caa 2026-07-23] BenchLife reported that board partners face higher costs for cooling systems, PCBs and packaging, with increases smaller than those for graphics memory; it gave no amounts or period.\\n    Quote: BenchLife adds that board partners are also facing higher costs for cooling systems, PCBs and packaging. However, the report claims the increases for these components are smaller than those affecting graphics memory.\\n- e839 [rumour; videocardz.com; original source videocardz.com ; from gnews:546daa557a27073326935caa 2026-07-23] Videocardz, citing an unnamed source, reported that NVIDIA's GeForce RTX 50 SUPER series remains on hold because 3GB GDDR7 modules cost $60\\u2013$70 each, versus about $20 for a 2GB module.\\n    Quote: The report follows our information that NVIDIA\\u2019s GeForce RTX 50 SUPER series remains on hold because of the cost of 3GB GDDR7 memory modules. Our source said these modules cost between $60 and $70 each, compared with around $20 for a 2GB module.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e835; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e840 [fact; Observer.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2e520919628357782c29775b 2026-07-23] NVIDIA reported record quarterly revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year; the quarter is not specified.\\n    Quote: The company posted a record $81.6 billion in quarterly revenue, up 85 percent year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e674; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e842 [fact; Observer.com; original source Agile Robots, Doosan Robotics, Siemens and Skild AI ; from gnews:2e520919628357782c29775b 2026-07-23] Agile Robots, Doosan Robotics, Siemens and Skild AI are evaluating NVIDIA\\u2019s Cosmos 3 Edge framework.\\n    Quote: Companies currently evaluating the framework include Agile Robots, Doosan Robotics, Siemens and Skild AI.\\n- e843 [rumour; 247wallst.com, summarizing a leaked investor call attributed to DeepSeek CEO Liang Wenfeng; original source DeepSeek CEO Liang Wenfeng ; from gnews:f0a407226a9a889404fbb9f8 2026-07-23] DeepSeek reportedly is working closely with Huawei and believes it can secure about 16,000 Huawei AI chips; the period is not specified.\\n    Quote: DeepSeek is working closely with Huawei and believes it can secure roughly 16,000 Huawei AI chips.\\n- e844 [rumour; 247wallst.com, summarizing a leaked investor call attributed to DeepSeek CEO Liang Wenfeng; original source DeepSeek CEO Liang Wenfeng ; from gnews:f0a407226a9a889404fbb9f8 2026-07-23] DeepSeek CEO Liang Wenfeng allegedly said AI code generation and TileLang could lower CUDA\\u2019s barriers to entry, and that DeepSeek had reduced its NVIDIA software dependence using its own compiler and a TileLang-based environment; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: Wenfeng allegedly argued that AI-powered code generation and languages such as TileLang could rapidly lower the CUDA ecosystem\\u2019s barriers to entry, and that DeepSeek has already cut its software dependence on NVIDIA using its own compiler and a TileLang-based environment.\\n- e845 [rumour; 247wallst.com, summarizing a leaked investor call attributed to DeepSeek CEO Liang Wenfeng; original source DeepSeek CEO Liang Wenfeng ; from gnews:f0a407226a9a889404fbb9f8 2026-07-23] The leaked-call argument was that porting DeepSeek\\u2019s software stack to Huawei silicon could allow Huawei\\u2019s 950 SuperNode to replace workloads handled by NVIDIA GB200 and GB300; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: Port that stack to Huawei silicon, the argument goes, and Huawei\\u2019s 950 SuperNode could replace workloads currently handled by NVIDIA\\u2019s GB200 and GB300.\\n- e846 [third_party_view; Citrini Research analyst Jukan, quoted by 247wallst.com; original source Citrini Research analyst Jukan ; from gnews:f0a407226a9a889404fbb9f8 2026-07-23] Citrini Research analyst Jukan said the CUDA moat could end and Chinese-chip ecosystem barriers could be solved within a year, with production the remaining bottleneck.\\n    Quote: \\u201cThe end of CUDA\\u2019s moat is approaching. The ecosystem problem for Chinese chips could be solved within a year. The only real bottleneck left is production itself.\\u201d\\n- e847 [rumour; 247wallst.com, summarizing a leaked investor call attributed to DeepSeek CEO Liang Wenfeng; original source DeepSeek CEO Liang Wenfeng ; from gnews:f0a407226a9a889404fbb9f8 2026-07-23] The leaked remarks reportedly said about four Huawei cards are needed to match one NVIDIA card and Huawei is about two years behind in performance.\\n    Quote: It reportedly takes roughly four Huawei cards to match one NVIDIA card, with Huawei described as about two years behind.\\n- e848 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0a407226a9a889404fbb9f8 2026-07-23] NVIDIA reported quarterly total revenue of $81.61 billion; the quarter is not specified in the item.\\n    Quote: the company just posted $81.61 billion in quarterly revenue\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e451; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e851 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, reporting NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0a407226a9a889404fbb9f8 2026-07-23] NVIDIA guided to $91 billion in revenue, plus or minus 2%, and 75% non-GAAP gross margin for fiscal Q2 2027, excluding Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA\\u2019s guidance calls for revenue of $91 billion plus or minus 2% with non-GAAP gross margin of 75%, and it explicitly excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e240, e878; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e861 [third_party_view; Precedence Research; original source Precedence Research 2026-07-23; from gnews:d3a7084f8c58ba693bd27464 2026-07-23] Precedence Research reported that NVIDIA held roughly 80% of the AI training market; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA dominates the AI training landscape, holding roughly 80% market share.\\n- e877 [third_party_view; fool.com analyst; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:2493b412c4d7cbb20cac1e7f 2026-07-24] The article reports that Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA total revenue growth of 82% in FY2027 and 42% in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is expected to generate 82% growth this year and 42% next year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e800; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e878 [party_statement; Dave Kovaleski, The Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:bfd479c07812f14b70042fda 2026-07-24] NVIDIA's current-quarter revenue outlook calls for $91 billion, up 11% sequentially.\\n    Quote: So, while it's beating the benchmarks, the return has fallen short of recent years, despite ridiculously strong revenue and earnings growth numbers and a current quarter outlook that calls for revenue to increase 11% sequentially to $91 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e851; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e881 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source Liang Wenfeng, DeepSeek founder ; from gnews:9476c94361d79b2a660e9df5 2026-07-24] DeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng said DeepSeek remained constrained by compute availability despite operating roughly 20,000 H100-equivalent GPUs; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: According to the transcript, DeepSeek remains constrained by compute availability despite operating roughly 20,000 H100-equivalent GPUs.\\n- e882 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source Liang Wenfeng, DeepSeek founder ; from gnews:9476c94361d79b2a660e9df5 2026-07-24] DeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng said DeepSeek expected to receive large batches of Nvidia-powered systems in the coming months; no quantity was specified.\\n    Quote: Liang also said Huawei\\u2019s production capacity remains limited and that DeepSeek expects to receive \\u201clarge batches\\u201d of Nvidia-powered systems in the coming months following the Trump administration\\u2019s decision to permit certain Nvidia AI chip sales into China.\\n- e883 [fact; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source Trump administration ; from gnews:9476c94361d79b2a660e9df5 2026-07-24] The Trump administration permitted certain Nvidia AI chip sales into China; the item gives no date or further details.\\n    Quote: following the Trump administration\\u2019s decision to permit certain Nvidia AI chip sales into China.\\n- e894 [party_statement; Alex Sirois; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said agentic AI had arrived, was generating real value, and was scaling rapidly across companies and industries.\\n    Quote: Jensen Huang said \\u201cAgentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries\\u201d\\n- e895 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.2%, and non-GAAP gross margin of 75.0%.\\n    Quote: $81.615 billion in Q1 FY2027 revenue, up 85.2%, with 75.0% non-GAAP gross margin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e904 [fact; SK hynix; original source SK Group and NVIDIA 2026-07-25; from gnews:db759b7fd9bedbcd0f632fe6 2026-07-25] SK Group and NVIDIA signed letters of intent for a planned partnership spanning AI factory construction and AI memory supply; no completion date or amount for the agreement is stated.\\n    Quote: The two sides signed Letters of Intent to formalize the agreement, which spans from AI factory construction to AI memory supply.\\n- e905 [party_statement; SK hynix; original source SK Telecom ; from gnews:db759b7fd9bedbcd0f632fe6 2026-07-25] SK Telecom plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera Rubin accelerated computing with SK hynix HBM4 in its AI cloud, with its first AI factory planned to come online in 2027.\\n    Quote: This cloud will use the NVIDIA DSX platform and deploy NVIDIA Vera Rubin accelerated computing powered by SK hynix HBM4, with the first AI factory planned to come online in 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e870, e908; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e906 [fact; SK hynix; original source SK hynix and NVIDIA 2026-07-25; from gnews:db759b7fd9bedbcd0f632fe6 2026-07-25] SK hynix and NVIDIA are entering a long-term AI memory partnership intended to give NVIDIA stable supply of next-generation AI memory.\\n    Quote: SK hynix is also entering into a long-term AI memory partnership with NVIDIA. As a follow-up measure to solidify their previous long-term technical partnership, this agreement allows NVIDIA to secure a stable supply of next-generation AI memory, while enabling SK hynix to expand the foundation for growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e948; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e907 [party_statement; SK hynix; original source SK hynix and NVIDIA 2026-07-25; from gnews:db759b7fd9bedbcd0f632fe6 2026-07-25] SK hynix and NVIDIA plan to co-develop and optimize next-generation AI memory, including HBM, for AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: The two companies will co-develop and optimize next-generation AI memory solutions, including HBM, to meet evolving infrastructure demands ranging from large language model training to agentic AI and physical AI.\\n- e912 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95dad828b4f67626286c9fa3 2026-07-25] NVIDIA reported revenue of $82 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially; it recorded 14 consecutive quarters of sequential revenue growth.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported exceptional financial performance, with revenue reaching $82 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, marking its fourteenth consecutive quarter of sequential growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e269; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e914 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95dad828b4f67626286c9fa3 2026-07-25] NVIDIA said InfiniBand revenue increased more than fourfold year over year.\\n    Quote: Networking has also become a meaningful growth engine, as Spectrum-X has surpassed the combined scale of competing Ethernet networking peers while InfiniBand revenue increased more than fourfold year over year.\\n- e915 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95dad828b4f67626286c9fa3 2026-07-25] NVIDIA guided to second-quarter revenue of $91 billion and stable margins.\\n    Quote: With second-quarter guidance of $91 billion in revenue, stable margins, the upcoming Vera Rubin platform, and continued AI infrastructure investment\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e343; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e917 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:95dad828b4f67626286c9fa3 2026-07-25] Michael Burry's bearish thesis characterizes NVIDIA's market environment as exposed to a speculative bubble and semiconductor cyclicality.\\n    Quote: Michael Burry's bearish thesis on Nvidia relies on a macro market-bubble framework combined with classic semiconductor cyclicality.\\n- e918 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:95dad828b4f67626286c9fa3 2026-07-25] Michael Burry argued that enterprise monetization and end-user software returns will lag the upfront costs of hardware deployment.\\n    Quote: His core argument posits that enterprise monetization and end-user return on investment from software applications will inevitably lag far behind the front-loaded costs of hardware deployment.\\n- e919 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:95dad828b4f67626286c9fa3 2026-07-25] Michael Burry argued that diminishing returns on hyperscaler infrastructure could prompt severe capex cuts and an abrupt drop in semiconductor demand.\\n    Quote: When hyperscalers realize their current infrastructure yields diminishing marginal returns, capital expenditure budgets will face severe rationalization, exposing the semiconductor supply chain to an abrupt demand drop.\\n- e920 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:95dad828b4f67626286c9fa3 2026-07-25] Michael Burry argued that GPU double-ordering during shortages can be followed by purchase pauses, order cancellations, and inventory drawdowns when supply catches up with utilization.\\n    Quote: During periods of extreme chip supply constraints, enterprise customers typically double-order GPUs to secure allocation. Once supply catches up with actual immediate utilization, buyers pause purchases to absorb excess inventory, leading to sharp order cancellations and inventory drawdowns.\\n- e921 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:95dad828b4f67626286c9fa3 2026-07-25] Michael Burry argued that maturing custom enterprise silicon and competing hardware could reduce NVIDIA's pricing power toward historical industry averages.\\n    Quote: as custom enterprise silicon and competing hardware options mature, Nvidia's pricing power must normalize down toward historical industry averages.\\n- e922 [third_party_view; Micah Zimmerman, fool.com; original source Micah Zimmerman 2026-07-25; from gnews:2e0ce0af0cb6d6e484bd1967 2026-07-25] The author estimates that NVIDIA has 80%\\u201390% of the AI data-center GPU market.\\n    Quote: Nvidia still controls somewhere between 80% and 90% of the AI data center graphics processing unit (GPU) market.\\n- e924 [third_party_view; Micah Zimmerman, fool.com; original source Micah Zimmerman 2026-07-25; from gnews:2e0ce0af0cb6d6e484bd1967 2026-07-25] The author says NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform has ramped into full production and has demand visibility in the trillions of dollars.\\n    Quote: its new Vera Rubin platform has ramped into full production with demand visibility stretching into the trillions of dollars.\\n- e925 [third_party_view; Micah Zimmerman, fool.com; original source Micah Zimmerman 2026-07-25; from gnews:2e0ce0af0cb6d6e484bd1967 2026-07-25] The author says AMD reported record Data Center revenue, up 57% year over year; the amount and reporting period are not specified.\\n    Quote: record data center revenue, up 57% from a year earlier\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e926 [third_party_view; Micah Zimmerman, fool.com; original source Micah Zimmerman 2026-07-25; from gnews:2e0ce0af0cb6d6e484bd1967 2026-07-25] The author says AMD landed multiyear commitments from OpenAI and Meta Platforms, providing revenue visibility; no amounts are given.\\n    Quote: AMD landed enormous multiyear commitments from OpenAI and Meta Platforms -- deals that give it real revenue visibility.\\n- e927 [third_party_view; Micah Zimmerman, fool.com; original source Micah Zimmerman 2026-07-25; from gnews:2e0ce0af0cb6d6e484bd1967 2026-07-25] The author estimates AMD has a mid-single-digit share of the AI GPU market.\\n    Quote: AMD still holds only a mid-single-digit slice of the AI GPU market\\n- e928 [third_party_view; Micah Zimmerman, fool.com; original source Micah Zimmerman 2026-07-25; from gnews:2e0ce0af0cb6d6e484bd1967 2026-07-25] The author says Intel's next-generation data-center AI chip is not expected until well into 2027.\\n    Quote: its next-generation data center AI chip is not expected until well into 2027.\\n- e935 [fact; John Ballard, fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5d4b94245e962f4185fcd9f3 2026-07-25] NVIDIA\\u2019s edge computing segment, comprising robotics, automotive, and PCs, generated $6.4 billion of revenue, up 29%.\\n    Quote: The new edge computing segment (robotics, automotive, and PCs) is small by comparison, generating only $6.4 billion in revenue, up 29%.\\n- e948 [party_statement; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:99a1ba68b933cb3eeb687120 2026-07-25] Nvidia plans to help SK Hynix design future high-bandwidth memory chips, which Nvidia said would help guarantee its supply access.\\n    Quote: Nvidia will help Hynix design future high-bandwidth memory chips, an effort that will help guarantee access to supply, the US company said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e906; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e949 [party_statement; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:99a1ba68b933cb3eeb687120 2026-07-25] Nvidia said its AI initiative with SK Group will involve more than US$500 billion in business in total.\\n    Quote: In total, the \\u201cAI initiative\\u201d with SK Group will be worth more than US$500 billion, Nvidia said.\\n- e951 [fact; businesstimes.com.sg; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:99a1ba68b933cb3eeb687120 2026-07-25] Nvidia signed a US$1.5 billion agreement with Amkor Technology earlier in the week to help bolster Amkor\\u2019s chip packaging facilities.\\n    Quote: Earlier this week, Nvidia signed a US$1.5 billion agreement with Amkor Technology to help bolster that company\\u2019s chip packaging facilities.\\n- e962 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-26; from gnews:963e6f7ff30e3ab482d17d8d 2026-07-26] The author says Qualcomm\\u2019s data-center revenue is expected to reach roughly $5 billion by fiscal 2027 and $15 billion by fiscal 2029.\\n    Quote: Revenue from its data center business is expected to reach roughly $5 billion by fiscal 2027 and $15 billion by fiscal 2029.\\n- e963 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Qualcomm ; from gnews:963e6f7ff30e3ab482d17d8d 2026-07-26] Qualcomm recently signed a long-term agreement to supply Meta Platforms with Dragonfly C1000 CPUs, which are planned to enter production in 2028.\\n    Quote: The company recently signed a long-term agreement with Meta Platforms to supply Dragonfly C1000 CPUs, which will enter production in 2028.\\n- e967 [fact; Will Healy; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:806c81fe02efa14402c325dd 2026-07-26] NVIDIA revenue increased 85% year over year in fiscal Q1 2027, ended April 26, 2026.\\n    Quote: In the first quarter of fiscal 2027 (ended April 26), revenue increased by 85% yearly\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e238; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e974 [fact; fool.com author Lyle Daly; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: Most recently, revenue was up 85% year over year to a record $81.6 billion in its Q1 fiscal year 2027 (which ended April 26, 2026).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e749; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e975 [party_statement; fool.com author Lyle Daly; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] Jensen Huang forecast combined Blackwell and Vera Rubin sales of $1 trillion through 2027.\\n    Quote: Earlier this year, Huang provided a sales forecast of $1 trillion combined for Blackwell and Vera Rubin through 2027, so there's little room for setbacks.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e817; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e980 [third_party_view; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com 2026-07-26; from gnews:6a7b598572c4a07e2199ed47 2026-07-26] The article reports that persistent shortages have driven GDDR6 and GDDR7 DRAM costs to 10 times their prior level, increasing graphics-card prices; it gives no period for the comparison.\\n    Quote: DRAM costs for GDDR6 and GDDR7 products 10-fold, resulting in a significant increase in graphics card prices.\\n- e981 [fact; wccftech.com; original source Colorful ; from gnews:6a7b598572c4a07e2199ed47 2026-07-26] Colorful raised its official mainland China distributor prices for RTX 50 graphics cards by 40\\u201350%; the article labels the prices 7/25/26.\\n    Quote: Colorful, the leading graphics card supplier in mainland China, has bumped up the prices, and its official distributor prices show a 40-50% bump in RTX 50 prices.\\n- e982 [fact; wccftech.com; original source MSI ; from gnews:6a7b598572c4a07e2199ed47 2026-07-26] MSI raised its graphics-card prices in China by 10\\u201320% versus the prior week; the article labels the prices 7/25/26.\\n    Quote: MSI's prices have also seen a 10-20% bump in prices versus what they were selling for a week ago.\\n- e983 [rumour; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com 2026-07-26; from gnews:6a7b598572c4a07e2199ed47 2026-07-26] The article reports that NVIDIA sent Chinese graphics-card manufacturers, including ASUS, MSI, Colorful and Inno3D, a notification of a price increase; it gives no amount or effective date.\\n    Quote: So far, the price increase notification from NVIDIA was sent out to ASUS, MSI, Colorful, Inno3D, and other Chinese manufacturers.\\n- e984 [third_party_view; wccftech.com; original source wccftech.com ; from gnews:6a7b598572c4a07e2199ed47 2026-07-26] Research analysts forecast DRAM prices to rise 50% in calendar Q3 2026 and a further 40% in calendar Q4 2026; the analysts are not named.\\n    Quote: research analysts have already warned of 50% price bumps in DRAM prices in Q3 2026, followed by another 40% bump in Q4 2026 .\\n\\n[Themes in force, 4]\\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-23; reasons: The reference is rung 2: the evidence does not establish that Chinese authorities have authorized purchases, and no explicit market consensus is supplied. The forecast is nevertheless rung 1: named Chinese AI companies were reportedly told they would be permitted to buy some H200s, and reports descr\\n- v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-23; reasons: The reference is the observable status as of July 23: RASA had passed the House but was still awaiting Senate action, placing the reference at rung 2. The White House disclosure of remote GB300 access may add urgency, but the evidence gives no Senate action or schedule; on balance, non-passage by th\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-26; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is around $650 billion for the four hyperscalers\\u2019 2026 data-center capex, which is rung 3. The company capex figures and recent spending reports add upside, but several are total capex or broader AI-infrastructure measures, not the specific four-company data-center total. Taken togethe\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-22; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management's forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, alongside projected 2026 spending of $650 billion; that reference sits in rung 3. The evidence continues to imply spending around $1 trillion, but supplies no quantified amount that would take the forecast to the $1.1 tr\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, based on the reported 82% growth estimate, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The Q1 result of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for the year if Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth of roughly 11.5% carries through Q3 and Q4. Order and delivery evidence, as well as easing export access, support some upside toward the reference; memory constraints and the lack of quantified FY2027 China sales or product volumes limit it. The implied outcome remains below $400 billion, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"old-2\", 5474, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v21 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 60%. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 50% and below 60%. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 35% and below 50%, bracketing Wall Street's 42% estimate cited in the item. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 total revenue growth is at least 20% and below 35%. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 total revenue growth is below 20%.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 46 items]\\n[Not shown: 19 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e966 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool; original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:cb4d70e84bc7272b22598c93 2026-07-26] Wall Street expects Micron revenue to grow 84% in its fiscal year ending August 2027 and estimates NVIDIA revenue growth of 42% in FY2027.\\n    Quote: By the end of fiscal year 2027 (ending August 2027), Wall Street expects an additional 84% growth for Micron, while it estimates only 42% for Nvidia.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1148, e1285; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1097 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-27; from gnews:ac966c48ae5afea2a79acaf3 2026-07-27] The author judged that Alphabet was likely to buy more NVIDIA AI chip systems as it raised spending, creating a solid chance of faster NVIDIA revenue growth.\\n    Quote: With Alphabet poised to significantly raise its spending this year, and possibly in the future as well, due to the huge backlog that it is sitting on, it is likely to purchase more of Nvidia's AI chip systems. So, there is a solid chance of an improvement in Nvidia's growth rate.\\n- e1278 [party_statement; Neil Patel, retelling Jensen Huang's remarks; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:b04b7ff5e821871892455ef6 2026-07-30] At Nvidia's GTC conference in March, CEO Jensen Huang forecast $1 trillion of cumulative chip sales through 2027, up from his previous $500 billion forecast through 2026.\\n    Quote: During Nvidia's GTC conference in March, Huang said that the business will collect $1 trillion in sales from its chips through 2027, double the previous $500 billion forecast through 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e975; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1333 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts ; from gnews:019ddb75bb67d2fb9b212f18 2026-07-31] Analysts expect NVIDIA revenue to grow 82% in FY2027.\\n    Quote: analysts expect revenue to grow 82% this year.\\n- e1348 [fact; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6f53d290d7aa54066ad3f160 2026-07-31] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially.\\n    Quote: For the fiscal first quarter of 2027, NVIDIA\\u2019s revenues were $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, according to the company\\u2019s press release .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e269; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1350 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:6f53d290d7aa54066ad3f160 2026-07-31] NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q2 2027 revenue to $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Quote: Management further expects AI demand to remain robust, as they project NVIDIA\\u2019s fiscal second-quarter 2027 revenues to come in at $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e332, e2059, e2142, e2144, e2330; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1388 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:0b1b72dac911085466f3a3b1 2026-08-02] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA revenue growth of 96% in fiscal Q2 2027.\\n    Quote: For the second quarter of fiscal year 2027 (ending in late July), Wall Street analysts expect an impressive 96% growth rate.\\n- e1390 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts ; from gnews:0b1b72dac911085466f3a3b1 2026-08-02] Analysts estimate NVIDIA revenue growth of 81% in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Quote: Right now, analysts estimate 81% revenue growth for Q3.\\n- e1705 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo, 24/7 Wall St.; original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] CoreWeave reported Q1 revenue growth of 111.6% year over year and a backlog of nearly $100 billion.\\n    Quote: Its Q1 revenue grew 111.6% year over year, backlog soared to nearly $100 billion\\n- e1706 [party_statement; Gerelyn Terzo, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Michael Intrator, CoreWeave CEO ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] CoreWeave CEO Michael Intrator said the company\\u2019s bookings in the last quarter were the strongest in its history.\\n    Quote: CEO Michael Intrator described the last quarter as \\u201cthe strongest bookings quarter in CoreWeave\\u2019s history.\\u201d\\n- e1764 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source CoreWeave 2026-08-10; from gnews:011203b517e045ffe773b1fb 2026-08-12] CoreWeave reported a $104 billion revenue backlog, excluding more than $25 billion in new commitments booked since July.\\n    Quote: and a $104 billion revenue backlog that excludes more than $25 billion in new commitments booked since July.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1868, e2064; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e1705 (); both are in force: CoreWeave reported Q1 revenue growth of 111.6% year over year and a backlog of nearly $100 billion.\\n- e1883 [fact; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3ef8c07472c4da3ddb8d4350 2026-08-15] NVIDIA reported FY2026 total revenue of $215.94 billion, up 65.47% year over year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported full-year FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, up 65.47% year over year, with net income of $120.07 billion and free cash flow of $96.58 billion.\\n- e2036 [third_party_view; CNBC, quoting Cantor analysts; original source Cantor analysts 2026-08-17; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] Cantor analysts said NVIDIA's latest agreement signaled that the AI investment cycle would be prolonged and durable.\\n    Quote: In a note to clients Monday, analysts at Cantor brushed off concerns that Nvidia is effectively buying revenue through its financial maneuvering. They reiterated their buy rating and said the latest agreement is a \\\"clear signal that the current AI investment cycle will be elongated and durable.\\\"\\n- e2040 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] Anthropic told investors that its annualized revenue run rate reached $65 billion in July 2026, seven times the year-earlier level.\\n    Quote: Anthropic told investors over the weekend that its annualized revenue run rate hit $65 billion in July, up sevenfold from a year earlier.\\n- e2041 [fact; CNBC; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:c2c16796ce95c445eb0bb9b5 2026-08-18] OpenAI's revenue run rate recently reached $40 billion.\\n    Quote: OpenAI's run rate recently reached $40 billion.\\n- e2118 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar expects industry AI capex to accelerate in calendar 2026 and models 80% growth in NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027.\\n    Quote: Given the acceleration in AI capex we expect within the industry in calendar 2026, we model 80% total revenue growth for Nvidia in fiscal 2027.\\n- e2142 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] NVIDIA guided to fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue of $91.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Quote: The company's own guidance, issued back in May, calls for revenue of $91.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1350, e2860; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2145 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Daniel Sparks 2026-08-19; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] Daniel Sparks cited NVIDIA fiscal 2026 second-quarter revenue of $46.7 billion and calculated that the fiscal 2027 second-quarter guidance midpoint implies nearly 100% year-over-year revenue growth.\\n    Quote: The year-ago quarter brought in $46.7 billion, so the guide's midpoint implies revenue close to doubling year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1788; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2146 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, about $2 billion above the top of its earlier $78.0 billion-plus-or-minus-2% guidance range; Data Center revenue was $75.2 billion, up 92%.\\n    Quote: In May, Nvidia reported $81.6 billion of fiscal first-quarter revenue -- about $2 billion above the top of the $78.0 billion, plus-or-minus-2%, range it had guided to three months earlier. Growth ran 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, with data center revenue climbing 92% to $75.2 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e204, e2340; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2147 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Yahoo! Finance ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] Yahoo! Finance data showed five upward and no downward analyst revisions to NVIDIA fiscal 2027 second-quarter estimates over the month before August 19, 2026.\\n    Quote: analysts have been nudging this quarter's numbers higher into the report -- five upward revisions over the past month, and none down, per Yahoo! Finance.\\n- e2149 [party_statement; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter outlook assumed no Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: The company said it is not assuming any data center compute revenue from China in its outlook\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e350; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2151 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Daniel Sparks 2026-08-19; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] Daniel Sparks calculated that NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue guidance midpoint implies about $9 billion of sequential growth, or 11%, versus roughly $13.5 billion and 20% sequential growth in the first quarter.\\n    Quote: The midpoint asks for about $9 billion of new revenue versus the first quarter, which had itself added about $13.5 billion versus the one before it. In growth terms, that's a step down to about 11% sequentially from 20%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e878; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2154 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang (the $1 trillion target); fool.com (analyst) (the FY2027 and FY2028 revenue estimates) ; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] The author says Jensen Huang set a $1 trillion total-revenue target for 2026\\u20132027 and estimates NVIDIA could generate roughly $400 billion in revenue in FY2027 and $600 billion in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has given $1 trillion in total revenue as a target for 2026-2027, implying the company could hit roughly $400 billion in revenue this year and $600 billion in fiscal 2028, which ends in Jan. 2028.\\n- e2244 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] NVIDIA said its order book for 2026 and 2027 was worth $1 trillion.\\n    Quote: The company has already noted that it has an order book worth a whopping $1 trillion for 2026 and 2027 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e817, e2494; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2399 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:40b7c3034fa386a9e08dd2f5 2026-08-24] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.23% year over year.\\n    Quote: Last quarter, Nvidia posted revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.23% year over year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e770; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2419 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source LSEG analysts ; from gnews:58fb7b4d430994417016dc48 2026-08-25] LSEG analysts expected NVIDIA\\u2019s second-quarter total revenue to reach $92.2 billion, almost double the year-earlier level.\\n    Quote: In total, analysts see Nvidia's revenue almost doubling from a year earlier to $92.2 billion, according to LSEG.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2289, e2485; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2421 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source LSEG analysts ; from gnews:58fb7b4d430994417016dc48 2026-08-25] LSEG analysts expected NVIDIA total revenue to grow 83% to $396 billion in FY2027, then grow 44% in FY2028.\\n    Quote: For the full year, analysts expect 83% revenue growth to $396 billion, before slowing to 44% expansion next year, according to LSEG.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2268, e2697; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2491 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-08-25; from gnews:30a741ab577591d30575d0c8 2026-08-25] The author says NVIDIA\\u2019s trailing-12-month revenue exceeds $250 billion and is nearly twice its level a year earlier.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's trailing 12-month revenue is more than $250 billion, and is nearly double what it was a year ago.\\n- e2493 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-08-25; from gnews:30a741ab577591d30575d0c8 2026-08-25] The author says NVIDIA trades at about 25 times forward earnings, with a 74% gross margin and 85% year-over-year revenue growth in the most recent quarter.\\n    Quote: It trades for about 25 times forward earnings, and this is for a business with a 74% gross margin and an 85% year-over-year revenue growth rate in the most recent quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1335; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2494 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:30a741ab577591d30575d0c8 2026-08-25] The author says NVIDIA has reported an order book worth about $1 trillion across 2026 and 2027.\\n    Quote: Plus, the company has reported an order book worth about $1 trillion across 2026 and 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2244; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2497 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 18% quarter over quarter and 106% year over year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA (NASDAQ: NVDA) today reported revenue for the second quarter ended July 26, 2026, of $96.2 billion, up 18% from the previous quarter and up 106% from a year ago.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2515, e2573, e2666, e2709, e2722, e2853; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2499 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2518, e2566; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2531 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 total revenue of $96 billion, more than double the prior-year quarter.\\n    Quote: Total revenue of $96 billion more than doubled year-over-year as growth accelerated for the fourth consecutive quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2515, e2654, e2953; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2666 [third_party_view; zacks.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA; Zacks ; from gnews:3bf42a544ef77114bed3c232 2026-08-26] NVIDIA's quarterly earnings increased 120% year over year and revenue increased 106%; Zacks had expected earnings growth of 99% and revenue growth of 96.5%.\\n    Quote: This marks a +120% year-over-year increase on the bottom line and +106% on the top. Expectations had been for +99% earnings growth and +96.5% on revenues.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2497; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2697 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source CNBC 2026-08-26; from gnews:6ac7c90b82734575d8de60bf 2026-08-26] CNBC calculated that NVIDIA revenue would reach $673 billion in FY2028 if FY2027 revenue is $396 billion, the consensus projection.\\n    Quote: Based on the consensus projection for revenue of $396 billion for fiscal 2027, which ends in January, sales next year would hit $673 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2421; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2762 [fact; zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1de23b28ee2875342ea8aa8a 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $215.9 billion for FY2026, up 65% from FY2025.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported revenues of $215.9 billion in fiscal 2026, up 65% from $130.5 billion in fiscal 2025.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2001, e3102; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2772 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com 2026-08-26; from gnews:fba5b7a8754c89bba4190c96 2026-08-26] Analysts\\u2019 consensus revenue estimate is $92.18 billion, versus $46.74 billion a year earlier, implying 97% sales growth for the period being discussed.\\n    Quote: Analysts who cover the stock are looking for a little more, with the consensus estimate coming in at $92.18 billion versus $46.74 billion a year ago \\u2013 essentially sales growth of 97%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2773 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:fba5b7a8754c89bba4190c96 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported fiscal first-quarter revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85% year over year, and net income of $58.32 billion, up 211%.\\n    Quote: In the fiscal first quarter, Nvidia's revenue was $81.61 billion, up 85% from a year ago, and net income was $58.32 billion, up 211% from the previous year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e254; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2975 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Gil Luria ; from gnews:4145b3f0ea72f5cfb0e25301 2026-08-27] D.A. Davidson technology research head Gil Luria questioned how large the AI market would be without NVIDIA funding its customers.\\n    Quote: D.A. Davidson's Head of Technology Research, Gil Luria, raised a similar fear about how durable AI demand is, given Nvidia's financer status. In a New York Times interview, Luria questioned, \\\"How big would the market be if Nvidia weren't funding its customers?\\\"\\n- e2976 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:4145b3f0ea72f5cfb0e25301 2026-08-27] Anthropic said it is targeting a total addressable market of $30 trillion in revenue.\\n    Quote: Anthropic said it's targeting an incredible total addressable market of $30 trillion in revenue\\n- e2988 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7450fcffe5e33aadd1b4a7e9 2026-08-27] NVIDIA reported quarterly revenue of $96 billion, up 106% year over year.\\n    Quote: Last quarter, Nvidia's revenue grew 106% year over year to $96 billion, driven by rising demand for data center chips for AI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2594, e3170; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2989 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7450fcffe5e33aadd1b4a7e9 2026-08-27] NVIDIA expects Q3 revenue of $108 billion; the author says this puts FY2027 revenue on track to exceed $400 billion if Q4 grows further.\\n    Quote: In Q3, it expects to generate $108 billion in sales, putting it on track to exceed $400 billion in total revenue in fiscal year 2027, provided Q4 shows further phenomenal growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3296; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e3108 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Colette Kress 2026-08-26; from gnews:b39d8c4b1f73b622347a66e4 2026-08-28] CFO Colette Kress guided to 70% growth for NVIDIA revenue for the full fiscal year; the article says this was ahead of analyst estimates.\\n    Quote: As if this weren't enough, Kress guided 70% growth for the full fiscal year, which was ahead of analyst estimates.\\n- e3109 [third_party_view; InsiderMonkey, recounting Steve Eisman\\u2019s remarks on CNBC\\u2019s Fast Money; original source Steve Eisman 2026-08-13; from gnews:afc4112f1dd4c804f27af950 2026-08-28] Steve Eisman estimated that OpenAI and Anthropic account for roughly 70% of AI-related revenue and 25%\\u201335% of cloud revenue at Microsoft, Amazon, Google, and Oracle.\\n    Quote: OpenAI and Anthropic account for roughly 70% of AI-related revenue at Microsoft, Amazon, Google, and Oracle, and as much as 25% to 35% of those companies' cloud revenue.\\n- e3110 [third_party_view; InsiderMonkey, recounting Steve Eisman\\u2019s remarks on CNBC\\u2019s Fast Money; original source Steve Eisman 2026-08-13; from gnews:afc4112f1dd4c804f27af950 2026-08-28] Steve Eisman warned that problems at OpenAI or Anthropic could let cheaper Chinese open-weight models trigger an industry price war.\\n    Quote: if something goes wrong at either lab, cheaper Chinese open-weight models could spark a price war across the industry.\\n- e3112 [third_party_view; InsiderMonkey, recounting Michael Burry\\u2019s view; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:afc4112f1dd4c804f27af950 2026-08-28] Michael Burry warned that NVIDIA\\u2019s reliance on Microsoft, Meta, Amazon, and Alphabet means a pullback by even one could meaningfully hurt revenue.\\n    Quote: Michael Burry argues Nvidia's own customer base carries a version of Eisman's concentration risk. He has focused on Nvidia's reliance on a small group of hyperscale customers, including Microsoft, Meta, Amazon, and Alphabet, warning a pullback from even one could meaningfully hurt revenue.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 7]\\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-08-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or Chinese authorization deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no authorization by the due date. The evidence implies a limited, case-by-case authorization is more likely than no authorization at all by 2027-01-31\\u2014not blanket access. Reports of \\n- v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: No explicit market consensus or Senate timetable is supplied. The observable reference is that RASA had not passed the Senate as of August 19, which sits at rung 2. The alleged remote-access cases could add pressure for legislation, but the recent evidence reports no Senate action or scheduled vote.\\n- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan's Ministry of Economic Affairs introduce performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evi\\n- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan introduce a criminal ban on AI chip exports to China by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The status-quo reference is rung 2, and the evidence still makes no ban by the deadline more likely than a ban. Taiwan is considering a criminal ban, and the alleged export case\\u2014along with lawmakers\\u2019 and experts\\u2019 stated view that the law needs to change\\u2014adds pressure toward legislation. But the supp\\n- v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced U.S. chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is rung 2: the investigation is formally underway and described as likely to remain an ongoing point of contention, with no Commerce deadline or consensus supplied. The access allegations and Taiwanese smuggling enforcement show the issue remains active, but they do not report a Commer\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-08-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the four-hyperscaler estimate of about $760 billion, which sits on rung 4 ($700 billion to below $800 billion). The evidence still implies roughly $760 billion: the direct four-company estimate remains the best fit, while the roughly $800 billion NVIDIA figure is for the top five an\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion; judged 2026-08-28; reasons: The market reference is Bank of America's $1.08 trillion calendar-2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The higher forecast is NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion for the top five hyperscalers in 2027. A rough scope adjustment using the disclosed 2026 figures\\u2014more than $730 billion for the four companies versus \\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, or 83% growth, which is rung 1. The reported first two quarters and Q3 guidance already total about $285.8 billion; carrying the Q3 sequential growth rate (108/96.2, about 12.3%) into Q4 implies roughly $121.3 billion in Q4, or about $407 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies about 88.5% growth, comfortably above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The reported acceleration, Q3 guide, and the specific estimate that revenue could exceed $400 billion if Q4 grows further support that run-rate.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"old-3\", 3348, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v3 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA total revenue\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA report as total revenue for FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion (110% of the $392 billion analyst estimate). / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion (within 10% below the analyst estimate). / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion (more than 20% below the analyst estimate).\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 145 items]\\n[Not shown: 924 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e25 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] Wall Street analysts estimate NVIDIA total revenue of $392 billion in FY2027 and $554 billion in FY2028, versus trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Quote: Wall Street analysts estimate that Nvidia's business could double over the next couple of years, from trailing-12-month revenue of $253 billion to $392 billion this fiscal year, and $554 billion the following year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e209, e432; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e59 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source CNBC 2026-07-05; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA\\u2019s current-generation Rubin systems were reported to be in full production and scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n- e60 [third_party_view; CNBC, reporting SemiAnalysis\\u2019s projection; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] SemiAnalysis projected NVIDIA data-center compute revenue for the second half of FY2027 to be 20% above Wall Street consensus.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e140, e1116; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e73 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis author; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says its supply-chain tracking indicates NVIDIA\\u2019s second-half 2026 shipment-volume and revenue estimates are materially above general-market estimates.\\n    Quote: Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\\n- e97 [fact; TradingView.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85% year over year, and non-GAAP EPS of $1.87, up 140%.\\n    Quote: First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion, while non-GAAP earnings per share jumped 140% to $1.87.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e35, e326; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e98 [party_statement; TradingView.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA guided Q2 FY2027 revenue to approximately $91 billion, nearly 95% higher year over year.\\n    Quote: For the second quarter of fiscal 2027, NVIDIA projects revenues of approximately $91 billion, representing nearly 95% year-over-year growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114, e258, e347, e392, e1260; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e208 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b40db2c507df72ec016e99e6 2026-07-08] NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, up 65% year over year.\\n    Quote: The company's most recent fiscal 2026 revenue topped $215.9 billion, up 65% year over year and well ahead of the $11.7 billion it posted in 2019.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e703; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e221 [third_party_view; TradingView; original source Bloomberg Intelligence ; from gnews:a09988fd9c5c1a739a14f873 2026-07-08] NVIDIA is projected to report FY2027 profit of $228 billion and sales (total revenue) of $393 billion, representing expected growth of 90% and 82%, respectively.\\n    Quote: company is projected to generate $228 billion in profit on $393 billion in sales in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31, representing expected growth of 90% and 82%, respectively\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e48, e284, e818; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e261 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, supported by multiyear customer commitments.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36, e431, e955, e1506; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e311 [party_statement; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA expects its Vera CPU line to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of FY2027.\\n    Quote: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e545; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e408 [fact; Vandita Jadeja, 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped no H20 compute products to China in Q1 FY2027 and guided that Q2 FY2027 Data Center compute revenue would exclude China.\\n    Quote: There were no H20 compute products shipped to China in Q1 FY27, and the Q2 FY27 guidance excludes any Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e400, e413; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e481 [fact; Tom's Hardware, reporting Reuters; original source U.S. government 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported on 2026-07-14 that ZTE and Maginfra received U.S. approval to purchase Nvidia H200 chips.\\n    Quote: Today, Reuters reports that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra have received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-gen H200 \\\"Hopper\\\" chips.\\n- e484 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware, quoting CNBC's account of a congressional hearing; original source U.S. trade official 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official said on 2026-07-14 that very few shipments had taken place under licenses for H200 chips and equivalents, describing the quantity as very small.\\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \\\"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It\\u2019s a very small quantity of chips\\\" during a congressional hearing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e424, e530, e939; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e522 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-15; from gnews:4e8785bda74ec6e3d7abe4ef 2026-07-15] NVIDIA said it maintained on-time delivery performance across its wafer-to-server supply plans amid demand growth.\\n    Quote: Orchestrated wafer-to-server supply plans across GPUs, switches, NICs, and cables\\u2014keeping delivery performance intact through unprecedented demand growth.\\n- e558 [party_statement; Trefis, quoting NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:48ea00c0bf7c01bdb80850bb 2026-07-15] NVIDIA management said it had visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue for FY2027.\\n    Quote: visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e545, e894, e1040; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e733 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware; original source SK hynix ; from gnews:fbce0469dbb5b33e2c34a0f5 2026-07-18] SK hynix said 2027 would be the worst year of the memory shortage and that the shortage would last until 2030.\\n    Quote: SK hynix, one of the big three memory chip manufacturers, even says that 2027 is set to be the \\u201cworst year\\u201d for the memory shortage and said that the crunch will last until 2030.\\n- e785 [fact; CNBC; original source Meta ; from gnews:b79717064762299fc9426a13 2026-07-20] Meta announced plans to use up to 6 GW of AMD GPUs over time, beginning with 1 GW deployed on Helios racks later in 2026.\\n    Quote: In February, Meta announced it'll use up to 6 gigawatts of AMD GPUs over time, starting with 1 gigawatt deployed on Helios racks later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e574; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e818 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Yahoo Finance (Wall Street average estimates) ; from gnews:0993b79d5b6b817b8d65b011 2026-07-21] Yahoo Finance's average Wall Street estimates put NVIDIA revenue at $393 billion in FY2027 and $559 billion in FY2028.\\n    Quote: According to Wall Street's average estimates (provided by Yahoo! Finance), Nvidia's revenue could grow to $393 billion during its current 2027 fiscal year, and then to $559 billion in fiscal 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e221; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e835 [party_statement; Tom\\u2019s Hardware; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:86fb1717cf3f39b6b9957662 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said Vera Rubin will roll out in the second half of 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin is in production, and will roll out in the second half of 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e743 (2026-07-18); both are in force: TIKR\\u2019s model assumed Vera Rubin would enter production in the second half of 2026 and identified its ramp, hyperscaler spending, and the UAE opening as NVIDIA revenue-growth drivers.\\n- e969 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Anthropic and AMD 2026-07-22; from gnews:78152408aa7a67ee94806acd 2026-07-23] Anthropic and AMD announced a partnership to deploy up to 2 GW of GPUs through AMD\\u2019s Helios rack system.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Anthropic and AMD announced a strategic partnership Wednesday to deploy up to