d AI use at Blackstone portfolio companies had increased sevenfold in 2026.\\n    Quote: Demand for AI is outstripping supply, with use at Blackstone portfolio companies surging sevenfold this year, Gray said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1792; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1722 [fact; Reuters; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-10; from gnews:050de2698429396b00ffe197 2026-08-10] NVIDIA signed MOUs with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to launch compute-financing platforms intended to raise more than $500 billion in third-party capital for AI infrastructure; no deployment timetable was disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said Monday it has partnered with six major financial institutions to launch compute financing platforms aimed at raising over $500 billion in third-party capital for AI infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1718, e1737; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1723 [third_party_view; Reuters; original source Reuters 2026-08-10; from gnews:050de2698429396b00ffe197 2026-08-10] Reuters reported that Big Tech companies\\u2019 combined AI spending is expected to exceed $730 billion in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: Big Tech companies have signaled that spending on AI won\\u2019t slow down, with combined outlays set to surpass $730 billion this year.\\n- e1725 [rumour; scmp.com; original source scmp.com 2026-08-10; from gnews:b628bac1c0f476125421e4f7 2026-08-10] SCMP, citing sources at major Chinese LLM developers, reports that China\\u2019s most advanced AI models are still trained on Nvidia chips because the high cost of switching to local semiconductors is hampering the transition.\\n    Quote: China\\u2019s most advanced artificial intelligence models are still being trained on Nvidia chips, sources at major Chinese large language model (LLM) developers say, as the prohibitively high cost of switching to local semiconductors continues to hamper Beijing\\u2019s push for self-sufficiency .\\n- e1728 [fact; BBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:40ce2efc601a2365b3739101 2026-08-10] NVIDIA said it struck deals with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to raise $500 billion for AI infrastructure projects.\\n    Quote: The chipmaker said it had struck deals with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, and that the investors were for the first time treating AI hardware and infrastructure, often referred to as \\\"compute\\\", as an asset class.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1726, e1753; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1729 [party_statement; BBC; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:40ce2efc601a2365b3739101 2026-08-10] Anthropic said Claude demand requires significant new compute and that it recently entered a deal with Macquarie Asset Management and GIC to invest in AI infrastructure; the deal size was not specified.\\n    Quote: Anthropic also recently entered into a deal with Macquarie Asset Management and GIC, a sovereign wealth fund in Singapore, for its own investment in AI infrastructure.\\nThe company did not specify the size of the deal, but said more financing was needed as its popular chatbot Claude had become so popular that the \\\"demand requires significant new compute\\\".\\n- e1735 [fact; BBC; original source Nvidia 2026-08-10; from gnews:04a61e80230f06330016870f 2026-08-10] Nvidia said it struck deals with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to raise $500bn in capital for AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has teamed up with some of Wall Street's largest banks and investors to raise $500bn (\\u00a3370bn) in capital for artificial intelligence infrastructure. The chipmaker said it had struck deals with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, and that the investors were for the first time treating AI hardware and infrastructure, often referred to as \\\"compute\\\", as an asset cl\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1714; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1736 [fact; BBC; original source BBC 2026-08-10; from gnews:04a61e80230f06330016870f 2026-08-10] Technology and AI companies collectively spent over $1tn on AI projects and infrastructure over the three years preceding the report; the BBC said more spending was expected.\\n    Quote: Such companies have collectively spent over $1tn in just three years on AI projects and infrastructure, with much more spending expected.\\n- e1756 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-11; from nvblog:nvidia-ai-factory-compute 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported that one-year H100 rental pricing rose from about $1.70 per GPU-hour in October 2025 to about $2.35 per GPU-hour in March 2026.\\n    Quote: One-year H100 rental pricing rose from about $1.70 per GPU-hour in October 2025 to about $2.35 per GPU-hour in March 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1778, e1853; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1757 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-11; from nvblog:nvidia-ai-factory-compute 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported that cross-provider on-demand median GPU pricing rose from roughly $2.00 per GPU-hour in October 2025 to $2.70 in June 2026.\\n    Quote: Cross-provider on-demand median pricing rose from roughly $2.00 per GPU-hour in October 2025 to $2.70 in June 2026.\\n- e1758 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-11; from nvblog:nvidia-ai-factory-compute 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported B200 cloud rates of approximately $5.30 to $7.05 per GPU-hour, describing Blackwell capacity as commanding a premium.\\n    Quote: Blackwell capacity commands a premium, with reported B200 cloud rates spanning approximately $5.30 to $7.05 per GPU-hour.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1854; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1766 [third_party_view; Investing.com, quoting Bloomberg Intelligence analyst Vasu Kasibhotla; original source Bloomberg Intelligence analyst Vasu Kasibhotla ; from gnews:59a612d6bcf31d162a9a3a8c 2026-08-11] Vasu Kasibhotla warned that financing could pull accelerator demand forward rather than signal broad weakness in prime AI infrastructure, and raised concerns about backlog quality and risk if usage falls short of expectations.\\n    Quote: Vasu Kasibhotla warns that the central danger may be that financing simply \\\"pulls accelerator demand forward, rather than signaling broad weakness in prime AI infrastructure,\\\" while raising questions about backlog quality and who bears risk if actual usage trails expectations.\\n- e1767 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2e751a0d48924fef38cdaace 2026-08-11] Nvidia released its Nemotron 3.5 Lightning AI model, which the company says can run on one GPU on a PC.\\n    Quote: Less than three weeks later, Nvidia is releasing Nemotron 3.5 Lightning, which the company says is \\\"lightweight\\\" and can run on a single graphics processing unit on a PC.\\n- e1768 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2e751a0d48924fef38cdaace 2026-08-11] Nvidia made Nemotron 3.5 Lightning free for companies to download, use, and modify.\\n    Quote: The new Nemotron offering is free for companies to download, use and modify without getting permission or paying Nvidia.\\n- e1769 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2e751a0d48924fef38cdaace 2026-08-11] Nvidia launched an AI safety consortium, including Microsoft, focused on using open models and open-source software for cybersecurity.\\n    Quote: Days later, Nvidia launched an AI safety consortium with companies including Microsoft that would focus on using open models and open-source software for cybersecurity.\\n- e1770 [fact; CNBC; original source Meta 2026-08-10; from gnews:2e751a0d48924fef38cdaace 2026-08-11] Meta released its Muse Spark coding model on Monday, as CEO Mark Zuckerberg advocated for open-source AI.\\n    Quote: On Monday, Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg published a lengthy manifesto arguing for open-source AI, as his company released a coding model called Muse Spark .\\n- e1771 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2e751a0d48924fef38cdaace 2026-08-11] Nvidia released NeMo Switchyard software to select the cheapest and most appropriate AI model for a task.\\n    Quote: Additionally, Nvidia released software called NeMo Switchyard that can determine the cheapest and most appropriate AI model for any given task.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1804; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1778 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang ; from gnews:41009a91b20c262d2de41f25 2026-08-11] Jensen Huang noted that NVIDIA H100 rental rates rose from about $1.70 per GPU-hour in late 2025 to about $2.35 per GPU-hour in 2026, amid hyperscaler capacity expansion.\\n    Quote: Driven by scarcity as hyperscalers race to build out capacity, rental rates for Nvidia's H100 chips rose from roughly $1.70 per GPU-hour in late 2025 to about $2.35 per GPU-hour this year, Huang noted .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1756; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1789 [party_statement; Futurum Group, recounting Szlezak\\u2019s statement; original source Waldemar Szlezak, Global Head of Digital Infrastructure at KKR ; from gnews:fad9b5d27a8c0929b5daebc5 2026-08-11] KKR\\u2019s global head of digital infrastructure Waldemar Szlezak said NVIDIA A100 GPUs remain revenue-generating at high utilization six or seven years after launch.\\n    Quote: Waldemar Szlezak, Global Head of Digital Infrastructure at KKR, supplied the supporting evidence for long collateral life, noting that A100 GPUs remain revenue-generating with high utilization six or seven years after launch.\\n- e1791 [party_statement; Futurum Group, recounting Szlezak\\u2019s statement; original source Waldemar Szlezak ; from gnews:fad9b5d27a8c0929b5daebc5 2026-08-11] KKR\\u2019s Waldemar Szlezak said token prices had fallen 99%.\\n    Quote: token prices, which Szlezak notes have already fallen 99%,\\n- e1792 [party_statement; Futurum Group, recounting Gray\\u2019s statement; original source Jon Gray ; from gnews:fad9b5d27a8c0929b5daebc5 2026-08-11] Blackstone\\u2019s Jon Gray said LLM usage across Blackstone portfolio companies increased sevenfold in six months.\\n    Quote: demand that Gray measured as a sevenfold increase in LLM usage across Blackstone portfolio companies in six months.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1720; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1794 [third_party_view; Futurum Group, recounting Becky Quick\\u2019s statement; original source Becky Quick ; from gnews:fad9b5d27a8c0929b5daebc5 2026-08-11] CNBC\\u2019s Becky Quick cited $35 billion of Apollo and Blackstone financing for Broadcom-linked infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Quick citing Apollo and Blackstone financing for Broadcom-linked infrastructure at $35 billion,\\n- e1802 [fact; siliconangle.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-11; from gnews:1b482236073ca94c0a9df31f 2026-08-11] NVIDIA announced the release of Nemotron 3.5 Lightning and NeMo Switchyard on 2026-08-11.\\n    Quote: Nvidia Corp. today announced two new services: a highly customizable Nemotron model and an agentic AI model router named NeMo Switchyard.\\n- e1803 [fact; siliconangle.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-11; from gnews:1b482236073ca94c0a9df31f 2026-08-11] NVIDIA released Nemotron 3.5 Lightning, an open model for high-volume tasks powering always-on agents, on 2026-08-11.\\n    Quote: Nemotron 3.5 Lightning joins the open model family to support high-volume tasks when powering always-on agents.\\n- e1804 [fact; siliconangle.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-11; from gnews:1b482236073ca94c0a9df31f 2026-08-11] NVIDIA released NeMo Switchyard, an open-source library that routes AI-agent prompts among models based on task needs.\\n    Quote: NeMo Switchyard is an open-source model routing library for AI agents that takes into account what numerous AI models are available and their capabilities, and routes prompts to the most capable and efficient model available at each step of the agent workflow based on specific needs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1771; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1805 [party_statement; siliconangle.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-11; from gnews:1b482236073ca94c0a9df31f 2026-08-11] NVIDIA said it is working with Boomi, Cadence, Classmethod, Cognition AI, Kong, LangChain, Nous Research and Siemens on intelligent AI model routing alongside developers\\u2019 existing tools.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said it\\u2019s working with a number of partners across the AI ecosystem on intelligent AI model routing alongside tools developers already use, including Boomi LP, Cadence Design Systems Inc., Classmethod Inc., Cognition AI Inc., Kong Inc., Langchain Inc., Nous Research Inc. and Siemens AG.\\n- e1808 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $81.6 billion in FY2027 Q1, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia generated $81.6 billion in total revenue during its fiscal 2027 first quarter (ended May 28), an 85% increase from the year-ago period.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e704; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1809 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] NVIDIA's data center segment generated $75.2 billion of revenue in FY2027 Q1, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: The data center segment alone accounted for $75.2 billion of that revenue, and it grew at an even faster pace of 92%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e346; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1810 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] NVIDIA guided to FY2027 Q2 total revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\n    Quote: According to the company's own guidance, the upcoming second-quarter report on Aug. 26 could show $91 billion in total revenue (plus or minus 2%)\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e985; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1811 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-08-11; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] The author reports average analyst expectations of $91.8 billion in NVIDIA FY2027 Q2 revenue.\\n    Quote: the average analyst expects $91.8 billion in second-quarter revenue.\\n- e1812 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-08-11; from gnews:c8d3108acfbd2f30deaf6fb2 2026-08-11] Analysts expect NVIDIA to guide to about $103.1 billion in FY2027 Q3 total revenue.\\n    Quote: Analysts believe the company will forecast around $103.1 billion in total revenue for the fiscal 2027 third quarter\\n- e1816 [third_party_view; Network World; original source Mark Tauschek, Info-Tech Research Group ; from gnews:b60bc5a0f82bac5679239ec6 2026-08-11] Info-Tech Research Group distinguished analyst Mark Tauschek projected that enterprise costs would rise 15%\\u201320% because of the financing initiative; the period was not specified.\\n    Quote: In fact, for enterprises, Tauschek projected a 15%-20% cost hike.\\n- e1817 [third_party_view; Network World; original source Sanchit Vir Gogia, Greyhound Research ; from gnews:b60bc5a0f82bac5679239ec6 2026-08-11] Greyhound Research chief analyst Sanchit Vir Gogia estimated that AI chip prices would stay roughly unchanged for another year, with good prices increasingly requiring long-term customer commitments.\\n    Quote: He estimated that AI chip prices would be roughly the same for another year, and increasingly only at a good price if customers sign a long-term commitment.\\n- e1818 [third_party_view; Justin Greis, quoted by Network World; original source Justin Greis, Acceligence ; from gnews:b60bc5a0f82bac5679239ec6 2026-08-11] Acceligence CEO Justin Greis expected the financing to accelerate AI infrastructure creation but not provide meaningful near-term enterprise price relief, forecasting elevated costs and constrained availability for the next 12\\u201318 months.\\n    Quote: \\u201cMy view is that this financing will accelerate the creation of AI infrastructure, but it will not provide meaningful near-term price relief for most enterprises. In fact, I think the next 12-18 months could remain a period of elevated costs and constrained availability as the market absorbs this new wave of investment,\\u201d Greis said.\\n- e1835 [fact; wccftech.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:b3a677e25006d94d54229948 2026-08-11] Meta is releasing its Muse Glimmer model, optimized for NVIDIA RTX and DGX systems and also supporting Jetson platforms.\\n    Quote: Meta is also releasing Muse Glimmer, its latest 30-billion open-weight model with a 120K+ context video, which is purpose-built for coding and Agentic AI. The model is optimized for NVIDIA's RTX & DGX systems while also supporting NVIDIA's Jetson platforms, driving over 200 TPS on a single RTX 5090 GPU.\\n- e1836 [fact; The Spokesman-Review, reporting Reuters\\u2019 account; original source IBM and Together AI 2026-08-11; from gnews:0b8a5723ac1ab02ac7e9f0a3 2026-08-11] IBM and Together AI signed a $240 million multi-year agreement to build a U.S. IBM Cloud AI inference cluster using NVIDIA systems, initially with about 2,000 Blackwell 300 chips.\\n    Quote: IBM and startup Together AI have signed a $240 million multi-year agreement to build a large-scale artificial intelligence cluster \\u200bon IBM Cloud using Nvidia systems, the companies said on Tuesday.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1870; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1837 [party_statement; Kai Mak, quoted by Reuters; original source Kai Mak, Together AI\\u2019s chief revenue officer ; from gnews:0b8a5723ac1ab02ac7e9f0a3 2026-08-11] Together AI chief revenue officer Kai Mak said the initial NVIDIA-powered cluster was expected to be sold out two to three months before service and fully taken up well before it was ready.\\n    Quote: \\u201cWe think this will be sold out at least two \\u2060to three months ahead of time,\\u201d Mak added. \\u201cWe\\u2019ll have full offtake well before it\\u2019s ready for service.\\u201d\\n- e1838 [third_party_view; Alex Sirois; original source Alex Sirois 2026-08-11; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] The author estimates NVIDIA has over 80% share of the high-end AI accelerator market; no period is specified.\\n    Quote: NVDA commands over 80% of the high-end AI accelerator market\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1216; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1839 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.23% year over year and 3.16% above estimates.\\n    Quote: Q1 FY27 revenue landed at $81.615 billion, up 85.23% year over year, beating estimates by 3.16%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e774; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1840 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:85df0dfbd885d4e92e530768 2026-08-11] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Dat