nce points to $91.0 billion in revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e349, e540, e606; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e516 [party_statement; Kyodo News; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO 2026-07-16; from gnews:8fa80c5fdfe362f5c1cc1452 2026-07-16] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said on July 16, 2026, that NVIDIA would help Japan develop a domestic AI foundation model by providing advanced AI chips.\\n    Quote: Nvidia Corp. CEO Jensen Huang said Thursday his company will help Japan develop a domestic artificial intelligence foundation model by providing advanced AI chips as part of its deeper ties with the country's technology industry.\\n- e527 [third_party_view; ifre.com; original source ifre.com 2026-07-16; from gnews:dc1bf55ec327aa54f2561647 2026-07-16] Rental rates for older-generation NVIDIA H100 cards have rebounded in 2026 from their 2024\\u20132025 lows.\\n    Quote: While bankers also noted that GPUs have been considered as short-lived and weak collateral, there is room for optimism as rental rates for older\\u2011generation Nvidia H100 cards have rebounded this year from the lows of 2024\\u20132025.\\n- e530 [fact; Healthcare IT Today; original source Verkada 2026-07-01; from gnews:e71c7f52a1d727e1387644cd 2026-07-16] Verkada announced a collaboration with NVIDIA to develop and deploy physical AI across the built environment.\\n    Quote: Verkada , a leader in AI-powered physical security and operations, today announced a collaboration with NVIDIA to accelerate the development and deployment of physical AI across the built environment.\\n- e531 [party_statement; AFP; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:2e9d5650d9e525482062cc00 2026-07-16] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the AI technology cycle was at its beginning and that technology cycles typically last 10 to 15 years before plateauing.\\n    Quote: \\\"Most technology cycles last anywhere from 10 to 15 years before it kind of plateaus. We are at the beginning of this one,\\\" the US chip giant's CEO told reporters in Tokyo.\\n- e532 [party_statement; AFP; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:2e9d5650d9e525482062cc00 2026-07-16] Jensen Huang said AI demand would never decline, comparing it with electricity.\\n    Quote: \\\"Is it going to flatten out? It will never go down, just as electricity never went down,\\\" he said, adding that semiconductors will be the world's largest industry, as part of its \\\"social fabric.\\\"\\n- e533 [fact; AFP; original source Kawasaki Heavy Industries ; from gnews:2e9d5650d9e525482062cc00 2026-07-16] NVIDIA and Kawasaki Heavy Industries announced they will co-develop AI-powered robots for shipbuilding.\\n    Quote: Nvidia and Kawasaki Heavy Industries will co-develop AI-powered robots for use in shipbuilding, the Japanese firm said Thursday.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e536; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e535 [fact; AFP; original source NVIDIA and Fujitsu ; from gnews:2e9d5650d9e525482062cc00 2026-07-16] NVIDIA announced a robotics collaboration with Fujitsu and other parties.\\n    Quote: Other tie-ups announced included a collaboration on robotics with Japan's Fujitsu and others.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e487; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e536 [fact; The Standard; original source Kawasaki Heavy Industries 2026-07-16; from gnews:2f4507655e71fa84c802639f 2026-07-16] NVIDIA and Kawasaki Heavy Industries announced plans to co-develop AI-powered shipbuilding robots on 2026-07-16.\\n    Quote: Nvidia and Kawasaki Heavy Industries will co-develop AI-powered robots for use in shipbuilding, the Japanese firm said Thursday during a visit to Tokyo by the US chip giant's CEO Jensen Huang.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e533; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e539 [fact; 247Wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA 2026-05-20; from gnews:3cfa18ac4b56d1885013308d 2026-07-16] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.23% year over year.\\n    Quote: Q1 FY2027, reported on May 20, 2026, delivered: revenue of $81.615 billion grew 85.23% year over year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e345; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e540 [party_statement; 247Wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA management 2026-05-20; from gnews:3cfa18ac4b56d1885013308d 2026-07-16] NVIDIA guided to Q2 FY2027 revenue of $91.0 billion.\\n    Quote: management guided Q2 to $91.0 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e507; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e548 [fact; 247Wallst author Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2b5982d17c88b8e7433e1e15 2026-07-16] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year.\\n    Quote: Q1 fiscal 2027 revenue landed at $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e281; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e553 [fact; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0e5b33a0b98c921c5e5db08b 2026-07-16] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $81.6 billion in Q1 FY2027.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA just posted $81.6 billion in Q1 FY27 revenue\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e269, e571; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e554 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0e5b33a0b98c921c5e5db08b 2026-07-16] NVIDIA guided to Q2 FY2027 revenue of $91 billion, excluding China.\\n    Quote: Guidance for Q2 FY27 is $91 billion, and it excludes China entirely.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e240; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e561 [party_statement; arabnews.jp; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-16; from gnews:663af037ff2294d1ed81cd11 2026-07-16] NVIDIA said on July 16, 2026, that it was partnering with Fanuc and Yaskawa Electric to advance robotics and AI development.\\n    Quote: Nvidia \\u200csaid on Thursday it was partnering with Japanese companies including Fanuc and Yaskawa Electric to advance \\u200bthe development of robotics and AI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e424; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e562 [fact; arabnews.jp; original source ASML 2026-07-15; from gnews:663af037ff2294d1ed81cd11 2026-07-16] ASML raised its sales forecast and pledged a capacity expansion on July 15, 2026.\\n    Quote: Investors are weighing the strength of the AI investment cycle, with chipmaking equipment maker ASML on Wednesday raising its sales forecast and pledging capacity expansion.\\n- e563 [third_party_view; arabnews.jp; original source arabnews.jp ; from gnews:663af037ff2294d1ed81cd11 2026-07-16] TSMC was expected to post a fifth consecutive quarter of record earnings on July 16, 2026, attributed to the AI boom.\\n    Quote: TSMC, the world\\u2019s leading contract chipmaker, is expected to post a fifth consecutive quarter of record earnings on \\u200bThursday due \\u200bto the AI boom.\\n- e568 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-16; from gnews:bc598c1ba255001385a08723 2026-07-17] NVIDIA announced an expansion of its AI footprint in Japan through new partnerships.\\n    Quote: The company even announced yesterday that it is expanding its AI footprint in Japan with new partnerships.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e469; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e569 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:03f49670bc6aa3ce8c653170 2026-07-17] NVIDIA reported year-over-year revenue growth of 85% in its latest quarter.\\n    Quote: Despite reporting 85% yearly revenue growth in its latest quarter\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e223; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e571 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:03f49670bc6aa3ce8c653170 2026-07-17] NVIDIA generated $81.6 billion in total revenue in FY2027 Q1, ended April 26, 2026.\\n    Quote: the company generated $81.6 billion in revenue in the first quarter of fiscal 2027 (ended April 26).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e553; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e572 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts ; from gnews:03f49670bc6aa3ce8c653170 2026-07-17] Analysts forecast NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue to increase 82%.\\n    Quote: analysts forecast an 82% revenue surge for fiscal 2027\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e574 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:e33eeae2e6a898bcf6f75ab6 2026-07-17] Analysts expected NVIDIA to deliver nearly 100% growth in Q2 2026; the article does not specify the growth measure.\\n    Quote: That trend is expected to last through at least Q2, with analysts expecting nearly 100% growth from Nvidia during Q2.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e577 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c03377d9fbbf30b7a0933cd5 2026-07-17] NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of close to $215.9 billion, up 65.5% year over year.\\n    Quote: In FY 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, a significant jump of 65.5% over the previous year.\\n- e578 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c03377d9fbbf30b7a0933cd5 2026-07-17] NVIDIA\\u2019s two largest direct customers accounted for 22% and 14% of total sales in the most recent fiscal year.\\n    Quote: Revenue is highly concentrated, with two direct customers accounting for 22% and 14% of total sales in the most recent fiscal year.\\n- e579 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:99d11db52f240a9bdbff8731 2026-07-17] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $81.6 billion in fiscal Q1 2027, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia, which posted $81.6 billion in total revenue for its fiscal first quarter of 2027, an 85% increase from a year earlier.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e254, e601; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e589 [third_party_view; Precedence Research; original source Precedence Research 2026-07-17; from gnews:8104fb6cb8a4bcb37d1fe4b7 2026-07-17] Precedence Research projected the global GPU market at USD 136.06 billion in calendar 2026, with a 32.62% CAGR over 2026\\u20132035.\\n    Quote: The global graphics processing unit (GPU) market was valued at USD 101.54 billion in 2025 and is projected to grow from USD 136.06 billion in 2026 to approximately USD 1,712.56 billion by 2035, registering a CAGR of 32.62% during the forecast period from 2026 to 2035.\\n- e590 [fact; Ece Yildirim, Gizmodo; original source Moonshot 2026-07-16; from gnews:86e5102be4f45f9e4dfcdb9d 2026-07-17] Moonshot debuted its Kimi K3 AI model on Thursday, July 16, 2026; the article says it performed highly across multiple benchmarks compared with costlier frontier American AI models.\\n    Quote: On Thursday, Chinese artificial intelligence startup Moonshot debuted its latest model, Kimi K3, a gigantic soon-to-be open-weight model with 2.8 trillion parameters. The announcement sent waves across the American AI industry, particularly for the model\\u2019s high performance across multiple benchmarks compared to the latest costlier offerings from the frontier American AI companies.\\n- e598 [fact; SCMP.com; original source T-Head 2026-07-18; from gnews:4477f19fd6c69ffba37172af 2026-07-18] Alibaba chip unit T-Head announced that it would make the full SAIL software stack for its Zhenwu AI chips freely available to international developers starting 2026-07-18.\\n    Quote: At the World AI Conference (WAIC) in Shanghai on Saturday, T-Head announced that it was making the full technical stack of SAIL \\u2013 the foundational software architecture for the unit\\u2019s Zhenwu series of AI chips \\u2013 freely available to international developers starting on the same day.\\n- e599 [fact; tikr.com; original source US Commerce Department 2026-07-10; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] On July 10, 2026, the US Commerce Department moved the UAE into its highest-trust export tier, easing export authorization for NVIDIA Blackwell processors to UAE entities.\\n    Quote: On July 10, the US Commerce Department reclassified the United Arab Emirates into its highest-trust export tier, clearing the way for UAE entities to buy NVIDIA\\u2019s most advanced Blackwell processors without the months-long, shipment-by-shipment license reviews that had governed Gulf sales.\\n- e600 [rumour; tikr.com; original source tikr.com ; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] Gulf sovereign wealth funds have reportedly committed about $120 billion to global AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Gulf sovereign wealth has reportedly committed around $120 billion to global AI infrastructure\\n- e601 [fact; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-05-20; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: When NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 on May 20, revenue hit a record $81.6 billion, up 85% from a year earlier\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e579; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e603 [party_statement; tikr.com; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] At a June 2026 conference, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said agentic AI pushes customers toward best-of-breed systems, countering concerns that hardware is becoming interchangeable.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA\\u2019s CFO Colette Kress, Executive Vice President and CFO, met the commoditization fear head-on at the Bank of America technology conference in June. Asked whether hyperscalers grabbing any available compute meant hardware was becoming interchangeable, she said it was \\u201cactually the opposite,\\u201d arguing that agentic AI forces customers toward best-of-breed systems rather than away from them.\\n- e606 [party_statement; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] NVIDIA guided to about $91 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, with results scheduled for August 26, 2026.\\n    Quote: The next real test is August 26, when NVIDIA reports fiscal Q2 2027 against its own guidance of roughly $91 billion in revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e507; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-17\\n    Comparison point stated by that judgement: NVIDIA\\u2019s approximately $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU sales forecast is the market reference and sits at the lower boundary of rung 2.\\n    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): Approximately $19 billion in FY2027\\u2014an analytical estimate just below the company\\u2019s roughly $20 billion target, discounted because the evidence describes offers and customer intent but no quantified shipments or deployments.\\n    Reasons (excerpt): The reference is NVIDIA\\u2019s roughly $20 billion FY2027 sales forecast, which sits at the lower boundary of rung 2. I discount that target slightly: cloud offerings are reported, and a customer plans integration, but there is no disclosed shipment volume, deployment timing, or customer demand figure to validate the target. The favorable demand themes support sales but do not supply the quantified volume needed to move the estimate to $20 billion or more.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 3]\\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-17; reasons: No deadline-specific consensus or company guidance is supplied. The observable status quo\\u2014no established Chinese authorization\\u2014sits at rung 2, but the forecast is for the outcome by 2027-01-31, not the status today. Reports citing the same underlying claim say Chinese officials told named firms they\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-17; reasons: The market reference is around $650 billion for calendar-2026 data-center capex, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still implies roughly $650 billion for the specific data-center measure: the roughly $800 billion estimate is for broader AI infrastructure, and the $725 billion pledge has no stated s\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-16; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, after projected 2026 spending of $650 billion; that reference sits in rung 3. The evidence supports spending above $1 trillion, but does not imply the $1.1 trillion needed to enter rung 4. The qualitative power constrain\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for the year if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU estimate as incremental gives about $406 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and Huang's comments about orders support demand, but do not translate directly into additional FY2027 revenue. Memory-supply constraints and China-related risks temper the outlook, but are not quantified enough to pull the estimate below $400 billion.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"linked-1\", 8761, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 132 items]\\n[Not shown: 51 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e74 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA\\u2019s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\\n    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e169, e278; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e108 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] NVIDIA said its profit-sharing plan reflects a shift in AI demand from model building toward production inference that requires infrastructure to run continuously at scale.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said that this plan highlights a transition in AI demand from model building towards production inference tasks, needing continuous running of infrastructure at scale.\\n- e109 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet ; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA\\u2019s AI computing products.\\n    Quote: The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors.\\n- e114 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SpaceX ; from gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9 2026-07-07] SpaceX plans to use Nvidia chips in its AI1 orbital data-center satellites, with Nvidia set to be an initial hardware supplier.\\n    Quote: That involvement with SpaceX will expand further, as Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers. SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as data centers in space is called AI1 and will use Nvidia chips.\\n- e116 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Micron said tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028.\\n    Quote: tight market conditions could last until 2028\\n- e117 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author said new capacity for many companies in the memory-chip industry would not be online until 2027 or later.\\n    Quote: new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later\\n- e124 [rumour; mlq.ai; original source Reuters, citing three unnamed sources with knowledge of the matter 2026-07-06; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek is developing a custom AI inference chip, according to a Reuters report citing three unnamed sources, creating a potential competitor to NVIDIA GPUs in inference workloads.\\n    Quote: DeepSeek, the Hangzhou-based AI startup behind some of China's most advanced large language models, is developing its own custom AI chip, Reuters reported on Monday, citing three people with knowledge of the matter [1] . The chip is designed for inference \\u2014 the phase where trained models generate responses to user queries \\u2014 rather than for training new models.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e129; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e125 [rumour; mlq.ai; original source Reuters, citing three unnamed sources with knowledge of the matter 2026-07-06; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] DeepSeek has been expanding its chip engineering team and discussing its inference-chip project with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers; the project is described as early-stage.\\n    Quote: The project has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with DeepSeek quietly expanding its chip engineering team without public job postings [2]. The company is in discussions with chip design firms, semiconductor foundries, and memory suppliers, though no specific partners have been named.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130, e131; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e127 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Industry surveys ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, versus roughly 30% currently, potentially reducing demand for NVIDIA accelerators in China.\\n    Quote: Chinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5].\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e128 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Analyst Richard Windey ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Analyst Richard Windey said U.S. export rules have effectively eliminated NVIDIA's China revenue and also constrain DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced chips outside China.\\n    Quote: Analyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3].\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e153 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Chinese company executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they\\u2019ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e155 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, quoting the Bloomberg Intelligence report; original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence report, based on a survey of 60 executives, said China\\u2019s shift from foreign to domestic AI semiconductors is progressing and is likely to benefit Huawei and Hygon.\\n    Quote: \\u201cChina\\u2019s drive to substitute locally made AI-semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic markers such as Huawei and Hygon,\\u201d said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\\n- e156 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence survey expects Nvidia\\u2019s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips become harder to find and local suppliers gain share.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia\\u2019s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm\\u2019s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n- e158 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg News ; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Bloomberg News reported that domestic companies will supply at least 80% of core technologies, including chips, for China\\u2019s data-center initiative.\\n    Quote: At least 80% of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\n- e162 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America reported that NVIDIA\\u2019s sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year over year, nearly twice the growth in cloud capital expenditures.\\n    Quote: The report also says Nvidia\\u2019s hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, suggesting wallet-share gains rather than share loss.\\n- e163 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America modeled NVIDIA retaining roughly 65% to 70% or more of AI capital expenditures over time, with the remainder going to ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n    Quote: BofA still models Nvidia holding roughly 65% to 70% plus of AI capex over time, with the rest split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n- e170 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management guided to accelerating Data Center segment revenue growth in the second quarter; no amount is stated.\\n    Quote: management guided for further revenue growth acceleration for the second quarter.\\n- e171 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across calendar 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e304; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e201 [fact; 247wallst.com, reporting NVIDIA's figures; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year; networking revenue grew 199%.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year, and networking alone grew 199%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161, e205, e210, e288, e508; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e202 [party_statement; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA guided to total revenue of $91 billion for Q2 FY2027.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance points to $91 billion in revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e204 [fact; David Moadel, citing NVIDIA\\u2019s reported results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, including Data Center revenue of $75.2 billion.\\n    Quote: The company reported record revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, with Data Center revenue reaching $75.2 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161, e254, e458, e492; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e227 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI starting late 2026 and continuing into 2027.\\n    Quote: However, Broadcom is also making custom AI chips for Meta Platforms , Anthropic, and OpenAI, and production for those chips is scheduled to ramp up late this year and into next.\\n- e239 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 Data Center revenue of $75.2 billion, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue climbed 92% YOY to $75.2 billion, highlighting that AI infrastructure remains the company's primary growth engine.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e242 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix reported quarterly revenue of about $35 billion, up 198% year over year and a record.\\n    Quote: In the recent quarter, for example, the company reported revenue growth of 198% to about $35 billion dollars, a record level.\\n- e243 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix reported that quarterly net income rose 398% year over year.\\n    Quote: Net income soared 398%\\n- e244 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix reported a 72% operating margin in the recent quarter.\\n    Quote: operating margin came in at 72%.\\n- e245 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Counterpoint Research ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] Counterpoint Research estimated SK Hynix held 58% of the global HBM market at the end of the first quarter.\\n    Quote: SK Hynix held 58% share globally of the HBM market as of the end of the first quarter, according to Counterpoint Research.\\n- e246 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source SK Hynix and NVIDIA ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] SK Hynix and NVIDIA signed a multi-year technology partnership under which SK Hynix will supply memory for areas including AI infrastructure, personal AI, and robotics.\\n    Quote: The two companies even reinforced their relationship recently, signing a multi-year technology partnership. It involves SK Hynix supplying memory across a broad range of areas, from AI infrastructure to personal AI and robotics .\\n- e247 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted by fool.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:03c38d2fe2e6e02ba5736307 2026-07-12] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said SK Hynix has been NVIDIA's largest memory partner and will continue to be its largest memory partner.\\n    Quote: \\\"SK Hynix has been Nvidia's largest memory partner and will continue to be our largest memory partner,\\\" Nvidia chief Jensen Huang said at the time.\\n- e249 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:635f19578778394086009f6f 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $75.2 billion in its fiscal quarter ended in late April 2026, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data center revenue of $75.2 billion climbed 92%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e210; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e254 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported record FY2027 first-quarter revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: During the fiscal first quarter of 2027, Nvidia generated record revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year-over-year (YOY).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e204, e269, e272, e277, e340; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e255 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management guided FY2027 Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, assuming no China Data Center revenue.\\n    Quote: Looking ahead, management guided Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202, e271, e292, e332, e350; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e265 [fact; gurufocus.com, citing The Information; original source Google ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Google approached neocloud companies about using its TPUs; the item gives no date or scale.\\n    Quote: Google has approached so-called neocloud companies about using its tensor processing units, or TPUs, according to a report from The Information.\\n- e266 [party_statement; gurufocus.com, relaying a reported statement by Nscale; original source Nscale ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Nscale told Google that its active projects remain centered on GPU capacity; the item gives no date or scale.\\n    Quote: One provider, Nscale, reportedly told Google that its active projects remain centered on GPU capacity.\\n- e267 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Google and Blackstone ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Google and Blackstone are working on a new cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms in 2027.\\n    Quote: Google is also working with Blackstone on a new cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms next year.\\n- e270 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3a9054a950c410e249837c91 2026-07-13] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $75 billion for FY2027 Q1, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: The results were driven by record data center revenue of $75 billion, up 92%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e210, e409; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e273 [fact; 247Wallst.com, citing NVIDIA's Q1 FY2027 results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:799ec454c15ddf61f08d042f 2026-07-13] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center segment revenue of $75.246 billion.\\n    Quote: with Data Center at $75.246 billion\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e278 [fact; Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:301b0738dab6ca32e88b019a 2026-07-13] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center segment revenue of $75.25 billion.\\n    Quote: Data Center at $75.25 billion\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e74; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e279 [fact; Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $75.246 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion , up 92% YoY.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e346, e352, e452, e549; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e280 [fact; Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b68c019528300c6a1fcd3515 2026-07-13] NVIDIA reported Data Center networking revenue of $14.8 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center networking alone did $14.8 billion, up 199%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e205, e330; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e288 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year, and Networking growth of 199% for the quarter discussed.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion, up 92%, with Networking exploding 199%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e201, e440; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e289 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped zero H20 chips to China in the quarter discussed, compared with $4.6 billion of H20 shipments a year earlier.\\n    Quote: zero H20 chips shipped to China this quarter, versus $4.6 billion a year ago.\\n- e293 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (headline citing Dan Ives); original source Dan Ives ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Dan Ives said demand for NVIDIA was outpacing supply by 12 to 1.\\n    Quote: Demand Is Outpacing Supply \\u201c12 to 1\\u201d\\n- e294 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, summarizing Morgan Stanley; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in 2027 and add 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity.\\n- e295 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] The author says the four hyperscalers added roughly 6.7 GW of compute capacity in 2025, while Morgan Stanley forecasts Google alone will add 6.8 GW in 2027.\\n    Quote: In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\\n- e296 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, citing Milk Road AI; original source Milk Road AI ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Milk Road AI estimates one GW of AI infrastructure costs $6\\u201311 billion with Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks, about $19 billion with Nvidia GB300-based racks, and $25 billion with Vera Rubin racks.\\n    Quote: Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks. Under Vera Rubin, rack costs jump to $25 billion.\\n- e297 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, summarizing Morgan Stanley; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley says hyperscalers continue expanding proprietary-silicon investment to improve total cost of ownership and reduce dependence on merchant GPU suppliers.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley says hyperscalers continue to expand their investments in proprietary silicon to improve total cost of ownership and reduce dependence on merchant GPU suppliers.\\n- e304 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from the Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, CEO Jensen Huang has said that he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from Nvidia's Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e171; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e307 [fact; Harsh Chauhan, Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported $193.7 billion of Data Center revenue in FY2026.\\n    Quote: For comparison, Nvidia's data center revenue in fiscal 2026 (which ended in January this year) was $193.7 billion.\\n- e308 [fact; Harsh Chauhan, Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA's FY2026 Data Center revenue included $162.3 billion from compute chips, with the remainder from networking components.\\n    Quote: It is worth noting that $162.3 billion of its fiscal 2026 data center revenue came from sales of compute chips, while the rest was from networking components.\\n- e312 [fact; Harsh Chauhan, Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported FY2026 networking revenue of $31.4 billion, up 142% year over year.\\n    Quote: The company reported a 142% year-over-year increase in networking revenue in fiscal 2026 to $31.4 billion.\\n- e313 [fact; Harsh Chauhan, Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported networking revenue of $14.8 billion in the first quarter of FY2027, triple the year-earlier amount.\\n    Quote: It has started fiscal 2027 on a stronger note in this segment, with networking revenue tripling year-over-year to $14.8 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e205, e347; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e325 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), reporting Broadcom's figures and statement; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:cf6b0ccecbe4028542c4e178 2026-07-14] Broadcom reported 48% growth in its most recent quarter and said AI semiconductor revenue rose 143%, above its forecast.\\n    Quote: Its growth rate was 48% in its most recent quarter (which ended May 3). And the company says that semiconductor revenue due to AI rose by 143%, coming in even higher than its forecast.\\n- e326 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Advanced Micro Devices ; from gnews:cf6b0ccecbe4028542c4e178 2026-07-14] AMD reported revenue of $10.3 billion, up 38%, for the period ending March 28, 2026.\\n    Quote: For the period ending March 28, the company's revenue rose by 38% to $10.3 billion.\\n- e327 [party_statement; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source Nokia Oyj ; from gnews:e153ff10e5adda87a52745a4 2026-07-14] Nokia said it developed a commercial AI-driven radio access network platform with NVIDIA, integrating NVIDIA into mobile-network equipment.\\n    Quote: Nokia Oyj says it developed the first commercial artificial intelligence-driven radio access network platform together with Nvidia Corp.\\n- e328 [party_statement; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source Nokia Oyj ; from gnews:e153ff10e5adda87a52745a4 2026-07-14] Nokia said its jointly developed AI-driven radio access network hardware is expected to become available to operators in 2027; it forecast spectrum-efficiency gains of 50%, versus 20% currently achieved.\\n    Quote: Next year, when the hardware will become available to operators, spectrum efficiency gains are expected to reach 50%, while currently 20% have been achieved.\\n- e330 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] NVIDIA Data Center Networking revenue reached $14.8 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: with Data Center Networking alone growing 199% YoY to $14.8 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e280, e541; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e334 [third_party_view; fool.com author Harsh Chauhan; original source Third-party estimates; the reported view that NVIDIA\\u2019s control is slipping is the author\\u2019s. ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Third-party estimates put NVIDIA\\u2019s share of the AI chip market at 80%; the author says that share is gradually slipping.\\n    Quote: The good news is that Nvidia still controls an impressive 80% of the AI chip market, according to third-party estimates. However, its control over this lucrative market is gradually slipping.\\n- e335 [third_party_view; fool.com author Harsh Chauhan; original source Harsh Chauhan 2026-07-14; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] The author estimates Marvell controls 20% to 25% of the custom AI processor market.\\n    Quote: Marvell is one of the key players in custom AI processors, controlling 20% to 25% of this market.\\n- e336 [party_statement; fool.com author Harsh Chauhan; original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it could pursue more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, with potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\\n- e341 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported that Data Center Networking revenue grew 199% year over year in Q1 FY2027, citing InfiniBand, NVLink, and Spectrum-X pull-through.\\n    Quote: with Data Center Networking alone growing 199% on InfiniBand, NVLink, and Spectrum-X pull-through.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e205; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e342 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the buildout of AI factories was accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\n    Quote: \\u201cThe buildout of AI factories, the largest infrastructure expansion in human history, is accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\u201d\\n- e346 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA's Data Center segment generated $75.246 billion in Q1 FY2027 revenue, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center alone contributing $75.246 billion (+92% YoY).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e279; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e347 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported $14.8 billion in networking revenue in Q1 FY2027, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Networking was the sleeper hit at $14.8 billion (+199% YoY), driven by InfiniBand, Spectrum-X, and NVLink.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e313, e453; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e352 [fact; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: with Data Center at $75.25B (+92%).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e279, e552; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e357 [fact; Rest of World; original source Humain ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Humain is building data centers in Riyadh and Dammam that are planned to use several hundred thousand Nvidia chips.\\n    Quote: Humain is also building data centers in Riyadh and Dammam, powered by several hundred thousand Nvidia chips.\\n- e358 [fact; Rest of World; original source G42 ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] G42 is building the Stargate data center in Abu Dhabi; its first stage is planned to run on 400,000 Nvidia chips.\\n    Quote: The UAE is investing on the same scale through its state-backed AI firm G42, which is building a data center in Abu Dhabi called Stargate. Its first stage will run on 400,000 Nvidia chips .\\n- e359 [party_statement; Peng Xiao, quoted by Rest of World; original source Peng Xiao ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] G42 CEO Peng Xiao said in a January interview that Stargate\\u2019s machines would be mostly Nvidia despite G42\\u2019s efforts to spread orders among several suppliers.\\n    Quote: G42 has spent months trying to spread its orders among several suppliers, yet the machines inside Stargate would be \\u201cmostly\\u201d Nvidia, CEO Peng Xiao said in an interview in January.\\n- e360 [third_party_view; Kamil Dimmich, quoted by Rest of World; original source Kamil Dimmich ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Kamil Dimmich said Humain\\u2019s first order from Nvidia was for 18,000 Blackwell chips.\\n    Quote: Humain\\u2019s first order from Nvidia was 18,000 of its newest Blackwell chips \\u2014 the hardware that does that heavy lifting, which no rival can yet match, he said.\\n- e361 [third_party_view; Kamil Dimmich, quoted by Rest of World; original source Kamil Dimmich ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Kamil Dimmich said Qualcomm and Groq chips serve a narrower set of AI tasks, while training powerful models still calls for Nvidia chips.\\n    Quote: The deals give the Gulf chips that can run AI, but for a narrower set of tasks than those from Nvidia. The Qualcomm and Groq chips are built to run finished AI models cheaply, for everyday tasks like answering a user\\u2019s questions, while training a powerful model still calls for Nvidia\\u2019s chips, Kamil Dimmich, a partner at emerging-markets investment firm North of South Capital, told Rest of World .\\n- e362 [third_party_view; Kamil Dimmich, quoted by Rest of World; original source Kamil Dimmich ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Kamil Dimmich said Nvidia chips remain essential for AI training, while increasingly available ASIC designs are cheaper for inference but still require TSMC foundry capacity.\\n    Quote: \\u201cNvidia chips are essential for training,\\u201d Dimmich said. \\u201cFor this type of inference compute, increasingly there are ASIC [application-specific integrated circuit] designs that are cheaper than Nvidia but still need foundry capacity at TSMC.\\u201d\\n- e370 [fact; mlq.ai; original source FBI ; from gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402 2026-07-14] The FBI published an RFI seeking market input for AI supercomputer hardware at its CJIS Division, including Nvidia Blackwell Ultra GPUs; the RFI does not commit the FBI to a procurement.\\n    Quote: The FBI has published a Request for Information on SAM.gov seeking market input on AI supercomputer hardware for its Criminal Justice Information Services (CJIS) Division in Clarksburg, West Virginia. The RFI outlines four distinct categories of accelerator systems spanning training, inference, and analytics workloads \\u2014 including both Nvidia Blackwell Ultra GPUs and Google TPUs [1] .\\n- e371 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Federal government ; from gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402 2026-07-14] Potential federal AI contract awards reached $91.8 billion in 2026, up from $4.6 billion in 2024.\\n    Quote: Potential contract awards ballooned to $91.8 billion, up 1,912% from 2024's $4.6 billion [2] .\\n- e389 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Japan ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Japan launched a government-backed physical AI initiative with NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang participating at its kick-off.\\n    Quote: At the Physical AI Initiative kick-off event, NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang joined Ryosei Akazawa, Japan\\u2019s Minister of Economy, Trade and Industry, as the country launched a government-backed physical AI initiative.\\n- e390 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Astellas ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Astellas has deployed nearly all BioNeMo NIM microservices in NVIDIA\\u2019s digital biology portfolio and is running BioNeMo Agent Toolkit.\\n    Quote: Astellas has deployed nearly all BioNeMo NIM microservices within NVIDIA\\u2019s digital biology portfolio and is running BioNeMo Agent Toolkit, NVIDIA\\u2019s open platform that turns any AI agent into an autonomous life sciences scientist.\\n- e391 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Takeda ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Takeda announced a collaboration with Boltz to deploy the BoltzMol-1 and BoltzProt-1 models across its research organization.\\n    Quote: Takeda recently announced a collaboration with Boltz to deploy the BoltzMol-1 and BoltzProt-1 biomolecular models across its research organization, giving scientists tools for structure prediction, affinity estimation and generative design that integrate into existing discovery workflows.\\n- e392 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Canon and Fujifilm ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Two leading Japanese medical imaging companies are shipping next-generation CT systems built on NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Two of Japan\\u2019s leading medical imaging companies are now shipping next-generation CT systems built on NVIDIA GPUs.\\n- e393 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Fujifilm ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Fujifilm commercialized Japan\\u2019s first whole-body CT system powered by NVIDIA Blackwell.\\n    Quote: Fujifilm has commercialized Japan\\u2019s first whole-body CT system powered by NVIDIA Blackwell, using diffusion-based deep learning reconstruction to improve image quality.\\n- e394 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Mizuho ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Mizuho plans to build what is expected to be Japan\\u2019s largest on-premises financial-industry AI factory, starting with NVIDIA DGX B200 systems and scaling toward a larger cluster.\\n    Quote: Mizuho plans to build what is expected to be the largest on-premises AI factory in Japan\\u2019s financial industry, starting with NVIDIA DGX B200 systems and scaling toward a larger cluster.\\n- e395 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source RIKEN ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Two RIKEN supercomputers driven by NVIDIA GB200 and NVIDIA Quantum-X800 are beginning operations.\\n    Quote: At RIKEN, Japan\\u2019s leading national comprehensive research institute, two supercomputers driven by NVIDIA GB200 and NVIDIA Quantum-X800 are beginning operations.\\n- e396 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source RIKEN ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] RIKEN\\u2019s RIKYU AI-for-science supercomputer is deploying 1,600 NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs using the GB200 NVL4 platform.\\n    Quote: RIKYU, a new supercomputer for \\u201cAI for Science\\u201d development, deploying 1,600 NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs using the GB200 NVL4 platform, will support RIKEN\\u2019s development of open foundation models and contribute to accelerating AI adoption across broad fields, including life sciences, materials science and laboratory automation.\\n- e397 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source RIKEN ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] RIKEN\\u2019s ROQUO quantum-HPC system integrates quantum processors with accelerated computing from 540 NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs using the GB200 NVL4 platform.\\n    Quote: JHPC-quantum GPU supercomputer \\u201cROQUO\\u201d is a quantum-HPC system tightly integrating quantum processors with accelerated computing from 540 Blackwell GPUs accessed through the GB200 NVL4 platform.\\n- e398 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Fujitsu and NVIDIA ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Fujitsu and NVIDIA are investigating CUDA-Q for large-scale quantum-chemistry simulation, and Fujitsu has started a trial of NVQLink.\\n    Quote: Fujitsu and NVIDIA are now investigating efficient ways to use NVIDIA CUDA-Q for large-scale quantum-chemistry simulation. Through the collaboration, Fujitsu has started the trial of NVQLink to determine if it can be utilized to realize efficient control of their quantum-classical hybrid computing environment.\\n- e399 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source SoftBank Corp. ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] SoftBank is using NVIDIA GB200-class infrastructure, RTX PRO-based AI RAN with AI Aerial, and Nemotron-based telecom models for its network.\\n    Quote: SoftBank showed how it\\u2019s already using NVIDIA\\u2019s full stack \\u2014 from GB200-class AI infrastructure and NVIDIA RTX PRO-based AI RAN with the NVIDIA AI Aerial platform to NVIDIA Nemotron-based large telecom models \\u2014 to turn its communications network into an intelligence delivery network.\\n- e400 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA and SoftBank Corp. ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] NVIDIA and SoftBank confirmed joint initiatives using Nemotron and SB Intuitions\\u2019 Sarashina, and physical-AI work with partners including Yaskawa using NVIDIA Cosmos and Isaac GR00T.\\n    Quote: The companies also confirmed joint initiatives to drive Japanese innovation using open models such as NVIDIA Nemotron, together with SB Intuitions\\u2019 homegrown generative AI model series Sarashina, while advancing physical AI with partners such as Yaskawa Electric Corporation using NVIDIA Cosmos and Isaac GR00T.\\n- e401 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source SEGA ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] SEGA will support NVIDIA RTX Spark, including with the upcoming VIRTUA FIGHTER CROSSROADS.\\n    Quote: SEGA will support RTX Spark, giving gamers new ways to experience SEGA\\u2019s iconic franchises, including the upcoming VIRTUA FIGHTER CROSSROADS.\\n- e409 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db8d58512ec8bb97266d4b48 2026-07-15] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $75 billion in fiscal Q1 2027, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: In the first quarter of fiscal 2027, Nvidia's Data Center revenue surged 92% year-over-year (YOY) to $75 billion, fueled by strong Blackwell demand as customers deployed hundreds of thousands of GPUs in what became the fastest product rollout in the company's history.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e270; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e430 [fact; fool.com author; original source Cerebras Systems ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] Cerebras reported first-quarter revenue of $193 million, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: This has translated into growth for Cerebras, with first-quarter revenue soaring 92% to $193 million.\\n- e431 [fact; fool.com author; original source Cerebras Systems ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] Cerebras recently signed a compute deal with OpenAI.\\n    Quote: And the company recently signed key deals with OpenAI for compute\\n- e432 [fact; fool.com author; original source Cerebras Systems and Amazon's cloud unit ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] Cerebras signed a deal with Amazon's cloud unit to make its wafer-scale engine systems more broadly available.\\n    Quote: and with Amazon 's cloud unit to make its WSE systems more broadly available.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e473; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e433 [third_party_view; fool.com author; original source fool.com author 2026-07-15; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] The author says NVIDIA trades at 23 times forward earnings estimates.\\n    Quote: The AI giant is trading at 23x forward earnings estimates\\n- e440 [fact; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:48ea00c0bf7c01bdb80850bb 2026-07-15] NVIDIA Data Center revenue rose 92% year over year in the latest quarter.\\n    Quote: with data center revenue surging 92% year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e288; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e444 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, Rich Duprey; original source 247wallst.com, Rich Duprey 2026-07-15; from gnews:7ce055d995cada5e9a801163 2026-07-15] The author says hyperscalers now view AMD as a strategic second source rather than a backup, after it narrowed the AI gap faster than expected; no period is specified.\\n    Quote: That said, AMD has narrowed the gap at a pace few expected, transforming itself from an AI outsider into a company that hyperscalers now view as a strategic second source rather than a backup plan.\\n- e445 [fact; TweakTown; original source Financial Times ; from gnews:96607f3f5939f53a4b3cdea7 2026-07-15] NVIDIA implemented a whitelist with stricter compliance checks for authorized buyers of its high-end AI accelerators, aimed at preventing diversion to prohibited destinations.\\n    Quote: According to a new report from the Financial Times , the chipmaker has implemented a new \\\"white list\\\" of vetted companies, which have passed stricter compliance checks ensuring NVIDIA's high-end AI accelerators don't end up in places they aren't legally allowed to be.\\n- e446 [fact; TweakTown; original source Financial Times ; from gnews:96607f3f5939f53a4b3cdea7 2026-07-15] NVIDIA's revised authorization restrictions excluded more than half of its previous customers; companies that failed the initial check can reapply.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA's new restrictions notably excluded more than half of NVIDIA's previous customers, and companies that failed the initial compliance check are eligible to reapply.\\n- e452 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA\\u2019s Data Center segment produced $75.246 billion, up 92% YoY,\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e279; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e453 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] NVIDIA networking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion.\\n    Quote: networking alone growing 199% YoY to $14.8 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e347; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e458 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year, and Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion; Networking revenue rose 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia\\u2019s Q1 FY2027 revenue hit $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center alone contributing $75.246 billion and Networking revenue rising 199% YoY.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e204, e550; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e459 [party_statement; Gerelyn Terzo, reporting Huang's remarks; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had more orders than at his previous GTC remarks and expected to keep its supply chain busy for several more years.\\n    Quote: He added that \\u201cWe have more orders today than we did at the last time I spoke about orders at GTC\\u201d and that NVIDIA will \\u201ckeep our supply chain quite busy for several many more years coming.\\u201d\\n- e462 [third_party_view; Gerelyn Terzo, reporting Morgan Stanley's estimate; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] Morgan Stanley raised its Vera Rubin rack-system price assumption to about $49 billion per gigawatt, implying higher customer spending per deployment than for Blackwell.\\n    Quote: The delay narrative that surfaced in early July collided with a more bullish Wall Street read this morning: Morgan Stanley raised its Vera Rubin rack-system price assumption to about $49 billion per gigawatt, implying materially higher customer spending per deployment than Blackwell.\\n- e464 [third_party_view; Gerelyn Terzo; original source Gerelyn Terzo 2026-07-15; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] Gerelyn Terzo judged that if Vera Rubin volumes are large and Morgan Stanley's higher average selling prices hold, current forward estimates may understate NVIDIA's FY2028 earnings power.\\n    Quote: If Huang\\u2019s \\u201cgiant\\u201d volumes materialize on Rubin at Morgan Stanley\\u2019s higher ASPs, current forward estimates likely understate FY2028 earnings power.\\n- e472 [third_party_view; Geoffrey Seiler; original source Geoffrey Seiler 2026-07-16; from gnews:f9a3a3ef9a56e7099140e164 2026-07-16] The author says Cerebras's wafer-scale chip is 6 times faster than NVIDIA's LPUs and 15 times faster than NVIDIA's GPUs.\\n    Quote: The result is a chip that is 6 times faster than Nvidia's LPUs and 15 times faster than its GPUs.\\n- e473 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler; original source Cerebras Systems ; from gnews:f9a3a3ef9a56e7099140e164 2026-07-16] The author reports that Cerebras won a $20 billion deal with OpenAI and signed a deal with Amazon Web Services; no period is specified.\\n    Quote: That said, the company won a $20 billion deal with OpenAI and also has inked a deal with Amazon Web Services.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e432; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e474 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler; original source AMD ; from gnews:f9a3a3ef9a56e7099140e164 2026-07-16] The author says AMD recently acquired MEXT, a memory-optimization software company, which the author says can increase effective memory capacity and reduce costs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, its recent acquisition of memory optimization software company MEXT gives AMD a cost-effective way to increase effective memory capacity and reduce costs.\\n- e476 [rumour; Geoffrey Seiler; original source Geoffrey Seiler 2026-07-16; from gnews:f9a3a3ef9a56e7099140e164 2026-07-16] The author says AMD already has inference deals with OpenAI and Meta Platforms and reports rumors that Anthropic is about to become a big customer; no deal amounts or dates are given.\\n    Quote: It already has some big inference deals in place with OpenAI and Meta Platforms , and there are rumors that Anthropic is about to become a big customer.\\n- e480 [fact; fool.com (Keithen Drury); original source Nvidia and Palantir ; from gnews:e4e37eb5cc64ccfb65317770 2026-07-16] Nvidia and Palantir said they formed a partnership to deploy sovereign AI for government agencies; the item gives no deployment date or scale.\\n    Quote: Nvidia and Palantir said they formed a partnership to deploy sovereign artificial intelligence (AI) for government agencies.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e106; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e481 [third_party_view; fool.com (Keithen Drury); original source Keithen Drury 2026-07-16; from gnews:e4e37eb5cc64ccfb65317770 2026-07-16] Keithen Drury said U.S. federal agencies could spend hundreds of billions of dollars on data centers if AI adoption grows, potentially making the government a large Nvidia client; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: However, AI could become so powerful for the federal government and its agencies that it would need to spend hundreds of billions of dollars on data centers like the AI hyperscalers, which could propel the U.S. government into becoming a large client of Nvidia's.\\n- e483 [rumour; finance.yahoo.com; original source DeepSeek 2026-07-07; from gnews:4edc0959d81f2b32d70e47f9 2026-07-16] DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on Nvidia chips; Reuters reported this on July 7, 2026.\\n    Quote: Chinese tech startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on Nvidia chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e166; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e484 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Richard Windsor, analyst at Radio Free Mobile ; from gnews:4edc0959d81f2b32d70e47f9 2026-07-16] Radio Free Mobile analyst Richard Windsor said NVIDIA's AI chip business in China is at zero and will stay there; the item gives no original statement date.\\n    Quote: Windsor noted that Nvidia is \\\"at zero in China and staying there,\\\" referring to Nvidia's already shrinking business in China due to US restrictions on chip exports.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e485 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Richard Windsor, analyst at Radio Free Mobile ; from gnews:4edc0959d81f2b32d70e47f9 2026-07-16] Radio Free Mobile analyst Richard Windsor said DeepSeek has almost zero chance of challenging NVIDIA outside China, citing China's limited access to advanced chip manufacturing and the time and capital needed to design AI chips; the item gives no original statement date.\\n    Quote: Second, Windsor commented that DeepSeek has almost zero chance of challenging Nvidia outside China. Not only is China shut out of the world's most advanced chip manufacturing capabilities, but designing perfect AI chips takes years and requires massive capital.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e134; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e492 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a2bb93d437d80c346c1d80a4 2026-07-16] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, and Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, up 92%.\\n    Quote: Q1 FY27 revenue hit $81.61 billion , up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion (+92% YoY).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e204; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e501 [fact; Tom's Hardware, reporting NVIDIA's announcement; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-16; from gnews:4cf92ff695f51dbbb675399a 2026-07-16] NVIDIA announced a plan with Japan's Noetra Corp. to build a 140-megawatt AI factory with 27,500 Rubin GPUs and 13,750 Vera CPUs for Japan's FRONTia program; no deployment date was given.\\n    Quote: Nvidia today announced that it's working with Japan's Noetra Corp. to build a 140-megawatt AI factory packing 27,500 Rubin GPUs and 13,750 Vera CPUs, the compute foundation for FRONTia, the Japanese government's state-funded physical AI program.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e466, e504, e534; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e502 [fact; Tom's Hardware, citing Asia Times; original source NEDO 2026-06-30; from gnews:4cf92ff695f51dbbb675399a 2026-07-16] Noetra and AIST won the NEDO tender on June 30 to run FRONTia from fiscal 2026 through fiscal 2030, with \\u00a5387.3 billion in first-year funding and up to \\u00a51 trillion over five years, subject to stage-gate reviews after the first two years.\\n    Quote: Noetra and the national research institute AIST won a NEDO public tender on June 30 to run the FRONTia project from fiscal 2026 through fiscal 2030, with \\u00a5387.3 billion (roughly $2.4 billion) in first-year funding and up to \\u00a51 trillion (roughly $6.1 billion) over five years, Asia Times reported.\\n- e508 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:19d750b9d8c73eecff5a0018 2026-07-16] The author reports NVIDIA Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year, and Data Center Networking revenue of $14.8 billion, up 199%.\\n    Quote: None of them are running Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion at 92% YoY growth, or Data Center Networking at $14.8 billion growing 199%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e201; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e512 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Fireworks ; from gnews:6531767723bdbbe10fb94b8d 2026-07-16] Fireworks said it was generating more than $1 billion in annualized revenue, five times its level the prior year.\\n    Quote: The company, which is backed by Nvidia, is now generating more than $1 billion in annualized revenue, five times more than it had last year.\\n- e513 [fact; CNBC; original source Fireworks ; from gnews:6531767723bdbbe10fb94b8d 2026-07-16] Fireworks raised $1.5 billion at a $17.5 billion valuation; NVIDIA is among the investors in the round.\\n    Quote: Fireworks, a startup whose cloud service runs open-source AI models for software developers, has raised $1.5 billion at a $17.5 billion valuation.\\n- e514 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Lin Qiao, Fireworks CEO ; from gnews:6531767723bdbbe10fb94b8d 2026-07-16] Fireworks CEO Lin Qiao said the service handled 40 trillion AI tokens per day.\\n    Quote: Fireworks now handles 40 trillion AI tokens per day, Qiao said.\\n- e515 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source Fireworks ; from gnews:6531767723bdbbe10fb94b8d 2026-07-16] Fireworks plans to use proceeds from its new financing to obtain more GPUs and hire more technologists.\\n    Quote: The new money will also help Fireworks obtain more GPUs and hire more technologists.\\n- e534 [party_statement; AFP; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2e9d5650d9e525482062cc00 2026-07-16] NVIDIA said it would sell 27,500 Rubin-series AI chips to Noetra for a government-supported robotics AI-model project.\\n    Quote: Nvidia also said Thursday it would sell 27,500 AI chips from its cutting-edge Rubin series to Noetra, a new consortium of firms including SoftBank and Sony, for a government-supported project to develop a homemade AI model for robotics.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e501; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e537 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-16; from gnews:4a689c7ec59054514716a14e 2026-07-16] NVIDIA announced it is working with Noetra to launch a Japan AI factory using 13,750 Vera CPUs and 27,500 Rubin GPUs; the announcement gave no launch date.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA today announced it is working with Noetra Corp. to launch an NVIDIA Vera Rubin AI factory with 13,750 NVIDIA Vera CPUs and 27,500 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs for national physical AI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e466; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e538 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-16; from gnews:4a689c7ec59054514716a14e 2026-07-16] NVIDIA said the planned Noetra AI factory will deliver 140 megawatts of data-center capacity using its Vera Rubin DSX architecture and related networking and DPU products.\\n    Quote: Built on the NVIDIA Vera Rubin DSX AI factory architecture, the AI factory will deliver 140 megawatts of data center capacity combined with the NVIDIA Spectrum-X Ethernet networking platform, NVIDIA BlueField \\u00ae DPUs, and tightly codesigned silicon, systems and software to provide breakthrough AI performance, lower token costs and massive scale for frontier AI training.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e467; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e541 [fact; 247Wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA 2026-05-20; from gnews:3cfa18ac4b56d1885013308d 2026-07-16] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center networking revenue of $14.8 billion, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data center networking alone was $14.8 billion, up 199% year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e330; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e545 [fact; 247Wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source AMD ; from gnews:3cfa18ac4b56d1885013308d 2026-07-16] AMD reported Q1 FY2026 Data Center revenue of $5.78 billion, up 57% year over year.\\n    Quote: AMD\\u2019s Q1 FY26 data center revenue of $5.78 billion grew 57% year over year\\n- e546 [party_statement; 247Wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Broadcom ; from gnews:3cfa18ac4b56d1885013308d 2026-07-16] Broadcom reported last-quarter AI semiconductor revenue of $10.8 billion, up 143%, and guided Q3 AI revenue to $16.0 billion.\\n    Quote: AVGO printed $10.8 billion in AI semiconductor revenue last quarter, up 143%, and guided Q3 AI to $16.0 billion.\\n- e547 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source Bank of America ; from gnews:3cfa18ac4b56d1885013308d 2026-07-16] Bank of America framed NVIDIA networking as a $20 billion business; the author judged that framing not aggressive given the reported growth trajectory.\\n    Quote: Bank of America\\u2019s $20B business framing is not aggressive at that trajectory.\\n- e549 [fact; 247Wallst author Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2b5982d17c88b8e7433e1e15 2026-07-16] NVIDIA Data Center revenue was $75.246 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion, up 92%\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e279; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e550 [fact; 247Wallst author Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2b5982d17c88b8e7433e1e15 2026-07-16] NVIDIA networking revenue grew 199% year over year in Q1 FY2027.\\n    Quote: with networking alone up 199%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e458; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e552 [fact; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0e5b33a0b98c921c5e5db08b 2026-07-16] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, up 92% year over year, in Q1 FY2027.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA\\u2019s Data Center segment reached $75.25 billion, up 92% year over year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e352; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e556 [fact; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source TSMC ; from gnews:0e5b33a0b98c921c5e5db08b 2026-07-16] TSMC's first-half 2026 revenue was up 35.6% year over year.\\n    Quote: First-half 2026 revenue is already up 35.6% year over year.\\n- e557 [fact; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source TSMC ; from gnews:0e5b33a0b98c921c5e5db08b 2026-07-16] TSMC's June 2026 revenue grew 67.9% year over year.\\n    Quote: The June revenue report grew 67.9%\\n- e558 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source TSMC ; from gnews:0e5b33a0b98c921c5e5db08b 2026-07-16] TSMC plans capital expenditures of $52 billion to $56 billion in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: TSMC plans $52 billion to $56 billion in 2026 capex.\\n- e559 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source TSMC ; from gnews:0e5b33a0b98c921c5e5db08b 2026-07-16] TSMC's full-year 2026 outlook was for near 30% revenue growth in U.S. dollars, dependent on 3nm and 2nm ramps holding through the second half.\\n    Quote: TSMC\\u2019s full-year 2026 outlook of near 30% USD growth depends on 3nm and 2nm ramps holding through H2.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v14 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish, judged 2026-07-16\\n    Comparison point stated by that judgement: Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 sales projection is the clearest quantified market reference and sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\\n    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): About $407 billion: $81.6 billion in Q1 plus $91 billion guided for Q2, with Q2's sequential growth carried into Q3 and Q4, implies roughly $387 billion; adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion stand-alone CPU forecast gives about $407 billion.\\n    Reasons (excerpt): The reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which sits in rung 3. The Q1 result and Q2 guidance imply about $387 billion for the year if Q2's sequential growth carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19.5 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast, treated as incremental to that run-rate, gives about $407 billion and crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. Booked supply commitments and the updated AI-capex view support the upside, while memory-supply risk and custom-chip alternatives temper confidence; neither supplies a quantified adjustment that\\n\\n[Themes in force, 3]\\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-16; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the reported current position\\u2014no confirmed authorization by China's authorities\\u2014which sits at rung 2; no consensus or deadline-specific guidance is supplied. The forecast also favors rung 2: the direct report that Beijing refused to allow purchases is a material counterweight to ear\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-07-16; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is around $650 billion of 2026 data-center capex, which sits in rung 3. The implied outcome is in the low-to-mid $700 billions: start from the direct $650 billion data-center estimate, then treat the roughly $800 billion estimate as an upper comparator because it covers broader \\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-16; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, after projected 2026 spending of $650 billion; that reference sits in rung 3. The evidence supports spending above $1 trillion, but does not imply the $1.1 trillion needed to enter rung 4. The qualitative power constrain\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg\\u2019s $393 billion FY2027 total-revenue projection; applying the observed 92% Data Center mix puts the reference at about $362 billion, in rung 3. The implied Data Center forecast is about $356 billion: carrying the sequential growth from Q1 total revenue of $81.6 billion to the $91 billion Q2 guide forward gives roughly $387 billion of FY2027 total revenue, and applying the latest 92% Data Center mix gives about $356 billion. Management\\u2019s Q2 Data Center growth-acceleration comment and multiyear platform visibility add upside, but the visibility spans calendar 2026\\u201327 and Blackwell and Rubin, so it does not establish a FY2027 Data Center amount that crosses $400 billion. Dependency v14\\u2019s roughly $407 billion total-revenue forecast includes about $19.5 billion \\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"linked-1\", 8769, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 99 items]\\n[Not shown: 95 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e74 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA\\u2019s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\\n    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e169, e278, e602; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e109 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet ; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA\\u2019s AI computing products.\\n    Quote: The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors.\\n- e116 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Micron said tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028.\\n    Quote: tight market conditions could last until 2028\\n- e117 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author said new capacity for many companies in the memory-chip industry would not be online until 2027 or later.\\n    Quote: new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later\\n- e127 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Industry surveys ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, versus roughly 30% currently, potentially reducing demand for NVIDIA accelerators in China.\\n    Quote: Chinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5].\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e153 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Chinese company executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they\\u2019ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e155 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, quoting the Bloomberg Intelligence report; original source Bloomberg Intelligence 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence report, based on a survey of 60 executives, said China\\u2019s shift from foreign to domestic AI semiconductors is progressing and is likely to benefit Huawei and Hygon.\\n    Quote: \\u201cChina\\u2019s drive to substitute locally made AI-semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, which is likely to benefit domestic markers such as Huawei and Hygon,\\u201d said the report, which surveyed 60 executives at Chinese software, finance, manufacturing and retail companies.\\n- e156 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence survey expects Nvidia\\u2019s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips become harder to find and local suppliers gain share.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia\\u2019s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm\\u2019s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n- e158 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg News ; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Bloomberg News reported that domestic companies will supply at least 80% of core technologies, including chips, for China\\u2019s data-center initiative.\\n    Quote: At least 80% of core technologies, such as chips, will be supplied by domestic companies, Bloomberg News reported.\\n- e162 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America reported that NVIDIA\\u2019s sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year over year, nearly twice the growth in cloud capital expenditures.\\n    Quote: The report also says Nvidia\\u2019s hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, suggesting wallet-share gains rather than share loss.\\n- e163 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America modeled NVIDIA retaining roughly 65% to 70% or more of AI capital expenditures over time, with the remainder going to ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n    Quote: BofA still models Nvidia holding roughly 65% to 70% plus of AI capex over time, with the rest split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n- e170 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management guided to accelerating Data Center segment revenue growth in the second quarter; no amount is stated.\\n    Quote: management guided for further revenue growth acceleration for the second quarter.\\n- e171 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across calendar 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e304; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e201 [fact; 247wallst.com, reporting NVIDIA's figures; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year; networking revenue grew 199%.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year, and networking alone grew 199%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161, e205, e210, e288, e508; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e202 [party_statement; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f0423de52e9bd77b530ba76e 2026-07-10] NVIDIA guided to total revenue of $91 billion for Q2 FY2027.\\n    Quote: Q2 FY27 guidance points to $91 billion in revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e204 [fact; David Moadel, citing NVIDIA\\u2019s reported results; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbb9ff1a37ebb451f9321da5 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, including Data Center revenue of $75.2 billion.\\n    Quote: The company reported record revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, with Data Center revenue reaching $75.2 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e161, e254, e458, e492; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e227 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI starting late 2026 and continuing into 2027.\\n    Quote: However, Broadcom is also making custom AI chips for Meta Platforms , Anthropic, and OpenAI, and production for those chips is scheduled to ramp up late this year and into next.\\n- e255 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management guided FY2027 Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, assuming no China Data Center revenue.\\n    Quote: Looking ahead, management guided Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202, e271, e292, e332, e350; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e265 [fact; gurufocus.com, citing The Information; original source Google ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Google approached neocloud companies about using its TPUs; the item gives no date or scale.\\n    Quote: Google has approached so-called neocloud companies about using its tensor processing units, or TPUs, according to a report from The Information.\\n- e267 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Google and Blackstone ; from gnews:1c47fd978ef50b1d7f48cbd8 2026-07-13] Google and Blackstone are working on a new cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms in 2027.\\n    Quote: Google is also working with Blackstone on a new cloud venture that plans to rent TPU capacity to AI labs and financial firms next year.\\n- e289 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped zero H20 chips to China in the quarter discussed, compared with $4.6 billion of H20 shipments a year earlier.\\n    Quote: zero H20 chips shipped to China this quarter, versus $4.6 billion a year ago.\\n- e294 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, summarizing Morgan Stanley; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in 2027 and add 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity.\\n- e296 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, citing Milk Road AI; original source Milk Road AI ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Milk Road AI estimates one GW of AI infrastructure costs $6\\u201311 billion with Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks, about $19 billion with Nvidia GB300-based racks, and $25 billion with Vera Rubin racks.\\n    Quote: Milk Road AI estimates that building one gigawatt of AI infrastructure using Broadcom- or Marvell-designed ASIC racks cost between $6 billion and $11 billion, compared with roughly $19 billion using Nvidia GB300-based racks. Under Vera Rubin, rack costs jump to $25 billion.\\n- e297 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, summarizing Morgan Stanley; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley says hyperscalers continue expanding proprietary-silicon investment to improve total cost of ownership and reduce dependence on merchant GPU suppliers.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley says hyperscalers continue to expand their investments in proprietary silicon to improve total cost of ownership and reduce dependence on merchant GPU suppliers.\\n- e304 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from the Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, CEO Jensen Huang has said that he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from Nvidia's Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e171; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e307 [fact; Harsh Chauhan, Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported $193.7 billion of Data Center revenue in FY2026.\\n    Quote: For comparison, Nvidia's data center revenue in fiscal 2026 (which ended in January this year) was $193.7 billion.\\n- e357 [fact; Rest of World; original source Humain ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Humain is building data centers in Riyadh and Dammam that are planned to use several hundred thousand Nvidia chips.\\n    Quote: Humain is also building data centers in Riyadh and Dammam, powered by several hundred thousand Nvidia chips.\\n- e358 [fact; Rest of World; original source G42 ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] G42 is building the Stargate data center in Abu Dhabi; its first stage is planned to run on 400,000 Nvidia chips.\\n    Quote: The UAE is investing on the same scale through its state-backed AI firm G42, which is building a data center in Abu Dhabi called Stargate. Its first stage will run on 400,000 Nvidia chips .\\n- e360 [third_party_view; Kamil Dimmich, quoted by Rest of World; original source Kamil Dimmich ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Kamil Dimmich said Humain\\u2019s first order from Nvidia was for 18,000 Blackwell chips.\\n    Quote: Humain\\u2019s first order from Nvidia was 18,000 of its newest Blackwell chips \\u2014 the hardware that does that heavy lifting, which no rival can yet match, he said.\\n- e389 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Japan ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Japan launched a government-backed physical AI initiative with NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang participating at its kick-off.\\n    Quote: At the Physical AI Initiative kick-off event, NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang joined Ryosei Akazawa, Japan\\u2019s Minister of Economy, Trade and Industry, as the country launched a government-backed physical AI initiative.\\n- e390 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Astellas ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Astellas has deployed nearly all BioNeMo NIM microservices in NVIDIA\\u2019s digital biology portfolio and is running BioNeMo Agent Toolkit.\\n    Quote: Astellas has deployed nearly all BioNeMo NIM microservices within NVIDIA\\u2019s digital biology portfolio and is running BioNeMo Agent Toolkit, NVIDIA\\u2019s open platform that turns any AI agent into an autonomous life sciences scientist.\\n- e391 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Takeda ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Takeda announced a collaboration with Boltz to deploy the BoltzMol-1 and BoltzProt-1 models across its research organization.\\n    Quote: Takeda recently announced a collaboration with Boltz to deploy the BoltzMol-1 and BoltzProt-1 biomolecular models across its research organization, giving scientists tools for structure prediction, affinity estimation and generative design that integrate into existing discovery workflows.\\n- e392 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Canon and Fujifilm ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Two leading Japanese medical imaging companies are shipping next-generation CT systems built on NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Two of Japan\\u2019s leading medical imaging companies are now shipping next-generation CT systems built on NVIDIA GPUs.\\n- e393 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Fujifilm ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Fujifilm commercialized Japan\\u2019s first whole-body CT system powered by NVIDIA Blackwell.\\n    Quote: Fujifilm has commercialized Japan\\u2019s first whole-body CT system powered by NVIDIA Blackwell, using diffusion-based deep learning reconstruction to improve image quality.\\n- e394 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Mizuho ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Mizuho plans to build what is expected to be Japan\\u2019s largest on-premises financial-industry AI factory, starting with NVIDIA DGX B200 systems and scaling toward a larger cluster.\\n    Quote: Mizuho plans to build what is expected to be the largest on-premises AI factory in Japan\\u2019s financial industry, starting with NVIDIA DGX B200 systems and scaling toward a larger cluster.\\n- e395 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source RIKEN ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Two RIKEN supercomputers driven by NVIDIA GB200 and NVIDIA Quantum-X800 are beginning operations.\\n    Quote: At RIKEN, Japan\\u2019s leading national comprehensive research institute, two supercomputers driven by NVIDIA GB200 and NVIDIA Quantum-X800 are beginning operations.\\n- e396 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source RIKEN ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] RIKEN\\u2019s RIKYU AI-for-science supercomputer is deploying 1,600 NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs using the GB200 NVL4 platform.\\n    Quote: RIKYU, a new supercomputer for \\u201cAI for Science\\u201d development, deploying 1,600 NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs using the GB200 NVL4 platform, will support RIKEN\\u2019s development of open foundation models and contribute to accelerating AI adoption across broad fields, including life sciences, materials science and laboratory automation.\\n- e397 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source RIKEN ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] RIKEN\\u2019s ROQUO quantum-HPC system integrates quantum processors with accelerated computing from 540 NVIDIA Blackwell GPUs using the GB200 NVL4 platform.\\n    Quote: JHPC-quantum GPU supercomputer \\u201cROQUO\\u201d is a quantum-HPC system tightly integrating quantum processors with accelerated computing from 540 Blackwell GPUs accessed through the GB200 NVL4 platform.\\n- e398 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Fujitsu and NVIDIA ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] Fujitsu and NVIDIA are investigating CUDA-Q for large-scale quantum-chemistry simulation, and Fujitsu has started a trial of NVQLink.\\n    Quote: Fujitsu and NVIDIA are now investigating efficient ways to use NVIDIA CUDA-Q for large-scale quantum-chemistry simulation. Through the collaboration, Fujitsu has started the trial of NVQLink to determine if it can be utilized to realize efficient control of their quantum-classical hybrid computing environment.\\n- e399 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source SoftBank Corp. ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] SoftBank is using NVIDIA GB200-class infrastructure, RTX PRO-based AI RAN with AI Aerial, and Nemotron-based telecom models for its network.\\n    Quote: SoftBank showed how it\\u2019s already using NVIDIA\\u2019s full stack \\u2014 from GB200-class AI infrastructure and NVIDIA RTX PRO-based AI RAN with the NVIDIA AI Aerial platform to NVIDIA Nemotron-based large telecom models \\u2014 to turn its communications network into an intelligence delivery network.\\n- e400 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA and SoftBank Corp. ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] NVIDIA and SoftBank confirmed joint initiatives using Nemotron and SB Intuitions\\u2019 Sarashina, and physical-AI work with partners including Yaskawa using NVIDIA Cosmos and Isaac GR00T.\\n    Quote: The companies also confirmed joint initiatives to drive Japanese innovation using open models such as NVIDIA Nemotron, together with SB Intuitions\\u2019 homegrown generative AI model series Sarashina, while advancing physical AI with partners such as Yaskawa Electric Corporation using NVIDIA Cosmos and Isaac GR00T.\\n- e401 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source SEGA ; from nvblog:japan-ecosystem-2026 2026-07-15] SEGA will support NVIDIA RTX Spark, including with the upcoming VIRTUA FIGHTER CROSSROADS.\\n    Quote: SEGA will support RTX Spark, giving gamers new ways to experience SEGA\\u2019s iconic franchises, including the upcoming VIRTUA FIGHTER CROSSROADS.\\n- e409 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db8d58512ec8bb97266d4b48 2026-07-15] NVIDIA reported Data Center revenue of $75 billion in fiscal Q1 2027, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: In the first quarter of fiscal 2027, Nvidia's Data Center revenue surged 92% year-over-year (YOY) to $75 billion, fueled by strong Blackwell demand as customers deployed hundreds of thousands of GPUs in what became the fastest product rollout in the company's history.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e270; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e430 [fact; fool.com author; original source Cerebras Systems ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] Cerebras reported first-quarter revenue of $193 million, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: This has translated into growth for Cerebras, with first-quarter revenue soaring 92% to $193 million.\\n- e431 [fact; fool.com author; original source Cerebras Systems ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] Cerebras recently signed a compute deal with OpenAI.\\n    Quote: And the company recently signed key deals with OpenAI for compute\\n- e432 [fact; fool.com author; original source Cerebras Systems and Amazon's cloud unit ; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] Cerebras signed a deal with Amazon's cloud unit to make its wafer-scale engine systems more broadly available.\\n    Quote: and with Amazon 's cloud unit to make its WSE systems more broadly available.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e473; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e433 [third_party_view; fool.com author; original source fool.com author 2026-07-15; from gnews:96b4183f0818dac8161cef91 2026-07-15] The author says NVIDIA trades at 23 times forward earnings estimates.\\n    Quote: The AI giant is trading at 23x forward earnings estimates\\n- e440 [fact; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:48ea00c0bf7c01bdb80850bb 2026-07-15] NVIDIA Data Center revenue rose 92% year over year in the latest quarter.\\n    Quote: with data center revenue surging 92% year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e288; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e444 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, Rich Duprey; original source 247wallst.com, Rich Duprey 2026-07-15; from gnews:7ce055d995cada5e9a801163 2026-07-15] The author says hyperscalers now view AMD as a strategic second source rather than a backup, after it narrowed the AI gap faster than expected; no period is specified.\\n    Quote: That said, AMD has narrowed the gap at a pace few expected, transforming itself from an AI outsider into a company that hyperscalers now view as a strategic second source rather than a backup plan.\\n- e445 [fact; TweakTown; original source Financial Times ; from gnews:96607f3f5939f53a4b3cdea7 2026-07-15] NVIDIA implemented a whitelist with stricter compliance checks for authorized buyers of its high-end AI accelerators, aimed at preventing diversion to prohibited destinations.\\n    Quote: According to a new report from the Financial Times , the chipmaker has implemented a new \\\"white list\\\" of vetted companies, which have passed stricter compliance checks ensuring NVIDIA's high-end AI accelerators don't end up in places they aren't legally allowed to be.\\n- e446 [fact; TweakTown; original source Financial Times ; from gnews:96607f3f5939f53a4b3cdea7 2026-07-15] NVIDIA's revised authorization restrictions excluded more than half of its previous customers; companies that failed the initial check can reapply.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA's new restrictions notably excluded more than half of NVIDIA's previous customers, and companies that failed the initial compliance check are eligible to reapply.\\n- e452 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA\\u2019s Data Center segment produced $75.246 billion, up 92% YoY,\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e279; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e453 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e7ea83570963898cb3dfbb37 2026-07-15] NVIDIA networking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion.\\n    Quote: networking alone growing 199% YoY to $14.8 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e347; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e458 [fact; Gerelyn Terzo; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year, and Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion; Networking revenue rose 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia\\u2019s Q1 FY2027 revenue hit $81.615 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with Data Center alone contributing $75.246 billion and Networking revenue rising 199% YoY.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e204, e550; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e459 [party_statement; Gerelyn Terzo, reporting Huang's remarks; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had more orders than at his previous GTC remarks and expected to keep its supply chain busy for several more years.\\n    Quote: He added that \\u201cWe have more orders today than we did at the last time I spoke about orders at GTC\\u201d and that NVIDIA will \\u201ckeep our supply chain quite busy for several many more years coming.\\u201d\\n- e462 [third_party_view; Gerelyn Terzo, reporting Morgan Stanley's estimate; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] Morgan Stanley raised its Vera Rubin rack-system price assumption to about $49 billion per gigawatt, implying higher customer spending per deployment than for Blackwell.\\n    Quote: The delay narrative that surfaced in early July collided with a more bullish Wall Street read this morning: Morgan Stanley raised its Vera Rubin rack-system price assumption to about $49 billion per gigawatt, implying materially higher customer spending per deployment than Blackwell.\\n- e464 [third_party_view; Gerelyn Terzo; original source Gerelyn Terzo 2026-07-15; from gnews:2e37109213bd9753af2f50e0 2026-07-15] Gerelyn Terzo judged that if Vera Rubin volumes are large and Morgan Stanley's higher average selling prices hold, current forward estimates may understate NVIDIA's FY2028 earnings power.\\n    Quote: If Huang\\u2019s \\u201cgiant\\u201d volumes materialize on Rubin at Morgan Stanley\\u2019s higher ASPs, current forward estimates likely understate FY2028 earnings power.\\n- e472 [third_party_view; Geoffrey Seiler; original source Geoffrey Seiler 2026-07-16; from gnews:f9a3a3ef9a56e7099140e164 2026-07-16] The author says Cerebras's wafer-scale chip is 6 times faster than NVIDIA's LPUs and 15 times faster than NVIDIA's GPUs.\\n    Quote: The result is a chip that is 6 times faster than Nvidia's LPUs and 15 times faster than its GPUs.\\n- e473 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler; original source Cerebras Systems ; from gnews:f9a3a3ef9a