ded April 26).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e553; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e572 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Analysts ; from gnews:03f49670bc6aa3ce8c653170 2026-07-17] Analysts forecast NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue to increase 82%.\\n    Quote: analysts forecast an 82% revenue surge for fiscal 2027\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e574 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:e33eeae2e6a898bcf6f75ab6 2026-07-17] Analysts expected NVIDIA to deliver nearly 100% growth in Q2 2026; the article does not specify the growth measure.\\n    Quote: That trend is expected to last through at least Q2, with analysts expecting nearly 100% growth from Nvidia during Q2.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e577 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c03377d9fbbf30b7a0933cd5 2026-07-17] NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of close to $215.9 billion, up 65.5% year over year.\\n    Quote: In FY 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, a significant jump of 65.5% over the previous year.\\n- e578 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c03377d9fbbf30b7a0933cd5 2026-07-17] NVIDIA\\u2019s two largest direct customers accounted for 22% and 14% of total sales in the most recent fiscal year.\\n    Quote: Revenue is highly concentrated, with two direct customers accounting for 22% and 14% of total sales in the most recent fiscal year.\\n- e579 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:99d11db52f240a9bdbff8731 2026-07-17] NVIDIA reported total revenue of $81.6 billion in fiscal Q1 2027, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: Nvidia, which posted $81.6 billion in total revenue for its fiscal first quarter of 2027, an 85% increase from a year earlier.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e254, e601; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e589 [third_party_view; Precedence Research; original source Precedence Research 2026-07-17; from gnews:8104fb6cb8a4bcb37d1fe4b7 2026-07-17] Precedence Research projected the global GPU market at USD 136.06 billion in calendar 2026, with a 32.62% CAGR over 2026\\u20132035.\\n    Quote: The global graphics processing unit (GPU) market was valued at USD 101.54 billion in 2025 and is projected to grow from USD 136.06 billion in 2026 to approximately USD 1,712.56 billion by 2035, registering a CAGR of 32.62% during the forecast period from 2026 to 2035.\\n- e590 [fact; Ece Yildirim, Gizmodo; original source Moonshot 2026-07-16; from gnews:86e5102be4f45f9e4dfcdb9d 2026-07-17] Moonshot debuted its Kimi K3 AI model on Thursday, July 16, 2026; the article says it performed highly across multiple benchmarks compared with costlier frontier American AI models.\\n    Quote: On Thursday, Chinese artificial intelligence startup Moonshot debuted its latest model, Kimi K3, a gigantic soon-to-be open-weight model with 2.8 trillion parameters. The announcement sent waves across the American AI industry, particularly for the model\\u2019s high performance across multiple benchmarks compared to the latest costlier offerings from the frontier American AI companies.\\n- e598 [fact; SCMP.com; original source T-Head 2026-07-18; from gnews:4477f19fd6c69ffba37172af 2026-07-18] Alibaba chip unit T-Head announced that it would make the full SAIL software stack for its Zhenwu AI chips freely available to international developers starting 2026-07-18.\\n    Quote: At the World AI Conference (WAIC) in Shanghai on Saturday, T-Head announced that it was making the full technical stack of SAIL \\u2013 the foundational software architecture for the unit\\u2019s Zhenwu series of AI chips \\u2013 freely available to international developers starting on the same day.\\n- e599 [fact; tikr.com; original source US Commerce Department 2026-07-10; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] On July 10, 2026, the US Commerce Department moved the UAE into its highest-trust export tier, easing export authorization for NVIDIA Blackwell processors to UAE entities.\\n    Quote: On July 10, the US Commerce Department reclassified the United Arab Emirates into its highest-trust export tier, clearing the way for UAE entities to buy NVIDIA\\u2019s most advanced Blackwell processors without the months-long, shipment-by-shipment license reviews that had governed Gulf sales.\\n- e600 [rumour; tikr.com; original source tikr.com ; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] Gulf sovereign wealth funds have reportedly committed about $120 billion to global AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Gulf sovereign wealth has reportedly committed around $120 billion to global AI infrastructure\\n- e601 [fact; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-05-20; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: When NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 on May 20, revenue hit a record $81.6 billion, up 85% from a year earlier\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e579; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e603 [party_statement; tikr.com; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] At a June 2026 conference, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said agentic AI pushes customers toward best-of-breed systems, countering concerns that hardware is becoming interchangeable.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA\\u2019s CFO Colette Kress, Executive Vice President and CFO, met the commoditization fear head-on at the Bank of America technology conference in June. Asked whether hyperscalers grabbing any available compute meant hardware was becoming interchangeable, she said it was \\u201cactually the opposite,\\u201d arguing that agentic AI forces customers toward best-of-breed systems rather than away from them.\\n- e606 [party_statement; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0ece014204de971d2176e9c2 2026-07-18] NVIDIA guided to about $91 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, with results scheduled for August 26, 2026.\\n    Quote: The next real test is August 26, when NVIDIA reports fiscal Q2 2027 against its own guidance of roughly $91 billion in revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e507, e615; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e615 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c840e055f0c00ff7c6fe18e9 2026-07-20] NVIDIA management guided revenue to $91 billion for the summer quarter, up almost 2%; the comparison basis is not specified.\\n    Quote: The guidance provided by management indicates the momentum is not slowing down for the summer quarter. Revenue guidance was projected at $91 billion, up almost 2%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e606; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e618 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:24da4cc269dbf1a4b9fdf76b 2026-07-20] NVIDIA management reported that total revenue increased 85% year over year in the latest quarter.\\n    Quote: Management recently reported that total revenue was up 85% year over year in its latest quarter\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e569; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e619 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:24da4cc269dbf1a4b9fdf76b 2026-07-20] NVIDIA management described the Blackwell platform ramp as the fastest product ramp in the company's history.\\n    Quote: calling the ramp of its Blackwell platform the \\u201cfastest product ramp in our company\\u2019s history.\\u201d\\n- e621 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:24da4cc269dbf1a4b9fdf76b 2026-07-20] NVIDIA is planning for no revenue contribution from China's data center market.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is planning for no contribution from China\\u2019s data center market\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e441; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e623 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb5448c6b2a24453da7439e5 2026-07-20] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, and Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, up 92%.\\n    Quote: The pullback follows a blowout Q1 FY27 report : revenue of $81.61 billion beat estimates by 3.16% and grew 85.2% year over year, with Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion up 92%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e492; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e624 [party_statement; 247wallst.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:cb5448c6b2a24453da7439e5 2026-07-20] NVIDIA management guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion at the midpoint.\\n    Quote: Management guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion at the midpoint.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e343; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e625 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source 247wallst.com 2026-07-20; from gnews:cb5448c6b2a24453da7439e5 2026-07-20] 24/7 Wall St. said NVIDIA's multi-generation customer commitments included an OpenAI 10 GW deployment and CoreWeave 5 GW by 2030, and estimated forward EPS could exceed $8.26.\\n    Quote: The Blackwell 300 ramp, the Vera Rubin platform announcement, and multi-generation commitments from Meta, OpenAI (10GW deployment), and CoreWeave (5GW by 2030) frame a Data Center run-rate that could push forward EPS well past $8.26.\\n- e627 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com; original source 247wallst.com 2026-07-20; from gnews:cb5448c6b2a24453da7439e5 2026-07-20] 24/7 Wall St. said NVIDIA shares could reach $290 if China Data Center compute revenue returns and gross margin remains near the 75% guided level.\\n    Quote: If China DC compute revenue returns and gross margin holds near the 75% guided level, the multiple can expand and $290 becomes reachable.\\n- e645 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ea762cb1f3b29a72b16e067a 2026-07-20] NVIDIA is working with Adobe, Blender, Unreal, Epic Games, SideFX, Foundry, and Canva to integrate MCP connections for AI agents into their creative and engineering tools; the item gives no completion date.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is also working with its partners such as Adobe, Blender, Unreal, Unreal Engine, Epic Games, SideFX, Foundry, and Canva to integrate MCP (Model Context Protocol) connections, allowing AI agents to work inside the tools where scenes, shots, timelines, assets, and edits are being rendered.\\n- e646 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ea762cb1f3b29a72b16e067a 2026-07-20] NVIDIA's DGX Station workstation is available for order through ASUS, Dell, Exxact, Gigabyte, HP, MSI, and Supermicro; no order volume or availability date is given.\\n    Quote: The DGX Station is available for order through ASUS, Dell, Exxact, Gigabyte, HP, MSI, and Supermicro.\\n- e647 [third_party_view; siliconangle.com, reporting Dave Vellante; original source Dave Vellante, chief analyst at theCUBE Research ; from gnews:8890c18dd82c2260b7ded589 2026-07-20] Dave Vellante expects NVIDIA to retain 75%\\u201380% of the AI accelerated computing market; no period is specified.\\n    Quote: Vellante expects the company to retain between 75% and 80% of the AI accelerated computing market.\\n- e648 [third_party_view; siliconangle.com, reporting John Furrier; original source John Furrier, executive analyst at theCUBE Research ; from gnews:8890c18dd82c2260b7ded589 2026-07-20] John Furrier expects more alternatives to emerge in AI inference as enterprises seek smaller, less expensive systems that can operate outside hyperscale data centers; no period is specified.\\n    Quote: Furrier expects more alternatives to emerge as enterprises seek smaller, less expensive inference systems that can operate outside hyperscale data centers.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v8 (open; first_is_bullish) How much revenue will NVIDIA generate from stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-20\\n    Comparison point stated by that judgement: NVIDIA's $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU sales target; it sits at the lower boundary of rung 2. No separate market-consensus figure is supplied.\\n    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): Approximately $19 billion in FY2027, near but below $20 billion. This discounts NVIDIA's target because shipment volume is not shown to be in hand, while the customer interest and reported cloud offering activity provide some support for approaching it.\\n    Reasons (excerpt): The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 stand-alone CPU sales target of $20 billion, at the lower boundary of rung 2; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The target remains an important forecast input, but the customer plans and reported offering activity do not specify shipment volume or timing, so they do not establish that the full target is likely. They modestly reduce the discount to the target, while competitive incumbency and AMD's disclosed CPU deployments remain counterweights. The $200 billion TAM is not a revenue forecast. The evidence therefore supports sales near, but below,\\n\\n[Themes in force, 3]\\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-20; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is rung 2 for the observable status quo: no Chinese authorization is established in the evidence. However, the specific forward-looking reports that Chinese officials told named firms they would be permitted to buy limited H200 quantities, together with reported progress in import revi\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-20; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is around $650 billion for calendar-2026 data-center capex, which is in rung 3. I keep the forecast near that level: the new $800 billion estimate is for broader AI infrastructure, not the four hyperscalers\\u2019 data-center capex, while Meta\\u2019s $125\\u2013$145 billion and Google\\u2019s $190 billion ar\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-20; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management's forecast of more than $1 trillion of 2027 spending, following projected 2026 spending of $650 billion; that reference sits in rung 3. The forecast implies growth of roughly 54% to the $1 trillion level, but does not quantify a figure of $1.1 trillion or more. Pow\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The market reference is Bloomberg's $393 billion FY2027 projection, which is in rung 3. Q1 revenue of $81.615 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for FY2027 if the Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth rate carries through Q3 and Q4; adding the dependency's approximately $19 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU estimate gives about $406 billion, above rung 2's $400 billion boundary. The $119 billion in supply-related commitments and management's order comments support demand but are not added directly to revenue. New evidence of HBM supply pressure and China-related restrictions is a downside risk, while the UAE export change is a partial offset; none supplies a quantified FY2027 revenue adjustment large enough to change the estimate across a rung boundary.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"linked-1\", 8842, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 102 items]\\n[Not shown: 355 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e74 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA\\u2019s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\\n    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e169, e278, e602, e680, e771; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e109 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet ; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA\\u2019s AI computing products.\\n    Quote: The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors.\\n- e116 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Micron said tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028.\\n    Quote: tight market conditions could last until 2028\\n- e117 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-07; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The author said new capacity for many companies in the memory-chip industry would not be online until 2027 or later.\\n    Quote: new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later\\n- e127 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Industry surveys ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, versus roughly 30% currently, potentially reducing demand for NVIDIA accelerators in China.\\n    Quote: Chinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5].\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e153 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Chinese company executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they\\u2019ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e156 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] The Bloomberg Intelligence survey expects Nvidia\\u2019s market share in China to shrink as H20 chips become harder to find and local suppliers gain share.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia\\u2019s products remain popular, the Santa Clara-based firm\\u2019s market share is expected to shrink as its H20 chips, which Beijing has urged its tech firms not to use, become harder to find and local firms take over, according to the survey.\\n- e162 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America reported that NVIDIA\\u2019s sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year over year, nearly twice the growth in cloud capital expenditures.\\n    Quote: The report also says Nvidia\\u2019s hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, suggesting wallet-share gains rather than share loss.\\n- e163 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America modeled NVIDIA retaining roughly 65% to 70% or more of AI capital expenditures over time, with the remainder going to ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n    Quote: BofA still models Nvidia holding roughly 65% to 70% plus of AI capex over time, with the rest split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n- e170 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management guided to accelerating Data Center segment revenue growth in the second quarter; no amount is stated.\\n    Quote: management guided for further revenue growth acceleration for the second quarter.\\n- e171 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across calendar 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e304; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e227 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e 2026-07-12] Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI starting late 2026 and continuing into 2027.\\n    Quote: However, Broadcom is also making custom AI chips for Meta Platforms , Anthropic, and OpenAI, and production for those chips is scheduled to ramp up late this year and into next.\\n- e255 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management guided FY2027 Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, assuming no China Data Center revenue.\\n    Quote: Looking ahead, management guided Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202, e271, e292, e332, e350, e768, e1503; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e289 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] NVIDIA shipped zero H20 chips to China in the quarter discussed, compared with $4.6 billion of H20 shipments a year earlier.\\n    Quote: zero H20 chips shipped to China this quarter, versus $4.6 billion a year ago.\\n- e294 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, summarizing Morgan Stanley; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure in 2027 and add 19.5 GW of incremental compute capacity.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity.\\n- e304 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from the Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, CEO Jensen Huang has said that he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from Nvidia's Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e171, e936; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e307 [fact; Harsh Chauhan, Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported $193.7 billion of Data Center revenue in FY2026.\\n    Quote: For comparison, Nvidia's data center revenue in fiscal 2026 (which ended in January this year) was $193.7 billion.\\n- e633 [fact; CNBC; original source Meta ; from gnews:b79717064762299fc9426a13 2026-07-20] Meta announced in February that it plans to use up to 6 GW of AMD GPUs over time, beginning with 1 GW deployed on Helios racks later in 2026.\\n    Quote: In February, Meta announced it'll use up to 6 gigawatts of AMD GPUs over time, starting with 1 gigawatt deployed on Helios racks later this year.\\n- e636 [party_statement; AMD told CNBC; original source AMD ; from gnews:b79717064762299fc9426a13 2026-07-20] AMD said it plans to book tens of billions of dollars in data-center AI revenue starting in 2027, with most of it from Helios.\\n    Quote: AMD told CNBC that it plans to book tens of billions in data center AI revenue starting in 2027, the majority coming from Helios.\\n- e638 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source The Futurum Group ; from gnews:b79717064762299fc9426a13 2026-07-20] The Futurum Group estimated NVIDIA has more than 95% of the data-center GPU market and AMD has about 4.5%.\\n    Quote: Nvidia controls more than 95% of the data center GPU market, according to the Futurum Group. AMD only holds some 4.5% of the market, but Helios could change that.\\n- e670 [third_party_view; Anthony Di Pizio, Motley Fool; original source Anthony Di Pizio 2026-07-21; from gnews:0993b79d5b6b817b8d65b011 2026-07-21] The author estimates that around 90% of NVIDIA's projected FY2027 and FY2028 revenue will come from its Data Center business, citing AI GPU demand.\\n    Quote: If recent results are anything to go by, around 90% of that revenue will come from the data center business alone, thanks to red-hot demand for AI GPUs.\\n- e758 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-22; from gnews:7c49e1c66c5c148a5c0123ef 2026-07-22] SemiAnalysis expects NVIDIA\\u2019s Rubin Oberon NVL72 to ramp into production faster than Blackwell Oberon NVL72, citing a simpler cableless compute tray and lessons from Blackwell\\u2019s backplane deployment.\\n    Quote: Another area where Rubin Oberon NVL72 will fare better than Blackwell Oberon NVL72 is in a much faster production ramp period. This is thanks to Rubin\\u2019s simpler cableless compute tray design and learnings from Nvidia\\u2019s experience with deploying a rack-scale copper backplane, having invested much effort into ironing out issues with Blackwell\\u2019s copper backplane.\\n- e759 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-22; from gnews:7c49e1c66c5c148a5c0123ef 2026-07-22] SemiAnalysis\\u2019s July 2026 benchmark comparison estimates Rubin NVL72 throughput at nearly 2x Blackwell\\u2019s through 100 tokens/second/user and roughly 4x around 200 tokens/second/user, versus GB300 NVL72.\\n    Quote: Let\\u2019s first compare Rubin against the July 2026 GB300 NVL72 baseline. Rubin\\u2019s lead is smallest at low interactivity and continues growing through the middle of the interactivity curve. Rubin sits at near 2x the throughput as Blackwell up through 100 tok/s/user, then widens to roughly 4x around 200 tok/s/user, where the gap peaks.\\n- e761 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-22; from gnews:7c49e1c66c5c148a5c0123ef 2026-07-22] SemiAnalysis\\u2019s TCO comparison estimates Rubin\\u2019s cost per million output tokens at about 1.5x below GB200 through 100 tokens/second/user and 3x below it from 200 to 250 tokens/second/user, against July 2026 benchmarks.\\n    Quote: Against July 2026 GB200 & GB300, Rubin is cheaper at every interactivity, and the gap widens as you climb. The gap starts at about 1.5x cheaper than GB200 through 100 tok/s/user, and improves to 3x by 200 tok/s/user through to 250 tok/s/user.\\n- e828 [fact; TechCrunch; original source AMD and Anthropic 2026-07-22; from gnews:78152408aa7a67ee94806acd 2026-07-23] AMD and Anthropic announced a partnership to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of GPUs using Helios.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Anthropic and AMD announced a strategic partnership Wednesday to deploy up to two gigawatts of GPUs via the new rack system.\\n- e858 [fact; theregister.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:1238e7fa686e264d18a93f27 2026-07-23] Anthropic plans to deploy 2 GW of AMD MI455X GPUs in exchange for an investment of up to $5 billion in Anthropic.\\n    Quote: Similarly Anthropic plans to deploy two gigawatts of the chips in exchange for an up to $5 billion investment in the model dev.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1048; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e880 [fact; SiliconANGLE; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:599e4c6d3c322f3c5663256d 2026-07-24] Anthropic plans to buy up to 2 GW of GPU capacity from AMD, a competing supplier of AI compute products relevant to NVIDIA\\u2019s Data Center revenue.\\n    Quote: Anthropic to buy up to 2 gigawatts of GPU capacity from AMD\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e891 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 gigawatts of GPUs.\\n    Quote: OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 gigawatts of GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1077; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e941 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source SK Telecom ; from gnews:0b7814cb036244f998f69f18 2026-07-25] SK Telecom plans a 2-gigawatt South Korean AI data center using Nvidia's DSX platform and Vera Rubin systems with SK hynix HBM4 memory, with the first data center planned to enter service in 2027.\\n    Quote: The key part of the strategic relationship is SK Telecom's planned 2-gigawatt AI data center in South Korea. The installation will rely on Nvidia's DSX AI factory platform and deploy Vera Rubin accelerated computing systems equipped with SK hynix HBM4 memory. The first AI data center is set to enter service in 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e903, e988; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e976 [third_party_view; fool.com author Lyle Daly; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] SemiAnalysis forecast that NVIDIA Data Center revenue in the second half of FY2027 would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\\n    Quote: And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\\n- e994 [third_party_view; Investing.com, quoting Bernstein analysts; original source Bernstein analysts ; from gnews:c871b9605dd781113ba40cef 2026-07-27] Bernstein analysts said the announced amounts primarily reflect NVIDIA and Broadcom securing memory supply and that the $500 billion-plus headline figure may overstate the near-term revenue impact for NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: \\\"We believe the announced amounts are more for memory and indicate the need for NVIDIA & Broadcom to secure memory supply,\\\" they wrote. While the read is highly constructive for SK Hynix, Bernstein warns the $500B+ headline figure may overstate the near-term revenue impact for Nvidia.\\n- e1028 [fact; Hannah Pedone; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:bb0d23846735c94b7c1722d2 2026-07-27] OpenAI has committed $300 billion to Oracle for data-center capacity, much of which Oracle will use to buy NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Additionally, OpenAI has committed $300 billion to Oracle (ORCL) to secure data-center capacity, much of which Oracle will use to buy Nvidia GPUs.\\n- e1125 [third_party_view; James Royal, MarketWise; original source Wall Street Journal, as relayed by MarketWise ; from gnews:4f406aeae58d33485fd74025 2026-07-28] The first phase of OpenAI\\u2019s proposed southern Ohio data center is not expected to open until 2028 and would start at 800 MW, 8% of the center\\u2019s expected capacity.\\n    Quote: The first phase of the new data center wouldn\\u2019t open until 2028, starting with just 800 megawatts of power, only 8% of the total expected capacity of the center.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1036; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1266 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware; original source A researcher at a major Chinese technology firm who works on model training ; from gnews:142b11abb4f31a2e38b7aeb2 2026-07-29] A researcher at a major Chinese technology firm said Chinese AI accelerators were one or two generations behind NVIDIA's current offerings and reportedly had several months of backorders.\\n    Quote: They further added that training frontier models is difficult or impossible with the promising but slowly developed homegrown chips. By that source's account, Chinese AI accelerators remain a generation or two behind Nvidia's current offerings and are reportedly several months in backorder.\\n- e1290 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware, quoting a Fubon Research note to clients; original source Fubon Research ; from gnews:aa07afabbc7c07a3cd91d240 2026-07-30] Fubon Research estimated that Google plans 12\\u201315 million V9 TPUs in calendar 2028, with TPU capacity consumption more than doubling versus 2027.\\n    Quote: \\\"Based on our checks, Google plans to have 12 \\u2013 15 million TPUs in 2028,\\\" the paper reads. \\\"Entering 2028, Google\\u2019s TPUs will enter the V9 generation with four compute dies, suggesting that their capacity consumption will more than double in 2028 versus 2027.\\\"\\n- e1291 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware; original source Fubon Research ; from gnews:aa07afabbc7c07a3cd91d240 2026-07-30] Fubon Research estimated NVIDIA supplied 8.2 million data-center AI GPUs in calendar 2026 and was on track to supply 12.4 million in calendar 2028.\\n    Quote: Fubon estimates that Nvidia supplied 8.2 million data center AI GPUs in 2026 and is on track to increase the number to 12.4 million in 2028.\\n- e1362 [fact; themanufacturer.com; original source Wistron ; from gnews:a9f90ace8d264bcebaaaab43 2026-07-31] Wistron opened its first U.S. manufacturing facility in Fort Worth and began mass-producing NVIDIA GB300 Grace Blackwell Ultra Superchips as part of a $700 million investment.\\n    Quote: Wistron has opened its first US manufacturing facility in Fort Worth, Texas, where it has begun mass production of NVIDIA\\u2019s GB300 Grace Blackwell Ultra Superchip as part of a $700m investment aimed at strengthening domestic AI infrastructure manufacturing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e737; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1374 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source 247wallst.com (Joel South) 2026-08-01; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] The author estimates that nearly all of Meta\\u2019s FY26 capital expenditures will go to NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: Nearly every dollar of that flows to NVIDIA.\\n- e1376 [fact; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Microsoft reported $30.88 billion of capital expenditures in Q3 FY26, up 84.4% year over year, with much of the spending on NVIDIA silicon for Azure.\\n    Quote: Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ) spent $30.88 billion on CapEx in Q3 FY26, up 84.4%, much of it on NVIDIA silicon for Azure.\\n- e1377 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Joel South, quoting Satya Nadella); original source Satya Nadella ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella said its AI business exceeded a $37 billion annual revenue run rate, up 123% year over year.\\n    Quote: Satya Nadella said \\u201cOur AI business surpassed an annual revenue run rate of $37 billion, up 123% year-over-year.\\u201d\\n- e1378 [fact; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Microsoft\\u2019s commercial remaining performance obligations were $627 billion, up 99% year over year; the author says this gives Microsoft leverage to pass GPU costs through.\\n    Quote: Commercial RPO of $627 billion, up 99%, gives Microsoft the pricing leverage to pass GPU costs through.\\n- e1379 [fact; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Google Cloud revenue grew 82% year over year to $24.77 billion in Q2 FY26.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue grew 82% to $24.77 billion in Q2 FY26\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1371; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1380 [fact; 247wallst.com (Joel South); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Alphabet reported $44.92 billion of capital expenditures in Q2 FY26, up 100% year over year; the author says a meaningful share bypasses NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: CapEx still ran $44.92 billion in Q2 FY26, up 100%, but a meaningful share bypasses NVIDIA.\\n- e1409 [third_party_view; english.cw.com.tw, reporting a leaked conversation; original source DeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng ; from gnews:62ea477f766b5061a81b39e8 2026-08-03] DeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng said AI-generated code is rapidly weakening NVIDIA\\u2019s CUDA software-ecosystem advantage.\\n    Quote: \\u201cNvidia\\u2019s CUDA moat is being dismantled rapidly,\\u201d because, \\u201cAI can write code. Building a software ecosystem is much easier than before.\\u201d\\n- e1410 [fact; english.cw.com.tw; original source Anthropic, through co-founder Tom Brown ; from gnews:62ea477f766b5061a81b39e8 2026-08-03] Anthropic announced plans to deploy 2 GW of AMD Helios racks at an unspecified future date.\\n    Quote: Anthropic co-founder Tom Brown announced on stage that the company would deploy 2 GW of Helios racks.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e880; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1411 [party_statement; english.cw.com.tw, quoting Tom Brown; original source Anthropic co-founder Tom Brown ; from gnews:62ea477f766b5061a81b39e8 2026-08-03] Anthropic co-founder Tom Brown said the company recently tested one AMD rack, which one engineer brought up using Claude over a weekend, illustrating a lower engineering burden for adopting AMD hardware.\\n    Quote: Brown explained that, in the past, bringing up a new hardware platform required an enormous engineering effort. But recently, his team tested a single AMD rack. All it took was one engineer giving Anthropic\\u2019s own Claude model a simple instruction: \\u201cLet\\u2019s bring up this machine.\\u201d Over the course of the weekend, they simply watched as the model\\u2019s performance continued to improve on its own.\\n- e1412 [third_party_view; english.cw.com.tw, quoting SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:62ea477f766b5061a81b39e8 2026-08-03] SemiAnalysis said AI agents are diminishing the importance of NVIDIA\\u2019s CUDA advantage and could help AMD offset NVIDIA\\u2019s engineering headcount advantage.\\n    Quote: \\u201c...As AI agents begin taking over work previously performed by human software engineers, the importance of the so-called \\u2018CUDA moat\\u2019 is diminishing. That allows AMD to offset Nvidia\\u2019s engineering headcount advantage.\\u201d\\n- e1415 [fact; Interesting Engineering; original source NVIDIA and SpaceX 2026-08-04; from gnews:3766fedf81bf4f63a64f9ccb 2026-08-04] NVIDIA and SpaceX announced on 2026-08-04 that they are jointly developing the computing payload for SpaceX\\u2019s Starmind AI1 satellite, which is intended to process AI workloads in low Earth orbit.\\n    Quote: The companies announced on August 4 that they are jointly developing the compute payload for the Starmind AI1 satellite, a spacecraft intended to process AI workloads in low Earth orbit using Nvidia\\u2019s latest computing hardware.\\n- e1416 [third_party_view; Interesting Engineering; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:3766fedf81bf4f63a64f9ccb 2026-08-04] SpaceX plans to use NVIDIA Vera CPUs and Rubin GPUs in its first Starmind satellites.\\n    Quote: SpaceX plans to power the first Starmind satellites with Nvidia\\u2019s Vera CPUs and Rubin GPUs\\n- e1417 [party_statement; Interesting Engineering; original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:3766fedf81bf4f63a64f9ccb 2026-08-04] Elon Musk said SpaceX has committed to using NVIDIA GPUs exclusively.\\n    Quote: Elon Musk also endorsed the partnership, saying SpaceX has committed to using Nvidia GPUs exclusively because \\u201cthey are the best.\\u201d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1436, e1445; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1419 [third_party_view; Interesting Engineering; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:3766fedf81bf4f63a64f9ccb 2026-08-04] SpaceX plans to test the Starmind AI1 satellite prototype in early 2027 and expects mass production to begin later in 2027, conditional on development staying on schedule.\\n    Quote: Prototype testing for the Starmind AI1 satellite is scheduled for early 2027. SpaceX expects mass production to begin later that year if development stays on schedule.\\n- e1436 [party_statement; Elon Musk, as quoted by finance.yahoo.com; original source Elon Musk 2026-08-05; from gnews:baaf41a9a32da4e5efcfd131 2026-08-05] SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX will build its AI services exclusively on NVIDIA systems going forward.\\n    Quote: CEO Elon Musk told investors during the company's first-ever earnings call that going forward, SpaceX will build its AI services exclusively on Nvidia's systems.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1417, e1457; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1437 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com, reporting Elon Musk's remarks; original source Elon Musk 2026-08-05; from gnews:baaf41a9a32da4e5efcfd131 2026-08-05] SpaceX expects its compute capacity to exceed 2 GW by the end of 2026.\\n    Quote: According to the CEO, SpaceX expects to end the year with more than 2 gigawatts of compute\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1508; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1438 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com, reporting Elon Musk's remarks; original source Elon Musk 2026-08-05; from gnews:baaf41a9a32da4e5efcfd131 2026-08-05] SpaceX anticipates having close to 10 GW of computing capacity by the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: and anticipates having close to 10 gigawatts of computing capacity by the end of next year.\\n- e1439 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com, reporting Elon Musk's remarks; original source Elon Musk 2026-08-05; from gnews:baaf41a9a32da4e5efcfd131 2026-08-05] SpaceX plans to begin launching Starmind satellites in 2027.\\n    Quote: According to Musk, SpaceX will begin launching Starmind satellites next year.\\n- e1441 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:baaf41a9a32da4e5efcfd131 2026-08-05] SpaceX reported AI revenue of $2.6 billion, up 213% quarter over quarter and 247% year over year.\\n    Quote: SpaceX' Al revenues climbed 213% quarter over quarter and 247% since the same period last year to $2.6 billion.\\n- e1442 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source SpaceX, Google and Anthropic ; from gnews:baaf41a9a32da4e5efcfd131 2026-08-05] SpaceX entered into multibillion-dollar leasing agreements with Google and Anthropic for data-center capacity to power their AI services.\\n    Quote: SpaceX entered into multibillion-dollar leasing agreements with Google and Anthropic that will see the companies rent out SpaceX's data center capacity to power their AI services.\\n- e1445 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Elon Musk 2026-08-04; from gnews:7ce0b7c3361fc5060098941f 2026-08-05] SpaceX committed to use Nvidia AI chips exclusively, according to Elon Musk\\u2019s comments referenced by AMD CEO Lisa Su on August 5, 2026.\\n    Quote: Advanced Micro Devices CEO Lisa Su on Wednesday brushed off comments from Elon Musk that SpaceX was committing to exclusively use Nvidia artificial intelligence chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1417, e1451; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1446 [fact; CNBC; original source AMD ; from gnews:7ce0b7c3361fc5060098941f 2026-08-05] AMD reported second-quarter 2026 revenue of $11.54 billion, up 50% year over year and above analyst expectations of $11.28 billion.\\n    Quote: AMD revenue climbed 50% to $11.54 billion in the second quarter, ahead of analyst expectations of $11.28 billion, in a sign of the company's central position in the market for AI chips.\\n- e1447 [fact; CNBC; original source AMD ; from gnews:7ce0b7c3361fc5060098941f 2026-08-05] AMD reported second-quarter 2026 Data Center sales of $6.7 billion, up 107% year over year, driven by CPU and GPU sales.\\n    Quote: Data center sales drove much of the company's growth, up 107% on an annual basis to $6.7 billion, which the company attributed to central processing unit and graphics processing unit sales.\\n- e1448 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Lisa Su 2026-08-04; from gnews:7ce0b7c3361fc5060098941f 2026-08-05] AMD CEO Lisa Su forecast server revenue growth of 80% year over year in the second half of 2026 and more than 70% in 2027.\\n    Quote: Su told analysts on the earnings call Tuesday that the company expects server revenue to grow 80% year-over-year in the second half of 2026 and more than 70% for 2027.\\n- e1449 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Lisa Su 2026-08-05; from gnews:7ce0b7c3361fc5060098941f 2026-08-05] AMD CEO Lisa Su said customers were giving AMD more opportunity to plan server supply.\\n    Quote: Su told CNBC on Wednesday that its customers are giving the company \\\"much more opportunity to plan\\\" with server supply.\\n- e1450 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Lisa Su 2026-08-05; from gnews:7ce0b7c3361fc5060098941f 2026-08-05] AMD CEO Lisa Su said AMD was investing across its supply chain, including backend and ecosystem investments, to increase supply to meet customers\\u2019 requests.\\n    Quote: \\\"As a result, we're making investments across the supply chain,\\\" she said. \\\"We're working with our partners. We're making backend investments. We're making the investments in the entire ecosystem, such that we'll be able to supply at the much higher rate that our customers are asking for.\\\"\\n- e1451 [party_statement; Elon Musk, quoted by Tom\\u2019s Hardware from SpaceX\\u2019s earnings call; original source Elon Musk 2026-08-04; from gnews:f2bbeb9e369dca841a5a0381 2026-08-05] Elon Musk said SpaceX and xAI had decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA going forward.\\n    Quote: Going forward, we have decided to build exclusively on Nvidia, because we think the Vera Rubin architecture is the best architecture\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1445, e1495; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1456 [fact; CNBC; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:4a98f08dff6ec85e82597c02 2026-08-05] SpaceX\\u2019s capital expenditures rose sixfold to $18.4 billion, with most spending directed to AI.\\n    Quote: Capital expenditures jumped sixfold to $18.4 billion, with the majority going toward AI.\\n- e1457 [party_statement; CNBC; Elon Musk is quoted directly; original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:4a98f08dff6ec85e82597c02 2026-08-05] Elon Musk said SpaceX would use Nvidia processors exclusively for its AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Musk's pledge that SpaceX would only use Nvidia processors for its AI infrastructure sent the chipmaker's shares up more than 3%. \\\"We're exclusive to Nvidia,\\\" he said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1436; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1458 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:4a98f08dff6ec85e82597c02 2026-08-05] Michael Burry argued that financing arrangements fueling AI-infrastructure demand may prove unsustainable.\\n    Quote: Burry has been among Wall Street's most outspoken skeptics of the artificial intelligence boom, arguing that demand for AI infrastructure is being fueled by financing arrangements that may prove unsustainable.\\n- e1467 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:005ecb2260c71807a6d9b7c1 2026-08-06] NVIDIA said data center revenue from hyperscale buyers, about half of data center revenue, grew 12% sequentially in fiscal Q1 2027.\\n    Quote: hyperscale buyers, about half of data center revenue, grew 12% sequentially\\n- e1468 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:005ecb2260c71807a6d9b7c1 2026-08-06] NVIDIA said data center revenue from its AI-cloud, industrial and enterprise group grew 31% sequentially in fiscal Q1 2027.\\n    Quote: while the AI-cloud, industrial and enterprise group grew 31%.\\n- e1472 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:005ecb2260c71807a6d9b7c1 2026-08-06] NVIDIA\\u2019s outlook assumes no China data center compute revenue.\\n    Quote: the outlook carries no China data center compute revenue\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e851; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1476 [party_statement; Nokia; original source Zankore by Indosat ; from gnews:211ef1e93536bca4527f679e 2026-08-06] Zankore by Indosat set a deployment target of 1 GW of NVIDIA DSX AI Factory capacity.\\n    Quote: With a deployment target of 1 gigawatt (GW) of NVIDIA DSX AI Factory capacity, Zankore by Indosat lays the foundation for one of the region\\u2019s largest AI infrastructure platforms which will enable the next phase of agentic and enterprise AI adoption at scale.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1478; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1477 [party_statement; Nokia; original source Zankore by Indosat ; from gnews:211ef1e93536bca4527f679e 2026-08-06] Zankore plans to deliver approximately 200 MW of AI capacity in the first half of 2027, powered by NVIDIA GB300 NVL72.\\n    Quote: Zankore will deliver approximately 200 megawatts (MW) of AI capacity in the first half of 2027, powered by NVIDIA GB300 NVL72.\\n- e1478 [fact; Fortune, reporting by Melissa Hancock; original source Zankore ; from gnews:5da1731c30b6d1c3a1602cb3 2026-08-06] Zankore plans to contract 1 GW of AI capacity over the next three years, using NVIDIA technology.\\n    Quote: The platform, known as Zankore, aims to contract 1 gigawatt (GW) of AI capacity over the next three years, powered by Nvidia\\u2019s latest technology.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1476; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1479 [fact; Fortune, reporting by Melissa Hancock; original source Zankore ; from gnews:5da1731c30b6d1c3a1602cb3 2026-08-06] Zankore has secured customers for about 200 MW of AI capacity planned for H1 2027.\\n    Quote: It has already secured blue-chip customers for around 200 MW of AI capacity planned for the first half of 2027.\\n- e1480 [third_party_view; Fortune, reporting by Melissa Hancock; original source Zankore ; from gnews:5da1731c30b6d1c3a1602cb3 2026-08-06] Zankore is forecast to generate about $13 billion of revenue and $9 billion of cumulative EBITDA over five years.\\n    Quote: The venture is forecast to generate around $13 billion in revenue and $9 billion in cumulative EBITDA over five years.\\n- e1481 [party_statement; Ooredoo Group CEO Aziz Aluthman Fakhroo, quoted by Fortune; original source Aziz Aluthman Fakhroo ; from gnews:5da1731c30b6d1c3a1602cb3 2026-08-06] Ooredoo CEO Aziz Aluthman Fakhroo said IOH\\u2019s NVIDIA-powered AI cloud services will own and operate about 28 MW of GPU capacity by Q3 2026.\\n    Quote: Fakhroo said that, by the third quarter of this year, IOH\\u2019s Nvidia-powered AI cloud services will own and operate around 28 MW of GPU capacity.\\n- e1482 [fact; Fortune, reporting by Melissa Hancock; original source IOH ; from gnews:5da1731c30b6d1c3a1602cb3 2026-08-06] IOH\\u2019s neocloud business generated $33 million of revenue in H1 2026, versus $28 million for all of 2025.\\n    Quote: In the first half of 2026, IOH\\u2019s existing neocloud business raked in $33 million in revenue , surpassing the $28 million it recorded for all of 2025.\\n- e1483 [fact; Fortune, reporting by Melissa Hancock; original source IOH ; from gnews:5da1731c30b6d1c3a1602cb3 2026-08-06] IOH\\u2019s neocloud business has $1.2\\u2013$1.5 billion of contracted revenue for the coming years.\\n    Quote: It has between $1.2 and $1.5 billion in contracted revenue for the coming years.\\n- e1484 [party_statement; Ooredoo Group CEO Aziz Aluthman Fakhroo, quoted by Fortune; original source Aziz Aluthman Fakhroo ; from gnews:5da1731c30b6d1c3a1602cb3 2026-08-06] Ooredoo CEO Aziz Aluthman Fakhroo said demand for Zankore\\u2019s initially planned 200 MW of compute capacity exceeds 200 MW.\\n    Quote: Fakhroo said that demand for Zankore\\u2019s initially planned 200 MW of compute capacity exceeds that figure.\\n- e1485 [party_statement; Ooredoo Group CEO Aziz Aluthman Fakhroo, quoted by Fortune; original source Aziz Aluthman Fakhroo ; from gnews:5da1731c30b6d1c3a1602cb3 2026-08-06] Ooredoo CEO Aziz Aluthman Fakhroo said Zankore was discussing multi-million-dollar contracts with prime customers.\\n    Quote: We\\u2019re talking about multi-million-dollar contracts from prime customers, so we\\u2019re confident that this is a very high-value proposition\\n- e1486 [third_party_view; Fortune, citing Wood Mackenzie; original source Wood Mackenzie ; from gnews:5da1731c30b6d1c3a1602cb3 2026-08-06] Wood Mackenzie projected Southeast Asia data-center power demand to rise from 2.6 GW in 2025 to 10.7 GW by 2035, driven partly by AI workloads.\\n    Quote: Southeast Asia\\u2019s data center capacity demand is expected to grow\\u202frapidly, driven by AI workloads, digital transformation, and cloud adoption, with total regional power demand projected to quadruple from 2.6 GW in 2025 to 10.7 GW by 2035, according to Wood Mackenzie.\\n- e1487 [fact; sdxcentral.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-06; from gnews:8e8554fa361143fbd7783fc6 2026-08-06] NVIDIA published the cuFile API on GitHub, with Google, Intel, and Meta joining as core maintainers.\\n    Quote: The cuFile API was published to GitHub, with Google, Intel, and Meta joining the chip giant as core maintainers.\\n- e1488 [fact; sdxcentral.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-06; from gnews:8e8554fa361143fbd7783fc6 2026-08-06] NVIDIA open-sourced its SCADA vertical storage software stack.\\n    Quote: Nvidia also open-sourced the scaled accelerated data access (SCADA) vertical storage software stack that sits beneath the API.\\n- e1495 [party_statement; TechRepublic; original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:21a8daa8b823bcbbfe13cbf8 2026-08-06] SpaceX plans to build its computing infrastructure exclusively on Nvidia hardware, Musk said; he praised the forthcoming Vera Rubin architecture.\\n    Quote: During SpaceX\\u2019s first public earnings call, Musk said the company plans to build its computing infrastructure \\u201cexclusively\\u201d on Nvidia hardware, praising the chipmaker\\u2019s forthcoming Vera Rubin architecture as the best AI computing platform available.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1451; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1496 [party_statement; TechRepublic, reporting a statement attributed to Elon Musk by Business Insider; original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:21a8daa8b823bcbbfe13cbf8 2026-08-06] Musk said SpaceX expects to receive a significant share of Nvidia\\u2019s GPU output next year; the allocation\\u2019s size was not disclosed.\\n    Quote: Musk said SpaceX expects to receive a significant share of Nvidia\\u2019s GPU output next year, according to Business Insider.\\n- e1497 [fact; TechRepublic, citing a SpaceX SEC filing; original source SpaceX and Google ; from gnews:21a8daa8b823bcbbfe13cbf8 2026-08-06] Under a disclosed agreement, Google will pay SpaceX up to $920 million per month as capacity containing approximately 110,000 Nvidia GPUs ramps up.\\n    Quote: One disclosed agreement covers access to infrastructure containing approximately 110,000 Nvidia GPUs, with Google agreeing to pay SpaceX as much as $920 million per month as capacity ramps up, according to a SpaceX SEC filing .\\n- e1498 [fact; TechRepublic, citing a SpaceX SEC filing; original source SpaceX and Anthropic ; from gnews:21a8daa8b823bcbbfe13cbf8 2026-08-06] A separate agreement gives Anthropic access to infrastructure containing approximately 325,000 Nvidia GPUs, with payments to SpaceX of up to $1.25 billion per month as capacity comes online.\\n    Quote: A separate agreement with Anthropic, disclosed in a separate SpaceX SEC filing , involves access to infrastructure containing approximately 325,000 Nvidia GPUs and payments of up to $1.25 billion per month as capacity comes online.\\n- e1501 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8dd001fa7bb111253999c4c 2026-08-07] NVIDIA cut its list of authorized Asian AI-chip buyers by more than half after introducing tougher screening requirements; the item gives no exact date.\\n    Quote: The company recently cut its list of authorized Asian AI-chip buyers by more than half after introducing tougher screening requirements, according to the Financial Times.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e446; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1502 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8dd001fa7bb111253999c4c 2026-08-07] NVIDIA reported fiscal first-quarter FY2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year, and Data Center segment revenue of $75.2 billion, up 92%.\\n    Quote: Fiscal first-quarter revenue surged 85% to $81.6 billion , while Data Center revenue jumped 92% to $75.2 billion .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e204; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1503 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8dd001fa7bb111253999c4c 2026-08-07] NVIDIA guided to roughly $91 billion in second-quarter FY2027 total revenue, assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: Nvidia expects second-quarter revenue of roughly $91 billion , notably assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1506 [fact; Zacks.com; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:c475c48b857a2b98a73dd2c4 2026-08-07] SpaceX reported capital expenditures of $18.4 billion in Q2, up from $10.1 billion in Q1.\\n    Quote: SpaceX\\u2019s CapEx climbed to $18.4 billion in the second quarter from $10.1 billion in Q1,\\n- e1507 [fact; Zacks.com; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:c475c48b857a2b98a73dd2c4 2026-08-07] SpaceX earmarked approximately $15.8 billion of Q2 capital expenditures for AI infrastructure and data-center expansion.\\n    Quote: with approximately $15.8 billion earmarked for AI infrastructure and data center expansion.\\n- e1508 [fact; Zacks.com; original source SpaceX ; from gnews:c475c48b857a2b98a73dd2c4 2026-08-07] SpaceX signed $6.7 billion in additional cloud and compute contracts since the end of Q2 and expects year-end compute capacity to exceed 2 gigawatts.\\n    Quote: On a positive note, SpaceX has signed $6.7 billion in additional cloud/compute contracts since the end of Q2 and expects year-end compute capacity to exceed 2 gigawatts, suggesting the spending is tied to a rapidly expanding backlog rather than speculative capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1437; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1509 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:51e243641cee0eb43681f82b 2026-08-07] AMD acquired Taalas, gaining access to an inference-chip design that embeds model weights in silicon and could reduce memory reliance and improve speed and energy efficiency.\\n    Quote: The acquisition gives AMD access to a chip design approach that embeds AI model weights directly into silicon, reducing reliance on external memory and potentially improving speed and energy efficiency.\\n- e1510 [party_statement; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:51e243641cee0eb43681f82b 2026-08-07] AMD plans to incorporate Taalas technology with its Instinct accelerators and EPYC processors in Helios rack-scale systems as part of its AI infrastructure strategy.\\n    Quote: AMD plans to incorporate Taalas technology into its broader AI infrastructure strategy, combining it with Instinct accelerators and EPYC processors within its Helios rack-scale systems.\\n- e1513 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:22623bc422187268324329ad 2026-08-07] Firmus plans to use the equity-round proceeds to accelerate its Project Southgate AI training and inference factory rollout in Australia and expand into Asia-Pacific, including Indonesia.\\n    Quote: The Australian company will use the proceeds to accelerate Project Southgate, its rollout of AI training and inference factories across Australia. The capital also funds expansion into Asia-Pacific markets, including a recently announced development in Indonesia.\\n- e1521 [fact; 247wallst.com, Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e5ae2752ae901af3268b2a3 2026-08-08] NVIDIA reported FY2027 Q1 Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion.\\n    Quote: Data Center alone hitting $75.246 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e273; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1522 [fact; 247wallst.com, Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e5ae2752ae901af3268b2a3 2026-08-08] NVIDIA Data Center networking revenue grew 199% year over year in Q1 FY2027.\\n    Quote: Networking inside that segment climbed 199%\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e341; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1529 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, Alex Sirois; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e5ae2752ae901af3268b2a3 2026-08-08] The author reports that NVIDIA had zero Data Center revenue from China.\\n    Quote: zero China Data Center revenue.\\n- e1530 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, Alex Sirois; original source 247wallst.com, Alex Sirois 2026-08-08; from gnews:1e5ae2752ae901af3268b2a3 2026-08-08] The author calculates NVIDIA Data Center revenue at 92.3% of total revenue and says analyst price targets are near $302.83.\\n    Quote: Its 92.3% Data Center revenue mix is a moat, and analyst targets sit near $302.83.\\n- e1532 [fact; Vandita Jadeja, 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0214359f2efcb2f2aad79168 2026-08-08] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue hit $75.246 billion, up 92% YoY\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e279; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1533 [fact; Vandita Jadeja, 24/7 Wall St.; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0214359f2efcb2f2aad79168 2026-08-08] NVIDIA Data Center Networking revenue was $14.8 billion in Q1 FY2027, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center Networking surging 199% YoY to $14.8 billion\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e821; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v14 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish, judged 2026-08-08\\n    Comparison point stated by that judgement: Bloomberg/Yahoo Finance market estimates of about $393 billion for FY2027, corresponding to rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion).\\n    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): About $410 billion (roughly $405\\u2013415 billion): Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion plus Q2 guidance of $91 billion, carrying the guided sequential growth through the remaining quarters, implies a base near $386 billion; customer demand and spending evidence supports a modest uplift.\\n    Reasons (excerpt): The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which sits in rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Carrying forward the guided growth from Q1 revenue of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion gives a roughly $386 billion operating base; strong customer spending and orders support a modest uplift, while supply constraints, China exposure, and spending that bypasses NVIDIA limit it. The implied estimate of about $410 billion crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but does not support $450 billion. The $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin orders span products and periods, and \\n\\n[Themes in force, 7]\\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-31; reasons: No explicit market consensus is supplied. The observable status reference\\u2014Chinese authorization is not established, with a report that Beijing refused purchases\\u2014sits at rung 2. The forecast is rung 1 because reports specifically describe plans to permit selected Chinese AI firms to buy limited H200 \\n- v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: The observable reference is rung 2: the bill had passed the House but was still awaiting Senate action on July 23; no separate market consensus is supplied. That status is not itself proof of the year-end outcome, but the evidence shows no Senate action or schedule in the remaining period. The White\\n- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan's Ministry of Economic Affairs introduce performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evi\\n- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan introduce a criminal ban on AI chip exports to China by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-29; reasons: No market consensus is supplied; the status-quo reference is no criminal ban by the deadline (rung 2). Taiwan's statement that it is considering a ban is a meaningful signal toward Yes, but it supplies neither a formal proposal nor a timetable. On the evidence in force, introduction by 2027-01-31 is\\n- v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced U.S. chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-07; reasons: The investigation was formally underway in late July, and the subsequent reports describe alleged access to Blackwell chips and tighter screening, not a public Commerce conclusion or a completion timetable. On balance, the evidence makes the investigation remaining publicly unconcluded by 2027-01-31\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-08-06; reasons: The reference is the four hyperscalers\\u2019 roughly $650 billion 2026 plan, which sits in rung 3. Updated company-level figures raise the implied total, but not enough to cross the $700 billion boundary into rung 4. Broader estimates for tech-giant or AI-infrastructure spending are not sized specificall\\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-08-01; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, following a projected $650 billion in 2026; that reference is in rung 3. The other evidence supports continued growth, including Alphabet\\u2019s statement that it will increase capex further in 2027 and Microsoft\\u2019s capex-grow\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The market reference is about $393 billion of total FY2027 revenue; applying the roughly 90% Data Center share estimate puts the segment reference near $354 billion, in rung 3. The updated total-revenue dependency implies approximately $400\\u2013410 billion for NVIDIA as a whole. Applying the roughly 90\\u201392% Data Center share indicated by the segment mix gives about $360\\u2013377 billion; the SemiAnalysis view that second-half Data Center revenue could beat estimates by 20%, plus demand and capacity disclosures, supports a modest uplift to roughly $375\\u2013385 billion, with a central estimate around $380 billion. That remains below the $400 billion rung boundary, and the segment forecast does not exceed the whole-company forecast. Competition and China-related risks temper the upside.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"linked-1\", 8920, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v16 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA total CPU revenue in FY2027\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total CPU revenue will NVIDIA generate in FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total CPU revenue is greater than $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total CPU revenue is greater than $20 billion and at most $25 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total CPU revenue is at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total CPU revenue is at least $10 billion and less than $15 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total CPU revenue is less than $10 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 44 items]\\n[Not shown: 14 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e443 [party_statement; trefis.com, quoting NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:48ea00c0bf7c01bdb80850bb 2026-07-15] NVIDIA management said it has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year (FY2027).\\n    Quote: visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e896; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e489 [fact; Los Alamos National Laboratory; original source Los Alamos National Laboratory 2026-07-16; from gnews:afcb313dd53b5e246ed37d78 2026-07-16] Los Alamos scheduled delivery of the first NVIDIA Vera CPU blade for initial testbed deployment in summer 2026.\\n    Quote: The delivery of the first Vera CPU blade for initial testbed deployment is scheduled for this summer at Los Alamos.\\n- e612 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c840e055f0c00ff7c6fe18e9 2026-07-20] NVIDIA management is targeting a $20 billion standalone CPU business from its Vera CPU, paired with Rubin GPUs; no target period is specified.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) itself has charted a path to an entry in the market of CPUs. With the custom ARM-based Vera CPU paired with Rubin GPUs, management is targeting a $20 billion standalone CPU business.\\n- e664 [party_statement; CNBC.com; original source NVIDIA representatives ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] NVIDIA representatives said Vera CPUs were delivered to OpenAI, Anthropic and SpaceX in June 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia representatives said Vera chips were delivered to clients, including OpenAI , Anthropic , and SpaceX , in June.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e723; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e666 [third_party_view; CNBC.com; original source Wolfe Research ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] Wolfe Research forecast a Vera CPU average selling price of about $5,000 per chip and shipments of about 1.3 million units in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: Wolfe Research said in May that it expected the average selling price to be about $5,000 per Vera chip, and forecast that Nvidia would ship about 1.3 million of them this year.\\n- e667 [third_party_view; CNBC.com; original source Gartner analyst Kevin Knox (market-share figures are reported by CNBC.com) ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] Gartner analyst Kevin Knox said AMD was gaining share in server CPUs; AMD reportedly held about 33% of the market and Intel 66.8%.\\n    Quote: Gartner analyst Kevin Knox said that AMD is currently the company to beat in enterprise AI server CPUs. AMD reportedly holds about 33% of the market for server CPUs, and Intel has 66.8%, but AMD is gaining share and has deep relationships with hyperscalers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e939; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e668 [party_statement; CNBC.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:69c31079960ffe4f9805834c 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said OpenAI planned to deploy Vera CPUs in large quantities starting in calendar Q3 2026.\\n    Quote: Coutand said that Vera was in \\\"early innings\\\" of adoption. The company didn't list any major cloud service providers except Oracle on its list of partners but said OpenAI plans to deploy Vera chips in large quantities starting this quarter.\\n- e694 [third_party_view; WIRED; original source WIRED 2026-07-21; from gnews:e4b99d721a7a9c46cefa4038 2026-07-21] AMD significantly increased its share of the data-center CPU market over the two years preceding 2026-07-21.\\n    Quote: Over the past two years, AMD has significantly grown its share of the market for CPUs used in data centers.\\n- e715 [third_party_view; SDxCentral; original source Gartner ; from gnews:2838708eba4178345bbc70e8 2026-07-21] Gartner named AMD the frontrunner in the increasingly diverse enterprise AI server CPU market; no period was specified.\\n    Quote: AMD finds itself as the Gartner-crowned frontrunner for an enterprise AI server CPU market that\\u2019s becoming increasingly diverse.\\n- e723 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2 2026-07-21] NVIDIA said it delivered Vera chips to OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceX in June 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said Vera chips were delivered to clients including OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceX in June, according to CNBC .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e664; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e724 [fact; qz.com; original source Hewlett Packard Enterprise ; from gnews:9ec05dc5e996b554b6f7a5f2 2026-07-21] Hewlett Packard Enterprise unveiled the Vera-powered ProLiant Compute DL394 Gen12 server, with availability expected in fall 2026.\\n    Quote: A separate Computex announcement saw Hewlett Packard Enterprise unveil a Vera-powered server , the ProLiant Compute DL394 Gen12, targeted at agentic AI orchestration and reinforcement learning workloads, with availability expected in fall 2026.\\n- e793 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source Bank of America Research ; from gnews:55b3b854d30ecb73679c2088 2026-07-22] Bank of America Research forecasts the server CPU total addressable market will reach $170 billion by 2030, roughly four times current levels.\\n    Quote: Bank of America Research sees the server CPU total addressable market reaching $170 billion by 2030, roughly four times current levels\\n- e794 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:55b3b854d30ecb73679c2088 2026-07-22] AMD estimates EPYC 9965 delivers approximately 2.4 times the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA Vera in a modeled 100kW deployment, and projects EPYC 6 at roughly 3.3 times Vera's total throughput.\\n    Quote: AMD estimates its EPYC 9965 (Turin) chip delivers approximately 2.4x the rack-level throughput of NVIDIA's Vera baseline in a modeled 100kW deployment, with the next-generation EPYC 6 (Venice) projected to extend that advantage to roughly 3.3x total throughput, according to BofA's characterization of AMD's own estimates.\\n- e813 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:eab64955df3b72569157c4bf 2026-07-23] NVIDIA is anticipating $20 billion of stand-alone Vera server CPU revenue in 2026.\\n    Quote: It is going to sell its Vera server central processing unit (CPU) as a stand-alone product, anticipating $20 billion in revenue from this product alone in 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e411; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e814 [third_party_view; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Mercury Research ; from gnews:eab64955df3b72569157c4bf 2026-07-23] Mercury Research estimates Intel held just over two-thirds of the server CPU market in Q1 2026.\\n    Quote: Mercury Research estimates that Intel controlled just over two-thirds of server CPUs in Q1 this year.\\n- e884 [third_party_view; Jake Roach, Tom\\u2019s Hardware, quoting AMD\\u2019s Ravi Kuppuswany; original source Ravi Kuppuswany, AMD corporate VP of compute and enterprise solutions 2026-07-24; from gnews:a60415628b7bd46d7f0f4a48 2026-07-24] AMD corporate VP Ravi Kuppuswany said AMD\\u2019s Venice CPUs showed a 20% per-core performance advantage over NVIDIA\\u2019s Vera CPU in AMD\\u2019s estimated SPEC comparison, with tuning unfinished.\\n    Quote: \\u201cWe were actually being a little conservative. I actually thought we were beating them by smaller numbers than what I have here\\u2026 we thought we\\u2019d have at least a 10% advantage. What we\\u2019re finding is\\u2026 we have 20% advantage, and we have not even finished completely tuning.\\u201d\\n- e885 [third_party_view; Jake Roach, Tom\\u2019s Hardware; original source AMD ; from gnews:a60415628b7bd46d7f0f4a48 2026-07-24] AMD\\u2019s slide claimed Venice had 2.2 times Vera\\u2019s throughput in a 2P comparison; the article says the throughput comparison used a 256-core Epyc 9996 against Vera\\u2019s 88-core configuration.\\n    Quote: Kuppuswany\\u2019s comments overlaid the slide you can see above, claiming 2.2x higher throughput with Venice compared to Vera, and 1.2x faster per-core performance.\\n- e886 [third_party_view; Jake Roach, Tom\\u2019s Hardware; original source NVIDIA (for Vera\\u2019s score); AMD (for the Epyc score) ; from gnews:a60415628b7bd46d7f0f4a48 2026-07-24] NVIDIA\\u2019s published SPEC results gave Vera an overall score of 925, while AMD said its 96-core High Frequency Epyc achieved 1,210 in its estimated comparison.\\n    Quote: Nvidia\\u2019s results show Vera achieving a score of 925 overall. AMD says a 96-core High Frequency Epyc CPU achieved a score of 1,210.\\n- e887 [third_party_view; Jake Roach, Tom\\u2019s Hardware; original source Jake Roach, Tom\\u2019s Hardware 2026-07-24; from gnews:a60415628b7bd46d7f0f4a48 2026-07-24] The article calculated that AMD\\u2019s per-core comparison implied about an 18.8% lead over Vera, based on approximately 6.3 versus 5.3 per-core scores in 2P configurations.\\n    Quote: AMD divided that score by the number of cores; about 6.3 for AMD, and about 5.3 for Nvidia (remember these are 2P configurations). AMD says 1.2x, which actually translates to about an 18.8% lead.\\n- e896 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-05-20; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera opens a $200 billion market for NVIDIA and that the company has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, FY2027.\\n    Quote: Colette Kress, Executive Vice President and CFO, said Vera \\u201copens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA, a market we have never addressed before,\\u201d adding: \\u201cWe have visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, setting us up to become the world-leading CPU supplier.\\u201d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e443, e2439; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e897 [party_statement; tikr.com (analyst), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO 2026-05-20; from gnews:f91cb77d6fea19116da4188b 2026-07-24] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the nearly $20 billion CPU revenue visibility for FY2027 is for standalone CPU sales.\\n    Quote: CEO Jensen Huang: \\u201cThe $20 billion is for standalone CPU.\\u201d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e429; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1018 [third_party_view; Morningstar analyst; original source Morningstar Equity Research 2026-07-27; from gnews:ba8e52b6f010b77f770e4483 2026-07-27] Morningstar said AMD\\u2019s recent upward revision to its server CPU market forecast suggested high demand for agentic AI and a need for substantially more AI infrastructure over the next few years.\\n    Quote: AMD\\u2019s recent boost to its server CPU market forecast suggests to us that agentic AI is in high demand and that much more AI infrastructure will be required over the next few years.\\n- e1338 [third_party_view; Insider Monkey; original source Insider Monkey 2026-07-31; from gnews:39deea97b690ac7b56dada0b 2026-07-31] The author estimates NVIDIA's Vera CPU revenue at $20 billion in the current fiscal year, against a target market of $200 billion.\\n    Quote: Its new Vera server CPU targets a $200 billion market, with $20 billion in Vera revenue expected this fiscal year alone despite only recently shipping as a standalone product.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e411; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1473 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:005ecb2260c71807a6d9b7c1 2026-08-06] NVIDIA described a $200 billion CPU market that it said it had never addressed before.\\n    Quote: a $200 billion CPU market the company says it has never addressed before\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e200; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2239 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Advanced Micro Devices ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] AMD expected the server CPU market to grow more than 50% annually through 2030 and generate $220 billion in annual revenue.\\n    Quote: AMD noted on the earnings call that it now expects the server CPU market to grow by more than 50% annually through 2030, generating $220 billion in annual revenue.\\n- e2240 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Advanced Micro Devices ; from gnews:36c5541b78e9258d831613b3 2026-08-21] AMD's current $220 billion annual server CPU market estimate for 2030 is above its $60 billion estimate given in November 2025.\\n    Quote: That's well above the $60 billion estimate for the server CPU market that AMD delivered in November 2025.\\n- e2262 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:822e18e27e42a11a5a7c692a 2026-08-22] NVIDIA management cited a $200 billion opportunity for Vera CPU products from agentic AI demand; the article does not specify a period or define the opportunity measure.\\n    Quote: The guide beat analyst estimates of $86.84 billion, with management countering claims of a growth slowdown through a $200 billion CPU opportunity for the Vera platform through agentic AI demand.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e620; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2294 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source SpaceXAI 2026-08-24; from nvidianews:spacexai-adopts-nvidia-vera-cpu-to-accelerate-agentic-ai-at-massive-scale 2026-08-24] SpaceXAI plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs for its next-generation agentic AI applications.\\n    Quote: SpaceXAI will deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs to accelerate its next generation of agentic AI applications\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2301, e2353; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2311 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6c6c4a4aa3b22bf433133e4d 2026-08-24] NVIDIA expects stand-alone Vera server CPU revenue of $20 billion in FY2027.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now selling its Vera server CPU as a stand-alone product, and it expects to generate $20 billion in revenue from its sales this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e429; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2353 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c7b38f10c7dbec17e7667d39 2026-08-24] NVIDIA said SpaceXAI will use Vera CPUs as it expands computing infrastructure for next-generation agentic applications.\\n    Quote: Nvidia (NVDA) said SpaceXAI will use its Vera CPUs as the AI company expands computing infrastructure for next-generation agentic applications.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2294, e2504; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2405 [party_statement; NVIDIA, in a company technical blog by Eduardo Alvarez and Praveen Menon; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-24; from gnews:caf5e64df9edf2e567ad2813 2026-08-24] NVIDIA said telemetry from 163,594 agentic sessions showed that more than 97% had unique trajectory profiles, making fleets with multiple specialized CPU designs impractical.\\n    Quote: Based on telemetry from 163,594 agentic sessions, over 97% of sessions showed unique trajectory profiles (Figure 1). This variability makes it impractical to right-size a fleet using multiple specialized CPU design points.\\n- e2439 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3abc2e72d5a960c883f535af 2026-08-25] NVIDIA estimates the addressable CPU market at roughly $200 billion and expects its CPU business to generate about $20 billion in revenue in FY2027.\\n    Quote: The company estimates the addressable CPU market at roughly $200 billion and expects its CPU business to generate about $20 billion in revenues this year, as quoted on Yahoo Finance.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e896; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2479 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:093b9d63d05081b51dec3c77 2026-08-25] AMD projected server CPU revenue growth of 80% year over year in the second half of 2026 and 70% in 2027.\\n    Quote: AMD projected second-half 2026 server CPU revenue to grow 80% year over year and 70% in 2027.\\n- e2542 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis as of the Q2 FY2027 call.\\n    Quote: Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 has been a great success, with revenue on a trailing 12-month basis, exceeding $5 billion.\\n- e2543 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said Vera CPU shipments were underway to lead partners including OCI and SpaceX AI, with AWS shipments starting in Q2 FY2027, and expected deployment by every major hyperscaler, NeoCloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\\n    Quote: We expect Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, NeoCloud, AI lab, and system OEM with shipments already underway to our lead partners, including OCI, SpaceX AI and starting this quarter, AWS.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2522, e2850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2544 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA estimated total server CPU demand at approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028.\\n    Quote: We continue to see demand for approximately $20 billion in total server CPUs. And based on our customer demand and improving supply outlook, our preliminary expectation is for CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal '28\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2735, e2794; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2601 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source SpaceXAI ; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000073:q2fy27pr.htm 2026-08-26] SpaceXAI plans to deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs for its next generation of agentic AI applications.\\n    Quote: Announced that SpaceXAI will deploy NVIDIA Vera CPUs to accelerate its next generation of agentic AI applications.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2504; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2636 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted in the CNBC interview transcript; original source Amazon Web Services, as reported by Jensen Huang ; from extra:b23f73a08a535993119430f5 2026-08-26] Jensen Huang said Amazon Web Services was going to buy millions of CPUs; no purchase period or exact quantity was specified.\\n    Quote: They're going to buy millions of CPUs.\\n- e2659 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Raymond James analyst Simon Leopold ; from gnews:78164844d9e041d3ebd8d86b 2026-08-26] Raymond James analyst Simon Leopold estimated NVIDIA CPU revenue at roughly 3% of total sales currently.\\n    Quote: \\\"CPUs represent a low single-digit percent of Nvidia sales \\u2014 roughly 3% today\\n- e2660 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Raymond James analyst Simon Leopold ; from gnews:78164844d9e041d3ebd8d86b 2026-08-26] Raymond James analyst Simon Leopold forecast NVIDIA CPU revenue would reach approximately 5% of total revenue by 2028.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia's CPU revenue is becoming material within the CPU market, we expect it to reach approximately 5% of total revenue by 2028 in our model \\u2014 still a single-digit portion, yet the growth is the fastest among the elements we model.\\n- e2715 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:58da18a5e0a271a50e9bbdaf 2026-08-26] NVIDIA had said it expected CPU sales to reach $20 billion in the current fiscal year, FY2027.\\n    Quote: Last quarter, Nvidia said it expected CPU sales in the current fiscal year to hit $20 billion, instantly making it one of the world's leading CPU companies.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e429; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2730 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source Colette Kress 2026-08-26; from gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387 2026-08-26] AWS's planned additional 2 million-GPU deployment will include NVIDIA Vera CPUs, both integrated with Rubin products and deployed as standalone processors.\\n    Quote: The deployment will include Vera CPUs, some integrated with Rubin products and some deployed as standalone processors, according to Kress.\\n- e2735 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said Vera CPU was in full production and expected total CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028, citing customer demand and an improving supply outlook.\\n    Quote: The company also said it is in full production of its Vera CPU and expects CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal 2028, based on customer demand and its improving supply outlook.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2544; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2794 [party_statement; taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:2d41619b8a2902306decca58 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said demand for total server CPUs was approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028.\\n    Quote: \\u201cWe continue to see demand for approximately $20 billion in total server CPUs. Based on our customer demand and improving supply outlook, our preliminary expectation is for CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal 2028 \\u201d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2544; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v14 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 2 Bullish, judged 2026-08-26\\n    Comparison point stated by that judgement: LSEG analysts' FY2027 revenue estimate of $396 billion, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to less than $400 billion).\\n    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): FY2027 revenue of about $407 billion: $81.6 billion reported in Q1 plus $96.2 billion reported in Q2, $108 billion guided for Q3, and an estimated roughly $121 billion for Q4 by carrying forward the approximately 12.3% sequential growth implied by Q2-to-Q3 guidance.\\n    Reasons (excerpt): The FY2027 market reference is LSEG's $396 billion estimate, which sits in rung 3. The reported Q1 and Q2 revenues plus Q3 guidance imply about $407 billion for the year: carrying Q3's roughly 12.3% sequential growth forward gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion. That crosses the $400 billion boundary into rung 2, but remains below $450 billion. Memory, power and data-center capacity constraints and the longer-dated custom-chip threat temper the forecast; NVIDIA's approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU-sales outlook is already part of total revenue, not incremental to it.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 7]\\n- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-08-26; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus or Chinese authorization deadline is supplied, and NVIDIA says PRC restrictions have prevented sales of some U.S.-licensed products. But the evidence also reports that Beijing allowed limited H200 imports, including reports of permission for named Ch\\n- v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: No explicit market consensus or Senate timetable is supplied. The observable reference is that RASA had not passed the Senate as of August 19, which sits at rung 2. The alleged remote-access cases could add pressure for legislation, but the recent evidence reports no Senate action or scheduled vote.\\n- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan's Ministry of Economic Affairs introduce performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evi\\n- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan introduce a criminal ban on AI chip exports to China by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The status-quo reference is rung 2, and the evidence still makes no ban by the deadline more likely than a ban. Taiwan is considering a criminal ban, and the alleged export case\\u2014along with lawmakers\\u2019 and experts\\u2019 stated view that the law needs to change\\u2014adds pressure toward legislation. But the supp\\n- v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced U.S. chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is rung 2: the investigation is formally underway and described as likely to remain an ongoing point of contention, with no Commerce deadline or consensus supplied. The access allegations and Taiwanese smuggling enforcement show the issue remains active, but they do not report a Commer\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-08-26; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is about $760 billion for the four largest hyperscalers, which is rung 4; the $745 billion tracking estimate is also in rung 4. The evidence still implies roughly $760 billion: the direct four-company estimate anchors it, while the later top-five estimate of nearly $800 billion \\n- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion; judged 2026-08-26; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Bank of America's reported calendar-2027 consensus of $1.08 trillion, which sits in rung 3. The strongest new quantified forecast is NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion for the top five hyperscalers in 2027. As a rough scope adjustment, the disclosed 2026 figures put the four-company AI-infrastr\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management\\u2019s FY2027 visibility/expectation of about $20 billion in CPU revenue, which sits at rung 3 (at least $15 billion and at most $20 billion). The evidence still implies roughly $19\\u201320 billion: management\\u2019s FY2027 figure remains the strongest direct forecast, while Wolfe\\u2019s 1.3 million Vera-chip shipment estimate at about $5,000 per chip implies about $6.5 billion for calendar 2026 and does not establish a higher FY2027 total. Customer deliveries, OpenAI\\u2019s planned large Q3 deployment, and SpaceXAI\\u2019s announced use support adoption, but are not sized enough to lift the forecast above $20 billion. AMD\\u2019s larger market outlook supports demand, while its reported performance advantages add competitive risk. The implied figure remains within rung 3, and is consistent \\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\", \"role\": \"user\"}], \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"reasoning_effort\": \"medium\", \"response_format\": {\"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"judgement\", \"schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"changed\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"cites\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"cites_views\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"implied_outcome\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"evidence_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"evidence_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"reasons\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"reference\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"rung\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}, \"theme_pushes\": {\"items\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"push\": {\"enum\": [\"toward_first\", \"toward_last\", \"none\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"view_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"view_id\", \"push\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"why_changed\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"rung\", \"reference\", \"implied_outcome\", \"reasons\", \"cites\", \"cites_views\", \"pushes\", \"changed\", \"why_changed\", \"theme_pushes\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"strict\": true}, \"type\": \"json_schema\"}}]\n[\"linked-1\", 8925, {\"max_completion_tokens\": 16000, \"messages\": [{\"content\": \"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging a thesis's current rung. Use only the supplied evidence, dependency conclusions and themes in force. The rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\\n\\n- Rejudge from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently), the current conclusions of dependencies and the themes in force. Pieces in force that no recent judgement relied on are not shown; the previous conclusion carries them. Do not add to or subtract from the previous score. The previous conclusion is supplied only for comparison.\\n- Judge against a reference: what the market expects for this question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on the ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. State the implied figure or date, check it against the dependencies' conclusions shown (a part cannot exceed its whole, and a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share), compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\\n- \\u201cThemes in force\\u201d lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on this thesis and report it in theme_pushes with the direction it pushes this thesis (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \\u201cupdated since this thesis was last judged\\u201d may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\\n- A dependency marked \\u201cnot yet created\\u201d supplies no input. A dependency marked \\u201chistorical only\\u201d is closed without a successor; its closing conclusion is background only.\\n- Your conclusion must be consistent with the conclusions of the theses it depends on and the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open; a component cannot exceed its whole.\\n- Return the rung's one-based position; reference (what the market expects and which rung that is); implied_outcome (the figure or date the evidence implies and how you derived it, in one or two sentences); reasons (the gap between the two and why it does or does not cross a rung boundary); citations to supplied evidence and dependency IDs; each evidence item's push (toward_first, toward_last or none); theme_pushes; changed; and why_changed. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\\n- changed is true for a different rung or a change to the key facts or figures supporting the same rung, including a changed implied outcome. Repetition or another source for the same statement does not alone change the conclusion. The first judgement always has changed true.\\n\", \"role\": \"system\"}, {\"content\": \"Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\\\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 233 items]\\n[Not shown: 580 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e74 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA\\u2019s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\\n    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e169, e278, e602, e680, e771, e1708, e1716, e1790; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e109 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet ; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA\\u2019s AI computing products.\\n    Quote: The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors.\\n- e116 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] Micron said tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028.\\n    Quote: tight market conditions could last until 2028\\n- e127 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Industry surveys ; from gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a 2026-07-07] Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, versus roughly 30% currently, potentially reducing demand for NVIDIA accelerators in China.\\n    Quote: Chinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5].\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e153 [third_party_view; Bloomberg, reporting the Bloomberg Intelligence survey; original source Bloomberg Intelligence survey respondents 2026-07-07; from gnews:f3ba15f5e14b15ee61d7fddc 2026-07-08] Chinese company executives surveyed by Bloomberg Intelligence said they plan to allocate 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% currently.\\n    Quote: Executives in the country say they\\u2019ll allocate 46% of their budget for artificial intelligence accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months, up from 30% today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e127; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e162 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America reported that NVIDIA\\u2019s sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year over year, nearly twice the growth in cloud capital expenditures.\\n    Quote: The report also says Nvidia\\u2019s hyperscaler sales rose 115% year over year, nearly twice cloud capex growth, suggesting wallet-share gains rather than share loss.\\n- e163 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America modeled NVIDIA retaining roughly 65% to 70% or more of AI capital expenditures over time, with the remainder going to ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n    Quote: BofA still models Nvidia holding roughly 65% to 70% plus of AI capex over time, with the rest split across ASICs, AMD and other merchant vendors.\\n- e170 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management guided to accelerating Data Center segment revenue growth in the second quarter; no amount is stated.\\n    Quote: management guided for further revenue growth acceleration for the second quarter.\\n- e171 [party_statement; fool.com (Adam Spatacco); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:931ee0196ca609e637235fad 2026-07-09] NVIDIA management cited roughly $1 trillion of revenue visibility for Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across calendar 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Quote: Management has also articulated roughly $1 trillion in revenue visibility for its Blackwell and Vera Rubin processors across 2026 and 2027, anchored by multiyear commitments from hyperscalers and large enterprise customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e304; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e255 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management guided FY2027 Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, assuming no China Data Center revenue.\\n    Quote: Looking ahead, management guided Q2 revenue to approximately $91 billion, despite assuming zero China data-center revenue because of export restrictions.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202, e271, e292, e332, e350, e768, e1503; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e304 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from the Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, CEO Jensen Huang has said that he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from Nvidia's Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e171, e936, e2326; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e636 [party_statement; AMD told CNBC; original source AMD ; from gnews:b79717064762299fc9426a13 2026-07-20] AMD said it plans to book tens of billions of dollars in data-center AI revenue starting in 2027, with most of it from Helios.\\n    Quote: AMD told CNBC that it plans to book tens of billions in data center AI revenue starting in 2027, the majority coming from Helios.\\n- e828 [fact; TechCrunch; original source AMD and Anthropic 2026-07-22; from gnews:78152408aa7a67ee94806acd 2026-07-23] AMD and Anthropic announced a partnership to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of GPUs using Helios.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Anthropic and AMD announced a strategic partnership Wednesday to deploy up to two gigawatts of GPUs via the new rack system.\\n- e891 [fact; Alex Sirois; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 gigawatts of GPUs.\\n    Quote: OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 gigawatts of GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1077; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e976 [third_party_view; fool.com author Lyle Daly; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] SemiAnalysis forecast that NVIDIA Data Center revenue in the second half of FY2027 would exceed analyst estimates by 20%.\\n    Quote: And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\\n- e994 [third_party_view; Investing.com, quoting Bernstein analysts; original source Bernstein analysts ; from gnews:c871b9605dd781113ba40cef 2026-07-27] Bernstein analysts said the announced amounts primarily reflect NVIDIA and Broadcom securing memory supply and that the $500 billion-plus headline figure may overstate the near-term revenue impact for NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: \\\"We believe the announced amounts are more for memory and indicate the need for NVIDIA & Broadcom to secure memory supply,\\\" they wrote. While the read is highly constructive for SK Hynix, Bernstein warns the $500B+ headline figure may overstate the near-term revenue impact for Nvidia.\\n- e1457 [party_statement; CNBC; Elon Musk is quoted directly; original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:4a98f08dff6ec85e82597c02 2026-08-05] Elon Musk said SpaceX would use Nvidia processors exclusively for its AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Musk's pledge that SpaceX would only use Nvidia processors for its AI infrastructure sent the chipmaker's shares up more than 3%. \\\"We're exclusive to Nvidia,\\\" he said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1436, e2023, e2152; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1472 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:005ecb2260c71807a6d9b7c1 2026-08-06] NVIDIA\\u2019s outlook assumes no China data center compute revenue.\\n    Quote: the outlook carries no China data center compute revenue\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e851, e2785; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1497 [fact; TechRepublic, citing a SpaceX SEC filing; original source SpaceX and Google ; from gnews:21a8daa8b823bcbbfe13cbf8 2026-08-06] Under a disclosed agreement, Google will pay SpaceX up to $920 million per month as capacity containing approximately 110,000 Nvidia GPUs ramps up.\\n    Quote: One disclosed agreement covers access to infrastructure containing approximately 110,000 Nvidia GPUs, with Google agreeing to pay SpaceX as much as $920 million per month as capacity ramps up, according to a SpaceX SEC filing .\\n- e1498 [fact; TechRepublic, citing a SpaceX SEC filing; original source SpaceX and Anthropic ; from gnews:21a8daa8b823bcbbfe13cbf8 2026-08-06] A separate agreement gives Anthropic access to infrastructure containing approximately 325,000 Nvidia GPUs, with payments to SpaceX of up to $1.25 billion per month as capacity comes online.\\n    Quote: A separate agreement with Anthropic, disclosed in a separate SpaceX SEC filing , involves access to infrastructure containing approximately 325,000 Nvidia GPUs and payments of up to $1.25 billion per month as capacity comes online.\\n- e1876 [party_statement; Elon Musk, quoted by fool.com (analyst); original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:4a6db5f4d17bc985e47b0e49 2026-08-15] SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX expects to have over 2 GW of compute by the end of 2026 and cumulative compute online closer to 10 GW than 5 GW by the end of 2027.\\n    Quote: We expect to end this year with over 2 gigawatts of compute. And probably our cumulative compute online by the end of next year will be several times higher. It may, let's say, be closer to 10 gigawatts of compute than 5 gigawatts of compute.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1863; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1877 [party_statement; Elon Musk, quoted by fool.com (analyst); original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:4a6db5f4d17bc985e47b0e49 2026-08-15] SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX had decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA systems in the future, citing the Vera Rubin architecture.\\n    Quote: Additionally, Elon Musk said SpaceX would only use Nvidia systems in the future. \\\"We've decided to build exclusively on Nvidia because we think the Vera Rubin architecture is the best architecture,\\\" he told analysts. \\\"We think it's the best AI computer.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1495; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1942 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-17; from nvidianews:nvidia-guarantees-sb-energy-s-ports-pike-technology-campus-in-ohio-to-exclusively-host-nvidia-ai-compute 2026-08-17] PORTS-Pike\\u2019s planned capacity is expected to come online in phases beginning in 2028.\\n    Quote: The planned capacity is expected to come online in phases beginning in 2028.\\n- e1953 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-17; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000069:nvda-20260817.htm 2026-08-17] OpenAI plans to use Portsmouth Site capacity supporting approximately 4.25 GW of IT load to deploy NVIDIA\\u2019s DSX AI factory platform.\\n    Quote: OpenAI, as the tenant, will utilize capacity at the Portsmouth Site supporting approximately 4.25 gigawatts of IT load to deploy NVIDIA\\u2019s full-stack DSX AI factory platform, subject to limited exceptions.\\n- e1972 [third_party_view; CRN, quoting an unnamed U.S. systems-integrator executive; original source Unnamed U.S. systems-integrator executive ; from gnews:0fde651790f7d387a0d4836b 2026-08-17] An unnamed U.S. systems-integrator executive said customers were urgently seeking equipment because of a shortage.\\n    Quote: \\u201cThere\\u2019s definitely a shortage out there. We have very large companies coming to us, saying, \\u2018Hey, can you find it somewhere in a warehouse?\\u2019 People are desperate to get the consumables, so that that\\u2019s a little more telling,\\u201d he added.\\n- e2115 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar estimates NVIDIA Data Center revenue will exceed $300 billion in FY2027 and perhaps $500 billion in FY2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia should generate well over $300 billion of data center revenue in calendar 2026, which is effectively fiscal 2027, and perhaps over $500 billion in fiscal 2028.\\n- e2146 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:712a161a59386d843542f6ac 2026-08-19] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, about $2 billion above the top of its earlier $78.0 billion-plus-or-minus-2% guidance range; Data Center revenue was $75.2 billion, up 92%.\\n    Quote: In May, Nvidia reported $81.6 billion of fiscal first-quarter revenue -- about $2 billion above the top of the $78.0 billion, plus-or-minus-2%, range it had guided to three months earlier. Growth ran 85% year over year and 20% sequentially, with data center revenue climbing 92% to $75.2 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e204, e2340; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2153 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source SpaceX (10 GW target); fool.com (analyst) ($150 billion\\u2013$250 billion chip-spending estimate) ; from gnews:7ad5bd51d8d392d6f45c966f 2026-08-19] SpaceX is targeting 10 GW of compute by the end of 2027; the author estimates it will spend $150 billion\\u2013$250 billion on chips.\\n    Quote: SpaceX is targeting 10GW of compute by the end of 2027, meaning it will spend an estimated $150 billion-$250 billion chips.\\n- e2179 [third_party_view; Brendan Burke, quoted by The Verge; original source Brendan Burke ; from gnews:9d8a9b8bf88a2be11484a45e 2026-08-19] AI industry analyst Brendan Burke said some Nvidia-referenced projections assume $70 billion of revenue per gigawatt per year, while operators are not currently achieving that level.\\n    Quote: In some cases, the projections are for $70 billion a year in revenue per gigawatt, which is not what anyone in the field is getting today.\\n- e2221 [party_statement; Poolside, in its investor letter as quoted by thenextweb.com; original source Poolside ; from gnews:7c42792d6f3e1fbdc877492e 2026-08-21] Poolside said it failed to raise $2 billion in time to secure a 40,000-GPU GB300 cluster that was to come online in January, and lost the cluster.\\n    Quote: At the end of last year, we had a 6 week window in which to raise $2 billion dollars to pay for a 40,000 GB300 cluster coming online in January. We didn\\u2019t close it in time, and we lost the cluster.\\n- e2222 [party_statement; Poolside, in its investor letter as quoted by thenextweb.com; original source Poolside ; from gnews:7c42792d6f3e1fbdc877492e 2026-08-21] Poolside argued that frontier models next year would require clusters more than an order of magnitude larger, with physical data-center space and contracted compute among the constraints.\\n    Quote: Next year\\u2019s frontier, it argues, needs \\u201cfar more than an order of magnitude larger cluster\\u201d. The constraint \\u201cis not only capital, it is physical data center space and contracted compute\\u201d.\\n- e2228 [third_party_view; Insider Monkey; original source Meta; Microsoft; OpenAI; Oracle ; from gnews:5949ab20902baf1daa4c38bd 2026-08-21] Meta plans to deploy up to six gigawatts of AMD chips, while Microsoft, OpenAI, and Oracle have also committed to AMD's Helios system.\\n    Quote: Major customers have already committed: Meta plans to deploy up to six gigawatts of AMD chips, and Microsoft, OpenAI, and Oracle have all signed on.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e633; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2279 [rumour; Reuters; original source Bloomberg News, citing people familiar with the process 2026-08-22; from gnews:8607f6edfa61d5ed94357c7f 2026-08-23] Bloomberg News, citing people familiar with the process, reported that prices for servers containing Nvidia AI chips will rise by more than 15% in many cases on systems shipped early next year, including Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell systems; the increases vary by chip generation and memory configuration.\\n    Quote: Some of Nvidia\\u2019s largest customers have been told prices of servers containing its AI chips will rise by more than 15% in many cases with memory chip costs soaring, Bloomberg News reported on Saturday. The price hikes will go into effect on systems shipped early next year and will impact systems including those with the flagship Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips, the report added, citing people\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2319, e2679; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2282 [fact; powermag.com, reporting Cloverleaf's announcement; original source Cloverleaf Infrastructure 2026-08-21; from gnews:4f1513663152e58106526751 2026-08-23] Cloverleaf said on August 21, 2026, that it entered a strategic partnership with NVIDIA to advance U.S. digital-infrastructure development.\\n    Q