ta-center economics require launch costs of $50\\u2013$100 per kilogram, compared with roughly $600 per kilogram for a Falcon 9 at the time.\\n    Quote: Bain estimated in July that the math only works once a launch costs $50 to $100 per kilogram, against roughly $600 for a Falcon 9 today.\\n- e3018 [fact; Fortune; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:a0836f078f1ee82cb6a99fa4 2026-08-27] NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $89.0 billion in fiscal Q2 2026, up 117% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center revenue reached $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2855, e3029; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e3021 [party_statement; Colette Kress, quoted by Fortune; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; from gnews:a0836f078f1ee82cb6a99fa4 2026-08-27] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said non-hyperscaler AICE customers are expected to represent roughly half of NVIDIA's Data Center business; she identified sovereign, regional NeoCloud, enterprise edge and air-gap data centers as part of AICE.\\n    Quote: \\u201cHyperscalers will remain a major growth driver, but non-hyperscaler growth, our AICE segment, spanning sovereign, regional NeoClouds, enterprise edge and air-gap data centers, will represent roughly half of our data center business,\\u201d Kress said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2547; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e3022 [fact; Fortune; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:a0836f078f1ee82cb6a99fa4 2026-08-27] NVIDIA reported $49 billion of fiscal Q2 2026 Data Center revenue from hyperscalers, up 13% sequentially; the article says the rest of the segment revenue came from ACI&E customers.\\n    Quote: That $89 billion in Data Center revenue splits into $49 billion from hyperscalers, up 13% sequentially, and what Nvidia calls ACI&E \\u2014 enterprise, industrial and NeoCloud cu