year and compute as a subset implies a gross total-revenue uplift below roughly 10%, or less than the $431.2 billion rung-1 threshold. The resulting central estimate is in the low $400 billions,\n\nraw 2 About $425 billion for FY2027: retain the prior roughly $420 billion central outlook supported by reported Q1 revenue and Q2 guidance, then allow a limited uplift for the $20 billion stand-alone CPU forecast because the planned fall launch leaves few selling quarters and shipments are not yet shown. The resulting estimate remains below $431.2 billion. The rung remains 2, but the implied outlook is modestly higher than the previous roughly $420 billion estimate because management’s specific $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast is new evidence. It is substantially discounted for the late planned launch and lack of volume in hand; new product-transition and China-substitution risks temper, but do not erase, that increase.\nold-1 978 judge [4924, 100790] True\nThesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\n[Not shown: 99 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n\n- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.     Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\n- e154 [third_party_view; tikr.com; original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis expects NVIDIA data-center compute revenue in the second half of FY2027 to be 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.     Quote: SemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.\n- e234 [third_party_view; tradingview.com; original source Bloomberg ; from gnews:a09988fd9c5c1a739a14f873 2026-07-08] Bloomberg-tracked estimates project NVIDIA FY2027 profit of $228 billion and total revenue of $393 billion, growth of 90% and 82%, respectively.     Quote: The company is projected to generate $228 billion in profit on $393 billion in sales in fiscal 2027, which ends Jan. 31, representing expected growth of 90% and 82%, respectively\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is Wall Street’s approximately $393 billion FY2027 revenue estimate, which sits in rung 2. The evidence continues to imply an outcome near $430 billion: SemiAnalysis’s estimate is 20% above consensus for second-half data-center compute, not for all revenue, and the prior calculation attributes roughly $37 billion of upside to that portion. That leaves the forecast just below rung 1’s $431.2 billion boundary. Q1 actual revenue and Q2 guidance support a strong run-rate, while the newly specified product delays and cancellations, China substitution, and limited licensed H2\n\nraw 1 About $435 billion: starting from the prior estimate of roughly $430 billion after applying SemiAnalysis’s 20% second-half data-center compute upside to consensus (e154), I allow a net few billion dollars for a discounted share of the $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU forecast (e65), given the planned fall launch and limited selling quarters (e64). The prior estimate was roughly $430 billion, below the rung 1 threshold. The FY2027 stand-alone CPU sales forecast, discounted for the fall launch and execution risk, raises the implied estimate by enough to cross that boundary; this changes both the implied figure and the rung.\nold-1 3464 judge [4924, 93816] True\nThesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\n[Not shown: 785 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n\n- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.     Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392–393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The forecast remains about $440 billion, above the rung-1 boundary of $431.2 billion: Q1 revenue was $81.6 billion, management guided Q2 to $91 billion, and analysts estimate Q3 at $103.1 billion. Those quarterly figures support a strong run-rate, while the carried evidence that H2 data-center compute could exceed Wall Street expectations supports an outcome above the reference. The new SpaceX estimate of up to $300 billion in potential orders is not counted in full: it is conditional, and the a\n\nold-1 4163 judge [4924, 78468] True\nThesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\n[Not shown: 949 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n\n- e65 [party_statement; fool.com analyst Adria Cimino; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales in FY2027.     Quote: Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is Wall Street's roughly $392–$393 billion FY2027 estimate, which is rung 2. The quarterly path remains above that reference: Q1 was $81.6 billion, BofA expects $94–$95 billion for Q2 versus the $91 billion company guide, and sees potential Q3 guidance of $107–$108 billion. Carrying 12% sequential growth into Q4 gives about $404 billion before any incremental contribution from CPU or SpaceX demand; those provide upside, but their FY2027 contribution is uncertain and partly spans later periods. Morningstar's 80% growth model and the roughly $400 billion estimate are coun\n\nold-2 1788 judge [4924, 49829] True\nThesis v14 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n[Not shown: 166 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n\n- e238 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year.     Quote: For the first quarter of fiscal 2027, revenue jumped 85% year-over-year (YOY) to $81.6 billion, easily beating analyst expectations.\n- e240 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff 2026-07-12] NVIDIA management forecast approximately $91 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 revenue, assuming no China data-center sales.     Quote: Management also issued strong guidance, forecasting approximately $91 billion in Q2 revenue despite assuming no China data-center sales because of export restrictions.\n- e878 [party_statement; Dave Kovaleski, The Motley Fool; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:bfd479c07812f14b70042fda 2026-07-24] NVIDIA's current-quarter revenue outlook calls for $91 billion, up 11% sequentially.     Quote: So, while it's beating the benchmarks, the return has fallen short of recent years, despite ridiculously strong revenue and earnings growth numbers and a current quarter outlook that calls for revenue to increase 11% sequentially to $91 billion.\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, based on the reported 82% growth estimate, which is rung 3 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The Q1 result of $81.6 billion and Q2 guidance of $91 billion imply about $387 billion for the year if Q1-to-Q2 sequential growth of roughly 11.5% carries through Q3 and Q4. Order and delivery evidence, as well as easing export access, support some upside toward the reference; memory constraints and the lack of quantified FY2027 China sales or product volumes limit it. The implied outcome remains below $400 billion, so it does not cross a ru\n\nold-2 5474 judge [4924, 27851] True\nThesis v21 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue growth in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's total revenue grow in FY2027?\n[Not shown: 19 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The market reference is LSEG's FY2027 estimate of $396 billion, or 83% growth, which is rung 1. The reported first two quarters and Q3 guidance already total about $285.8 billion; carrying the Q3 sequential growth rate (108/96.2, about 12.3%) into Q4 implies roughly $121.3 billion in Q4, or about $407 billion for FY2027. Against FY2026 revenue of $215.94 billion, that implies about 88.5% growth, comfortably above the 60% rung-1 boundary. The reported acceleration, Q3 guide, and the specific estimate that revenue could exceed $400 billion if Q4 grows further support that run-rate.\n\nold-3 3348 judge [4924, 74246] True\nThesis v3 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA report as total revenue for FY2027?\n[Not shown: 924 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n\n- e1116 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source SemiAnalysis ; from gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26] SemiAnalysis forecast NVIDIA data-center revenue in the second half of FY2027 to exceed analyst estimates by 20%.     Quote: And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second hal