{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '2559,2572p;3049,3068p;6457,6477p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; rg -n 'D(720|719)' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; sed -n '118,130p' docs/prd/06-views.md\"","aggregated_output":"## D198 · 2026-10-27 · A judgement reads the evidence in view, not everything in force\n\n- Alex, 2026-10-27, on the finding that evidence in force grows without bound (v6, NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center revenue: 574 pieces after six weeks, 415 never cited by any of its 33 judgements; 40 of 316 judge requests over 30k tokens, max 67k; judging 38% of spend and rising; the run's stall came from enumerating 300+ ids): \"这是什么问题你深度思考之后改\".\n- What the problem is (design: `docs/research/2026-10-27-evidence-in-view/design.md`): two mechanisms. (1) Extraction links wide: `role-v14.txt` says \"every statement and number is, by default, evidence linked to a shown thesis\" and also \"link only to the thesis it directly concerns; omit statements with no thesis\"; the model attaches every business mention to the broadest thesis (2.5 pieces per item on v6; a liquid-cooling note, a SuperPOD purchase, a rack R&D story). Not changed here; the prompt audit is reported to Alex as the next slice. (2) Judging-v4 reads everything in force every time (\"Rejudge from all evidence currently in force\"); an analyst keeps what his view rests on and reads what arrived.\n- Decided: from judging-v5 (`poc-v30`) a judgement of a thesis is shown, of its evidence in force, the arrivals (pieces linked after the thesis's fifth most recent judgement, READINGS = 5; everything when it has fewer) and the basis (pieces cited by one of its last ten judgements, BASIS = 10). The rest stays in force in the ledger; the request names the count (\"[Not shown: N more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\"); the answer may cite and push only shown ids, checked against the request that was sent (`judging.evidence_shown_in`, as the theme block is). Nothing leaves the ledger; the repeat / replace / new rules, \"in force\", the rejudgement triggers, eligibility, the theme block and dependencies are unchanged. Gated on the prompt version: v4 ledgers and configurations read in force as before.\n- Measured ($0, `census.py`, two ledgers): among judgements after a thesis's first, the rule would have hidden 1.5% (nvda-3m) and 1.1% (r5) of the citations the judge actually made (22 of 325 and 7 of 261 judgements lose one cite, mostly third-party risk mentions listed in a \"pull the other way\" sentence); shown mean 95 and 109 pieces where in force averaged 225 and 178. Tighter rules hid 16% (cited last time + new since) or kept growing (ever cited + three readings). Arm beside control (`ab.py`, $0.03): v6, v11, v22 from the same ledger state, v4 fresh against v5: the same rung and `changed` on all three, reasons consistent, request tokens 76,476 → 12,516, 53,151 → 11,204, 25,806 → 8,425. Tests: `tests/test_evidence_in_view.py` (4, red first); suite 199.\n- Applied to the three-month run at a day boundary: 2026-08-18 finished under `poc-v29` on the serial path (the parallel runner refuses a half-done day after a code change), `poc-v30` from 2026-08-19. Prompt `judging-v5` is `judging-v4` with the first rule reworded (2,264 → 2,437 characters; the growth is the sentence that says what is not shown and who carries it).\n- Also in this slice, from the stall at 2026-08-07: a code-refused judgement answer is retired from the archive's lookup (`model.Archive.retire`; the row stays; schema-refused and malformed rows already carried a suffix), so a resume re-samples instead of replaying the refused answer; and the request's closing line names a closed set that exists (\"cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis\"; \"a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views\"): 4 of the run's 5 retried first answers had put theme ids into cites_views.\n\n## D199 · 2026-10-27 · One extraction call stays; its gate rule becomes decidable and the empty-answer exit goes (v16, poc-v32)\n\n- Alex, on the instability trace: \"不这个一次一定是可以搞过的 → 读明白，抽取 … 这一定是prompt 的问题，你如果再拆成两步这个消耗的luna calls 太多了\"; then \"你要反思自己的错误，然后下去改然后马上测试啊，不要一直diverge scope\". The two-call design (`docs/research/2026-10-27-two-step-extraction/design.md`, two review rounds) is recorded and not built.\n- Found ([log](research/2026-10-27-single-call/log.md)): the run's 493 empty extraction answers in 1,553 calls (32%; 65% of the first week's items) were the model following the written rules: \"every statement is, by default, evidence\" against \"link only to the thesis it directly concerns … omit statements with no thesis\", decided once per page, so a customer, partner or product page was omitted whole (asked directly, the model names that rule: a national lab buying Vera Rubin CPU servers \"describes a customer project, not NVIDIA's reported sales\"). The five-send benchmarks before this were sent with an alphabetised schema (the archive stores sorted keys; production sends the dict as built) and overstated the empties; in production order no send of three articles was empty. A second mistake stopped the first week test: code edited while the parallel runner was mid-day.\n- Decided: extraction v16 (`role-v16.txt`: one rule, \"a statement is evidence to each shown thesis whose answer its fact or figure makes more or less likely\", with the customer/supplier/competitor/regulator and policy cases and the omit cases; the closing line \"Return empty arrays only for a page with no business content\", `extraction.EMPTY_LINE_FROM`; v12's admission reasons, `V12_REASON_VERSIONS`). `poc-v32.json` is the configuration. Measured on the same 98 items of 2026-07-01..07 against the paused run (v12, whole pages): empty handovers 65% → 32%, the model's own empties 55 → 8, evidence per item 1.1 → 2.7, candidates 9 → 11, week cost $0.22. v17 (`poc-v33`, an account per mandatory paragraph in the same call) held the piece set across sends (A3: Jaccard 0.6–0.8 against 0.4–0.5) but tripled the pieces with court-case minutiae and opened 5 theses where v16 opened 11, at twice the extraction and matching cost: refuted for production, kept as the record of the shape.\n## D260 · 2026-11-02 · A judgement is a forecast against a reference: the rung the evidence in force makes most likely, judged against what the market expects; the answer states the reference and the implied outcome (judging-v6)\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-02, asked whether the judge's stance should stay an absolute-band lookup (the current reading) or be judged relative to expectations: \"门槛没有标准，按照你作为 hedge fund analyst 的认识来定\". Decided by Claude under that delegation: the definition below is Claude's analyst judgement, recorded as such, not Alex's words.\n- Mechanism (arm 4 draw 1, all 356 judgements read, `research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/review-2026-11-02.md` §7 and `proof-judging-v6.txt`): 8 of 314 later judgements moved a rung and 320 of 356 reasons say \"remains\"; the FY2027 total revenue question was judged 12 times at neutral while its upward pushes grew from 1 to 35, every reason \"the $392 billion estimate is inside the band … does not establish $400 billion\"; the gross-margin judgement wrote \"leans toward rung 2 but does not warrant moving\"; the 2027 hyperscaler-capex theme stayed below the band management's own forecast names because the forecast \"is not fact\". The judging-v5 prompt asked the judge to \"be precise about what is established\" and said only fact can establish a rung: the model read \"established\" as the bar for leaving a rung and the consensus figure as the anchor. A second cause stays open: the ladder's neutral band is written at opening without naming what it compares against (PRD 06 ladder rule 2 asks for it); that is the extraction's rule and is not changed here.\n- Decided, the analyst's rule: (1) the rung is a forecast, the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. (2) The reference is what the market expects: consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline; a consensus or guidance figure alone keeps the judgement at the reference's rung and, once other evidence has arrived, is never the reason to stay. (3) The judge derives the outcome the evidence implies (reported figures and guidance give a run-rate; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it), checks it against the dependencies' conclusions (a part cannot exceed its whole; a figure spanning several periods or products gives the question only its share), compares it with the rung boundaries and moves when it crosses one. (4) Weight follows tier and novelty: fact fixes what is known; party statements are the strongest forecast input; third-party views and rumours move the forecast in proportion to their specificity; a repeat, another outlet or an opinion with no new fact or calculation moves nothing. (5) The answer states `reference` and `implied_outcome` as required fields from `judging-v6` (`judging.schema_for`; code refuses an empty one; stored beside the rung in `judgements.reference` / `implied_outcome`; a ledger written before v6 gains the columns at its first write), so the D3 stance gold can read the reasoning, not only the rung. Written where the judgement is specified: PRD 06 State rules (`docs/system-design/new-facts.js` STATE_RULES, the generated block) and this record.\n- Proof (`proof-judging-v6.txt`, `proof-judging-v6b.txt`: the last stored judgement request of 14 theses of arm 4 draw 1 re-sent once each under v6 and read beside the stored v5 answers, about a cent): every answer names the reference and derives the implied outcome. Six rungs moved against the stored ones: non-GAAP gross margin 3 → 2 (Q1 reported 75% and Q2 guided 75% sit on the rung-2 boundary), 2027 hyperscaler capex 4 → 5 (management's own \"more than $1 trillion\"), China accelerator share 4 → 5 (share \"fallen to zero\"), China H200 authorization No → Yes (a reported plan to permit named firms and reported progress in reviews, six months before the deadline), Data Center revenue 2 → 3 ($355–360 billion derived from Q1 and the total-revenue proxy, below the $360 billion boundary), Blackwell+Rubin revenue 2 → 1 in the first send by annualising a two-year \"$1 trillion visibility\" figure to $545 billion, a part above its whole: clause (3)'s consistency check was added for it and the re-send gives rung 2 ($320–360 billion, a share of the Data Center segment). Total revenue stays at 3 with the arithmetic shown (H1 $172.6 billion from Q1 and the Q2 guide; $400 billion needs more than $227 billion in H2). Prompt 2,453 → 3,782 characters: the request defined neither what a rung means nor a reference and its \"established\" wording forced the defect; the two sentences that carried it were cut. Configuration `poc-v90` (poc-v89 with judging-v6); the default stays `poc-v69`. Tests: `tests/test_stance_fields.py`. Measured by re-sent stored requests, not by an arm (Alex: \"不要再跑这些有的没的臂了\").\n\n## D261 · 2026-11-02 · A ruler or gold that is built and reviewed is never changed again\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-02, while the judgement ruler (D3) was being built: \"你的尺子做出来和金标真的不能再动了，已经造成了3天的各种漂移，非常的严重，我需要你记录到我们的决策和原则里面，我们做完的review过的尺子金标不能再动了\".\n- The drift he names, counted: the extraction ruler went from the first standard (D229, 2026-10-29) to the signed page version 3 (D243, the same day) to version 5 (D250, 2026-10-31), the gold was re-derived from version 3 to version 5 with 62 recorded changes, and every score between D248 and 2026-10-31 had been read against the version-3 gold (the \"假的测量\" of 2026-10-31); the judgement step had no ruler at all until today. Three days of numbers that cannot be compared with each other.\n- Decided (his words; written at the decision it governs, AGENTS §7.1): once a ruler and its gold have been reviewed (readers who did not write them read them against the decisions; Alex signed), neither the definition nor the gold changes again. A defect found later is a dated correction record beside the gold (`corrections-*.json`), printed with every score and never merged in. A new definition is a new version that only his decision opens, with its own gold derived from scratch; old scores stay as they were and are never compared with the new. A ruler under construction is edited only until that review, and nothing is scored against it before.\n- What is locked by this record: the extraction standard page (`docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html`, version 3 signed D243, with D250's four boundaries and counting unit) and `golden-v5/` (sha256 in `golden-v5.sha256`): no version 6; the loop's desk ruler (`research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/README.md`, D1–D3). What is still under construction and locks at his review: the judgement ruler `research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md`, its reader brief, question list and gold `gold-v1.json` (the sign-off is a separate record).\n\n## D262 · 2026-11-02 · The judgement ruler (D3) and its gold are fixed: the gold is reader B's answers; frozen at sha256 636c056d1e4252fa…\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-02, after reading the two readers' reasoning on every disagreement (readers A and B differed on 3 of 23 questions by range or yes/no and on 12 of 20 direction labels; the system disagreed with the draft on the total revenue and Vera CPU questions in every draw): \"那就不要理A了；我们就go with B 好吧，定下尺子金表\".\n- Fixed by that decision: the ruler `research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md` (unit: a gold question at the window end 2026-07-14; material: golden-v5's 309 items by sha; a numeric question agrees when the gold's implied range overlaps the band of the rung the judge chose, a yes/no question when the answers match; the two null baselines printed with every score); the question list and the must_open mapping as built (`questions-v1.json`, 22 seeds + the Ohio guarantee); the gold `gold-v1.json` = reader B's 23 answers verbatim (B: a fresh-context Claude reader, model-authored, 3 questions marked no material: CoWoS capacity, semiconductor growth, SK Telecom), sha256 `636c056d1e4252fa759660803067e6ccd67a81067b012905e918aa9dec4005f2` in `gold-v1.sha256` and in `score_judgement.py`, which refuses any other. Reader A (Codex) stays beside it as the control record (`reader-A.json`, `reconcile-v1.md`): its distance from B is the stick's own noise. Claude's two proposals of the afternoon (a three-level score when readers split; toss-up questions excluded) were not adopted: one range per question, every question counts. From here D261 applies: nothing in the ruler or the gold changes; a defect is a dated record in `corrections-v1.json`.\n- First measurement on the frozen gold (`score-r4-identity.txt`, arm 4, three draws, judging-v5): judged questions agreeing 13/19, 15/18, 12/17; the lookup baseline (the rung containing the gold's reference figure) 9/15, 8/14, 8/14; always-neutral 5/11, 5/10, 5/10. Disagreeing in every draw: FY2027 total revenue (gold $400–435 billion, judge neutral by consensus lookup) and Vera CPU revenue (gold $8–18 billion, judge the top rung on management's $20 billion at face value); in two of three: the financing-commitments total (gold $25–60 billion, judge $10–20 billion). Never judged in any draw: NVIDIA's own capex (q12); the operating margin in two draws (q10): evidence never reached them, counted apart as \"no judgement\". The new judge (`judging-v6`, D260) has no run to score yet; a run on `poc-v90` is Alex's call.\n\n## D537 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency-selection census has one clear case and negative controls; before any send, the edge's effect on filing and judging is proved on stored data (council consensus, reflection round 28)\n\n- **The census** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/DEPENDENCY-SELECTION-CENSUS.md`): over the fifteen\n  questions the old system opened in the stored unseeded fresh week, 97 pairs on which a dependency could have been chosen (each\n  new question against the questions it was compared with and its same-article siblings). Claude's own read written first, then\n  two blind readers of different model families (the same mark on 68 of 97; both mark \"the new question would want the earlier\n  one's conclusion\" on 11, three of them strong). One clear case: data-center revenue FY2027 against total revenue FY2027 (the\n  matching engine recorded \"part of\", 1.00): the total's conclusion carried the year's consensus ($406.05 billion from 09-23,\n  about $412 billion from 09-25) and the $108 billion guidance (from 09-26), none of them anywhere in the data-center question's\n  seven requests; its last answer names the gap and adds a step of its own. Negative controls: the Hugging Face commitments\n  question (\"none\" in all three readings); a weakly granted pair whose line would carry a judge's own assumed numbers. The\n  strongest driver relations of the week (Rubin shipping against the revenue questions) run from a later question to an earlier\n  one and cannot be chosen at creation (D191 point 2, decided, unbuilt).\n- **The council's corrections:** the $96.2 billion quarter was already in all seven requests (not a figure the line would\n  supply); the one \"same question\" pair is one unordered pair; the line would not by itself make the answer supported.\n- **Decided, citing D439:** the pair is a positive lead for the decided dependency-selection slice (D368 item 2), not a\n  validation of it. Before the council reviews any six-call send on it: a bounded proof on stored data of what the edge would do\n  to filing and to judging (a statement linked to both questions, the direction, ordering, cycle rejection, what the judge's\n  request would contain, the unrelated pair as a negative case), with no frozen file changed. Stop, and return the conflict to\n  the council as a design question, if the edge makes filing discard a home contrary to the governing rule, creates a cycle,\n  cannot be represented without conflating the two meanings of the edge, or fails to show the intended conclusion.\n448:**The one next task: the double-count design at $0, through the council, then one recommendation and one test to Alex (D720).**\n449:- Alex opened it (2026-11-22; his words are in the decision log). The citation line stays stopped (D719). poc-v118 stays the product.\nB depends on A | What kind of thesis is A? (For an asset thesis B, state which end of A is bullish for B. For a theme thesis B, state whether their ladders have the same or opposite orientation.)\n├─ Numeric thesis → Read the specified year's thesis (Example: JPM 2027 net interest income → Fed year-end 2027 rate)\n│   ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons\n│   ├─ A is closed → Read A's closing state and reasons (The frozen outcome is what B needs; keep reading it)\n│   └─ A does not exist yet → Wait for it (Record the awaited year on the chain containing the shown thesis. When that year's successor is created, bind the dependency and rejudge B once.)\n└─ Event thesis → Follow succession (Example: NVDA FY2027 China revenue → chip export licensing to China)\n    ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons\n    ├─ A is closed with a successor → Read the successor (Record the change. The successor changes B's input, so rejudge B once.)\n    └─ A is closed without a successor → Read A's closing state and reasons, marked “historical only”\n```\n\n- Theme theses do not judge outcomes as good or bad. Each dependent asset thesis records its direction: rate cuts are bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM.\n- A dependency has one direction. An apparent “good first, bad later” relationship needs an intermediate thesis: steel tariffs raising domestic steel prices and weakening downstream steel demand are separate paths.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nfor a,vs in [(\\\"old-1\\\",[\\\"v4\\\",\\\"v15\\\"]),(\\\"old-2\\\",[\\\"v14\\\",\\\"v16\\\"]),(\\\"old-3\\\",[\\\"v3\\\",\\\"v14\\\"]),(\\\"linked-1\\\",[\\\"v14\\\",\\\"v9\\\",\\\"v21\\\",\\\"v16\\\"]),(\\\"control-1\\\",[\\\"v14\\\",\\\"v16\\\"])]:\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n conn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n print(a)\n for v in vs:\n  print(v, conn.execute(\\\"select count(*) from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>?\\\",(v,0 if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchone())\n  if v in (\\\"v15\\\",\\\"v16\\\") or (a==\\\"old-3\\\" and v==\\\"v14\\\"):\n   print(conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,substr(implied_outcome,1,410) from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? and publication_day>=\\\\x272026-08-26\\\\x27 and (rung\"'!=1 or publication_day='\"\\\\x272026-08-26\\\\x27)\\\",(v,0 if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall())\n if a==\\\"old-2\\\":\n  for r in conn.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,quote,one_line,original_day from evidence where (lower(quote) like \\\\x27%grace cpu%\\\\x27 or lower(quote) like \\\\x27%vera%20 billion%\\\\x27) and (lower(quote) like \\\\x27%revenue%\\\\x27 or lower(quote) like \\\\x27%sales%\\\\x27)\\\"): print(r)\n if a==\\\"old-1\\\":\n  for r in conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,why_changed from judgements where view_id=\\\\x27v4\\\\x27 and call_id in (1569,1691,1782,2313,2695,2756,2807,2907,3293,3464,3612,3767,3902,4200)\\\"): print(r)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json\nfor a,vs in [(\\\"old-1\\\",[\\\"v4\\\",\\\"v15\\\"]),(\\\"old-2\\\",[\\\"v14\\\",\\\"v16\\\"]),(\\\"old-3\\\",[\\\"v3\\\",\\\"v14\\\"]),(\\\"linked-1\\\",[\\\"v14\\\",\\\"v9\\\",\\\"v21\\\",\\\"v16\\\"]),(\\\"control-1\\\",[\\\"v14\\\",\\\"v16\\\"])]:\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{a}/prod\\\") if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(a+\\\"-copy\\\")\n conn=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n print(a)\n for v in vs:\n  print(v, conn.execute(\\\"select count(*) from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>?\\\",(v,0 if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchone())\n  if v in (\\\"v15\\\",\\\"v16\\\") or (a==\\\"old-3\\\" and v==\\\"v14\\\"):\n   print(conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day,rung,substr(implied_outcome,1,410) from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? and publication_day>=\\\\x272026-08-26\\\\x27 and (rung\"'!=1 or publication_day='\"\\\\x272026-08-26\\\\x27)\\\",(v,0 if a.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall())\n if a==\\\"old-2\\\":\n  for r in conn.execute(\\\"select evidence_id,quote,one_line,original_day from evidence where (lower(quote) like \\\\x27%grace cpu%\\\\x27 or lower(quote) like \\\\x27%vera%20 billion%\\\\x27) and (lower(quote) like \\\\x27%revenue%\\\\x27 or lower(quote) like \\\\x27%sales%\\\\x27)\\\"): print(r)\n if a==\\\"old-1\\\":\n  for r in conn.execute(\\\"select call_id,why_changed from judgements where view_id=\\\\x27v4\\\\x27 and call_id in (1569,1691,1782,2313,2695,2756,2807,2907,3293,3464,3612,3767,3902,4200)\\\"): print(r)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"old-1\nv4 (80,)\nv15 (55,)\n[(5094, '2026-08-26', 1, 'Around or slightly above $25 billion: roughly $20 billion of FY2027 standalone Vera revenue, plus Grace CPU revenue running above $5 billion on the reported trailing-12-month basis, carried forward into FY2027.'), (8328, '2026-09-15', 2, 'Approximately $20 billion in total FY2027 CPU revenue: NVIDIA’s nearly $20 billion total-CPU visibility and subsequent $20 billion-level Vera outlook anchor the forecast, while the more-than-$5 billion Grace figure is trailing-12-month revenue and is not established as incremental to that total.'), (8500, '2026-09-16', 2, 'About $20 billion in FY2027 total CPU revenue: management’s total-CPU visibility is nearly $20 billion and its Vera outlook is around $20 billion. Grace’s more-than-$5 billion trailing-12-month revenue is not a separately stated FY2027 increment, so it is not added in full to that outlook.')]\n(1569, 'The rung and implied total remain about $425 billion, but the supporting execution facts changed: Vera Rubin has moved from a planned ramp to production ramp, with systems validated or running at named customer sites. This strengthens confidence in the forecast without supplying a quantified FY2027 revenue increment.')\n(1691, 'The rung and implied estimate remain about $425 billion, but the supporting facts have changed: Rubin is now reported in full production and running at named customer sites, and new performance/ramp evidence strengthens execution confidence. These developments do not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to move the estimate across the $431.2 billion boundary.')\n(1782, 'The rung and implied estimate remain about $425 billion, but the evidence now includes additional specific NVIDIA/SK and NAVER infrastructure plans and competing AMD deployment commitments. These change the relevant demand and competitive facts supporting the same forecast; they do not provide a measure-matched FY2027 NVIDIA revenue revision sufficient to cross a boundary.')\n(2313, 'The rung is unchanged, but the implied estimate is modestly below the prior roughly $430 billion figure: the newly visible AMD customer commitments and reported 10% workload migration add competitive downside, though their FY2027 revenue effect is not quantified. Rubin production and customer use support the forecast but do not provide a quantified volume revision.')\n(2695, 'The rung remains 1, but the supporting facts and implied figure have changed: the current evidence adds reported Rubin production and customer deployments, a reiterated standalone-CPU sales forecast, and a roughly $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin order or sales signal. These support a modestly higher estimate, while the multi-year figures are not counted as FY2027 revenue in full.')\n(2756, 'The rung and approximate implied revenue range are unchanged, but the key execution evidence is stronger: NVIDIA now says Vera Rubin NVL72 entered full production in June, beyond the previously reported production ramp and live deployments.')\n(2807, 'The rung is unchanged, but the supporting information has changed: the supplied Q2 and Q3 growth estimates, the reported order-book figure, and additional quantified AMD deployment plans update the evidence behind the forecast. These additions strengthen the growth case while also sharpening the competitive offset; the implied FY2027 estimate remains around $445–455 billion.')\n(2907, 'The rung is unchanged, but recent evidence updates the key support for the roughly $440 billion forecast: NVIDIA reported Q1 revenue and gave Q2 guidance, Rubin is reported in full production with Q3 shipping and a Q4 ramp, and roughly $1 trillion of Blackwell/Rubin orders was reported. China exclusions and reported delays and competing deployments remain counterweights.')\n(3293, 'The rung and approximate implied revenue are unchanged, but the supporting execution picture has strengthened: NVIDIA now says Vera Rubin NVL72 entered full production and is being deployed, and has stated a $1 trillion Blackwell/Rubin revenue outlook across calendar 2025–2027. Those updates support the existing forecast without justifying a higher FY2027 figure; the SpaceX target remains too late-dated and insufficiently sized for this fiscal year.')\n(3464, 'The rung and approximately $440 billion forecast remain, but recent evidence adds a more specific SpaceX capacity and potential-orders case, alongside concrete competing-chip plans and a current-quarter China exclusion. These change the supporting facts and the balance of risks, even though they do not justify counting the prospective SpaceX spending as FY2027 NVIDIA revenue or moving the forecast across a rung boundary.')\n(3612, 'The forecast moves from rung 1 to rung 2, and the central implied estimate is lower. The specific Q3 estimate and the roughly 12% sequential-growth calculation put the baseline near $391 billion; the in-force second-half compute upside and discounted CPU contribution lift it above $392 billion, but not to $431.2 billion.')\n(3767, \"The central implied estimate remains about $410 billion and the supporting FY2027 run-rate logic remains within rung 2. The new large OpenAI and SpaceX commitments do not change that estimate: the cited OpenAI capacity starts in 2028, while the SpaceX target is principally beyond NVIDIA's FY2027 end date.\")\n(3902, 'The rung is unchanged, but the supporting facts have changed: Rubin NVL72 is reported in full production and running at customers, and SpaceX’s exclusive NVIDIA plan now has a quantified capacity target and a third-party orders estimate. These strengthen demand visibility, but their FY2027 revenue contribution is not established; the implied outcome therefore remains around $410 billion.')\n(4200, 'The previous conclusion was rung 1 with an implied outcome near $434 billion. Rejudging the path from the official $91 billion Q2 guide rather than relying on higher possible quarterly outcomes, and discounting the unshipped CPU target and SpaceX’s multi-period ambition, brings the estimate to about $420 billion and below the rung-1 boundary. Recent SpaceX capex and its exclusive NVIDIA commitment support meaningful upside, but do not establish enough FY2027 shipments to restore the prior estimate.')\nold-2\nv14 (71,)\nv16 (56,)\n[(4942, '2026-08-26', 3, 'About $20 billion in FY2027. The current FY2027 visibility remains the strongest direct forecast; the specific estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each implies roughly $6.5 billion for calendar 2026, while the more-than-doubling expectation is for FY2028 and does not raise the FY2027 figure.'), (5129, '2026-08-27', 3, 'Approximately $20 billion, likely just below that amount: management’s FY2027 visibility is the direct year-specific anchor. The more-than-doubling statement is for FY2028, so it is not carried backward into FY2027.')]\n('e211', 'Nvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.', 'NVIDIA expects Vera CPU sales to reach $20 billion by the end of FY2027.', '')\n('e411', 'Nvidia estimates that Vera will open an entirely new $200 billion total addressable market (TAM), and believes that it will generate nearly $20 billion of CPU revenue this year, positioning itself to become a major player in the server CPU market.', 'NVIDIA estimates Vera will address a $200 billion total market and generate nearly $20 billion in CPU revenue this year.', '')\n('e813', 'It is going to sell its Vera server central processing unit (CPU) as a stand-alone product, anticipating $20 billion in revenue from this product alone in 2026.', 'NVIDIA is anticipating $20 billion of stand-alone Vera server CPU revenue in 2026.', '')\n('e819', \"Nvidia is new to this space, but it's taking the market by storm. The company is launching its first stand-alone CPU this fall as part of its Vera Rubin platform and says it's on track for $20 billion in CPU sales this year.\", 'NVIDIA says it is on track for $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year (FY2027).', '')\n('e896', 'Colette Kress, Executive Vice President and CFO, said Vera “opens a brand new $200 billion TAM for NVIDIA, a market we have never addressed before,” adding: “We have visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, setting us up to become the world-leading CPU supplier.”', 'NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera opens a $200 billion market for NVIDIA and that the company has visibility to nearly $20 billion in total CPU revenue this year, FY2027.', '2026-05-20')\n('e1338', 'Its new Vera server CPU targets a $200 billion market, with $20 billion in Vera revenue expected this fiscal year alone despite only recently shipping as a standalone product.', \"The author estimates NVIDIA's Vera CPU revenue at $20 billion in the current fiscal year, against a target market of $200 billion.\", '2026-07-31')\n('e2311', 'Nvidia is now selling its Vera server CPU as a stand-alone product, and it expects to generate $20 billion in revenue from its sales this year.', 'NVIDIA expects stand-alone Vera server CPU revenue of $20 billion in FY2027.', '')\n('e2542', 'Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 has been a great success, with revenue on a trailing 12-month basis, exceeding $5 billion.', 'NVIDIA Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis as of the Q2 FY2027 call.', '2026-08-26')\n('e3379', \"The company's Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis\", 'NVIDIA Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion over the trailing 12 months.', '')\n('e3975', \"Considering that Nvidia's data center segment makes up more than 90% of the company's total revenue, it's reasonable to forecast Vera Rubin being on track for something close to $20 billion of sales in its first real quarter of shipments.\", 'The author estimated that Vera Rubin could generate close to $20 billion in sales in its first real quarter of shipments.', '2026-09-04')\n('e4311', 'He added that orders for just one product, a computer system that combines 36 Grace CPUs with 72 Blackwell GPUs, is currently experiencing 27% month-to-month sales growth.', 'Jensen Huang said orders for NVIDIA’s system combining 36 Grace CPUs with 72 Blackwell GPUs were growing 27% month over month.', '2026-09-10')\n('e4796', \"Nvidia's Grace CPU business has already surpassed $5 billion in trailing twelve-month revenue,\", 'NVIDIA Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion over the trailing twelve months.', '')\nold-3\nv3 (80,)\nv14 (60,)\n[(5194, '2026-08-26', 2, 'Approximately $20 billion of total CPU revenue for FY2027, using the current FY2027 guide and the company’s near-$20-billion visibility as the direct estimate. Production, shipment and customer evidence support that level, but the more-than-double statement applies to FY2028 and does not provide a quantified FY2027 uplift.')]\nlinked-1\nv14 (71,)\nv9 (71,)\nv21 (54,)\nv16 (65,)\n[(8920, '2026-08-26', 3, 'Approximately $20 billion in FY2027. I carry forward management’s FY2027 outlook: the new more-than-double statement applies to FY2028, while the Grace figure is trailing-12-month revenue and the AWS CPU purchases have no specified period or exact quantity.'), (8924, '2026-08-27', 2, 'About $25 billion: roughly $20 billion of standalone CPU revenue plus an approximately $5 billion annual Grace CPU run-rate based on the disclosed TTM figure. That implies revenue above $20 billion but does not make revenue above $25 billion most likely.'), (8928, '2026-08-28', 2, \"About $25 billion total: approximately $20 billion of FY2027 standalone CPU revenue plus Grace's more-than-$5-billion trailing-12-month revenue, carried forward as a run-rate. That implies rung 2; the evidence does not make a total above $25 billion the most likely outcome.\")]\ncontrol-1\nv14 (59,)\nv16 (43,)\n[(8887, '2026-08-26', 3, 'About $20 billion in FY2027: management’s nearly $20 billion visibility anchors the forecast, and the latest production, shipment, and customer-demand evidence supports execution toward that level. The FY2028 more-than-doubling expectation is for the following year, while the $6.5 billion Wolfe estimate covers Vera in calendar 2026 rather than total CPU revenue for FY2027.'), (8891, '2026-08-27', 3, 'Approximately $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue, based on the FY2027 management visibility and supported by shipments, production and customer-demand evidence. The FY2028 more-than-doubling outlook is not carried backward into FY2027.'), (8895, '2026-08-28', 2, 'Roughly the $20 billion standalone-CPU FY2027 outlook plus some additional Grace CPU revenue, implying total CPU revenue above $20 billion and most plausibly in the low-to-mid $20 billions. The reported Grace figure is over $5 billion trailing twelve months, but because that period straddles fiscal years it is not added in full as an FY2027 amount.'), (8901, '2026-08-29', 2, 'Approximately $24–25 billion for FY2027: start from the roughly $20 billion standalone-CPU outlook and consider Grace’s greater-than-$5 billion TTM revenue as an additional contribution, but discount that historical TTM amount because it is not a distinct FY2027 forecast. This points to the upper end of rung 2, without making revenue above $25 billion the likeliest outcome.')]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json,re,collections\nwho={\\\"old-1\\\":{\\\"v4\\\":[\\\"e65\\\"]},\\\"old-2\\\":{\\\"v14\\\":[\\\"e51\\\"],\\\"v16\\\":[\\\"e51\\\",\\\"e897\\\",\\\"e2542\\\",\\\"e2311\\\"]},\\\"old-3\\\":{\\\"v3\\\":[\\\"e59\\\",\\\"e65\\\"]},\\\"control-1\\\":{\\\"v14\\\":[\\\"e51\\\"],\\\"v16\\\":[\\\"e51\\\",\\\"e897\\\",\\\"e2542\\\",\\\"e2311\\\"]},\\\"linked-1\\\":{\\\"v14\\\":[\\\"e51\\\"],\\\"v16\\\":[\\\"e51\\\",\\\"e897\\\",\\\"e2542\\\",\\\"e2311\\\"]}}\nfor arm,vs in who.items():\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\n cx=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n ax=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n print(arm)\n if arm in (\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"old-3\\\",\\\"old-2\\\"):\n  for r in cx.execute(\\\"select e.evidence_id,l.view_id,l.seq,e.quote from evidence e join evidence_links l using(evidence_id) where e.created_from_input=\\\\x27gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348\\\\x27 and e.quote like \\\\x27%already forecasts%\\\\x27\\\"):print(\\\"filed\\\",r)\n for v,es in vs.items():\n  found=collections.defaultdict(list)\n  for id,day in cx.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)):\n   user=json.loads(ax.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"]\n   for e in es:\n    if re.search(f\\\"(?m)\"'^- {e} \",user): found[e].append((id,day))\n   if re.search(r\"(?m)''^- .+'\"\\\\[.+\\\\] .*(?:(?:\\\\\"'$20 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$20B).{0,65}(?:stand.?alone|Vera CPU)|(?:stand.?alone|Vera CPU).{0,65}(?:'\"\\\\\"'$20 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$20B))\",user,re.I): found[\"by text\"].append((id,day))\n  print(v,{k:(len(ls),ls[0],ls[-1]) for k,ls in found.items()})\n if arm==\"old-2\":\n  for r in cx.execute(\"select evidence_id,quote,one_line from evidence where created_from_input='\"\\\\x27truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\\\\x27 and (lower(quote) like \\\\x27%cpu%\\\\x27 or lower(quote) like \\\\x27%rubin%revenue%\\\\x27)\\\"):print(r)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport json,re,collections\nwho={\\\"old-1\\\":{\\\"v4\\\":[\\\"e65\\\"]},\\\"old-2\\\":{\\\"v14\\\":[\\\"e51\\\"],\\\"v16\\\":[\\\"e51\\\",\\\"e897\\\",\\\"e2542\\\",\\\"e2311\\\"]},\\\"old-3\\\":{\\\"v3\\\":[\\\"e59\\\",\\\"e65\\\"]},\\\"control-1\\\":{\\\"v14\\\":[\\\"e51\\\"],\\\"v16\\\":[\\\"e51\\\",\\\"e897\\\",\\\"e2542\\\",\\\"e2311\\\"]},\\\"linked-1\\\":{\\\"v14\\\":[\\\"e51\\\"],\\\"v16\\\":[\\\"e51\\\",\\\"e897\\\",\\\"e2542\\\",\\\"e2311\\\"]}}\nfor arm,vs in who.items():\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\n cx=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n ax=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"archive.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n print(arm)\n if arm in (\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"old-3\\\",\\\"old-2\\\"):\n  for r in cx.execute(\\\"select e.evidence_id,l.view_id,l.seq,e.quote from evidence e join evidence_links l using(evidence_id) where e.created_from_input=\\\\x27gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348\\\\x27 and e.quote like \\\\x27%already forecasts%\\\\x27\\\"):print(\\\"filed\\\",r)\n for v,es in vs.items():\n  found=collections.defaultdict(list)\n  for id,day in cx.execute(\\\"select call_id,publication_day from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>? order by seq\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)):\n   user=json.loads(ax.execute(\\\"select request_json from model_calls where call_id=?\\\",(id,)).fetchone()[0])[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"]\n   for e in es:\n    if re.search(f\\\"(?m)\"'^- {e} \",user): found[e].append((id,day))\n   if re.search(r\"(?m)''^- .+'\"\\\\[.+\\\\] .*(?:(?:\\\\\"'$20 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$20B).{0,65}(?:stand.?alone|Vera CPU)|(?:stand.?alone|Vera CPU).{0,65}(?:'\"\\\\\"'$20 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$20B))\",user,re.I): found[\"by text\"].append((id,day))\n  print(v,{k:(len(ls),ls[0],ls[-1]) for k,ls in found.items()})\n if arm==\"old-2\":\n  for r in cx.execute(\"select evidence_id,quote,one_line from evidence where created_from_input='\"\\\\x27truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26\\\\x27 and (lower(quote) like \\\\x27%cpu%\\\\x27 or lower(quote) like \\\\x27%rubin%revenue%\\\\x27)\\\"):print(r)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"old-1\nfiled ('e65', 'v4', 49, 'Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.')\nv4 {'e65': (62, (150, '2026-07-05'), (6928, '2026-09-05')), 'by text': (62, (150, '2026-07-05'), (6928, '2026-09-05'))}\nold-2\nfiled ('e51', 'v8', 49, 'Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.')\nv14 {}\nv16 {'by text': (52, (1212, '2026-07-20'), (8735, '2026-09-20')), 'e897': (48, (1644, '2026-07-24'), (8735, '2026-09-20')), 'e2311': (26, (4282, '2026-08-24'), (8735, '2026-09-20')), 'e2542': (24, (4942, '2026-08-26'), (8735, '2026-09-20'))}\n('e2536', \"AWS is deploying an additional 2 million GPUs starting this quarter through the second quarter of fiscal '29 along with Vera, CPUs, some integrated with Rubin, other standalone.\", 'AWS plans to deploy an additional 2 million NVIDIA GPUs beginning in Q2 FY2027 through Q2 FY2029, alongside Vera CPUs, some integrated with Rubin and some standalone.')\n('e2542', 'Our Grace CPU introduced in 2021 has been a great success, with revenue on a trailing 12-month basis, exceeding $5 billion.', 'NVIDIA Grace CPU revenue exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis as of the Q2 FY2027 call.')\n('e2544', \"We continue to see demand for approximately $20 billion in total server CPUs. And based on our customer demand and improving supply outlook, our preliminary expectation is for CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal '28\", 'NVIDIA estimated total server CPU demand at approximately $20 billion and preliminarily expected its CPU revenue to more than double in FY2028.')\n('e2567', 'We see Vera Rubin accounting for about 20% of data center revenue in Q3.', 'NVIDIA expected Vera Rubin to account for approximately 20% of Q3 FY2027 Data Center revenue.')\nold-3\nfiled ('e65', 'v3', 49, 'Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year.')\nv3 {'e59': (58, (147, '2026-07-05'), (6457, '2026-09-01')), 'e65': (11, (147, '2026-07-05'), (993, '2026-07-15')), 'by text': (49, (147, '2026-07-05'), (4298, '2026-08-23'))}\ncontrol-1\nv14 {}\nv16 {'by text': (39, (8762, '2026-07-20'), (8935, '2026-09-07')), 'e897': (35, (8774, '2026-07-24'), (8935, '2026-09-07')), 'e2311': (13, (8879, '2026-08-24'), (8935, '2026-09-07')), 'e2542': (11, (8887, '2026-08-26'), (8935, '2026-09-07'))}\nlinked-1\nv14 {}\nv16 {'by text': (61, (8771, '2026-07-20'), (9025, '2026-09-20')), 'e897': (57, (8784, '2026-07-24'), (9025, '2026-09-20')), 'e2311': (27, (8911, '2026-08-24'), (9025, '2026-09-20')), 'e2542': (25, (8920, '2026-08-26'), (9025, '2026-09-20'))}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import sqlite3,re,pathlib\nbase=pathlib.Path(\\\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad\\\")\nimport re\nW=re.compile(r\\\"total revenue|data center segment revenue|data-center revenue|total cpu revenue\\\",re.I)\nC=re.compile(r\\\"\\\\bCPU|stand-?alone|\\\\bVera\\\\b|\\\\bGrace\\\\b|\\\\bRubin\\\\b|networking\\\",re.I)\nfor arm in [\\\"old-1\\\",\\\"old-2\\\",\\\"old-3\\\",\\\"control-1\\\",\\\"linked-1\\\"]:\n p=pathlib.Path(f\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/{arm}/prod\\\") if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else base/(arm+\\\"-copy\\\")\n cx=sqlite3.connect(\\\"file:\\\"+str(p/\\\"views.sqlite\\\")+\\\"?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n print(arm)\n for v,q in cx.execute(\\\"select view_id,question from views where object_id=\\\\x27NVDA\\\\x27\\\"):\n  if W.search(q) or re.search(\\\"revenue|sales\\\",q,re.I) and re.search(\\\"data.center|overall|company.wide|whole|total|combined|aggregate\\\",q,re.I):\n   rows=cx.execute(\\\"select implied_outcome,reasons from judgements where view_id=? and call_id>?\\\",(v,0 if arm.startswith(\\\"old\\\") else 8737)).fetchall()\n   print(v,len(rows),sum(bool(C.search((a or \\\"\\\")+\\\" \\\"+(b or \\\"\\\"))) for a,b in rows),W.search(q) is not None,q)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
