{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"agent_message","text":"DIAGNOSIS NEEDS CHANGES\n\nThe double count is real, and the judge is the right place to fix it. In linked-1 call `8756`, the judge takes a $387B company-wide forecast, adds $19.5B of CPU sales without showing how much CPU revenue the forecast already assumed, and crosses the $400B boundary. The frozen product makes the same kind of mistake through filed evidence. The diagnosis overstates how many answers show the addition and understates the risk of counting the same upside twice.\n\n## Part 1 — Independent recount\n\nI selected the requested questions by stored ID, excluded copied pilot answers, and classified the arithmetic before reading Claude’s classifications. I counted an addition when the answer put all or part of a component forecast on top of a whole without supporting that increment. The source material usually leaves the exact amount already assumed by consensus or by the run-rate unknown.\n\n| Store and question | Answers read | Call IDs and effect on the rung |\n|---|---:|---|\n| old-1 v4, total revenue | 80 | **26 explicit additions. Yes:** `978`, `1142`, `1208`, `4163`. **No:** `354`, `1236`, `1250`, `4037`, `4074`, `4200`, `4263`, `4456`, `4580`. **Amount too unclear to decide:** `2539`, `2631`, `2727`, `2870`, `2946`, `2978`, `3056`, `3224`, `3464`, `3561`, `3612`, `3767`, `3902`. |\n| old-2 v14 and old-3 v3, total revenue | 71 + 80 | **0 confirmed.** Some adjustments may overlap other adjustments, as detailed below. |\n| linked-1 v14, total revenue | 71 fresh | **4 additions, all rung-decisive:** `8756`, `8759`, `8763`, `8766`. About $387B becomes $406–407B, moving from rung 3 to rung 2. |\n| linked-1 v9, Data Center revenue | 71 fresh | **3 direct additions:** `8761`, `8765`, `8768`. **1 inherited inflated total:** `8769`. None changes the rung. About $356B before the error and $374–376B after it are both in rung 3. |\n| linked-1 v21, revenue growth | 54 fresh | **0.** `8794` says the CPU forecast supports growth but “does not quantify an uplift.” |\n| old-1 v15, old-2 v16, old-3 v14, control-1 v16, linked-1 v16, total CPU | 55 / 56 / 60 / 43 / 65 | **0 confirmed double counts.** I found 99 answers that combined Vera and Grace, including partial allowances for Grace. The source and period distinction is below. |\n\nI also checked the 59 fresh control-1 v14 answers for the comparison in §1. I found zero stand-alone CPU additions.\n\n**Differences from Claude’s classifications and §1**\n\n- **Six old-1 calls move from “added” to unclear.** `1569` says the evidence does not “establish the volume needed to count the $20 billion standalone-CPU target in full.” `1691` says the target “is not counted in full.” `1782` says it is “discounted rather than added in full.” `2313` says “I … discount the unshipped CPU sales target.” `2907` says discounted CPU sales “provide additional support.” `3293` says it “discounts the $20 billion standalone-CPU target.” Those answers leave open whether any CPU dollars were added or carried forward. The same ambiguity occurs outside Claude’s list in `2695`, `2756`, and `2807`, which say “the unshipped CPU target is discounted,” “I discount the $20 billion standalone-CPU forecast,” and “the … unshipped CPU target [is] not counted in full,” respectively. `1334` says it is “carrying forward the prior estimate,” `1462` says “the prior estimate translates that uplift to about $425 billion,” and `2674` says it is “retaining a midpoint near $438 billion.” Those earlier estimates had included CPU dollars, so these are possible carryovers.\n- **Four old-1 rung labels go from “no” to unclear.** `2727` puts the estimate near $450B “with only a discounted contribution” from the $20B CPU target and the multi-year product figure. It gives no amount for either. `3612`, `3767`, and `3902` start around $391B and end around $410B, with the rung boundary at $392B. They cite “a discounted contribution from the standalone-CPU target,” “discounted CPU/Rubin contributions,” or “discounted CPU/Rubin contributions [that] support a modest uplift.” The combined adjustments cross the boundary. The answers do not say whether the other adjustments would have crossed it on their own.\n- **The upside-only column is incomplete.** In old-1, `150` calls the CPU forecast “discounted,” `439` calls it “upside,” `514` calls it an “upside input,” `1853` says it “support[s] upside,” and `5092` calls it an “upside input.” The stated calculations allocate no separate dollars to it. In old-3, beyond `574` and `1575`, the same distinction applies to `645` (“upside indicators”), `732` and `858` (“discounted”), `993` (“not added in full”), `1220` (“supportive”), `1351` (“not added again”), `2558` (“a further upside input”), `2630` (“not sufficiently … incremental”), and `3348` (“CPU expectations … support the upside”).\n- **The linked v9 answers use two different calculations.** `8761`, `8765`, and `8768` explicitly add CPU sales after applying the Data Center share. `8769` says “v14’s approximately $406 billion total-revenue forecast, multiplied by the latest 92% Data Center mix.” It inherits the inflated total, with no separate CPU addition. Also, the 77 old-1 and old-3 answers are counts since filing, rather than counts of requests that showed the target. The archived requests show old-1 `e65` or `e544` in **62** answers and old-3’s explicitly FY2027 `e311` or `e545` in **44**. Old-3 `e65` describes the same quote as “in 2026.” [Diagnosis §1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/DIAGNOSIS.md:7) · [Claude’s classifications](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/readings-claude.json)\n\n## Findings, most serious first\n\n1. **A genuine change in a part can still be counted twice.** Old-1 `572` is a reasonable control for applying a premium only to the relevant share. Old-3 `5195` and `5369` add “roughly $4.1 billion for Q3 guidance above consensus” and then reuse “roughly $40 billion” of second-half compute upside. Q3 sits inside that second half. Old-3 `4101` explicitly avoids a separate Q3 addition “because it overlaps the second-half tracking uplift.” The $396B annual estimate predates the earnings release, so updating the quarters is reasonable. The unanswered question is how much of the older compute premium remains after those updates. The full $40B addition decides the rung in `5195` and `5369`. The amount that overlaps cannot be recovered from the answers. Similarly, old-2 `1788` says the Blackwell/Vera Rubin forecast “supports further growth,” but gives no separate amount from that forecast. The census should distinguish these uncertain cases from clear additions. A search for component names cannot establish the full count. [Candidate lister](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-whole-and-part/census_candidates.py:21)\n2. **The prompt helps explain the mistake, with more than one instruction in play.** The unshipped-target sentence tells the judge how much to credit a target before it has decided whether there is anything new to credit. The consistency sentence only asks whether a part exceeds the whole. The adjacent “a customer’s demand number … shift[s] it” and “a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities” can also invite a calculation before that check. Old-1 `978` is especially revealing. The request shows `e65` and `e544` as the same claim, and the answer calls the $20B forecast “new.” Linked `8759`, `8763`, and `8766` then receive the bad arithmetic in the previous conclusion, which the prompt also says carries evidence left out of view. These are plausible contributors. The stored answers do not establish which sentence caused the choice, and §1’s direct follow-up to the model is still missing. [Judge prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v7.txt:1) · [Working rules §1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:59)\n3. **The judge owns the calculation.** The same judge text produced both `8752`, which correctly kept CPU inside the total, and `8756`, which added it. Old-1 `354` had no dependency and added a discounted amount anyway. The builder labels the linked forecast as the system’s own judgement and gives its period and value. A relation label could identify a part, but it could not identify what consensus already assumed or resolve overlap between two forecast revisions. Filing determines which facts arrive. The judge decides how those facts change the total. The existing checks accepted both answers because they check the answer’s structure and references, rather than its arithmetic. [Request builder](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:161) · [Answer checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:259)\n4. **The Vera-plus-Grace sum is a valid control when the answer uses the Vera-specific forecast.** Old-2 `e897` clarifies “The $20 billion is for standalone CPU.” `e51` repeats the stand-alone scope, and `e2311` explicitly names stand-alone **Vera**. `e2542` separately reports more than $5B of **Grace** revenue over the trailing 12 months. Using that as an estimate for FY2027 is a forecasting assumption. `e896` says “total CPU revenue,” and `e2544` says “demand for approximately $20 billion in total server CPUs,” so the scope matters. Control-1 `8926` addresses that conflict explicitly. Old-2 `7402` gives an even sharper check. It adds Grace and explicitly leaves out the separate $6.5B Vera shipment estimate because that estimate falls inside the Vera forecast.\n5. **Keep the target discount wherever the target legitimately enters the forecast.** D263 supports discounting an unshipped target, including one for a part whose sales were explicitly left out of the whole forecast. Scoping the discount to questions about the product would lose that protection. D260 supports using new, quantified changes against a reference. It does not say every company-wide estimate already prices every published target. I would shorten the discount to “Discount unshipped product targets unless evidence shows volume in hand.” Then, immediately before the answer instructions, add “Adjust totals only by supported changes to what they assume for a part. Sum disjoint, comparable parts.” Shortening the discount and cutting the repeated part-cannot-exceed-whole clause leaves **4,924 characters before and after** in a read-only comparison. Prompt tokens still need checking. The wording is company-neutral and proposed for review. [D260 and D263](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3049) · [Judge prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/prompts/judging-v7.txt:4)\n\n## Recommended next step and test\n\n**Revise the case list and write down the pass and stop conditions at $0.** Keep the eight clear rung-changing answers. Add `354` for the first addition that did not change a rung, `8752` for a judge that handled the same link correctly, and `8761` for an error passed downstream. Keep the positive controls for a valid share adjustment, a part reading its whole, and two disjoint parts. Add one case where the basis explicitly leaves out a part, and one where two adjustments may cover the same sales. Write the expected arithmetic before sending anything.\n\n**One test, after Alex opens a version and approves spend:** send the same stored requests under both texts on the same day, three times each. Reconstruct the production requests, change only the judge text, and read the calculations blind to version. Three draws are enough for a small diagnostic, with limited evidence about other companies or a live chain of forecasts. For the downstream case, give the child the parent answer from that same draw. If the old text does not reproduce the error through both routes, call the test inconclusive. Stop if the candidate still makes a clear double count, passes one downstream, or suppresses a supported revision or disjoint sum. Count errors even when the rung stays the same. This review was read-only, with no model calls."}}
